.SH DESCRIPTION
.B socketley
is one program.
.B "socketley daemon"
runs the daemon, which creates, starts, stops and supervises runtimes.
Most other invocations, such as
.BR "socketley create" " or " "socketley ls" ,
are the command line: each sends its command to the daemon over a unix socket,
and starts the daemon first if none is running.
A runtime made with
.B create
runs in its own worker process, which the daemon restarts if it exits.
In that process, one io_uring event loop on one thread serves the runtime.
The daemon saves each runtime's configuration as a JSON file and restores
every saved runtime when it starts.
.PP
.B socketley
is a high-performance Linux daemon and CLI that manages network runtimes
in a Docker-like style: servers, clients, proxies, Redis-compatible caches,
S3 object storage, authoritative DNS, file sync jobs, an OpenID Connect
provider, JSON document stores, MQTT brokers, mail servers, tunnels, a
Prometheus-compatible metrics store, MCP servers and static web sites.
It supports clustering, WebSocket, Lua scripting, and io_uring async I/O.
.PP
.B "TLS can terminate on a server or cache listener, on both legs of a proxy, and on a client's outgoing connection."
A
.B server
runtime encrypts its own listening port with
.BR \-\-tls " " \-\-cert " " \-\-key ,
and verifies client certificates with
.BR \-\-ca .
A
.B proxy
does the same on its own port and verifies client certificates with
.BR \-\-client\-ca .
The proxy reaches an
.B https://
backend over TLS: it verifies the backend's certificate chain and name, and
presents a client certificate with
.BR \-\-client\-cert " " \-\-client\-key .
A
.B client
runtime connects to its target over TLS with
.BR \-\-tls .
It verifies the chain against
.B \-\-ca
or the system trust store, matches the host from
.B \-t
against the certificate, and presents its own certificate with
.BR \-\-cert " " \-\-key .
Neither check can be switched off.
An unverified TLS client protects against someone recording the traffic, but
not at all against someone in the path who can intercept it.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS on its own listener with the same three flags as a server.
RESP runs on the decrypted stream, so a Redis client needs no change.
The only combination the cache refuses is
.B \-\-tls
together with
.BR \-\-replicate :
replication is not encrypted, so the pair would send the whole dataset over
the network in cleartext.
An
.B mqtt
broker terminates TLS on its MQTT and WebSocket ports with the same three flags.
With
.BR \-\-ca ,
it takes the name in a verified client certificate as the client's user.
.PP
A runtime whose configuration cannot be honoured (for example, one restored
from a state file written by an older release) refuses to start rather than
run in cleartext. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.PP
The io_uring event loop uses advanced kernel features when they are available:
multishot receive (one SQE services many reads)
and registered file descriptors (which skip the per-operation
.BR fget / fput
overhead). Each feature is probed at startup. On older kernels the loop falls
back to standard operations, so no configuration is needed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon
Start the socketley daemon.
When socketley is installed system-wide (with the .deb package or install.sh),
systemd manages the daemon and starts it automatically, so you do not need to
run this command yourself. Use
.B systemctl start socketley
or
.B systemctl enable socketley
to control the service.
.PP
When running from the build directory (development mode), start the daemon
manually:
.B ./bin/Release/socketley daemon &
.PP
The daemon creates its IPC socket at /run/socketley/socketley.sock (system
install) or /tmp/socketley.sock (development mode).
On startup, it restores the saved runtime configurations.
Runtimes that were running when the daemon last stopped are started again
automatically.
.PP
The daemon ignores
.BR SIGHUP ,
so closing the terminal or a stray
.B kill \-HUP
does not stop it.
.PP
.B daemon
accepts
.BI \-\-cpus " set" ,
the ring setting described under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
Here it sets the CPUs of the ring this daemon is about to create, rather than
stating what a runtime requires of its ring.
It is the command-line form of
.BR SOCKETLEY_CPUS ;
when both are given, the flag wins.
.B daemon
also accepts
.BR \-\-fast\-lo ,
.B \-\-fast\-lo\-limit
(below) and the experimental Raft flags described under
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" .
It refuses any other argument.
.IP
These flags cannot be combined with
.B \-\-name
or
.BR \-\-cluster ,
which configure a running daemon.
When none is running, they start one without these flags first.
A ring's CPU set is fixed when the ring is created, so there would be nothing
left for them to change.
.IP
Every runtime runs in its own process and inherits this CPU set, so the set
also decides how many runtimes share a core.
In a test with fifty idle servers beside one server doing a ping-pong, the
median round trip did not change (30.2 against 29.4 microseconds), but the
99th percentile did: 156.5 microseconds on six cores against 51.7 on twelve.
Idle runtimes still cost something, because each one wakes on its own stats
interval.
That cost shows up in the tail latency of whichever runtime is working, and a
wider CPU set is the way to reduce it.
The daemon itself stayed under one per cent of a core with fifty runtimes.
Creating a runtime took the same fourteen milliseconds with five runtimes as
with fifty. Neither of these is worth watching.
.TP
.BR "daemon \-\-fast\-lo " [ own | all | trust ]
Put TCP connections whose two ends are both 127.0.0.1 or ::1 on a kernel
shortcut.
A BPF
.B sockops
program adds both sockets to a sockhash when the connection is established.
An
.B sk_msg
program then places every send directly into the peer's receive queue,
skipping TCP/IP, the loopback device and its softirq. Off by default.
.IP
.B all
is the mode used when no mode is given. It accelerates any local client that
connects to a port one of this daemon's runtimes listens on, and the
connections of the runtimes among themselves.
It also accelerates every connection a runtime makes to a listener on
127.0.0.1, whoever owns that listener.
Examples are a proxy with
.B \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8080
in front of a local application, a client runtime, or a Lua HTTP call to a
local service.
.IP
.B own
accelerates only pairs whose two ends are both this daemon's runtimes, such as
a client runtime connected to a server runtime.
.IP
.B trust
is
.B all
without the receiver rule described below: it also redirects a send into a
foreign receiver.
A foreign client of a cache and a proxy's foreign backend are then
accelerated in both directions.
It is meant for an operator who knows that every local process reading a
socket of such a pair reads with
.BR recv ,
.B read
or
.BR recvmsg .
.RS
.PP
.B Warning:
a byte redirected into a socket waits in an ingress queue that only
.B recvmsg
drains.
A local process never sees those bytes if it reads with
.BR splice (2),
.BR sendfile (2)
or
.BR TCP_ZEROCOPY_RECEIVE ,
or through a relay built on them (such as Go's
.B io.Copy
between two TCP connections, or HAProxy with splicing).
Under
.BR trust ,
the bytes are lost, and neither side gets an error.
In a test, a backend reading with
.B splice
behind a proxy echoed 0 of 500 bytes.
Such readers cannot be detected from the outside, which is why
.B all
never redirects into a foreign socket.
Use
.B all
unless you know every local client and backend.
.B socketley daemon stats
reports which mode is running.
.RE
.IP
Under
.B own
and
.BR all ,
a send is redirected only when the socket that will
.I receive
it belongs to one of this daemon's workers.
That is either the socket of a runtime that connects (the socket is marked
when it connects), or a socket accepted on a runtime's listener.
A send to a foreign receiver goes over ordinary TCP.
Each direction of a pair is decided by its own receiver.
The reason is that a socket receiving through the kernel's ingress queue can
only be read with
.BR recvmsg .
A foreign reader that uses
.BR splice (2),
.BR sendfile (2),
Go's
.B io.Copy
between two TCP connections, or HAProxy's splicing would lose the redirected
bytes, and such readers cannot be detected from the outside.
So the shortcut accelerates only what it can accelerate safely: sends into a
runtime always, and sends out to a foreign peer never (unless
.B trust
is given).
.IP
Results for common setups, measured on one machine with alternating passes:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Runtime to runtime (a client runtime to a server runtime, both ends this
daemon's) is accelerated in both directions under every mode.
64 B round trips are 1.3\(en1.4x faster with one connection and with eight,
and 16 KB round trips about 1.2\(en1.5x.
For a client runtime against a cache with one request in flight, off against
.BR all :
110\(en116K against 83\(en85K round trips a second.
.IP \(bu 2
A foreign client of a cache is accelerated only on the requests it sends; the
replies come back over TCP.
So a workload of small requests and large replies gains little under
.BR all .
.B redis\-benchmark
(50 connections, 4 threads, six alternating passes) measured these requests
per second, off /
.B all
/
.BR trust .
.B GET
.BR \-P1 :
240\(en279K / 222\(en250K / 387\(en667K.
.B SET
.BR \-P1 :
222\(en286K / 197\(en273K / 462\(en632K. GET
.BR \-P16 :
2.3\(en4.0M / 2.7\(en3.4M / 5.3\(en6.9M.
A bulk reader of 64 KiB values:
1377\(en1488 / 1276\(en1322 / 2464\(en3040 MB/s.
A request that carries the payload does gain under
.BR all :
a 64 KiB
.B SET
pipelined 8 deep made 49.9K against 59.1K, 1.18x.
Only
.B trust
gives a foreign client the full gain.
.IP \(bu 2
For a proxy in front of a foreign local backend,
.B all
accelerates the two legs that end in the proxy.
It leaves the two legs that end in a foreign socket on TCP, so a foreign
backend that reads with
.B splice
keeps every byte.
Under
.BR trust ,
both kinds of leg are redirected.
64 B round trips through a proxy to a C echo backend, over one connection:
44\(en48K off, 41\(en45K under
.BR all ,
and 86\(en88K under
.BR trust .
.RE
.IP
The shortcut needs
.B CAP_BPF
and
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
(root, or
.B setcap cap_bpf,cap_net_admin+ep
on the binary) and cgroup v2 at /sys/fs/cgroup.
Without them the daemon logs
.B fast\-lo: unavailable, serving without it
with the reason, and serves on the ordinary path.
.B own
attaches to the daemon's cgroup;
.B all
and
.B trust
attach to the root cgroup.
The daemon's own processes are the workers its supervisor forked, and the
daemon keeps their pids in a map.
Under
.B all
and
.BR trust ,
the program runs for every process on the host, so it accepts only sockets in
the daemon's own network namespace.
The namespace's netns cookie is built into the program at load time, so a
container's identical 127.0.0.1 tuple is left alone.
This needs
.B SO_NETNS_COOKIE
in the kernel.
Without it, the shortcut refuses to attach rather than serve across
namespaces.
The programs and maps are anonymous, and they are detached when the daemon
exits.
.IP
What it costs:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The redirected backlog is bounded by
.BR "daemon \-\-fast\-lo\-limit " "\fIper\-pair\-MiB\fR,\fIglobal\-MiB\fR (default 4,64)."
A redirected send does not wait for the receiver, so the program counts each
pair's sends.
Each time the count passes a mark, the program asks a reconciler thread,
through a BPF ring buffer, for the size of the peer's real queue.
The thread reads it with one exact
.B sock_diag
lookup and answers within tens of microseconds.
If the reader keeps up, the pair's count is reset to the real value, and the
pair stays on the shortcut however much data it moves.
A pair is tainted to TCP when its peer really holds the per-pair bound, or
when its count reaches twice the bound with no answer.
TCP then gives the ordinary
.B EAGAIN
backpressure.
Once the sum across all pairs reaches the global budget, a flag taints new
sends.
Once a second, a sweep recomputes that sum and asks again for any check whose
answer was lost. An idle pair costs nothing.
Without the bound, a peer that stopped reading took 731 MiB before the
kernel's global TCP memory
.RB ( tcp_mem )
ran out. With the default bound it stays near 4 MiB per pair.
.IP \(bu 2
A pair stays tainted for the rest of its life. This keeps bytes in order:
.B tcp_bpf_recvmsg
drains the redirect queue before the TCP receive queue, so a byte sent over
TCP after the taint is still read after the bytes already redirected.
.IP \(bu 2
The bound is not tight in two cases.
First, a pair moving gigabytes a second whose answer is delayed by more than
about a millisecond (the reconciler is starved of CPU) reaches the hard
ceiling and is tainted. This costs speed, not bytes.
Second, pairs that stall together are each checked at least every sixteenth
of the global budget.
So the sum they reach before the flag is raised is the budget plus a
sixteenth per pair.
In a test, eight pairs against
.B 4,12
held 13.4 MiB; checking only at the per-pair marks let them hold 23.5.
.IP \(bu 2
Shortcut bytes skip netfilter, conntrack and the loopback device.
nftables rules on
.BR lo ,
.B tcpdump \-i lo
and the byte counters in
.B ss
do not see them.
.B socketley daemon stats
and the metrics endpoint report what the shortcut is carrying.
.IP \(bu 2
The shortcut covers 127.0.0.1 and ::1, including 127.0.0.1 IPv4\-mapped on a
dual\-stack socket. The rest of 127/8 stays on TCP.
Runtimes listen, and proxies and client runtimes connect, over IPv4.
So a ::1 pair here is a Lua
.B socketley.http()
call to a local service on ::1, or to a name that resolves to ::1 first.
With both ends entered, a ::1 pair gains about as much as a 127.0.0.1 pair
(64 B round trips 1.3x against 1.4x, 16 KB 1.2x against 1.3x).
A call that opens a connection per request gains nothing, because entering
and leaving the sockhash costs about as much as the request saves (\-3% at
64 B).
.IP \(bu 2
A pair enters the shortcut only when both ends are in the map before its first
byte.
A socket that sent a byte over TCP stays on TCP for its lifetime, so no byte
overtakes another. A client sending TCP Fast Open data stays on TCP.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B \-\-protocol tcp
proxy splices, and splice reads a queue that a socket on the shortcut does not
fill. So a proxied pair with a 127.0.0.1 leg stays on the copy path.
.IP \(bu 2
Under
.B own
and
.BR all ,
a send is never redirected into a process that is not this daemon's.
Only that process's sends into one of this daemon's workers are redirected,
and the workers read with
.BR recvmsg .
So a foreign relay that copies between two TCP connections with Go's
.B io.Copy
or HAProxy with splicing keeps every byte on the leg it receives, and does not
need
.B own
to be safe. This is the receiver rule above;
.B trust
gives it up.
.RE
.RS
.PP
.nf
sudo socketley daemon --fast-lo &        # all
sudo socketley daemon --fast-lo own &
sudo socketley daemon --fast-lo trust &  # only if no local reader splices
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "daemon \-\-name " "name " "[\-\-cluster " "dir" "]"
Configure the running daemon for cluster mode.
These flags are sent as IPC commands to the daemon that is already running
(the daemon is started automatically if it is not running).
.B \-\-name
sets the daemon's identity in the cluster.
.B \-\-cluster
enables cluster discovery in the given shared directory (see
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" ).
The name can be changed at any time. If cluster mode is active, the daemon
republishes its cluster file under the new name.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon --name node1 --cluster /shared/cluster
socketley daemon --name new-name   # rename while cluster is active
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "create " "type name " "[flags]"
Create a new runtime instance.
.I type
is one of: server, client, proxy, cache, storage, dns, sync, idp, db, mqtt, mail, tunnel, metrics, mcp, web, or any registered type (see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" ).
.I name
is a unique identifier for the runtime.
.IP
With
.B \-s
and a
.BR \-\-lua " script,"
.B on_start
runs inside this command.
If
.B on_start
raises an error, the runtime is not taken down.
It is created and running, with whatever
.B on_start
set up before it failed.
But the command exits 1 and names the callback and the reason, so a start
whose set-up did not run is not reported as a clean one.
Fix the script and run
.B reload\-lua
to run it again.
.TP
.BI "attach " "type name port " "[\-\-owner " "name" "] [\-\-pid " "pid" "]"
Register an external process as a runtime that appears in
.BR ls / ps .
The daemon does NOT bind the port or forward any I/O; the external process
manages its own network connections. The runtime appears as running and can
be inspected or removed like any other runtime.
.RS
.TP
.I type
Runtime type: server, client, proxy, or cache.
.TP
.I name
Unique name for this external runtime.
.TP
.I port
Port number the external process is listening on (informational only).
.TP
.B \-\-owner \fIname\fR
Optional owner runtime name.
.TP
.B \-\-pid \fIpid\fR
The pid of the process behind this runtime.
The daemon records it so that
.B "socketley stop " \fIname\fR
sends that process
.BR SIGTERM .
Without a pid the daemon has nothing to stop, and stopping only removes the
runtime from the listing.
An SDK-embedded process passes its own
.BR getpid() .
.IP
The pid must belong to the user who issues the command.
The daemon signals the process on the caller's behalf, and it will not do that
for a process the caller could not signal themselves.
On the packaged daemon, whose socket is shared with the
.B socketley
group, doing so would lend out the daemon user's privileges.
A daemon running as root has no such restriction, since root can signal any
process anyway.
.RE
.TP
.BI "add " "path " "[\-\-name " "name" "] [\-s]"
Register an external binary (built with the C++ SDK) and manage it.
The daemon will fork+exec the binary and manage its full lifecycle:
it restarts the binary after a crash, launches it again when the daemon boots,
and handles the normal start/stop/remove commands.
.RS
.TP
.I path
Path to the executable (must exist and be executable).
.TP
.B \-\-name \fIname\fR, \fB\-n\fR \fIname\fR
Name for the runtime. Default: the binary's file name without its extension.
.TP
.B \-s
Start immediately after adding.
.PP
The binary receives the
.B SOCKETLEY_MANAGED=1
and
.B SOCKETLEY_NAME=\fIname\fR
environment variables. When the binary uses
.BR daemon_attach() ,
these variables make it re-attach to the existing runtime entry
and skip the atexit self-removal.
.PP
.nf
socketley add /usr/local/bin/myapp -s
socketley add /usr/local/bin/myapp --name chat
socketley stop chat
socketley start chat
socketley remove chat
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "start " "name|pattern" "... [\-i]"
Start one or more runtimes. Accepts multiple names, glob patterns
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [] ),
or a mix. With
.BR * ,
starts all runtimes that are not running.
.RS
.TP
.B \-i
Interactive mode: start the runtime and open an interactive session.
For a server, stdin is broadcast to its clients, and received messages appear
on stdout.
For a client, stdin is sent to the server, and received messages appear on
stdout.
For a cache, stdin accepts cache commands, and responses appear on stdout
(like redis-cli).
.B \-i
is refused for a proxy: a proxy relays between a client and a backend, and has
no stream of its own for typed input to join.
The refusal comes before the start, so the runtime is left exactly as it was;
see
.BR LIMITATIONS .
Press Ctrl+C to detach (the runtime keeps running).
If the runtime is already running, the session attaches without restarting it.
.IP
A runtime lives in its own process, and the session connects to that process.
Typed input is delivered to the workers that hold the connections.
Each worker is also handed the session's descriptor, so what a client sends
reaches the terminal without passing through the daemon.
Under
.BI \-\-shards " N"
this covers every shard: a typed line reaches the clients of all shards, and
any of those clients can be heard back.
.IP
A session survives a
.B restart
of the runtime it is attached to, and re-attaches to the new workers.
It does not end when the runtime is
.BR stop ped:
nothing notifies the session, so the operator has to press Ctrl+C.
.RE
.IP
For a runtime that runs in a worker process,
.B start
waits for the child to report before it returns.
So the port is accepting connections by the time the command exits.
A refusal that only the child can judge becomes the command's own exit status,
rather than something
.B ls
shows two seconds later.
Exit 1 names the runtime if the worker would not take its configuration, and
gives the reason if the script's
.B on_start
raised an error.
The whole command has one budget of two seconds, not one per runtime.
The children are forked together and come up concurrently, so a
.B start
of twenty runtimes waits about as long as a start of one.
A child that is still starting after that, such as a storage runtime reading
a large store's files after a crash, is waited for by the CLI rather than the
daemon, so the daemon stays free.
The CLI asks the daemon five times a second whether the runtime is serving
yet, for as long as its workers keep using CPU.
The CLI exits 2 once the workers have used no CPU for 15 seconds.
.BR create " \-s"
and
.B restart
wait the same way.
.TP
.BI "stop " "name|pattern" "..."
Stop one or more running runtimes. Accepts multiple names, glob patterns
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [] ),
or a mix. With
.BR * ,
stops all running runtimes.
.IP
A stopped runtime runs no script.
Its
.B on_stop
callback runs first.
Then every
.B set_timeout
and
.B set_interval
timer and every
.B socketley.subscribe
registration the runtime holds is cancelled, so nothing it set up can fire
afterwards.
None of the three can be set up again until the runtime is started.
This stops a script that called
.B socketley.stop
on itself from immediately setting one up again.
.TP
.BI "remove " "name|pattern" "..."
Remove one or more runtimes from the registry. Cannot be undone.
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix. Running runtimes
are stopped first. With
.BR * ,
stops and removes all runtimes.
.TP
.IB runtime " verb " \fR[\fIargs\fR...]
Run a verb on one runtime: the runtime's name comes first, and the verb is
interpreted according to the runtime's type.
The global commands above and below are the ones that no single runtime owns.
.B socketley
.I runtime
.B help
lists a runtime's verbs. Every runtime accepts
.BR start ", " stop ", " restart ", " remove ", " show ", " stats ", " edit ", "
.BR reload ", " reload\-lua " and " owner
in this form as well. The types add these verbs:
.RS
.TP
.B cache
.BR get ", " set ", " del ", " exists ", " incr ", " decr ", " incrby ", "
.BR decrby ", " append ", " mget ", " mset ", " keys ", " size ", " expire ", "
.BR ttl ", " persist ", " lpush ", " rpush ", " lpop ", " rpop ", " llen ", "
.BR lrange ", " lindex ", " sadd ", " srem ", " sismember ", " scard ", "
.BR smembers ", " hset ", " hget ", " hdel ", " hlen ", " hgetall ", "
.BR publish ", " flush ", " load ", " memory
(each runs the RESP command of the same name, with its arguments after it)
.TP
.B db
.BR query ", " backup ", " restore
.TP
.B mqtt
.BR pub ", " sub ", " sessions ", " kick ", " retained
.TP
.B idp
.BR user ", " login ", " logout ", " ssh\-cert ", " ssh\-ca
.TP
.B dns
.B dnssec
.TP
.B tunnel
.BR invite ", " set ", " list ", " shares ", " revoke
.TP
.B mail
.BR send ", " queue ", " show ", " flush ", " drop ", " dns ", " trace ", " usage ", "
.BR suppressed ", " forgive ", " unsubscribed ", " resubscribe ", " learn ", "
.BR export ", " import ", " sieve
.TP
.B metrics
.BR query ", " query_range ", " series ", " targets ", " rules ", " alerts ", "
.BR logs ", " stats
.TP
.BR server ", " client
.B send
.RE
.IP
A type's verb takes precedence over a lifecycle verb of the same name.
For example, a metrics runtime's
.B stats
reports its store, while
.B socketley stats
.I name
reports the runtime.
A runtime whose name is a global command, such as
.B stop
or
.BR mail ,
is reached through the older forms at the end of this section.
For
.BR metrics ,
.B tunnel
and
.BR mail ,
the command named after the type passes its verbs to the runtime of that name.
So
.B socketley metrics query
queries the runtime named
.BR metrics .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley shop query customers '{"where":{"country":"de"}}'
socketley broker pub \-\-qos 1 sensors/temp 21.5
socketley auth user add jonas
socketley edge invite pi \-\-http home=idp:auth
socketley metrics logs '{level="error"}' \-\-since 1d
socketley mycache get session:42
socketley web stop
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "send " "name" " [message]"
Send a message to a running server or client runtime.
For a server, the message is broadcast to all connected clients.
For a client, the message is sent to the connected server.
The command respects the runtime's mode setting, and fails if the mode is
receive-only.
.TP
.IB mail " send \fB\-\-to \fIaddress\fR [\fB\-\-cc\fR|\fB\-\-bcc \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-from \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-reply\-to \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-subject \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-text \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-html \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-attach \fIfile\fR[:\fItype\fR]] [\fB\-\-inline \fIimage\fR] [\fB\-\-header \fR'\fIName\fR: \fIvalue\fR'] [\fB\-\-raw\fR]"
Write a message and queue it on a mail runtime, which answers with the
message's queue id.
The text comes from
.B \-\-text
or stdin.
An HTML part from a file can go beside the text, and attachments are sent in
base64.
.BR \-\-to ,
.BR \-\-cc ,
.BR \-\-bcc ,
.BR \-\-header ,
.B \-\-attach
and
.B \-\-inline
can be given as often as needed.
For any other flag, the last value given is used.
A name or address may take the form
.BR "Name <a@b>" .
Without
.BR \-\-from ,
a runtime with
.B \-\-no\-reply
sends from no\-reply@ at its first domain.
.B \-\-raw
queues stdin as the whole message, with its own headers.
A message larger than 4 MiB is passed to the runtime as a file in its outbox
under the state directory, so a message up to
.B \-\-max\-size
is sent whole.
A message to a server or a client that begins with \- must be given after
\-\-.
A
.B :type
after an attachment is used as the attachment's type only if it has the form
type/subtype and no file exists under the name that includes it.
.B \-\-inline
sends an image inside the message.
The HTML shows the image as
.BI cid: name ,
where
.I name
is the image's file name, so a reader sees it without fetching anything. See
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley send m \-\-to ops@example.com \-\-subject "Backup done" \-\-text ok
df \-h | socketley send m \-\-to "Ops <ops@example.com>" \-\-subject "Disks" \e
    \-\-attach report.pdf:application/pdf
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB mail " send \fB\-\-template \fIfile\fR \fB\-\-to \fIaddress\fR|\fBidp:\fIidp\fR[\fB/\fIgroup\fR]|\fBdb:\fIdb\fB/\fIcollection\fR|\fBjson:\fIfile\fR [\fB\-\-set \fIname\fB=\fIvalue\fR] [\fB\-\-lua \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-ad\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-check\fR] [\fB\-\-rate\-limit \fIn\fR[\fB/m\fR|\fB/h\fR]] [\fB\-\-test\fR] [\fB\-\-test\-to \fIaddress\fR]"
Send each recipient a message of their own, filled in from a template.
The template is an HTML file (or
.B \-\-text
for plain text only) together with the subject, and both can contain
placeholders.
.B {{name}}
is the recipient's value for that name.
.B {{name|otherwise}}
gives the text to use when the recipient has no value.
.B {{{name}}}
inserts a value into the HTML unescaped; every other placeholder is escaped.
.IP
The values come from
.BR \-\-set ,
which applies to every recipient, and from each recipient's source.
An address given to
.B \-\-to
provides its
.BR email .
.BI idp: idp
provides the idp's accounts that are not disabled, or only those of a group
named after a slash.
Each account has its username, name, email and every other field it keeps,
except its password.
.BI db: db / collection
provides the collection's documents, each with its fields.
The fields of a nested object are named below the object's name
.RB ( address.city ).
.BI json: file
provides a JSON array of objects, or one object per line.
Each recipient needs an
.BR email ,
and a
.BR name ,
if present, is written into the message's To header.
With an HTML template, the plain-text part beside it is the HTML's text, with
its links written out.
.IP
The messages are sent at
.B \-\-rate\-limit
per second, or per minute or per hour when a slash follows
.RB ( 300/m ).
Default: two per second.
Sending gradually keeps a new sender's first thousand messages from landing in
the spam folder for arriving all at once.
The output names each recipient that was skipped or failed, and one line
reports how many messages were sent.
.IP
A template is Lua.
.B {{ expr }}
inserts the value of any Lua expression, and
.B {% code %}
runs the code where it stands.
A tag alone on its line removes the whole line from the output:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{% if vip then %}<p>Your code: {{code}}</p>{% end %}
{% for _, p in ipairs(products) do %}
<li>{{p.title}}: {{ string.format("%.2f", p.price) }}</li>
{% end %}
.fi
.RE
.IP
A name with dots refers to a nested field.
If a field along the way is missing, the result is no value rather than an
error.
A list of words is written joined by commas.
.BR html (s),
.BR url (s)
and
.BR raw (s)
escape a value, percent-encode it, and keep it unescaped, respectively.
.BR include (\(dqfile\(dq)
inserts another template from the same directory, filled in for the same
recipient.
A template can use the recipient's values, whatever the script defines, the
string, table and math libraries, os.date and os.time.
It cannot reach any file or process.
.IP
.B \-\-lua
.I file.lua
runs once before the first recipient and can use all of Lua.
.BI on_recipient( r )
is called with each recipient's values before their message is written.
It can change the values, or leave the recipient out by returning false and a
reason.
.BI on_link( url ", " r )
is called with each link in the HTML except the unsubscribe link.
Its return value is the link that is written; nil keeps the link unchanged.
An error in Lua means a message cannot be written, and is reported with its
file and line.
.IP
A template is filled strictly.
A recipient whose message uses a name with no value and no default is skipped.
If this happens to the first recipient, the whole send stops before any
message is sent.
The send also stops if the runtime refuses the first message, since a template
that fails for its first recipient is wrong for all of them.
.B \-\-no\-check
lets a name with no value be empty, and keeps sending after a refused first
message.
.B \-\-test
shows the first recipient's message and how many recipients there are, and
sends nothing.
.B \-\-test\-to
sends that message, with its subject marked [test], only to the address it
names.
.IP
.B \-\-ad
marks the messages as advertisements.
Each advertisement goes to a single recipient, and never to one who
unsubscribed.
It carries the recipient's own unsubscribe link, placed where the template says
.BR {{unsubscribe}} ,
or below the content if the template does not say.
It also carries List-Unsubscribe, naming that link and a mailto: address
(RFC 2369), and List-Unsubscribe-Post for a provider's one-click unsubscribe
(RFC 8058).
List-Id names the list after its sender, such as <news.example.com>
(RFC 2919).
The link is
.BI https:// webmail /unsubscribe
when the runtime has
.B \-\-web
and
.BR \-\-domain .
A GET on the link asks for confirmation and a POST unsubscribes, so a scanner
that opens every link unsubscribes no one.
The mailto: address is
.BI unsubscribe@ domain
with the token in its subject.
A token is the address plus an HMAC of it under the runtime's key, so it is
stored nowhere and nobody can forge it.
A message that is not an advertisement still goes to an address that
unsubscribed.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley send m \-\-template autumn.html \-\-inline logo.png \-\-to db:shop/customers \e
    \-\-from news@example.com \-\-subject "Hello {{name|there}}, your code {{code}}" \e
    \-\-ad \-\-rate\-limit 5
socketley send m \-\-template autumn.html \-\-to idp:auth/vip \-\-set code=VIP30 \-\-test
socketley send m \-\-template order.html \-\-lua shop.lua \-\-to db:shop/orders \-\-test\-to me@example.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
If no message is given,
.B send
reads it from stdin:
.nf
echo "hello" | socketley send myserver
cat data.txt | socketley send myclient
.fi
.PP
The word
.B send
can be left out when input is piped in, so
.B "echo \(dqhello\(dq | socketley myserver"
is the same command.
socketley uses this form only when standard input is not a terminal.
That is also the case in every script, pipeline and CI step, so a mistyped
command can end up here too.
When nothing arrives on standard input, socketley cannot tell a misspelled
command from a runtime that was piped zero bytes.
It says so and names both readings, with the typo first:
.nf
$ socketley gibtsnicht < /dev/null
unknown command: gibtsnicht
  If gibtsnicht is a runtime you meant to send to, nothing arrived on standard input:
    echo 'data' | socketley gibtsnicht
.fi
.TP
.BI "stats " "name|pattern" "..."
Display runtime statistics, including total connections, messages processed,
bytes in/out, and type-specific counters. Accepts multiple names, glob
patterns, or a mix. With
.BR * ,
shows stats for all runtimes.
.IP
The
.B cpus
field describes how the runtime's I/O is running, not what it has done.
It is read from the ring itself rather than from the requested setting,
because a cpuset can refuse the pinning, and nobody can debug a runtime whose
real CPU set cannot be read back.
These fields need a note:
.RS
.TP
.B cpus
The CPU set in effect, written in the same form that
.B \-\-cpus
accepts.
.B (not applied)
is appended when the pinning was attempted and refused.
.TP
.B dial_attempts
Client only: the number of connection attempts this client has made.
Compare it with
.BR total_connections ,
which counts the attempts for which the kernel found a peer.
A non-blocking connect answers EINPROGRESS for every target that is not
already there, so an attempt only records an intention.
The two figures differ exactly when a target is flapping or gone: a client
that keeps reconnecting all afternoon shows one climbing and the other
standing still.
Every other runtime counts
.B total_connections
when something has actually happened to it: the server and the cache at an
.BR accept (2),
the proxy once
.B on_auth
has admitted the client.
.TP
.B upstreams
Server only, and shown only when
.B \-u
was given: the number of configured upstreams that the kernel reports as
connected, over the number configured.
The left number counts real connections, not attempts, for the same reason as
.B dial_attempts
does for a client.
A flapping upstream makes the two numbers differ, and an unreachable one keeps
the left number at zero.
.RE
.IP
For a runtime that runs in its own process,
.B cpus
describes that process's ring, not the daemon's.
.TP
.B daemon stop
Stop the daemon this invocation would talk to, together with every runtime it
supervises.
The command reaches the daemon over its socket, not by pid.
The daemon answers before it exits.
The command exits 2 when nothing is serving that socket.
.TP
.B daemon stats
Print the daemon's own figures as
.IB key : value
lines, the same format that
.B stats
uses for a runtime.
Currently these cover the loopback shortcut
.RB ( "daemon \-\-fast\-lo" ),
whose bytes
.B tcpdump \-i lo
and nftables never see.
.IP
.B fast_lo
is
.BR off ,
.BR own ,
.BR all ,
.BR trust ,
or
.BR unavailable ;
when it is unavailable,
.B fast_lo_reason
says why.
The other fields appear when the shortcut is attached.
.B fast_lo_sockets_shortcut
counts the entered sockets that send through the shortcut, and
.B fast_lo_sockets_tcp
counts those pinned to TCP for good.
Both are as of the last once\-a\-second sweep; a pair is two sockets, and each
direction belongs to its sender.
.B fast_lo_entered
counts the sockets entered since start.
.IP
.BR fast_lo_tainted_bound ,
.BR _unanswered ,
.B _budget
and
.B _peer_off
count taints by reason, in that order: the peer really held the per\-pair
bound; no answer came before twice the bound; the global budget was reached;
the peer was not on the shortcut (it received over TCP before it could enter,
or it had closed).
.B fast_lo_held_bytes
is the unread redirected backlog summed across pairs.
The global budget
.RB ( fast_lo_budget_bytes )
is compared against it.
For a pair below 64 KiB it is an upper bound.
.B fast_lo_over_budget
is 1 while new sends are being tainted because of the budget.
.IP
The metrics endpoint exports the same figures as
.BR socketley_fast_lo_info{mode} ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_sockets{path} ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_entered_total ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_tainted_total{reason} ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_held_bytes ,
.BR socketley_fast_lo_budget_bytes ,
.B socketley_fast_lo_pair_limit_bytes
and
.BR socketley_fast_lo_over_budget .
The counting costs one atomic add per entered socket and per taint, never one
per send.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon stats
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B \fIidp\fB user add \fIuser\fR [\fB\-\-password \fIpw\fR|\fB\-\fR] [\fB\-\-name \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-email \fIaddr\fR] [\fB\-\-groups \fIa,b\fR] [\fB\-\-set \fIfield\fB=\fIvalue\fR]... [\fB\-\-reset\fR]
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR passwd \fIuser\fR \fB\-\-password \fIpw\fR|\fB\-\fR [\fB\-\-reset\fR]
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR set \fIuser field\fB=\fIvalue\fR...
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR disable\fR|\fBenable\fR|\fBlogout\fR|\fBremove\fR|\fBshow \fIuser\fR
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR list\fR|\fBimport \fIstorage\fR
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR totp \fIuser\fR on\fR|\fBoff
.TQ
.B idp\-user \fIidp\fR app\-password \fIuser\fR add \fIlabel\fR|\fBremove \fIlabel\fR|\fBlist
Manage the accounts an
.B idp
runtime keeps. The changes are made in the idp's own process.
.B add
creates an account under a new fixed id and prints the id.
Every token identifies the account by this id, so the account stays the same
if its username is changed later.
.B \-\-password \-
reads the password from the first line of stdin, which keeps it off the
command line where others can read it.
.B \-\-reset
makes the user set the password again at the next sign-in.
.IP
.B set
gives any field a value; an empty value removes the field.
A field the provider does not know is kept and passed on unchanged, for the
applications that use it.
Setting a new
.B username
ends the account's sessions and tokens, because they carry the old username to
every runtime that accepts them.
.B logout
ends every session and refresh token the account holds;
.B passwd
does too.
.B show
prints the fields. It shows the password only as set, never its hash.
.IP
.B totp on
turns on a second sign-in step (RFC 6238).
It prints the key, and the otpauth:// link that loads the key into an
authenticator app.
From then on, a web sign-in asks for the six digits the app shows, after the
password.
Each code is accepted only once, at every shard and across a restart, because
the last time step used is stored with the account.
.B off
turns the second step off.
.IP
.B app\-password add
creates a password for one mail app: four groups of four letters, printed
once.
It signs in over SMTP, IMAP, POP3 and JMAP, and
.B remove
revokes it on its own, without affecting anything else.
With the second step on, mail apps can sign in only with these passwords,
since they have no code to give.
A user can do all of this on their own account page,
.BR /account .
There, the second step is turned on only after a code proves that the app has
the key.
.IP
.B import
copies a storage runtime's users, each with the password it has there.
An account here with the same name is kept as it is.
A storage admin is not an admin here, since an admin here is root through an
SSH certificate.
The output names each such user, so that
.B set \fIuser\fB groups=admins
can make them one.
A refusal makes the command exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley auth user add smiley \-\-password \- \-\-groups admins
socketley auth user set smiley picture=https://storage.example.com/me.png
socketley auth user import ui
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "login " "idp" " \fR[\fB\-\-client \fIid\fR]"
.TQ
.BI "logout " idp
Sign a person in to an
.B idp
runtime, on this machine or another, with the device grant (RFC 8628), or sign
them out.
.B login
prints an address and a code to approve there.
It then waits until a browser signed in as that person approves the code.
The approval page names the application that is asking and what approving it
grants.
For a client marked
.B ssh
under
.BR \-\-ssh\-ca ,
the page says that the client gets SSH certificates (as root for an admin),
and asks for the password again, or for the code if the second step is on.
.B login
keeps the tokens in
.I ~/.config/socketley/tokens/
(0600).
From there,
.B ssh\-cert
and
.B tunnel
act as that person, and refresh the access token when it expires.
The client is
.B socketley
unless
.B \-\-client
names another.
The provider allows the grant only to a client marked
.B device
in its
.BR \-\-clients .
.B logout
forgets the tokens. A refused or expired code makes the command exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley login auth.example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "ssh\-cert " idp " \fR[\fB\-\-key \fIfile.pub\fR]"
Get an SSH certificate for the person signed in at
.I idp
with
.BR login .
The certificate is for
.IR ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub .
If that key does not exist, the command creates
.I ~/.ssh/socketley_ed25519
and uses it instead.
The certificate is written beside the key as
.IR \-cert.pub ,
where ssh finds it.
It is valid for the number of hours set by the provider's
.BR \-\-ssh\-ca .
Its principals are the username, root for members of the admins group, and
.I name
for each group named
.BI ssh: name .
If the provider refuses, as it does for a token given to a client not marked
.BR ssh ,
the command exits 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley ssh\-cert auth.example.com
ssh root@server.example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB idp " ssh\-ca"
.TQ
.BI "idp\-ssh " idp " host\-sign " "file.pub " "\fB\-\-names \fIhost,...\fR [\fB\-\-days \fIN\fR]"
.TQ
.BI "idp\-ssh " idp " revoke " user \fR|\fIserial
.TQ
.BI "idp\-ssh " idp " krl"
Manage the SSH certificate authority of an idp runtime on this daemon.
.B ssh\-ca
prints the authority's public key, for sshd's
.B TrustedUserCAKeys
and for a client's
.B @cert\-authority
line.
.B host\-sign
certifies a host key for the given names, valid for 365 days unless
.B \-\-days
says otherwise.
It writes the certificate beside the key, for sshd's
.BR HostCertificate .
.B revoke
ends a person's current user certificates now rather than at their expiry, or
revokes one certificate by the serial the log shows for it.
.B krl
prints the path of the revocation list the idp keeps, for sshd's
.BR RevokedKeys .
.IP
The idp writes the list when it starts and at every revocation.
It also revokes a person's certificates without being asked when that person
is signed out everywhere, disabled, reset or removed, or has their groups
changed.
This is because a certificate made earlier may carry a principal the person no
longer has.
A server that cannot read the file can fetch the same bytes from
.BR /ssh/krl .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley auth ssh\-ca > /etc/ssh/socketley_user_ca.pub
socketley idp\-ssh auth host\-sign /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub \-\-names server.example.com
echo "RevokedKeys $(socketley idp\-ssh auth krl)" >> /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/socketley.conf
socketley idp\-ssh auth revoke alice
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB edge " invite\fR|\fBset " agent " \fR[\fB\-\-http \fIname\fR[\fB=\fIauth\fR]\fI,...\fR] [\fB\-\-tcp \fIname\fB:\fIport,...\fR] [\fB\-\-udp \fIname\fB:\fIport,...\fR]"
.TQ
.BI "tunnel " edge " list\fR|\fBshares\fR|\fBrevoke \fIagent"
Manage the agents a tunnel edge accepts.
.B invite
prints a one-time invitation for an agent to offer the named services.
An HTTP service is served at
.IB name . domain .
A TCP or UDP service is served on its port, on every address of the machine,
or only on one address when given as
.IB name : address : port .
A TCP and a UDP service may share a port number.
An agent that is invited again gets a new invitation.
.IP
A UDP service carries each visitor's datagrams to the agent as a separate
flow, and carries the answers back.
It forgets a flow that has been silent for a minute.
It drops a datagram rather than queue it while the agent is away or the
agent's link is full.
An HTTP service with
.BI = auth
asks each visitor to pass
.IR auth ,
as a proxy's
.B \-\-auth
does.
.IP
.B set
changes what an agent offers and keeps its key.
.B shares
lists the ports people currently share at the edge: who may visit each, whose
it is, and until when.
.B revoke
cuts the agent off.
.IP
A TCP service on
.B 127.0.0.1
alone lets the edge's own machine reach a service at home without opening it
to anyone else.
A proxy's
.B \-\-backend
or a sync's
.B \-\-storage
names that port like any other port, and the connection is refused while the
agent is away. See
.BR "TUNNEL FLAGS" .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley edge invite pi \-\-http home=idp:auth,nas \-\-tcp mc:25565 \-\-udp bedrock:19132
socketley edge set pi \-\-tcp nas:127.0.0.1:9000
socketley create sync backup \-\-dir /srv/data \-\-storage http://127.0.0.1:9000/backup
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "tunnel join " edge " \fR[\fB\-\-name \fIagent\fR] [\fB\-\-http \fIservice\fB=\fIhost\fB:\fIport,...\fR] [\fB\-\-tcp \fIservice\fB:\fIedge-port\fB=\fIhost\fB:\fIport,...\fR] [\fB\-\-udp \fIservice\fB:\fIedge-port\fB=\fIhost\fB:\fIport,...\fR] [\fB\-\-replace\fR] [\fB\-\-idp \fIidp\fR]"
Make this machine an agent of an edge, authorized by an admin of the edge's
idp.
If no
.B login
is kept here, the admin signs in with a code approved in a browser.
That sign-in is for this join only and is not stored.
The edge adds the agent, named after this machine's host name unless
.B \-\-name
gives another name.
The agent runtime of that name is then created and joins at once.
Each HTTP service asks its visitors to sign in at the edge's idp, until the
edge's owner changes that with
.BR "tunnel " "edge" " set" .
If the edge already holds a joined agent of that name, the agent is replaced
only with
.BR \-\-replace ,
for example when the machine has been set up anew.
If a runtime of that name already exists on this machine, the command exits 1
and the edge is not asked.
The command prints where each service is.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley tunnel join https://edge.example.com \-\-http ollama=127.0.0.1:11434
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "tunnel connect " "edge host" : port \fR|\fIagent\fB/\fIservice " \fR[\fB\-\-idp \fIidp\fR]"
.TQ
.BI "tunnel ssh " edge " \fR[\fB\-\-agent \fIagent\fR] [\fB\-\-idp \fIidp\fR]"
Connects stdin and stdout to a
.B \-\-forward
target of a tunnel edge.
The stream belongs to the person signed in at
.I idp
with
.BR login .
Default: the edge's own idp, which the command asks the edge for.
.IP
The command trusts an idp that the edge names with a sign-in stored on this
machine only when that idp is beside the edge, such as
.B auth.example.com
for
.BR edge.example.com .
Beside means in the same registrable domain according to the Public Suffix
List in
.IR /usr/share/publicsuffix ,
so two names under
.B duckdns.org
do not count.
Without the list, only the edge's own name counts.
Any other idp is trusted only when it is given with
.BR \-\-idp .
.IP
.B tunnel ssh
connects to 127.0.0.1:22 on the edge, for use as ssh's
.BR ProxyCommand .
An admin of the edge's idp may instead name an agent's TCP service as
.IB agent / service ,
and
.B \-\-agent
names that agent's
.BR ssh .
This way, ssh reaches a machine at home through port 443 with no port opened
for it anywhere.
Errors go to stderr, since stdout carries the stream.
A target that the edge does not name exits with status 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
ssh \-o ProxyCommand='socketley tunnel ssh edge.example.com' root@server
ssh \-o ProxyCommand='socketley tunnel ssh edge.example.com \-\-agent pi' me@pi
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "tunnel share " "edge port" "\fR|\fIaddress" : port " \fR[\fB\-\-public\fR] [\fB\-\-for \fIduration\fR] [\fB\-\-idp \fIidp\fR]"
Shares a port of this machine through a tunnel edge, under a name of six
letters and digits that the edge assigns.
The name sits beside the edge's own name, as the names of its agents' services
do
.RB ( k7f3q2.example.com
for
.BR edge.example.com ).
The share lasts as long as the command runs.
It ends at Ctrl+C, at the end of
.BR \-\-for ,
or when the sign-in it was made with ends.
.B \-\-for
takes a duration from a minute to a day; default: two hours.
.IP
Visitors sign in at the edge's idp, unless
.B \-\-public
lets everyone in.
Only admins of the edge's idp can share.
Each admin may hold three shares at a time, and an edge holds at most 32.
The edge logs who shares what, and
.B stats
counts the shares as
.BR shares .
The command prints the address on stdout and errors on stderr.
When the edge was reached by its address, the command prints the name alone,
because there is no domain to put it under.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley tunnel share edge.example.com 3000
socketley tunnel share edge.example.com 127.0.0.1:8080 \-\-public \-\-for 30m
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB db " backup " file
Writes every document of a db runtime into
.IR file .
The runtime writes it off its event loop while it goes on serving.
The command waits until the file is written and prints nothing.
See
.BR "DB FLAGS" .
.TP
.IB db " restore " file
Replaces a db runtime's documents with those of a backup.
The command first reads the whole file, and a file that is not a backup
changes nothing.
Then it stops the runtime and moves the current documents aside, next to them, as
.IB dir .before\-restore .
It puts the backup in their place and starts the runtime again if it was
running.
.TP
.IB db " query " collection " \fR['\fIquery\fR']"
Prints the documents of a collection that a query finds, in the db API's
answer format,
.BR {"docs":[...]} .
.I query
is the object that
.B POST /c/\fIcollection\fB/_query
takes, with its where, sort, limit (up to 1000) and skip.
Without a query, the command prints every document, up to the limit.
The query runs as the operator, the same as a query from a script.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley shop query customers '{"where":{"country":"de"},"limit":50}'
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB metrics " query \fIpromql\fR [\fB\-\-at \fItime\fR] | query_range \fIpromql\fR [\fB\-\-start\fR] [\fB\-\-end\fR] [\fB\-\-step\fR] | series \fIselector\fR | targets | rules | alerts [\fB\-\-recent\fR] | logs [\fIlogql\fR] [\fB\-\-since\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR] | stats [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
Asks a metrics runtime what it holds, the way Grafana asks its Prometheus API.
.IP
.B query
runs a PromQL query at one time, and
.B query_range
runs it over a range.
.B series
lists the series a selector names.
.B targets
lists the targets the runtime scrapes, with their health.
.B rules
lists its rules.
.B alerts
lists the alerts pending and firing.
With
.B \-\-recent
it also lists the recent notifications, as the dashboard reads them.
.B logs
prints the log lines a LogQL query selects, oldest first.
It needs the runtime's
.BR \-\-logs ,
and by default covers an hour and prints 100 lines.
.B stats
prints the figures of the runtime's store.
.IP
By default the output is one line per series or target;
.B \-\-json
prints the API's answer instead.
See
.BR "METRICS FLAGS" .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley metrics query 'sum by (name) (rate(socketley_runtime_messages_total[5m]))'
socketley metrics query_range 'up' \-\-start \-6h \-\-step 5m
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB mqtt " pub \fR[\fB\-\-qos \fR0|1|2] [\fB\-\-retain\fR] \fItopic\fR [\fIpayload\fR]"
Publishes a message on an mqtt runtime, as the runtime's own message.
.B \-\-qos
sets the QoS, and
.B \-\-retain
makes the message retained.
The payload is the words after the topic, or stdin when there are none.
Its last newline is dropped, and the rest is carried byte for byte.
The command prints nothing once the broker has the message.
A topic that contains
.B +
or
.BR # ,
or that starts with
.BR $ ,
is refused with exit 1.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley broker pub \-\-qos 1 \-\-retain firmware/latest 2.4.1
gzip \-c report.json | socketley broker pub reports/daily
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB mqtt " sub " filter " \fR[\fB\-\-count \fIn\fR]"
Prints every message the broker accepts whose topic matches
.IR filter ,
as one JSON line per message as it arrives.
Each line is
.BR {"topic":..,"payload":..,"qos":..,"retain":..,"client":..} ;
a payload that is not UTF-8 is printed as
.BR {"base64":..} .
The command runs until it is interrupted, or until
.I n
messages have been printed.
It takes no session and changes nothing.
A line
.B {"dropped":n}
means that n messages were not read in time.
.TP
.IB mqtt " sessions"
Prints the broker's sessions, from every shard, as a JSON array.
Each entry has the client id, the user, whether the client is connected, the
protocol version and the peer.
It also has the client's subscriptions, the messages queued and in flight, the
seconds left until the session expires while the client is away, and its
shard.
.TP
.IB mqtt " kick " client " \fR[\fB\-\-end\fR]"
Disconnects a client without sending its will.
A 5.0 client is told that it was an administrative action.
The session stays for the client to come back to; with
.B \-\-end
the session ends too.
A client id with no session exits with status 1.
.TP
.IB mqtt " retained \fR[\fIfilter\fR] | \fB\-\-clear \fItopic"
Prints the retained messages whose topics match the filter, as a JSON array.
Default filter:
.BR # ,
which matches every topic except
.BR $SYS .
With
.BR \-\-clear ,
the command instead clears the retained message on
.IR topic .
.TP
.BI "mqtt passwd " "file user " "\fR[\fB\-\-delete\fR]"
Sets the password of
.I user
in a users file for
.BR \-\-users .
The command reads the password from stdin, or on a terminal asks for it twice
without echo.
It stores the password as a scrypt hash.
It creates the file with mode 0600 and replaces it whole, keeping every other
line.
With
.BR \-\-delete ,
it removes the user instead.
It needs no daemon.
A broker, and a mail runtime's
.BR \-\-users ,
read the file again within two seconds.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley mqtt passwd /etc/socketley/mqtt.users sensor\-17
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB dns " dnssec"
Prints the DS record that each zone of a dns runtime with
.B \-\-dnssec
needs at its parent.
The parent is the registrar.
The command prints each DS together with the DNSKEY it stands for, one per
line.
If the runtime has not made the keys yet, the command makes them, so the DS
can go to the registrar before the zone is served signed.
.IP
Every validating resolver treats a zone whose parent has no DS for it as
unsigned.
A zone whose parent has a DS for another key gets no answer at all.
For that reason, a key is never removed while a DS names it.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley ns dnssec
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB mail " \fR[\fBqueue \fR[\fIfilter\fR] | \fBshow \fIid\fR | \fBflush \fR[\fIid\fR] | \fBdrop \fIid\fR | \fBdns \fR[\fB\-\-check\fR] | \fBtrace \fIaddress\fR | \fBusage \fR[\fIuser\fR] | \fBsuppressed\fR | \fBforgive \fIaddress\fR | \fBunsubscribed\fR | \fBresubscribe \fIaddress\fR | \fBlearn \fIuser\fB/\fIfolder \fB\-\-as spam\fR|\fBham\fR | \fBexport \fIuser \fR[\fIdir\fR] | \fBimport \fIuser file\fR|\fIdir\fR | \fBsieve \fIuser \fBlist\fR|\fBget \fIname\fR|\fBput \fIname file\fR|\fBcheck \fIfile\fR|\fBactivate \fIname\fR|\fBdeactivate\fR|\fBdelete \fIname\fR]"
Shows the state of a mail runtime and manages its spool, its users' mailboxes
and their Sieve scripts.
The runtime's name can be left out when the daemon has only one mail runtime.
.IP
Every hour, and when it starts, the runtime checks two things about its host.
First, it checks that the PTR record of each address of its host names that
host again.
This is forward-confirmed reverse DNS.
Without it, Gmail and Outlook refuse the runtime's mail or file it as spam.
The PTR record is set where the address is rented.
Second, it checks whether a blocklist of its
.B \-\-dnsbl
lists one of those addresses.
The runtime reports either problem in its summary and in the log.
.IP
Without a verb, the command prints a few lines about the runtime and how it is
doing.
They cover its domains, users and mailboxes, its ports, the webmail's name,
what is waiting, what was delivered, deferred, bounced, received and stored,
and its lists.
.IP
The spool verbs cover every shard.
.B queue
lists the messages that are waiting, with each one's id, size, sender, the
recipients still waiting, and the tries.
With
.IR filter ,
it lists only those whose id or sender contains
.IR filter .
.B show
prints the envelope of one message, with the last answer each recipient got.
.B flush
tries every message now, or only the one named.
.B drop
deletes one message without telling anybody.
.IP
.B dns
prints the MX, SPF, DKIM, DMARC and SRV records that the runtime's domains
need, for a zone kept somewhere other than a dns runtime of this daemon.
With
.BR \-\-check ,
it asks this machine's resolvers for each record and reports whether it is
there, missing, or something else.
.IP
.B trace
.I address
reports what became of the latest mail from or to an address, the newest last.
For each message it says whether it was delivered, deferred (with the reason),
bounced, or refused (with the answer it got).
Each of these is also a line of the log.
.IP
.B usage
.RI [ user ]
shows what each user's mailboxes hold against their quota.
Without a user, it shows every user of the runtime's idp.
.IP
.B suppressed
lists the addresses that bounces reported as dead.
The runtime does not mail them again until
.B forgive
releases one.
.B unsubscribed
lists the addresses that unsubscribed from its advertisements.
An address stays on that list until
.B resubscribe
takes it back.
.IP
.B learn
teaches the spam filter one of a user's folders, as spam or as not spam.
An example is a user's Junk and INBOX on a new machine, where the filter has
learned nothing yet.
.IP
.B export
writes a user's mailbox out as mbox files, one per folder, as
.IB dir / folder .mbox\fR.
Without
.IR dir ,
the files go under
.IR /var/tmp/socketley\-mail .
The daemon makes the directory for itself alone.
It refuses a directory that already holds files, is a link, or lies in a
folder that another user may change.
Flags are written as Status and X\-Status, keywords as X\-Keywords, and the
internal date goes in each "From " line.
.IP
.B import
reads an mbox file, or every
.I .mbox
file under a directory, into a user's folders.
Each file goes into the folder its name says, and an empty file becomes an
empty folder.
It reads what
.B export
wrote, Thunderbird's mbox files, and a Google Takeout's.
In a Takeout, X\-Gmail\-Labels gives the folder: Inbox, Sent, Drafts, Spam as
Junk, Trash, a label of the user's, and Archive for the rest.
Its read and starred labels give the flags.
Both
.B export
and
.B import
wait until they finish and report how many messages they moved.
To move a mailbox from another IMAP server, use IMAP itself, with
.BR imapsync (1)
or a mail client.
.IP
.B sieve
manages a user's Sieve scripts, as ManageSieve does.
It lists them with the active one marked, prints one, or stores one from a
file
.RB ( \-
for stdin) once it compiles.
It also checks one, makes one active or none active, or deletes one.
See
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley m queue
socketley m show 65e5113d0e2120000000
socketley m dns >> /etc/bind/example.com.zone
socketley m sieve alice put main filter.sieve
socketley m sieve alice activate main
socketley m export alice /var/backups/alice
socketley m import alice ~/Takeout/Mail/All\ mail\ Including\ Spam\ and\ Trash.mbox
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B git\-sync \-\-url \fIurl\fR \-\-branch \fIname\fR|\fB*\fR \-\-work \fIdir\fR \-\-endpoint \fIhost\fB:\fIport\fR \fR[\fB\-\-tls\fR [\fB\-\-ca \fIfile\fR]] \fB\-\-bucket \fIlibrary\fR \fB\-\-name \fIfolder\fR [\fB\-\-keep \fIdays\fB,\fIweeks\fB,\fImonths\fR] [\fB\-\-move\-from \fIlibrary\fR]
Runs one sync of a repository that a storage runtime keeps (see
.B Git repositories
under
.BR "STORAGE FLAGS" ).
The storage runtime starts this command itself, and a run by hand works the
same way.
.IP
The command fetches into the bare clone under
.IR dir .
When a ref has moved, it writes the branch's files and a bundle into the
library at
.IR host : port .
It takes the credentials from
.BR SOCKETLEY_GIT_ACCESS_KEY ,
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_SECRET
and, for temporary credentials,
.BR SOCKETLEY_GIT_SESSION_TOKEN .
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_TOKEN
is a GitHub token for a private repository; the command sends it only to an
https:// URL.
.IP
.B \-\-ca
names the certificate that the runtime at
.IR host : port
must present over
.BR \-\-tls .
The storage runtime passes its own certificate here.
.IP
.B \-\-move\-from
names a library the folder was written to before.
Once the tree is complete in the new library, the command copies the backups
over from the old one and removes the folder's files there.
It removes the old library itself once nothing else is in it.
An old library this user may not read is left as it is.
.IP
Exit status is 0 when the run ended well, 2 when it did not, and 3 when a run
of the same directory is already under way.
.IR dir /state.json
gives the details.
.TP
.B acme issue \-\-domains \fIname\fR[\fB,\fIname\fR...] \fR[\fB\-\-cert \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-key \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-email \fIaddress\fR] [\fB\-\-directory \fIurl\fR] [\fB\-\-challenge http\-01\fR|\fBtls\-alpn\-01\fR|\fBdns\-01\fR]
Gets a certificate from an ACME CA by hand, as certbot does.
The command creates the account once per CA and keeps it in
.IR state\-dir /.acme/.
.IP
By default it proves the names over HTTP-01, through the daemon's runtimes on
port 80.
With
.BR "\-\-challenge tls\-alpn\-01" ,
it proves them through the daemon's TLS runtime on port 443.
With
.BR "\-\-challenge dns\-01" ,
it proves them through a
.B dns
runtime of the daemon that serves the name's zone, as
.B \-\-acme
describes.
A wildcard name takes dns-01 by itself.
.BI "\-\-dns\-zones " zone\fR[\fB,\fIzone\fR...]
names zones that a
.B dns
runtime of the daemon serves.
A name in one of them is proven over dns-01 whatever
.B \-\-challenge
says, and the other names use
.BR \-\-challenge .
.IP
The command writes the chain and a new key to the
.B \-\-cert
and
.B \-\-key
files, the key first.
By default these are the files that
.B \-\-acme
uses for the first name, so a runtime serving them picks them up.
It does not talk to the daemon.
It writes each answer to a file that the daemon's runtimes read, so one of
them must be running on the port the challenge is asked on.
On success it prints nothing; otherwise it prints the CA's reason and exits
with status 2.
A runtime with
.B \-\-acme
runs this command to renew.
.TP
.B daemons
Lists every daemon that has registered itself, with its pid, whether it still
answers, and the executable serving it.
The executable differs per socket once a daemon built on the SDK is running.
.IP
An asterisk marks the daemon this invocation would talk to;
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
selects another.
The list is a set of files, one per daemon.
A daemon writes its file after it starts serving its socket, and removes the
file on a clean exit.
The command probes every entry rather than trusting it.
So a daemon that was killed outright is reported as
.B dead
rather than listed as running, and its file is removed once its pid is gone
too.
.TP
.BI "restart " "name " "[edit flags] [\-i]"
Stops and starts a runtime in one step, and applies any
.B edit
flags given in between.
This is how a setting that cannot change while a runtime runs is changed:
.nf
socketley restart api \-p 9001
socketley restart ns\-proxy \-\-shards 1
.fi
.IP
It is one command, not a
.B stop
followed by a
.BR start ,
because the runtime being restarted may carry the command itself.
For example, an entrance proxy carries the dashboard the command was sent
from.
With two commands, the stop arrives and the start never does, and the
entrance stays down with its configuration unchanged.
The daemon does all of it, so the runtime ends up running whatever happens to
the caller's connection.
If
.B edit
refuses a flag, the runtime keeps running unchanged.
.IP
The flags are
.BR edit 's.
.B edit
reads them, and they are never handed to
.BR start .
.B \-i
belongs to restart itself, and attaches to the runtime once it is up.
A runtime that was stopped is started too, with or without flags: restart is
the lifecycle verb, and it means one thing.
To store a change WITHOUT starting anything, see
.B edit \-\-restart
below.
.IP
A runtime's new worker is forked only after its previous worker has exited,
because that process holds the ports, unix paths and files until then.
The
.BR start ,
.B reload
or
.B create \-s
that brought the runtime up answers after that fork, and the daemon serves
everything else meanwhile.
The previous worker gets five seconds before it is killed.
A cache that is writing its dump gets that dump's own bound instead.
So a command can outlast the CLI's five-second wait for an answer while the
runtime is still coming up.
.TP
.BI "reload " "name|pattern" "..."
Restarts one or more running runtimes (stop + start).
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix.
Runtimes that are not running are skipped.
With
.BR * ,
it restarts all running runtimes.
.IP
For a runtime that lives in a worker process, the daemon takes the child down
and forks it again.
The child then re-reads the script from disk, so a change to the script takes
effect.
The command waits for the new child, exactly as
.B create \-s
does, and reports on it.
It exits 1 and names the runtime if the worker refuses the configuration it
was handed.
It exits 1 with the reason if the reloaded script's
.B on_start
raises.
On success it prints nothing.
.TP
.BI "reload-lua " "name|pattern" "..."
Hot-reloads the Lua script attached to one or more runtimes.
The command destroys the Lua context and re-creates it from the original
script path.
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix.
Runtimes without Lua scripts are skipped.
With
.BR * ,
it reloads Lua on all runtimes that have scripts loaded.
.IP
.B on_start
runs again, on the new script.
This is the whole difference between reloading a file and reloading a
runtime.
Everything a script sets up at run time lives in its Lua context: every
.B socketley.subscribe
registration and every
.B set_timeout
or
.B set_interval
timer.
Destroying the context destroys all of it.
The usual place to create those is
.BR on_start ,
so a reload that did not re-run it would leave the runtime serving with no
subscriptions and no timers, without a word.
So anything
.B on_start
does must be safe to do twice.
.B socketley.create
is the case there that costs something, and it refuses a name that already
exists rather than creating a duplicate.
.IP
.B on_stop
does
.B not
run for the outgoing script.
The runtime is not stopping: it keeps its listener, its clients and its state
throughout the reload.
.B on_stop
is where a script says goodbye to exactly those.
.IP
An
.B on_start
that raises is reported like a script that would not load: exit 1, with the
reason on the terminal.
In that case the new script is in force, with whatever its
.B on_start
did before it failed.
A script that does not
.I compile
leaves the previous one running instead.
The message says which case happened.
.IP
A runtime that runs in a worker is asked to reload, rather than reloaded by
the daemon: its script lives in that process, and only that process can re-run
it.
The command waits for the worker to report the declaration that the new script
makes.
That wait is bounded the same way
.B create
bounds its own wait.
So when the command returns, a gateway in front of that runtime already
enforces what the edited script says, even when the edit removed the
.B routes
table altogether.
If the worker does not answer in time, the previous declaration stays in force
and the log says so.
A script that does not compile leaves the previous one running, as it does in
the daemon.
.IP
A reload that succeeds prints nothing and exits 0.
A script that would not load is named on stdout with the reason: the parse
error, or the
.B filters
or
.B routes
rule it broke.
The command then exits 1.
The runtimes that did reload stay reloaded, and the others go on running the
script they had.
So the message is the only sign that an edit is not live.
.TP
.BI "show " "name|pattern" "..."
Prints a runtime's JSON configuration to stdout.
Accepts multiple names, glob patterns, or a mix.
With
.BR * ,
it prints all runtimes.
.TP
.BI "edit " "name " "[flags] [\-\-restart]"
Edits a runtime's configuration.
With flags (for example \-p 9001), it applies the changes directly.
Without flags (or with only \-r/\-\-reload), it opens the runtime's JSON
configuration in
.BR $VISUAL ,
.BR $EDITOR ,
or
.BR vim ,
and validates and applies the changes after saving.
.IP
.B \-\-restart
stops the runtime first and starts it again afterwards.
A setting refused "while running" needs this.
.B import
takes
.B \-\-restart
the same way, before the document:
.nf
socketley edit ns\-proxy \-\-shards 1 \-\-restart
socketley show ns\-proxy | socketley import ns\-proxy \-\-restart
.fi
.IP
The daemon does the stop, the change and the start as one command, so a
caller whose connection the runtime carries cannot be cut off between them;
see
.BR restart .
If the runtime refuses a change, it keeps running unchanged.
It was still stopped and started around the refusal, because the only way to
learn whether a setting needs a restart is to apply it, and a refused edit
rolls back whole.
A runtime that was already stopped stays stopped.
.IP
The flags accepted here are a subset of those that
.B create
takes.
They are the common settings
.RB ( \-p ,
.BR \-b / \-bp / \-bt / \-bpt ,
.BR \-\-log ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
.BR \-\-rate\-limit ,
.BR \-\-global\-rate\-limit ,
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
.BR \-g ).
They also include the server and client flags, and the proxy mesh flags
.RB ( \-\-strategy ,
.BR \-\-health\-* ,
.BR \-\-circuit\-* ,
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-retry ,
.BR \-\-retry\-all ).
A tunnel's own flags are accepted too, such as an agent's
.BR \-\-offer ,
which a running tunnel takes only with
.BR \-\-restart .
.IP
Every other flag is refused with
.B "unknown flag"
and exit status 1.
This includes
.BR \-\-lua ,
.BR \-\-drain ,
.BR \-\-reconnect ,
the proxy's
.BR \-\-backend ,
.B \-\-protocol
and
.BR \-\-sidecar ,
and every cache flag.
Change those through the editor or
.BR import ,
which reach the full field set.
.PP
Four flags take effect at once on a
.I running
runtime of any type:
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
.BR \-\-rate\-limit ,
.B \-\-global\-rate\-limit
and
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout .
They are limits that a runtime reads at the moment it uses them: on each
accept, each message, or each idle sweep.
So a change affects the next decision and nothing already decided.
The daemon sends the edited configuration to every shard.
.PP
On a
.BR proxy ,
nine more flags take effect at once, for the same reason.
They are routing and mesh
.IR policy :
the proxy reads them when it picks a backend, arms a request or counts a
failure, and builds nothing from them.
They are
.BR \-\-strategy ,
.BR \-\-health\-interval ,
.BR \-\-health\-path ,
.BR \-\-health\-threshold ,
.BR \-\-circuit\-threshold ,
.BR \-\-circuit\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
.B \-\-retry
and
.BR \-\-retry\-all .
.B \-\-health\-interval
re-times the sweep that is already armed, so at most one period of the old
value passes before the new one applies.
.PP
.B \-\-health\-check
is not among them, and it is the one proxy flag with a different reason.
It arms a sweep timer when the runtime starts, so turning it on later has no
timer to arm.
It is stored and applies at the next start, like everything below.
.PP
Every other flag that
.B edit
accepts on a running runtime is stored and takes effect at that runtime's next
start.
This is not a policy choice.
The TLS flags are a context that the open handshakes already used.
A proxy's backends are open connections, and a server's
.B \-\-http
directory decides what a request in flight is answered from.
None of those can change under an existing connection.
So
.B show
reports the new value while the runtime holding the port keeps the old one,
until
.RB "\(oq" "socketley restart " name "\(cq."
Some flags cannot even be stored while the runtime runs; they say so and change
nothing:
.BR \-p ,
.B \-\-listen
and
.BR \-\-shards .
.PP
The TLS flags are an exception only in what they refuse.
Editing is the usual way to add encryption to something that already exists.
So
.I every
runtime type accepts
.BR \-\-tls ,
.BR \-\-cert ,
.B \-\-key
and
.B \-\-ca
here, and a proxy also accepts
.BR \-\-client\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.BR \-\-client\-key .
Certificates are read when a runtime starts, so a running runtime keeps the
identity it handshook with until it is restarted.
.PP
The settings that decide what a runtime trusts are refused while it runs,
instead of stored.
A stored value would be shown while the old one went on deciding who gets in.
These are
.B \-\-tls
on a runtime without it,
.B \-\-ca
naming another file, and on a proxy
.BR \-\-client\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
plus the same fields in
.BR import .
.B \-\-restart
takes them.
.PP
An edit that would leave the set incomplete is refused and changes nothing, so
a mistyped flag cannot leave a runtime half-configured.
Incomplete means a certificate with no
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B \-\-tls
with no key pair on a listener, half a client-certificate pair, or
.B \-\-tls
on a cache that replicates.
See
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.TP
.BI "edit " "name " "\-r"
Opens the interactive editor and hot-reloads the runtime's Lua script after
the changes are saved.
Alias:
.BR \-\-reload .
.TP
.BI "export " dir
Writes this daemon's setup as Lua into
.IR dir .
.IP
The output is
.BR config.lua ,
with an
.B include
of one file per runtime under
.BR runtimes/ .
A dns runtime's zones go under
.B zones/
and a handler script under
.BR lua/ .
The runtimes are ordered so that each one comes before the runtimes that name
it.
A runtime that another runtime made, such as a dns runtime's entrance, is left
out, because that runtime makes it again.
The daemon's own settings are carried over from its configuration file.
.IP
Files that the runtimes read but the setup does not hold are named at the top
of
.BR config.lua ,
so they can be put on the next machine as well.
Examples are a credentials file, a token file, and a certificate outside the
state directory.
The runtimes' data is not in the setup.
.IP
Before the command ends, it reads the setup back and plans each runtime from
it.
If the setup would make a runtime differently, the command names that runtime
and exits with status 1.
A directory that already holds a
.B config.lua
is refused.
See
.B "A machine's setup"
under
.BR "LUA CONFIGURATION" .
.TP
.BI "export \-\-full " file " \fR[\fB\-\-compress fast\fR|\fBbest\fR|\fBnone\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIn\fR]"
Writes the setup and its data into one tar archive.
.IP
The archive is compressed with gzip unless
.B \-\-compress none
is given.
.B best
looks harder for what repeats, as
.B gzip \-9
does.
Data that does not get smaller, such as photos and attachments, goes in as it
is.
Compression works one MiB at a time, on as many threads as the CPUs the
command may run on, or on
.I n
threads with
.BR \-\-threads .
The result is one stream that any gzip reads.
.IP
The archive holds the state directory: every runtime's file, the mailboxes'
index, the idp's accounts and keys, the Sieve scripts, the calendars'
journals, the DKIM and DNSSEC keys, and the certificates.
It also holds the daemon's configuration directory, and what each runtime
keeps elsewhere.
That includes a storage runtime's buckets, a cache's file and its journal, a
zone, a script, and a file of tokens.
The Lua setup that
.B export
writes goes in beside them.
.IP
A db runtime that is running is written by its own backup, which holds its
contents at one moment, rather than copied while it writes.
A cache gives what its file and its journal hold, which is what a crash would
leave.
When the cache journals, that is every write except those of the last
.BR \-\-persistent\-interval .
.IP
The archive does not hold a log, what a runtime writes as its output, a
backups directory, or the files a sync runtime carries, which are the user's
own.
The command names each of these when it ends.
Run it as root or as the daemon's user, while the daemon runs or not.
The archive is written with mode 0600, and only once it is complete.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley export \-\-full /var/backups/socketley\-$(date +%F).tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "restore " file " \fR[\fB\-\-force\fR]"
Puts what
.B export \-\-full
wrote back where it was.
Each file is owned by the user that owned it, where that user exists on this
machine.
Each file is written beside its place and then moved there whole.
.IP
The daemon must be stopped first.
When it is started again, it brings every runtime back from its state as it
was, running where it ran.
A state directory that already holds runtimes is refused unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
A path outside those the archive's manifest names makes the command refuse the
archive.
.IP
Some paths are left where they are and named, for
.B tar
to take out by hand.
These are the paths the manifest names under
.IR /etc ,
.IR /usr ,
.IR /root ,
.IR /boot ,
.IR /run ,
.I /var/spool
and the like, or among a home's hidden files.
This machine's state and configuration directories are not among them.
No file is restored setuid or setgid.
Outside the state and configuration directories, a file that the archive says
root owns is owned by the daemon's user.
.RS
.PP
.nf
systemctl stop socketley
socketley restore /var/backups/socketley\-2026\-10\-25.tar.gz
systemctl start socketley
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "import " "name " "[\-\-restart] " "json"
Updates a runtime's configuration from a JSON string.
Without the
.I json
argument, the command reads the document from standard input, so
.B show
pipes straight into
.BR import :
.nf
socketley show name | socketley import name
.fi
The standard-input form has no command-line length limit; the quoted form
does.
Whitespace outside string literals is stripped before the document is sent,
so the pretty-printed output of
.B show
is accepted as it is.
.B import
accepts the same fields as the interactive editor (see below).
Fields marked
.B (read-only)
are ignored.
Fields marked
.B (requires restart)
cannot be changed while the runtime is running.
Port, protocol, and UDP settings cannot be changed while running.
.PP
.B "A field the document does not mention is left alone."
Only the fields named are applied, so a partial document changes only those
fields.
To clear a field, write it out, for example
.B """tls"": false"
or
.BR """cert_path"": """"" .
Leaving a field out does not clear it.
If it did, a runtime configured for TLS could be handed
.B {"name":"x"}
and answer in cleartext afterwards, while the command reported success.
.PP
.B import
is also the fuller of the two ways to reconfigure a runtime.
.B edit
takes only a subset of the flags: it has no cache\-specific ones at all, and no
.BR \-\-backend " or " \-\-lua .
Anything that
.B edit
refuses with "unknown flag" can still be set: dump the document with
.BR show ,
change the field, and import it back.
.PP
A JSON document is held to the same rules as the flags.
On every runtime type,
.BR tls ,
.BR cert_path ,
.BR key_path " and " ca_path
are accepted as a complete set and refused as an incomplete one.
On a
.BR cache ,
a document that sets
.B tls
alongside
.B replicate_target
is rejected outright and nothing is applied, because replication is not
encrypted (see
.BR LIMITATIONS ).
.BR mesh_client_ca ,
.B mesh_client_cert
and
.B mesh_client_key
belong to a proxy alone.
.PP
Clearing any of these fields must also be written out; as for every field, a
document that leaves a field out keeps the runtime's own value.
This is how to make a runtime startable again after it was restored from an
older state file with certificate paths it can no longer read.
Set the paths to empty; do not delete the lines:
.nf
socketley show name > cfg.json
# set the TLS and mesh paths to "" in an editor, do not delete the lines
socketley import name "$(cat cfg.json)"
.fi
.PP
A document that leaves out
.B name
and
.B type
applies to the runtime the command names, with that runtime's type.
So one field can be changed with one key:
.nf
socketley import ui '{"domain":"storage"}'
.fi
.SS Edit: JSON Configuration Fields
The interactive editor opens a JSON file with the following fields.
Fields marked
.B (read-only)
are ignored on import.
Fields marked
.B (requires restart)
cannot be changed while the runtime is running; stop the runtime first.
.PP
.B Identity fields:
.TS
l lw(56).
name	Runtime name \fB(requires restart)\fR
id	6-char hex identifier (read-only, ignored on import)
type	T{
.ad l
Runtime type: server, client, proxy, cache, storage, dns, sync, idp, db, mqtt, mail, tunnel, metrics, mcp, web (read-only, ignored on import)
T}
was_running	Internal daemon state (ignored on import)
.TE
.PP
.B Common fields (all types):
.TS
l l lw(42).
Field	Type	Description
_
port	number	T{
.ad l
Listener/connection port \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
listen	string	T{
.ad l
"unix:/abs/path.sock" \(em an AF_UNIX listener instead of a port \fB(server/proxy/cache; requires restart)\fR
T}
log_file	string	State transition log file path
write_file	string	Message write file path
lua_script	string	Lua script path (absolute)
bash_output	bool	Output messages to stdout
bash_prefix	bool	Prefix messages with [name]
bash_timestamp	bool	Prefix messages with [HH:MM:SS]
max_connections	number	Max concurrent connections (0 = unlimited)
rate_limit	number	T{
.ad l
Messages/sec per connection (0 = unlimited)
T}
global_rate_limit	number	T{
.ad l
Messages/sec across all connections (0 = unlimited)
T}
idle_timeout	number	T{
.ad l
Close idle connections after N seconds (0 = disabled)
T}
congestion	string	T{
.ad l
TCP congestion control of accepted connections (\fB\-\-congestion\fR)
T}
drain	bool	T{
.ad l
Graceful shutdown (flush writes before close)
T}
tls	bool	T{
.ad l
Terminate TLS on the listener \fB(server, proxy)\fR; dial TLS \fB(client)\fR
T}
cert_path	string	T{
.ad l
PEM certificate \fB(server, proxy; needs tls)\fR; on a \fBclient\fR, the certificate it presents
T}
key_path	string	T{
.ad l
PEM private key \fB(server, proxy; needs tls)\fR; on a \fBclient\fR, the key for cert_path
T}
ca_path	string	T{
.ad l
Client CA for mutual TLS \fB(server, needs tls)\fR; on a \fBproxy\fR, the trust store for TLS backends; on a \fBclient\fR, the trust store for its target
T}
target	string	Client target host:port \fB(requires restart)\fR
cache_name	string	Connected cache runtime name
group	string	Group tag for dynamic proxy discovery
.TE
.PP
.B Server fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
mode	string	"inout", "in", "out", or "master"
udp	bool	Use UDP instead of TCP \fB(requires restart)\fR
master_pw	string	Static password for master mode
send_to	string	"sender", "others", "master", comma separated
http_dir	string	Static file root, as \fB\-\-http\fR
http_cache	bool	Hold those files in memory, as \fB\-\-http\-cache\fR
compress	bool	Compressed copies (\fB\-\-compress\fR)
origins	string	Other web origins that may open a WebSocket, as \fB\-\-origins\fR
upstreams	array	Upstream addresses, as \fB\-\-upstream\fR
.TE
.PP
.B Client fields:
.TS
l l l.
Field	Type	Description
_
mode	string	"inout", "in", or "out"
udp	bool	Use UDP instead of TCP \fB(requires restart)\fR
reconnect	number	Auto-reconnect max attempts (-1=off, 0=infinite)
.TE
.PP
.B Proxy fields:
.TS
l l lw(42).
Field	Type	Description
_
protocol	string	"http" or "tcp" \fB(requires restart)\fR
strategy	string	"round-robin", "random", or "lua"
root	string	A FastCGI proxy's directory (\fB\-\-root\fR)
fastcgi_root	string	It as the application server sees it
index	string	The index script (default "index.php")
compress	bool	Compressed copies (\fB\-\-compress\fR)
backends	array	T{
.ad l
List of backend addresses ["ip:port", "@group", ...]
T}
health_check	number	T{
.ad l
0 disabled, 1 TCP connect, 2 HTTP GET \(em the number \fB\-\-health\-check\fR stored
T}
health_interval	number	T{
.ad l
Health check interval in seconds (default 5)
T}
health_path	string	HTTP health check path (default "/health")
health_threshold	number	Failures to mark unhealthy (default 3)
circuit_threshold	number	Errors to open circuit (default 5)
circuit_timeout	number	Seconds before half-open (default 30)
backend_timeout	number	T{
.ad l
Seconds for the first response byte (default 2, 0 = off)
T}
retry_count	number	Retry count (0 = disabled)
retry_all	bool	Also retry non-idempotent methods
mesh_client_ca	string	T{
.ad l
CA for certificates clients present \fB(needs tls)\fR
T}
mesh_client_cert	string	T{
.ad l
Client certificate presented to backends
T}
mesh_client_key	string	Its private key (both or neither)
.TE
.PP
These are the names used in the document.
They are not always the names of the flags or of the Lua configuration keys
that set the same things.
The document spells them
.BR retry_count ,
.BR mesh_client_ca ,
.B mesh_client_cert
and
.BR mesh_client_key ,
while the flags are
.B \-\-retry
and
.B \-\-client\-ca
and the config-file keys are
.B retry
and
.BR client_ca .
There is no
.B sidecar
field.
Sidecar is a flag and a config key that sets several of the fields above, and
nothing writes or reads it in a state document.
.PP
.B Storage fields:
.TS
l l lw(42).
Field	Type	Description
_
dir	string	T{
.ad l
Directory holding the buckets, as \fB\-\-dir\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
anonymous	bool	Accept requests without credentials, as \fB\-\-anonymous\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
idp	string	The idp runtime whose sign-in the web interface takes, as \fB\-\-idp\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
max_bytes	number	T{
.ad l
Quota on the bytes held, as \fB\-\-max\-bytes\fR (0 = none); takes effect on a serving runtime
T}
sse_key_file	string	T{
.ad l
The SSE\-S3 master key file, as \fB\-\-sse\-key\-file\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
domain	string	T{
.ad l
The domains of virtual\-hosted\-style addressing, comma\-separated, as \fB\-\-domain\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
website_domain	string	T{
.ad l
The domains buckets' websites are served under, comma\-separated, as \fB\-\-website\-domain\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
notify_targets	string	T{
.ad l
The event notification webhooks file, as \fB\-\-notify\-targets\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
replication_targets	string	T{
.ad l
The replication endpoints file, as \fB\-\-replication\-targets\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
fsync	string	T{
.ad l
"always", "interval" or "never", as \fB\-\-fsync\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
sync	string	T{
"never", "active" or "per\-user", as \fB\-\-sync\fR; absent is never \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
fsync_interval	number	T{
.ad l
Seconds between the filesystem syncs of \fB\-\-fsync interval\fR, as \fB\-\-fsync\-interval\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
T}
.TE
.PP
.B Db fields:
.TS
l l lw(42).
Field	Type	Description
_
schema	string	The collections file, as \fB\-\-schema\fR; read again when it changes
keys	string	The service keys file, as \fB\-\-keys\fR; read again when it changes
idp	string	The idp runtime whose accounts own documents, as \fB\-\-idp\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
origins	string	The web origins it answers, as \fB\-\-origins\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
max_bytes	number	The most bytes the documents hold, as \fB\-\-max\-bytes\fR (0 = none) \fB(requires restart)\fR
cache	number	The memory of documents read, as \fB\-\-cache\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
replicate	string	The database this one copies, as \fB\-\-replicate\fR; empty for none \fB(requires restart)\fR
replicate_key	string	The file of the key it asks with, as \fB\-\-replicate\-key\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
backup_dir	string	Where scheduled backups go, as \fB\-\-backup\-dir\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
backup_every	number	Seconds between backups, as \fB\-\-backup\-every\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
backup_keep	number	Backups kept, as \fB\-\-backup\-keep\fR \fB(requires restart)\fR
.TE
.PP
.B Cache fields:
.TS
l l lw(42).
Field	Type	Description
_
persistent_path	string	Persistence file path
persistent_interval	number	Journal flush cadence in seconds (0 = no journal) \fB(requires restart)\fR
cache_mode	string	"readonly", "readwrite", or "admin"
resp_forced	bool	Force RESP2 protocol \fB(requires restart)\fR
replicate_target	string	Leader host:port \fB(requires restart)\fR
max_memory	number	Max memory in bytes (0 = unlimited)
eviction	string	T{
.ad l
"noeviction", "allkeys-lru", "allkeys-random"
T}
.TE
.PP
.B Example JSON (server):
.nf
{
    "name": "api",
    "id": "a1b2c3",
    "type": "server",
    "port": 9000,
    "was_running": true,
    "lua_script": "/home/user/handler.lua",
    "max_connections": 100,
    "rate_limit": 1000,
    "mode": "inout"
}
.fi
.TP
.B settings \fR[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-j\fR|\fB\-\-json\fR]
Lists every
.B SOCKETLEY_*
setting as the daemon and the processes it starts see it.
For each setting, it shows the value, where the value came from, and what the
setting is for.
The value comes from the daemon's environment, from the
.B settings
table of its configuration file (see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ),
or from the default.
.IP
The plain listing holds the knobs an operator may turn.
.B \-\-all
adds the test suite's hooks, the variables a command reads from its own
environment, and the ones the daemon sets for what it starts.
A
.B FOR
column marks each of these.
.B \-\-json
prints the same rows as an array of objects with
.BR name ,
.BR env ,
.BR value ,
.BR from ,
.B for
and
.BR meaning .
Every variable under
.B ENVIRONMENT
is one row.
.TP
.B dashboard
Prints the URL of the built-in web dashboard.
It requires
.B daemon.metrics_port
to be set in the daemon configuration file (see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ).
The dashboard gives a live view of all runtimes, connections, and throughput.
.IP
The dashboard listens on loopback.
It answers 403 to a request whose Host is not this machine's, so a page whose
name was rebound to 127.0.0.1 reads nothing.
A request that carries
.BR Via ,
which no page can set, comes from a proxy and is served whatever its Host.
So a proxy with
.B \-\-domain
and
.B \-\-auth
can put the dashboard on the internet.
Its pages are never framed, sniffed, cached or named in a Referer.
Example output:
.nf
Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
.fi
.TP
.B ls \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-j\fR | \fB\-\-format json\fR] [\fB\-\-silent\fR | \fB\-s\fR] [\fIcolumns\fR]
Lists all runtimes, in every state.
The output is Docker-style, with the columns
ID, NAME, TYPE, PORT, CONN (connections), OWNED BY, STATUS, CREATED, GROUP.
With \fB\-\-json\fR or \fB\-\-format json\fR, it outputs a JSON array of runtime objects.
With \fB\-\-silent\fR or \fB\-s\fR, it prints only runtime names (one per line).
.IP
For a running runtime, the STATUS column shows its uptime, followed by up to
two notes when they apply.
.BI ( N " restarts)"
means the daemon found the worker dead and forked a replacement that many
times.
The uptime is the replacement's.
A runtime that holds state in memory, above all a cache, lost that state at
every restart.
The JSON form reports the same number in
.BR restarts .
.IP
.B NOT RESPONDING
means the worker process is alive but has said nothing for fifteen seconds
while using at least half a core.
A worker publishes on every completion, so one doing any work at all is heard
from within a quarter second.
Silence means the worker is either idle, which costs no CPU, or stuck inside a
callback, which costs a core.
Only the second case is reported, and it is only reported: nothing is killed.
A script that deliberately computes for a long time looks the same from
outside the process.
A worker that is stuck without using CPU (blocked rather than spinning) looks
like an idle one and is not reported.
.IP
The JSON form has a
.B listen
field beside
.BR port .
It is empty for an ordinary runtime, and
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
for a server started with
.BR \-\-listen .
The PORT column shows
.B \-
for such a runtime, since it has no port.
.IP
When more than one daemon is running, a line under the table says how many
there are and which one answered.
The line appears even when the table is empty, because a listing from the
wrong daemon looks exactly like an idle machine.
It is printed only for the default table.
.BR \-\-json ,
.B \-s
and the column selectors are what scripts read, and they carry nothing extra.
.B ps
does the same.
.RS
.PP
.B Column selectors
\(em pass one or more to show only those columns:
.BR \-\-id ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-port ,
.BR \-\-status ,
.BR \-\-conn ,
.BR \-\-owner ,
.BR \-\-created ,
.BR \-\-group .
.RE
.TP
.B ps \fR[\fB\-a\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-j\fR | \fB\-\-format json\fR] [\fB\-\-silent\fR | \fB\-s\fR] [\fIcolumns\fR]
Lists only running runtimes, in the same format as
.BR ls .
With \fB\-\-json\fR, it outputs a JSON array (with an uptime field).
With \fB\-\-silent\fR or \fB\-s\fR, it prints only runtime names (one per line).
.RS
.PP
A runtime that did not come up is in state
.BR failed ,
and
.B ps
does not list it.
So a runtime that failed to start produces no row here, and if nothing else
is running, there is no output at all.
That is the state to look for when something did not start, so look for it
with
.B ps \-a
or
.BR ls .
.PP
.B \-a\fR, \fB\-\-all
.br
Lists runtimes in every state, not only
.BR running .
The STATUS column then shows the state
.RB ( Created ,
.BR Stopped ,
.BR Failed )
instead of an uptime, and \fB\-\-json\fR reports it in the \fBstatus\fR field.
Bare
.B ps
is unchanged, so anything that parses it still sees only running runtimes.
.PP
.B Column selectors:
.BR \-\-id ,
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-type ,
.BR \-\-port ,
.BR \-\-uptime " (or " \-\-status ),
.BR \-\-conn ,
.BR \-\-owner ,
.BR \-\-created .
.PP
.B \-\-status
and
.B \-\-uptime
are two names for one column, headed
.BR UPTIME .
It holds the uptime for a running runtime and the state
.RB ( Created ,
.BR Stopped ,
.BR Failed )
for anything else.
So under
.BR \-a ,
a row can read
.B Failed
beneath a header saying
.BR UPTIME .
.B ls
heads the same column
.BR STATUS ;
.B ps
keeps its header because scripts select on it.
.RE
.TP
.BI "owner " "name"
Shows ownership information for a runtime: its parent's name, its children,
and its on_parent_stop policy (stop or remove).
.TP
.BI "cluster " "[subcommand]"
Inspects the cluster.
It requires a running daemon with cluster mode enabled (via
.B daemon \-\-name
+
.BR "daemon \-\-cluster" ).
The command reads the cluster directory from the daemon automatically.
.RS
.TP
.B ls
Lists all daemons in the cluster, with their runtime counts and heartbeat
status.
.TP
.B ps
Lists all runtimes across the entire cluster.
.TP
.BI "group " "name"
Shows all members of a group across the cluster.
.TP
.BI "show " "daemon\-name"
Prints a daemon's raw cluster JSON file.
.TP
.B stats
Shows a cluster summary: daemon counts, runtime totals and group membership.
.TP
.B watch
Refreshes the cluster view every 2 seconds until Ctrl+C.
.RE
.TP
.B ping
Asks the daemon to identify itself.
It prints
.BI "socketley\-daemon " "version pid dev" : "ino path"
for the daemon serving the socket.
The last two fields name the executable the daemon is running: its device and
inode, and its
.I /proc/self/exe
link.
The link carries
.B (deleted)
once the file the daemon was started from has been replaced.
The path comes last because a path may contain spaces.
A daemon built before those fields existed answers with the version and pid
alone, and is not treated as a different build because of it.
.RS
.PP
Every command runs this check first, before it sends anything.
A
.BR connect (2)
that succeeds only proves that something is listening on the path, not that
it is a daemon.
A command written to a stranger is a command delivered to a stranger.
A path held by something that does not answer this check is refused, and
nothing is sent to it.
.RE
.TP
.B help \fR| \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-h\fR
Prints help; what it prints depends on where the output goes.
.RS
.PP
On a
.BR terminal ,
it opens the interactive browser.
The browser is a menu, not a page: you move through it by number, and it takes
no topic argument, because everything is behind the menu.
.PP
Anywhere else, such as a pipe, a redirect or a CI step, it prints a
plain\-text summary of the commands and exits
.BR 0 .
It opens the browser only when both standard input and standard output are
terminals.
There is no flag, because
.B socketley \-\-help
is what a person types either way, and only the destination differs.
A menu drawn into a pipe is a menu nobody can answer.
.PP
It does not require or start a daemon.
The browser is
.I man/socketley\-help.sh
under the source tree the binary was built in, and
.I /usr/share/socketley/socketley\-help.sh
otherwise.
The nearest one wins, so a binary built in a tree prints that tree's help
rather than an installed copy from another version.
If neither is readable, the command says so and exits 2: the installation is
at fault, not the input.
This page is the reference and the browser is the tour.
They cover the same ground and are kept in step with each other.
.RE
.TP
.B version \fR| \fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-v\fR
Prints the socketley version number and exits.
It does not require or start a daemon.
Standard output is the version number in every case.
Anything about the daemon on the socket goes to standard error, so
.B $(socketley version)
is unaffected by it.
.RS
.PP
Two things are reported there.
The first is a daemon whose version differs.
The second is a daemon still executing a file that has since been replaced at
the path it was started from.
The version alone cannot show that, because it is one compile-time literal
that every build of a release carries.
The second case is what ordinarily happens when you rebuild while a daemon
holds its runtimes.
It is also why a daemon reporting the same version as the binary can reject a
new flag that the binary has.
.PP
The replaced file is reported only when the daemon and this CLI name the same
executable path.
Running the CLI from a copy elsewhere on disk reports nothing.
A copy is not evidence about anybody's build, and comparing inodes across
different paths would report a replaced file on every command.
.RE
.TP
.BI "\-\-lua " "lua_file"
Loads a Lua orchestration configuration.
It can configure the daemon name and cluster mode, and then creates multiple
runtimes from a single config file.
.TP
.B Older forms
These are the command forms from before the runtime name came first.
They are kept so that existing scripts go on working.
.RS
.TP
.BI "db\-backup " "db file"
The same as
\fIdb\fB backup \fIfile\fR.
.TP
.BI "db\-restore " "db file"
The same as
\fIdb\fB restore \fIfile\fR.
.TP
.BI "db\-query " "db collection"
The same as
\fIdb\fB query \fIcollection\fR.
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-pub " mqtt
The same as
\fImqtt\fB pub\fR.
Likewise,
.BR mqtt\-sub ", " mqtt\-sessions ", " mqtt\-kick " and " mqtt\-retained
are its
.BR sub ", " sessions ", " kick " and " retained ,
and
.B mqtt\-passwd
is
.BR "mqtt passwd" .
.TP
.BI "mqtt\-sub " mqtt
.TQ
.BI "mqtt\-sessions " mqtt
.TQ
.BI "mqtt\-kick " mqtt
.TQ
.BI "mqtt\-retained " mqtt
.TQ
.BI "mqtt\-passwd " file
Older forms of the mqtt verbs, as above.
.TP
.BI "idp\-user " idp
The same as
\fIidp\fB user\fR.
Likewise,
.BI "idp\-ssh " idp " ca"
is
\fIidp\fB ssh\-ca\fR.
.TP
.BI "idp\-ssh " idp
An older form, as above.
.TP
.BI "dnssec " dns
The same as
\fIdns\fB dnssec\fR.
.TP
.BI "tunnel " edge " invite"
This form, with
.BR set ", " list ", " shares " or " revoke ,
is the same as
\fIedge\fB invite\fR.
.TP
.BI "metrics " "metrics verb"
The same as
\fImetrics\fB verb\fR.
.TP
.BI "mail " "\fR[\fImail\fR] \fIverb"
The same as
\fImail\fB verb\fR.
Without a name, it uses the daemon's only mail runtime.
.TP
.BI "send " "mail " "\fB\-\-to\fR ..."
The same as
\fImail\fB send\fR.
.RE
.SH DAEMON FLAGS
These flags configure the running daemon through IPC commands. If no daemon is
running, one is started automatically.
.PP
Each socket is served by one daemon. While it runs, the daemon holds a lock on
the socket's path with
.B .lock
appended. A second daemon started meanwhile, by hand or by a command's
auto-start, waits for the first one to answer and then exits 0. If the lock
holder never answers, the second daemon exits 1 after ten seconds.
.TP
.BI "\-\-name " "name" " | \-n " "name"
Sets this daemon's identity. Cluster mode requires it.
The name can be changed at any time. If cluster mode is active, the daemon
republishes its cluster file under the new name and removes the old one.
.TP
.BI "\-\-cluster " "dir"
Enables cluster mode on the given shared directory. Every 2 seconds, each
daemon publishes its runtime state there and scans for peer daemons. Proxy
.B @group
backends automatically discover runtimes on remote daemons.
In Docker Compose, mount a shared volume at this path.
The advertised host address is set automatically from
.BR gethostname (3).
Requires
.B \-\-name
to be set first.
.SH COMMON FLAGS
These flags work with all runtime types:
.TP
.BI "\-p " "port"
Sets the listener port of a server, proxy, cache, storage or dns runtime, or
the target port of a client. The form
.BI "\-p " address : port
also sets
.BR \-\-bind .
.TP
.BI "\-\-bind " address
The IPv4 address that the listener port binds to. Default: every address, which
.B 0.0.0.0
also means. For example,
.B 127.0.0.1
limits a runtime to clients on the same host, such as a reverse proxy there.
The address can be changed while the runtime is stopped, with
.B edit
or with the
.B bind
field of
.BR import ,
and
.B "ls \-\-json"
shows it. A value that is not an IPv4 address is refused when it is given. An
address the host does not have makes the start fail.
.TP
.B \-s
Starts the runtime immediately after creation.
.TP
.B \-\-test
Checks a create command without creating anything (a dry run). The type, the
name and every flag are checked, and then nothing is kept. No runtime appears in
.BR ls ,
no state file is written, and the name stays free for the
.B create
that follows.
.IP
The port is not bound. A
.B \-\-lua
script is loaded, which validates it and runs its top level, but
.B on_start
is not called, because nothing starts. The result says whether
.B create
accepts this command line.
.TP
.BI "\-\-log " "file"
Appends one line per state transition to
.IR file :
.IP
.EX
2026\-09\-10 11:48:22 api created
2026\-09\-10 11:48:22 api running
2026\-09\-10 11:48:31 api stopped
.EE
.IP
Each line carries the date as well as the time, because this file outlives a
day, unlike the daemon's own log. The file is created with mode 0600 and opened
for append, so a restart adds to it instead of replacing it.
.IP
The first line records the state the runtime was in when the flag was given.
That is why
.B created
appears at all: a runtime is constructed in that state, before any flag is
parsed. So when
.B edit
gives
.B \-\-log
to a runtime that is already serving, the file starts with a
.B running
line. That line marks where the record starts, not a transition anyone made.
.IP
The recorded state is the one
.B ls
reports, which is the daemon's. A runtime lives in its own process, and that
process keeps its own copy of the same state. Both carry the path, but only the
daemon writes, because two writers would double every line. One visible consequence:
when an out-of-process runtime's worker refuses its configuration, the file
records
.BR created ,
.B running
and then
.BR failed .
The
.B running
line appears because the daemon had requested that state and reported it until
the worker answered.
.IP
The path must be absolute. A relative path would be resolved against the
.I daemon's
working directory, which is wherever the daemon was started, or
.B /
under systemd. It is refused so that the file is never written where the
operator would not think to look.
.IP
A path that cannot be opened or written is reported once, at
.BR ERROR ,
and not again.
.TP
.BI "\-w " "file"
Writes every received message to
.IR file ,
one per line, in the exact bytes it arrived in. This is the file counterpart of
.BR \-b ,
and it is written at the same point in the runtime where the message is
counted.
.IP
The file is created with mode 0600 on the first message, not at start, so a
runtime that receives nothing leaves no file behind. It is opened for append, so
a restart adds to it instead of truncating it. Under
.BI \-\-shards " N"
the N workers share one path. An append of this size is atomic on a regular
file, so the lines interleave without tearing.
.IP
Unlike
.BR \-b ,
this flag does not scrub control characters from the bytes. The scrubbing
exists to stop a peer from driving the operator's terminal with an escape
sequence, and a file is not a terminal.
.IP
The path must be absolute, for the same reason as with
.BR \-\-log .
.IP
.B \-w
takes effect on a runtime that is already serving, so
.BI "edit " "name" " \-w other.log"
moves the writing without a restart. A path that cannot be opened or written is
reported once, at
.BR ERROR ,
and not again. Otherwise a runtime under load would log one line per message.
.IP
The files written by
.B \-w
and
.B \-\-log
stay open once written, so a file that is renamed away keeps receiving lines.
Rotate them with logrotate's
.BR copytruncate .
.TP
.BI "\-\-lua " "script"
Attaches a Lua configuration script with callbacks. If the script does not load,
the command is refused with exit 1. The output gives the reason, not only the
fact: the parse error, or the
.B filters
or
.B routes
rule that the script broke.
.TP
.B \-b
Prints received messages to stdout (bash mode).
.TP
.B \-bp
Prints messages with the runtime name as a prefix: [name] message
.TP
.B \-bt
Prints messages with a timestamp: [HH:MM:SS] message
.TP
.B \-bpt ", " \-btp
Prints messages with both timestamp and prefix: [HH:MM:SS] [name] message.
Both spellings are accepted and mean the same thing.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-connections " "n"
Limits the number of concurrent connections. New connections beyond the limit
are closed immediately. 0 means no limit (default). Alias:
.BR \-\-max\-conn .
.IP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
the limit is the runtime's, not each shard's.
A shard counts its siblings' connections as they published them on their last
turn, so connections accepted on two shards in the same instant can pass the
limit by a few.
.TP
.BI "\-\-read\-buffer " "size"
The most data the runtime accumulates from one connection before it refuses
more. Takes a size with K, M or G. Default 1M. This is the inbound counterpart of
.BR \-\-write\-buffer ,
and like it, every runtime has one.
.IP
The largest single value inside the buffer is half the buffer's size, which
leaves room for the framing around it. So the default gives a cache a 512K limit
on any one value.
.IP
Raise it for a cache that holds documents rather than fields. For comparison,
Redis's own
.B proto\-max\-bulk\-len
defaults to 512M, a thousand times this default. Lower it for a runtime facing
the open internet, where the buffer is memory that a client controls. Values
under 64K are raised to 64K rather than refused. A smaller buffer would make the
value limit smaller than an ordinary form post or JSON document needs.
.IP
The limit is per connection, and nothing bounds the sum but
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
which defaults to 0 (no limit). So the memory to plan for is
.BR \-\-read\-buffer " \(mu " \-\-max\-connections .
At both defaults that product is unbounded: the only ceiling is the worker's
descriptor limit, which is over a million on the test machine. Measured on a
cache with 200 connections, a connection that has sent nothing costs about
13 KB. A connection holding an unfinished command costs everything it has sent,
up to this buffer. An unfinished command is not a malformed one, and nothing
refuses it. Only its size is bounded, and only per connection.
.IP
A value larger than the limit is answered with
.B "\-ERR value is larger than this cache accepts (limit N bytes)"
and the connection is closed. Redis does the same for a bulk length it will not
accept: the body was never read, so there is no framed position to continue
from. The answer is deliberately not
.BR "\-ERR protocol error" .
The command was framed correctly and only its value was too big, and a client
library that is told its framing is broken reconnects and sends the same value
again. A length that really does not parse still gets the protocol error.
.IP
One measured caveat: the runtime writes the refusal and then closes the
connection. If the client is still sending at that moment, it receives an RST,
and Linux discards the queued reply. A client that overshoots by up to about
two megabytes reads the reason; one that sends eight times the limit does not.
If a deployment needs the larger values, raise the limit.
.IP
The flag can be changed with
.B edit
on a running runtime, and the change takes effect at once. The bound is compared
on every read and the value limit is derived at each parse, so nothing keeps
the old number.
.TP
.BI "\-\-write\-buffer " "size"
The most unsent data the runtime queues for one connection before it drops that
connection. Takes a size with K, M or G. Default 8M. Every runtime has a write
queue, so this applies to every type. On a client, the queue that fills is
the one to a server that has stopped reading.
.IP
The queue is per connection, so the bound multiplies with the number of
connections:
.IP
.nf
100 streams        800 MB
1,000 streams        8 GB     (a box with 11 GiB serves about this many)
10,000 streams      80 GB     not possible
.fi
.IP
8M suits a few large transfers. For many concurrent streams it is about sixty
times too large; nginx serves ten thousand streams with 32\(hy64 KB each.
Measured with 400 sends of 64 KB into a peer that is not reading, a runtime at
the default accepts 129 of them, and one at
.B "\-\-write\-buffer 256K"
accepts 5.
.IP
The setting also affects throughput, and the right direction depends on the
number of streams. The next table is for a 1 MiB chunked response. It gives
microseconds per request above a C baseline that puts the same bytes on the
wire with one
.BR write (2)
per chunk:
.IP
.nf
                8 streams    64 streams    RSS
128K             +75.7        +139.0        45 MB
256K             +51.6        +128.1        52 MB
1M               +17.6        +218.1        74 MB
8M (default)     +27.3        +234.0       106 MB
.fi
.IP
A deep queue wins with few streams and loses with many. At 64 streams, lowering
the default to
.B 256K
gives 28 per cent more throughput for 22 per cent less CPU per request. At 8
streams the same change costs 24 per cent.
.IP
The cause is the CPU cache, not the queue itself. The two settings below run the
same code on the same bytes with the same number of copies; only this number
differs. The table gives CPU per MiB served at 64 streams. Each profile was taken
in the same time window as its rate, so every component is divided within one
run:
.IP
.nf
                total   memmove   kernel copy   page clearing
8M (default)     315      103          87            29
256K             254       40          43            30
.fi
.IP
Both copies halve. The runtime does one
.B memmove
per byte either way, and the kernel's
.B _copy_from_iter
does one copy per byte either way, so neither does less work. The bytes are
simply closer in cache. On the test machine, L2 is 4 MiB per core and L3 is
16 MiB shared. At 64 streams the default queues up to about 69 MB at once, more
than either cache holds, while
.B 256K
queues about 8 MB, which fits. A C baseline that does the same one copy per byte,
into a buffer it reuses, spends 29 and 21 on those two components. So once the
working set is accounted for, little difference remains.
.IP
The sweep also measured
.B 64K
and got a figure within 0.6 to 0.9 per cent of
.BR 128K .
That is expected: a value under 128K is raised to 128K, so both runs measured
the same buffer, and the spread is the harness's own variation.
.IP
The pacing watermark that
.B on_writable
fires on is derived from this value and has no setting of its own. Two separate
settings could be set inconsistently, and a watermark above the bound would
make a runtime pace only after it had already closed the connection. A script
reads the derived value with
.BR self.watermark() .
It is a call rather than a field because the value changes.
.IP
While the runtime is keeping up, the watermark is half the bound. When the
runtime holds more than 8 MiB in total, the watermark becomes that budget
divided by the live connections. It never goes above the configured half or
below 64 KB, and it returns to the half once the backlog has been gone for
50 milliseconds. The operator sets the bound, because the bound is memory and
decides when a connection is closed. How far a producer may run ahead before it
pauses is a CPU cache question: these bytes are written here and then read again
by the kernel as it copies them into a socket.
.IP
Measured on a 1 MiB chunked response over seven interleaved passes, with
byte\(hyidentical responses in every arm: 4,480 responses a second at 16 streams
against 5,273, and 3,634 at 64 streams against 4,221. At 8 and 128 streams the
result was unchanged within the spread. Dividing by the connection count without
checking for a backlog cost 19 per cent at 8 streams. There the queues never
fill, and the only effect was stopping a producer four times as often.
.IP
The watermark does not bound memory. The peak is still the bound times the
number of connections that reach it. A bound that shrank under a live connection
would close that connection for a backlog it had been allowed to build. Limiting
memory for many clients at once is still this flag's job: on the same load, the
peak was 60 MB at
.B "\-\-write\-buffer 256K"
against 146 MB at the default.
.IP
Values under 128K are raised to 128K rather than refused. The watermark is half
the bound, and the largest single entry these queues build is 64 KB, so a
smaller bound would leave a runtime unable to send one message. The flag can be
changed with
.B edit
on a running runtime, and the script sees the change at once, because the bound
is read on every send rather than cached.
.TP
.BI "\-\-rate\-limit " "n"
Limits messages per second on each connection, using a token bucket. A server
drops excess messages silently. A
.B cache
answers
.BR "error: rate limited" ,
since its protocol has a way to say so. A
.B proxy
closes the connection instead, because a request dropped in silence would leave
its client waiting for a response that never comes: a hang rather than a
refusal. The bucket starts full, so a connection may spend a whole second's
allowance at once and then wait for it to refill. 0 means no limit (default).
.IP
A
.B client
does not accept this flag, nor
.B \-\-max\-connections
or
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout .
All three act on inbound traffic. A client opens one connection outward and
accepts none, so there is nothing for any of them to act on, and accepting them
would let
.B show
report a control that is not in force. They are refused at
.BR create ", " edit " and " import
alike. Import needs its own check because it writes the field without going
through the flag parser.
.IP
They are refused rather than given a meaning on a client, because each possible
meaning is a design decision. An outbound throttle would have to decide what
happens to a message that may not go yet: queue it and report success for
something that leaves ten seconds later, fail the send, or drop it. An idle
timeout would have to decide what happens after the close, since a client
reconnects as soon as its connection is dropped.
.IP
On a proxy, one token is charged per read completion rather than per HTTP
request. That is the same event
.B \-\-global\-rate\-limit
is charged on, and the same one
.B total_messages
counts. So a client that pipelines four requests into one segment pays one
token.
.TP
.BI "\-\-global\-rate\-limit " "n"
Limits total messages per second across all connections, using a shared token
bucket. A server drops excess messages silently; a cache answers them with an
error. 0 means no limit (default).
.IP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
the rate is the runtime's: each shard refills its own bucket with its share,
the rate divided by the shard count and at least one a second.
The kernel spreads connections over the shards, so this holds for many
clients; a single connection lives on one shard and gets that shard's share.
.IP
A client refuses it for the same reason as
.BR \-\-rate\-limit :
it caps the total across all inbound connections, and a client has one outbound
connection.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idle\-timeout " "duration"
Closes connections that have received no data for longer than
.IR duration .
0 disables it (default). A sweep timer checks for idle connections every
30 seconds. A bare number is seconds; a suffix of
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " or " w
means seconds, minutes, hours, days or weeks
.RB ( 300 " and " 5m " both work)."
.IP
This flag closes connections that are
.IR quiet ,
which is not the same as closing connections that are
.IR dead ,
and dead connections do not need it. Every accepted TCP connection gets TCP
keepalive at 60/10/3: after a minute of silence, three probes ten seconds apart.
So a peer that loses power, or vanishes behind a NAT that forgot it, is closed
without any timeout being set. A websocket that sends nothing for an hour is
quiet but alive, and keepalive leaves it alone; only
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
closes it. A unix listener does not get keepalive, because its peer cannot
disappear silently.
.TP
.BI "\-\-congestion " name
The TCP congestion control algorithm for connections that a server, proxy,
cache or storage runtime accepts. Give the kernel's name for it, such as
.BR bbr ,
.B cubic
or
.BR reno .
It is set once on the listener, and each accepted socket inherits it. Default:
the host's default
.RB ( net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control ).
A loss-based algorithm such as cubic halves its rate at every lost packet. bbr
paces to the path's measured bandwidth and holds that rate. One 32 MB download
over 100 Mbit with a 50 ms round trip, three interleaved runs:
.IP
.nf
loss   cubic              bbr
0%     10.21 MB/s         10.13 MB/s
1%     0.38\-0.49 MB/s    9.76\-9.87 MB/s
2%     0.26\-0.27 MB/s    9.59\-9.63 MB/s
.fi
.IP
The kernel decides which algorithms may be chosen. A process without
CAP_NET_ADMIN may choose only one that
.B net.ipv4.tcp_allowed_congestion_control
lists and that is already loaded
.RB ( "modprobe tcp_bbr" ).
If the kernel refuses the name, the start fails and the log says why. The
runtime never falls back to the default silently.
.TP
.B \-\-drain
Enables graceful shutdown. On stop, the runtime closes its listener but flushes
all pending write queues before it closes connections.
.IP
A client refuses this flag, because it has no listener and no inbound
connections. What a client owes its peer when it stops is a separate question.
.TP
.BI "\-\-cpus " "set"
The CPUs this runtime's I/O may run on. Accepts
.BR max ,
a range such as
.BR 0\-3 ,
or a list such as
.BR 0,1,2,3 ,
whose elements may themselves be ranges
.RB ( 0\-1,3 ).
Default
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all. It is not a mask of the CPUs that are online
now, so a CPU that comes online later is still available to the loop.
.IP
A CPU this machine does not have, a descending range, or a set that comes out
empty is refused when the runtime is created. Such a value is never silently
rounded up to
.BR max .
Otherwise a config written for a sixteen-core machine and deployed on a
four-core one would run somewhere other than its author believes, and every
later measurement would be measuring something else.
.IP
The kernel fixes a ring's CPU set when the ring is created, and a ring belongs
to a process, not to a runtime. Every runtime inside one daemon shares that
daemon's ring. So this flag states what a runtime requires, and
.B create
refuses a requirement that the ring does not meet. The refusal names the
.B socketley daemon
flag or the
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS
setting that would satisfy it. The flag cannot be changed with
.BR edit .
.B "socketley stats"
reports the value in force.
.IP
The setting is persisted with the runtime, and the same check runs again when a
persisted runtime is restored. If the daemon restarts without the setting that
satisfied the requirement, the runtime is left
.BR failed ,
with the reason in the daemon log. It is not started on a ring that does not
meet the requirement. A runtime that asked for nothing records nothing and is
never refused for it.
.TP
.B \-\-tls
.TQ
.BI "\-\-cert " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-key " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-ca " "file"
.B "All runtime types."
Encrypts this runtime's transport with TLS. What that means depends on whether
the runtime listens or dials.
.PP
On a
.BR server ", " proxy " or " cache ,
TLS terminates on the runtime's own listening port. There,
.B \-\-tls
requires both
.BI \-\-cert " file"
(a PEM certificate, chain allowed) and
.BI \-\-key " file"
(its PEM private key), because a listener needs something to terminate TLS
with.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS exactly as a server does, and everything above the record layer
is unchanged. RESP is still auto-detected from the first byte of the
.I decrypted
stream, so a Redis client speaks the same protocol with TLS switched on, and the
line protocol works on the same port. This lets a cache be reached across a
network it does not trust.
.PP
On a
.BR client ,
TLS is dialled rather than terminated, and
.B \-\-tls
alone is a complete configuration. There is no listening port that needs a
certificate, and by default the peer is verified against the system trust
store.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file"
are optional there. They set the certificate this client
.I presents
when the peer asks for one (mutual TLS). Giving only one of the two is refused,
because the client would present nothing while the flags said otherwise.
.PP
.BI \-\-ca " file"
means something different on each role, because only some roles verify a peer
they dialled. On a
.BR server " or " cache ,
it enables mutual TLS: a client must present a certificate signed by that CA,
and a client that does not is rejected during the handshake. On a
.BR proxy ,
it is the trust store used to verify the certificate a
.B backend
presents. The proxy's own listener asks for client certificates with
.BI \-\-client\-ca " file"
instead. On a
.BR client ,
it is the trust store for the peer named by
.BR \-t ;
without it, the system store is used.
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
under
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" .
.PP
A dialled connection, such as a client's target or a proxy's backend, is
verified on two counts, and neither check can be turned off. First, the chain
must be signed by something in the trust store. Second, the certificate must be
valid for the host that was dialled. For a client, that is the name in
.BR \-t ,
matched against the certificate's DNS SAN, or against its IP SAN for an address
literal. The name checked is always the configured one, never one read from the
certificate that arrived. The minimum version is TLS 1.2. There is deliberately
no skip-verify option. A TLS client that does not verify protects against an
eavesdropper but not against someone in the path, while it reports itself as
encrypted either way.
.PP
A listener speaks TLS 1.2 and 1.3. Over 1.2, it offers only ECDHE key exchange
with AEAD ciphers (Mozilla's intermediate list). It resumes sessions from its
own cache, not from tickets. A ticket key lives as long as the process, and
anyone who learned it could open every session it ever sealed.
.PP
Trust stores are read when the runtime starts. A listener's certificate and key
are checked again every two seconds. When they are replaced on disk, by
.BR \-\-acme ,
certbot or a copy, new handshakes use them from then on, without a restart and
without dropping a connection. This happens only once both files load and match.
A pair that does not is not used: the log reports it once, and the old pair goes
on serving. The whole certificate file is sent, so a CA's intermediates go with
the leaf.
.PP
The runtime refuses a certificate that cannot be loaded and a key that does not
match it. It also refuses any incomplete combination (\c
.B \-\-tls
without a key pair on a listener, a key pair or a trust store without
.BR \-\-tls ,
or half a client-certificate pair). A refused runtime goes to the
.B failed
state, and nothing binds the port or dials the target. No path falls back to
cleartext. A client whose peer fails verification drops the socket and re-dials
on its usual backoff; it never retries in the clear.
.PP
These flags are refused on a
.B client
or a
.B server
combined with
.BR \-\-udp ,
which would need DTLS. They are refused on a server combined with
.BR \-\-upstream ,
because the upstream leg would still dial in cleartext. On a
.BR cache ,
they are refused together with
.BI \-\-replicate " host:port" .
Replication is not encrypted, so the pair would stream the whole dataset in the
clear from a port that reports itself as encrypted. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.TP
.BI "\-\-acme " "name" \fR[\fB,\fIname\fR...]
.TQ
.BI "\-\-acme\-email " "address"
.B "server, proxy, cache, storage."
Gets a certificate for the given names from an ACME CA, and the runtime itself
issues and renews it. The CA is Let's Encrypt unless
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DIRECTORY
names another.
.B \-\-acme
turns on
.BR \-\-tls .
The certificate and its key are kept in
.IR state\-dir /.acme/ name /,
under the first name.
.IP
Until the CA has issued a certificate, a self-signed one valid for a week stands
in, so the listener starts at once. The issued certificate replaces it within
seconds. It is renewed when it has less than thirty days left. A failed attempt
is retried after a minute, then after twice as long each time, up to six hours,
with the CA's reason in the log.
.IP
Let's Encrypt issues no certificates for names reserved for examples, tests or
local use, so the runtime does not request them. These names are
.BR example.com ,
.BR example.net ,
.BR example.org ,
and anything under
.BR .example ,
.BR .test ,
.BR .invalid ,
.BR .localhost ,
.BR .local ,
.BR .internal ,
.B .home.arpa
or
.BR .alt .
For those names it keeps a self-signed certificate valid for a year, and the log
says so. With
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DIRECTORY
set, they are requested like any other name, since a CA of one's own may issue
them.
.B \-\-acme\-email
is the account's contact address, where the CA writes before a certificate
expires.
.IP
A runtime on port 443 proves its names over TLS-ALPN-01, on its own listener.
The CA's handshake asks for
.B acme\-tls/1
for the name and is shown a challenge certificate for it; no other handshake is
shown that certificate. So a proxy on 443 alone is enough.
.IP
Any other runtime proves its names over HTTP-01. The CA requests
.BI http:// name /.well\-known/acme\-challenge/ token
on port 80. Every socketley
.BR server ,
.B proxy
and
.B storage
of the daemon answers that path on any port, for a token the daemon is waiting
on. It answers before it routes, serves or runs a script. So one runtime on
port 80, such as the proxy that redirects to HTTPS, is all it takes. A request
for any other token goes where the path would have gone, so a site's own files
there, such as those certbot's webroot writes, are served as before. A name must
be a DNS name with a dot; an address is refused.
.IP
A wildcard such as
.B *.example.com
is proven over DNS-01, the only challenge that can prove one. The answer goes to
the daemon's
.B dns
runtimes as the TXT record of
.BI _acme\-challenge. name
(see
.B Names runtimes are reached by
under
.BR "DNS FLAGS" ).
The CA is told to look
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_WAIT
seconds later, and the record is removed once it has. This needs a
.B dns
runtime in the same daemon that is authoritative for the zone. If the zone has
secondaries, the wait must also give them time to take the new serial.
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_CHALLENGE=dns\-01
proves every name over DNS-01, for a host whose port 80 is closed.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy web \-p 443 \-\-acme example.com,www.example.com \\
    \-\-acme\-email ops@example.com \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8080 \-s
socketley create proxy plain \-p 80 \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8080 \-s
.fi
.TP
.BR \-\-domain " [" \fIname\fR[\fB,\fIname\fR...]]
The DNS names the runtime is reached by. With no name, the flag uses the
runtime's own name
.RB ( "create server chat \-\-domain"
is reached as
.BR chat ).
A
.B dns
runtime with
.B \-\-publish
answers for these names with this machine's addresses. A name without a dot is
placed under each zone the dns runtime serves
.RB ( chat
becomes
.B chat.example.com
for
.BR example.com ).
A name with a dot is used as written, and
.BI *. name
covers every name beneath it. When a runtime is removed, its names go with it.
On a
.B storage
runtime the flag is the storage's own, and it also names the virtual hosts of
its buckets (see
.BR "STORAGE FLAGS" );
those are published through the wildcard beneath the name. The names given to
.B \-\-acme
are published too.
.TP
.BI "\-g " "name"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-group " "name"
Assigns the runtime to a named group. Groups enable dynamic proxy backend
discovery: a proxy with
.B @groupname
as a backend routes to all running members of that group at connection time.
New members are discovered automatically, without restarting the proxy.
.IP
A member joins its group when it is actually listening, not when it is created.
For a runtime that runs in its own process, that is a few milliseconds after
.B create
returns: the port is bound in that process, and the daemon publishes the member
once the worker reports it. Until then the member is absent from the group,
rather than present and refusing connections. Routing to an address that does
not accept connections yet would trip the proxy's circuit breaker against a
member that is merely starting up.
.TP
.BI "\-\-listen unix:" "/path/to.sock"
Listens on an AF_UNIX socket instead of a TCP port. Every runtime that listens
accepts it: server, proxy, cache and storage. A client dials out and listens on
nothing, so a client refuses the flag and points to
.BR "\-t unix:" ,
the connect side of the same socket. This flag is the other half of
.BI \-\-backend " unix:" /path/to.sock
under
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" .
With the two, a proxy and the backend it fronts can talk on one host without a
loopback TCP stack between them. A proxy can also serve its own front door on a
unix socket.
.RS
.PP
Measured on a cache serving the same client over both, on Linux 7.2.2: a round
trip falls from 10.64 to 6.57 microseconds, and throughput at pipeline depth 1
rises 2.2x to 2.4x. Most of that gain comes from the kernel, not from this
daemon: Redis over its own unix socket gains 2.0x on the same machine, so any
server on one machine can have it. TCP exists to make an unreliable link
reliable, and over loopback there is no link. But sequence numbers, windows,
retransmit timers, an skb per direction through IP, and netfilter on every
packet all run regardless. On a host running Docker, netfilter alone costs
0.9 microseconds of that round trip. An AF_UNIX socket has none of this.
.PP
The saving is per \fImessage\fR, not per operation, so a pipelining client sees
much less of it: at depth 100 it is 0.016 microseconds per operation. That is
still 1.25x here, because at that depth the cache's own per-message work is only
about 8 microseconds, and the saving is a quarter of it. Redis, whose
per-message work is nearer 32 microseconds, gains 1.02x, which is not worth the
change.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-shards ,
a runtime on a unix socket uses one core. With it, each shard binds a node of
its own (see below). Use a unix socket for a local sidecar.
.PP
.B Every unix stream socket on the data path has a 1 MiB send buffer.
This covers each socket accepted on a
.B \-\-listen unix:
node or a companion node. It also covers the socket a proxy dials to a
.BI \-\-backend " unix:"
or to a local runtime's node, and the socket a client dials with
.BR "\-t unix:" .
On a unix stream socket, the sender's buffer is the only flow control. An
accepted socket inherits nothing from its listener, and the kernel's default is
208 KB. socketley requests 512 KiB, which the kernel doubles. The request is
capped at
.IR net.core.wmem_max ,
which is 212992 on a stock kernel, so there the buffer is 416 KB. Measured on
one connection of pipelined 64 KB GETs:
.IP \(bu 2
A reader that pays for each read (a Python client reading 4 MiB at a time)
went from 2.2 to 3.0 GB/s, and the cache spent 41% less CPU per byte.
.IP \(bu 2
A TCP proxy in front of a local cache, reaching it over its node, gained 10%.
.IP \(bu 2
A reader that reads cheaply was already at about 10 GB/s, bound by the cache's
core, and is unchanged. Small requests are unchanged too.
.PP
The buffer is a ceiling, not an allocation. An idle connection holds nothing in
it. A connection whose reader has stopped holds at most 1 MiB in the kernel,
instead of the default 208 KB.
.PP
This flag is mutually exclusive with:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-p .
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-udp .
That would be a unix datagram socket, which is a different thing.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-tls .
A unix socket is not on a network; the permissions on the node are what
restrict it.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-cluster
on a cache. Cluster members advertise ports computed by a formula, and RESP
cannot write a filesystem path as a node address.
.PP
Each combination is refused at start, with the reason in the daemon log, so
neither flag silently wins.
.PP
.B \-\-shards is not among them.
A sharded runtime binds one node per shard,
.IR name #i.sock,
which is the rule the daemon's companion nodes already follow. If N processes
bound
.I one
path, each would unlink the others' node, and only the last to bind would stay
reachable. N paths give N listeners. See
.B Sharding
under
.B SERVER FLAGS
for which node a caller should pick. That choice matters on a cache but not on
a server.
.PP
The path must be absolute and shorter than 108 bytes, the size of the kernel's
.IR sun_path .
Both rules are checked when the flag is read. The kernel truncates a long path
silently, and a truncated path names a different socket, possibly a real one
that belongs to something else.
.PP
.B Under \-\-shards the rule is applied to the longest node, not to the path as typed.
.RI name #15.sock
is three bytes longer than
.IR name .sock,
so a path that fits can still produce a set of nodes that does not. The shard
that did not fit would fail at its bind while the earlier shards were already
serving. So the path is refused at configuration instead, and the message names
the longest node rather than the path that was typed.
.PP
.B The socket node is created 0600.
Connecting to a unix socket needs write permission on the node. If that were
left to the umask, a daemon running under
.B umask 0
would publish a listener that anyone on the machine could talk to. There is
deliberately no flag to widen it, so a peer running as another user cannot
connect. The way around that today is a parent directory that the operator owns
and controls.
.PP
The node is removed when the runtime stops. A node left behind by a killed
daemon or a power loss is taken over on the next start, but only after the
runtime connects to it to check that nobody is serving it. A path that something
IS serving is refused, so a second runtime cannot take a live socket away from
the first.
.PP
A server listening this way is reachable as a proxy backend by its path and by
its runtime name. It is deliberately NOT offered as an
.BI @ group
member. A group's members are resolved to network addresses per request, and an
entry that the group path cannot dial would take a turn of the round\-robin and
answer 503.
.PP
.nf
socketley create server app --listen unix:/run/app.sock --http /srv/www -s
socketley create proxy  gw  -p 8080 --backend app -s
socketley create cache  kv  --listen unix:/run/kv.sock -s   # redis-cli -s works
.fi
.RE
.SS The node beside the port
A
.B server
or an unsharded
.B cache
that listens on a TCP port also listens on an AF_UNIX node. The daemon creates
the node in a directory named after its own socket,
.IR <socket> .rt/,
and names the node after the runtime. The directory is named after the socket
rather than placed beside it, because the default socket is
.IR /tmp/socketley.sock ,
and every user on the machine shares that directory. Nothing needs to be
configured, and nothing about the port changes: a runtime created with
.B \-p 9000
is reached on 9000 from anywhere, as before. The node is an addition, never a
replacement, and nothing outside this machine can reach it.
.PP
The node exists for one caller: another runtime on this machine. A proxy whose
backend is written as a
.I runtime name
uses it. A proxy whose backend is written as an
.I address
uses it too, but only while a running runtime publishes exactly that host and
port. A name asks for the runtime, and the proxy may choose how to reach it. An
address asks for the address, so the substitution holds only while the two are
the same thing. If the runtime moves to another port, the address means what it
says again.
.PP
Measured on Linux 7.2.2, with the same proxy in front of the same server and
only the backend leg differing, a request falls from 25.33 to 22.68
microseconds. The whole process tree spends 4.0 microseconds less CPU on each
one. This held in 12 of 12 interleaved passes (median of the per\-pass
differences). The CPU saving is larger than the latency saving, which is what a
saving under load looks like. A serial round trip waits on wakeups, and the
freed CPU has no use until a second request needs it.
.PP
.B The node is 0600 in a directory the daemon creates 0700.
Only the user the daemon runs as can reach it. Measured: another local user
reaches the port but is refused on the node with
.BR EACCES ,
and cannot list the directory or create a node in it either. None of this
narrows the port, which is exactly as open as it was. But the leg between two
runtimes no longer travels over something every local user could also connect
to.
.PP
The daemon checks the directory, not only creates it. A
.BR mkdir (2)
that answers
.B EEXIST
says a directory is there but not who owns it, so the daemon stats it. If it is
not a directory the daemon owns with no group or other access, companion nodes
are turned off for the whole daemon, and the log says so once. Without that
check, another user could create the directory first and hold a live node at a
path the daemon was about to publish. The bind refuses to take over a live node,
so the path would stay published and be served by that other user.
.PP
The node takes away two things, both worth knowing before relying on it:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B A firewall rule on loopback does not apply.
nftables and iptables see packets, and an AF_UNIX socket produces none. A rule
that drops traffic to a runtime's port does not stop another runtime from
reaching it over the node.
.IP \(bu 2
.B tcpdump \-i lo shows nothing.
At start, the proxy writes one line per backend to the daemon log, naming the
path it will use. That line is the only evidence that the node was used.
.RE
.PP
A runtime gets no node in these cases:
.IP \(bu 2
It is neither a server nor a cache.
.IP \(bu 2
It has no port. A runtime already on
.B \-\-listen unix:
has nothing to add.
.IP \(bu 2
It uses
.BR \-\-udp .
.IP \(bu 2
Its listener is TLS. A cleartext node beside an encrypted port would undo what
the port was configured to give.
.PP
A proxy keeps only its port, and so does a cache with
.BR \-\-shards .
A path is one node, and N processes would unlink each other's; that is why
.B \-\-listen
gives each shard a node of its own. One rule decides both what the daemon
publishes and what a runtime binds. So a type that is not wired to a listener is
never advertised as having one, because a path published with nothing behind it
is a backend that is simply down. For the same reason, a proxy configured with
backend TLS keeps TCP rather than silently dropping the encryption it was asked
for.
.SS Between runtimes on one machine
Requests from one runtime to another include
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR self.route ,
and a server's
.BI \-\-cache " name" .
The daemon carries the first such request between two runtimes, up one control
socket and down the other. It then introduces the two processes to each other
with a new socket pair, and later requests go straight across.
.PP
There is nothing to configure and nothing to see from outside. The pair has no
path in the filesystem, so nobody can connect to it or squat on it; only the two
processes that were handed the ends can use it. The daemon logs one line naming
the pair when it introduces them.
.BI "socketley stats " name
reports
.BR peer_links ,
.B peer_direct
and
.BR peer_relayed ,
which tell a request that went through the daemon from one that went straight
across.
.PP
Measured with a server posting every cache command to a cache runtime, the two
arms differing only in whether they were introduced, a round trip falls from
31.60 to 21.14 microseconds. The process tree spends 10.75 microseconds less CPU
per command. This held in 12 of 12 interleaved passes (median of the
per\-pass differences). A third of the round trip was the daemon.
.PP
A request falls back to the daemon whenever the link is not usable, so nothing
depends on the link. If a link never forms, or its peer has gone, the only cost
is the speed it would have saved. A routed connection is pinned to the path it
opened on for its whole life, because a message that changed route mid\-stream
could arrive ahead of one sent before it.
.SS Which port a runtime is on
.BR ls ,
.BR ps ,
.B stats
and the metrics endpoint report the port a runtime
.IR binds ,
which is not always the port it was configured with. A runtime created without
.B \-p
takes its type's default: 8000 for a server, 8080 for a proxy, 9000 for a
cache. The
.B PORT
column,
.B ps \-\-json
and
.B stats
show that port, not the configured 0, so an operator can see which port the
runtime is actually answering on.
.PP
.B show
is deliberately not in that list. Its output is the state document, which goes
back in through
.BR import .
Writing the default into it would pin a runtime that had been left to take
whatever default its type has.
.PP
A runtime that binds no port at all reports
.BR 0 .
That is a client, a
.BI \-\-listen " unix:"
listener, a process registered with
.BR attach ,
or a server reachable only through its owner.
.SS One address, one runtime
A runtime's start is refused when another runtime on this daemon is already
serving the address it would bind: a TCP port, a UDP port, or a
.BI \-\-listen " unix:"
path. The refusal names both runtimes and reaches the terminal. During the
restore at boot, it names both in the daemon log.
.PP
The kernel would not refuse it. Every listener is opened with
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
because that is the whole mechanism behind
.BR \-\-shards :
N worker processes of
.I one
runtime bind one port, and the kernel hands each connection to one of them. A
second
.I runtime
claiming the same port looks the same as a second shard, so the bind succeeds and
the connections split between the two at random. Measured without this check,
with two servers created on one port: both started, both listened, nothing was
logged, and twenty messages arrived split nine and eleven.
.PP
These are still accepted, because they are not the same address:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "\-\-udp beside TCP" .
UDP and TCP are separate namespaces in the kernel, so a datagram server and a
stream server on the same number are two listeners that never meet.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "A restart" .
It passes through
.B stopped
and takes back its own port, because the rule reads state, not configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "\-\-shards" .
Its workers are one runtime.
.IP \(bu 2
A runtime that binds nothing at all: a client, a process registered with
.BR attach ,
or a server reachable only through its owner.
.PP
A clustered cache holds more than one address: the configured port, and
.IR base+1 .. base+N
for the shards, which is the formula
.B CLUSTER SLOTS
publishes. A runtime that took one of those ports would be sent keys it does
not hold.
.PP
The refused runtime stays in the state it was in,
.B created
or
.BR stopped ,
not
.BR failed ,
because nothing was built and nothing broke. To resolve it, stop the runtime
named in the refusal, or give one of the two an address of its own.
.SH SERVER FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Sets the direction messages flow:
.B inout
(bidirectional, default),
.B in
(receive only),
.B out
(send only), or
.B master
(one client claims master, and only the master's messages are broadcast).
.IP
The mode alone does not decide who receives a message. In
.B inout
with no script, every message is repeated to the other connections but not to
the one that sent it. So a single client talking to itself hears nothing, and a
second client hears everything. See
.BR \-\-send\-to .
.IP
The server holds relayed messages for a connection until it knows which protocol
the connection speaks. It then delivers them in that protocol's framing, so a
WebSocket client receives them as frames rather than as raw text. A connection is
classified by its first message. Without the hold, a client whose first move is
an HTTP request or a WebSocket upgrade would receive relay bytes ahead of the
response it is waiting for, which corrupts the handshake.
.IP
A client that connects and sends nothing is served anyway, after a delay of at
most 250 ms. From then on it is treated as a plain line\-delimited TCP client.
Such a client has nothing outstanding for the relayed bytes to arrive ahead of,
so there is no handshake to protect. The delay is paid once per connection, not
once per message: everything relayed after the first delivery goes out
immediately. A client that has sent part of a request is not covered by this. It
is held until its request completes, however long that takes, because it is
waiting for an answer, and the answer must come first.
.IP
The hold is capped at 64 KB per connection. Past that, the oldest held messages
are dropped to make room. So a client that stays silent through more than 64 KB
of relay traffic in under 250 ms receives the most recent messages, not the
whole backlog. The cap applies only before classification. Once a connection is
classified, relay traffic goes straight to its write queue, and the ordinary
.B \-\-max\-connections
and write\-queue limits apply instead.
.IP
A connection that speaks HTTP is not in the relay set. It receives no relayed
messages at all, starting from the request that identifies it as HTTP. HTTP has
no framing for a message the server sends on its own. Before a response, such a
message replaces the status line the client is parsing for. Between two
keep\-alive requests, it is read as the status line of the next response. It
would never be well formed, so it is not sent at all rather than delayed. A
WebSocket connection is unaffected: it starts over HTTP, but once upgraded it has
framing for server\-initiated messages and receives everything. That is the
supported way for a browser client to receive relay traffic.
.IP
A connection that speaks RESP is excluded for the same reasons, starting from
the command that identifies it as RESP. RESP2 matches replies to commands only
by their order on the stream, so a relayed message would be read as the reply
to the client's next command. Also, the relay writes the message text, not a
RESP value, so a Redis client reads a plain letter where a type byte belongs and
answers with a protocol error. Encoding the message as a value would not help.
The only channel RESP2 has for a server\-initiated value is pub/sub, which the
client enters with SUBSCRIBE, and a server runtime does not implement it. A RESP
client on a relaying server still receives its own replies, anything a script
addresses to it by client id, and the answers to
.B "cache <command>"
messages. What it no longer receives is other clients' traffic.
.TP
.BI "\-\-send\-to " "who[,who...]"
Sets who receives a message on the built\-in path when no script handles it.
The value is a comma separated set of recipients:
.B sender
(the connection the message came from),
.B others
(every other connection except the master), and
.B master
(the master connection, which only
.B "\-\-mode master"
has).
.IP
The recipients do not overlap, so they combine:
.B "\-\-send\-to sender,others"
reaches everyone including the sender, and
.B "\-\-send\-to sender,master"
answers the sender and sends the master a copy. That is why there is no
.B all
recipient:
.B sender,others
already says it, and the flag avoids one behaviour under two spellings.
.IP
When the flag is not set, each mode keeps its usual behaviour:
.B inout
sends to
.BR others ,
and
.B master
drops what a non\-master says.
.IP
In
.BR "\-\-mode master" ,
the flag decides what happens to a NON\-master's message. The master's own
message still reaches everyone else, which is what the mode means.
.B "\-\-send\-to master"
forwards the message with a prefix: the sender's descriptor stays in front of
the line, so the master can tell two clients apart.
.B "\-\-send\-to others,master"
makes a moderated room that the master reads along with.
.IP
Invalid recipients are refused rather than accepted and ignored. Modes
.B in
and
.B out
answer nobody at all, so they refuse the flag. The
.B master
recipient names a connection that no other mode has, so only master mode
accepts it.
.IP
In
.BR inout ,
and for the master's own messages in
.BR master ,
a script's
.B on_message
takes precedence over every recipient. When it is defined, nothing is sent
automatically, and
.B self.send
or
.B self.broadcast
decides. A non\-master's message in
.B master
mode never reaches
.B on_message
at all, only
.BR on_client_message ,
which does not suppress anything. So there the recipients apply whether or not
a script is loaded.
.IP
Unset, UDP and TCP both send to
.BR others .
Before this flag existed, a UDP sender also received its own datagram back,
while a TCP sender did not.
.B "\-\-send\-to sender,others"
gives that old UDP behaviour.
.TP
.B \-\-udp
Uses UDP instead of TCP. The server binds a single SOCK_DGRAM socket. A peer is
registered automatically on its first datagram. Each datagram is one message,
with no line parsing. Broadcast sends to all known peers with sendto().
Not available for cache or proxy.
.IP
.B "An exposed UDP server can reflect traffic."
With no Lua handler, every datagram is relayed to every other peer that has
sent one. A peer is learned from a source address, which a sender can forge.
Keep such a server on a trusted network, or give it a Lua handler that decides.
.IP
Refused with
.BR "\-\-mode master" .
Master mode claims a connection as the master, and a datagram is not a
connection, so the pair is worse than useless. Without the refusal, a datagram
beginning with
.B "master "
would reach that code with no sender and take the worker down.
.TP
.BI "\-\-master\-pw " "password"
A static password for master mode authentication. A client sends "master <password>"
to claim the master role. Only one master exists at a time, and the role is
released on disconnect. For dynamic authentication, use the
.B on_master_auth(client_id, password)
Lua callback instead.
.IP
Failed attempts are counted per source address. Once an address has failed ten
attempts within sixty seconds, its connections are refused at accept, before a
byte is read. The count decays after sixty seconds of quiet. This is a property
of master mode, not of this flag, so it applies to whichever of the two
authenticators is in use.
.IP
Requires
.BR "\-\-mode master" ,
and is refused without it. The
.B "master "
prefix is read in only one place. In any other mode the password is not a
credential but an ordinary message, and a client that sends it has its password
relayed verbatim to every other connection.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-master\-pw
Removes the password from a server that has one. Only
.B edit
accepts it. This is what makes leaving master mode possible:
.B \-\-master\-pw
and
.B "\-\-mode master"
are checked as a pair, so a mode change alone is refused while the password
remains. It needs a flag of its own because an empty string passed to
.B \-\-master\-pw
cannot express it. The daemon receives one line and splits it on whitespace, so
an empty argument does not survive the wire. It arrives as the flag with nothing
after it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-origins " list
Lists the web origins, besides the server's own, whose pages may open a
WebSocket to this server.
The list is comma\-separated, such as
.BR https://app.example.com .
.B *
admits any origin.
.IP
The server refuses a handshake whose
.B Origin
is not in the list and is not the
.B Host
the handshake was sent to (or the
.B X\-Forwarded\-Host
set by a proxy in front).
It answers
.B 403 Forbidden
and closes the connection.
This stops a page on another site from driving the server, or the cache linked
to it, through the browser of someone who can reach them.
A client that sends no
.B Origin
is not affected, and only browsers send one.
.TP
.BI "\-\-http " "directory"
Serve static files from the given directory over HTTP, for pages that talk to
this server.
A site that does not is a
.B web
runtime's; see
.BR "WEB FLAGS" .
When a browser sends a plain HTTP GET request (one without a WebSocket upgrade
header), the server answers with the requested file.
GET / serves index.html.
The server injects a WebSocket connection script before </head> in every HTML
file (.html, .htm).
.IP
.B "Note that this modifies the HTML it serves."
Every .html and .htm response is rewritten as it is sent.
Documents this server did not author, such as uploads, a generated report or a
third\-party page, are therefore delivered altered: they carry a script that
opens a WebSocket back to this runtime.
.B \-\-no\-inject
turns this off.
.IP
Content-Type is detected from the file extension.
Every file response carries an
.B ETag
built from the file's inode, modification time and size.
A request whose
.B If\-None\-Match
names that ETag is answered
.B 304
with no body.
The server sends no
.BR Last\-Modified ,
so a browser asks again on every load instead of trusting a copy it guessed was
fresh.
A changed or replaced file is therefore always sent anew.
WebSocket upgrades still work on the same port.
Only GET requests are served; other HTTP methods fall through to raw TCP
handling.
.IP
.B "Range requests."
Every whole\(hyfile response carries
.BR "Accept-Ranges: bytes" .
A request with a
.B Range
field is answered
.BR "206 Partial Content" .
Its
.B Content-Range
states the file's whole length, and its
.B Content-Length
states the length of the slice.
This lets a media player seek and an interrupted download resume.
Without it, a player scrubs by downloading from the beginning, and Safari does
not play
.B <video>
at all.
The server accepts all three forms:
.IP
.nf
Range: bytes=0\-499      the first 500 bytes
Range: bytes=500\-       from 500 to the end
Range: bytes=\-500       the last 500 bytes
.fi
.IP
An end past the file is clamped to the last byte.
A range that starts at or past the end is answered
.B "416 Range Not Satisfiable"
with
.BR "Content-Range: bytes */<length>" .
This is how a client that was not told the length learns it.
.IP
Two kinds of request are deliberately answered with the whole file instead.
A multi\(hyrange request
.RB ( "bytes=0\-9,20\-29" )
would need a
.B multipart/byteranges
body, which the server does not produce.
RFC 9110 14.2 permits ignoring the field, and the whole file is more useful
than a wrong slice.
A range on
.B .html
or
.B .htm
is also ignored, because the injected script described above changes the
body's length.
A byte offset into the file is therefore not an offset into the response.
Those responses do not send
.B Accept-Ranges
either, so no client is invited to make a seek the server cannot honour.
With
.BR \-\-no\-inject ,
a page is served like any other file, ranges included.
.IP
.B "Concurrency of the zero-copy path."
The server sends a file with
.BR sendfile (2)
from the event loop.
The socket is made non\-blocking for that call only, so no pipe is held and
nothing limits how many files are sent at once.
Compared with the io_uring splice it replaced, a 2 MiB file costs 85 us of CPU
instead of 158, and 64 slow readers hold no kernel threads instead of 64.
When a window of the file is not in the page cache, the server splices that
window through a pipe instead, so the event loop never waits on the disk.
The rest of this section is about that pipe.
.IP
In the table below, the before column is the earlier design.
It sent every file with
.BR splice (2)
through a pipe, which keeps the bytes out of user space entirely.
It had one pipe per runtime, so only one connection at a time could use the
splice path.
Every other connection fell back to a read into memory followed by a write.
Measured on 4 MiB files, seven interleaved passes of each build:
.IP
.nf
concurrent   before      after     factor
1            2.9 ms      2.9 ms     0.98
2           11.9 ms      4.2 ms     2.80
4           26.1 ms      5.3 ms     4.97
8           55.8 ms     10.5 ms     5.33
.fi
.IP
Each connection now makes its own pipe.
The number of pipes is limited by a budget in bytes, not by a count: 64 MB of
kernel pipe, divided by the pipe size.
The pipe size follows
.BR \-\-write\-buffer ,
capped at 1 MB.
So the default allows 64 pipes.
A runtime configured with a small buffer because it serves many streams gets
MORE pipes out of the same budget, not fewer.
Past that count, the remaining connections use the read path.
.IP
A file takes this path from 64 KiB upward on a plaintext connection.
Only a window that is not in the page cache needs a pipe, so a server of warm
images never reaches this budget.
A server that does reach it falls back to the read path.
.IP
This boundary is worth knowing, because the fallback is not merely slower.
Worker throughput and RSS on 4 MiB files:
.IP
.nf
                     at/below the count      past it
default (64)         12.7-14.2 GB/s          1.20 GB/s
                     RSS 35 MB               RSS 234 MB
\-\-write\-buffer 512K  12.3-14.0 GB/s at 128 concurrent, RSS 36 MB
.fi
.IP
The read path allocates the WHOLE file for each request, so its memory grows
with concurrency times file size.
The pipes do not grow that way, because the kernel fills a pipe's buffer only as
it is used.
That is also why the count is 64 and not 8.
The pipes cost less than the fallback they avoid, and a count of 8 put the
boundary at about ten concurrent requests.
.IP
.B "On a TLS listener there is no zero-copy path at all."
Know this limit before serving large files over TLS.
Splicing would hand the peer plaintext in the middle of an encrypted stream, so
every request takes the read path, whatever the pipe budget allows.
Measured worker RSS on a 256 MB file:
.IP
.nf
plaintext, one request        +2 MB          (splice)
TLS, one request            505 MB          about twice the file
TLS, three concurrent     1,250 MB
.fi
.IP
The memory is twice the file because the file is read into memory and the
response is then assembled around it.
The same doubling was recorded for a 400 MiB .html, where it came to 1219 MiB.
The memory is held for the whole request, and the client decides how many such
requests there are.
Ten concurrent 256 MB requests would take about 5 GB.
.IP
.B \-\-http\-cache
limits the multiplication but not the size.
It holds each file once and shares it, so concurrency stops mattering.
The cost is that it holds the file permanently from startup.
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
each shard reads the directory and holds its own copy, so the files are held
once per shard.
A 64 MB file was measured at 154 MB, which is the file plus the two prebuilt
responses.
Otherwise, keep the files a TLS listener serves small, or put a plaintext
listener behind something else that terminates TLS.
.IP
The fix would be a read path that works in bounded pieces, as the request side
does with
.BR on_http_body ,
instead of one allocation the size of whatever was requested.
That change touches the most intricate function in this path and has not been
made.
.IP
A slice is never stored in the revalidation map and never answered from it.
The map is keyed by path and holds the whole file's response.
A stored 206 would answer every later request for that path with one reader's
slice and that reader's
.BR Content-Range .
.IP
The client's
.B Connection
request header decides whether the connection is reused.
The HTTP version supplies only the default: keep\-alive for 1.1, close for 1.0.
A request carrying
.B Connection: close
is answered with
.BR "Connection: close" ,
and the server then shuts down its own write side first.
The 60\-second TIME\-WAIT that follows is therefore held on the server's fixed
listening port, not on one of the client's ephemeral ports.
The header is parsed token by token and case\-insensitively, so
.B "Connection: close, TE"
counts as close.
.IP
Without
.BR \-\-http\-cache ,
this is the development mode: the next request picks up a file edited on disk.
The server does this without re\-reading the file every time.
It keeps the response in memory, and one
.BR statx (2)
per request decides whether that response still matches the file.
The check compares the device, the inode number, the size and the modification
time together.
The inode number is included because of how files are usually replaced.
.BR mv ,
.BR cp ,
.B "git checkout"
and most editors write a new file and rename it over the old one.
That can leave the size unchanged and can restore the old timestamp exactly.
.IP
The memory this uses is bounded, unlike
.BR \-\-http\-cache 's.
The reason is that it fills from whatever is requested, not from a directory
whose size the operator can see before starting.
Once the kept responses reach 64 MiB in total, no further file is added.
The cap per file depends on whether the requesting connection has a faster
path available at all:
.IP
.nf
plaintext, not .html    64 KiB   anything larger goes by sendfile instead
TLS, or an .html         1 MiB   neither has a sendfile arm
.fi
.IP
That split comes from measurement, not from tidiness.
A kept response is sent by copying it into the socket.
sendfile sends the page cache by reference and copies nothing, but it costs a
system call and a descriptor of its own.
Measured on one core, interleaved, in us of CPU per request, kept response
against sendfile:
4.7 against 7.9 at 8 KiB, 10.4 against 11.4 at 64 KiB, 15.6 against 14.3 at
128 KiB, and 27.8 against 23.9 at 256 KiB.
So above 64 KiB, keeping a file makes serving it more expensive, and it also
costs the map twice the file's size.
.IP
A TLS connection cannot use sendfile at all, because that would hand the peer
plaintext in the middle of an encrypted stream.
An .html file has a script injected into it.
For those two cases the larger cap applies.
There the map is what keeps a file from being read, and allocated, once per
request.
.IP
Outside those two cases, a file that is not kept is not read either: it goes
from the page cache to the socket without passing through this process.
To switch the whole mechanism off, see
.BR SOCKETLEY_HTTP_REVALIDATE .
Switching it off also puts every file size back on the copying path.
.TP
.B \-\-http\-cache
With \-\-http, read all files into memory at startup instead of checking the
filesystem on each request.
This is faster for production; the difference from plain
.B \-\-http
is one
.BR statx (2)
per request.
The cache is rebuilt on
.B reload\-lua
and at no other time.
A file edited on disk is therefore
.B not
picked up.
That is both the reason to use this flag and the reason not to.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-inject
With \-\-http, serve a page as the file holds it, without the script that
opens a WebSocket here: a site that does not use the global
.B socketley
does not open a connection per visitor it never reads. A page is then a file
like any other, so it is answered for a
.BR Range ,
sent zero\-copy, and given its
.B \-\-compress
copy. WebSocket upgrades on the port are unchanged; a page that wants one opens
it itself.
.B \-\-inject
puts the script back, which is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-compress
With \-\-http, send a file's precompressed copy, made by the site's build, to
clients that accept it.
For example,
.B app.css.br
or
.B app.css.gz
is sent for
.B app.css
to a client whose
.B Accept\-Encoding
accepts that coding.
br is preferred over gzip.
The response carries
.B Content\-Encoding
and the copy's own ETag.
With this flag, every file response also carries
.BR "Vary: Accept\-Encoding" .
.IP
A copy is never sent when it is older than its file, or for a range request.
Nor is one sent for a page, because the copy lacks the injected script, unless
.B \-\-no\-inject
is set.
Nor is one sent for an image, a video, a woff font, a zip or a pdf.
A copy requested by its own name is sent as itself.
With
.BR \-\-http\-cache ,
the copies are loaded at startup along with the files and compared with them at
that time.
In the same measurement as the proxy's, a page's 3.8 MB of scripts loaded over
10 Mbit and 50 ms in 0.97 s instead of 3.51 s.
The server itself never compresses or decompresses anything.
.IP
Each file is held twice: once with a
.B Connection: keep\-alive
response header and once with
.BR "Connection: close" .
A cache hit therefore stays a reference\-count increment and still answers
with the header the client asked for.
.TP
.BI "\-u " "host:port"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-upstream " "host:port"
Add an outbound connection to an upstream.
The server connects to the given host:port on startup.
If the connection drops, the server reconnects automatically with exponential
backoff (1s\(en16s).
To add several upstreams, give \-u several times, or give several addresses
separated by semicolons (e.g. \-u "feed:9000;cache:6379").
Each upstream gets a stable 1-based conn_id for use in Lua callbacks.
.PP
.RS
Upstream Lua callbacks:
.TP
.B on_upstream(conn_id, data)
Called for each newline-delimited message received from an upstream.
.TP
.B on_upstream_connect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream connects (including reconnects).
.TP
.B on_upstream_disconnect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream disconnects.
.RE
.RS
Upstream Lua actions:
.TP
.B self.upstream_send(conn_id, msg)
Send a message to a specific upstream.
.TP
.B self.upstream_broadcast(msg)
Send a message to all connected upstreams.
.TP
.B self.upstreams()
Returns a table of currently connected upstream conn_ids.
.RE
.TP
.BI "\-\-cache " "name"
Link this server to an existing cache runtime.
Clients can then send "cache <command>" messages, such as "cache set foo bar"
or "cache get foo".
The server runs them against the linked cache and returns the response to the
sender only.
The server's mode still handles normal messages.
.IP
.B "The link also archives: every ordinary message is written to that cache."
Every message that is not a
.B cache
command is stored under an auto\-incrementing integer key
.RB ( 1 ,
.BR 2 ,
.B 3
and so on).
The server's mode also handles it as usual.
For example, if two clients send
.B hello
and
.B world
to a linked server,
.B "get 1"
then answers
.B hello
and
.B "get 2"
answers
.BR world .
The counter is per server and starts again when the server restarts.
A
.B "\-\-persistent"
cache therefore keeps growing across restarts, while keys from
.B 1
upward are overwritten.
Link a cache to a server only when you want that archive.
A server that only wants its clients to reach a cache pays for one write per
message.
.IP
The server does not execute the command on the connection that asked for it.
It hands the command to the daemon and answers when the reply comes back, one
turn of the event loop later.
A
.B cache
command sent this way therefore costs a round trip that the same command sent
to the cache's own port does not.
See
.B Cache Access via Server
below for what this guarantees and what it costs.
.SS Sharding
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " "N"
Serve this server from
.I N
worker processes instead of one, sharing the listening socket through
.BR SO_REUSEPORT .
The kernel gives each incoming connection to one of them.
.I N
is 1 to 64; the default is 1.
.PP
A runtime is one process, and therefore one event loop on one core.
This flag is the only way to give a single server more than that.
.PP
.B "With or without \-\-lua."
A server may be sharded whether or not it runs a script.
The three things a script can ask about its clients cover the whole runtime,
across shard boundaries:
.IP \(bu 2
.B self.connections()
sums the figure each shard publishes in a shared mapping, with no round trip.
.IP \(bu 2
.B self.broadcast
and
.B socketley.send
addressed to this runtime reach the clients of every shard, once each.
.IP \(bu 2
.B self.clients()
lists the whole runtime: this shard's descriptors, plus a stable handle for
each client that a sibling shard holds.
.B self.send
on one of those handles reaches the shard that owns the client.
.PP
.B "Per-connection state is per shard, and that is what sharding means."
A connection belongs to exactly one shard, so every callback about it runs on
that shard and nowhere else.
For a client that a sibling accepted,
.B on_connect
fired in that sibling, and
.B on_message
will fire there too.
A script therefore holds no state for the ids that
.B self.clients()
returns from another shard.
It can name them, count them and send to them, but it never saw them arrive.
Design the script for that.
Keep cross-connection state in a
.B cache
runtime, which every shard can reach.
Do not keep it in a Lua table at file scope, because each shard has its own
copy of that table.
.PP
.B "self.route works from a shard."
Routing hands a live client to another runtime.
The target runtime answers the client, while this shard keeps the socket and
relays the bytes.
The target is reached across the process boundary, as from any worker, so
.B \-\-shards
changes nothing about routing.
A routed client belongs to the shard that accepted it, like every other
connection.
.BR unroute ,
.B get_route
and
.B owner_send
read this shard's own tables.
.PP
.B "What it bought, measured."
One server runtime in
.B \-\-http
mode with
.BR \-\-http\-cache ,
driven by
.B "socketley_bench proxy http-keepalive"
over 64 keep-alive connections.
Seven interleaved passes; the figures are the median of the per-pass ratios.
The cores are those of the SERVER processes only (daemon plus workers, from
.IR /proc/<pid>/stat ),
not the whole machine's.
A machine-wide figure would include the benchmark client, which at this
concurrency is the larger half of it.
.PP
.RS
.TS
l r r r.
shards	throughput	cores	per core
_
1	126,497 req/s	0.99	127,775 req/s
8	451,097 req/s	4.78	94,372 req/s
.TE
.RE
.PP
Eight shards give 3.57x the throughput of one, with the per-pass ratios spread
from 3.18x to 4.26x.
.PP
.B "That figure is a server's, and does not transfer to a cache."
A server's shards are interchangeable, so any shard can answer any request.
A cache's shards are not.
The shard a connection lands on relays every key it does not own, and behind
one address that is
.RI ( N \-1)/ N
of the keys.
At four shards a cache measured 1.75x, against a server's 3.57x.
At two shards it measured 1.05x for 1.91 cores.
See
.B CACHE FLAGS
for the table.
.PP
.B "One shard is exactly one core."
One shard used 0.99 of a core at every concurrency tried, while the machine had
fifteen more cores sitting idle.
That measurement is the whole case for this flag.
A server does not run out of machine; it runs out of the single event loop it
is allowed.
.PP
.B "Per-core efficiency falls by about a quarter, and that is the price."
Throughput per core was 127,775 req/s at one shard against 94,372 at eight.
Sharding buys throughput with cores, not with efficiency.
If the machine has cores that are otherwise idle, that is a good trade.
If it does not, this flag makes things worse rather than better.
Do not set more shards than the machine has cores to give them.
.PP
.B "Measure with enough connections to feed the shards, or the flag looks worse than it is."
The same eight shards driven by only
.I 16
connections reached 294,917 req/s and 2.54x.
They were not slower; they were starved.
That run used 4.12 cores, where the 64-connection run used 4.78.
.B SO_REUSEPORT
hands out whole connections, so sixteen connections over eight shards is two
each with an uneven tail, and the slowest shard limits the pass.
A shard count above the client's concurrency measures the client.
.PP
.B "The load is balanced per connection, not per request."
.B SO_REUSEPORT
picks a shard when it accepts a connection and never changes that choice.
One heavy long-lived connection therefore makes one shard hot while the others
idle.
Over a small number of connections the spread is uneven: eight connections over
four shards were measured landing 3/2/2/1.
Sharding helps a server carrying many comparable connections; it does nothing
for a server carrying one big one.
.PP
.B "The surface still answers about the runtime, not the shard."
.IP \(bu 2
.B ls
shows one entry.
.IP \(bu 2
.B stats
reports the connection count summed across the shards, and labels each shard's
detail text.
.IP \(bu 2
.B stop
and
.B remove
take all the shards down.
.IP \(bu 2
The count is persisted, so a daemon restart brings back the number that was
asked for.
.PP
The count can be changed with
.B edit
only while the runtime is stopped, for the same reason as
.BR \-p .
.PP
.B "\-\-shards gives each shard a node of its own."
On a unix socket the path is not shared, because
.B SO_REUSEPORT
does not work that way there.
.I N
processes binding one path would unlink each other's node, and only the last
would be reachable.
A base of
.I /run/app.sock
therefore becomes
.IR /run/app#0.sock ,
.IR /run/app#1.sock ,
and so on, one per shard, and a caller can pick any of them.
A base that does not end in
.B .sock
gets the index appended instead.
.PP
The combination is refused only when the longest of those names would not fit.
The kernel's limit applies to the node, and a shard whose name is truncated is a
shard nothing can reach.
The refusal names that longest member.
.PP
See
.B Sharding
under
.B PROXY FLAGS
for the fuller treatment of the flag.
.SS Multi-Protocol Auto-Detection
Server connections detect the protocol automatically from the first bytes
received.
No flags are needed; all four protocols coexist on the same port:
.TP
.B TCP (newline-delimited text)
The default.
Lines terminated by
.B \\en
are delivered as messages.
.TP
.B HTTP
Detected when the first bytes are a valid HTTP method (GET, POST, etc.).
The
.B on_http_request
Lua callback handles requests if it is defined.
Otherwise the static file server handles them if
.B \-\-http
is set, and the server responds with 404 if it is not.
HTTP/1.1 keep-alive is supported.
An explicit
.B Connection
request header overrides the version's default; see
.B \-\-http
for what the server does with it.
.TP
.B WebSocket
Detected when an HTTP request contains
.BR "Upgrade: websocket" .
The server completes the RFC 6455 handshake and frames and deframes messages
transparently.
The
.B on_websocket
callback fires after the upgrade.
A browser page from another origin is refused with 403 unless
.B \-\-origins
lists it.
.IP
A text message that is not valid UTF\-8 fails its connection with close code
1007 and never reaches a script.
A fragmented message is checked once it is complete.
A message the server relays without a script (to other clients, back to its
sender, or to the master) goes out as the same kind of frame it arrived in,
text or binary.
A message a script sends goes out as text when it is valid UTF\-8 and as binary
otherwise.
A line relayed from a TCP client is sent the same way.
.TP
.B RESP (Redis protocol)
Detected when the first byte is
.BR * .
Useful for cache-linked servers or custom RESP-based protocols.
Like an HTTP connection, and for the same reason, a RESP connection is not in
the relay set.
The server answers it, but does not send it other clients' messages.
See
.B \-\-mode
under
.BR "SERVER FLAGS" .
.PP
.B "How much it waits for before deciding."
The tests above read four bytes.
Every HTTP method is matched on four characters, and the RESP markers on two.
So a connection that has sent fewer than four bytes is normally left to send
more.
.PP
A completed line is the exception, and it has to be.
A client that writes
.B "hi\\en"
and waits for an answer will not send a fourth byte until it gets one.
Waiting for that byte would mean waiting for something that only arrives after
the answer.
A line that ends within four bytes cannot be an HTTP method, because each method
is four characters and none contains a newline.
So the server decides the protocol there and then: RESP if the first two bytes
say so, and TCP otherwise.
Short lines are ordinary input on a line protocol and are delivered like any
other.
.PP
This applies to the
.I first
line only.
Once a connection's protocol is known, lines of any length are delivered as
they arrive.
.PP
Browser example, from a page the server serves itself or one that
.B \-\-origins
lists:
.nf
const ws = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:9000");
ws.onmessage = e => console.log(e.data);
ws.send("hello");
.fi
.SS Request Framing
The server frames an HTTP request by exactly the same rules as the proxy,
because both use the same code.
If a request's header block gives no answer, or two answers, to "how long is
the body", the server answers
.B 400 Bad Request
and closes the connection.
That happens when the header block contains any of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a bare LF or a bare CR \(em every line must end with CRLF
.IP \(bu 2
whitespace between a field name and its colon
.RB ( "Content\-Length : 6" )
.IP \(bu 2
a continuation (obs\-fold) line, i.e. one starting with a space or tab
.IP \(bu 2
a field line with no colon, or with an empty field name
.IP \(bu 2
more than one
.B Content\-Length
field, or a
.B Content\-Length
together with a
.B Transfer\-Encoding
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Content\-Length
that is not a whole decimal number
.RB ( "Content\-Length: 6x" )
.RE
.PP
A tab is legal optional whitespace around a field value.
A
.B Content\-Length
value separated from the colon by a tab instead of a space is an ordinary
request and is served, as is a value with trailing whitespace.
Values reach
.B on_http_request
with that whitespace removed from both ends.
.PP
How the server handles
.B Transfer\-Encoding
depends on the coding and on whether the script reads bodies:
.IP \(bu 2
.B chunked
alone, with a script that defines
.BR on_http_body :
the server decodes the body and delivers it in pieces like any other.
There is no length to bound it by, which is what chunked is for.
.IP \(bu 2
.B chunked
alone, without that callback:
.BR "501 Not Implemented" .
Nothing is going to read the body incrementally.
A body whose end the server cannot find is refused rather than treated as
though it had none.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "gzip, chunked" :
.B 501
even with the callback.
Chunked is the final coding, so the chunk sizes do delimit the body, but the
bytes inside are still compressed.
Removing the framing and handing those bytes on would pass gzip data off as the
body.
.IP \(bu 2
A coding that is not chunked at all, such as
.B gzip
by itself:
.BR "400 Bad Request" ,
because nothing can say where the body ends.
.PP
So a chunked request sent through a socketley proxy to a socketley server is
served when the server's script takes bodies, and answered 501 when it does
not.
Either way, the proxy forwards the framing verbatim and relays whatever comes
back.
.B Content\-Length
remains the simpler form, and a client that knows its body's length should send
it.
.PP
A server is the last recipient, so it may seem to have nobody to disagree with.
Usually it does.
As soon as anything sits in front of it, whether this proxy or any other, two
parsers read one byte stream.
A header that a strict parser ignores and a lenient one honours moves the
boundary between one request and the next.
The server serves pipelined requests without waiting for another read.
So a body it wrongly measured as empty is read as the next request: one message
goes in, two are answered, and the client chooses what the second one says.
Compliant clients send none of these shapes.
.PP
A request whose header block is still arriving is held until the terminating
blank line.
The server applies a 16 KiB header limit
.RB ( "431 Request Header Fields Too Large" )
and a 10 second header deadline
.RB ( "408 Request Timeout" ).
The server answers a missed deadline with that status instead of closing
silently, as the proxy does in the same case.
A client that gets a bare close cannot tell a deadline from a crash, a firewall
or the wrong port.
.PP
The deadline is the same one the proxy applies, for the reason given there.
It is fixed, not configurable, and deliberately independent of
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout .
That timeout defaults to 0, so it cannot end a half\-sent request.
A client that sends a request line and then stops sends no further bytes for
the 16 KiB limit to count.
The deadline starts at the first read that left the header block incomplete,
and later bytes do not renew it.
It applies neither to an idle keep\-alive connection between requests nor to a
slow request body.
.PP
The server checks the deadline on a sweep that runs every 30 seconds.
A connection is therefore closed between 10 and 40 seconds after it stops
sending.
A proxy has a timer chain of its own and closes between 10 and 20 seconds.
The deadline itself is the same in both.
.SS Cache Access via Server
When a server is linked to a cache with
.BR \-\-cache ,
clients can send "cache <command>" messages to run commands directly against
the linked cache.
The response goes back to the sender only and is never broadcast.
The server's mode still handles normal messages.
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache store -p 9001 -s
socketley create server api -p 9000 --cache store -s
# Client sends: "cache set foo bar" \(-> receives "ok"
# Client sends: "cache get foo" \(-> receives "bar"
# Client sends: "hello"            \(-> handled by the server's mode, AND
#                                     stored in `store` under the key "1"
.fi
.PP
The last line is the part that surprises people.
The server archives an ordinary message in the linked cache under an
auto\-incrementing integer key, as well as handling it normally; see
.B \-\-cache
under
.BR "SERVER FLAGS" .
.PP
The request is asynchronous.
The server does not run the cache's command interpreter itself.
It posts the command and answers the client when the reply arrives, on the next
turn of the daemon's event loop.
That costs a round trip, on the order of tens of microseconds on loopback.
In return, the cache can later become a different process without any change on
the client's side.
Nothing else waits on the reply: the server keeps serving every other
connection while it is outstanding.
.PP
What holds:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Replies on one connection come back in request order, so a client may
pipeline
.B cache
commands and match answers to questions by position.
.IP \(bu 2
A command addressed to a cache that is not running is answered with an
.B error:
line, not with silence.
.IP \(bu 2
A client that shuts down its write side after asking still receives its
answer: the server holds the connection open until it has written every reply
the client is owed.
.RE
.PP
What does not hold: nothing is promised about the order of a
.B cache
reply relative to other traffic on the same connection.
A broadcast goes to every client except the sender, and a cache reply goes to
the sender alone, so there is no interleaving to define.
.PP
A connection may have at most 4096 unanswered
.B cache
commands outstanding, and the daemon at most 32768 across all connections.
A client that pipelines past its share is disconnected rather than sent a
refusal.
A refusal written at that point would arrive ahead of the answers already
queued in front of it.
A client that cannot tell which answer belongs to which question has no
protocol left.
.PP
Default port: 8000
.SH CLIENT FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-t " "host:port | unix:/path/to.sock"
The server the client connects to.
Default: 127.0.0.1:8000.
.IP
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
dials the socket node that a runtime with
.B \-\-listen unix:
serves.
It is the connect side of the same address and follows the same rules: the
path must be absolute and fit within the kernel's
.I sun_path
limit.
It is refused together with
.B \-\-udp
and with
.BR \-\-tls ,
for the reasons given for the listener under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
The direction in which messages flow:
.B inout
(both ways, the default),
.B in
(receive only) or
.B out
(send only).
.TP
.B \-\-udp
Use UDP instead of TCP.
The client creates a connected SOCK_DGRAM socket.
Each datagram is one message; there is no line parsing.
Not available for cache or proxy.
.TP
.BI "\-\-reconnect " "[max\-attempts]"
Reconnect automatically, with exponential backoff, when the connection is lost.
The backoff starts at 1 second and doubles with each attempt, up to a ceiling of
16 seconds, with up to half a second of jitter.
A client whose target is gone therefore dials again after 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 16,
16 seconds.
.I max\-attempts
limits the number of attempts.
0 means no limit, and is the default when the flag is given without a value.
A negative count is refused, because \-1 is the stored value for "off".
Reconnection is off unless this flag is given.
.IP
This is a client flag.
It is refused on a server, a proxy or a cache: it re-dials a connection the
runtime made, and those three accept connections rather than make them.
.IP
"When the connection is lost" includes a peer that never says so.
A close sends a FIN or an RST, and the client acts on it at once.
But a machine that loses power, a link that partitions, or a firewall that
forgets an idle flow sends nothing at all.
An idle client that never writes would never find out.
Every client socket therefore uses TCP keepalive: 60 seconds idle, then three
probes ten seconds apart.
A peer that has silently gone is noticed in about 90 seconds, and the backoff
above starts from there.
No flag changes this.
The kernel's own default is two hours, which for this purpose is the same as
never.
.PP
A client also takes the TLS flags; see
.B \-\-tls
under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.B \-\-tls
alone dials the target over TLS and verifies it against the system trust
store.
.BI \-\-ca " file"
supplies a trust store instead.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
with
.BI \-\-key " file"
presents a certificate to a peer that asks for one.
The host in
.B \-t
is the name the certificate must be valid for.
A verification failure closes the socket, and the client re-dials on the
backoff above.
The client never retries in cleartext, and there is no way to skip the check.
.B \-\-tls
with
.B \-\-udp
is refused, because that would need DTLS.
.SH PROXY FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-listen unix:" "/path/to.sock"
Serve the proxy's front door on an AF_UNIX socket instead of a
.B \-p
port.
See
.B \-\-listen
under
.B "COMMON FLAGS"
for the full description.
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
each shard binds a node of its own,
.IR base#N.sock ,
because SO_REUSEPORT cannot share a path.
.TP
.BI "\-\-backend " "addr"
Add a backend server for the proxy to forward requests to.
The address can be ip:port,
.BI unix: /path/to.sock ,
a runtime name, or
.BI @ groupname
to target all running members of that group dynamically.
The flag is repeatable, or takes comma-separated addresses.
Group backends are resolved at connection time, so the proxy picks up new group
members automatically, without a restart.
.IP
.B A hostname is resolved off the request path.
The proxy looks up a backend named by hostname when it starts.
If that lookup fails, the proxy tries again on each periodic sweep, on a thread
of its own, and never while a request is being served.
A request that arrives before the name resolves is refused rather than kept
waiting.
That is deliberate.
The daemon runs all the runtimes it holds on one thread.
A resolver that hangs on the request path would stop not only this proxy's
clients but every server, cache and client in the same daemon.
The cost is that a name which starts resolving is picked up within one sweep
instead of immediately.
.IP
.B IPv4 only.
Every address this daemon resolves is AF_INET: a backend, an upstream, a client
target and every listener.
A hostname that has only an AAAA record resolves to nothing, and the backend is
reported unreachable, with no log line anywhere saying why.
This is a property of the whole daemon, not of this flag.
It is described here because this is where an operator meets it first.
The one address that is not an internet address at all is
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
below, which needs no resolving.
.IP
An address may carry a scheme.
.BI https:// host : port
is reached over TLS, and
.BI http:// host : port
in cleartext.
A bare
.IB host : port
follows the backend TLS options described below.
After a scheme the port may be omitted; it is then 443 or 80.
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
for what verifies such a connection.
.IP
.BI unix: /path/to.sock
reaches a backend over an AF_UNIX socket instead of loopback TCP.
A sidecar proxy and the process it fronts are on one host.
Loopback TCP makes that pair pay for a whole network stack (sequence numbers,
checksums, windows) on a connection that never touches a wire.
Measured on a quiet machine with 64\-byte round trips through this proxy:
.IP
One unpinned connection took 41.4 us over TCP against 37.0 us over AF_UNIX.
Eight unpinned connections made 66,620 round trips a second over TCP against
85,217 over AF_UNIX.
The gain is 16 to 28%, depending on where the scheduler puts the three
processes.
.IP
Plan with the throughput figure, for the following reason.
What the swap removes is CPU: 17.5 us per round trip across proxy and backend
over TCP, against 14.5 us over AF_UNIX.
That matches, to the tenth, what a two\-process ping\-pong predicts for that
leg.
On one core with a single connection, almost none of it shows up as latency
(21.5 us against 20.7 us).
A serial round trip there waits on wakeups rather than on work, so the freed
CPU has nothing to do.
A proxy under load is short of exactly that CPU.
That is why the same change is worth under a microsecond in the first
measurement and a fifth of the throughput in the second.
.IP
The path must be absolute and shorter than 108 bytes, which is what the
kernel's
.I sun_path
holds.
Both rules are checked at
.BR create / start ,
not at traffic time.
The kernel truncates a long path silently, and a trimmed path names a
different socket, quite possibly a real one belonging to something else.
A relative path is refused because it would be resolved against the daemon's
working directory, which the operator writing the flag cannot see.
.IP
A unix backend is never reached over TLS.
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
encrypt every backend connection at once.
A proxy given both those flags and a
.B unix:
backend is therefore refused at start, rather than quietly connecting in the
clear.
Filesystem permissions on the socket node are what restrict access to it.
.IP
If the socket path does not exist, or a process that has exited left a stale
socket node behind, the client gets
.BR 502 .
That is the same answer an unreachable TCP backend gives, for the same reason:
the far end failed, not this one.
.IP
.BI fastcgi://unix: /path/to.sock
or
.BI fastcgi:// host : port
names an application server that the proxy speaks FastCGI to instead of HTTP.
Examples are php\-fpm, or anything else that takes the responder role.
Such a proxy needs
.BR \-\-root ,
speaks
.B \-\-protocol http
to its clients, and has no backend of any other kind.
.B create
refuses a proxy that breaks any of these rules.
Deadlines, retries, the circuit breaker and the TLS listener work on it as on
any backend.
.IP
The proxy does not keep its connection to the application server open between
requests.
A php\-fpm worker serves one connection for as long as that connection lasts.
A kept connection would therefore hold that worker away from every other
request.
With more kept connections than workers, the other requests would wait in the
backlog until their deadline.
.IP
The proxy sends a request to the first of these that matches.
First, the first path segment ending in
.B .php
that is a regular file beneath
.BR \-\-root ;
whatever follows that segment becomes
.BR PATH_INFO .
Second, a directory's
.BR \-\-index .
Third, an existing file that is not a script, which the proxy serves itself.
Otherwise, the index script at the top of
.BR \-\-root ,
as for a front controller, with the path in
.BR REQUEST_URI .
.IP
The proxy finds a script with
.BR openat2 (2)
and
.BR RESOLVE_BENEATH ,
so a link that leads out of the root never counts as a script.
An absolute link is followed only when its target stays inside the root.
Because a script must exist,
.B /upload.jpg/x.php
is never handed to the interpreter, whatever
.B cgi.fix_pathinfo
says.
.IP
The proxy never serves the following, either as a script or as a file, and
answers 404 instead.
It refuses a path that walks up the tree, and a hidden segment other than
.BR .well\-known .
It also refuses the names a site keeps but does not hand out.
Those are a name ending in
.BR ~ ,
a name containing
.BR .php. ,
and names with any of these extensions, also when followed by
.B .gz
or
.BR .br :
.BR bak ,
.BR old ,
.BR orig ,
.BR save ,
.BR swp ,
.BR swo ,
.BR tmp ,
.BR dist ,
.BR inc ,
.BR sql ,
.BR ini ,
.BR log ,
.BR phtml ,
.BR phar ,
.B phps
and
.BR php3 \(en php8 .
.IP
The proxy serves a file with its type, an ETag and Last\-Modified.
It answers If\-None\-Match, If\-Modified\-Since, If\-Match and
If\-Unmodified\-Since (RFC 9110 13.2.2), and one range with If\-Range.
It accepts GET and HEAD only (405 otherwise), under keep\-alive.
A file up to 16 KiB is kept in memory after its first GET, up to 64 MiB in
total.
Each request checks the kept file against the disk with one
.BR statx (2),
which notices a file replaced under its name.
A larger file is sent with
.BR sendfile (2)
on a plain connection, at most 4 MiB a turn.
Where that file is not in the page cache, it is read through the ring instead of
waited on.
Over TLS, a larger file is read 256 KiB at a time.
.IP
The proxy never passes on a
.B Proxy
header (httpoxy).
It also drops any header whose name contains an underscore, because a server
would read it as the same variable as its dashed twin.
.B HTTPS
is on when this proxy terminated TLS, or when an entrance in front of it did
and said so over the loopback.
When an upload sends
.BR "Expect: 100\-continue" ,
the proxy tells it to continue, because an application server never does.
Whatever a script writes to STDERR reaches the log, at most twenty lines a
minute.
An
.B X\-Accel\-Redirect
(nginx's internal redirect) is answered 502; a script hands out a file with
.BR readfile() .
.TP
.BI "\-\-root " directory
The absolute path of the directory that holds a FastCGI proxy's scripts and
files.
.TP
.B \-\-compress
Send a file's precompressed copy, made by the site's build, to clients that
accept it.
For example,
.B app.css.br
or
.B app.css.gz
is sent for
.B app.css
to a client whose
.B Accept\-Encoding
accepts that coding.
br is preferred over gzip.
The response carries
.B Content\-Encoding
and the copy's own ETag, and every file response then carries
.BR "Vary: Accept\-Encoding" .
.IP
A copy older than its file is never sent, since that means a build changed one
and not the other.
Nor is a copy sent for a range, which refers to the file.
Nor is one sent when its file is missing or kept from the public.
Nor is one sent for an image, a video, a woff font, a zip or a pdf, whose
formats are already compressed.
A compressed copy of a name kept from the public is kept from it too, with or
without this flag.
The proxy itself never compresses or decompresses anything.
So it links no compression library, and it decodes nothing a client sends.
.TP
.BI "\-\-fastcgi\-root " directory
The same directory as the application server sees it, when that path differs
(for a server in a container).
The proxy passes it to the application server in
.B SCRIPT_FILENAME
and
.BR DOCUMENT_ROOT .
.TP
.BI "\-\-index " name
The script that a directory and an unmatched path go to.
Default:
.BR index.php .
.TP
.BI "\-\-strategy " "strategy"
The load\-balancing strategy:
.B round-robin
(the default),
.BR random ,
or
.B lua
(custom, via the on_route callback).
.TP
.BI "\-\-protocol " "protocol"
The protocol the proxy speaks:
.B http
(the default, with path-based routing) or
.B tcp
(raw byte forwarding).
.IP
In
.B tcp
mode, a client may shut its write side with
.BR shutdown (2).
The proxy passes that half\-close on to the backend and holds the return
direction open until the backend itself closes.
A byte relay has no framing, so this is the only way it can tell a backend
where a request ended.
This is what makes
.BR "nc \-N" ,
a one\-shot
.BR redis\-cli ,
and any shell pipeline into the proxy work.
Each of those sends, half\-closes, and then reads the answer.
The proxy issues the half\-close only once everything the client sent has
reached the backend socket, so the half\-close cannot overtake the request it
ends.
.IP
The hold ends when the backend closes, when the client resets, or at
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
if one is set.
A half\-closed client is held no longer than one that never half\-closed.
A TCP relay has no request and no response, so no deadline sweep applies to
either.
.PP
Default port: 8080
.PP
HTTP mode: requests to /<proxy-name>/* are forwarded with the prefix stripped.
A request for any other path, / included, is answered 404.
.PP
In
.B http
mode, a request carrying an upgrade offer is forwarded with both of the headers
that make the offer intact, instead of having them stripped as hop-by-hop.
An upgrade offer is an
.B Upgrade
header together with a
.B Connection
header that lists the
.B upgrade
token.
If the backend answers
.BR "101 Switching Protocols" ,
the proxy relays that response verbatim.
The connection then becomes a bidirectional byte relay until one end closes.
This is what makes
.B ws://
work through a proxy in http mode.
See
.B WebSocket and other protocol upgrades
under
.BR LIMITATIONS
for what the proxy does and does not do with such a connection.
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " name" [, name ...]
On a proxy, the names by which it is reached through the entrance (see
.B \-\-proxy
under
.BR "DNS FLAGS" ).
When it is reached by one of these names, the proxy serves without a
.BI / name /
prefix.
The path and the Host go to the backend unchanged, as a site behind it expects.
From a hop on this machine (the entrance in front of it), the proxy keeps
.B X\-Forwarded\-Proto
and
.B X\-Forwarded\-Host
instead of overwriting them.
The backend is therefore told the scheme the client used, not the cleartext
hop between entrance and proxy.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy hub \-p 8089 \-\-bind 127.0.0.1 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:8088 \-\-domain hub \-\-backend\-timeout 5m \-s
.fi
.TP
.BI \-\-auth " bearer:file\fR|\fBbasic:\fIfile\fR|\fBidp:\fIruntime\fR|\fBnone"
Require clients to authenticate before any part of their request reaches a
backend.
.B bearer:
admits
.BI "Authorization: Bearer " token
when the token matches a line of
.IR file .
Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
.B basic:
admits HTTP Basic with an access key and its secret key from
.IR file .
The file has the format that a storage runtime's
.B \-\-credentials
reads, and the key must belong to a user who is an admin there.
A key outside any
.B [user]
section belongs to the default owner, who is an admin.
.IP
A request without valid credentials is answered
.B 401
with the
.B WWW\-Authenticate
header to answer in.
The backend is never sent the
.B Authorization
field.
The proxy reads the file again when it changes, at most once a second.
If the file is gone or does not parse, the proxy refuses everybody and says so
in the log.
.IP
An address that fails five times within fifteen minutes is refused even when it
gives the right answer.
It gets
.B 429
with
.B Retry\-After
until fifteen minutes after its first failure.
An IPv6 address is counted by its /64.
Behind the entrance (a hop on this machine), the address is the last one in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-For ,
which is the one the entrance heard from.
Each failure is logged with that address.
.IP
The proxy checks an admitted connection again every second for as long as its
exchange lasts, an upgrade's tunnel included.
It closes the connection once the credential that admitted it would no longer
do so: a token removed from the file, a sign-in ended or an account disabled.
Set at
.BR create ;
.B edit
changes it on a stopped proxy.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy stats \-p 9191 \-\-bind 127.0.0.1 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:9190 \-\-domain metrics \\
    \-\-auth basic:/etc/socketley/storage\-ui.creds \-s
.fi
.IP
.BI idp: runtime
puts a sign-in in front of every route.
The proxy accepts the sign-in cookie of the
.B idp
runtime of that name in this daemon.
It verifies the cookie here against the key that runtime publishes beside its
state, once per session, and then remembers the session until it expires.
A browser without the cookie, meaning a GET or HEAD that accepts HTML, is sent
with
.B 302
to sign in and then back to the address it asked for.
Any other request without it is answered
.BR 401 .
.IP
The backend is told the account's fixed id in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-User ,
its username in
.B X\-Forwarded\-Preferred\-Username
and its email address in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-Email .
A client's own headers of those names never reach the backend.
The proxy removes the provider's own cookies from the
.B Cookie
field it sends to the backend, and keeps every other cookie.
The cookie reaches a proxy on another name only when the provider's
.B \-\-cookie\-domain
covers both names.
.IP
.nf
socketley create proxy wiki \-p 8100 \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    \-\-domain wiki.example.com \-\-auth idp:auth \-s
.fi
.TP
.BR \-\-redirect\-https " [\fIport\fR|" none ]
Answer every request with a
.B 308
redirect to the same name and path over HTTPS.
The redirect goes to
.IR port .
Default: 443, which is then not written into the URL.
An ACME HTTP\-01 challenge that this daemon holds is still answered first.
A
.B dns
runtime whose entrance is on 443 creates a child proxy on port 80 that uses
this flag.
.SS Sharding
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " "N"
Serve this proxy from
.I N
worker processes instead of one, sharing the listening socket through
.BR SO_REUSEPORT .
The kernel gives each incoming connection to one of them.
.I N
is 1 to 64; the default is 1.
.PP
A runtime is one process, and therefore one event loop on one core.
This flag is the only way to give a single runtime more than that.
It is worth using only when a proxy is saturated.
Below saturation, a single connection is limited by the cost of a cross-core
wakeup, and another process cannot help with that.
.PP
A shard is an implementation detail of a runtime, not a runtime of its own:
.IP \(bu 2
.B ls
shows one entry.
.IP \(bu 2
.B stats
reports the connection count summed across the shards, and labels each shard's
detail text.
.IP \(bu 2
.B stop
and
.B remove
take all the shards down.
.IP \(bu 2
The count is persisted, so a restart brings back the number that was asked
for.
.PP
.B "It is a declaration, and not every type may make it."
Sharding is correct exactly when nothing is shared between connections, and
nothing can detect that.
The flags cannot, and the script cannot either: a table at file scope is
indistinguishable from one that happens never to be read twice.
So the operator declares it, and the flag is offered only where the operator is
in a position to judge.
How each type handles it:
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B cache
shards by key ownership.
Each shard owns the slice of the keyspace that hashes to it.
A command naming a key that another shard owns is forwarded to that shard,
executed there, and answered back through the connection that asked.
A
.B SET
on one connection and a later
.B GET
on another therefore agree, whichever shards they landed on.
See
.B Sharding
under
.B CACHE FLAGS
for what that costs.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B client
refuses the flag, because it has no listening socket to share.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B server
may shard with or without
.BR \-\-lua .
Each shard loads the script into its own interpreter, so per\-shard state stays
per shard and is not shared between shards.
See
.B Sharding
under
.B SERVER FLAGS
for what that means for a script.
.PP
.B "\-\-shards with \-\-listen unix:"
gives each shard a node of its own,
.IR base#N.sock ,
because
.B SO_REUSEPORT
cannot share a path: on one path,
.I N
processes would unlink each other's node.
The pair is refused only when the longest of those paths is too long for a
unix socket address.
.PP
.B "What it bought, measured."
An
.B http
proxy in front of four
.B \-\-http
backends, driven by
.BR wrk :
.PP
.RS
.TS
l r r.
shards	throughput	CPU per shard
_
1	40,285 req/s	1.00 cores
2	94,401 req/s	0.99 cores
4	150,175 req/s	0.91 cores
8	142,775 req/s	0.47 cores
.TE
.RE
.PP
Four shards give 3.73x the throughput of one, close to linear: each shard stays
at a full core until the machine runs out of cores.
Eight shards on a ten-core box are past that point and gain nothing.
The per-shard CPU figure halving is what running out looks like.
Do not set more shards than the machine has cores to give them.
.PP
.B "The backend tier saturates first."
That is the first limit anyone will hit, and it is not a property of this flag.
The same proxy in front of a
.I single
backend stops at 2.14x however many shards it is given.
The backend is then pinned at exactly 1.00 cores, and the shards are idle,
waiting on it.
A sharded proxy needs something behind it that is more than one process:
several backends, a
.B \-\-strategy
across a group, or a backend that is itself sharded.
Before reading a number as this flag's ceiling, find out what saturated.
.PP
.B "It does nothing for \-\-protocol tcp, for a different reason."
A
.B tcp
proxy moves bytes with
.BR IORING_OP_SPLICE ,
which io_uring preps with
.BR REQ_F_FORCE_ASYNC .
So that data path never runs on the shard's event loop at all; it runs on
io-wq kernel threads.
It was therefore never limited by the number of processes.
At
.I one
shard, a tcp proxy under 100 connections was measured holding 11 threads, 10 of
them
.BR iou\-* ,
and using 1.85 cores.
Sharding such a proxy adds processes to a path that is already parallel, and
throughput does not change.
If you shard a tcp proxy and see no change, this is why.
The ceiling that
.B \-\-shards
exists to lift is the
.B http
one above, where the runtime really is one event loop on one core.
.PP
.B "The load is balanced per connection, not per request."
.B SO_REUSEPORT
picks a shard when it accepts a connection and never changes that choice.
One heavy long-lived connection therefore makes one shard hot while the others
idle.
Over a small number of connections the spread is uneven: eight connections over
four shards were measured landing 3/2/2/1.
Sharding helps a proxy carrying many comparable connections; it does nothing
for a proxy carrying one big one.
.PP
The shard count can be changed with
.B edit
only while the runtime is stopped, for the same reason as
.BR \-p :
applying it to a running proxy would mean retiring workers out from under
live connections.
.SS Health Check Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-check " "[tcp|http]"
Enables active health checks of the backends.
In
.B tcp
mode, the proxy opens a TCP connection to each backend at the configured
interval, and the check succeeds if the port is listening.
In
.B http
mode, the proxy sends an HTTP GET to
.B \-\-health\-path
and treats a 2xx response as healthy.
The proxy removes an unhealthy backend from the load balancer pool until it
recovers.
Under
.BR \-\-shards ,
each of the first 64 backends of a
.B \-\-backend
list is probed by one shard and every shard uses its verdict,
so a backend sees one probe per interval at any shard count;
a shard that has gone has its share probed by the others until it is back.
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-interval " "duration"
The time between health checks.
Default: 5 seconds.
A bare number is seconds; the suffixes
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " and " w
are accepted
.RB ( 30 " and " 5m " both work)."
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-path " "path"
The path that HTTP health checks request.
Default:
.BR /health .
Used only when
.B \-\-health\-check
is set to
.BR http .
.TP
.BI "\-\-health\-threshold " "n"
The number of consecutive failed health checks after which a backend is marked
unhealthy.
Default: 3.
One successful check restores the backend.
.SS Circuit Breaker Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-circuit\-threshold " "n"
The number of consecutive backend errors that trips the circuit breaker to the
.B open
state.
A refused connection, a timeout and a 5xx response each count as an error.
While the circuit is open, every request to that backend fails at once, without
a connection attempt.
Under
.BR \-\-shards ,
the errors are counted for the whole runtime and a circuit one shard opens is
open on every shard; each shard sends its own half\-open probe.
Default: 5.
.TP
.BI "\-\-circuit\-timeout " "duration"
How long an open circuit stays open before it changes to
.BR half-open .
In the half\-open state, the proxy lets one probe request through.
If the probe succeeds, the circuit closes and the backend is fully healthy
again; if it fails, the circuit opens again.
Default: 30 seconds.
A bare number is seconds; the suffixes
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " and " w
are accepted
.RB ( 30 " and " 5m " both work)."
.TP
.BI "\-\-backend\-timeout " "duration"
How long a backend may take to send the
.I first
byte of a response before the proxy gives up on the request.
The proxy then retries the request on another backend if
.B \-\-retry
allows it, and otherwise answers
.BR "504 Gateway Timeout" .
In both cases the backend is charged with an error, so the circuit breaker
above opens and takes the backend out of rotation.
While the request body is still arriving, the wait is measured from the latest
bytes received.
If a client stops sending a body and the backend has already taken all of it
so far, the proxy answers
.B 408 Request Timeout
instead, with no retry and no error charged.
Default: 2 seconds.
0 disables the deadline, and the proxy waits indefinitely.
A bare number is seconds; the suffixes
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d " and " w
are accepted
.RB ( 30 " and " 5m " both work)."
.IP
This deadline is the only mechanism that notices a backend which accepts
connections and then stops answering.
Such a backend passes a TCP health check.
A request that never completes counts as neither a success nor a failure, so
without a deadline the backend keeps its full share of the rotation.
Every request routed to it then waits until the client gives up.
The deadline applies in HTTP mode only, because TCP mode has no request and
response framing to measure.
.IP
Only the wait for the first byte is bounded.
Once the backend has begun answering, a slow or streaming response body is
never cut short, with one exception: the client has closed its side.
Such a client cannot end the response itself, so from then on the backend may
go at most this long without sending a byte.
After that, the proxy closes both connections, with no error charged.
A backend that keeps sending is never cut short.
.IP
The 2\-second default applies whether or not the flag is given.
Some endpoints normally need longer than that to
.I begin
answering, such as a report computed on demand, a cold cache or a long query.
If any endpoint behind this proxy does, raise
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
to cover it, or set it to
.B 0
to disable the deadline.
At the default, such an endpoint has its requests cut off at 2 seconds and is
charged an error each time.
That opens the circuit breaker on a backend that is working correctly.
.SS Retry Flags
.TP
.BI "\-\-retry " "n"
Retries a failed request up to
.I n
times on the next backend in the pool.
Default: 0, which disables retries.
Only idempotent HTTP methods (GET, HEAD, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS) are retried,
unless
.B \-\-retry\-all
is set.
In TCP mode, every failed connection attempt is retried.
.TP
.B \-\-retry\-all
Also retries non\-idempotent methods (POST, PATCH).
Use it with caution: the proxy replays the request body to the next backend,
which may cause duplicate side effects.
.SS What a Request That Never Reaches a Backend Is Answered
In
.B \-\-protocol http
mode, a client that has sent a complete request always gets a status line back.
The status tells which side failed.
In every case below, the proxy first retries the request if
.B \-\-retry
allows it.
The status is sent when no retry is available or every retry has also failed.
.TP
.B 502 Bad Gateway
The backend failed.
It refused the connection, was unreachable, could not be resolved, closed
before the first byte of the response arrived, or rejected the TLS handshake.
The error is charged to the backend's circuit breaker.
A client that retries a 502 immediately asks a backend that has just failed.
.TP
.B 503 Service Unavailable
The proxy itself ran short of a resource before it asked the backend anything.
The resource is the local ephemeral port range
.RB ( EADDRNOTAVAIL ),
descriptors
.RB ( EMFILE ", " ENFILE ),
or memory or buffers
.RB ( ENOMEM ", " ENOBUFS ).
A submission that did not reach the io_uring ring also gives 503.
Nothing is charged to any circuit breaker: the backend is healthy, and the same
request will very likely succeed on the next attempt.
The proxy also answers 503 when there is no backend to send to at all: every
member of an
.B @group
is out, or the single backend's breaker is open.
.TP
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
The backend accepted the connection, or never finished accepting it, and sent
no first byte within
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout .
The error is charged to the breaker.
.PP
Two cases deliberately get no status line:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-protocol tcp
has no requests or responses.
When the backend of such a byte pipe never comes up, the proxy closes the
connection and does not invent a status for it.
.IP \(bu 2
Once the backend has put bytes on the client's connection, a status line can
no longer be written.
It would land in the middle of a message, and the client would read it as part
of the response it is already receiving.
The proxy closes such a connection.
It charges the backend that produced the unfinished response to its breaker, so
the next client is routed elsewhere.
.PP
.B "A script runs when its runtime starts, not when it is created."
.B create
compiles the file, so a file that does not parse is refused at once, by the
command that asked.
The worker process runs the script.
Without
.B \-s
there is no worker yet, so nothing has run: not the top level, not
.BR socketley.subscribe ,
and no check of a global such as
.BR tick_ms .
These happen at the
.B start
that forks the worker, and that command reports any refusal.
.SS Process Isolation
Every runtime made with
.B create
or restored at boot runs in its own supervised worker process.
There is no flag for this and nothing to configure: servers, clients, proxies
and caches all run this way.
The one exception is a runtime that a script makes with
.BR socketley.create :
a server, client, proxy, cache, storage or dns runtime made that way runs in the
daemon.
.PP
A configuration file that contains
.B daemon_process
is refused rather than ignored, whatever its value.
The error names the line to delete.
.PP
The whole Lua API works in a worker.
The thirteen calls that a worker used to refuse are all answered now.
They fall into three groups:
.RS
.TP
.B Reads of the map
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and all four
.B socketley.cluster
calls.
The daemon pushes each worker a replica of the runtime map and a snapshot of
the cluster view, and the worker answers these calls from them.
.TP
.B Changes to the map
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop ,
.BR .remove .
These are not answered from the replica, because a copy of the map gives no
permission to change it.
The worker sends them to the daemon over a socket of its own and waits for the
answer.
So
.B "if socketley.create(...) then"
branches on what happened, not on whether the request was accepted.
Measured over three runs of 20000 calls: 9.95, 10.20 and 10.52 microseconds.
A script takes this path deliberately, never once per message.
.TP
.B Moving a connection
.B socketley.pipe
between two runtimes, and
.BR self.route() .
Despite the names, neither hands a descriptor over.
The original runtime keeps the socket and relays the bytes, and the target
runtime answers as though the connection were its own.
Names travel; the connection does not.
.RE
.PP
One refusal remains, for one case: a worker forked by a daemon that could not
give it the control plane these calls need.
That is an older build, or a daemon that could not create the socket.
The error says so and says to restart the daemon.
.PP
A cross\-runtime request costs about 50 microseconds more than the same call
within one address space, as measured.
A server using
.B \-\-cache
pays this on every command.
.PP
A script's
.B routes
declaration still works.
Running the script produces it, and the script runs in the worker.
The worker sends the declaration back to the daemon over the control socket it
already holds, and the daemon's placeholder publishes it to the proxies in front
of the runtime.
See
.BR "Declared Routes" .
.PP
The daemon forks and execs itself, and hands the worker its configuration and
its routing table over a control socket.
The daemon keeps a placeholder for the worker in the runtime map.
The CLI output is unchanged:
.BR ps ,
.BR ls ,
.BR show ,
.B stats
and the Prometheus endpoint report the same figures as for an in\-process
runtime.
The worker publishes the figures, and the daemon stores them where an
in\-process runtime would have written them.
.PP
The worker binds the port; no descriptor is passed between the processes.
If the worker dies, the daemon restarts it and the runtime stays
.BR running .
If it dies more than five times in ten seconds, the runtime is left
.B failed
instead of being forked again in a loop.
In both cases the daemon tells every other runtime to release the connections
routed to or from it, because a killed worker runs no teardown of its own.
.PP
Lua callbacks run inside the worker, against the worker's own state.
A cache's
.B PUBLISH
reaches the worker's
.B subscribe
callbacks.
The figures the daemon reports were read by the worker when they were printed.
See
.B "Out-of-process runtimes"
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.SS mTLS Flags
A proxy has two legs, and each leg has its own half of mutual TLS.
The flag names say which leg: \fBclient\fR always means "a TLS client".
The proxy is a TLS client towards its backends, and its own callers are TLS
clients towards the proxy.
.TP
.BI "\-\-client\-ca " "path"
The CA that must have signed the certificate presented
.IR "by a client" .
This is mutual TLS on the proxy's own listening port, so it requires
.BR \-\-tls " " \-\-cert " " \-\-key .
Without them it is refused: on a cleartext port it would authenticate nobody,
while
.B show
reported mutual TLS.
.TP
.BI "\-\-client\-cert " "path"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-client\-key " "path"
The certificate and key this proxy presents
.I to a backend
that asks for one.
Give both or neither.
Half a pair loads nothing, so it is refused rather than leaving a leg that
presents no certificate although the flag promised one.
.PP
Any of
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
also switches the backend leg itself to TLS, for every backend.
None of the three means anything against a cleartext peer, and honouring them
without encrypting would fail open.
To encrypt one backend and leave the others alone, use an
.B https://
backend address instead.
.PP
The proxy always
.B verifies
a TLS backend connection.
It checks the chain against
.BI \-\-ca " file"
if one is given, and against the system CA store otherwise.
It checks the certificate against the endpoint the request was routed to.
A host name must match one of the certificate's DNS names, and partial
wildcards are refused; an address literal must match an IP SAN.
TLS 1.2 is the minimum version.
None of this can be disabled.
A backend that presents a private\-CA or self\-signed certificate needs that CA
in
.BI \-\-ca " file"
or in the store OpenSSL reads.
If a backend's certificate fails either check, the proxy answers 502 (or
retries, where
.B \-\-retry
is set) and charges the backend's circuit breaker.
The request is never sent in the clear as a fallback.
.PP
Connections to a TLS backend are pooled and reused, like cleartext ones.
Forty requests in a row to one
.B https://
backend cost one handshake, not forty.
The TLS session is pooled together with the descriptor.
So a reused connection continues the same record layer in the same stream; it
is not a new session over an old socket.
A pooled socket is reused only for the endpoint it was opened to.
That endpoint is also the name its certificate was verified against and the
name sent as SNI.
A pooled socket never survives a restart.
The trust store and client certificate the leg verifies against are rebuilt
when the runtime starts, and the pool is emptied when it stops.
Before the proxy reuses a socket, it decrypts and inspects whatever is waiting
on it.
A session ticket or a key update is consumed, and the connection is kept.
Application data is a response nobody asked for, so the proxy drops the
connection rather than handing it to the next client.
.PP
.B \-\-health\-check http
works with TLS backends and probes over TLS.
It verifies the backend's certificate to the same standard as the data path,
and marks a backend it cannot verify as unhealthy rather than healthy.
.SS Sidecar Mode
.TP
.B \-\-sidecar
A shorthand that enables a service\-mesh\-ready configuration with one flag.
It is equivalent to:
.nf
\-\-health\-check tcp \-\-circuit\-threshold 5 \-\-circuit\-timeout 30 \-\-retry 2 \-\-drain
.fi
.IP
You can still give individual flags alongside
.B \-\-sidecar
to override these defaults, for example
.BR "\-\-sidecar \-\-health\-check http \-\-health\-path /ready" .
.IP
.B \-\-sidecar
does not set
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout ,
but the two interact.
The deadline is 2 seconds by default, and each expiry is charged to the circuit
breaker that
.B \-\-sidecar
turns on.
Five slow requests in a row open the circuit for 30 seconds.
If the application behind the proxy can take longer than 2 seconds to send its
first response byte, add
.BI "\-\-backend\-timeout " seconds
alongside
.BR \-\-sidecar ,
or add
.B \-\-backend\-timeout 0
to switch the deadline off.
.PP
Example:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    -p 8080 \-\-health\-check tcp -s

socketley create proxy slowapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 \\
    -p 8080 \-\-backend\-timeout 30 -s
.fi
.SH CACHE FLAGS
.TP
.BI "\-\-listen unix:" "/path/to.sock"
Serves the cache on an AF_UNIX socket instead of the
.B \-p
port.
.B "redis\-cli \-s"
connects to it, and both the plaintext and RESP protocols are detected as on a
port.
The full description is under
.B \-\-listen
in
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
It is refused with
.BR \-\-cluster ,
because cluster members advertise ports computed from a formula, and RESP has
no way to write a filesystem path as a node address.
.IP
.B It is worth measuring before it is worth arguing about.
On one test machine, one cache and one redis\-server were loaded with the same
keys and driven with GET by eight client processes:
.IP
.nf
socketley over TCP      2,969,848/s        redis over TCP    1,411,960/s
socketley over AF_UNIX  4,517,387/s        redis over AF_UNIX 2,319,573/s
.fi
.IP
Both programs gain about half again.
That gain is the TCP and netfilter path dropping out of the measurement, not
anything either program does differently.
On a machine with conntrack loaded and nftables rules on the OUTPUT hook, which
includes any machine running Docker, that path is not free.
A peer on the same machine does not need it.
.TP
.B \-\-listen with \-\-shards binds one node per shard.
Shard
.I i
gets
.IR name #i.sock :
the shard number is inserted before the suffix, by the same rule the daemon's
own companion nodes follow.
N processes binding
.I one
path would unlink each other's node, and only the last would stay reachable.
N paths are N listeners.
.IP
.B The formula is the interface.
On a server, every shard answers every request, so a caller can use any node.
On a
.I cache
the shards are not interchangeable, and choosing the right one pays off.
A caller that hashes the key the way
.B CLUSTER KEYSLOT
does and connects to that shard's node causes no crossing between shards.
That is the same saving
.B \-\-cluster
gives between ports.
A caller that picks a node at random still gets correct answers, because the
shard crosses to the owner, but it pays the cost of that crossing.
.IP
.B "What that crossing costs, measured."
The setup was one daemon pinned to four cores, and eight
.B redis-benchmark
processes of twenty\-four connections each on the other twelve cores, running
GET at depth 1.
There were six interleaved passes with the order rotated, the first discarded,
and a clock probe at both ends of the run.
Client CPU is recorded beside every row, because a client that hits its CPU
limit inverts the sign of a comparison before it changes its size.
The clients never used more than 5.8 of their twelve cores.
.IP
.RS
.TS
l r r r r.
arrangement	GET/s	x1	cores	us/op
_
1 shard	240,671	1.00	1.00	4.16
2 shards, one address	251,959	1.05	1.91	7.60
4 shards, one address	421,093	1.75	3.95	9.38
4 shards, slot-routed	673,017	2.80	3.65	5.43
.TE
.RE
.IP
.B "Behind one address the shards buy far less than the cores they take."
Two shards take 1.91 cores and return 1.05x; four take 3.95 cores and return
1.75x.
With two shards, half the keys belong to a sibling; with four, three quarters
do.
Serving those keys roughly doubles the cost per operation: 4.16 us with one
shard, against 7.60 with two and 9.38 with four.
With a client that picks the owning shard, the same four shards return 2.80x at
5.43 us, which recovers most of what the crossings were costing.
.IP
.B "How well the crossings batch decides this cost, and the client decides how well they batch."
The mesh queues each crossing and flushes once per loop turn, so requests that
arrive together cross together.
At the same 192 connections, twelve client processes of sixteen connections
give the two\-shard arm 0.90x, while eight processes of twenty\-four give it
1.05x.
In the same two shapes the one\-shard arm reads 235,421 and 240,671, unchanged.
Measured on a floor of two processes and two rings, one crossing at a time costs
8.13 us, while thirty\-two in a batch cost 282 ns each.
So a figure for this flag is a figure for one arrival pattern.
The figure above is the more favourable of the two shapes tried.
.IP
.B "The ceiling above that is the machine, not this flag."
Four shards routed by key reach 2.80x.
Four independent
.B redis\-server
processes on the same four cores, driven the same way, reach 2.88x.
socketley is ahead of them in absolute throughput at every count tried: 1.12x
at one and 1.09x at four.
Neither scales linearly on loopback here.
So a cache that must scale needs
.B \-\-cluster
and a client that routes.
The 3.57x quoted for a server under
.B SERVER FLAGS
does not carry over to a cache.
.IP
Against the same cache on TCP with
.BR \-\-cluster ,
with both clients routing by key and four shards, AF_UNIX measured at least
.B 1.22x
faster.
Two later passes read higher but are not quoted.
In them the TCP arm dropped for a reason that was not the port pool
(TIME\-WAIT peaked at 1,235 of 28,231), and that reason is not understood.
.IP
.B A remote client gets none of this.
AF_UNIX is local, so the benefit applies only to hops between processes on one
machine.
The hop that cannot use it today is a proxy in front of a
.I sharded
cache.
A proxy does not read keys, so it would reach the wrong shard
.RI ( N \-1)/ N
of the time.
Teaching the proxy
.B cache_shard_of
would solve that, but it is not done.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent " "file"
The file the cache is stored in.
The cache is saved on stop and loaded on start.
.IP
The file is written with mode
.BR 0600 .
The dump holds whatever was cached.
Because the cache
.I loads
it on start, the dump is not just a record of what the cache held: it decides
what the cache will hold.
Earlier versions wrote it with mode
.BR 0644 ,
readable by every account on the machine.
When the cache next loads such a dump, it narrows the file's mode and writes a
line to the daemon log.
.IP
The file mode is all this flag can control.
Replacing a file needs write permission on the
.IR directory ,
not on the file.
So put
.I file
in a directory that only this daemon's user can write to, not in a
world\-writable directory shared with other accounts.
.IP
.B With \-\-shards each shard writes its own slice, and the set survives a changed shard count.
With one shard, the cache writes
.I file
itself; with more than one, the shards write
.IR file .shard0 ,
.IR file .shard1
and so on.
Each file records the shard count it was written under and a fingerprint of the
function that decides which shard owns a key.
So a cache dumped at four shards and started at eight is redistributed rather
than loaded into the wrong slices.
Every key survives; this was measured across 1\->4, 4\->1, 4\->8, 8\->4 and
4\->4.
Each shard reads every file of the old set and keeps the keys it now owns as it
reads them, so a shard holds its own slice and never a whole old dump.
.IP
If the set of files cannot be trusted, the start is refused rather than serving
part of the data.
The error names a missing slice.
Files that disagree about the shard count, such as the dumps of two runs in one
place, are refused together.
The dump that replaces them removes slices left by a run with more shards.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent\-interval " "duration"
Journals every accepted write next to the dump, and flushes the journal at this
interval.
An acknowledged write reaches the kernel at most
.I duration
after it was accepted.
So a killed or OOM\-killed worker loses at most the last interval of writes,
instead of everything since the last stop or FLUSH.
Requires
.BR \-\-persistent ;
refused without it.
Durations are read as everywhere else
.RB ( 30s ", " 5m ", " 1h ),
and a bare number is seconds.
.IP
The journal is
.IB file .journal
(per shard,
.IB file .shardN.journal ),
written with mode
.BR 0600 .
A clean stop and the admin
.B FLUSH
command write the dump and cut the journal behind it.
An unclean stop leaves a tail, which the next start replays on top of the dump
before serving.
If the crash tore the tail in the middle of a record, the tail is dropped back
to the last complete record, and the daemon log gives the number of dropped
bytes.
TTLs are journaled as absolute deadlines.
So a key that had five seconds left when it was written has whatever remains of
those five seconds after a replay, possibly nothing.
.IP
The flush is a write into the kernel's page cache, and deliberately not an
fsync.
The guarantee covers a dead process, not a dead machine: a power cut can also
lose what the kernel had not yet written back.
.IP
A journal with records written under a different shard count, or a different
ownership function, refuses the start.
The cache does not guess at an order for one key's history across two files.
To recover, start under the shard split that wrote the journal and stop
cleanly, or delete the
.B .journal
files to discard the tail.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "mode"
Sets the access control mode.
.B readonly
allows GET and SIZE only;
.B readwrite
allows GET, SET, DEL and SIZE, and is the default;
.B admin
allows all commands, including FLUSH and LOAD.
.IP
.B readonly
also applies to this cache's own Lua script, and there it fails more quietly
than on the wire.
A client that sends a write is told
.RB \(dq "denied: readonly mode" \(dq.
The twelve mutating
.B self.*
calls refuse by returning exactly what they return when there was nothing to
do:
.B false
for nine of them, an empty string for
.B self.lpop
and
.BR self.rpop ,
and
.B nil
for
.BR self.sadd .
A script calls
.B self.cache_mode()
to tell the two cases apart.
The return values are listed with the calls under
.BR "LUA API" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-maxmemory " "size"
The most memory the cache may use for its data.
Takes a size with K, M or G (for example 100M or 1G).
When the limit is exceeded, the eviction policy decides what happens.
0 means no limit, which is the default.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
the limit is the cache's: a key lives on one shard, so each shard holds its
share, the limit divided by the shard count, and
.B maxmemory
answers the sum.
.IP
The only suffixes are
.BR K ", " M " and " G .
A value the parser cannot read in full is refused, not partly read.
So
.B 100MB
is an error rather than a hundred bytes.
A value so large that the multiplication would wrap is also an error, rather
than 0, which means "unlimited".
.TP
.BI "\-\-eviction " "policy"
The eviction policy used when maxmemory is reached.
.B noeviction
rejects writes and is the default;
.B allkeys\-lru
evicts the least recently used keys;
.B allkeys\-random
evicts random keys.
.TP
.B \-\-resp
Forces the RESP2 protocol (the Redis wire protocol) on all connections.
By default, the cache switches a connection to RESP when its first byte is '*'.
With this flag, every connection uses RESP regardless.
.TP
.BI "\-\-replicate " "host:port"
Connects to the given leader cache as a follower.
The follower receives a full data dump when it connects, and then the leader's
write commands as they happen.
The follower is read\-only.
.IP
Values that contain a newline replicate intact.
The link carries one command per line, and a cache value can be any bytes.
A command that cannot be written as one line is sent length\-framed instead.
Any other command is sent unchanged.
So a leader and a follower of different versions agree on every dataset that
older versions replicated correctly.
A follower too old to understand a frame stores nothing for that command,
rather than storing a truncated value.
.IP
Replication is
.I not
encrypted, in either direction, so it cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-tls .
That pair is refused at
.BR create ,
by
.BR edit " and " import ,
and again at every
.BR start .
A
.B \-\-tls
cache also refuses the
.B replicate
command, so it never becomes a leader either.
See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.IP
.B A sharded cache cannot be a replication leader, and says so.
A follower arrives as one connection, and
.B SO_REUSEPORT
gives it to one shard.
That shard would have to be the only writer to the follower's socket, and the
other shards have no socket to the follower at all.
Promoting the cache anyway would send the follower only one shard's writes and
report success.
So
.B replicate
is refused on a cache with more than one shard, and the error names the reason.
Fanning the stream out would first need a per\-shard sequence and an
end\-of\-dump marker in the follower protocol, and it has neither.
.IP
A sharded cache may be a
.IR follower .
Every shard connects to the leader and keeps the part of the stream that its
slice owns.
So the cache holds the leader's keys once rather than once per shard, at the
cost of every shard reading the whole stream.
.TP
.B \-\-tls
.TQ
.BI "\-\-cert " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-key " "file"
.TQ
.BI "\-\-ca " "file"
Terminates TLS on this cache's listening port, exactly as a server runtime
does on its own port.
.B \-\-tls
requires
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file";
.BI \-\-ca " file"
also demands a certificate from every connecting client (mutual TLS).
RESP and the line protocol both run on the decrypted stream, so an existing
Redis client needs no change beyond speaking TLS.
The full description is under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.PP
Default port: 9000
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " "N"
Runs the cache as
.I N
worker processes that share one keyspace.
The shard that owns a key is chosen by CRC16 of the key modulo 16384.
That is Redis's own hash slot function, hash tags included.
Each shard owns a contiguous range of those slots.
.IP
The
.I N
shards share one port through
.BR SO_REUSEPORT .
When a client connects, the kernel gives the connection to whichever shard its
four\-tuple hashes to.
A key that another shard owns is fetched from that shard over the mesh.
To any client, the cache is one store.
.IP
That crossing costs CPU, and with
.I N
shards a random key belongs to another shard
.RI ( N \-1)/ N
of the time.
Measured on this build with 8 client processes \(mu 50 connections, three
interleaved passes per arm, medians, in requests per second per
.I server
core:
.IP
.nf
four shards, clients on the shared port     63,849   15.66 us/req
the same runtime with \-\-cluster, clients
  routed to the per\-shard ports             87,401   11.44 us/req
four independent single\-shard caches        87,663   11.40 us/req
.fi
.IP
Absolute levels cannot be compared between machines or between sessions; what
is claimed is the ratio within one interleaved sweep.
The same pair measured again hours later on the same binary read 84,828 and
116,894.
Every level was a third higher, and the ratio was 1.387 against 1.369.
.IP
.B "\-\-lua under \-\-shards: the script runs in every shard, and each one keeps its own slice."
The keyspace calls of the
.B self.*
table act only on the keys
.I this
shard owns.
These are
.BR self.set ,
.BR self.get ,
.B self.del
and seventeen others.
For a key the shard does not own, they answer exactly as they answer a
refusal:
.B false
for the mutating calls, an empty string for
.B self.get
and the two pops, and
.B nil
for
.BR self.sadd .
They cannot cross to another shard, because the value must be returned on the
same turn and the mesh answers on a later one.
.IP
For a callback that runs in all
.I N
processes, such as
.B on_start
or a timer, this is exactly right: the shards split the work between them and
the keyspace is written once.
For a callback driven by a connection
.RB ( on_miss ,
.BR on_write ,
.BR on_delete ),
the key the callback was handed is always owned by this shard, and any
.I other
key it touches may belong to another shard.
A script calls
.B self.owns(key)
to ask before it acts.
It returns true for every key when the cache is one process.
.IP
.B self.publish
is the one exception that is not refused.
The message goes to every shard over the same mesh that the wire's
.B PUBLISH
uses, because a subscriber is not a key and no shard owns it.
Only the returned
.I count
is this shard's alone: the other shards report back on a later turn, and this
call returns on the turn it was made.
.TP
.B \-\-public
Lets the cache serve peers on other machines.
A cache has no password and answers every client with everything it holds.
So by default it serves only this machine: a TCP peer whose address is not
loopback is answered
.B \-DENIED
and closed before any of its bytes are read.
This is Redis's protected mode, for the same reason Redis has it.
.B \-\-public
lifts this restriction, and so does mutual TLS
.RB ( \-\-tls " " \-\-ca ),
where the client certificate is the credential.
For the same reason, a follower on another host needs its leader to be public.
Set the flag at
.BR create ;
a running cache takes it through
.BR import .
.TP
.B \-\-cluster
Publishes a routable slot map, so a cluster\-aware client sends each key
straight to the shard that owns it and no crossing happens at all.
Off by default.
.IP
.B "Read this before you type it: a cache with \-\-cluster cannot sit behind a proxy used for access control."
With client\-side routing, the client connects to every shard
.IR directly .
The cache tells the client where the shards are, using the address that the
.I asking
connection arrived on.
Behind a proxy, the asking connection is the proxy's, so the map names the
address at which the
.I proxy
reaches the cache.
Either the client can reach that address too, and the proxy's rules are
bypassed by a map the cache handed out.
Or it cannot, and the client spends its retry budget on refused connections.
Neither outcome produces a log line.
.IP
This is not a limitation of this implementation.
It is what client\-side routing is, and Redis Cluster has exactly the same
property.
No setting makes a routing client and a proxy enforcement point work together;
you have to choose one of the two.
Without
.B \-\-cluster
the cache is reached only through its one port, and a proxy in front of it
depends on that.
.IP
.B Each shard binds its own port.
At two or more shards, shard
.I i
binds
.IR port + 1 + i ,
in addition to the shared port.
The shared port keeps working exactly as it does without the flag.
A client that connects to a per\-shard port has chosen that shard on purpose.
A key that shard does not own is answered with
.BI \-MOVED " slot host:port"
rather than fetched.
That lets
.BR "redis\-cli \-c" ,
.BR "redis\-benchmark \-\-cluster" ,
redis\-py, go\-redis, lettuce and node\-redis route for themselves.
.IP
.B A cache with \-\-cluster and two or more shards therefore needs port+1 .. port+N free, and refuses to start otherwise.
Whichever shard answers
.B "CLUSTER SLOTS"
computes the addresses it sends from a formula.
That shard has no way to learn whether its siblings bound their ports.
A shard that ran without its port would be advertised anyway, and every client
would spend its retries on refused connections.
So the start fails instead, and the error names the port and this flag.
Without
.BR \-\-cluster ,
only the shared port is bound, and two four\-shard caches one port apart work
as they always did.
.IP
.B \-\-cluster is legal at any shard count.
At
.B "\-\-shards 1"
it publishes a cluster of one node on the base port and binds no extra port.
So a client can be written once and deployed at any
.IR N .
.IP
.B CLUSTER SLOTS, SHARDS, NODES, INFO, MYID, KEYSLOT and COUNTKEYSINSLOT
are answered on every port and at every shard count, with or without this
flag, because a client that asks deserves a straight answer.
.B CLUSTER INFO
reports
.B cluster_enabled:1
only when
.B \-\-cluster
was given; without it the reply is
.BR cluster_enabled:0 ,
and
.B CLUSTER SLOTS
publishes a single node that holds every slot on the shared port.
That answer is both true and safe.
There is no cluster bus, no failover, no slot migration and no
.BR ASK .
The map is a formula, and each slot has one owner for the life of the run.
.IP
.B On a per-shard port, a command that asks about the keyspace answers for that shard.
.BR DBSIZE ", " KEYS ", " SCAN ", " FLUSH " and " LOAD
report on or act on the slice that shard holds.
That is what a cluster client means when it asks a node, and
.B redis\-cli \-\-cluster call
adds up the results itself.
On the shared port these commands still fan out across every shard and merge
the results, as they always have, except
.BR DBSIZE ,
which adds up the key count each shard publishes and crosses to none.
A shard publishes its count before any reply it sends, so a
.B DBSIZE
already counts a write whose reply the client has.
.B PUBLISH
fans out on both kinds of port, because a subscriber on another shard is still
a subscriber.
.IP
A multi\-key command on a per\-shard port whose keys are all owned by one other
shard is answered with
.BR \-MOVED .
One whose keys span several shards is answered with
.BR \-CROSSSLOT .
That is not pedantry: a redirect would send the client to a shard that would
redirect it back.
.SS Cache Protocol
.PP
.B "A pipelined batch is answered in full or the connection is closed, and either way its cost is bounded."
A connection may owe at most 8 MiB of replies.
The replies to a batch are built before the loop returns to the ring, so
nothing drains while the batch runs, and the batch must fit inside that bound.
Past it, the cache closes the client and logs a line naming the client and how
much it was owed.
.IP
So the useful pipeline depth is one whose replies fit.
Sixteen 256 KiB values make 4 MiB and are answered in full; sixty\(hyfour make
16 MiB and the connection is cut.
For small values the bound is far away: a 64 byte value at depth 16 is a
kilobyte.
.IP
Earlier builds did not keep this bound.
The batch loop did not stop once the connection was marked closing, and
.B flush_responses()
returns true for a closing connection, which its callers read as success.
So the 1 MiB cap on the reply buffer called a flush that cleared nothing, and
the rest of the batch was still parsed and answered into the buffer.
Measured with one connection asking for a 256 KiB value and reading none of it:
.IP
.nf
                        before        after
    64 pipelined GET    +17 MB        +11 MB
   512 pipelined GET   +141 MB        +10 MB
  4,096 pipelined GET  +1,063 MB      +11 MB
 16,384 pipelined GET    +941 MB      +11 MB
.fi
.IP
A GET is about thirty bytes against a 256 KiB answer, so the growth was
roughly 8,700 to one, bounded only by how many commands fit in one read.
Memory now stays flat.
.PP
.B "A cache serving large values spends most of its time copying them."
This is a property of the design, not a defect to tune around.
Profiled at 256 KiB values, 16 clients and one command in flight, sampling the
worker process:
.IP
.nf
              userspace memcpy   kernel copy   page clearing
  GET               28%             18%            13%
  SET               42%             28%             \(em
.fi
.IP
A GET copies the stored value once, into the reply buffer, and the kernel
copies it again into the socket.
A SET copies twice: once from the receive ring into the connection's parse
buffer, and once from there into the store.
Removing the GET copy means sending straight from the store while a send is in
flight.
That needs the store to promise that a value outlives the send.
Every mutating path would have to keep that promise, including LRU eviction
and TTL expiry, which a write to an unrelated key can trigger.
Removing a SET copy means collecting a bulk body outside the parse buffer, and
the replication, sharding and cluster paths would all have to support that.
Neither is a local change, and neither is made here.
.IP
.B "Sharding does not solve this, which is worth knowing before reaching for"
.BR \-\-shards .
Measured at 256 KiB values and 32 clients, against one shard:
.IP
.nf
                                   SET       GET    cpu/op
  1 shard                       14,903    14,503     66 us
  4 shards, client not routing  23,175     8,306    151 us
  4 shards, \-\-cluster, routed   19,920    19,960    158 us
.fi
.IP
A sharded cache without
.B \-\-cluster
relays a command that arrived on the wrong shard instead of answering \-MOVED.
That is what lets an ordinary client work against it at all.
But relaying a 256 KiB value passes it through a second process, so GET drops
to 0.57 of the single shard.
With
.B \-\-cluster
and a client that routes, four shards give 1.38x on GET and 1.34x on SET.
Small messages see 3.2\(en3.6x at eight shards.
Either way, the sharded arm uses about 2.4 times the CPU per operation.
.IP
So what an operator can change is the value size itself.
In one run with sixteen clients, the CPU cost per operation was 4.4 us at 64
bytes, 4.8 us at 4 KiB, 11.4 us at 64 KiB and 40.8 us at 256 KiB.
No setting closes that gap.
.IP
.B "The receive ring is sixty\(hyfour buffers of 64 KiB, and its total footprint is what counts."
A provided\(hybuffer ring is used in rotation, so its cost is count times size
measured against the core's cache, not the size of one buffer.
The ring was 512 buffers of 4 KiB.
Raising only the buffer size, to a 32 MiB ring, cost 4.5% at 64 byte values and
24.5% at 256 KiB.
Keeping the footprint near the size of the core's cache instead gave the
results below.
They were measured on one binary with only the two variables differing, in
seven interleaved passes, 4 KiB x 512 against 64 KiB x 64:
.IP
.nf
  SET, 16 deep, 1 KiB values, 50 clients     +22.4%
  SET, 16 deep, 1 KiB values, 1000 clients   +24.5%
  SET, 16 deep, 4 KiB values                  +9.8%
  small values, 64 KiB and 256 KiB values     within 1.5%
.fi
.IP
The same buffer size in 128 buffers, an 8 MiB ring, lost 12% at 256 KiB
values, and 32 buffers ran dry under 1000 pipelining clients.
The knob exists to be measured, and it has been.
.PP
.B "A read of a key held as another type answers \-WRONGTYPE over RESP."
For example,
.B get
on a list,
.B llen
on a string and
.B hgetall
on a sorted set all answer \-WRONGTYPE.
Answering as though the key were absent (nil, 0, an empty array) would be worse
than an error.
The caller would believe it, and fetch from its database something the cache
already holds.
The type check runs only after a read has missed its own container.
So a hit costs nothing, and a store holding one type pays four
.B empty()
tests.
.B mget
is the exception, as it is in Redis: it answers nil for each wrong\-typed key.
.PP
The text protocol keeps its own answers ("nil", "end", 0), because they are its
documented contract and a text client is not a Redis client.
.PP
Connect via TCP and send commands (newline-terminated, all lowercase).
.PP
Nine of the commands listed below are RESP only, and the line protocol answers
"failed: unknown command" to them:
.BR client ,
.BR config ,
.BR eval ,
.BR evalsha ,
.BR hello ,
.BR psubscribe ,
.BR punsubscribe ,
.B script
and
.BR smismember .
.PP
Six of them are protocol and administration commands.
The other three share one reason: the line protocol cannot express a list of
arguments.
Everything after the key is the value, so that a value may contain spaces.
So there is no way to write the members of smismember, or the keys and
arguments that eval and evalsha take after their script.
.PP
.B Strings:
.TP
.BI "set " "key value"
Store a key-value pair. Returns "ok", "error: type conflict", or
"error: out of memory".
.TP
.BI "get " "key"
Retrieve value. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "del " "key"
Delete key (any type). Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "exists " "key"
Check if key exists. Returns "1" or "0".
.TP
.BI "incr " "key"
Increment integer value by 1. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "decr " "key"
Decrement integer value by 1. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "incrby " "key delta"
Increment integer value by delta. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "decrby " "key delta"
Decrement integer value by delta. Returns new integer.
.TP
.BI "append " "key value"
Append to string value. Returns new length.
.TP
.BI "strlen " "key"
Return string length. Returns 0 if missing.
.TP
.BI "getrange " "key start end"
.TQ
.BI "substr " "key start end"
Return the substring between two inclusive offsets.
Negative offsets count from the end.
An inverted range or a missing key returns an empty string, not an error.
.TP
.BI "setrange " "key offset value"
Overwrite the value from
.I offset
onward and return the new length.
A shorter value is padded with zero bytes up to
.IR offset .
.TP
.BI "incrbyfloat " "key delta"
Add a floating-point delta and return the new value.
The command is replicated as its
.I result
rather than as itself.
Two builds may round the same addition differently, and a follower would then
drift from its leader.
.TP
.BI "getdel " "key"
Return the value and remove the key in one step.
.TP
.BI "getset " "key newvalue"
Set new value and return old value. Returns old value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "mget " "key [key ...]"
Get multiple keys. Returns one value per line (nil if missing), then "end".
.TP
.BI "mset " "key value [key value ...]"
Set multiple key-value pairs atomically. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "type " "key"
Return type of key: "string", "list", "set", "hash", "zset", "stream", or "none".
.TP
.BI "keys " "pattern"
List all matching keys (glob patterns, "*" for all). Returns keys then "end".
.TP
.BI "setnx " "key value"
Set key only if it does not exist. Returns "1" (set) or "0" (not set).
.TP
.BI "setex " "key seconds value"
Set key with expiry. Equivalent to SET + EXPIRE. Returns "ok".
.TP
.B "set " "key value [nx|xx] [ex " sec "|px " ms "] [keepttl] [get]"
Over RESP,
.B set
takes Redis's options.
.B nx
writes only if the key is absent, and
.B xx
only if it exists.
.BR ex " and " px
set a deadline in seconds or milliseconds.
The deadline must be positive: zero or less is refused, and NOTHING is written.
Otherwise a key stored with a deadline that came out at zero would carry no
deadline at all, so it would never expire, while its owner was told the write
worked.
An argument that is not a number is refused as not a number, rather than as a
syntax error about the word after it.
.IP
.B keepttl
keeps the existing deadline, which a plain
.B set
clears.
A session refresh needs this: the value changes, but the hour it has left does
not.
Asking for
.B keepttl
together with
.BR ex " or " px
is a contradiction, so it is refused rather than resolved in either direction.
.IP
.B get
returns the value that was replaced instead of "ok", or nil when there was
none.
It returns the value even when
.B nx
refused the write, which makes it a read and a write in one round trip.
A key holding something other than a string answers
.B \-WRONGTYPE
rather than nil, because a caller asking for the old value needs to know that.
.TP
.BI "psetex " "key milliseconds value"
Set key with millisecond-precision expiry. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "scan " "cursor [match pattern] [count n] [valuematch glob] [withvalues]"
.TQ
.BI "     " "[type t] [field f]... [valuerange " min " " max "]"
Iterates over the keys with a cursor.
Returns the next cursor (0 when done), then the matching keys, then "end".
Start with cursor 0, and repeat with the returned cursor until 0 is returned.
.IP
.B A numeric range, which a glob cannot express.
.I valuerange
takes the same bounds as a zset range:
.BR \-inf ", " +inf ", and a leading " (
for an exclusive bound.
It keeps the values that fall between the bounds.
With
.IR field ,
the named field must be in range.
.IP
A value that is not a number matches NO range, not even
.BR "\-inf +inf" .
The whole value must be the number: "30kg" is not thirty.
A reader that stopped at the digits would answer a question nobody asked.
.IP
.B More than one condition at once.
.I field
may be given up to four times.
The rule is one sentence: a
.I field
applies to the value predicates that follow it, up to the next
.IR field .
So
.RS
.IP
.B "scan 0 field city valuematch berlin field age valuerange 25 40"
.RE
.IP
returns the hashes that satisfy BOTH conditions.
It is a conjunction, not a choice, and a hash missing either field does not
match.
The order of the conditions does not change the answer.
.IP
Doing the same with two scans and an intersection in the client costs two
walks and the transfer of both result sets.
Measured over 50,000 records, where each condition keeps about a quarter and the
pair keeps a fifteenth: 96.5 ms and 25,841 keys, against 37.8 ms and 3,335.
One scan is also the only way to get an answer that was true at one moment.
Two scans are two walks of a keyspace that may have changed between them.
.IP
A value predicate given before any
.I field
tests the key's own value, as it does without
.IR field .
Mixing the two kinds in one scan matches nothing, because no key is both a
string and a hash.
.IP
.B Asking what a FIELD of a hash holds.
.I field
walks the hashes and matches
.I valuematch
against the value of the named field in each hash, not against the key's own
value.
That is the question a database is asked, for the layout records are usually
kept in.
A hash WITHOUT the field does not match, not even a predicate that matches
everything.
That is a different answer from a hash whose field is empty; to match the empty
field, use
.IR "valuematch \(dq\(dq" .
With
.IR withvalues ,
the field's value is returned beside the key.
Done from outside, this is one
.B hget
per hash key: measured at 15.8 ms against 1.5 ms over 5,000 hashes, against a
client already pipelining and already using
.IR type .
.IP
.B Returning the value beside the key.
.I withvalues
returns keys and values interleaved, the way
.B hscan
returns fields and values.
So a client that wanted the records does not send a second
.B mget
for them: the walk had the value in hand when it matched the key.
It is limited to strings for the same reason as
.IR valuematch .
Measured as one page used as a query, it saves about a sixth of the operation,
because two round trips become one.
Over a whole keyspace it saves less, because the bytes move either way and only
the second lookup and the extra commands go away.
Note that the reply is then sized by VALUES rather than by keys.
That is why
.I count
keeps its small default here, and only a value predicate raises it.
.IP
.B Restricting to one type.
.I type
takes one of
.BR string ", " list ", " set ", " hash " or " zset
in any case, and returns only keys of that type.
It costs almost nothing.
A key's type is the map it is stored in, and the walk visits the five maps in a
fixed order anyway, so a restriction walks one map instead of five.
A name that is not a type matches nothing, not everything.
.IP
.B Keep the options constant across an iteration.
.IR match ", " count ", " valuematch " and " type
belong to the whole walk, not to one call.
The cursor names the map the walk has reached.
So a cursor from a restricted walk, handed back without the restriction,
resumes partway through the keyspace and never returns the keys before that
point.
The reverse case is caught: a value or type walk given a cursor from elsewhere
restarts its map rather than trusting a bucket index from a different table.
.IP
.B Filtering on the value.
.I valuematch
returns only the keys whose VALUE matches the glob.
Without it, a client would fetch the whole keyspace and discard nearly all of
it.
Only a string key holds a single value, so a list, set, hash or zset never
matches, not even when it contains the pattern as a member.
With
.I valuematch
present,
.I count
limits the keys EXAMINED rather than the keys returned, and defaults to 1000.
The predicate is selective, so a page limited by results would walk the whole
keyspace in a single call.
This is a scan and not an index.
It costs about 8 per cent more than a plain scan per key examined, and nothing
is maintained on the write path.
.IP
.B What an iteration guarantees.
A key that is present for the whole iteration is returned at least once,
provided the store does not grow enough during it to rehash its table.
A key may be returned more than once.
A key added or removed during the iteration may or may not appear.
So a caller that is counting should count distinct keys, not replies.
.IP
The exception needs a precise statement, because Redis does not have it.
A rehash moves keys between buckets, and a key moved into a bucket that this
iteration has already passed is not returned by it.
Redis avoids that with reverse\-binary iteration, which needs a power\-of\-two
bucket count.
The tables here use prime bucket counts and cannot offer it.
The cursor carries the bucket count, so a rehash is at least noticed, and the
map being walked restarts.
That trades the skip, which is not allowed, for duplicates, which are allowed
above.
.IP
.B count
is a hint, as it is in Redis.
Whole buckets are returned, so a reply may carry somewhat more or fewer keys
than asked for.
.TP
.BI "size " "[type t]"
Return the number of stored keys.
Without
.I type
it counts every type together, which is what
.B DBSIZE
answers over RESP; with
.IR type ,
it counts only that one type.
.IP
Both are O(1): the count of a type is the size of the map it is stored in, so
this is a lookup and not a walk.
Counting the same thing with
.B "scan ... type"
walks the whole map and sends back every key name.
Measured over 50,000 hashes, that took 50.6 ms against 0.107 ms.
The gap widens with the keyspace, because only the scan's cost grows.
A name that is not a type counts nothing, not everything.
.PP
.B Lists (ordered, deque-backed):
.TP
.BI "lpush " "key value"
Insert at front. Returns "ok" or "error: type conflict".
.TP
.BI "rpush " "key value"
Insert at back.
.TP
.BI "lpop " "key"
Remove and return front element. Returns value or "nil".
.TP
.BI "rpop " "key"
Removes and returns the last element of the list.
.TP
.BI "llen " "key"
Returns the length of the list.
.TP
.BI "lindex " "key index"
Returns the element at
.IR index .
A negative index counts from the end.
.TP
.BI "lrange " "key start stop"
Returns a range of elements, one per line, followed by an "end" line.
Negative indices are supported.
.TP
.BI "lset " "key index value"
Replaces the element at
.IR index .
It answers "nil" when the key does not exist, and refuses an index past the end
of the list.
.TP
.BI "lrem " "key count value"
Removes up to
.I count
occurrences of
.I value
and returns how many it removed.
A positive
.I count
removes from the head, a negative one from the tail, and zero removes every
occurrence.
.TP
.BI "ltrim " "key start stop"
Keeps only the elements in the inclusive range and removes the rest.
A range that selects nothing removes the key, because an empty list is not a
key here.
.TP
.BI "linsert " "key before|after pivot value"
Inserts
.I value
before or after the first occurrence of
.IR pivot .
It returns the new length of the list, 0 when the key does not exist, and \-1
when
.I pivot
is not found.
.TP
.BI "blpop " "key [key ...] timeout"
.TQ
.BI "brpop " "key [key ...] timeout"
These work like
.B lpop
and
.BR rpop ,
but wait when every list is empty.
The keys are tried in the order given, and the first key that holds an element
answers at once.
When none does, the caller waits until something is pushed or
.I timeout
seconds pass.
A timeout of
.B 0
waits forever.
The reply names the key as well as the element, because with several keys the
caller could not otherwise tell which key the element came from.
.IP
.B The longest-waiting client is served first.
A worker pool that served its newest waiting member first would starve the
member that has been idle longest, and that member is the one most likely to
be healthy.
.IP
.B Everything pipelined behind a blocking pop waits for it.
A blocking pop is the only command here that answers on a later turn than the
one it arrived on.
The rest of that connection's input therefore stays unparsed until the wait
ends; otherwise the second reply would overtake the first.
.IP
.B Not available on a sharded cache, and refused by name.
Every shard is a separate process.
The client would wait in one shard, while the key it waits on can be owned by
another.
The protocol between shards carries answers to questions; it does not carry a
push to a shard that never asked for one.
The command is therefore refused, as
.B replicate
is, rather than blocking on a key this process will never see written.
.IP
Replication carries the pop, not the wait.
A follower running
.B blpop
would wait for its own push and would never see the leader's.
.TP
.BI "rpoplpush " "source destination"
.TQ
.BI "lmove " "source destination left|right left|right"
Moves one element from one end of
.I source
to one end of
.I destination
and returns it.
.B rpoplpush
is the older name for
.BR "lmove src dst right left" .
The two keys may be the same, which rotates the list.
.IP
.B Every refusal happens before the element is taken.
This is the move a reliable queue uses: a job leaves the pending list and
arrives in the processing list.
The pattern exists because a worker can die between those two steps.
A wrong-typed destination, a memory limit and a destination on another shard
are therefore all checked first, so the element is never missing from both
lists.
.IP
On a sharded cache, both keys must hash to the same shard.
Otherwise the command answers
.BR \-CROSSSLOT ,
which is Redis's answer, rather than crossing to the other shard.
A cross-shard move would be a write in one process that a refusal in another
process could not undo.
.PP
.B Sets (unique unordered members):
.TP
.BI "sadd " "key member"
Adds a member. Returns "ok", "exists", or "error: type conflict".
.TP
.BI "srem " "key member"
Removes a member. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "sismember " "key member"
Checks whether a member is in the set. Returns "1" or "0".
.TP
.BI "scard " "key"
Returns the number of members in the set (its cardinality).
.TP
.BI "spop " "key"
Removes and returns one member.
The member is the first one that this process's iteration order reaches.
The command is therefore replicated as the
.B srem
of that member, not as itself: a follower running
.B spop
would pick a different member.
.TP
.BI "srandmember " "key"
Returns the member that
.B spop
would take, without removing it.
.TP
.BI "smismember " "key member [member ...]"
Returns 1 or 0 for each member, in the order the members were given.
.TP
.BI "smembers " "key"
Returns all members, one per line, followed by an "end" line.
.PP
.B Hashes (nested key-value maps):
.TP
.BI "hset " "key field value"
Sets a hash field. Returns "ok", "error: type conflict", or
"error: out of memory".
.TP
.BI "hget " "key field"
Returns the value of a hash field, or "nil".
.TP
.BI "hdel " "key field"
Deletes a hash field. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "hlen " "key"
Returns the number of fields in the hash.
.TP
.BI "hgetall " "key"
Returns all fields and values, one "field value\\n" line per field, followed by
an "end" line.
.TP
.BI "hkeys " "key"
.TQ
.BI "hvals " "key"
.B hkeys
returns only the fields, and
.B hvals
returns only the values.
.TP
.BI "hmget " "key field [field ...]"
Returns one value per field, with "nil" for a field that does not exist.
.TP
.BI "hmset " "key field value [field value ...]"
Sets several fields at once.
.TP
.BI "hexists " "key field"
Returns 1 if the field exists and 0 if it does not.
.TP
.BI "hstrlen " "key field"
Returns the length of a field's value, or 0 when the field does not exist.
.TP
.BI "hincrby " "key field delta"
Adds
.I delta
to a field read as an integer and returns the new value.
A missing field is created with the value
.IR delta .
.TP
.BI "hsetnx " "key field value"
Sets the field only if it does not exist. Returns 1 or 0.
.TP
.BI "hscan " "key cursor" " [match " pattern "] [count " n "] [valuematch " glob "]"
.TQ
.BI "sscan " "key cursor" " [match " pattern "] [count " n "]"
.TQ
.BI "zscan " "key cursor" " [match " pattern "] [count " n "]"
These commands work like
.BR scan ,
but walk one key's collection instead of the keyspace.
Start with cursor 0 and pass back the returned cursor on each call; a returned
cursor of 0 means the walk is done.
.I valuematch
exists only on
.BR hscan :
it matches what a field holds, while
.I match
matches the field's name.
A set's members and a sorted set's scores are not values in that sense, so
.B sscan
and
.B zscan
answer a syntax error for it instead of pretending.
One option that meant three different things would be worse than one option
fewer.
.I count
is a hint and defaults to 10, as on Redis.
A collection smaller than one bucket-load comes back whole, with cursor 0.
.IP
The cursor encodes the collection's bucket count as well as its position.
A resumed walk can therefore tell whether the table is still the one it left.
A rehash between two calls restarts the walk rather than silently skipping the
elements that moved.
.IP
.B The order of fields is not the order they were set in.
A hash is backed by an unordered map here, so
.BR hgetall ", " hkeys " and " hvals
return the fields in an order this process decides.
Redis keeps insertion order for a small hash because it stores a small hash as
a listpack.
It stops keeping that order once the hash grows into a hashtable.
A client that depends on the order therefore depends on Redis's encoding, not
on its contract.
.PP
.B Sorted sets:
.PP
A sorted set holds a score for each member and is read back in score order.
The type is exclusive, like the other four: a key that holds a string refuses
.BR zadd .
.TP
.BI "zadd " "key score member"
Adds a member or changes its score.
Returns "1" when the member is new, and "0" when it already existed and only
its score changed.
A score may be fractional.
The whole score is parsed, so
.B 1x
is refused rather than read as 1.
.TP
.BI "zrem " "key member"
Removes a member. Returns "ok" or "nil".
The key is removed when its last member is.
.TP
.BI "zscore " "key member"
Returns the member's score, or "nil".
.TP
.BI "zcard " "key"
Returns the number of members.
.TP
.BI "zrank " "key member"
Returns the member's zero-based rank, counting from the lowest score, or "nil".
.TP
.BI "zrevrank " "key member"
Returns the same rank as
.BR zrank ,
counted from the highest score.
.TP
.BI "zrange " "key start stop" " [withscores]"
Returns members by rank, with both ends inclusive.
Negative indices count from the last member, following the same rules as
.BR lrange .
With
.BR withscores ,
each line is "member score".
The reply ends with "end".
.TP
.BI "zrevrange " "key start stop" " [withscores]"
Returns the same as
.BR zrange ,
walked from the highest score.
.TP
.BI "zincrby " "key delta member"
Adds
.I delta
to a member's score and returns the new score.
A missing member is created with the score
.IR delta .
.TP
.BI "zrangebyscore " "key min max" " [withscores]"
.TQ
.BI "zrevrangebyscore " "key max min" " [withscores]"
Returns the members whose score falls in the range.
This is the query a sorted set is kept for.
Both ends are inclusive.
A leading
.B (
makes an end exclusive, and
.BR \-inf " and " +inf
are the open ends.
.B zrevrangebyscore
takes its bounds the other way round, as on Redis.
.TP
.BI "zcount " "key min max"
Returns how many members fall in the score range, with the same bound syntax.
.TP
.BI "zpopmin " "key"
.TQ
.BI "zpopmax " "key"
Removes and returns the lowest-scoring member, or the highest, with its score.
Each is replicated as the
.B zrem
of that member, for the reason given under
.BR spop .
.TP
.BI "zremrangebyrank " "key start stop"
.TQ
.BI "zremrangebyscore " "key min max"
Removes a range of members by rank or by score, and returns how many it
removed.
Emptying the set removes the key.
.PP
.B Streams:
.PP
The stream commands follow Redis 7.0: the same arguments, answers and errors.
The line protocol splits words on spaces, so a field or value cannot contain a
space there.
It answers an array one element per line, followed by "end".
An empty stream is still a key, as in Redis.
.TP
.BI "xadd " "key [nomkstream] [maxlen|minid [=|~] n [limit n]] *|id field value ..."
Appends an entry to the stream and returns its id.
.B *
takes the clock in milliseconds.
When the clock has not moved past the last id, it takes the last id instead,
with its sequence counted up.
.I ms\-*
asks for the next sequence in that millisecond.
An id at or below the last one is refused.
Replicas and the journal receive the id the entry got, never
.BR * .
.B maxlen ~
removes only whole blocks of up to a hundred entries, as Redis removes whole
nodes, so it can leave more entries than asked for.
.TP
.BI "xlen " "key"
Returns the number of entries.
.TP
.BI "xrange " "key start end [count n]"
.TQ
.BI "xrevrange " "key end start [count n]"
Returns entries in id order, or in reverse order, one per line as
"id field value ...".
.B \-
and
.B +
are the ends of the stream.
A lone millisecond takes every sequence in it, and a leading
.B (
makes a bound exclusive.
.TP
.BI "xdel " "key id [id ...]"
Removes entries and returns how many of them existed.
.TP
.BI "xtrim " "key maxlen|minid [=|~] n [limit n]"
Removes entries from the oldest end and returns how many it removed.
.TP
.BI "xread " "[count n] [block ms] streams key [key ...] id [id ...]"
Returns the entries past each given id, one per line as
"key id field value ...".
.B $
means the last id at the time of the read.
When there is nothing yet,
.B block
waits for the next
.B xadd
to any of the keys; 0 waits forever.
A blocking read inside
.B multi
answers "nil" at once, and a script may not wait at all.
On a sharded cache every key must belong to one shard.
A blocking read is served only by the shard that owns its streams, through
that shard's
.B \-\-cluster
port.
.TP
.BI "xgroup " "create key group id|$ [mkstream] [entriesread n]"
.TQ
.BI "xgroup " "setid|destroy|createconsumer|delconsumer key group ..."
Creates, moves or removes a consumer group, or one of its consumers.
.TP
.BI "xreadgroup " "group g consumer [count n] [block ms] [noack] streams key ... id ..."
Reads entries for one consumer of a group.
.B >
delivers the entries the group has not delivered yet, and records them as
pending for the consumer unless
.B noack
is given.
Any other id re-reads that consumer's own pending entries.
.TP
.BI "xack " "key group id [id ...]"
Removes entries from the pending list and returns how many were on it.
.TP
.BI "xpending " "key group [[idle ms] start end count [consumer]]"
Shows the pending list: either a summary, or entries with their consumer, idle
time and delivery count.
.TP
.BI "xclaim " "key group consumer min-idle id ... [idle ms] [time ms] [retrycount n] [force] [justid] [lastid id]"
.TQ
.BI "xautoclaim " "key group consumer min-idle start [count n] [justid]"
Takes over entries that another consumer left pending.
.B xautoclaim
walks the pending list from
.IR start ,
and its answer also includes the ids that
.B xdel
removed from under the group.
.TP
.BI "xinfo " "stream key [full [count n]] | groups key | consumers key group"
Reports what a stream, its groups, or a group's consumers hold.
.TP
.BI "xsetid " "key id [entriesadded n] [maxdeletedid id]"
Sets the stream's last id and counters.
This is how a replica is told them.
.PP
.B Pattern subscriptions:
.TP
.BI "psubscribe " "pattern [pattern ...]"
.TQ
.BI "punsubscribe " "pattern [pattern ...]"
Subscribes to, or unsubscribes from, every channel that a glob pattern matches.
Patterns use
.BR * ,
.B ?
and character classes, the same matching that
.B keys
uses.
Messages arrive as
.BR pmessage ,
which carries the pattern as well as the channel.
A client subscribed to two patterns could not otherwise tell which one matched.
.IP
.B Over RESP, every subscribe verb confirms with a count.
.BR subscribe ", " unsubscribe ", " psubscribe " and " punsubscribe
each answer a three\-element reply: the verb, the channel or pattern, and how
many subscriptions the connection now holds.
A client library counts down from that number to know that its own batch is
complete.
.IP
.B A pattern costs a sharded cache its publish filter, and only then.
Normally a publish wakes only the shards whose filter says they may hold a
subscriber of that channel.
A pattern matches channels that do not exist yet, so it has no bit to set in
the filter.
A shard that holds a pattern subscription is therefore woken by every publish.
Nothing changes for a cache that has no pattern subscriber.
.PP
.B Transactions with a check:
.TP
.BI "watch " "key [key ...]"
.TQ
.B unwatch
.B watch
makes the next
.B exec
do nothing if a watched key is written between the
.B watch
and the
.BR exec .
Over RESP the reply is then a nil
.IR array ;
its type is how a client tells "did not run" from "ran and returned nothing".
Over the text protocol the reply is the word
.BR aborted .
Either way the watch ends, so a retry starts with a fresh watch.
.IP
Without a watch,
.B multi
is only a batch: the commands run together, and nothing checks that the data
still looks the way the client read it.
With a watch, the compare\-and\-set loop that a distributed lock or a counter
needs becomes possible.
.IP
.B A write to a different key does not abort.
The watch mark is set in one place, before the switch that runs the command.
It is driven by a list of read\-only commands, not by a list of writes.
A write missing from a list of writes would let a transaction commit silently
over a change it never saw.
A read missing from the read\-only list costs only one retry too many.
Commands that can touch any key mark every watcher instead of using the list:
.BR mset ", " del
over several keys,
.BR flush " and " eval .
.IP
.B watch
inside
.B multi
is refused, as on Redis.
A watch taken after queueing started could not be checked against the reads
that decided what to queue.
.PP
.B Scripting:
.PP
The cache runs Lua scripts that the client sends, over RESP.
.TP
.BI "eval " "script numkeys key... arg..."
.TQ
.BI "evalsha " "sha1 numkeys key... arg..."
Runs a Lua script, given as source or by its SHA1.
Inside
.IR script ,
the declared keys are in
.B KEYS
and the remaining arguments in
.BR ARGV ,
both 1-based.
.B redis.call
runs a command and raises its answer if that answer is an error.
.B redis.pcall
returns the error as a table with an
.B err
field instead.
.BR redis.status_reply ", " redis.error_reply " and " redis.sha1hex
are available.
.B redis.log
is accepted and writes nothing.
.IP
A command that would outlive the script on the caller's connection is refused
with
.BR "\-ERR This Redis command is not allowed from script" ,
as Redis refuses it.
These commands are
.BR eval ", " evalsha ", " script ", " multi ", " exec ", " discard ,
.BR watch ", " unwatch ,
the four subscribe commands,
.BR quit ", " reset ", " hello ", " auth " and " client .
A blocking pop answers at once, as its non-blocking twin would.
.IP
The return value is converted as Redis converts it:
.IP \(bu 2
A number becomes an integer, truncated towards zero.
.IP \(bu 2
A string becomes a bulk reply.
.IP \(bu 2
A table becomes an array
.IR "up to its first hole" ,
which is Lua's own rule for where an array ends.
.IP \(bu 2
.B false
becomes a nil, and
.B true
becomes the integer 1.
.IP
A table that carries
.B err
or
.B ok
becomes an error or a status rather than a list.
.IP
.B The interpreter is not the one \-\-lua uses, and that is the point.
.B COMMON FLAGS
says that the
.B \-\-lua
interpreter is not a sandbox: it has
.BR io ", " os ", " package
and the whole of
.B base
open.
That is the right trade for a script the operator wrote, and the wrong one for
a script that arrived over the network.
The script interpreter is opened the other way round: it has only
.BR base ", " string ", " table " and " math ,
with every loader removed by name.
Under LuaJIT the library that decides this is
.BR ffi :
.B require("ffi")
gives arbitrary memory access, which amounts to a shell on the machine.
.B require
is therefore not there to reach it with.
A client's script also cannot see the operator's globals, because the two
states never meet.
.IP
.B A script holds the event loop of its shard while it runs.
It therefore runs under the same count hook as a Lua callback.
A runaway script is stopped with
.B "a script exceeded the 10000ms deadline"
instead of holding every other client on that shard forever.
The limit is
.B callback_timeout_ms
or
.BR SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS ,
the same as for callbacks.
.IP
.B On a sharded cache a script is routed by the keys it declares.
This is the routing itself, not a convention: the shard that owns
.B KEYS[1]
runs the script.
Keys that belong to two shards answer
.BR \-CROSSSLOT ,
and a script that declares no keys runs on the shard it was sent to.
A script that reaches a key it did not declare reaches into whatever store the
shard it landed on happens to have.
.TP
.BI "script load " "script"
.TQ
.BI "script exists " "sha1 [sha1 ...]"
.TQ
.B "script flush"
These commands manage the script cache.
.B eval
loads a script as well as running it.
A client can therefore try
.B evalsha
first and fall back to
.B eval
on
.BR \-NOSCRIPT ,
which is the usual pattern.
.IP
.B The cache is per shard, as it is per node on Redis Cluster.
A sharded cache's shared port hands each connection to whichever shard the
kernel picks, so a
.B script load
reaches only one shard.
.B eval
makes this self-healing.
A client that wants
.B evalsha
to work on every shard sends
.B script load
to each per-shard port, as a cluster client does with each master.
.PP
.B Connection and introspection:
.PP
A client library sends these commands before it sends any of its own.
Answering them lets the connection finish being set up.
Refusing one by name lets the client fall back instead of failing.
.TP
.BI "hello " "[protover]"
Performs the protocol handshake.
Only RESP2 is supported.
.B hello
with no argument or with
.B 2
answers a flat array of name and value pairs: the server, the version, the protocol, this connection's id,
whether the cache is clustered, and whether it is a replica.
.B hello 3
is refused with
.BR \-NOPROTO .
That is what Redis before version 6 answers, and every client falls back from
it.
An unknown-command error, by contrast, is not a protocol negotiation failure,
and a client need not handle it as one.
.TP
.B info
Returns the fields a monitor reads.
They are the version, connected clients, memory, commands processed, role,
whether clustering is on, and the key count.
On the shared port of a sharded cache the clients, memory and key count are the
whole cache's, added up from what each shard publishes, and maxmemory is the
value it was given.
On a per-shard port they are
.I that shard's
slice and share, for the same reason that
.B dbsize
answers that way there.
.IP
The replication section differs by role, because half of its fields have no
meaning on the other side.
.B role
answers
.BR master " or " slave .
A follower adds
.BR master_host ,
.BR master_port ,
.B master_link_status
and
.BR master_sync_in_progress .
A leader adds
.BR connected_slaves :
the number of followers whose connection is live, not the number that ever
attached.
.IP
.B master_link_status
reads
.B up
once a full sync has finished, not when the socket opens.
.B master_sync_in_progress
tells apart the two reasons it can read
.BR down :
no leader was reached at all, or a dump is still arriving.
A follower whose target was never usable leaves
.B master_host
empty and
.B master_port
at zero, which is the honest answer to "where".
.TP
.BI "config get " "parameter"
Returns the value of a configuration parameter.
.B maxmemory
answers with the configured limit.
.BR save " and " appendonly
answer empty, which is accurate here.
Any other parameter answers an empty array rather than an invented value.
.TP
.B command
Answers with an empty array.
A client asks
.B command
for a table of arity and flags.
This cache keeps no such table, and saying so is more useful than a table that
is wrong.
.TP
.BI "echo " "message"
.TQ
.BI "select " "index"
.B select 0
succeeds, because the cache has a single keyspace.
Any other index is refused rather than ignored.
A client addressing database 3 therefore learns that it cannot, rather than
quietly sharing database 0 with everybody else.
.TP
.BI "client " "id|getname|setname|setinfo"
Answers the client subcommands that Redis client libraries send.
.B setname
and
.B setinfo
are accepted; redis\-py and node\-redis send them on connect.
.B id
answers this connection's descriptor.
.TP
.B auth
The cache refuses this command by name, because a cache has no password.
A silent success would tell a client it had authenticated.
.PP
.B Transactions:
.PP
A transaction groups commands; it does not roll them back.
Between
.B multi
and
.BR exec ,
each command is queued and answered "queued".
Nothing has run yet, and a second connection reading the same key still sees
the old value.
EXEC then runs the queue back to back on the single\-threaded event loop, so no
other connection's command lands in the middle of it.
A command that fails inside
.B exec
fails alone, and the commands before it stay applied.
.TP
.B multi
Begins queueing commands. A nested
.B multi
is refused.
.TP
.B exec
Runs the queue in order, then answers "end". It is refused without a
.BR multi .
.TP
.B discard
Throws the queued commands away.
.PP
.B TTL / Expiry:
.TP
.BI "expire " "key seconds"
Sets a key's time-to-live in seconds.
Returns "ok", or "nil" when the key is not found.
A
.I seconds
value of zero or less deletes the key rather than being an error: the key is
removed and the answer is "ok".
Redis does the same, and it is what a caller means when its own arithmetic came
out at zero.
.TP
.BI "ttl " "key"
Returns the remaining seconds, -1 if the key has no TTL, or -2 if it is not
found.
.TP
.BI "persist " "key"
Removes a key's expiry. Returns "ok" or "nil".
.TP
.BI "pexpire " "key milliseconds"
Sets a key's time-to-live in milliseconds.
Returns "1" when it is set, or "0" when the key is not found.
Zero or less deletes the key, as with
.BR expire .
.TP
.BI "pttl " "key"
Returns the remaining TTL in milliseconds, -1 if the key has no TTL, or -2 if
it is not found.
.TP
.BI "expireat " "key unix_seconds"
Sets a key's expiry to an absolute Unix timestamp in seconds.
Returns "1", or "0" if there is no such key.
A timestamp already in the past deletes the key and answers "1".
.TP
.BI "pexpireat " "key unix_ms"
Sets a key's expiry to an absolute Unix timestamp in milliseconds.
It gives the same answers as
.BR expireat .
.PP
All four commands saturate out-of-range deadlines rather than refusing them.
A deadline beyond one end of the clock's range means "never", and one beyond
the other end means "already gone".
Redis refuses these with an error instead.
This is the one place where this family answers differently from Redis on
purpose: the number a client sent is honoured as far as the clock goes, rather
than rejected.
.PP
.B Pub/Sub:
.TP
.BI "subscribe " "channel"
Subscribes to a channel. Returns "ok". Messages are delivered as
"message <channel> <payload>\\n".
.TP
.BI "unsubscribe " "channel"
Unsubscribes from a channel. Returns "ok".
.TP
.BI "publish " "channel message"
Publishes a message to all subscribers of the channel.
Returns the number of subscribers reached.
.PP
.B Memory Management:
.TP
.BI "maxmemory " ""
Returns the current maxmemory setting in bytes, or "0" for unlimited.
.TP
.BI "memory " ""
Returns the current memory usage in bytes.
.PP
.B Admin (requires --mode admin):
.TP
.BI "flush " "[path]"
Saves the cache to a file.
Without
.IR path ,
it uses the \fB\-\-persistent\fR path.
.TP
.BI "load " "[path]"
Loads the cache from a file.
.PP
Type enforcement: a key can hold only one data type.
A write to a key that holds a different type returns "error: type conflict".
Under \fB\-\-maxmemory\fR, a write that does not fit is refused with "error: out of memory"
(over RESP: "OOM command not allowed when used memory > 'maxmemory'").
The key it would have written is left exactly as it was.
This includes a write over a key that already exists: overwriting a value,
appending to it or replacing a hash field is checked against the limit like
any other write.
Multi-line responses (lrange, smembers, hgetall) end with "end\\n".
.SS RESP Protocol (Redis Compatibility)
The cache supports the RESP2 wire protocol, so Redis clients (redis-cli and
Redis client libraries) can use it.
RESP mode is detected automatically when a connection's first byte is '*', and
can be forced with
.BR \-\-resp .
.PP
The supported Redis commands are listed below; command names are
case-insensitive.
A command not listed here is answered with an error that names it, never
silently ignored.
.PP
.B Strings
.RS 2
SET (EX, PX, NX, XX, KEEPTTL, GET), GET, GETSET, GETDEL, GETRANGE, SUBSTR,
SETRANGE, SETNX, SETEX, PSETEX, MGET, MSET, APPEND, STRLEN, INCR, DECR,
INCRBY, DECRBY, INCRBYFLOAT
.RE
.PP
.B Keys
.RS 2
DEL, UNLINK, EXISTS, TOUCH, TYPE, KEYS, SCAN, DBSIZE, RANDOMKEY, RENAME,
RENAMENX, COPY, FLUSHALL, FLUSHDB, EXPIRE, PEXPIRE, EXPIREAT, PEXPIREAT, TTL,
PTTL, EXPIRETIME, PEXPIRETIME, PERSIST
.PP
RENAME and COPY carry the key's expiry with them, so renaming or copying
cannot make an expiring key permanent.
RENAME and COPY name two keys.
On a sharded cache both keys must belong to one shard, and keys that do not are
refused with
.BR \-CROSSSLOT ;
a key cannot be half-moved.
FLUSHALL and FLUSHDB are the same command here, because this cache has one
keyspace and no SELECT-able databases to leave behind.
.RE
.PP
.B Lists
.RS 2
LPUSH, RPUSH, LPOP, RPOP, LLEN, LRANGE, LINDEX, LSET, LREM, LTRIM, LINSERT,
LMOVE, RPOPLPUSH, BLPOP, BRPOP
.RE
.PP
.B Sets
.RS 2
SADD, SREM, SISMEMBER, SMISMEMBER, SCARD, SMEMBERS, SPOP, SRANDMEMBER, SSCAN,
SINTER, SUNION, SDIFF, SINTERSTORE, SUNIONSTORE, SDIFFSTORE, SMOVE
.PP
Set algebra reads whole sets and returns one result.
On a sharded cache it therefore cannot be split across shards the way MGET or
DEL can: an intersection computed separately on two shards is not an
intersection.
Every key must belong to one shard, and keys that do not are refused with
.BR \-CROSSSLOT .
Redis Cluster answers the same, for the same reason.
Use a hash tag
.RB ( "{user}:seen" )
to place the keys together.
.RE
.PP
.B Hashes
.RS 2
HSET, HSETNX, HGET, HMSET, HMGET, HDEL, HLEN, HEXISTS, HKEYS, HVALS, HGETALL,
HSTRLEN, HINCRBY, HSCAN
.RE
.PP
.B Sorted sets
.RS 2
ZADD, ZREM, ZSCORE, ZCARD, ZCOUNT, ZINCRBY, ZRANK, ZREVRANK, ZRANGE,
ZREVRANGE, ZRANGEBYSCORE, ZREVRANGEBYSCORE, ZPOPMIN, ZPOPMAX,
ZREMRANGEBYRANK, ZREMRANGEBYSCORE, ZSCAN
.RE
.PP
.B Streams
.RS 2
XADD, XLEN, XRANGE, XREVRANGE, XREAD (BLOCK), XDEL, XTRIM, XSETID,
XGROUP (CREATE, SETID, DESTROY, CREATECONSUMER, DELCONSUMER), XREADGROUP (BLOCK,
NOACK), XACK, XPENDING, XCLAIM, XAUTOCLAIM, XINFO (STREAM [FULL], GROUPS,
CONSUMERS)
.PP
Replies and errors match Redis 7.0's;
.B test_cache_streams_vs_redis.sh
compares them against a redis-server.
The radix-tree-nodes field of XINFO STREAM counts no tree, because the entries
are stored in blocks indexed by a sorted array.
XADD, XGROUP CREATE and CREATECONSUMER are refused with OOM at the memory
limit, as Redis refuses its denyoom commands.
XREADGROUP and XCLAIM are not refused, which is also Redis's rule.
An XREAD naming streams on several shards is answered
.BR \-CROSSSLOT .
.RE
.PP
.B Pub/Sub
.RS 2
SUBSCRIBE, UNSUBSCRIBE, PSUBSCRIBE, PUNSUBSCRIBE, PUBLISH
.RE
.PP
.B Transactions
.RS 2
MULTI, EXEC, DISCARD, WATCH, UNWATCH
.RE
.PP
.B Scripting
.RS 2
EVAL, EVALSHA, SCRIPT LOAD, SCRIPT EXISTS, SCRIPT FLUSH
.RE
.PP
.B Connection and server
.RS 2
PING, ECHO, AUTH, HELLO, SELECT, QUIT, RESET, CLIENT (GETNAME, SETNAME, ID,
REPLY, SETINFO), CONFIG GET/SET, COMMAND, INFO, CLUSTER
.RE
.PP
.B Where the answers differ from Redis on purpose
.RS 2
The RESP array limit is 1024 arguments, not 1024*1024.
args[] is a stack array, and a million views would take sixteen megabytes of
it.
A command over the limit is refused with an error that names the limit, and
the connection is closed.
The stream is framed, but the arguments were never stored, so the connection
cannot continue past a message it did not take.
.PP
Lua scripts share one interpreter state, so its globals are sealed.
Writing a global raises an error that names the variable.
The globals table itself is unnamed, so rawset and table.insert cannot reach
it either.
Use
.B local
for everything, including functions: write
.B "local function f() end"
rather than
.BR "function f() end" .
Redis requires this too.
.PP
One difference from Redis remains on purpose: reading an undefined global
returns nil here, as in ordinary Lua, where Redis raises an error.
Blocking the WRITE is what closes the channel between two clients.
An error on the read would only catch typos, and it would break a script that
tests a global to see whether it is there.
.RE
.PP
Example with redis-cli:
.nf
redis-cli -p 9000 SET mykey myval
redis-cli -p 9000 GET mykey
redis-cli -p 9000 EVAL "return redis.call('GET', KEYS[1])" 1 mykey
redis-cli -p 9000 SUBSCRIBE news
.fi
.SS Database Backend
Cache runtimes support four Lua callbacks for a database-backed cache.
Attach a Lua script with
.BR \-\-lua
and implement the callbacks you need with any Lua database library
(luasql-mysql, luasql-postgres, lsqlite3, etc.).
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
l l lw(30).
Callback|Signature|When called
_
on_miss(key)|\(-> value [, ttl_seconds]|GET returns nil \(em fetch from DB
on_write(key, value, ttl)|\(-> nothing|T{
.ad l
After successful SET/SETEX/SETNX/MSET
T}
on_delete(key)|\(-> nothing|After DEL \(em delete from DB
on_expire(key)|\(-> nothing|After TTL sweep removes key
.TE
.PP
\fBon_miss\fR may return two values (a Lua multi-return): the fetched string
and an optional TTL in seconds.
If the value is nil or empty, the GET returns nil to the client as usual.
If a value is returned, the cache stores it and returns it to the client
transparently (read-through, or lazy population).
.PP
\fBon_write\fR is called synchronously on the event loop thread.
For write-heavy loads, use the write-behind pattern: buffer writes in Lua
inside \fBon_write\fR, and flush them to the database in \fBon_tick\fR every
few seconds.
This decouples SET latency from database latency entirely.
.PP
Example (SQLite write-behind):
.nf
tick_ms = 2000
local db, pending = nil, {}

function on_start()
    local sq = require("lsqlite3")
    db = sq.open("/tmp/cache.db")
    db:exec("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS cache_kv (key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, value TEXT)")
end

function on_miss(key)
    for row in db:nrows("SELECT value FROM cache_kv WHERE key=" .. db:quote(key)) do
        return row.value, 300  -- value + 300 s TTL
    end
end

function on_write(key, value, ttl)
    pending[key] = value  -- buffer
end

function on_tick(dt)
    for k, v in pairs(pending) do
        db:exec("INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cache_kv(key,value) VALUES("..db:quote(k)..","..db:quote(v)..")")
    end
    pending = {}
end

function on_delete(key)
    db:exec("DELETE FROM cache_kv WHERE key="..db:quote(key))
end
.fi
.PP
See
.B examples/cache/db-backend.lua
for a full example with MySQL, Postgres, and SQLite backends.
.SS Replication
Cache runtimes support leader-follower replication.
.PP
A follower connects to the leader and receives a full data dump.
After that, the leader forwards ongoing write commands in real time.
Followers are read-only.
.PP
.nf
# Start leader cache
socketley create cache leader -p 9000 -s

# Start follower
socketley create cache follower -p 9001 --replicate 127.0.0.1:9000 -s

# Writes on leader are replicated to follower
echo "set key val" | nc localhost 9000
echo "get key" | nc localhost 9001    # Returns "val"
.fi
.PP
Example with netcat:
.nf
echo "set user:1 alice" | nc localhost 9000
echo "get user:1" | nc localhost 9000
echo "lpush queue task1" | nc localhost 9000
echo "hset user:1 name alice" | nc localhost 9000
echo "expire user:1 300" | nc localhost 9000
echo "subscribe news" | nc localhost 9000
.fi
.SH STORAGE FLAGS
A
.B storage
runtime serves the S3 API over one directory.
Unmodified S3 clients (boto3, the AWS CLI, rclone, SDKs) can then put, get,
list and delete objects on this machine's disk:
.PP
.nf
socketley create storage media \-p 9000 \-\-dir /srv/objects \\
    \-\-access\-key AKIAEXAMPLE \-\-secret\-key wJalrXUtnFEMI \-s
.fi
.PP
Like every other runtime, it runs in a worker process of its own.
It speaks HTTP/1.1 with path\-style addressing (\fB/bucket/key\fR).
Given
.BR \-\-domain ,
it also accepts virtual\-hosted style (\fBbucket.domain/key\fR).
It answers errors as S3 XML (\fB<Error><Code>\fR\|.\|.\|.) and stamps every
reply with an
.BR x\-amz\-request\-id .
The flags below are the runtime's own.
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-listen ,
.BR \-\-tls ,
.BR \-\-cert ,
.BR \-\-key ,
.BR \-\-ca ,
.BR \-\-cpus ,
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
and the common flags apply as documented under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
Sets the directory that holds the buckets.
It is required, is created if it does not exist, and is made absolute against
the caller's directory.
A runtime is one process on one directory or, with
.BR \-\-shards ,
several processes that share it.
Two runtimes on one path would append to each other's journals.
.TP
.BI \-\-access\-key " key"
.TQ
.BI \-\-secret\-key " secret"
Sets the one pair of credentials that requests are signed with (AWS Signature
Version 4).
The two flags go together: one without the other is refused.
The pair is kept in the state file and printed by
.BR show ;
prefer
.B \-\-credentials
where that matters.
.TP
.BI \-\-credentials " file"
Names a file of several credential pairs, one
.IB access : secret
per line.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are skipped.
The secret is everything after the first colon, so the secret may itself
contain a colon.
A line
.BI [ id ]
begins a user; that user's settings and keys follow it until the next such
line.
A file without one has a single owner, as described under
.BR Users .
The path is made absolute against the caller's directory.
.IP
The file is read at start.
.B create
and
.B start
refuse it if it does not parse or holds nothing.
The runtime reads the file again when it changes, checking at most once a
second, on the next request:
.IP \(bu 2
A key added to the file works at once.
.IP \(bu 2
A key taken out of the file stops working.
.IP \(bu 2
A file that has gone, is empty or no longer parses leaves the keys in force
until it is repaired, and a warning is logged.
A half\-written edit therefore locks nobody out.
.IP
A user's policy file is watched the same way.
A reload does not drop connections.
A key given with
.B \-\-access\-key
is part of the same set.
.TP
.BI \-\-sse\-key\-file " file"
Names the file with the master key that wraps every object's data key under
SSE\-S3.
SSE\-S3 is server\-side encryption with S3\-managed keys.
The file holds exactly 32 bytes, or 64 hex digits and an optional newline, and
only its owner may read it.
A file that the group or others may read, or of any other length, is refused at
.B create
and
.BR start .
The key is therefore never on a command line or in the state file; only its
path is, made absolute against the caller's directory.
The file is read at every start.
.IP
Without this flag, a request for
.B AES256
and a bucket default that would need the key are refused with
.BR "501 NotImplemented" .
SSE\-C needs no master key.
Keep the file: an object sealed under one key is not opened by another, and a
request for it is answered
.B "500 InternalError"
saying so. See
.BR "Server\-side encryption" .
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " name" [, name ...]
Sets the domains the endpoint is reached under, such as
.BR s3.example.com .
A request whose
.B Host
is
.IB bucket . domain
(with or without a port) addresses that bucket, and its whole path is the key.
A Host equal to one of the domains, an address, or any other name uses path
style, as without the flag, so both styles are served at once.
Names must be DNS names and are compared without regard to case; anything else
is refused at
.BR create .
See
.BR "Virtual\-hosted\-style addressing" .
.TP
.BI \-\-website\-domain " name" [, name ...]
Sets the domains that buckets' websites are reached under, such as
.BR web.example.com .
A request whose
.B Host
is
.IB bucket . domain
is a browser's request for that bucket's website rather than for the S3 API.
It is answered from the bucket's WebsiteConfiguration.
Names are checked as for
.BR \-\-domain ,
and a Host under both belongs to the website. See
.BR "Website hosting" .
.TP
.BI \-\-fsync " always" | interval | never
Sets when a write that a client was answered for is on the disk.
.IP \(bu 2
.B always
(the default) syncs the object's file and its directory before answering, so
an answered write survives a crash of the machine.
.IP \(bu 2
.B interval
answers without those syncs, and syncs the whole filesystem every
.BR \-\-fsync\-interval .
.IP \(bu 2
.B never
leaves the syncing to the kernel's own writeback.
.IP
In every mode a crash of the process loses nothing, and a stop syncs
everything before it reports that it was clean. See
.BR Durability .
.TP
.BI \-\-sync " never" | active | per\-user
Chooses which
.B sync
runtimes the storage creates to pull its users' git repositories.
.IP \(bu 2
.B never
(the default) creates none.
.IP \(bu 2
.B active
creates one for the whole storage.
.IP \(bu 2
.B per\-user
creates one for each user.
.IP
Each sync runtime is a separate process, which is why none is created unless
asked for.
The setting is applied by
.BR import .
After a change, the runtimes that the previous setting created are removed at
the storage's next start. See
.B Git repositories
under the storage runtime.
.TP
.BI \-\-fsync\-interval " duration"
Sets how often
.B \-\-fsync interval
syncs the filesystem.
The range is 1 second to an hour. Default:
.BR 1s .
.TP
.BI \-\-notify\-targets " file"
Names the file of webhooks that buckets' event notifications may be sent to.
Each line holds one
.IR "name url" .
An optional third field
.BI ca= file
names the CA that an https webhook's certificate is checked against; without
it, the system's CAs are used.
Blank lines are skipped.
A field that begins with
.B #
is skipped together with the rest of its line:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# name   url
audit    http://10.0.0.5:8080/s3\-events
thumbs   https://thumbs.internal/hook ca=/etc/ssl/internal\-ca.pem
feed     cache://events/stream/s3
live     cache://events/channel/s3
panel    server://dashboard
bus      mqtt://broker/storage/events
oncall   mail://m/ops@example.com,dev@example.com
.fi
.RE
.IP
A url may also name another runtime of the daemon:
.IP \(bu 2
.BI cache:// runtime /stream/ key
appends each event to that cache's stream, as an entry with one field,
.BR event .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI cache:// runtime /channel/ channel
publishes each event on that channel.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI server:// runtime
broadcasts each event to the server's clients.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI mqtt:// runtime / topic
publishes each event on that broker's topic at QoS 1.
The delivery counts as answered once the broker holds the event.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI mail:// runtime / address\fR[,\fIaddress\fR...]
mails each event to those addresses through that mail runtime, with a subject
that says what the event is.
The delivery counts as answered once the message is in the spool.
.IP
Such a line takes no
.BR ca= .
An event that a cache refuses, or one for a runtime that is not running, is
retried like a webhook that does not answer.
A configuration names a target as
.BI arn:socketley:webhook::: name\fR.
.IP
.B create
and
.B start
refuse the file if a line does not parse.
The file is read at every start.
Its host names are resolved off the event loop, and again when a connection is
refused.
A host that does not resolve is logged, and its events wait until it resolves.
Without this flag every destination is refused. See
.BR "Event notifications" .
.TP
.BI \-\-replication\-targets " file"
Names the file of S3 endpoints that a bucket's replication may write to.
Each line holds one
.IR "name url region access_key secret" ,
with an optional sixth field
.BI ca= file
for an https endpoint.
Comments and blank lines work as in
.BR \-\-notify\-targets :
.RS
.PP
.nf
# name   url                          region     access key            secret
backup   https://s3.backup.internal   eu\-west\-1  AKIAREPLICATOR000001  wJal.../replicator
.fi
.RE
.IP
A configuration's
.B Role
names a target by its last path segment, as
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :role/ name\fR.
Every copy to that endpoint is signed with the line's key and secret, for the
line's region.
.B create
and
.B start
refuse the file if a line does not parse, and it is read at every start.
.B show
and the state file keep its path, and its secrets are never logged.
Without this flag every replication configuration is refused. See
.BR Replication .
.TP
.B \-\-acls
Makes buckets that have no ownership controls accept ACLs.
S3 behaved this way before it disabled ACLs by default, and RGW still does.
Without the flag, such buckets are BucketOwnerEnforced. See the ACLs under
.BR "Supported S3 operations" .
.TP
.BI \-\-tls\-offloaded " addresses"
Lists the proxies that terminate TLS in front of the runtime.
The entries are comma\-separated addresses or blocks
.RB ( 10.0.0.5 ,
.BR 10.0.0.0/24 ,
IPv6 alike).
A connection from one of them counts as one made over TLS:
.IP \(bu 2
it may carry SSE\-C headers;
.IP \(bu 2
.B aws:SecureTransport
is true for it;
.IP \(bu 2
the URLs the runtime answers with are https.
.IP
The client of such a request is the address that the proxy put last in
.BR X\-Forwarded\-For .
That address is used for
.BR aws:SourceIp ,
the access log and the count of failed sign\-ins, which would otherwise treat
every client behind the proxy as one.
.B create
refuses an entry that is not an address.
.IP
A proxy that speaks HTTP/2 to clients limits an upload to its flow\-control
window per round trip, which is 64 KiB unless raised.
Behind Apache,
.B "H2WindowSize 524288"
in the virtual host raised a browser's upload from 1.2 to 5 MB/s, on a line
that carries 6.
A far larger window costs the player: the browser sends its requests on the
same connection, behind as much of the upload as the window lets it queue.
At 16 MiB a request waited 0.5 to 1.1 s, against 0.05 to 0.1 s at 512 KiB.
.TP
.BI \-\-federation " file"
Names the file with the OIDC provider and LDAP directory whose identities STS
gives temporary credentials to.
The file also names the users those identities sign as.
It is read at every start. See
.BR "Federated identities" .
.TP
.BI \-\-tiers " file"
Names the file of S3 endpoints that a bucket's lifecycle may move versions to.
Each endpoint is a storage class, named by its first field.
The format is that of
.BR \-\-replication\-targets .
The URL's path names the bucket on the endpoint, optionally followed by a
prefix:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# name   url                                region     access key            secret
COLD     https://s3.archive.internal/cold/a  eu\-west\-1  AKIATIER000000000001  wJal.../tier
.fi
.RE
.IP
.B create
and
.B start
refuse the file if a line does not parse, and it is read at every start.
A tier's path belongs to this runtime alone: anything under it that is not a
version here is removed. See
.BR Tiers .
.TP
.BI \-\-nodes " file"
Makes the runtime a node of a cluster in which every node keeps every bucket
and takes writes.
The file names three things:
.IP \(bu 2
this node;
.IP \(bu 2
every node, this one included, by the S3 endpoint the other nodes reach it at,
with an optional
.BI ca= file
for an https endpoint;
.IP \(bu 2
the key the nodes sign requests to each other with, which is the same in every
node's file.
.IP
Comments and blank lines work as in
.BR \-\-notify\-targets :
.RS
.PP
.nf
self  a
node  a  https://s3\-a.internal:9000
node  b  https://s3\-b.internal:9000
node  c  https://s3\-c.internal:9000  ca=/etc/socketley/cluster\-ca.pem
key   AKIACLUSTERNODES0001  wJal.../nodes
sync  1  5000
.fi
.RE
.IP
The
.BI sync " n " "[ms]"
line is optional.
With it, a write is answered only once
.I n
other nodes have it, or once
.I ms
milliseconds (5000) have passed, whatever the nodes report.
After that, the write's copies continue as they would without the line.
Without it, a write is answered once it is in this node's outbox.
A file whose sync line names more nodes than there are is refused.
.IP
.B create
and
.B start
refuse the file if a line does not parse, if
.B self
is none of its nodes, or if it names no key.
The file is read at every start, so each node picks up a node added to the
file when that node next starts.
.B show
and the state file keep its path, and the key is never logged.
.IP
The cluster's key is accepted beside the runtime's own credentials, but it is
not one of them.
A request signed with it writes what another node made, is judged by no bucket
policy, and cannot be given to a user. See
.BR Cluster .
.TP
.B \-\-anonymous
Accepts requests that carry no credentials at all.
It is required unless credentials are given: a runtime created with neither is
refused, so an endpoint is never open by omission.
With credentials as well, both kinds of request are served.
A request that does carry a signature is always verified; a wrong signature is
refused and never taken for an anonymous request.
.IP
An anonymous request may send a body in unsigned aws\-chunked framing
(\fBSTREAMING\-UNSIGNED\-PAYLOAD\-TRAILER\fR); the body is decoded and its
trailing checksum checked.
A body signed in chunks (\fBSTREAMING\-AWS4\-HMAC\-SHA256\-PAYLOAD\fR) is
refused with
.BR NotImplemented ,
because there are no credentials to verify the signatures with.
Without
.BR \-\-anonymous ,
a request with no credentials is answered
.B "403 AccessDenied"
unless its bucket's policy allows it, as described under
.BR "Bucket policy" .
.TP
.BI \-\-idp " runtime"
Makes the web interface and WebDAV in a browser use the sign-in of the named
.B idp
runtime in this daemon.
An account signed in there is the user of the same name here, with the rights
that user has here and no others.
An account that is not a user here is answered
.BR 403 .
The page offers the provider's sign-in beside its own form, and signing out
there also signs out at the provider.
The cookie reaches the storage only when the provider's
.B \-\-cookie\-domain
covers the storage's name.
The provider can take over the accounts of the storage's own users with
.BR "socketley idp\-user \fIidp\fP import \fIstorage\fP" .
.TP
.BI \-\-mpu\-expire " duration"
Aborts a multipart upload that has been open this long (\fB24h\fR, \fB7d\fR).
A bare number is seconds, as for every duration flag.
Without this flag, an upload is kept until it is completed or aborted, however
old it is.
The runtime looks for expired uploads while it runs and once at every start.
An upload that outlived the setting while the runtime was stopped is therefore
gone when the runtime comes back.
.TP
.BI \-\-scrub " duration"
Reads every object and version back once in this period (\fB30d\fR, \fB7d\fR)
and checks its bytes.
The bytes are checked against the MD5 that the header names, so a file the
disk changed is found before a client reads it. See
.BR Scrub .
Without this flag, nothing is read in the background.
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " duration"
Compresses each version where it lies once it has gone unwritten this long
(\fB1h\fR, \fB7d\fR).
A compressed version takes less of the disk and reads back as it was. See
.BR Compression .
Without this flag, every body is stored as it was sent.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-bytes " size"
Sets a quota on the bytes the runtime holds.
It takes the
.BR K / M / G
suffixes of
.BR \-\-maxmemory .
A put that would exceed the quota is answered
.B "507 StorageFull"
and stores nothing.
The quota can be changed on a serving runtime with
.BR import .
0, the default, means no quota.
.PP
.B edit
does not change these flags; \fBimport\fR does, as it does for a cache.
While the runtime is running, \fBimport\fR refuses to change \fBdir\fR,
\fBanonymous\fR, \fBdomain\fR, \fBfsync\fR, \fBfsync_interval\fR, the access
key, the secret key, the credentials file, the SSE key file or the
notification targets file.
The contents of a credentials file are re\-read on their own.
The quota and the expiry can change under a serving runtime.
.B \-\-shards
splits the runtime over several processes on one directory, as described under
.BR Sharding .
.SS What it shares with the other runtimes
.TP
.B TLS
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B \-\-cert
and
.B \-\-key
terminate TLS on the listener, as for a server or a cache.
With
.BR \-\-ca ,
the runtime also asks every client for a certificate signed by that CA.
The runtime reads the certificates when it starts, so
.B reload
installs a replaced certificate.
The runtime sets up a TLS session for every accepted connection before it reads
the first byte. A plaintext client therefore meets the handshake and is
refused; it is never served in the clear. A peer that stays in the handshake for
thirty seconds is dropped.
Over TLS, a download is read from the file and encrypted. Only plain TCP
downloads are sent with sendfile, so
.B zero_copy_bytes
stays 0 for TLS downloads. TLS and
.B \-\-listen unix:
cannot be combined.
.TP
.B The node beside the port
A runtime with a port also listens on the Unix socket
.IB <daemon\ socket>.rt/ name .sock
for callers on the same machine.
A proxy whose
.B \-\-backend
is the runtime's
.IR name ,
or a group the runtime belongs to, connects to that socket instead of the port.
A proxy in front of the runtime carries GETs and PUTs of any size.
With
.BR "\-\-protocol tcp" ,
the request reaches the runtime exactly as the client signed it; use this mode
once requests are signed. In the default
.B http
mode, the client's path carries the proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix, and the proxy strips that prefix before it forwards the request.
.TP
.B Limits and timeouts
.B \-\-max\-connections
closes any connection beyond the limit.
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
closes a keep\-alive connection that stays idle between requests, and counts it in
.BR timeouts .
.TP
.B \-\-cpus
The CPU set is checked against the daemon's ring and shown by
.BR stats ,
as for every runtime type.
.TP
.B Persistence
The configuration is saved with the runtime. When the daemon restarts, it starts
every runtime that was running, with its objects, TLS settings and limits.
.TP
.B daemon \-\-fast\-lo
The loopback shortcut needs no exception for a storage runtime.
The runtime reads every request body with a receive call, and it splices only
from a file to a socket, never from a socket. Clients on the shortcut, directly
or through a proxy, therefore put and get objects byte for byte.
.SS Supported S3 operations
.TS
l l lw(40).
Operation	Request	Notes
_
ListBuckets	GET /	T{
.ad l
The buckets the caller owns, or every bucket for an admin (see \fBUsers\fR), sorted by name, with creation dates; max\-buckets (1 to 10000), continuation\-token and prefix page it as S3 does.
T}
CreateBucket	PUT /bucket	T{
.ad l
BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou (409) if the caller owns it, BucketAlreadyExists (409) if another user does; InvalidBucketName (400) for a name S3 would refuse. x\-amz\-bucket\-object\-lock\-enabled: true makes it versioned and locked for good; see \fBObject lock\fR below.
T}
DeleteBucket	DELETE /bucket	T{
.ad l
BucketNotEmpty (409) while it holds objects, noncurrent versions or delete markers; open multipart uploads are aborted with it.
T}
HeadBucket	HEAD /bucket	T{
.ad l
200, or 404 with no body.
T}
GetBucketLocation	GET /bucket?location	T{
.ad l
An empty LocationConstraint.
T}
PutBucketVersioning	PUT /bucket?versioning	T{
.ad l
Status Enabled or Suspended; MfaDelete Enabled, or an x\-amz\-mfa header, is 501 NotImplemented. See \fBVersioning\fR below.
T}
GetBucketVersioning	GET /bucket?versioning	T{
.ad l
An empty VersioningConfiguration for a bucket never versioned.
T}
PutObjectLockConfiguration	PUT /bucket?object\-lock	T{
.ad l
ObjectLockEnabled Enabled and an optional default retention; only while versioning is Enabled; Content\-MD5 or a checksum required. See \fBObject lock\fR below.
T}
GetObjectLockConfiguration	GET /bucket?object\-lock	T{
.ad l
ObjectLockConfigurationNotFoundError (404) for a bucket without object lock.
T}
PutObjectRetention	PUT /bucket/key?retention	T{
.ad l
Mode and RetainUntilDate, or an empty Retention to remove one; versionId; x\-amz\-bypass\-governance\-retention; Content\-MD5 or a checksum required.
T}
GetObjectRetention	GET /bucket/key?retention	T{
.ad l
NoSuchObjectLockConfiguration (404) for a version without one.
T}
PutObjectLegalHold	PUT /bucket/key?legal\-hold	T{
.ad l
Status ON or OFF; versionId; Content\-MD5 or a checksum required.
T}
GetObjectLegalHold	GET /bucket/key?legal\-hold	T{
.ad l
NoSuchObjectLockConfiguration (404) for a version whose hold was never set.
T}
PutBucketEncryption	PUT /bucket?encryption	T{
.ad l
A ServerSideEncryptionConfiguration whose one rule is AES256, or aws:kms with an optional KMSMasterKeyID; aws:kms:dsse is 501 NotImplemented, and so is any rule on a runtime without \fB\-\-sse\-key\-file\fR. See \fBServer\-side encryption\fR below.
T}
GetBucketEncryption	GET /bucket?encryption	T{
.ad l
The rule; ServerSideEncryptionConfigurationNotFoundError (404) for a bucket without one.
T}
DeleteBucketEncryption	DELETE /bucket?encryption	T{
.ad l
204; the objects keep the encryption they were written with.
T}
ListObjectVersions	GET /bucket?versions	T{
.ad l
prefix, delimiter, key\-marker, version\-id\-marker, max\-keys (at most 1000), encoding\-type=url; Version and DeleteMarker entries by key, each key's newest first.
T}
PutBucketCors	PUT /bucket?cors	T{
.ad l
A CORSConfiguration of at most 100 rules and 64 KiB, replacing the one before; Content\-MD5 checked. See \fBCORS\fR below.
T}
GetBucketCors	GET /bucket?cors	T{
.ad l
The rules as they are kept; NoSuchCORSConfiguration (404) when there are none.
T}
DeleteBucketCors	DELETE /bucket?cors	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not there were rules.
T}
(preflight)	OPTIONS /bucket[/key]	T{
.ad l
Answered from the bucket's CORS rules, with no signature.
T}
ListObjectsV2	GET /bucket?list\-type=2	T{
.ad l
prefix, delimiter, continuation\-token, max\-keys (at most 1000), start\-after, encoding\-type=url, fetch\-owner; CommonPrefixes count against max\-keys.
T}
ListObjects (V1)	GET /bucket	T{
.ad l
marker and NextMarker.
T}
PutObject	PUT /bucket/key	T{
.ad l
Streamed; Content\-MD5 checked; Content\-Type, Cache\-Control, Content\-Disposition, Content\-Encoding, Content\-Language, Expires and x\-amz\-meta\-* stored and returned; x\-amz\-tagging sets the object's tags; Expect: 100\-continue and chunked request bodies accepted; If\-Match (an ETag or *) writes only over that object, NoSuchKey (404) when the key holds none, and If\-None\-Match (* or an ETag) only when the key holds no such object, decided when the object is committed so two racing writers cannot both win; PreconditionFailed (412) otherwise; x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption: AES256 or the SSE\-C headers encrypt it.
T}
GetObject	GET /bucket/key	T{
.ad l
Range (one range), If\-Match, If\-None\-Match, If\-Modified\-Since, If\-Unmodified\-Since, response\-* header overrides on a signed request (InvalidRequest, 400, on an anonymous one); every answer outside a website endpoint carries X\-Content\-Type\-Options: nosniff and, but for a PDF, Content\-Security\-Policy: default\-src 'none'; sandbox, and is sent as Content\-Disposition: attachment unless its type is an image, audio, video, font, text/plain, text/csv, PDF, JSON, ZIP or octet\-stream, so nothing it holds runs on the web interface's origin; x\-amz\-tagging\-count when the object has tags (on HEAD too); ?versionId= reads that version, and x\-amz\-version\-id names it; an object stored under a client's key needs the SSE\-C headers, on HEAD too.
T}
HeadObject	HEAD /bucket/key	T{
.ad l
The headers of a GET, from memory, or from the object's header when another shard owns the key; ?versionId= as for GET.
T}
SelectObjectContent	POST /bucket/key?select&select\-type=2	T{
.ad l
A SQL expression over a CSV or JSON object, answered as an event stream. See \fBS3 Select\fR below.
T}
GetObjectAttributes	GET /bucket/key?attributes	T{
.ad l
x\-amz\-object\-attributes of ETag, Checksum, ObjectParts, StorageClass and ObjectSize; the parts of a multipart object paged by x\-amz\-max\-parts and x\-amz\-part\-number\-marker, with their numbers, sizes and, when the upload had a checksum algorithm, each part's own checksum; ?versionId= and the SSE\-C headers as for HeadObject.
T}
GetObject, HeadObject by part	GET|HEAD /bucket/key?partNumber=N	T{
.ad l
Part N of a multipart object as a 206 with its Content\-Range and x\-amz\-mp\-parts\-count; see \fBReading by part number\fR below.
T}
DeleteObject	DELETE /bucket/key	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not the key existed; in a versioned bucket a delete marker, and with ?versionId= that version removed for good. If\-Match (an ETag list or *), x\-amz\-if\-match\-last\-modified\-time and x\-amz\-if\-match\-size are asked of that version, or of the latest the marker would cover (a delete marker included, which only * passes): PreconditionFailed (412) when it does not match, and 204 with nothing removed when there is none, decided by the shard that owns the key as the delete is.
T}
PostObject	POST /bucket (multipart/form-data)	T{
.ad l
A browser's upload by HTML form; see \fBUploads by form\fR below.
T}
DeleteObjects	POST /bucket?delete	T{
.ad l
Up to 1000 keys, each with or without a VersionId and with the conditions DeleteObject takes as an ETag, a LastModifiedTime (an HTTP date or ISO 8601) and a Size, a key whose condition does not match answered PreconditionFailed among the errors; Quiet, Content\-MD5 checked.
T}
CopyObject	PUT /bucket/key + x\-amz\-copy\-source	T{
.ad l
Within or across buckets; x\-amz\-metadata\-directive COPY or REPLACE; x\-amz\-tagging\-directive COPY (the default, the source's tags) or REPLACE (those x\-amz\-tagging names, or none); x\-amz\-copy\-source\-if\-match and \-if\-none\-match; onto itself only with REPLACE, from an older version or naming an encryption or a checksum algorithm; a source of key?versionId=ID copies that version. The copy is a single\-part object whose ETag is the md5 of its bytes, encrypted as its own request or its bucket asks whatever its source's was; x\-amz\-copy\-source\-server\-side\-encryption\-customer\-* open an SSE\-C source. Its checksum is FULL_OBJECT, of its own bytes, in the algorithm x\-amz\-checksum\-algorithm names or else its source's (see \fBChecksums\fR below).
T}
PutObjectTagging	PUT /bucket/key?tagging	T{
.ad l
A Tagging document replacing the object's tags; Content\-MD5 checked. See \fBObject tags\fR below. The tagging operations take ?versionId= too.
T}
GetObjectTagging	GET /bucket/key?tagging	T{
.ad l
The tags in the order they were set; an empty TagSet for none.
T}
DeleteObjectTagging	DELETE /bucket/key?tagging	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not the object had tags.
T}
CreateMultipartUpload	POST /bucket/key?uploads	T{
.ad l
Content\-Type, the stored headers, x\-amz\-meta\-* and x\-amz\-tagging are kept for the object; x\-amz\-checksum\-algorithm names the checksum every part takes; x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption or the SSE\-C headers encrypt every part and the object.
T}
UploadPart	PUT /bucket/key?partNumber=N&uploadId=ID	T{
.ad l
Streamed like a put, Content\-MD5 and the checksums checked; a part sent again replaces the old one. N is 1 to 10000. A part of an SSE\-C upload carries the upload's key.
T}
UploadPartCopy	PUT /bucket/key?partNumber=N&uploadId=ID + x\-amz\-copy\-source	T{
.ad l
The whole source or x\-amz\-copy\-source\-range=bytes=first\-last, a range past the source's end being 416 InvalidRange; a source of key?versionId=ID reads that version.
T}
CompleteMultipartUpload	POST /bucket/key?uploadId=ID	T{
.ad l
InvalidPart, InvalidPartOrder, EntityTooSmall (a part but the last under 5 MiB), NoSuchUpload, MalformedXML; If\-Match and If\-None\-Match as for PutObject. A completion sent again after it succeeded is answered as the first was, for as long as the key holds the object it made.
T}
AbortMultipartUpload	DELETE /bucket/key?uploadId=ID	T{
.ad l
204; NoSuchUpload afterwards.
T}
ListParts	GET /bucket/key?uploadId=ID	T{
.ad l
max\-parts, part\-number\-marker.
T}
ListMultipartUploads	GET /bucket?uploads	T{
.ad l
prefix, delimiter, key\-marker, upload\-id\-marker, max\-uploads, encoding\-type=url.
T}
PutBucketLifecycleConfiguration	PUT /bucket?lifecycle	T{
.ad l
Replaces the bucket's rules; see Bucket lifecycle. Content\-MD5 checked.
T}
GetBucketLifecycleConfiguration	GET /bucket?lifecycle	T{
.ad l
NoSuchLifecycleConfiguration (404) for a bucket without rules.
T}
DeleteBucketLifecycle	DELETE /bucket?lifecycle	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not the bucket had rules.
T}
PutBucketPolicy	PUT /bucket?policy	T{
.ad l
A JSON policy of at most 20 KiB, replacing the one before; 204; MalformedPolicy (400) for one this runtime cannot honour exactly. See \fBBucket policy\fR below.
T}
GetBucketPolicy	GET /bucket?policy	T{
.ad l
The document as it was given; NoSuchBucketPolicy (404) when there is none.
T}
DeleteBucketPolicy	DELETE /bucket?policy	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not there was one.
T}
GetBucketPolicyStatus	GET /bucket?policyStatus	T{
.ad l
IsPublic: whether an Allow lets everyone in from any address; NoSuchBucketPolicy (404) when there is none.
T}
PutBucketLogging	PUT /bucket?logging	T{
.ad l
A BucketLoggingStatus naming a TargetBucket of the same owner, a TargetPrefix and SimplePrefix or PartitionedPrefix keys; an empty one turns logging off; TargetGrants is AccessControlListNotSupported (400). See \fBServer access logging\fR below.
T}
GetBucketLogging	GET /bucket?logging	T{
.ad l
The status as it is kept, an empty one for a bucket that is not logged.
T}
PutPublicAccessBlock	PUT /bucket?publicAccessBlock	T{
.ad l
A PublicAccessBlockConfiguration, every setting false when left out. See \fBPublic access block\fR below.
T}
GetPublicAccessBlock	GET /bucket?publicAccessBlock	T{
.ad l
The four settings; NoSuchPublicAccessBlockConfiguration (404) when there is none.
T}
DeletePublicAccessBlock	DELETE /bucket?publicAccessBlock	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not there was one; answers to s3:PutBucketPublicAccessBlock, as on S3.
T}
PutBucketNotificationConfiguration	PUT /bucket?notification	T{
.ad l
A NotificationConfiguration whose destinations are webhooks of \fB\-\-notify\-targets\fR, replacing the one before; an empty one removes it. See \fBEvent notifications\fR below.
T}
GetBucketNotificationConfiguration	GET /bucket?notification	T{
.ad l
The configuration as it is kept, an Id given to a rule that had none; an empty one for a bucket without.
T}
PutBucketReplication	PUT /bucket?replication	T{
.ad l
A ReplicationConfiguration whose Role names a target of \fB\-\-replication\-targets\fR, on a bucket whose versioning is Enabled, replacing the one before. See \fBReplication\fR below.
T}
GetBucketReplication	GET /bucket?replication	T{
.ad l
The configuration as it is kept, an ID given to a rule that had none; ReplicationConfigurationNotFoundError (404) when there is none.
T}
DeleteBucketReplication	DELETE /bucket?replication	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not there was one; versions already queued are still copied.
T}
PutBucketQuota	PUT /bucket?quota	T{
.ad l
socketley's own: a BucketQuota of MaxBytes and MaxObjects, admins only. See \fBQuotas\fR below.
T}
GetBucketQuota	GET /bucket?quota	T{
.ad l
The quota as it is kept; NoSuchBucketQuota (404) when there is none. Admins only.
T}
DeleteBucketQuota	DELETE /bucket?quota	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not there was one. Admins only.
T}
PutBucketWebsite	PUT /bucket?website	T{
.ad l
A WebsiteConfiguration: an IndexDocument, an ErrorDocument and up to 50 RoutingRules, or a RedirectAllRequestsTo alone. See \fBWebsite hosting\fR below.
T}
GetBucketWebsite	GET /bucket?website	T{
.ad l
The configuration as it is kept; NoSuchWebsiteConfiguration (404) when there is none.
T}
DeleteBucketWebsite	DELETE /bucket?website	T{
.ad l
204 whether or not there was one.
T}
GetSessionToken	POST / Action=GetSessionToken	T{
.ad l
STS's: temporary credentials for the signing user, DurationSeconds 900 to 129600 (43200). See \fBTemporary credentials\fR below.
T}
AssumeRole	POST / Action=AssumeRole	T{
.ad l
STS's: the same, DurationSeconds 900 to 43200 (3600), the RoleArn of a role the IAM API made, and a Policy that bounds the session.
T}
AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity	POST / Action=AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity	T{
.ad l
STS's, unsigned: temporary credentials for an OIDC provider's token, as \fB\-\-federation\fR maps it. See \fBFederated identities\fR below.
T}
AssumeRoleWithLDAPIdentity	POST / Action=AssumeRoleWithLDAPIdentity	T{
.ad l
Unsigned: temporary credentials for a user the LDAP directory binds, as \fB\-\-federation\fR maps it.
T}
.TE
.PP
A bucket's ownership controls decide whether the bucket takes ACLs. They are
managed with PutBucketOwnershipControls, GetBucketOwnershipControls and
DeleteBucketOwnershipControls, or with
.B x\-amz\-object\-ownership
on CreateBucket.
BucketOwnerEnforced is every bucket's default, as S3 makes it now, and it
disables ACLs. Every bucket and object then has one grant, FULL_CONTROL to the
bucket's owner, and GetBucketAcl and GetObjectAcl answer that grant. A write may
ask only for that ACL: \fBbucket\-owner\-full\-control\fR, or
\fBx\-amz\-acl: private\fR from the bucket's owner, which rclone and s3fs send by
default. Any other canned ACL or grant is
.B "400 AccessControlListNotSupported"
(\fBInvalidBucketAclWithObjectOwnership\fR on CreateBucket), and nothing is stored.
.PP
ObjectWriter and BucketOwnerPreferred enable ACLs. With
.BR \-\-acls ,
every bucket that has no ownership controls takes ACLs too. For such a bucket,
GetBucketOwnershipControls answers
.BR "404 OwnershipControlsNotFoundError" ,
as for an RGW bucket. Where ACLs are enabled, these set the ACL of a bucket or
of an object:
.IP \(bu 2
the canned ACLs: private, public\-read, public\-read\-write, authenticated\-read,
bucket\-owner\-read, bucket\-owner\-full\-control and log\-delivery\-write;
.IP \(bu 2
the
.B x\-amz\-grant\-*
headers: id=, uri= of AllUsers, AuthenticatedUsers or LogDelivery, and
emailAddress= of a user's
.B email
setting;
.IP \(bu 2
an AccessControlPolicy document.
.PP
A bucket's ACL is kept as a document beside the bucket's policy. An object's ACL
is kept in the object's header, together with the object's owner. Under
ObjectWriter the owner is the writer. Under BucketOwnerPreferred the owner is the
bucket's owner when the write gave that owner full control.
.PP
A bucket's grants allow listing (READ), writes and deletes (WRITE), and access to
the bucket's ACL (READ_ACP, WRITE_ACP). An object's grants allow reading the
object (READ) and access to its ACL. The runtime reads an object's grants when it
reads the object, and only after the bucket's grants did not decide the request.
.IP \(bu 2
An explicit Deny in a policy still refuses the request.
.IP \(bu 2
The bucket's owner and an admin are let in, whatever the grants say.
.IP \(bu 2
A public grant is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
where the public access block blocks public ACLs. Where the block ignores public
ACLs, a public grant counts for nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
A grantee that is not a user is
.B "400 InvalidArgument"
.RB ( UnresolvableGrantByEmailAddress
for an email address).
.IP \(bu 2
BucketOwnerEnforced is refused on a bucket whose ACL grants anything more than
the owner's full control.
.PP
A bucket can have tags of its own, managed with PutBucketTagging,
GetBucketTagging and DeleteBucketTagging. A bucket has at most 50 tags, under the
same rules as an object's tags. When there are none, the answer is NoSuchTagSet
(404). The tags are kept in
.IB dir / bucket /tagging
and carried between shards like the bucket's CORS rules.
.PP
The runtime accepts every storage class S3 has, and keeps the bytes the same way
as for STANDARD.
.IP \(bu 2
A class whose objects S3 can read at once (REDUCED_REDUNDANCY, STANDARD_IA,
ONEZONE_IA, INTELLIGENT_TIERING, GLACIER_IR, EXPRESS_ONEZONE) is kept. HEAD, GET,
the listings, GetObjectAttributes, and an upload's ListParts and
ListMultipartUploads name it.
.IP \(bu 2
An archive class is named STANDARD, because its own name would tell a client to
restore an object that it can read now.
.IP \(bu 2
A copy takes the class it names, or STANDARD when it names none. A copy may name
a class to change an object's class in place.
.IP \(bu 2
A version that a lifecycle rule moved to a tier is listed and read with the
tier's name as its class.
.PP
A class S3 does not have is
.BR "400 InvalidStorageClass" .
.B x\-amz\-website\-redirect\-location
is kept and returned like the other stored headers.
Any other feature, such as analytics or inventory, is answered
.B "501 NotImplemented"
and is never mistaken for the plain operation. A single put is at most 5 GiB, and
a key is at most 1024 bytes.
.SS Uploads by form
A POST to a bucket with a
.B multipart/form\-data
body is S3's POST Object: the upload that a web page makes with an HTML form. It
also takes the fields that
.B generate_presigned_post
and the SDKs' presigned POST write. A form posted to a key instead is
.BR "405 MethodNotAllowed" ,
as is a POST to any key without a subresource.
.PP
The fields before the file describe the upload. Together they may hold at most
20 KiB; more is
.BR MaxPostPreDataLengthExceeded .
The fields are:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR key ;
.B ${filename}
in it is replaced with the file's name;
.IP \(bu 2
the headers a PutObject would carry: Content\-Type, Cache\-Control,
Content\-Disposition, Content\-Encoding, Expires, x\-amz\-meta\-*,
x\-amz\-checksum\-* and x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption*;
.IP \(bu 2
.B acl
and
.B tagging
(a Tagging document);
.IP \(bu 2
.B success_action_status
(200, 201 with a PostResponse document, or 204, the default), or
.BR success_action_redirect ,
which answers a 303 to that address with the bucket, key and ETag in its query.
.PP
The file is streamed into the object like a put's body. Fields after the file are
ignored, as on S3.
.PP
A form with a
.B policy
carries its signature in its fields, in one of two forms:
.IP \(bu 2
SigV4: \fBx\-amz\-algorithm\fR, \fBx\-amz\-credential\fR, \fBx\-amz\-date\fR and
\fBx\-amz\-signature\fR, an HMAC\-SHA256 of the policy;
.IP \(bu 2
the older form that boto3 still writes by default: \fBAWSAccessKeyId\fR and
\fBsignature\fR, a base64 HMAC\-SHA1 of the policy. This is Signature Version 2,
which headers and presigned URLs may also use (see
.BR Authentication ).
.PP
The policy is a base64 JSON document with an
.B expiration
and
.BR conditions .
An expired policy is AccessDenied. The conditions are {"field": "value"},
["eq", "$field", "value"], ["starts\-with", "$field", "prefix"] and
["content\-length\-range", min, max]. Field names are compared without case.
"$bucket" is the bucket posted to, and a field the form leaves out counts as the
empty string.
.PP
A field that does not meet a condition is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
("Invalid according to Policy: ..."). So is a field that no condition names,
except the signature and its key, the policy, the file and the x\-ignore\-*
fields. A file outside the content\-length range is
.B EntityTooLarge
or
.BR EntityTooSmall .
In each case nothing is stored.
.PP
A form without a policy is an anonymous PutObject. It is taken by a runtime with
.BR \-\-anonymous ,
or where the bucket policy lets anyone put the key. Either way, the bucket policy
and a script's on_request see a form upload as PutObject.
.SS Virtual\-hosted\-style addressing
Against AWS, S3 clients address a bucket by host name by default
(\fBhttps://\fIbucket\fB.s3.amazonaws.com/\fIkey\fR). Most clients can be told
to do the same against a custom endpoint.
.PP
Given
.BR "\-\-domain s3.example.com" ,
a request with
.B "Host: photos.s3.example.com"
and path
.B /2024/a.jpg
names the object
.B 2024/a.jpg
in bucket
.BR photos .
The path\-style request
.B /photos/2024/a.jpg
names the same object, with
.B "Host: s3.example.com"
or with any Host that is not a subdomain of a given domain, including an IP
address.
.PP
Everything before the domain is the bucket name, dots included. With several
domains, the longest domain that matches decides. A Host equal to one of the
domains is that endpoint itself, so
.B "\-\-domain example.com,s3.example.com"
reads
.B s3.example.com
as the endpoint and not as bucket
.BR s3 .
.PP
Signatures need no special handling. SigV4 signs the path as sent and the Host
header, so a virtual\-hosted request is verified over
.BI / key
and its own host. A presigned URL in either style verifies as it was written.
.PP
The locations the runtime writes back follow the style of the request. This
applies to the
.B Location
of CreateBucket (\fB/\fIbucket\fR in path style, the bucket's URL in
virtual\-hosted style), the Location of a form upload, and the location in
.BR CompleteMultipartUploadResult .
.B x\-amz\-copy\-source
names
.IB bucket / key
in both styles, as in S3.
.PP
Clients need the bucket host names to resolve to the runtime. Use a wildcard DNS
record
.RB ( *.s3.example.com ),
or, on one machine,
.BR "\-\-domain localhost" :
glibc resolves every
.IB name .localhost
to the loopback address. The usual client settings are:
.IP \(bu 2
boto3's
.B "addressing_style: virtual"
(in the AWS CLI's config,
.BR "s3 = addressing_style = virtual" );
.IP \(bu 2
rclone's
.BR "force_path_style = false" ;
.IP \(bu 2
an mc alias set with path lookup
.BR off .
.PP
A bucket whose name is not a single DNS label, such as a name with dots, is still
served path style. Over TLS, the clients fall back to path style for such names.
With
.BR \-\-tls ,
the certificate must cover every bucket's host name. That takes a wildcard
certificate for
.BI *. domain
as well as the domain itself. A wildcard matches only one label, so a bucket with
dots in its name fails certificate verification in virtual\-hosted style and is
reached path style.
.SS Authentication
The runtime verifies requests signed with AWS Signature Version 4, in both forms:
the
.B Authorization
header, and a presigned URL (\fBX\-Amz\-Algorithm\fR and the other parameters in
the query, for GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE).
.PP
The runtime also verifies Signature Version 2, which awscli 1.x presigns with and
older tools sign with. It takes both forms:
.IP \(bu 2
the header
.B "Authorization: AWS"
.IR key : signature ,
dated by
.B Date
or
.B x\-amz\-date
within fifteen minutes;
.IP \(bu 2
a URL's
.BR AWSAccessKeyId ,
.B Expires
and
.BR Signature .
The URL is good until
.BR Expires ,
which may be at most a week (604800 seconds) ahead.
.PP
For Signature Version 2, the signed resource is the bucket and key as sent, with
the subresources S3 signs. No part of the body is signed.
.PP
Any region in the credential scope is accepted, including none.
Every
.B x\-amz\-*
header in a request must be among the signed headers, as S3 requires. The one
exception is an
.B x\-amz\-content\-sha256
header sent with a presigned URL. A request with an unsigned
.B x\-amz\-*
header is refused with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
.PP
The runtime honours every
.B x\-amz\-content\-sha256
mode a client sends:
.IP \(bu 2
A hex hash is checked against the body as the body streams. A body that is not
the one signed is
.B XAmzContentSHA256Mismatch
and is not stored. A presigned URL signs no payload, but when its sender states a
hash in the header, the body is checked against that hash all the same. A value
there that is not a hash is
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B UNSIGNED\-PAYLOAD
has nothing to check.
.IP \(bu 2
aws\-chunked bodies are decoded:
.BR STREAMING\-AWS4\-HMAC\-SHA256\-PAYLOAD ,
its
.B \-TRAILER
variant and
.BR STREAMING\-UNSIGNED\-PAYLOAD\-TRAILER .
Every chunk signature and a signed trailer are verified, even when the
aws\-chunked body is itself carried in
.BR "Transfer\-Encoding: chunked" .
.PP
No mode holds the body whole: the body goes through the same two staging buffers
as an unsigned put.
.PP
The error answers are S3's:
.IP \(bu 2
.B SignatureDoesNotMatch
(403) for a wrong secret, or for a request changed after signing;
.IP \(bu 2
.B InvalidAccessKeyId
(403);
.IP \(bu 2
.B RequestTimeTooSkewed
(403) for a request dated more than fifteen minutes from the runtime's clock;
.IP \(bu 2
.B AccessDenied
(403) for an expired presigned URL, for a request with no signature where no
bucket policy allows it, and for a request a bucket policy denies;
.IP \(bu 2
.B AuthorizationHeaderMalformed
(400).
.PP
A denied request is answered before its body is read, and the connection is
closed.
.SS Users
A credentials file may name users. Each has an id, a display name, its own keys,
optionally an identity policy, and optionally the admin flag:
.PP
.nf
# a key outside any user is the default owner, socketley, an admin
AKIAOPS000000000001:ops\-secret

[alice]
name = Alice Example
AKIAALICE0000000001:alice\-secret\-1
AKIAALICE0000000002:alice\-secret\-2

[bob]
policy = bob.json
AKIABOB000000000001:bob\-secret

[root]
admin = true
AKIAROOT00000000001:root\-secret
.fi
.PP
.BI [ id ]
starts a user. The id is 1 to 128 printable characters, with no spaces and none of
.BR "/:*?,=[]\(dq<>&" .
The lines after it, up to the next user, hold the user's keys
.RI ( access : secret )
and settings
.RI ( name " = " value ):
.IP \(bu 2
.B name
is the display name. Default: the id.
.IP \(bu 2
.B admin
is
.B true
or
.BR false .
.IP \(bu 2
.B policy
is the user's identity policy. A value that begins with
.B {
is the JSON itself, on that one line. Any other value is a file, relative to the
credentials file's directory.
.IP \(bu 2
.B max_bytes
is the most that the buckets the user owns may hold together, as a size such as
.B 10G
(see
.BR Quotas ).
.PP
In a setting line, the
.B =
comes before any colon. An access key cannot contain
.BR = .
.PP
A key outside any user, and the pair given with
.BR \-\-access\-key ,
belong to the default owner,
.BR socketley ,
an admin. A file without users describes only that owner: every key may do
everything to every bucket, except what a bucket policy denies.
.PP
These are errors: a user declared twice, a key given to two users, an unknown
setting, and a policy that does not compile.
.B create
and
.B start
refuse such a file and name the line. On a reload, such a file leaves the current
users in force.
.PP
An identity policy uses a bucket policy's grammar (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ),
with the same actions and conditions. It has no
.BR Principal ,
because the principal is the user itself. Its resources may be in any bucket:
.B *
or
.BI arn:aws:s3::: pattern .
A pattern is matched as IAM matches an ARN: against the bucket name for a bucket
action, and against
.IB bucket / key
for an object action. In a pattern,
.B *
also spans
.BR / .
So
.B arn:aws:s3:::logs\-*
names those buckets and every object in them, and
.B arn:aws:s3:::logs\-*/*
names only the objects.
.PP
A bucket is owned by the user that made it. The owner is recorded on the second
line of
.IB dir / bucket /created .
There is no such line for the default owner, as for every bucket made before
users. Every shard reads the owner when the bucket is made and at every start.
.PP
ListBuckets shows a user the buckets it owns, and shows an admin every bucket.
When one of the user's policies denies it
.B s3:ListAllMyBuckets
(on \fB*\fR or \fBarn:aws:s3:::*\fR), ListBuckets is 403.
Every Owner element is the bucket's owner, with the owner's display name. This
covers ListBuckets, ListObjects, ListObjectVersions, the uploads and parts
listings, GetBucketAcl, and GetObjectAcl on a bucket with ACLs disabled. The reason
is that an object of a bucket with ACLs disabled belongs to the bucket's owner.
Where ACLs are enabled, GetObjectAcl names the object's own owner, as
.B "Supported S3 operations"
describes.
.PP
A signed request is decided in this order:
.IP 1. 4
A matching
.B Deny
in the user's identity policy or in the bucket's policy refuses the request with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
.IP 2. 4
An admin and the bucket's owner are let in. So is anybody on a bucket that does
not exist; the operation then answers
.BR NoSuchBucket .
.IP 3. 4
A matching
.B Allow
lets the request in. It may be in the identity policy, or in the bucket's policy
naming the user by id, by one of its keys, or by
.BR * .
.IP 4. 4
Anything else is
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
.PP
A request without credentials to a bucket that does not exist is answered
.BR "404 NoSuchBucket" ,
as a signed one is.
A request is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
before anything is done when its
.B x\-amz\-expected\-bucket\-owner
names a user other than the bucket's owner. The same holds when
.B x\-amz\-source\-expected\-bucket\-owner
does so for a copy's source bucket.
A copy is also judged as a GetObject of its source in the source bucket.
.PP
A user of the credentials file may call CreateBucket unless its identity policy
denies
.B s3:CreateBucket
on the name. A role, or a user that the IAM API made, may call it only where one
of its policies allows it, as S3 judges them.
The bucket policy's own operations stay open to the owner and admins, whatever the
policy says. So do the operations that no action names: ownership controls and the
operations answered NotImplemented. Another user needs an Allow for the bucket
policy's operations, and is refused the operations that no action names.
.SS Temporary credentials
A user may ask the endpoint itself for temporary credentials, as from STS, with
any STS client pointed at the runtime's URL:
.RS
.PP
.nf
POST / HTTP/1.1
Content\-Type: application/x\-www\-form\-urlencoded

Action=AssumeRole&Version=2011\-06\-15&DurationSeconds=3600
    &RoleArn=arn:aws:iam::000000000000:role/reader&RoleSessionName=ci
    &Policy=<an identity policy, URL\-encoded>
.fi
.RE
.PP
The answer is an access key starting with
.BR ASIA ,
a secret and a session token. They sign as the user whose key asked for them.
The token goes in
.BR x\-amz\-security\-token ,
or in a presigned URL's
.BR X\-Amz\-Security\-Token .
.PP
The token carries the user's key, the expiry and the session policy, sealed with
that key's secret. The session's secret is derived from that key's secret too.
The runtime keeps nothing, so every shard accepts the token, and so does the
runtime after a restart. The token ends at its expiry
.RB ( "400 ExpiredToken" ),
or as soon as the key is gone from the credentials file
.RB ( "400 InvalidToken" ).
.PP
The request is a form posted to
.BR / ,
signed for the service
.B sts
or
.BR s3 .
Temporary credentials cannot ask for more, and a runtime without keys gives none.
.PP
AssumeRole of a role that the IAM API made (see
.BR IAM )
is refused unless the role's trust policy names the caller. It is also refused
for a duration longer than the role's MaxSessionDuration. The session holds the
role's policies, not the caller's. Any other RoleArn is
.B "404 NoSuchEntity"
and gives the caller nothing.
.PP
The session is bounded by its
.BR Policy ,
which uses an identity policy's grammar and is at most 2048 bytes. The Policy only
narrows the session: what it does not allow is refused even to an admin or a
bucket's owner, including the bucket policy's operations.
.PP
GetCallerIdentity names the user or the assumed role that signed. For a user that
the IAM API made, it gives the UserId and the path the user was given. A browser's
form upload is not signed with temporary credentials.
.SS Federated identities
With
.BI \-\-federation " file" ,
the endpoint gives temporary credentials, without a signed request, to an
identity that an OIDC provider or an LDAP directory vouches for. The file holds
one setting per line. Comments and blank lines work as in
.BR \-\-notify\-targets ,
and relative paths are taken from the file's own directory:
.RS
.PP
.nf
oidc issuer https://id.example.org/realms/main
oidc client storage\-console
oidc jwks   jwks.json
oidc claim  policy
role arn:aws:iam:::role/ci   builder
ldap url    ldaps://ldap.example.org ca=/etc/ssl/ldap\-ca.pem
ldap dn     uid=%s,ou=People,dc=example,dc=org
ldap user   alice  =
ldap user   *      reader
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity
takes a JWT signed with RS256, RS384, RS512, PS256, ES256 or ES384 by a key of
the JWK Set file. The keys may be RSA of 2048 bits and up, or EC P\-256 and
P\-384. The runtime reads the JWK Set file again whenever it changes, and keeps
the old keys while a new file does not parse. In the token,
.B iss
must be the issuer,
.B aud
must name a client, and
.BR exp ,
.B nbf
and
.B iat
must hold within a minute. Otherwise the answer is
.BR "400 InvalidIdentityToken" ,
or
.B ExpiredTokenException
once the token has run out.
A
.B role
line for the RoleArn names the user the session signs as. Without such a line,
the token's claim (by default
.BR policy )
must name exactly one user.
.PP
.B AssumeRoleWithLDAPIdentity
binds
.B LDAPUsername
with
.BR LDAPPassword ,
using the DN that the
.B dn
template makes (the name is escaped as a DN value). The connection is ldap:// or
ldaps://, checked against
.B ca=
or the system's CAs. The bind runs on a thread of its own and must finish within
five seconds. The answer is
.B "503 ServiceUnavailable"
when the directory does not answer, and
.B "403 AccessDenied"
when the directory refuses the bind. The user's own
.B ldap user
line, or else the
.B *
line, names the user the session signs as;
.B =
means the same name. An empty password is refused before anything is sent.
.PP
Both kinds of session belong to the mapped user. They are signed under that
user's first key and have the user's rights, narrowed by a
.B Policy
as for AssumeRole. A RoleArn that names a role the IAM API made is assumed when
the role's trust policy names the mapped user, as for AssumeRole. A mapping to a
user that does not exist, or that has no key, is
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
The runtime reads the file at every start.
.B create
and
.B start
refuse the file when a line does not parse.
.SS IAM
The endpoint also answers IAM's API. It answers an admin and a user whose own
policies allow the action, and never a session. The actions are:
.IP \(bu 2
CreateUser, GetUser, ListUsers, UpdateUser and DeleteUser;
.IP \(bu 2
CreateAccessKey (at most two per user), ListAccessKeys, UpdateAccessKey and
DeleteAccessKey;
.IP \(bu 2
CreateGroup, GetGroup, ListGroups, DeleteGroup, AddUserToGroup,
RemoveUserFromGroup and ListGroupsForUser;
.IP \(bu 2
CreateRole, GetRole, ListRoles, UpdateRole and DeleteRole;
.IP \(bu 2
CreateLoginProfile, GetLoginProfile, UpdateLoginProfile, DeleteLoginProfile and
ChangePassword;
.IP \(bu 2
TagUser, UntagUser and ListUserTags;
.IP \(bu 2
for users, groups and roles alike: Put, Get, List and Delete of an inline policy,
and Attach, Detach and ListAttached of the AWS managed policies
AmazonS3FullAccess, AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess and AdministratorAccess.
.PP
A request is a form posted to
.BR / ,
as
.B aws iam
and the SDKs send it, signed for
.B iam
with or without a region.
.PP
What the IAM API makes is kept in \fIdir\fB/.iam.json\fR. The file is written
under a lock and renamed over the old one. Every shard reads it beside the
credentials file. A change is in force at once on the shard that made it, and
within 50 milliseconds on the others. A restart keeps it.
.PP
A user's identity policy is the statements of its inline and attached policies
and of its groups' policies, taken together. A user with none owns and reaches
the buckets it makes, as a credentials-file user without a policy does.
A user of the credentials file cannot be made again through the API. Policies put
on such a user stand beside the file's own: a Deny in either refuses, and an Allow
in either admits. Such a user is not listed.
.PP
A user can be deleted once it has no keys, policies, groups or login profile
left. A group can be deleted once it has no members or policies, and a role once
it has no policies. Policies are checked as identity policies are. A trust
policy's statements with a condition let nobody in. ARNs name no account, as RGW
writes them:
.BR arn:aws:iam:::user/ name .
OIDC providers, customer managed policies and permission boundaries are not
served.
.PP
A login profile is a user's password for the web interface, and for a WebDAV
client's Basic credentials. It works the same for a user of the API and a user of
the credentials file. A password has at least 8 characters. The runtime keeps it
as scrypt (N=2^14, r=8, p=1), never as itself. After a password matches, the
runtime does not hash it again for five minutes, so a drive that sends it with
every request costs one hash, not one per request.
.B PasswordResetRequired
makes the page ask for a new password before anything else. ChangePassword takes
the old password and changes the caller's own. Sessions opened with a password
are sealed under a secret drawn with the profile. A session made before the
password was last set is no longer let in.
.PP
The runtime itself reads four tags of a user:
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley:display\-name
is the name shown;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley:email ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley:max\-bytes
is the quota on the bytes of the buckets the user owns, like the credentials
file's
.BR max_bytes ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley:disabled
set to
.B true
means that neither the user's password nor its keys sign in. Its buckets keep
their owner, and a share can still name the user.
.PP
Other keys with that prefix are refused. A member of the group
.B admins
is an admin, as the credentials file's
.B admin = true
makes one. An IAM user makes a bucket only where a policy allows it, as S3
judges. The web interface gives the users it makes the inline policy
.B socketley\-libraries
(\fBs3:CreateBucket\fR on \fB*\fR), and a user owns what it makes.
.PP
A user who is not an admin may call an IAM action that its identity policies
allow. The action is written
.BI iam: Action ,
with
.B *
and
.B ?
as wildcards (\fBiam:List*\fR, \fBiam:*\fR). A bare \fB"*"\fR names only S3's
actions, so a policy written before IAM could be delegated gives no IAM action;
AdministratorAccess names \fBiam:*\fR beside it. The resources are
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :user/ "path name" ,
.BR group/ ...
or
.BR role/ ...
in any account. In them,
.B ${aws:username}
is the caller's user id. ListUsers, ListGroups and ListRoles act on no resource,
so only
.B "Resource": "*"
covers them. A Deny wins, and an action with no Allow is
.BR AccessDenied .
.PP
An access key named by its id alone is judged as its owner's. A rename or a move
needs both ARNs. GetUser, CreateAccessKey, ListAccessKeys, GetLoginProfile,
ListUserTags and ListGroupsForUser without a UserName act on the caller.
Without any policy, a user may do to itself what IAM's console lets every user
do. It may read its own user, keys, tags and groups, and make, change and delete
its own keys. It may also set a first password and change its password.
.PP
An admin keeps every right, whatever its policies say. Only an admin changes an
admin or gives an identity IAM rights. Anybody else is refused a policy put or
attached whose Allow names an
.B iam:
action (AdministratorAccess included), a group that has such a policy, the group
.BR admins ,
and the
.B socketley:
tags. IAM actions that this runtime does not answer are accepted in a policy and
match nothing.
.SS CORS
A bucket's CORS rules let a page served from another origin use the bucket from a
browser: read objects, and upload with a presigned URL. PutBucketCors sets them
as S3's
.B CORSConfiguration
document, with up to 100
.B CORSRule
elements. Each rule holds:
.IP \(bu 2
one or more
.BR AllowedOrigin .
An origin may hold at most one
.BR * ,
which stands for any run of characters;
.B *
alone stands for every origin;
.IP \(bu 2
one or more
.B AllowedMethod
(GET, PUT, HEAD, POST or DELETE);
.IP \(bu 2
any number of
.B AllowedHeader
(at most one
.B *
each, compared without case) and
.BR ExposeHeader ;
.IP \(bu 2
an optional
.BR MaxAgeSeconds ,
and an optional
.B ID
of up to 255 characters.
.PP
A document that is not a CORSConfiguration is
.BR "400 MalformedXML" .
An unknown method, a second wildcard or a 101st rule is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
A refused document leaves the existing rules in place.
.PP
The rules are kept in
.IB dir / bucket /cors .
A new file is written beside it, synced and renamed over it. The rules therefore
survive a restart, and a crash leaves either the old rules or the new ones. The
rules are deleted with the bucket, and a bucket made again under the same name has
none. With
.BR \-\-shards ,
shard 0 writes the rules, whichever shard took the request, as described for the
policy under
.BR "Bucket policy" .
The request is answered once no shard can go on with the old rules. A shard that
was not running reads the rules from the file when it starts.
.PP
A preflight is an
.B OPTIONS
request on the bucket or on a key, with
.B Origin
and
.BR Access\-Control\-Request\-Method ,
and with
.B Access\-Control\-Request\-Headers
when the real request will send other headers. The runtime answers it without
authentication, from the first rule whose origin, method and every requested
header match. The answer is 200 with:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin
(the origin, or
.B *
when the rule allows every origin);
.IP \(bu 2
.BR Access\-Control\-Allow\-Methods ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Headers
(the headers asked for);
.IP \(bu 2
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Credentials: true
for a named origin;
.IP \(bu 2
.B Access\-Control\-Expose\-Headers
and
.BR Access\-Control\-Max\-Age ,
when the rule has them;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR Vary .
.PP
A preflight to a bucket without rules is
.B "403 AccessForbidden"
("CORSResponse: CORS is not enabled for this bucket."). A preflight that no rule
allows gets the same answer with "This CORS request is not allowed". A preflight
without
.B Origin
or with a method S3 does not have is
.BR "400 BadRequest" .
.PP
Any other request whose
.B Origin
some rule allows for its method gets the same headers, except
.BR Access\-Control\-Allow\-Headers .
A request that also carries
.B Access\-Control\-Request\-Method
is judged by that method and the headers it asks for, and gets the headers of
that preflight, as S3 answers it. These headers come on every answer, including
errors and authentication refusals, so a page can read why it was refused.
A request from an origin that no rule allows is served normally, with no CORS
headers. When none of a runtime's buckets has rules, the runtime looks nothing up
for a request.
.SS Checksums
The runtime computes a checksum over the body of a PutObject or an UploadPart
when the request names one of
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-crc32 ,
.BR \-crc32c ,
.BR \-crc64nvme ,
.B \-sha1
and
.BR \-sha256 .
The request names it in that header, in a trailer of an aws\-chunked body
(\fBx\-amz\-trailer\fR), or with
.BR x\-amz\-sdk\-checksum\-algorithm .
The runtime compares the result with the value the client gave. A mismatch is
.B "400 BadDigest"
and nothing is stored.
.PP
The checksum is kept in the object and echoed in the answer. GetObject and
HeadObject return it when the request has
.BR "x\-amz\-checksum\-mode: ENABLED" ,
except on a ranged read, which is not of the whole object.
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-type
says what the checksum is of.
.PP
A multipart object has the checksum of its parts' checksums, followed by
\fB\-N\fR for the number of parts (\fBCOMPOSITE\fR), as on S3. A part with no
checksum of its own still gets one in the upload's algorithm. An upload created
with
.B "x\-amz\-checksum\-type: FULL_OBJECT"
and a CRC instead has the CRC of all its bytes. So does every CRC64NVME upload, as
in S3. The runtime combines that CRC from the parts' CRCs and lengths, without
reading the parts again. FULL_OBJECT with SHA\-1 or SHA\-256, or COMPOSITE with
CRC64NVME, is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
A checksum that a CompleteMultipartUpload carries is compared with the one the
parts make. A mismatch is
.B "400 BadDigest"
and nothing is stored.
.PP
Each part's own checksum is kept with the completed object, beside the part's
size (see
.BR "Reading by part number" ).
A GetObject or HeadObject by part number with
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-mode: ENABLED
therefore answers the part's checksum, with the object's
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-type .
GetObjectAttributes lists the checksum beside each part. A single\-part object
answers part 1 with its own checksum. A copy keeps none of its source's part
checksums, so it answers part 1 with its own whole checksum. An object completed
before part checksums were kept answers its parts without one.
.PP
An object that arrives with no checksum gets S3's default: the
.B CRC64NVME
of its whole bytes, FULL_OBJECT. A PutObject, a POST form upload and an object a
script puts compute it in the same pass that computes the ETag's MD5. They answer
it as they would a checksum the client named.
An upload created with no algorithm keeps a CRC64NVME of each part beside the
part, shown to no client. UploadPart, ListParts and GetObjectAttributes answer
none, and its parts are read by number without one. Its CompleteMultipartUpload
combines these CRCs into the object's default without reading the parts again.
An object stored before this, and an upload whose parts were written before it,
has none, and is not given one until it is copied.
.PP
A CopyObject's checksum is of the copy's own bytes, FULL_OBJECT, as S3 makes it.
It is in the algorithm that
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-algorithm
names, or else in the source's algorithm, under either metadata directive. An
algorithm the runtime does not take is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
.PP
When the source's checksum is already of its whole bytes, the copy takes that
value. For a COMPOSITE source, or one in another algorithm, the copy's checksum is
computed from the bytes in the copy's one pass through them. The same holds for
every copy that is encrypted or decrypted on its way. The CopyObjectResult carries
the checksum with its
.BR ChecksumType .
A source with no checksum gives the copy the default, CRC64NVME, unless the
request names another algorithm.
.PP
A copy onto itself that names
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-algorithm
is allowed under either directive; S3 documents this for adding or changing an
object's checksum. A copy onto itself that names nothing new is still
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
A copy made before this answers with its source's COMPOSITE value, as it was
stored. UploadPartCopy computes the upload's algorithm over the bytes it copies,
whatever its source's checksum, and answers it in its CopyPartResult.
.SS Object tags
An object has at most 10 tags. They are set with PutObjectTagging, or with
.B x\-amz\-tagging
(a URL\-encoded query string,
.IR k1 = v1 & k2 = v2 ).
That header works on a PutObject, on a CreateMultipartUpload (the tags land on
the completed object), and on a CopyObject under
.BR "x\-amz\-tagging\-directive: REPLACE" .
.PP
A tag key is 1 to 128 characters and a value at most 256, counted as characters
and not bytes. They may hold letters, digits, spaces and
.BR "+ \- = . _ : / @" .
Outside ASCII, every character is taken except a control, a format or a
private\-use character. This is wider than S3, which takes letters, digits and
spaces of every script. Keys are unique, and none begins with
.BR aws: .
.PP
Eleven tags are
.BR "400 BadRequest" ,
and any other breach of these rules is
.BR "400 InvalidTag" .
A Tagging document that is not valid is
.BR MalformedXML .
A repeated key or a broken escape in
.B x\-amz\-tagging
is
.BR InvalidArgument .
A refused put stores nothing, and a refused PutObjectTagging leaves the tags that
were there. GetObject and HeadObject give the number of an object's tags in
.BR x\-amz\-tagging\-count .
.PP
Tags live in the object's own header, beside its other metadata. The journal
record mirrors them like the rest, so tags survive a stop, a crash and a lost
journal.
.PP
A tag change keeps the object's bytes, ETag and Last\-Modified, as on S3, because
it does not rewrite the object. The padding of the header's first 4 KiB holds two
tag slots of whole 512\-byte sectors. A change is written into the slot that the
last change did not use, with a sequence number and a CRC32C, and then synced (one
write and one
.BR fdatasync (2)).
A crash in the middle leaves a slot whose checksum fails, so the tags are those
from before the change, never a torn header. A start after a crash reads the tags
from the key's file, which is the truth.
.PP
A tag set too big for the room left in the slots is written instead as a new
file, with the tags in its header and the bytes copied behind them. The new file
is renamed into place like a put. This happens above about 1.5 KiB of tags with
little other metadata, or for any change to a header that fills its first 4 KiB.
On ext4, a change in place took 2.4 to 2.7 ms whatever the object's size. A
rewrite took 12.6 ms for 4 KiB, 102 ms for 64 MiB and 1.07 s for 1 GiB.
.B stats
counts the two as
.B tag_writes
and
.BR tag_rewrites .
.PP
A tag change goes to the shard that owns the key, and waits behind the key's other
writes in the key's lane. It is answered once the file holds the change and every
shard has dropped what it holds of the key. A GET or a GetObjectTagging on any
shard therefore sees the change at once.
.SS Multipart uploads
An upload is a directory,
.IB dir / bucket /mpu/ id .
It holds a
.B meta
file, with the object the upload will become in an object header, and one file
per part. Each part file is an object header followed by the part's bytes, so the
runtime finds uploads again after a stop or a crash.
.PP
A part is written like a put (streamed, hashed and synced) and then renamed to its
number, so it is either whole or not there. An upload is created, and its
directory synced, before its id is given out. A part still arriving when the
worker is killed leaves nothing behind. The upload id is 32 hex digits.
.PP
Most clients cut an object into parts of one size. When a part's place in the
object is known as the part starts, the runtime writes the part at that place.
For this, the upload also holds a
.B data
file, with room for the object's header at the front. The first part of at least
5 MiB fixes the part size. Part
.I n
of that size, or a smaller last part, goes into
.B data
at its offset. Its numbered file then holds only a header that names the place.
The shard that owns the upload hands out each place once. A part sent again, a
part of another size and a part sent by UploadPartCopy therefore get files of
their own, as described above.
.PP
CompleteMultipartUpload checks the list against what was uploaded. The listed
parts may be exactly 1 to N, all in place, and all of the fixed size except the
last. In that case the runtime writes the object's header in front of them, syncs
.BR data ,
and commits it as the object itself, so the bytes are written to the disk only
once.
.PP
Otherwise the runtime writes the header and copies the parts into a temporary
file, a megabyte at a time on the ring, reading the placed parts from
.BR data .
It then syncs that file and commits it. An upload that was open across a restart,
or whose copy\-free completion failed, always copies.
Either way, the commit is a put's commit: a rename and a directory sync, with the
journal record after the answer. A crash during completion therefore leaves the
upload intact and no object. Only after the commit are the parts removed.
The ETag is the md5 of the parts' md5s followed by \fB\-N\fR, as on S3.
.PP
A copy passes the bytes through the process, because io_uring has no copy between
two files. The loop never blocks on the disk, and the worker holds two
one\-megabyte buffers for the copy, whatever the object's size. warp, the AWS CLI
and boto3 upload anything above 8 to 16 MiB as a multipart upload, so this is the
path of every large upload. On a 4\-vCPU VM, removing the copy took 64 MiB puts at
four connections from 3.2 to 6.5 per second, and 256 MiB puts at two connections
from 0.90 to 1.81.
.PP
An upload is kept until it is completed or aborted, unless
.B \-\-mpu\-expire
is set. Its parts count against
.B \-\-max\-bytes
while they are held. Deleting a bucket that holds nothing but open uploads aborts
those uploads, as on S3.
A part sent again needs room for both copies until the new one lands.
.SS Reading by part number
.B "GET /bucket/key?partNumber=N"
(and HEAD) answers part
.I N
of the object, as on S3. The AWS CRT, the Java transfer manager and several Go
clients use this to download a multipart object in parallel. The answer is a
.B 206
of the part's bytes, with
.BI "Content\-Range: bytes " first \- last / size ,
the part's Content\-Length, the whole object's ETag and
.BR x\-amz\-mp\-parts\-count .
.PP
Parts are numbered from 1 to the part count, in the order the completion listed
them. A single\-part object (a put, or any copy) is its own part 1, answered the
same way but without the parts count. An empty object answers a 200 with no body.
A number past the part count is
.BR "416 InvalidPartNumber" ,
and a number outside 1 to 10000 is
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
A Range header together with a part number is
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
The conditional headers apply as on a plain GET, before the part is looked at.
.PP
CompleteMultipartUpload records the part sizes in the object's header, as
.BR x\-socketley\-part\-sizes ,
which is never sent to a client. The header holds one size when every part except
the last has that size, as in every in\-place completion. Otherwise it holds the
list of sizes, up to 4 KiB of it. An upload with a checksum algorithm also records
its parts' checksums, in base64, up to 4 KiB of them
.RB ( x\-socketley\-part\-checksums ).
The journal carries these headers as it carries the rest of the header.
.PP
A longer list, which any upload S3 takes may need, follows the object's body in
the file instead. It takes 20 bytes plus 12 per part. When it holds checksums, it
takes 24 bytes plus 12 and the checksum's width per part. The header names where
the list begins and its SHA\-256
.RB ( x\-socketley\-part\-layout ).
A read by part number of such an object first reads the list from the file. A list
that does not match its digest is
.BR "500 InternalError" .
An in\-place completion whose checksums do not fit the room its header was given
writes them after the last part in the same way, and still copies nothing.
.PP
A multipart object completed before part sizes were recorded answers any part
number within its count with
.B "501 NotImplemented"
rather than a guess. A plain GET or a Range reads it as before.
.PP
CopyObject makes a single\-part object, as on S3. The runtime hashes a multipart
source's bytes on the way through, and the copy's ETag is their md5, with no
.BR \-N .
When the copy's checksum must be computed, it is computed in that same pass.
.SS Versioning
.B PutBucketVersioning
with
.B Enabled
makes every PUT, CopyObject and CompleteMultipartUpload in the bucket a new
version of its key. Each version has an id of its own (32 hex digits), answered
in
.BR x\-amz\-version\-id .
The earlier versions stay. GET, HEAD, DeleteObject and the tagging operations take
.BI ?versionId= ID
to name one version. A GET without a version id reads the latest version.
.PP
A DELETE without a version id puts a delete marker in front of the key. The
answer is 204 with
.B "x\-amz\-delete\-marker: true"
and the marker's id. After that, a GET or HEAD of the key is
.B "404 NoSuchKey"
with
.BR "x\-amz\-delete\-marker: true" ,
and a GET of the marker by its id is
.BR "405 MethodNotAllowed" .
A listing leaves the key out.
.PP
A DELETE with a version id removes that version for good. Removing the marker
undeletes the key. Removing the latest version makes the version behind it the
latest.
.PP
An object written before versioning was enabled is the
.B null
version. A GET, HEAD or GetObjectTagging of it names it with
.BR "x\-amz\-version\-id: null" .
.B Suspended
keeps the existing versions and writes the
.B null
version, which the next write or delete marker replaces. As in S3, a bucket never
becomes unversioned again.
.B GetBucketVersioning
of a bucket that was never versioned answers an empty configuration. MFA delete is
refused with
.BR "501 NotImplemented" .
A version id that could not be one is
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ,
and a version id that the key has no version of is
.BR "404 NoSuchVersion" .
.PP
The latest version of a key is its file at the usual path. A read without a
version id, on any shard, is therefore the same read as in an unversioned bucket.
When a new latest version replaces a version, the runtime first links the
replaced version to
.IB dir / bucket /versions/ ab / sha256(key) . id
and syncs that directory. Only then does it rename the new file over the old one.
The 256 directories of
.B versions/
are made together with the configuration. Shard 0 changes the configuration like
the CORS and lifecycle documents (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ).
While the owner of a key is unsure of the configuration, it writes nothing to the
key, and a write through another shard is answered 503.
.PP
Each version's header carries its id and its order among the key's versions. The
shard that a body lands on writes them into the header before the body is synced.
The key's owner keeps these values unless a newer version of the key, or a change
of the bucket's versioning, came first. That includes a change the owner learns of
only by reading the configuration again before the write. In that case the owner
writes the header again with its own values.
.PP
A versioned put takes a file of the pool, as an unversioned put does. It is
answered at its rename, once the link of the version it replaces and that link's
directory are synced. The syncs of its own directory and of
.B pool/
follow the answer. A crash that takes the rename leaves the put whole in
.BR pool/ .
The next start then links the version the put would have replaced before it moves
the put into place. This also happens when the bucket was versioned only after
the put was answered. A versioned put therefore waits for one sync more than an
unversioned put when it replaces a version, and for none more when its key is
new. A version removed for good is unlinked. When it is the latest, the version
behind it is renamed over it instead.
.PP
The journal records each key's other versions and delete markers beside its
latest object. A start after a clean stop therefore reads a versioned bucket from
its journal, like any other bucket. A journal written before the journal recorded
versions is read from the files once and written again. Any other start reads
.B versions/
in the same parallel walk as
.BR data/ .
The headers give each key's versions in order, and its delete markers. A version
left under both its names by a crash between the link and the rename is kept
once, as the latest. A key whose latest file is gone gets its newest version back.
.PP
With a warm cache on four CPUs, 100,000 keys of three versions each start in
0.19 s after a clean stop and in 0.86 s after a kill. Both took 1.3 s when every
start read the files.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
all versions of a key belong to the key's owner. A shard that does not own the key
reads a version by its id from the file where it is. If a rename moved the file
meanwhile, the shard looks in the other place once. Before a new version, a marker
or a removal that changes the latest is answered, every shard drops its held copy
of the latest.
.B ListObjectVersions
asks every shard and merges their pages.
.SS Object lock
A bucket created with
.B "x\-amz\-bucket\-object\-lock\-enabled: true"
is versioned and locked from the start.
.B PutObjectLockConfiguration
also locks an existing bucket, as S3 now allows, but only when its versioning is
.BR Enabled ;
otherwise the request fails with 409
.BR InvalidBucketState .
Once a bucket is locked, its versioning cannot be suspended (409
.BR InvalidBucketState )
and its lock cannot be removed. A configuration without a
.B Rule
only removes the default retention.
The default retention is
.B GOVERNANCE
or
.B COMPLIANCE
for a number of
.B Days
or calendar
.BR Years .
Every new version that names no retention of its own gets the default, counted
from the moment the version is written.
.PP
To set a version's retention, send
.B x\-amz\-object\-lock\-mode
and
.B x\-amz\-object\-lock\-retain\-until\-date
on PutObject, CopyObject or CreateMultipartUpload, or call PutObjectRetention.
For CreateMultipartUpload, the completed object gets the retention. To set a
legal hold, send
.B x\-amz\-object\-lock\-legal\-hold
or call PutObjectLegalHold. GET and HEAD return all three headers. A copy never
inherits its source's lock; it gets only what its own request or its bucket
gives it. Asking for a lock in a bucket without object lock fails with
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
A retain\-until date that is not in the future fails with
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
.PP
PutObjectLockConfiguration, PutObjectRetention and PutObjectLegalHold must prove
the integrity of their body, and so must a PutObject that names a retention or a
legal hold. The runtime accepts and checks either of these:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Content\-MD5
or an
.B x\-amz\-checksum\-*
header of the body, as S3 requires.
.IP \(bu 2
A SigV4 signature over the body's SHA\-256, that is, an
.B x\-amz\-content\-sha256
that is a hash. This is all that an SDK from before checksums sends.
.PP
A PutObject that only receives the bucket's default retention needs neither.
.PP
A version cannot be removed while its legal hold is ON, or while its retention
date is still ahead of the server's clock (UTC). A DELETE or DeleteObjects entry
that names such a version fails with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
A DELETE without a version id still places a delete marker in front of it.
.PP
.B COMPLIANCE
retention cannot be overridden by anyone; it can only be lengthened.
.B GOVERNANCE
retention can be lengthened by anyone. Only a request with
.B "x\-amz\-bypass\-governance\-retention: true"
can shorten or remove it, change it to COMPLIANCE, or delete the version it
protects, and the caller must have
.BR s3:BypassGovernanceRetention .
Every key of the runtime has that permission unless the bucket's policy denies
it. An anonymous caller has it only when the policy allows it. No bypass
overrides a legal hold.
.PP
A lifecycle rule never expires a locked version. The expiry of a current version
may still place a delete marker in front of it. DeleteBucket refuses a bucket that
still holds versions.
.PP
The retention and the legal hold are entries in the version's header, so the
journal and the index carry them too. The shard that owns the key makes each
change under the key's lane. It writes the change in place into the header's tag
slots, which is crash\-safe in the same way as a tag change. Once a version has a
lock, its slot holds its tags, retention and hold together, so a torn write leaves
the previous slot intact. When the header has no room, the runtime writes the
whole header again. A version that never had a lock keeps the slot format that an
older binary reads.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
a change or removal that arrives on a shard that does not own the key is decided
by the shard that owns it, and the bypass travels with the request. Shard 0
decides the bucket's configuration, as it does for versioning, in the order of the
bucket's changes.
.SS Event notifications
A bucket's
.B NotificationConfiguration
(PutBucketNotificationConfiguration, GetBucketNotificationConfiguration) sends
S3's event records to webhooks. Its
.BR QueueConfiguration ,
.B TopicConfiguration
and
.B CloudFunctionConfiguration
entries are all treated the same way. Each entry has:
.IP \(bu 2
an
.BR Id ;
.IP \(bu 2
a destination
.BI arn:socketley:webhook::: name\fR,
where
.I name
is a line of
.BR \-\-notify\-targets ;
.IP \(bu 2
one or more
.B Event
elements;
.IP \(bu 2
an optional
.B Filter
with an
.B S3Key
prefix rule and suffix rule.
.PP
The runtime emits the following events. An entry names each one by its full name
or with
.BR * .
.IP \(bu 2
.BR s3:ObjectCreated:Put ,
.B :Post
(a form upload),
.B :Copy
and
.BR :CompleteMultipartUpload .
.IP \(bu 2
.B s3:ObjectRemoved:Delete
(a key deleted, or a version removed by its id) and
.B :DeleteMarkerCreated
(a delete in a versioned bucket).
.IP \(bu 2
.B s3:LifecycleExpiration:Delete
and
.B :DeleteMarkerCreated
(a lifecycle rule's expiry, which sends no ObjectRemoved event).
.IP \(bu 2
.B s3:ObjectTagging:Put
and
.BR :Delete .
.PP
The other event names S3 has (ObjectRestore, Replication, .\|.\|.) are accepted
and send nothing.
.PP
A destination that is not a webhook's ARN, or that names a webhook the targets
file does not list, fails with
.B "400 InvalidArgument"
("Unable to validate the following destination configurations", with the ARN as
.BR ArgumentName ).
The request can skip this check by sending
.BR "x\-amz\-skip\-destination\-validation: true" .
These are also refused with
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" :
.IP \(bu 2
an unknown event;
.IP \(bu 2
a filter rule other than one prefix and one suffix;
.IP \(bu 2
two configurations with the same Id;
.IP \(bu 2
two configurations that share an event on overlapping keys;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR EventBridgeConfiguration ,
which this endpoint cannot reach.
.PP
A document that is not a configuration fails with
.BR "400 MalformedXML" .
An empty
.B NotificationConfiguration
removes the configuration. The configuration is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /notification\fR,
and shard 0 changes it like the bucket's other configuration documents.
.PP
The runtime POSTs each event to its webhook as S3's message, with one record in a
.B Records
array. The request carries
.BR "Content\-Type: application/json" ,
.B "Connection: keep\-alive"
and
.BR X\-Socketley\-Event\-Id .
.B X\-Socketley\-Event\-Id
is a number that a receiver can use to deduplicate events:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{"Records":[{"eventVersion":"2.1","eventSource":"aws:s3",
  "awsRegion":"us\-east\-1","eventTime":"2026\-10\-02T12:00:00.123Z",
  "eventName":"ObjectCreated:Put",
  "userIdentity":{"principalId":"socketley"}, .\|.\|.,
  "s3":{"s3SchemaVersion":"1.0","configurationId":"thumbs",
    "bucket":{"name":"photos","ownerIdentity":{.\|.\|.},
              "arn":"arn:aws:s3:::photos"},
    "object":{"key":"2026/a+b.jpg","size":5821,
      "eTag":"9b2cf535f27731c974343645a3985328",
      "versionId":".\|.\|.","sequencer":"00063F7A2B1C0D9E"}}}]}
.fi
.RE
.PP
In the record:
.IP \(bu 2
The key is form\-encoded as S3 encodes it: a space becomes
.BR + ,
.B /
is kept, and other bytes become
.BI % XX\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A removal's record has no size or eTag.
.IP \(bu 2
versionId is present in a versioned bucket.
.IP \(bu 2
The sequencer is sixteen hex digits. It grows with every event of the shard that
owns the key, so sorting by it puts a key's events in order.
.IP \(bu 2
.B userIdentity.principalId
is the requester. For a signed request it is the id of the user who owns the
request's key, or
.B socketley
for a key that belongs to no user. For a request without credentials, let in by
.B \-\-anonymous
or by a bucket policy, it is
.BR anonymous .
.IP \(bu 2
.B requestParameters.sourceIPAddress
is the client's address. An IPv4 client on a dual\-stack socket appears by its
IPv4 address. A client on a unix socket has no address.
.PP
A lifecycle expiry names
.B s3.amazonaws.com
as the requester and gives no address. A change that a script makes through
.B socketley.storage
names neither a requester nor an address. With
.BR \-\-shards ,
both travel with the change to the shard that owns the key.
.PP
When a webhook is named by a host name, the runtime resolves it on a separate
thread. The event loop reads the answer from an eventfd through its ring, so no
delivery and no request waits on the resolver. The host is resolved:
.IP \(bu 2
once at start;
.IP \(bu 2
again after every one of its addresses has refused a connection, at most once a
second;
.IP \(bu 2
every minute while events are being delivered to it.
.PP
A host with several addresses is tried address by address before it is resolved
again. Stopping the runtime waits for a resolve in progress; the system
resolver's own timeout bounds that wait.
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_NOTIFY_HOSTS
names a file of
.I name address
lines, one per line, that the runtime consults before the system resolver. It is
a test aid.
.PP
The shard that owns a key emits the key's events, whichever shard took the
request, so each change produces one event. The shard appends the event's record to
.IB dir /.notify/outbox. shard
when the change starts. It syncs the record alongside the change's own syncs,
together with every other record waiting at that moment. The change is answered
only once both are durable. An answered change's event therefore survives a
crash, and the request's path pays no second sync.
.PP
The event is sent only after the change is durable and after a second record
saying the change was made. That record is synced before the next change of the
key may start. At start, the runtime keeps an event that has no such record only
when the object files show the change. For a write, they must show its ETag,
size, time and version; for a removal, the key or version must be gone. A crash therefore never sends an event for
a change that was not made.
.PP
A webhook receives up to four events at once, each on its own connection. The
events of one object are sent one at a time and in the order they happened, so a
removal never overtakes the write before it. An event that concerns no object
waits for the previous event of the same kind. A 2xx answer delivers the event.
Anything else is a failure, including a refused or failed connection and no
answer within 10 seconds. A failed event is tried again after 0.25 s, with the
delay doubling up to 8 s.
.PP
When an event is delivered, the runtime appends a record saying so. It writes
that record at once, but syncs it with the next event's record rather than on its
own. At start, the runtime sends again every event that has no such record. An
event is therefore delivered at least once. It is delivered more than once only
when a crash, or a 2xx answer lost on the way, leaves its delivery unrecorded.
.PP
At every start, the outbox is written again with only the undelivered events. It
is also emptied once it has grown past a megabyte with nothing left to send. At
most 10000 events wait per shard. Past that, a new event is dropped and counted in
.BR notify_dropped ,
and its change is answered all the same.
.PP
.B stats
shows these counters per shard:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR notify_sent ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR notify_failed :
failed attempts;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR notify_queued :
events waiting now;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR notify_dropped ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR notify_resolves :
host names resolved;
.IP \(bu 2
.B notify_connects
and
.BR notify_reuses :
deliveries that went on a connection kept from the previous delivery.
.PP
The runtime keeps a webhook's connection open for the next event, as a proxy
does with its backends. It does so when the answer is HTTP/1.1, does not say
.BR "Connection: close" ,
and ends where its framing says. The connection is closed:
.IP \(bu 2
after 4 seconds with nothing on it;
.IP \(bu 2
after a minute, so that a host name that moved is followed;
.IP \(bu 2
whenever the webhook has closed it. The runtime checks for this before it uses
the connection.
.PP
Over https, this saves a handshake per event: 3,000 events to a local https
webhook took 4.5 seconds with a connection each and 0.6 seconds on one kept
connection.
.SS Replication
A bucket's
.B ReplicationConfiguration
(PutBucketReplication, GetBucketReplication, DeleteBucketReplication) copies the
bucket's new versions, and the delete markers its clients make, to buckets of
another S3 endpoint \(em another storage runtime, or any S3. The configuration is
S3's document: a
.B Role
and one or more
.BR Rule s.
Each rule has:
.IP \(bu 2
an
.BR ID ;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.BR Priority ;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Status
of Enabled or Disabled;
.IP \(bu 2
the keys it covers: a rule\-level
.B Prefix
in the first schema, or in the second schema a
.B Filter
of a
.BR Prefix ,
a
.B Tag
or an
.B And
of both;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Destination
with the
.B Bucket
as
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket\fR,
and optionally an
.BR Account ,
a
.B StorageClass
(sent as the replica's
.BR x\-amz\-storage\-class )
and an
.B AccessControlTranslation
to Destination;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR DeleteMarkerReplication .
.PP
The last path segment of the Role names a target of
.BR \-\-replication\-targets .
Every rule of the configuration writes with that target's endpoint and
credentials:
.RS
.PP
.nf
<ReplicationConfiguration>
  <Role>arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/backup</Role>
  <Rule><ID>docs</ID><Priority>1</Priority><Status>Enabled</Status>
    <Filter><Prefix>docs/</Prefix></Filter>
    <Destination><Bucket>arn:aws:s3:::docs\-copy</Bucket></Destination>
    <DeleteMarkerReplication><Status>Enabled</Status></DeleteMarkerReplication>
  </Rule>
</ReplicationConfiguration>
.fi
.RE
.PP
As in S3, a bucket whose versioning is not Enabled cannot be given a replication
configuration
.RB ( "400 InvalidRequest" ).
While a bucket has one, its versioning cannot be suspended
.RB ( "409 InvalidBucketState" ).
These configurations are also refused, each with
.B 400
InvalidRequest or InvalidArgument:
.IP \(bu 2
a second\-schema rule without DeleteMarkerReplication;
.IP \(bu 2
delete marker replication with a tag filter;
.IP \(bu 2
rules of both schemas;
.IP \(bu 2
two rules with the same ID or the same priority;
.IP \(bu 2
overlapping first\-schema prefixes.
.PP
A Role that names no target fails with
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
A document that is not a replication configuration fails with
.BR "400 MalformedXML" .
.B ExistingObjectReplication
Enabled, ReplicaModifications Enabled, a
.BR ReplicaKmsKeyID ,
and replication time control or metrics fail with
.BR "501 NotImplemented" ,
rather than being accepted and not honoured. The runtime does not check the
destination bucket when the configuration is put. The document is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /replication\fR,
and shard 0 changes it like the bucket's other configuration documents.
.PP
For each destination bucket, the matching enabled rule with the highest priority
decides. A version is copied when such a rule exists. A delete marker is copied
when that rule replicates delete markers. A rule with a tag filter never matches
a delete marker, and the first schema always replicates them.
.PP
A version that is to be copied is written with
.B "x\-amz\-replication\-status: PENDING"
in its header, and GET and HEAD return that header. The status becomes
.B COMPLETED
once every destination has the version, or
.B FAILED
once one destination has refused it. A FAILED status stays FAILED. The status is
written in place into the header's slot, which then carries it together with the
tags, the retention and the hold.
.PP
The rules a version is copied by are decided when its metadata is made, which
happens on:
.IP \(bu 2
a PutObject or POST;
.IP \(bu 2
a CopyObject (the source's status is never copied);
.IP \(bu 2
a CreateMultipartUpload, for the object that its completion makes;
.IP \(bu 2
a script's put.
.PP
A later tag change or tag removal of a COMPLETED or PENDING version is copied too,
to the same version id at the destination, as in S3. It has no status of its
own. If the destination refuses it, the runtime logs it and gives up. As in S3,
these are not copied:
.IP \(bu 2
a version removed by its id;
.IP \(bu 2
a delete marker that a lifecycle rule made;
.IP \(bu 2
a version written before the configuration;
.IP \(bu 2
an SSE\-C version, which has no status.
.PP
The runtime copies a version with a signed PutObject streamed from the version's
file. The request carries the version's Content\-Type, Cache\-Control,
Content\-Disposition, Content\-Encoding, Content\-Language, Expires, website
redirect, user metadata and tags. It also carries the version's MD5 as
Content\-MD5 and its full\-object checksum, and the destination checks the bytes
against them. A multipart version is copied by a multipart upload with the same
parts and part numbers, so the replica has the same ETag and composite checksum.
.PP
An SSE\-S3 version is read in the clear, and sent over the target's https when
the target's URL is https. It is written with
.BR "x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption: AES256" ,
which a destination without a master key refuses. A delete marker is copied as a
signed DeleteObject of the key. A tag change is copied as a signed
PutObjectTagging or DeleteObjectTagging of the version. Every request carries
.BR "x\-amz\-replication\-status: REPLICA" .
.PP
A storage runtime that is the destination judges such a write as
.BR s3:ReplicateObject ,
such a delete as
.B s3:ReplicateDelete
and such a tag change as
.B s3:ReplicateTags
(see
.BR "Bucket policy" ).
It honours the header only for a user whom the policies allow that action and
do not deny the write it stands for. From anyone else, the request is the plain
write it is. The destination marks the version REPLICA. It never copies a
replica, or a replica's delete marker, any further, so two buckets that replicate
to each other copy each change once.
.PP
The request also names the source's version id in
.BR x\-socketley\-replica\-version\-id .
A storage runtime that is the destination gives that id to the replica or its
delete marker, as S3 does. It does so only while the id is still newer than every
version the key has there and is dated no more than fifteen minutes past the
destination's clock. Otherwise the replica gets an id of its own. Another S3
stores the replication header as S3 does and ignores the other header, so there a
replica's version id is its own. A replica's time and owner are always the
destination's own.
.PP
The shard that owns a key queues the key's copies in
.IB dir /.replicate/outbox. shard\fR,
one entry per destination bucket. As with events (see
.BR "Event notifications" ),
the entries are synced alongside the change itself and before the change is
answered, so a crash never copies a change that was not made.
.PP
Each target has up to 16 copies in flight, each on its own connection. A key's
copies go one at a time, in the order they were made. An unencrypted body of
64 KiB or more goes to an http target straight from its file by splice, without
passing through the runtime.
.PP
These failures are tried again after 0.25 s, with the delay doubling up to 8 s:
.IP \(bu 2
a refused connection;
.IP \(bu 2
a stall of 10 seconds;
.IP \(bu 2
no answer within 120 seconds for the whole request;
.IP \(bu 2
an answer of 408, 409, 429, or a 5xx other than 501.
.PP
The runtime logs the first failure of a run of retries. Any other answer, or a
version whose bytes cannot be read, makes the version FAILED and is logged with
the destination's error code.
.PP
An entry is retired after the version's status is written. A crash between the
two copies the version again, which is harmless. At start, the runtime queues the
outbox again, and also every version that its index still marks PENDING but that
has no entry. An answered version is therefore copied even if its entry was lost.
A target's host name is resolved off the event loop, as a webhook's is.
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_REPLICATION_HOSTS
is the test aid that points a host name elsewhere.
.PP
.B stats
shows these counters per shard:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR replication_pending :
entries waiting now;
.IP \(bu 2
.B replication_completed
and
.BR replication_failed :
versions and delete markers;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR replication_retries ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR replication_bytes :
body bytes a destination accepted;
.IP \(bu 2
.B replication_connects
and
.BR replication_reuses :
requests that went on a connection kept from the previous request, as for a
webhook.
.SS Cluster
Each node of a cluster (\fB\-\-nodes\fR) keeps every bucket, and every node takes
reads and writes, so a client or a load balancer may use any of them. A node
sends each change it makes to every other node. It sends from its replication
outbox, in the way a bucket's replication sends a version (see
.BR Replication ).
The change is in that outbox, synced, before the client is told it was made.
These changes are sent:
.IP \(bu 2
a version or an object, as a PutObject or as a multipart upload in the same
parts, so its ETag is the same everywhere;
.IP \(bu 2
a delete marker, a tag change, a removal, and a version removed by its id;
.IP \(bu 2
a version's retention or legal hold;
.IP \(bu 2
a bucket created with its owner, a bucket removed, and a configuration document
given to a bucket or removed from it (versioning, object lock, policy, lifecycle,
CORS, encryption, tags, website, logging, quota, public access block,
notification, replication);
.IP \(bu 2
the IAM API's users, groups and roles;
.IP \(bu 2
share links with their downloads.
.PP
A node does not send on what another node sent it. Each node runs a bucket's
lifecycle rules itself, so expiries are each node's own. So are a bucket's event
notifications, which only the node where the change was made sends. So is the
bucket's replication to another endpoint, which only that node copies.
.PP
Every node ends with the same state, whatever order the changes reach it in:
.IP \(bu 2
A version keeps the id, the time and the lock that its node gave it. A version
older than the key's latest goes behind the latest rather than in front of it. A
node that has not yet heard of the bucket's versioning answers such a version
.B "503 SlowDown"
until it has.
.IP \(bu 2
In a bucket that keeps no versions, the object is the write made last. The time
it was written on its node decides, and then the larger ETag.
.IP \(bu 2
A removal is stamped with its time as well. A node keeps a record of what it
removed for 24 hours, across a restart, in
.IB dir /.removed. shard\fR.
An older write that crosses the removal on its way is therefore not made. A node
sends a removal even if it did not have the key yet.
.IP \(bu 2
The IAM document is the one changed last on any node. If two admins change IAM on
two nodes at the same moment, only the later change is kept.
.IP \(bu 2
Each node counts a share link's downloads, and the counts are added up. The
link's limit holds across the nodes once their counts have met.
.PP
A node that is sent a write it already has, or one older than what it has,
answers
.B "412 PreconditionFailed"
to the node that sent it. That node takes the answer as delivered.
.PP
A node that was stopped is sent what it missed once it is back, as a replication
target is. A change for a bucket that another lane is still creating or emptying
waits for that lane for up to 15 minutes, and is then given up and logged. When a
node falls more than 100000 changes behind a shard, the shard drops its queue for
that node, which bounds the memory a long absence takes. Once the node answers
again, the shard sends it everything instead, as described next.
.PP
A node whose data is new to another node \(em one that joined the cluster, or
started again with an empty
.B \-\-dir
\(em is sent everything the other node holds. Every node keeps a random id of its
data in
.IB dir /.cluster\-id\fR.
It asks every other node for its id at start and every 30 seconds. When an id is
new, the node sends:
.IP \(bu 2
the IAM document;
.IP \(bu 2
the share links;
.IP \(bu 2
every bucket with its documents;
.IP \(bu 2
every version, delete marker and object it holds, 500 keys at a time, asking
first whether the other node already has each one;
.IP \(bu 2
last, what it removed in the last 24 hours.
.PP
The node notes how far it got in
.IB dir /.replicate/seed. shard . node
so a restart continues from there.
.PP
All nodes need the same credentials and the same
.BR \-\-sse\-key\-file .
An SSE\-S3 object is sent in the clear over the node's endpoint and encrypted
again there, so use https between nodes that are not on one host. The nodes'
clocks must be within a second of one another, since the time a write was made
decides between two writes of one key. An SSE\-C object is not sent, because its
key belongs to its client.
.PP
.B stats
shows these counters per shard:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cluster_pending :
changes waiting for another node;
.IP \(bu 2
.B cluster_sent
and
.BR cluster_failed :
changes the other nodes accepted, and changes given up;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cluster_in :
bucket, IAM and share records another node sent here;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cluster_seeds :
nodes this shard began to send everything to;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cluster_seeded :
versions and objects it queued for them;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cluster_seeding :
nodes it has not yet queued everything for, or not yet asked;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cluster_overflows :
queues dropped;
.IP \(bu 2
.B cluster_synced
and
.BR cluster_sync_timeouts :
writes answered once the number of nodes that
.B sync
names had them, and writes answered once the wait was over.
.PP
Three nodes on one host, 4 KiB PutObject from 16 clients for 15 seconds: the two
other nodes keep up as the writes come. When the clients stop, 4 changes are
waiting, and none 0.4 seconds later. On a 4\-vCPU VPS with the two other nodes on
tmpfs, a node takes 1800 PutObject a second, against 2660 alone. Its own work per
write grows from 450 to 557 microseconds of CPU, which is the write read back and
sent twice. With 1 MiB PutObject from 8 clients, a node takes 200 a second
against 415 alone, at 5.7 against 4.4 milliseconds of CPU each, with the two
other nodes taking every megabyte on the same four CPUs.
.SS Scrub
With
.BR \-\-scrub ,
every shard reads back each version it holds from its file, one key at a time in
order. It paces the reads so that one pass over the shard's bytes takes the given
period, but reads at least a megabyte a second. A pass begins one period after
the previous pass began. Every 30 seconds the shard notes how far the pass got in
.IB dir /.scrub. shard\fR,
so a restart continues from there.
.PP
The bytes are checked against the MD5 that the object's header names:
.IP \(bu 2
A whole object is checked against its MD5.
.IP \(bu 2
A multipart object is checked part by part against the ETag its parts make.
.IP \(bu 2
An SSE\-S3 or SSE\-KMS object is checked in its plaintext. A chunk whose tag fails
also makes the object corrupt.
.PP
An SSE\-C object, whose key only its client has, and an object whose parts are
not recorded, are passed over and counted as skipped.
.PP
A copy whose bytes differ is logged as an error once, however many passes find
it. On a runtime that is not a node of a cluster, the copy stays as it is, to be
restored from a copy elsewhere. On a node of a cluster (\fB\-\-nodes\fR), the
runtime asks the other nodes in turn for the version, named by its id, or for the
object of a bucket that keeps no versions. It asks with a HEAD that must answer
the same ETag. The first node that holds the copy whole is asked to send it again.
.PP
Before that, the runtime takes the corrupt copy away, but only while it is still
the copy the scrub read. Taking it away sends no event, sends no copy to the other
nodes and records no removal. The node's copy comes back under its own id, and
behind the latest version when it was behind it before. Until it is back, a read
of it on this node finds nothing.
.PP
.B stats
shows these counters per shard:
.IP \(bu 2
.B scrub_objects
and
.BR scrub_bytes :
read;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR scrub_corrupt :
found, each counted once;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR scrub_skipped ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B scrub_passes
and
.BR scrub_last_pass_ms :
how long the last whole pass took;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR scrub_heals :
corrupt copies another node was asked to send again;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR scrub_unhealed :
corrupt copies that no other node held whole.
.SS Compression
With
.BR \-\-compress ,
every shard looks, one key at a time, at the versions written since its last pass
that have now gone unwritten for the given duration. It compresses each such
version that is stored in the clear, has one part and holds at least 4 KiB:
.IP \(bu 2
The shard cuts the version's bytes into chunks of 64 KiB and compresses each
chunk as an LZ4 block. A chunk that compression does not make shorter is kept as
it is.
.IP \(bu 2
It writes the chunks beside the version, with a table of where each chunk lies.
.IP \(bu 2
It puts the result in the version's place under the key's lane, but only while
the version is still the one it read.
.PP
A version is left as it was when its first megabyte, or its whole body, does not
get an eighth shorter. A pass begins once a quarter of the duration, and at least
a second, has passed since the previous pass's window. What each pass covered is
noted in
.IB dir /.compact. shard ,
so each version is looked at once. The reads go at most 64 MiB a second.
.PP
Compression changes nothing a client sees:
.IP \(bu 2
The ETag, the checksums, the size and the time stay the version's own.
.IP \(bu 2
A range opens only the chunks it touches.
.IP \(bu 2
A copy is made of the bytes, not of the chunks.
.IP \(bu 2
Replication, the scrub, media and WebDAV read a compressed version as they read
any other.
.PP
A version under SSE, a multipart object and an object under 4 KiB are never
compressed.
.PP
.B stats
shows these counters per shard:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR compressed_objects :
versions compressed;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR compressed_skipped :
versions read and left, as no shorter or changed meanwhile;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR compressed_failed ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B compressed_bytes_in
and
.BR compressed_bytes_out :
the bytes of the compressed versions, and what they take on disk now;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR compressed_passes ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR compressed_gets :
GETs whose chunks were opened from the file rather than from memory.
.SS S3 Select
SelectObjectContent runs one SQL expression over an object, with an optional
ScanRange. The object may be stored as:
.IP \(bu 2
CSV, with FileHeaderInfo USE, IGNORE or NONE, any field and record delimiter,
quotes and comments;
.IP \(bu 2
JSON, as DOCUMENT or LINES.
.PP
The answer is AWS's event stream, sent as HTTP chunks while the object is read.
It holds Records events as they come, a Progress event when the request asks for
one, then Stats (the bytes scanned, processed and returned) and End. When the
object cannot be read after the 200 has been sent, the stream carries an error
event instead.
.PP
The expression supports:
.IP \(bu 2
.B SELECT
.B *
or a list of expressions with
.BR AS ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B FROM S3Object
with an alias and a JSON path
.RB ( S3Object[*].items[*] );
.IP \(bu 2
.B WHERE
and
.BR LIMIT ;
.IP \(bu 2
columns by position
.RB ( _1 ),
by header name or by JSON path
.RB ( s.a.b[0] );
.IP \(bu 2
the comparisons,
.BR AND ,
.BR OR ,
.BR NOT ,
.BR LIKE ,
.BR BETWEEN ,
.BR IN ,
.BR "IS NULL" ,
arithmetic and
.BR || ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR CAST ,
.BR LOWER ,
.BR UPPER ,
.BR TRIM ,
.BR CHAR_LENGTH ,
.BR SUBSTRING ,
.B COALESCE
and
.BR NULLIF ;
.IP \(bu 2
the aggregates
.BR COUNT ,
.BR SUM ,
.BR AVG ,
.B MIN
and
.BR MAX .
.PP
A record over 1 MiB fails with
.BR OverMaxRecordSize .
GZIP, BZIP2 and Parquet input fail with
.BR "501 NotImplemented" .
The object is read the way a GET reads it, whether it is sealed, compressed or at
a tier. The request needs everything a GetObject of the object needs, SSE\-C
headers included.
.B stats
counts
.B selects
and
.B select_scanned
(the bytes their scans read).
.SS Tiers
A lifecycle rule's
.B Transition
or
.B NoncurrentVersionTransition
names a tier of
.B \-\-tiers
as its
.BR StorageClass .
When a version comes due, the shard that owns its key reads it and writes it to
the tier with one signed PUT. For a version of one part, the PUT's Content\-MD5 is
the version's own. The object is stored under
.IB prefix bucket / mtime \- md5 \- version / key
in the tier's bucket, so the same version from every node of a cluster is one
object there. Once the tier has it, the version's file here is reduced to its
header alone. The shard puts that header in place under the key's lane, but only
while the version is still the one that was read. These versions stay here:
.IP \(bu 2
a version under SSE;
.IP \(bu 2
a version larger than 5 GiB;
.IP \(bu 2
a multipart object whose part list follows its bytes.
.PP
Listings, GET and HEAD show a moved version with the tier's name as its
.BR x\-amz\-storage\-class .
A GET, a range or a part of a moved version is read from the tier half a
megabyte at a time, two windows ahead. It keeps the size, ETag, time and
checksums it always had. CopyObject and UploadPartCopy from a moved version fail
with
.BR "403 InvalidObjectState" ,
as S3 answers for an archived object. The scrub, replication and a cluster's
seeding pass it over.
.PP
A quarter day after the previous walk, each shard walks every tier's listing. It
removes from the tier whatever is no longer a version of a key the shard owns,
but no sooner than an hour after the tier took it. A version deleted, expired or
written over here is therefore gone from the tier within six hours.
.PP
.B stats
shows:
.IP \(bu 2
.B tier_moved
and
.BR tier_moved_bytes :
versions moved and their bytes;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR tier_failed ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR tier_moving :
versions waiting and in flight;
.IP \(bu 2
.B tier_reads
and
.BR tier_read_failures :
windows read back;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR tier_swept :
objects removed at a tier;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR tier_gets :
GETs read from a tier.
.SS Server\-side encryption
.B "x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption: AES256"
on PutObject, CreateMultipartUpload or CopyObject selects SSE\-S3. The runtime
stores the object encrypted under a key of its own, and wraps that key with the
runtime's master key (\fB\-\-sse\-key\-file\fR). Every answer about the object
says so with the same header: the PUT, the upload's steps, GET, HEAD and the
copy. A GET needs nothing extra. It returns the plaintext, and its ETag is the
MD5 of the plaintext, as for any object. Ranges, partNumber and conditions work
as they do for unencrypted objects.
.PP
.B aws:kms
(SSE\-KMS) works the same way, except that the object's key is wrapped by a key
derived from the master key and a KMS key id. The KMS key id is the one the
request names in
.BR x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption\-aws\-kms\-key\-id ,
or else the bucket default's, or else
.B arn:aws:kms:us\-east\-1:000000000000:alias/aws/s3
when neither names one. Any key id is accepted, and no key is kept except the
master key. Every answer names the key id as the write gave it. An encryption
context is accepted and not kept.
.PP
These requests fail:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR aws:kms:dsse :
.BR "501 NotImplemented" ;
.IP \(bu 2
any other value:
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ;
.IP \(bu 2
a KMS key or context without
.BR aws:kms :
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ;
.IP \(bu 2
the header on a GET or HEAD:
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" ,
since only a request that writes an object chooses its encryption.
.PP
.B PutBucketEncryption
sets a default for every new object of the bucket whose request asks for nothing
else. An
.B AES256
rule makes such objects SSE\-S3, and an
.B aws:kms
rule makes them SSE\-KMS under the rule's KMSMasterKeyID. The default covers
puts, copies into the bucket, uploads begun in it and a script's
.BR socketley.storage.put .
A bucket's rule is a document like its CORS rules. Shard 0 changes it after
telling every shard first (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ).
It is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /encryption
and read at every start. A rule that no longer parses still encrypts, and the log
shows an error.
.B DeleteBucketEncryption
stops the default for new objects and leaves the objects it encrypted as they
are.
.PP
With SSE\-C, the client supplies its own key with every request that touches the
object, as boto3 and the AWS CLI send it. The key travels in three headers:
.BR "x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption\-customer\-algorithm: AES256" ,
.B \-customer\-key
(the 32 bytes in base64) and
.BR \-customer\-key\-MD5 .
The runtime accepts SSE\-C over TLS only: its own TLS, or that of a proxy that
.B \-\-tls\-offloaded
names. The headers over plain HTTP from anywhere else, or over a unix node, fail
with
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" ,
and nothing is written.
.PP
The key is never stored. The object's data key is wrapped under the client's key,
and only that key opens it. A GET or HEAD without the headers therefore fails
with
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" .
With another key it fails with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" ,
and with a key\-MD5 that does not match the key it fails with
.BR "400 InvalidArgument" .
CopyObject and UploadPartCopy open an SSE\-C source with the
.B x\-amz\-copy\-source\-server\-side\-encryption\-customer\-*
headers. The parts of an SSE\-C upload carry the upload's key. As in S3, the ETag
of an SSE\-C object or part is not the MD5 of its bytes; it is derived from that
MD5 under the data key. Nothing of an SSE\-C object is kept in memory between
requests. A script's
.B socketley.storage.get
of an SSE\-C object is refused, since the script has no key to give.
.PP
When an SSE\-C upload completes, its part list is bound to its key, so a header
rewritten to drop trailing parts cannot be read. S3 does not ask
CompleteMultipartUpload for the customer key, but boto3 and the AWS CLI can send
the three headers with it. When they do, the key must be the upload's
.RB ( "403 AccessDenied"
otherwise), and the data key is wrapped again with the digest of the part list,
as for SSE\-S3.
.PP
A completion without the headers is bound instead by an HMAC\-SHA256 of the
object's encryption record and its part list. The HMAC's key is derived (HKDF)
from the
.B \-\-sse\-key\-file
master key. The HMAC is recorded in the header as
.B x\-socketley\-sse\-parts
and checked before every read, so every read then needs that master key too.
With neither the headers nor a master key, the completion fails with
.BR "400 InvalidRequest" ,
naming both ways out, and the upload stays open to be completed again.
.PP
An upload begun before this binding existed completes only with the headers,
since its object could not be told apart from an older one whose HMAC was
removed. Without the headers, its completion fails with
.B "400 InvalidRequest"
saying so. An object of an upload begun since then is never read with its part
list unbound: a missing or wrong HMAC gives
.BR "500 InternalError" .
An object completed without the key before this binding existed is read as it
was, unless it carries an HMAC that does not hold.
.PP
On disk, an encrypted object is its header followed by its sealed body. The
header records the scheme
.RB ( x\-socketley\-sse :
format, SSE\-S3 or SSE\-C, the chunk size, whether the body is one stream or one
per part, the master key's id and the wrapped data key). The body is the
plaintext in chunks of 64 KiB. Each chunk is sealed on its own with AES\-256\-GCM
and stored as a random 12\-byte nonce, the ciphertext and a 16\-byte tag. That
adds 28 bytes per 64 KiB.
.PP
Every new object and upload is written in format 2. In format 2, each chunk is
sealed together with three values. They are the part number its stream was
uploaded as (0 for an object that is one stream), the chunk's index in that
stream, and whether it ends the stream. Every stream ends in a chunk shorter than 64 KiB, which is empty when the
stream's length is a whole number of chunks. A completed upload's data key is
wrapped again with the digest of its part list, sizes and numbers. As a result,
each of the following fails to open rather than read as a shorter or mixed
object:
.IP \(bu 2
a header cut back to a chunk boundary;
.IP \(bu 2
chunks moved to another place, or swapped between two parts of one upload;
.IP \(bu 2
a header that names fewer parts, or other ones.
.PP
A range or a part reads and opens only the chunks it touches. When the range
reaches the end of a stream, it also opens the empty chunk that ends the stream.
A chunk that does not open ends the response at that point, with a line in the
log. Some objects are answered
.B "500 InternalError"
before anything else is sent: an object small enough to be read whole with its
header, or one whose data key does not unwrap. Objects written in format 1, which
bound a chunk to its index alone, are read as they were, and so are the parts of
an upload begun under format 1.
.PP
Each part of a multipart upload is a stream of its own. It is written where the
object needs it, as unencrypted parts are. It is found again by the part sizes
and numbers the object records (see
.BR "Reading by part number" ).
A copy of an encrypted object, or into an encrypted one, is opened and sealed
again on its way through, under a key of its own. A tag change, a new version and
the assembly of an upload move the sealed bytes as they are.
.PP
For bodies and windows of more than 64 KiB, the chunks are sealed and opened on
the hashing threads (\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS\fR). Smaller ones are
handled in line. A GET of an encrypted object cannot be spliced from the file.
Instead, the runtime reads it a megabyte at a time, opens it and writes it from
memory, reading the next megabyte while it sends the current one. An SSE\-S3
object of up to 60000 bytes is kept opened in memory like any other object.
.PP
Not supported: bucket keys, and changing the master key. A rule's BucketKeyEnabled
is kept and given back, and changes nothing. An object
opens only under the key that wrapped its data key.
.SS Bucket lifecycle
A bucket's lifecycle configuration is S3's
.B LifecycleConfiguration
document, with at most 1000 rules. Each rule has:
.IP \(bu 2
an
.BR ID :
at most 255 bytes, unique, and generated when left out;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.BR Status :
.B Enabled
or
.BR Disabled ;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Filter
that selects the objects it covers. An empty Filter covers the whole bucket.
Otherwise it holds one of these: a
.BR Prefix ;
a
.B Tag
(a
.B Key
and a
.B Value
the object's tags must have);
.B ObjectSizeGreaterThan
or
.B ObjectSizeLessThan
(each in bytes and exclusive); or an
.B And
of a prefix, any number of tags and the two sizes, all of which must hold. The
older form, a
.B Prefix
of the rule's own, is also accepted.
.IP \(bu 2
at least one action.
.PP
The actions are:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Expiration
by
.B Days
or by
.B Date
(midnight UTC);
.IP \(bu 2
.B AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload
by
.BR DaysAfterInitiation ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B ExpiredObjectDeleteMarker
.RB ( true ,
in an
.B Expiration
of its own);
.IP \(bu 2
.B NoncurrentVersionExpiration
by
.BR NoncurrentDays .
With
.B NewerNoncurrentVersions
(1 to 100), it expires only a version that that many noncurrent versions of its
key are newer than.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Transition
by
.B Days
(zero included) or by
.BR Date ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B NoncurrentVersionTransition
by
.BR NoncurrentDays ,
which takes
.B NewerNoncurrentVersions
as well.
.PP
Each transition goes to a
.B StorageClass
that names a tier of
.B \-\-tiers
(any other is
.BR "400 InvalidStorageClass" ;
see
.BR Tiers ).
A second transition of either kind in one rule is refused with
.BR "501 NotImplemented" ,
and the configuration that was there stays. Other errors:
.IP \(bu 2
A document that is not a lifecycle configuration is
.BR MalformedXML .
.IP \(bu 2
A value S3 would not accept is
.BR InvalidArgument :
Days of zero outside a transition, a Date that is not midnight, a repeated ID,
1001 rules, an
ObjectSizeLessThan of 0, or a NewerNoncurrentVersions of 0 or over 100.
.IP \(bu 2
A tag key or value that an object could not have is
.BR InvalidTag .
.IP \(bu 2
What S3 refuses as
.B InvalidRequest
is refused the same way. That is one tag key twice in an
.BR And ,
or sizes with nothing between them. It is also a tag or size filter on a rule
that aborts uploads (an upload has neither yet) or on a rule with
.BR ExpiredObjectDeleteMarker .
.PP
The rules are synced into
.IB dir / bucket /lifecycle
before the answer, so they survive a stop or a crash. They are deleted with the
bucket. With
.BR \-\-shards ,
a change is answered once no shard can go on with the old rules. A shard that is
unsure of the rules expires nothing in the bucket until it has read them (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ).
.PP
Each shard carries out the rules with a sweep over the keys and uploads it owns.
An object expires
.B Days
days after its last write, rounded up to the next midnight UTC as S3 does. For
example, an object written on the 1st at 10:30 with Days 1 goes at the midnight
that begins the 3rd. With a
.BR Date ,
every object under the rule goes from that midnight on, including objects written
later. The sweep removes an object exactly as a DeleteObject removes it \(em the
unlink synced, then the journal record \(em and a script's
.B on_delete
is told.
.PP
An object written again or given other tags after the sweep found it is judged as
it is then. A configuration changed or removed meanwhile is obeyed. The tags and
size that a filter checks come from the index in memory, so a filter on them
costs the walk no reads. An upload is aborted
.B DaysAfterInitiation
days after it began, rounded the same way. In a versioned bucket:
.IP \(bu 2
An expiring latest version is covered by a delete marker rather than removed, as
S3 does.
.IP \(bu 2
A noncurrent version is removed for good
.B NoncurrentDays
days after the version that replaced it was written. This is rounded the same
way and filtered by the version's own tags and size.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR ExpiredObjectDeleteMarker ,
a delete marker that is its key's only remaining version is removed.
.PP
A pass runs at each start, within a second of a change to the rules, and then
hourly. It walks the index a page at a time, with at most eight removals in
flight and a pause between steps. A bucket with a great many expiring keys is
therefore worked through without holding up other requests.
.B stats
counts what the sweep did in
.B lifecycle_expired
and
.BR lifecycle_aborted ,
and each pass that removed anything says so in the log.
.PP
When a rule will expire an object, the answer to a PutObject, and to a GetObject
or HeadObject of the latest version, carries
.B x\-amz\-expiration
(\fBexpiry\-date="\fIdate\fB", rule\-id="\fIid\fB"\fR). As in S3, the header
names the rule that expires the object first.
.PP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_LIFECYCLE_DAY_SECS
is a test aid that only the sweep reads. It makes a lifecycle day last that many
seconds (at most 86400), so a rule can be watched taking effect, with passes as
often as every second. It is never meant for a runtime holding data anyone wants.
.SS Bucket policy
A bucket's policy is S3's JSON document. It can make a bucket, or part of one,
readable by anyone, for static assets or downloads, while every other request to
the runtime still has to be signed. It can also refuse a signed key what the key
would otherwise be allowed to do. For example, this policy lets anyone read the
objects under
.BR public/ :
.PP
.nf
{
  "Version": "2012\-10\-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Sid": "PublicRead",
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": "*",
    "Action": "s3:GetObject",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::media/public/*"
  }]
}
.fi
.PP
The runtime accepts the part of S3's grammar that it can honour exactly. A
document that asks for anything else is refused with
.BR "400 MalformedPolicy" ,
naming what the runtime could not accept. A policy is never accepted to do less
than it says.
.TP
.B Version
The version must be 2012\-10\-17 or 2008\-10\-17, or left out. The
.B Id
may be any string.
.TP
.B Statement
The policy holds one statement or a list of them. Each statement has an optional
.B Sid
(unique), an
.B Effect
.RB ( Allow
or
.BR Deny ),
a
.BR Principal ,
an
.BR Action ,
a
.B Resource
and an optional
.BR Condition .
.BR NotPrincipal ,
.B NotAction
and
.B NotResource
are refused.
.TP
.B Principal
This element names the requesters that the statement applies to.
.B *
or
.B {"AWS": "*"}
means everyone, signed or not.
.B {"AWS": [...]}
names this runtime's users and access keys. Each entry is the access key itself,
or
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :user/ name
(where the name is a user's id or an access key), or
.BI arn:aws:iam:: account :root
for every key.
.IP
A
.B {"Service": ...}
principal is accepted but names no requester here. A statement that names only
services therefore matches nothing, whatever its conditions, and those
conditions are not checked. This way the policy that S3 documents for a log
target, which lets
.B logging.s3.amazonaws.com
write, is accepted as it is written. Federated and canonical\-user principals and
roles are refused.
.TP
.B Action
This element names the operations the statement covers, as one
.BI s3: Name
or a list of them, or
.B *
alone. Names take
.B *
and
.B ?
wildcards and are compared without case. A name must be one of S3's. Names that
this runtime does not implement (s3:RestoreObject,
s3:GetBucketAnalyticsConfiguration, .\|.\|.) are accepted and match nothing.
.IP
Some actions cover more than one operation. GetObject covers GetObject and
HeadObject, by part too. PutObject covers PutObject, CopyObject,
CreateMultipartUpload, UploadPart, UploadPartCopy and CompleteMultipartUpload.
DeleteObject covers DeleteObject and DeleteObjects. ListBucket covers ListObjects,
ListObjectsV2 and HeadBucket. PutBucketCORS, PutLifecycleConfiguration and
PutEncryptionConfiguration also cover the deletes of those configurations.
GetEncryptionConfiguration covers GetBucketEncryption. The rest are named as S3
names them (GetObjectTagging, AbortMultipartUpload, ListMultipartUploadParts,
ListBucketMultipartUploads, GetBucketLocation, CreateBucket, DeleteBucket,
GetBucketVersioning, PutBucketVersioning, .\|.\|.).
.IP
As in S3, a request that names a version with ?versionId= is judged as the
version's action. GET and HEAD of a version are GetObjectVersion, and so is a
copy whose source is named key?versionId=. DELETE of a version is
DeleteObjectVersion, and so is each DeleteObjects entry with a VersionId. Those
entries are judged one by one, and a refused entry is answered AccessDenied in the
result. The tagging operations on a version are GetObjectVersionTagging,
PutObjectVersionTagging and DeleteObjectVersionTagging. ListBucketVersions covers
ListObjectVersions.
.IP
Object lock has GetBucketObjectLockConfiguration and
PutBucketObjectLockConfiguration. It also has GetObjectRetention and
PutObjectRetention, which also covers a write that names a retention. It has
GetObjectLegalHold and PutObjectLegalHold, which also covers a write that names a
hold. Last, it has BypassGovernanceRetention, which is checked beside the action
of a request that sends the bypass header. GetBucketNotification and
PutBucketNotification cover the two notification configuration operations.
GetReplicationConfiguration and PutReplicationConfiguration cover the three
replication ones.
.IP
A write that sends
.B "x\-amz\-replication\-status: REPLICA"
is judged as a replication action. PutObject and the steps of a multipart upload
are then ReplicateObject instead of PutObject. Such a DeleteObject is
ReplicateDelete, and such a PutObjectTagging or DeleteObjectTagging is
ReplicateTags. A user allowed those three actions and nothing else can therefore
be the destination's replication user. The header counts only for a user allowed
the replication action, and a Deny of the write it stands for still refuses the
request. Anyone else's request is the plain write, and its header is ignored.
.TP
.B Resource
This element names what the statement covers, as one ARN or a list of them.
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket
is the bucket itself, and
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket / pattern
is its objects. In a pattern,
.B *
matches any run of characters and
.B ?
matches one. The bucket must be this one, or a pattern that covers it, as
.B arn:aws:s3:::*
does. Because IAM's
.B *
spans a slash, that pattern, which ends in
.BR * ,
also covers the bucket's objects. A statement must have an action that applies
to one of its resources: an object action to a key pattern, or a bucket action to
the bucket. Policy variables are refused.
.TP
.B Condition
This element sets conditions that a request must meet for the statement to
apply; every condition of a statement must hold.
.B IpAddress
and
.B NotIpAddress
test
.B aws:SourceIp
against addresses and CIDR ranges, IPv4 and IPv6. An IPv4 address mapped into
IPv6 counts as the IPv4 address.
.B Bool
on
.B aws:SecureTransport
tests whether the connection is TLS.
.BR StringEquals ,
.B StringNotEquals
and
.B Null
test
.BR s3:x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption ,
which is the request's
.B x\-amz\-server\-side\-encryption
header, compared as given. A request that does not send the header equals no
value. So
.B StringNotEquals
holds for it, and
.B Null
\fB"true"\fR holds for it alone.
.IP
PutObject, CopyObject and CreateMultipartUpload carry the header. UploadPart,
UploadPartCopy and CompleteMultipartUpload take the encryption that their
upload's creation chose and was judged on. So no statement with a condition on
the header applies to them, whether Allow or Deny. A bucket's default encryption
is not the header. S3's two statements that deny a PutObject without
.B AES256
therefore refuse a put that leaves encryption to the default. A client on a unix
socket has no address, so it is in no range: an
.B IpAddress
never holds for it and a
.B NotIpAddress
always does.
.IP
Beyond those, IAM's operator families work on the keys that a request here
carries. The operators are
.BR String (Not) Equals (IgnoreCase),
.BR String (Not) Like
with
.B *
and
.BR ? ,
.BR Arn (Not) Equals
and
.BR Arn (Not) Like
as their string forms,
.BR Numeric \&.\|.\|.
and
.BR Date \&.\|.\|.
(an epoch second or an ISO 8601 time),
.BR Bool ,
.B Null
and the IP pair. Each takes
.B IfExists
and the
.B ForAnyValue:
and
.B ForAllValues:
qualifiers.
.IP
The global keys are
.BR aws:Referer ,
.BR aws:UserAgent ,
.BR aws:SourceIp ,
.BR aws:SecureTransport ,
.BR aws:CurrentTime ,
.BR aws:EpochTime ,
.BR aws:username ,
.B aws:userid
(the user's id, absent for a request without credentials) and
.BR aws:PrincipalType .
The listing keys are
.BR s3:prefix ,
.B s3:delimiter
and
.B s3:max\-keys
(a listing's query). The other S3 keys are
.BR s3:versionid ,
the
.BR s3:x\-amz\- .\|.\|.
and
.BR s3:object\-lock\- .\|.\|.
keys (the request's headers of those names),
.B s3:RequestObjectTag/\fIkey\fR
and
.B s3:RequestObjectTagKeys
(the request's
.B x\-amz\-tagging
header),
.BR s3:signatureversion ,
.B s3:authType
and
.BR s3:TlsVersion .
.IP
A key the request does not carry fails its condition, except under
.BR IfExists ,
a negated operator or
.BR ForAllValues ,
as in IAM.
.B s3:ExistingObjectTag/\fIkey\fR
is refused, since the object's tags are not read before the request is judged.
Any other key or operator is refused too.
.PP
A request to a bucket with a policy is authenticated as usual and then judged. A
statement applies when its action, principal and resource match the request and
its conditions hold. An applying
.B Deny
refuses the request with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" ,
whatever else applies. An applying
.B Allow
lets in a request that carries no credentials, on a runtime without
.BR \-\-anonymous .
It also lets in a signed request from a user who does not own the bucket (see
.BR Users ).
.PP
A request whose signature is wrong is refused as usual, and is never taken for an
anonymous request. A presigned URL is a signed request. On a runtime with
.BR \-\-anonymous ,
every request without credentials is already let in, but a
.B Deny
for
.B *
still refuses it.
.PP
A copy is also judged as a GetObject of its source, by the source bucket's
policy, so an anonymous copy needs both. DeleteObjects names its keys in the
body, so it is judged against every key of the bucket. A
.B Deny
on any key refuses it, and only an
.B Allow
on
.BI arn:aws:s3::: bucket /*
lets an anonymous one in. A request that a policy refuses is answered before its
body is read.
.PP
The policy's own four operations are decided by ownership alone, for the bucket's
owner and an admin. A policy therefore can neither let an anonymous request read
or change it, nor lock the owner out of removing it. The Lua
.B on_request
hook can still refuse a request that a policy let in (see
.BR Lua ).
The hook never sees a request that the policy refused.
.PP
The runtime compiles the document when it is set, and again when a shard starts,
so each request is judged against compiled statements. As a result:
.IP \(bu 2
If none of the runtime's buckets has a policy, nothing is looked up.
.IP \(bu 2
A signed request to a bucket whose policy has no
.B Deny
looks up the bucket and nothing more.
.IP \(bu 2
Only a statement that compares
.B aws:SourceIp
asks the kernel for the client's address, once per connection.
.IP \(bu 2
The other keys are read from the request only when a statement that names them
applies.
.PP
As in S3, a request that a policy lets in learns that a key is missing only if it
may list the bucket the key would be in. A GET, HEAD or other object request for a
missing key gets
.B "404 NoSuchKey"
when
.B s3:ListBucket
on the bucket applies to the requester. For an anonymous request, that takes an
.BR Allow .
For a signed request, or on a runtime with
.BR \-\-anonymous ,
it takes no
.BR Deny .
When it does not apply, the request gets
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
For a copy, the bucket is the source's.
.PP
The policy is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /policy .
A new policy is written beside the file, synced and renamed over it, so it
survives a restart, and a crash leaves either the old one or the new one. The
policy is deleted with the bucket.
.PP
A stored policy may fail to compile when a shard starts, because the file was
edited or because this version refuses what an older one accepted. Such a policy
is not skipped. The bucket then refuses every request with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" ,
naming why, and the log has an error saying so. The exceptions are
PutBucketPolicy, GetBucketPolicy, DeleteBucketPolicy and GetBucketPolicyStatus by
the runtime's keys, so the policy can be read back and replaced or removed.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
shard 0 makes every change to a bucket's policy and to its CORS, lifecycle and
versioning configuration, whichever shard took the request. It makes them one at
a time per bucket, and answers a change only once no running shard can go on
with the old document. The steps are:
.IP \(bu 2
Shard 0 writes the new document beside the file as
.IB kind .new
(empty for a removal).
.IP \(bu 2
It asks every other shard to stop serving the bucket until that shard learns how
the change ended.
.IP \(bu 2
Only when all of them have promised, it renames the new document over the file
(or removes the file), tells the shards the new document and answers.
.PP
If shard 0 cannot reach a shard, because it is stopped, starting, wedged or cut
off, the change fails with
.BR "503 ServiceUnavailable" .
Nothing changes anywhere: shard 0 removes the new file and releases the shards
that promised.
.PP
A shard that promised and is never told the outcome serves nothing of that bucket
until it reads the outcome from the files. Until then it answers
503 ServiceUnavailable, which clients retry. While
.IB kind .new
exists, the change is undecided; once it is gone, the file is the document. A
shard reads the files again at its next request to the bucket and at its periodic
sweep. A lost message therefore delays a shard but never leaves it with the old
document. Between a shard's promise and the rename, a request on another shard
can be answered 503 in the same way. A shard that starts while a change is
undecided, or that takes a bucket up later than the others, reads the bucket's
documents the same way before serving it. A runtime with no change in flight pays
one counter load per request for this.
.SS Server access logging
For a logged bucket, the runtime writes every answer as a line in S3's server
access log format, and delivers the lines as objects to the bucket's target
bucket:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /photos?logging
<BucketLoggingStatus>
  <LoggingEnabled>
    <TargetBucket>logs</TargetBucket>
    <TargetPrefix>photos/</TargetPrefix>
  </LoggingEnabled>
</BucketLoggingStatus>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each line holds these fields:
.IP \(bu 2
the bucket's owner and the bucket;
.IP \(bu 2
the time;
.IP \(bu 2
the client's address;
.IP \(bu 2
the requester's access key (\- for none);
.IP \(bu 2
the request id;
.IP \(bu 2
the operation
.RB ( REST.GET.OBJECT ,
.BR REST.PUT.PART ,
.BR REST.PUT.VERSIONING ,
.B WEBSITE.GET.OBJECT
and so on). A DeleteObjects writes a
.B REST.POST.MULTI_OBJECT_DELETE
line and a
.B BATCH.DELETE.OBJECT
line for each key.
.IP \(bu 2
the key and the request line;
.IP \(bu 2
the status and the error code;
.IP \(bu 2
the bytes of body sent, and a put's size;
.IP \(bu 2
the milliseconds from the request's start to its answer;
.IP \(bu 2
the referer and user agent;
.IP \(bu 2
the signature version and how it was given;
.IP \(bu 2
the TLS cipher, the Host header and the TLS version.
.PP
A field this runtime does not know is \-, as S3 writes a field it has nothing
for. A line is written when the answer starts, so a download that is cut off is
logged as sent.
.PP
Each shard holds its lines for each target. It delivers them as one object once
it holds a minute's worth or 1 MiB. The object is named
.IB prefix YYYY-MM-DD-hh-mm-ss- unique,
or, with a PartitionedPrefix,
.IB prefix owner /us\-east\-1/ bucket / YYYY / MM / DD / YYYY-MM-DD-hh-mm-ss- unique
by the delivery's date. The object is put as a client's object would be. It is
encrypted when the target encrypts, and replicated and notified when the target
is. An object that cannot be put, because the target was deleted or has gone past
its quota, is dropped with a warning in the log. Lines still held when the
runtime stops are lost, which is at most a minute of them. The target must be a
bucket with the same owner as the logged one. It may be the logged bucket itself,
and the deliveries are not logged. The configuration is kept in
.IB dir / bucket /logging
and changed by shard 0 like the bucket's other documents.
.SS Public access block
A bucket's public access block keeps it from being opened to everyone by mistake:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /photos?publicAccessBlock
<PublicAccessBlockConfiguration>
  <BlockPublicPolicy>true</BlockPublicPolicy>
  <RestrictPublicBuckets>true</RestrictPublicBuckets>
</PublicAccessBlockConfiguration>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B BlockPublicPolicy
makes the runtime refuse a PutBucketPolicy whose policy is public with
.BR "403 AccessDenied" .
A policy is public when GetBucketPolicyStatus reports it as public.
A public policy that is already in place stays.
.B RestrictPublicBuckets
stops a public policy from admitting any request without credentials, including
requests to the bucket's website.
The runtime's users keep the access that the policy and their credentials give
them, because they all belong to one account.
.B BlockPublicAcls
and
.B IgnorePublicAcls
are stored and read back but have no effect, because the runtime refuses a public
ACL regardless of these settings. The block is stored in
.IB dir / bucket /public\-access\-block
and is changed by shard 0, like the bucket's other documents. A runtime started with
.B \-\-anonymous
admits everyone regardless of a bucket's block, just as it ignores the bucket's policy.
.SS How objects are stored
Each object is one file at
.IB dir / bucket /data/ ab / sha256(key) .
The file starts with a checksummed header that holds the key, size, ETag, content
type, user metadata, tags and time.
The file therefore describes itself, and any byte sequence is a valid key.
The 256 directories \fBab\fR (the first two hex digits of the hash) are created
and synced together with the bucket, so a put never has to create one.
Each bucket also keeps an append\-only journal of puts and deletes:
\fBindex.0\fR, or \fBindex.\fIshard\fR for a sharded runtime.
The journal is CRC32C\-framed and is rewritten when its garbage outgrows the live objects.
The runtime answers listings from memory, using the journal.
.PP
A put writes a temporary file, syncs it, renames it into place and syncs the
directory, and only then answers 200. A delete removes the file and syncs the
directory.
.PP
A small put takes a faster path. This applies to a put of up to 1 MiB whose length
is known and that carries no condition (no \fBIf\-None\-Match\fR, not a part).
The bucket keeps a few empty files ready in \fBpool/\fR, and their names are synced
before any put takes one.
The put writes into one of these files, syncs it and renames it into place, and is
then answered.
The syncs of \fBpool/\fR and of the object's directory happen after the answer.
The next put or delete of the same key waits for them.
.PP
Either way, the answered object is on disk.
If a crash comes before the rename is durable, the object is still whole in
\fBpool/\fR, and the next start moves it into place.
A file in \fBpool/\fR counts as whole only when its length and MD5 match the ones its
header names.
For an encrypted body this is the MD5 of the sealed bytes, kept in the header as
.BR x\-socketley\-stored\-md5 ,
because a start holds no key that could open the body.
.PP
For both kinds of put, the journal record is appended after the answer.
The journal is synced once a megabyte of records has gathered, before a compaction
and at a stop.
It is never synced merely because the queue drained, because that sync would delay
the next put's own sync.
The object files are the truth and the journal is only their index, so an answer
never waits for the journal.
A crash at any point leaves either the old object or the new one, never a torn one,
and nothing that was answered is undone.
.PP
A start after a clean stop trusts the journals, because a clean stop writes and syncs
them last.
Any other start removes temporary files and reads every object header to reconcile
the journals with the files.
A journal that is lost, damaged or missing its last records is therefore rebuilt
from the objects.
A delete whose record was lost does not bring the object back, because its file is gone.
This reading happens before the listener opens and takes time in proportion to the
object count.
It is the only place where the event loop blocks on the disk: every request is
served on the runtime's io_uring.
.PP
A stop first closes the listener and the connections.
It then lets journal writes, directory syncs and compaction that have already begun
finish, for at most a second.
If they cannot finish in that time, the next start treats the stop as a crash.
Each shard holds a lock on
.BI .lock. n
in the directory until its stop is done.
A start waits up to a second and a half in total for other processes to let go.
These are the process it replaces on its shard, and every process still holding a
shard under another
.B \-\-shards
value, whose files the start takes over.
The start fails if they do not let go.
This is a safety net, because the daemon forks a runtime's new workers only once the
previous ones have exited (see
.BR restart ).
When a stop comes, a request that a shard has sent to a sibling and that has not been
answered is answered as failed, so it does not hold up the stop.
.PP
The start after a crash reads every header, even for a file that the journal lists
with the same size.
An overwrite whose record was lost can leave exactly that situation, and only the
header tells the two versions apart.
The start is fast because it does many reads at once.
The 256 directories of every bucket are shared out among threads, one per CPU the
runtime may run on, at most 16 (\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_THREADS\fR).
These threads have all finished before the listener opens.
Each thread reads its directories with 128 KiB
.BR getdents64 (2)
calls, and keeps 32 opens and header reads in flight on an io_uring of its own
(\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_DEPTH\fR; 0 reads one file at a time without a ring).
All of this stays within half of the descriptors that
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
leaves.
Only a file at the one path its key gives, \fIab\fB/\fIsha256(key)\fR, is read.
This lets a bucket whose files match its journal one for one skip the search for
records whose file is gone.
If a directory cannot be read whole, or the descriptor table runs out during the
scan, the start fails with the reason rather than dropping the objects it did not see.
.PP
Measured with 1 KiB objects on an ext4 virtio disk and four CPUs:
100,000 objects take 0.24 s with a warm cache (0.8 s before this was parallel) and
about 1 s from a cold cache (11 to 33 s before).
For 1,000,000 objects a start takes 2.3 s warm (9.8 s before) and 19 s cold (344 s before).
A start after a clean stop reads only the journals: 0.07 s for 100,000 objects and
0.66 s for 1,000,000.
.PP
A body is never held in memory whole.
It streams from the socket into a one\-megabyte staging buffer and is hashed as it
passes.
From 256 KiB up, the hashing runs on helper threads, so the event loop's one core
does not hash every upload.
The body is written to disk while the next bytes arrive, so a gigabyte upload costs
a few megabytes of memory.
.PP
A GET of 64 KiB or more is sent by
.BR sendfile (2)
from the file to the socket without passing through the process.
The event loop sends it a burst at a time; a stretch that is not in the page cache
is read through the ring instead.
The bytes sent this way are counted as
.B zero_copy_bytes
in
.BR stats .
Smaller objects are read once, together with their header, and sent from memory with
the response headers in one write.
.PP
After an object of up to 60000 bytes is first read, every shard that reads it keeps
a copy in memory.
Each shard keeps up to 32 MiB of copies (\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HOT_BYTES\fR) and evicts
the least recently read first.
Later GETs on that shard are answered from the copy without opening the file.
On the shard that owns the key, a copy is used only while it is the version the index
names.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
a shard that does not own the key has no index entry to compare with.
The keys are spread over 16384 slots.
The first copy such a shard keeps of a key in a slot must be one that the key's owner
has confirmed is the indexed version.
From then on, the owner tells that shard to drop its copy of any key in that slot.
The owner does this after it commits or deletes the key and before it answers the client.
This costs one round trip to those siblings, in parallel, against the milliseconds of
the syncs.
It costs nothing for a key whose slot no sibling has read from.
A read during which a drop of its key arrived is not kept.
As a result, after a PUT, copy, multipart completion or DELETE has been answered, no
GET on any shard returns the bytes it replaced.
The owner does not wait for a sibling that is down, and that sibling holds no copies
when it starts again.
A shard that starts tells the others to drop every copy of its keys, because it may
have died between renaming a new version into place and telling them.
.PP
Measured with a 4 KiB GET at 32 connections on four shards, keeping the copy on every
shard lowered worker CPU (summed over the shards) from 45 us to 30 us per request.
Throughput rose from about 50 thousand to about 56 thousand requests per second.
At one connection, CPU fell from 30 to 20 us and throughput rose from 7100 to 8100
requests per second.
A 4 KiB PUT into memory\-backed storage takes about 14 us longer for the round trip.
Once a copy has answered a plain GET, it also keeps that GET's response headers, so a
shard spends the same on a GET whether or not it owns the key.
In a later measurement on four CPUs with another client, four shards took about 26 us
per 4 KiB GET at 32 connections, against about 17 us for one shard.
The difference is four half\-idle loops that each sleep and wake every few requests,
not work that only a shard does.
.PP
.SS Performance
The first set of measurements used warp and boto3 over plain HTTP with SigV4 on one
16\-core VM with an ext4 virtio disk.
The comparison was MinIO as a single node with a single drive on the same filesystem.
Each test ran three alternating passes, with the box quiet before each pass.
.PP
Reads are faster than MinIO's, each at a tenth or less of MinIO's CPU:
.IP \(bu 2
A 4 KiB GET at one connection is about 4x as fast (8200 against 1900 per second).
At 32 connections it is 1.7x with one shard and 3.6x with four shards.
.IP \(bu 2
A 1 MiB GET is 2x with one shard and 4x with four.
.IP \(bu 2
A 64 MiB GET is 1.4x with one shard and 3.8x with four.
.PP
A 64 MiB put is 1.3x at one connection and 1.1x at 32 connections.
That is about 600 MiB/s, which is the most this disk takes.
Mixed 64 MiB loads are level with MinIO.
.PP
The second machine was a 4\-vCPU VM (AMD EPYC, Debian 12, kernel 7.2).
The client, warp 1.3.1, ran on the same four CPUs against one shard, in three alternating passes,
and was compared with the same MinIO:
.IP \(bu 2
4 KiB GET: 3.0x at one connection and 3.9x at 32.
.IP \(bu 2
HEAD 3.9x, 1 MiB GET 2.5x, 64 MiB GET 1.8x, listing 3.1x, multi\-object delete 2.7x.
.IP \(bu 2
4 KiB put: 1.7x at one connection and 2.5x at 32 (about 900 and 3000 per second;
this disk's sync is fast).
.IP \(bu 2
1 MiB put 1.2x; 64 MiB put level (6.5 against 6.6 per second); mixed 1 MiB loads 1.6x.
.PP
Every row used less CPU per request than MinIO: about nine tenths of MinIO's for large
puts, a third to a half for small puts, and a seventeenth for reads.
On this machine four shards are slower than one, because the client shares the four
CPUs.
Shards are for a machine with cores to spare.
.PP
From a client 31 ms away over the internet (k6, 32 connections), both servers run
4 KiB GETs and puts as fast as the round trip allows, about 800 to 880 per second.
At p99, socketley takes 45 to 47 ms against MinIO's 48 to 147 ms, and it uses about
2.6x less CPU per put.
.PP
Small puts wait for the disk and for little else, so their speed follows the disk.
A put of up to 1 MiB now waits for one sync, of its pooled file, instead of two (the
file and then its directory).
No put waits behind a sync of the journal.
Previously the journal record was synced after each answer, and the next put's own
sync queued behind it.
Measured with warp 1.3.1, three alternating passes each:
.IP \(bu 2
Dropping the journal sync raised 4 KiB puts at one connection from 77 to 104 per second.
.IP \(bu 2
The pooled file raised 4 KiB puts at one connection from 107 to 118 per second in a
fast phase of the disk, and left them level (35 and 34) in a slow phase.
.IP \(bu 2
In the slow phase, the pooled file raised 4 KiB puts at 32 connections from about 430
to 600 per second, and 1 MiB puts at one connection from 22 to 28.
.IP \(bu 2
boto3's p50 for a 4 KiB put went from 13.0 ms to 11.3 ms and then to 7.6 ms.
MinIO's was 8.5 to 17 ms on the same disk, minutes apart.
.PP
Against MinIO, a 4 KiB put at one connection is level in the disk's fast phases
(about 120 per second each) and 0.6x in its slow phases (33 against 54).
At 32 connections it is 1.2x or more.
MinIO syncs its file once and never syncs its directory, so a power cut can lose a put
that MinIO has answered.
Here, an answered put survives a power cut.
The disk's sync latency drifts by a factor of two to three within an hour, so these
rows name the phase they were measured in.
.PP
No answer waits for the journal.
A stop syncs it, and a start after any other kind of stop reads every object header.
That takes about a quarter of a second per 100,000 objects with a warm cache, and
about a second from a cold cache, on four CPUs (see How objects are stored).
Uploads of 256 KiB or more are hashed on helper threads
(\fBSOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS\fR).
This lets a 64 MiB put at 32 connections be limited by the disk rather than by one core.
.PP
A slow body gets special handling.
This applies to a body of known length that arrives slower than 4 MB/s, judged over
10 ms.
The runtime lets it gather in the socket and reads it once per 10 ms tick, a tick
shared by every such connection.
The last 64 KiB of the body is excepted.
Each tick reads everything that has gathered, even over TLS, where one receive brings
16 KiB.
A body that began slowly and then speeds up is therefore read at the speed it arrives.
A slow upload thus does not cost a receive and a wakeup per TCP segment.
Its digests are handed to the hashing threads 64 KiB at a time.
In a test, a 1 MiB put was sent in 1448\-byte segments at 0.8 MB/s, eight at once.
It took about 35 to 60 ms of worker CPU per MiB before this change and about 6 to 9 ms
after.
The same put sent at loopback speed takes about 3 to 5 ms.
.
.SS HTTP/2
A storage runtime speaks HTTP/2 (RFC 9113) as well as HTTP/1.1.
It uses HTTP/2 in two cases:
.IP \(bu 2
over
.BR \-\-tls ,
with a client that offers \fBh2\fR by ALPN, as every browser does;
.IP \(bu 2
in the clear, with a client that opens with HTTP/2's preface (prior knowledge).
The curl tool does this when told to, and so does a proxy's h2c backend.
.PP
Each request is a stream on the one connection and runs exactly as it does over
HTTP/1.1.
This holds for S3, WebDAV, the web interface, share links and media alike.
A browser's uploads, its player's segments and its pictures therefore no longer wait
for one of the six connections a browser opens to an HTTP/1.1 server.
.PP
The runtime gives back a stream's window only as it consumes the stream's body.
A disk that cannot keep up therefore holds back the client, just as a full socket does.
For outgoing data, the runtime sends one frame of each stream in turn, as the
client's windows allow.
A stream is told that its bytes have left once they reach the connection's queue,
which is kept under 1 MiB.
The next piece of a file is therefore read while the previous one is being written.
.PP
The runtime's receiving limits are:
.IP \(bu 2
a window of 4 MiB per stream and 32 MiB per connection;
.IP \(bu 2
128 streams open at once (the next one is refused with REFUSED_STREAM);
.IP \(bu 2
header lists of up to 64 KiB, and frames of 16 KiB.
.PP
The runtime sends GOAWAY with ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM to a peer that, within ten seconds,
resets more than 400 streams, or sends more than 200 PINGs or SETTINGS or 1000 empty
DATA frames.
It does the same for a header block larger than 80 KiB or longer than 64 CONTINUATIONs.
This follows RFC 9113 and the rapid\-reset advisory (CVE\-2023\-44487).
.PP
Over HTTP/2 a stream's file is read through the process rather than spliced, as over TLS.
A connection with no open stream is closed with GOAWAY after the idle timeout.
At a stop, every connection is closed with GOAWAY.
.B stats
counts
.BR h2_conns ,
.BR h2_streams ,
.BR h2_refused ,
.BR h2_resets_in ,
.B h2_resets_out
and
.BR h2_goaways .
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP2=0
turns HTTP/2 off, so every client speaks HTTP/1.1.
.
.SS HTTP/3
With
.B \-\-tls
a storage runtime also speaks HTTP/3 (RFC 9114) over QUIC (RFC 9000), on the
same port number over UDP.
This requires a build with HTTP/3, which
.B stats
shows as \fBh3:1\fR.
\fBbin/build\-thirdparty.sh http3\fR builds the static OpenSSL 3.5, ngtcp2 and nghttp3
that HTTP/3 needs, and the install script runs it.
.PP
Every answer over TCP carries
.BR "Alt\-Svc: h3=\(dq:PORT\(dq" .
A browser then tries QUIC on its next request, and stays on TCP if UDP does not
get through.
Each request is a stream and runs exactly as over HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2.
A connection survives a client's change of address or network, and a lost packet
holds up only its own stream.
These are the reasons to use HTTP/3 on mobile and lossy links; on a clean wire,
HTTP/2 costs less CPU per byte.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
the shards share the UDP port, and the kernel picks a shard by the client's address.
A client that changed address may therefore reach a different shard.
Every connection ID names its shard, and a shard hands a packet for another shard's
connection to that shard, which answers from the same port.
.PP
The limits are:
.IP \(bu 2
A stream's window starts at 1 MiB and a connection's at 4 MiB; they grow to 16 MiB
and 64 MiB.
.IP \(bu 2
128 streams are open at once.
.IP \(bu 2
A handshake not done in 10 seconds is dropped.
.IP \(bu 2
A connection is closed after 30 seconds without a packet.
.PP
The runtime paces what it sends with BBR.
BBR follows the path's measured rate and round trip rather than backing off at each
lost packet.
Over a 50 ms path that loses one packet in a hundred, as Wi\-Fi and mobile links do,
a download ran ten to twenty times faster than with CUBIC.
On a clean path it was as fast.
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_CC=cubic
(or
.BR reno )
chooses another congestion control.
AES\-128\-GCM is offered first, because every packet is sealed on its own and
AES\-128\-GCM costs a quarter less than AES\-256.
.PP
While more than
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_RETRY_AFTER
handshakes are under way (default 64; 0 means always), a new client is sent a Retry.
The client must prove its address before the runtime keeps any state for it
(RFC 9000 8.1.2), so datagrams with forged sources cannot fill the table.
QUIC uses the same TLS setup as TCP: a certificate renewed on disk is picked up the
same way, and
.B \-\-ca
asks for a client certificate here too.
.PP
A reverse proxy forwards only TCP, and it passes the Alt\-Svc header on unchanged,
still naming the runtime's port.
To use HTTP/3 behind such a proxy:
.IP \(bu 2
forward UDP from the port browsers reach (usually 443, which the proxy does not take
for UDP) to the runtime's port;
.IP \(bu 2
give the runtime the certificate browsers expect;
.IP \(bu 2
set
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_ADVERTISE
to the port browsers reach, or to 0 to advertise none.
.PP
.B stats
counts
.BR h3_conns ,
.BR h3_streams ,
.BR h3_retries
and
.B h3_forwarded
(packets handed to another shard), and shows the connections open now as
.BR h3_open .
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3=0
turns HTTP/3 off.
.
.SS Quotas
A bucket's quota limits how many bytes its objects and versions hold, and how many
of them there are.
The quota is socketley's own document; S3 has no counterpart:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /photos?quota
<BucketQuota>
  <MaxBytes>10737418240</MaxBytes>
  <MaxObjects>100000</MaxObjects>
</BucketQuota>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Either element may be left out, and 0 means no limit.
Only an admin, the runtime's own key, or anyone on an
.B \-\-anonymous
runtime may set, read or delete a quota, because a quota limits what the bucket's
owner may store.
The quota is stored in
.IB dir / bucket /quota
and is changed by shard 0, like a bucket's other configuration documents.
It survives a restart.
A user's
.B max_bytes
(see
.BR Users )
limits the total that all the buckets owned by that user hold together.
.PP
A write that would take a bucket or its owner past a quota is answered
.B "403 QuotaExceeded"
and stores nothing.
This applies alike to a PUT, a POST, a copy and the completion of a multipart upload.
An overwrite counts only the bytes it adds beyond the object it replaces, and does
not add to the object count.
In a versioned bucket every version counts, including delete markers.
.PP
The shard that owns the key checks the quota at the commit, one write at a time, so
writes racing each other cannot pass it together.
That shard also refuses a body of known length before reading it.
Under
.BR \-\-shards ,
each shard holds its share of a quota, as it does of
.BR \-\-max\-bytes .
A bucket whose keys are spread unevenly over the shards is therefore refused somewhat
before the whole quota is reached.
The parts of a multipart upload are not counted until the upload is completed.
.
.SS Website hosting
A bucket with a website configuration is served to browsers under
.BR \-\-website\-domain ,
in the way S3's website endpoints serve a bucket:
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley create storage media \-p 9000 \-\-dir /srv/objects \\
    \-\-credentials /etc/socketley/s3.keys \-\-website\-domain web.example.com \-s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Any S3 client then puts the site's configuration on its bucket and uploads its files:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PUT /docs?website
<WebsiteConfiguration>
  <IndexDocument><Suffix>index.html</Suffix></IndexDocument>
  <ErrorDocument><Key>404.html</Key></ErrorDocument>
</WebsiteConfiguration>
.fi
.RE
.PP
After that,
.B http://docs.web.example.com:9000/
serves the bucket's
.BR index.html .
.PP
A website request is never treated as signed.
The runtime judges it as a request with no credentials, whatever it carries.
A page is therefore served only when the bucket's policy lets anyone read it
(\fBPrincipal "*"\fR, \fBs3:GetObject\fR).
On an
.B \-\-anonymous
runtime every page is served.
Only GET and HEAD are served, the query is ignored, and the page is the latest version
of the key.
.PP
A key that is empty or ends in
.B /
gets the index document's suffix appended, so
.B /guide/
serves
.BR guide/index.html .
A key without the trailing slash, whose folder has an index document, is answered
.B "302"
to the folder, so that the page's relative links resolve.
An object stored with
.B x\-amz\-website\-redirect\-location
is answered
.B "301"
to that location.
.PP
Routing rules redirect requests:
.IP \(bu 2
A rule whose only condition is KeyPrefixEquals redirects before the object is looked up.
.IP \(bu 2
A rule with HttpErrorCodeReturnedEquals redirects a failure with that status.
.IP \(bu 2
A rule's Redirect may replace the key or its prefix, and may name a host, a protocol
and a 3xx code.
The defaults are the request's own host and protocol, and 301.
.IP \(bu 2
RedirectAllRequestsTo sends every request to its host, keeping the key.
.PP
A failure that no rule redirects is answered with the ErrorDocument and the failure's
status, if the policy lets anyone read the ErrorDocument.
Otherwise it is answered with an HTML page that names the error's code, message and
request id, and with an
.B x\-amz\-error\-code
header.
As on S3, a missing key is
.B "403 AccessDenied"
rather than
.B "404 NoSuchKey"
when the policy lets anyone read the bucket's keys but not list them.
The S3 API is still served on the same port, so a site can be uploaded and read over
both.
.
.SS Drive, links, media and the web interface
Paths that begin with
.BR ~ ,
which no bucket name can, are the runtime's own applications.
They are served on the S3 port and use the S3 runtime's users, policies and
versioning:
.TP
.B /~dav/
A WebDAV drive (classes 1 and 2) for Windows, macOS, Linux file managers, rclone and phones.
It signs in with HTTP Basic: the access key is the user name and its secret is the
password.
A runtime without credentials asks for none.
.IP
The drive's root lists every bucket the user owns or that a bucket policy lets the
user list; an admin sees every bucket.
A bucket's keys appear as files, and the slashes in a key are folders.
A file's GET, PUT, DELETE, COPY and MOVE are the S3 operations, judged by the same
policies.
MKCOL at the root creates a bucket.
Inside a bucket, MKCOL creates an empty
.I folder/
marker object, as S3 browsers do.
.IP
A folder's COPY, MOVE and DELETE apply to every key under the folder.
A copy reads each object whole, so an object larger than 256 MiB, or one stored under
a client's own key, is not copied, and the answer is
.BR 500 .
A COPY or MOVE with
.B Overwrite: F
is answered
.B 412
when its Destination holds a file, or, for a folder, any object under it.
Otherwise the COPY or MOVE replaces the destination.
Locks are granted but not enforced, and properties are not kept.
.IP
Failed sign\-ins are rate\-limited per address.
The count covers Basic sign\-ins here, at
.B /~ui/
and at
.BR /~media/ ,
and passwords of share links.
After ten failures within a quarter hour, the address is answered
.B 429
with a
.B Retry\-After
until that quarter hour ends, even when it then sends the right secret.
Sessions already signed in from the address go on working.
An IPv6 address is counted by its /64.
Each shard counts on its own, so an address has ten tries per shard.
Behind a proxy every client has the proxy's address, so one client guessing there
also keeps the others from signing in.
.IP
A file may also be uploaded in parallel parts, using S3's multipart upload under the
drive's sign\-in.
Each step is judged as the S3 operation it is.
.B POST ?uploads
on the file begins an upload and answers its UploadId.
.B PUT ?partNumber=\fIn\fB&uploadId=\fIid\fR
streams a part and answers its ETag.
.B POST ?uploadId=\fIid\fR
with S3's CompleteMultipartUpload body assembles the file from the parts.
.B DELETE ?uploadId=\fIid\fR
abandons the upload.
.B GET ?uploadId=\fIid\fR
lists the parts that have arrived, with
.B max\-parts
and
.B part\-number\-marker
for the next page.
An interrupted upload can therefore resume where it stopped.
A file's other PUTs ignore their query.
.IP
.nf
[drive]                      # rclone.conf, then: rclone copy ~/photos drive:holiday
type = webdav
url = http://host:9000/~dav/
user = AKIA...
pass = <the secret, as rclone obscure prints it>
.fi
.IP
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIfolder\fB/?zip
downloads the folder as one ZIP, saved as
.IR folder .zip.
.B ?zip&n=\fIname\fB&n=\fIname\fR
includes only the named files and folders of it.
A bucket's root accepts these queries too.
The ZIP is streamed as it is read, and each file is stored without compression.
Its Content-Length is therefore known before the first byte, and a browser shows the
download's progress.
Each entry's CRC-32 is computed as its bytes pass and follows them in a data descriptor.
A file or an offset past 4 GiB uses ZIP64.
.IP
An entry's name is its key relative to the folder, with empty, \(lq.\(rq and
\(lq..\(rq segments removed.
\(lq\e\(rq and control characters become \(lq_\(rq.
Where another entry already has the name, \(lq\ (2)\(rq, \(lq\ (3)\(rq and so on is
added before the extension.
No key can therefore unpack outside the folder or over another file.
The ZIP holds only what the user may read, and leaves out objects that only their
client's key opens.
If an object changes while the ZIP is being sent, the connection ends, because the
length was already promised.
.IP
In a versioned bucket a deleted file becomes a delete marker, and its versions stay.
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIfolder\fB/?trash
lists, as JSON, the files under the folder whose latest version is a marker.
Each entry has the key, the time of the deletion and the size of the version behind
the marker.
.B POST /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB?restore
removes the marker and brings that version back.
A lifecycle rule's NoncurrentVersionExpiration empties the trash.
.IP
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIfolder\fB/?list
returns a folder's listing in the form the web interface reads.
It is JSON with the subfolders' names and the files' names, sizes, times in
milliseconds and types
(\fB{"d":[...],"f":[[name,size,mtime,type],...]}\fR).
It is about a tenth of the size of a PROPFIND answer, and has an ETag of its own.
A repeated request with
.B If\-None\-Match
that finds nothing changed is answered
.B 304
with no body.
A folder of 20,000 files took 9.9 MB and 44 ms as a PROPFIND, and takes 1.0 MB and
9 ms this way; a browser read it in 40 ms rather than 400.
.IP
.B POST /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB?unzip
unpacks a ZIP object on the server into a folder beside it.
The folder is named after the archive without its extension, numbered
(\fIname\fR (2)) when that name is taken.
Nothing is overwritten, and none of the archive's data crosses the client's line.
Each file is read from the archive, inflated if it is deflated, and checked against
its CRC-32 and size.
It is then written as an object of its own, four at a time, in the same way as the
drive's other writes: encrypted when the bucket encrypts, and typed by its extension.
An empty folder in the archive becomes that folder's marker object.
ZIP64 archives are read.
Names in UTF-8 are kept, and older names are read as code page 437.
.IP
The unpacking skips a file that is encrypted, packed other than stored or deflated,
or named outside the folder (\fB..\fR, a control character).
It also skips macOS's \fB__MACOSX\fR tree, \fB.DS_Store\fR and \fBThumbs.db\fR, without
reporting them.
Unpacking needs GET on the archive and PUT under the folder.
Once every file is written, the answer is JSON that names the folder.
It counts the files, folders and bytes written and the files skipped and failed, with
up to 20 names of each.
The status is 201 when none failed and 500 when any did.
An archive whose directory is over 64 MiB, or that holds more than 100,000 entries,
is refused.
The job runs to its end even if its request is abandoned.
In a test, 549 MiB unpacked from a 312 MiB archive of 502 files in 2.1 s, on four CPUs
with two shards; other requests were answered meanwhile in under 35 ms.
.TP
.B /~share/
Share links to an object or a folder, which anyone holding a link can read without
credentials.
.B POST /~share
with a JSON body creates a link; the request is signed with SigV4 or Basic, as above:
.IP
.nf
{"bucket": "holiday", "key": "2026/", "expires_in": 604800,
 "password": "...", "max_downloads": 0, "upload": false, "note": "..."}
.fi
.IP
A key that ends in
.BR / ,
or no key at all, makes a folder link.
A folder link is read as a page, as JSON with
.BR ?format=json ,
and file by file under it.
.B upload
lets a visitor PUT files into the folder.
The maker must be able to read what the link names, and, for an upload link, to
write there.
.IP
A link acts as its maker: each request is judged as the maker's own request would be.
Reading a file through the link is the maker's GetObject of that key.
A folder's page, list and ZIP are the maker's ListBucket, and the ZIP leaves out each
file the maker may not read.
An upload is the maker's PutObject.
A link that its maker may no longer use is answered
.BR 403 .
A link is answered
.B 410
when its maker is no longer a user of the runtime or is disabled, or when its bucket
was removed and created again under the same name.
.IP
A link's password is asked for with Basic, with any user name, and is stored salted
and hashed.
.B max_downloads
counts each read from the start of a file, and not a player's later range requests.
.B ?download
asks a browser to save the file.
A folder link read with
.B ?zip
returns the folder, or the part of it that the path names, as one ZIP, as the drive
gives it.
This counts as one download, and the folder's page offers it.
An expired link is answered
.B 410
and is removed at the next listing.
.B GET /~share
lists the caller's links, or every link for an admin.
.B DELETE /~share/\fIid\fR
revokes a link.
.IP
.B "\(dqburn\(dq: true"
makes a link that removes what it names when the link ends, like a file sent once.
The link ends at its expiry, at its revocation, or ten minutes after the last download
its limit allows (so a browser resuming that download still finds it).
A burning link needs
.B expires_in
or
.BR max_downloads ,
cannot be an upload link, and can be made only by a user who may also delete there.
A cluster does not make burning links.
A burning link to a file opens on a page that describes the file.
This page counts no download, so a chat's preview of the link costs nothing.
The file itself is read with
.BR ?download .
.IP
The runtime's sweep checks the links every five seconds.
It renames a link that has ended to
.IR id .burning,
which no reader opens, stamped with the time, and then removes what the link names.
The shard that renamed the link does the removal.
If a stop left a link stamped more than a minute ago, the first shard to see it takes
over the removal.
In a versioned bucket the removal is a delete marker, and the versions stay.
.IP
.B "\(dqnotify\(dq: \(dqhttps://...\(dq"
gives a link a webhook.
The runtime POSTs a JSON object to it at each download the link counts, and when a
burning link's target is removed.
The object has these fields:
.BR content " and " text
(a sentence, as Discord and Slack show it),
.BR event " (" downloaded " or " deleted ),
.BR bucket ", " key ", " downloads ", " max_downloads " and " expires .
A notification the hook does not accept is posted again, in the same way as a
bucket's webhook events.
It is kept across a restart in
.B .share\-notify
under the runtime's directory.
.IP
Because the runtime makes the request on behalf of the link's maker, the URL must
begin with one of the comma\-separated prefixes in
.BR SOCKETLEY_SHARE_NOTIFY_ALLOW .
By default these are the webhooks of Discord
.RB ( https://discord.com/api/webhooks/ ,
.BR https://discordapp.com/api/webhooks/ ),
Slack
.RB ( https://hooks.slack.com/ )
and
.BR https://ntfy.sh/ .
Any other URL is answered 400.
A webhook gets up to four requests at once, at most 16 wait, and requests about one
file are sent one at a time.
.IP
Links are files in
.IR dir /.shares/,
and every shard reads them.
Each link has a mark under
.I .shares/owner/
by its maker and, for a burning link, a mark under
.IR .shares/burn/ .
A user's list and the search for burning links therefore read only those marks.
A link with no marks, as an older build made them, is read once at a start.
.TP
.B /~media/\fIvariant\fB/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fR
Media that ffmpeg makes from an object, kept for later requests.
.IP
.BI thumb\- width
(64, 128, 256, 320, 480, 640, 960, 1280 or 1920) is a JPEG still of a picture, or of
a film a few seconds in.
.IP
.B info
describes the object as JSON.
It gives the length, picture size, frame rate and codecs, and whether the object is HDR.
It gives the MIME type with codecs, which the interface uses to ask a browser whether
it plays the file itself.
It lists the codecs of the copied stream, the renditions and the text subtitles.
It lists every sound track with its codec, channels, language, title and whether it
is the default.
It lists the chapters with their start, end and title.
For a song it gives the tags (title, artist, album, album artist, track, disc, date,
genre).
These come from the container or, for a file without a picture, from the sound
stream, as Ogg keeps them.
.IP
.B trick
is a film's stills for a seek bar: one JPEG of 160x90 tiles, ten per row.
There is one still every two seconds, or every 1/200 of the film if that is longer.
The
.B trick
field of info gives the seconds between stills, their count and their layout.
The stills are taken from the keyframes alone, or from every frame when the keyframes
give none.
They are made in a place of their own beside the thumbnails, one job at a time per shard.
.IP
.BI sub\- stream /subs.vtt
is a text subtitle stream (SubRip, ASS, mov_text, WebVTT) converted to WebVTT, on the
film's clock.
.BI sub\- stream /index.m3u8
is its HLS playlist.
Subtitles that are pictures are not offered.
.IP
.B hls/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB/master.m3u8
is an HLS master playlist of the renditions, with their size, peak bitrate and codecs.
A player switches between them as its bandwidth allows.
The master names one rendition for every rung of 2160, 1440, 1080, 720, 480, 360
and 240 that the picture fills in at least one dimension.
Each rendition is fitted into its rung's 16:9 box and never enlarged, and 720 is
listed first.
The master also names the source's own picture, copied, when it is H.264 or HEVC
(HEVC is tagged hvc1), and the text subtitles as a SUBTITLES group.
BANDWIDTH is the most a segment can carry, a quarter over the rung's cap.
The levels are 4.2 up to 1080 and 5.2 above.
The URLs a master names carry the object's version, so their answers can be cached
for good.
Every medium's answers carry an ETag that a client can revalidate with (answered 304),
and accept a Range.
.IP
.BI hls\- height /index.m3u8
is one rendition: H.264 held to its rung's bitrate, with AAC sound, in CMAF segments of
four seconds.
Its playlist is complete from the start, written from the film's length, so a player
can seek anywhere at once.
The segments are made as they are requested.
A request for a segment at or a little past the point where a job is working waits
for that job.
A request elsewhere starts a new job at that point.
Jobs work on the film's own clock, so the segments of every job join up, and every
job's init segment is the same bytes.
A length over 99,999 segments (about 111 hours), or a length that is not a number, is
treated as no length.
The info variant then gives 0, and a rendition is answered 415, as for a film with no length.
.IP
A request for an init segment with no job running starts a job at the beginning at once.
A seek that follows releases that job, and the job is paused after two seconds if no
segment of it is requested.
.IP
Several jobs can run over one film for viewers at different places.
A job ends when it reaches segments that were already made.
A job is paused when it is fifteen segments ahead of every player that requests from
it, or two ahead when nobody has requested from it for ten seconds.
It resumes when a player comes within eight segments.
A job ends when nobody requests from it for a minute while it is paused, or for ten
minutes while it runs.
A paused job with no requests gives its place to a new job when the shard has eight jobs.
Where renditions are made on a GPU, the limit is 24 jobs shared among the shards, and
never fewer than eight.
.IP
What the jobs make is kept, so a film watched once streams from the cache afterwards.
HDR (PQ or HLG) is tone-mapped to SDR for these renditions.
Where a GPU with VAAPI is present, a rendition is decoded, scaled, tone-mapped and
encoded on it, as
.B SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_HWACCEL
describes.
An HDR film without the mastering display metadata that the GPU tone-maps from is
made with x264 instead.
Where the copied stream has not yet been remuxed up to the current position, the
interface plays that part from a rendition until the copy catches up.
.IP
A variant with
.BI \-a n
appended, such as
.BR hls\-a1 ,
.B hls\-720\-a1
and
.BR hls\-copy\-a1 ,
carries the film's sound track
.I n
instead of the first.
Tracks are counted from 0 among the sound tracks, as info lists them.
The track is made or copied with its own codec and channels.
Its master names its streams with the same suffix.
Each stream is made and kept as a stream of its own, so the picture is made again
for the other track.
.B hls\-copy
streams the source's own picture without transcoding.
It copies the sound when the sound is AAC in two channels or fewer, and converts it to
AAC otherwise.
Renditions always convert the sound to AAC, because a copy begun at a seek would start
at the keyframe before the seek point.
.IP
The object is read as a GET of it would be: by its user or, under
.BI /~media/ variant /~share/ id\fR[\fB/\fIpath\fR],
through a share link.
ffmpeg, and the ffprobe beside it, read an unencrypted object's file directly; the
shard keeps that file open for a minute.
They read any other object over the runtime's own listener, at an address signed for
that version of the object by a key in
.IR dir /.media/key .
They read it with a single\-file demuxer, never with one that opens URLs that the
object names (HLS, DASH, concat).
What they make is stored in
.IR dir /.media/
under the variant, the object's key and its ETag.
A new version is therefore made anew, and every shard serves what any shard made.
A thumbnail is made only once, however many requests ask for it at the same time.
.IP
A shard runs as many thumbnails and probes at once as it has cores of the host's
share, and at least two.
Each runs ffmpeg with one thread: a 1600x1200 JPEG's thumbnail took 166 ms with
ffmpeg's threads and 72 ms without.
A shard also runs up to eight streams at once.
The ring polls a pidfd for each job, so the result is served as soon as ffmpeg exits,
not at the next check.
A request waits up to a minute for its medium.
An object ffmpeg cannot read is answered
.B 415
for two minutes.
An ffmpeg that a dying worker left behind is found by its pid and ended.
See
.B SOCKETLEY_FFMPEG
and
.BR SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_CACHE_MB .
.TP
.B /~ui/
A web interface: one page, served from the binary.
A browser that asks for the runtime's root is redirected here when the request has no
query and no signature and its
.B Accept
header includes
.BR text/html .
The host's name alone therefore reaches the interface.
An S3 client sends none of that, so
.B GET /
is still ListBuckets for it.
.IP
The interface signs in with an access key and its secret.
It sends them once, to get the session cookie described below, and stores them nowhere.
It browses libraries (buckets) as a media library.
Films, pictures, music and documents appear in their own rows, with thumbnails,
lengths, and what the user left half watched on any device.
Films named S01E02 or 1x02 are grouped by season, in order, and play on into the next
episode.
A folder's or library's card shows a picture in it called folder, cover or poster, or
else its first picture or film.
More than twelve pictures are grouped by the month of their date: the camera's date
(see
.BR /~ui/taken ),
or else the object's.
Pictures play as a slideshow.
A ZIP's menu unpacks the archive beside itself on the server (\fB?unzip\fR above).
.IP
The interface uploads several files at once, through the drive's multipart upload above.
A file of 48 MiB or more is sent in parts of at least 16 MiB.
Four requests run side by side, shared among up to three files, and each request
goes to the file with the fewest requests under way.
Over HTTP/1.1, while a film or a song plays in any of the browser's tabs, uploads use
only one request, because a browser opens at most six connections to such a server.
The tabs tell each other on a BroadcastChannel.
Uploads take four requests again once the player is closed.
Over HTTP/2 (see above), uploads keep all four requests beside the player.
Each file shows its percentage, rate, time left and start.
Folders can be uploaded too, by dropping them or choosing one.
.IP
A file in parts whose connection is lost is paused, and resumes when the connection
is back.
A file left by a closed or reloaded page is shown again with how far it came.
It resumes from the parts the server lists once the user chooses the file again: the
same name and size, and the same modification time or a confirmation.
Each part, and each file sent whole, carries its CRC-32 in
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-crc32 ,
which the server checks against what arrived.
A part refused as
.B BadDigest
is read and sent again.
.IP
The interface can select several items to download or delete, rename, share, restore
from the trash and show pictures one after another.
It opens text in an editor that highlights code, finds and replaces, previews
Markdown and saves with Ctrl+S, and it makes new text files.
Its search box filters the open folder and searches every library.
Items dragged onto a folder, a library or a breadcrumb are moved there.
The page requests the open folder again every five seconds while the page is visible
(every thirty seconds past 2000 entries), so changes from another device show within
seconds.
A download is the object byte for byte: the drive's GET with
.BR ?download .
A browser resuming an interrupted download sends that request again with the cookie.
The owner of a library shares it from the page with other users, to view or to edit,
through
.B /~ui/access
below.
.IP
Send a file sends files the way wormhole does.
The files are uploaded into the user's library for temporary files,
.BI tmp\- user .
This is the user's id in lowercase; the library is made when first needed and is
shown as Temporary files.
Each sending goes into a folder of its own, behind a burning link (see
.B /~share/
above).
It is deleted after a chosen time or number of downloads (for several files, that
number for each file).
It can have a password and a webhook that is told of each download.
Once a picture or a film is uploaded, the page requests its thumbnails and facts.
For a film this browser would not play itself, it also requests the start of its
stream.
The server therefore makes these before anyone waits for them.
Five colour themes can be chosen from the sidebar, and the choice is kept in the browser.
.IP
The interface signs in with
.B POST /~ui/session
and Basic credentials.
These are either a user's name and the password of its login profile (see IAM above),
or an access key and its secret.
The answer sets the cookie
.B skl_ui
(HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict, Secure over TLS).
The cookie holds the access key (or
.BI ~ user
for a password), the time it was made, an expiry, and an HMAC-SHA256 of these under
the key's own secret.
Every shard and node therefore checks it the same way.
When a key's secret changes or the key is removed, its sessions end.
A session lasts twelve hours and the browser's session, or thirty days with
.BR ?remember .
.B GET /~ui/session
says who is signed in, and
.B DELETE /~ui/session
signs out.
.IP
.B POST /~ui/session?everywhere
ends every session of the user made before it, in each browser.
It does this by storing its time in
.IR dir /.ui/sessions/.
The other shards follow within a second.
A sign\-in with Basic credentials is not a session and keeps working.
.IP
/~dav/, /~media/ and the management of /~share/ accept the cookie as they accept
Basic credentials, so the page's script never holds the secret.
A change made with only the cookie (any method but GET, HEAD, OPTIONS and PROPFIND)
must carry the header
.BR "X-Socketley-UI" ,
which no other site's page can add.
Without it, the change is answered 403.
A session never creates a new session.
A file's
.B GET /~dav/\fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fB?download
is saved under the file's name.
.IP
The page's Account section sets and changes the user's password.
It also creates, deactivates and deletes the user's access keys; a new secret is
shown once.
An admin's Users section lists every user with how the user signs in.
There an admin can create a user with a password (optionally to be changed at the
first sign\-in).
The admin can set a user's password, display name, email, quota, admin status and
whether the user makes libraries of its own, and can disable and delete users.
.IP
An admin can also invite people.
An invitation is a link,
.BI /~ui/#invite= token ,
that lets one person choose a user name and password of their own, once.
It is valid for one to thirty days, and makes the user an admin or gives a quota if
it says so.
Only the SHA\-256 of its token is stored, in
.IR dir /.iam.json
beside the users.
Sharing a library offers the users by name.
.IP
.B POST /~ui/iam
with
.B {"Action":
\fIaction\fB,\fR \fIits fields\fB}\fR
runs an IAM action as the signed\-in user, judged and answered as IAM's own.
.B GET /~ui/users
lists the users.
Any user sees each user's id and name; an admin sees all fields, and the open
invitations.
.B GET /~ui/invite?token=
says whether an invitation is valid.
.B POST /~ui/invite
with
.B {"token", "user", "password", "name", "email"}
accepts it without a session.
A token that is not valid counts as a failed sign\-in.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/progress
lists the films the signed\-in user has partly watched, most recent first, with the
position, the length, the size and the type.
It lists only films the user may still read, in the version watched.
A film replaced or removed since, or one whose access was taken away, is left out.
.B POST /~ui/progress
with
.B {"bucket", "key", "t", "d"}
stores where the user left a film; a t below 0 forgets the position.
Each user's positions are a file of their own in
.IR dir /.ui/progress/.
The file is changed entry by entry under a lock, so two devices watching different
films both keep their positions.
The latest 200 are kept.
Nodes of a cluster keep their own positions.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/search?q=\fItext\fR[\fB&bucket=\fIname\fR]
lists the files and folders whose own name contains the text, matching ASCII letters
in any case.
It searches every library the user lists, or only the one named.
It returns folders first, at most 200 results, with
.B "more"
set to true when there were more or a library was too large to list whole.
A file is listed only where the user may read it.
Each library is listed whole for the search.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/taken?bucket=\fIname\fB&prefix=\fIfolder/\fR
says when and where the photos directly in a folder were taken, from their EXIF data.
It covers each JPEG, TIFF or HEIF (HEIC) the user may read.
For each it gives the key, the
.B taken
time as the camera's clock wrote it ("2021-07-14T18:03:22", with "+02:00" where the
camera wrote its offset), and the
.B lat
and
.BR lon .
The runtime reads the first 64 KiB of each file, and a HEIF's Exif item where it lies
further in.
What was read is kept in the shard's memory, keyed by the object's ETag.
An object stored encrypted, compressed or in a tier is left out.
So is everything past the folder's first thousand keys
.RB ( "more" ).
The page groups a folder's pictures by the month they were taken, and shows the time
in the viewer.
It links to the place on openstreetmap.org, which the page never loads itself.
It shows a HEIF as its thumbnail, which ffmpeg makes from version 7.1 on.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/usage
lists every library the user lists.
For each it gives the owner, whether the library is shared with the user, and its
bytes and objects.
These are summed from the library's listing, which holds every shard's keys, and kept
for a minute.
.B partial
is set when the library was too large to list whole.
The library's quota is also given.
Besides the libraries, the answer gives the user's own quota over the libraries it
owns, and whether the user is an admin.
.B POST /~ui/usage
with
.B {"bucket", "max_bytes", "max_objects"}
sets a library's quota as
.B ?quota
does; 0 means no limit.
Like
.BR ?quota ,
it is for admins only.
The page's Storage section shows these figures as bars.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/access?bucket=\fIname\fR
says who else a library is shared with.
.B POST /~ui/access
with
.B {"bucket", "user", "access"}
shares it, by id, with a user of the credentials or the IAM API.
The access is
.B read
(list and read),
.B write
(also write, delete, and restore from the trash) or
.B none
(take the access back).
Each share is a statement in the bucket's policy, named by its Sid
.BI socketley\-ui\- access : user\fR.
Only those statements are read or changed: the rest of the policy is kept, and a Deny
in it still holds.
Both requests are for whoever may read or write the bucket's policy: its owner and
an admin.
The drive's root lists the buckets a user owns and also every bucket whose policy
lets the user list it.
.IP
A film's share link is a page that plays the film.
A chat embeds the page as a player once the film's web copy,
.BI /~media/web/ bucket / key\fR,
is made.
The web copy is H.264 in an MP4 of at most 46 MiB and 1440 lines.
It is made when a link asks for it, or when the film is put into a library tagged
.BR socketley:web\-copies=on .
.B GET /~ui/library?bucket=\fIname\fR
says whether a library has that tag.
.B POST /~ui/library
with
.B "{\(dqbucket\(dq, \(dqweb_copies\(dq}"
sets or removes the tag, for whoever may change the bucket's tags.
.IP
The interface plays films and music, with their subtitles.
Where the browser plays the file itself, it plays the file, which costs the server nothing.
Otherwise it plays the master playlist's streams through Media Source Extensions.
It uses the copied stream where the browser supports its codec and the connection can
carry it, and otherwise renditions chosen by measured bandwidth.
Where the browser has no Media Source at all, it uses the browser's own HLS.
The player's quality menu offers Original (the file, or the copied stream where the
browser does not play the file), Auto and each rendition.
A change of quality is made on the same media source.
It keeps the next six seconds already buffered while the new stream's first segment
is made.
.
.SS Git repositories
The page's Sync section keeps git repositories in a library.
Each repository is pulled when it is added, whenever its user asks, and on its own
schedule, as its Pull every setting says.
The schedule is every day, week or month from 3:00 local time, or never (the default).
When any ref has moved since the last run, the run writes the branch's files under
.IB name /
in the library.
It writes what changed, or the whole tree the first time, and leaves nothing else
beside the files.
It also writes a git bundle of the branch and the tags (or of every branch, with
.BR * )
under
.BI _backups/ name / date _ time _ commit .bundle .
.IP
.B "git clone" " file" .bundle
makes the repository from a bundle, with its branch checked out.
A run with nothing new writes nothing.
Every bundle of the last day is kept.
Before that, the newest bundle of each of the last 14 days, 8 weeks and 12 months is
kept, and the rest are deleted.
Symbolic links and submodules are not files and are left out.
.IP
.B \-\-sync
chooses which runtimes do the runs.
With
.B never
(the default), the storage makes no sync runtime, and the page says that an added
repository would not run.
With
.BR active ,
the runtime's first shard makes one
.B sync
runtime for the whole storage,
.IB storage \-sync .
It pulls every user's repositories, each with that user's own credentials.
With
.BR per\-user ,
the storage makes a separate
.B sync
runtime for each of its users,
.IB storage \-sync\- user .
.IP
Either kind of sync runtime is owned by the storage.
It stops when the storage stops and comes back when the storage starts.
.B "socketley ls"
and the dashboard list it with its
.BR runs ,
.BR failures ,
.B running
and
.BR last_error .
.IP
A sync runtime runs
.B socketley git\-sync
for that user's repositories alone, one at a time.
It uses temporary credentials of the user, which the storage hands it in
.IR dir /.ui/sync/
with mode 0600.
The credentials last twelve hours and are renewed when three hours are left.
It works through the runtime's own S3 API on its port.
It therefore cannot write what the user may not write, quotas and versioning apply,
and a missing library is created as the user.
.IP
A failed scheduled run is retried an hour later.
A run the user asks for is the file
.IR dir /.ui/git/ id /asked,
and the user's sync runtime starts it within a second.
Each repository's bare clone, the state of its last run and its log are under
.IR dir /.ui/git/ id /.
The list of repositories is
.IR dir /.ui/git.json .
Git must be installed.
A runtime on a unix socket has none of these features.
.IP
A repository is fetched over https://, http:// or git://, never with credentials in
its URL.
A private repository is read with the GitHub token the user keeps in the page.
The token is passed as git's http.extraHeader through git's environment, so it
appears on no command line.
It is stored in
.IR dir /.ui/git\-tokens/,
sealed with AES-256-GCM under a key that only the user's own secret derives.
It is never shown again.
.IP
.B GET /~ui/git
lists the repositories the user keeps (for an admin, all of them).
Each entry has the state its last run ended in, and the answer says whether the user
has a token.
.B POST /~ui/git
takes one of these bodies.
.B {"action":"add", "url", "branch", "bucket", "name", "private", "every"}
adds a repository and starts its first run.
The defaults are branch
.BR main ,
the user's own library
.BI git\- user ,
every
.BR never ,
and the name taken from the URL's last part.
.B {"action":"every","id","every"}
sets the schedule:
.BR never ,
.BR day ,
.B week
or
.BR month .
.B {"action":"sync","id"}
starts a run.
.B {"action":"remove","id"}
removes a repository; its files and bundles stay.
.B {"action":"token","token"}
sets the user's token, and an empty token removes it.
A user can add a repository only into a library the user may write.
.IP
Earlier, all users shared one library,
.BR git .
A repository still in that library moves to its owner's own library at the next start.
It runs there until its tree is complete and
.B "git\-sync \-\-move\-from git"
has taken over its backups and removed its files from the old library.
The old library is removed once it is empty.
.IR dir /.ui/git/ id /moved\-from
keeps track of the move across restarts.
.
.SS Durability
.B \-\-fsync
decides when a write that a client was answered for is on the disk, like a cache's
journal and Redis's appendfsync.
In every mode, a crash of the process loses no answered write, because the page
cache outlives the process.
Every mode syncs the whole filesystem at a stop, before it writes the clean marker.
.TP
.B always
Each write is synced before it is answered; this is the default.
A put is answered once its file, its name and its directory are synced.
A put of up to 1 MiB with no condition waits for one sync instead: its pooled file
is synced and renamed, and its directory is synced after the answer (see
.BR "How objects are stored" ).
A delete is answered once its directory is synced.
The same holds for versions, tags, uploads and parts, and for the event\-notification
and replication entries written beside them.
An answered write survives a crash of the machine.
A small put spends most of its time waiting for its one sync.
On one shard of the measuring box this gives about 1,500 4 KiB puts a second, or
about 700 into a versioned bucket.
.TP
.B interval
The per\-write syncs are skipped, and a thread syncs the filesystem periodically.
The thread calls \fBsyncfs\fR every
.B \-\-fsync\-interval
(default 1 second).
Under
.B \-\-shards
the thread runs in shard 0 alone, since one call covers the files of every shard.
A crash of the machine loses at most the writes of the last interval.
The same box handles about 18,000 puts a second, or about 9,600 into a versioned bucket.
.TP
.B never
The syncs are skipped; only the kernel's own writeback and a stop put the writes on disk.
The kernel's writeback typically happens within 30 seconds.
This mode is for data that can be made again, such as caches and build artefacts.
.PP
Under
.B interval
and
.BR never ,
what a crash of the machine leaves behind depends on the filesystem.
ext4 in its default data=ordered mode leaves an object either as it was or as it was
written, never partly written.
A start that finds a file whose header does not check out leaves the file unserved.
On other filesystems, a file written in the last interval may hold zeros where its
data was not yet written.
Bucket creation and removal and the configuration documents are synced in every mode.
Backups, and anything else that deletes its own copy once a put is answered, should
use
.BR always .
.PP
The cost of
.B always
depends on the disk.
A drive with power\-loss protection (most enterprise NVMe and SATA SSDs) answers a
sync from its protected cache in microseconds, so the mode costs little there.
A consumer or virtual disk answers a sync only once the data is on the medium.
XFS writes less per sync than ext4.
ext4's
.B fast_commit
feature (\fBtune2fs \-O fast_commit\fR) shortens a sync after a small change.
Concurrent puts already share the filesystem's journal commits, so
.B always
gains throughput with concurrency.
Three alternatives were measured against it: one sync of the whole filesystem shared
by every waiting put, files written ahead of time and overwritten, and
.B O_DSYNC
writes.
None did better under a sustained load of small puts.
.B stats
counts the syncs that ran in
.BR fsyncs ,
those a mode skipped in
.BR fsyncs_skipped ,
and the filesystem syncs of
.B interval
in
.B syncfs_rounds
and
.B syncfs_failed
(counted by shard 0);
a stop after a failed round is not a clean one.
.
.SS Sharding
.B \-\-shards
.I N
runs N worker processes on one port (\fBSO_REUSEPORT\fR), all on the same
.BR \-\-dir .
With
.BR \-\-listen ,
each worker is a node of its own instead (\fIname#i\fR.sock, as for a cache).
Clients see one runtime.
Every shard serves a GET or HEAD itself by opening the object's file, whose header
describes the object, so object bytes never move between shards.
.PP
.B "One shard is the default, and on a machine of a few cores it is the right one."
One shard answered 95,000 signed GETs of 4 KiB a second on one core, about 3 Gbit/s
of payload.
A runtime behind a 1 or 10 Gbit link therefore runs out of network before it runs out
of its core.
Shards help only where small reads saturate that core, and they cost CPU wherever a
request crosses shards.
The table below was measured on one box, with the runtime confined by
.B taskset
and a
.B wrk
client, on cores of its own, replaying signed GETs of 64 keys:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l r.
CPUs	shards	GET 4 KiB
_
2	1	96,000 req/s
2	2	185,000 req/s
2	4	177,000 req/s
4	2	187,000 req/s
4	4	253,000 req/s
4	8	218,000 req/s
8	4	258,000 req/s
8	8	359,000 req/s
.TE
.RE
.PP
The last row is limited by the client's six threads.
More shards than CPUs is slower, never faster.
.PP
Shards have a price, measured on the same box:
.IP \(bu 2
A ListObjectsV2 page of 1000 keys took 2.1x the CPU at two shards and 2.4 to 2.5x at
four, because every shard walks up to a page and one shard merges them.
.IP \(bu 2
A 4 KiB PUT took 1.2 to 1.3x the CPU at two shards and 1.4 to 1.6x at four.
At four shards, three keys in four are committed by a shard other than the one the
body landed on.
.PP
Large objects move at the same rate at any shard count: a 64 MiB GET was limited by
the client and a 64 MiB PUT by the disk.
.PP
A key's
.I owner
is the shard that commits its changes.
The slot is the CRC16/XMODEM of the string \fIbucket\fB/\fIkey\fR masked to 16384
slots, exactly the function of a cache's key slots.
The owner is shard \fIslot\fR * \fIN\fR / 16384.
A client can therefore compute the owner and send a request to the owner's node, so
that nothing crosses shards.
.PP
A put streams into a temporary file on whichever shard took the connection.
That shard then sends the owner the file's name and the object's metadata over the
shard link.
The owner renames the file into place, syncs the directory and answers.
After the answer, the owner appends to its own journal (\fBindex.\fIi\fR, written by
shard \fIi\fR alone).
A delete, the target of a copy and a create\-only put (\fBIf\-None\-Match: *\fR) go
the same way.
This keeps the changes to a key in one order, and ensures that one of two racing
creators is refused.
A listing is requested from every shard and the pages are merged, so the result is
the listing a single shard would give.
If a shard cannot be reached, the listing fails with a 500 rather than coming back
incomplete.
.PP
Shard 0 creates and drops buckets, and tells the other shards before it answers.
A bucket is dropped only when every shard agrees that it holds nothing and is idle.
Shard 0 also changes a bucket's CORS, lifecycle and versioning configurations and its
policy, whichever shard took the request.
It makes one change per bucket at a time.
It answers only once no shard can go on using the old document.
A change that a running shard cannot be told of is refused with 503 and changes
nothing (see
.BR "Bucket policy" ).
.PP
Each shard sweeps the keys it owns.
Temporary files are named for the shard that wrote them, so a shard that the
supervisor restarts removes only its own.
A shard killed between a rename and its journal record leaves the object in the
directory.
When the shard restarts, it adopts the object from its header.
From then on, either every shard serves the object or none does.
A put that was in flight through the killed shard is answered with an error.
.PP
A start with a different \fB\-\-shards\fR on a directory written with another shard
count is not refused, unlike a cache with a missing dump.
The object files are the record.
Each shard sees that its journal was written for another shard count, and rebuilds
its keys from the object headers, keeping those it owns.
Shard 0 removes the journals of shards that no longer exist.
Such a start reads every header once per shard, so its time grows with the object count.
.PP
\fB\-\-max\-bytes\fR is split into equal shares per shard.
The shard that took the put checks it against its own bytes, and the owner checks it
again.
The limit is therefore approximate when keys are unevenly sized.
\fBstats\fR gives a section per shard with
.BR shard_commits_sent ,
.BR shard_commits_served ,
.BR shard_deletes_sent ,
.BR shard_deletes_served ,
.BR shard_list_fanouts ,
.BR shard_list_pages_served ,
.BR shard_bucket_ops ,
.BR shard_mpu_sent ,
.B shard_mpu_served
(multipart steps),
.B shard_hot_drops_sent
and
.B shard_hot_drops_served
(a sibling told to drop its copy of a key, once per sibling),
.B shard_hot_holds_sent
and
.B shard_hot_holds_served
(a sibling asking the owner to keep a copy) and
.BR shard_peer_failures ;
at one shard nothing is sent and they read zero.
.PP
A multipart upload lives on the shard that owns its key.
Every step that names the upload (create, part, complete, abort, ListParts) is
handled by that shard, and ListMultipartUploads asks every shard.
A part's body is written on the shard where the connection landed, and only its name
crosses to the shard that keeps the upload.
CompleteMultipartUpload works in three steps:
.IP \(bu 2
the shard that keeps the upload checks the part list and seals the upload;
.IP \(bu 2
the shard the request landed on assembles the object from the parts on the shared
disk, and commits it through the key's owner;
.IP \(bu 2
the owner removes the upload.
.PP
No long task is therefore asked of a sibling over the link.
An upload whose assembler never reports back is opened again after six hours.
After a restart with another
.BR \-\-shards ,
the shard that owns an upload's key finds the upload again.
A bucket with an open upload on any shard is not dropped.
.PP
TLS, credentials, the credentials file, scripts and the metrics are each shard's own,
or their sum:
.IP \(bu 2
every shard terminates TLS and re\-reads the credentials file;
.IP \(bu 2
a script's hooks run on the shard that took the request;
.IP \(bu 2
a script's
.B socketley.storage
call is answered by whichever shard the daemon relays it to, and reaches the other
shards as any request does;
.IP \(bu 2
the
.B socketley_storage_
series add the shards together (a bucket that every shard holds is counted once).
.PP
.B stats
reports these fields.
For contents and traffic:
.BR buckets ,
.BR objects ,
.BR bytes ,
.BR requests ,
.BR gets ,
.BR heads ,
.BR puts ,
.BR deletes ,
.BR copies ,
.BR lists ,
.BR client_errors
and
.BR server_errors .
For reads and the journal:
.BR zero_copy_gets ,
.BR copied_gets ,
.B hot_gets
(the copied GETs answered from the hot copy),
.B hot_bytes
(what the hot copies hold),
.BR zero_copy_bytes ,
.B journal_records
and
.BR journal_compactions .
For syncs and starts:
.B fsyncs
(of files and directories alike, that ran),
.B fsyncs_skipped
(those
.B \-\-fsync interval
or
.B never
skipped),
.B syncfs_rounds
and
.B syncfs_failed
(the filesystem syncs of
.BR "\-\-fsync interval" ),
.B start_files_read
(object and version files whose headers the last start read: 0 when every journal was trusted),
.B pool_ready
(empty files the buckets' pools hold durable for small puts, 0 after a start until the first put).
For uploads, lifecycle and connections:
.BR multipart_uploads ,
.BR multipart_parts ,
.BR multipart_bytes ,
.BR multipart_created ,
.BR multipart_completed ,
.BR multipart_aborted ,
.BR lifecycle_expired ,
.BR lifecycle_aborted ,
.BR timeouts ,
.B tls
(1 when the listener is TLS),
.BR tls_handshakes ,
.BR tls_failures ,
.B body_recvs
(receives that brought bytes of an upload's body) and
.B hash_handoffs
(pieces of digest work handed to the hashing threads).
For encryption and media:
.B sse
(1 when the runtime has a master key),
.B sse_puts
and
.B sse_gets
(bodies written encrypted, and GETs answered by opening an object's chunks),
.BR media_made ,
.B media_failed
and
.B media_served
(thumbnails and streams ffmpeg made and failed to make, and the answers served
from them),
.B media_direct
(ffmpeg jobs that read the object's file rather than through the listener),
.B media_hw
(rendition jobs encoded on the GPU).
There is also one line
.BI error_ Code
for every S3 error code the runtime has answered, such as
.BR error_NoSuchKey .
Bytes in and out and
.B total_messages
(requests) are the common fields.
The runtime closes a connection whose request head is unfinished after ten seconds,
or whose body has not moved for a minute, and removes its temporary file.
.SS Metrics
With the metrics endpoint on, each running storage runtime adds these series,
all labelled
.BR name :
.BR socketley_storage_buckets ,
.BR socketley_storage_objects ,
.BR socketley_storage_bytes_stored ,
.BR socketley_storage_bytes_received_total ,
.BR socketley_storage_bytes_sent_total ,
.BR socketley_storage_zero_copy_bytes_total ,
.B socketley_storage_tls_handshakes_total
and
.BR socketley_storage_tls_failures_total .
It also adds
.B socketley_storage_requests_total
(label
.B op
= get, head, put, delete, list, copy) and
.B socketley_storage_errors_total
(label
.B code
= the S3 error code).
.PP
The subsystems have families of their own when a runtime runs them: event notifications
.RB ( socketley_storage_notify_ *),
replication
.RB ( socketley_storage_replication_ *),
a cluster
.RB ( socketley_storage_cluster_ *),
the scrub
.RB ( socketley_storage_scrub_ *),
compression
.RB ( socketley_storage_compressed_ *),
tiers
.RB ( socketley_storage_tier_ *),
and lifecycle, multipart, media and fsyncs.
Every other
.B stats
figure is exported as
.B socketley_storage_stat
with its name as the label
.BR stat ,
so nothing a runtime counts is missing from a scrape.
.PP
A mail runtime's figures are
.BR socketley_mail_received_total ,
.BR _delivered_total ,
.BR _deferred_total ,
.BR _bounced_total ,
.BR _stored_total ,
.BR _quarantined_total ,
.B _auth_failed_total
and the gauges
.B socketley_mail_queued
and
.BR _queued_bytes .
Its other figures are
.B socketley_mail_stat
by name.
A tunnel's gauges are
.BR socketley_tunnel_links ,
.BR _agents ,
.B _shares
and
.B _flows
(UDP visitors).
Its counters are
.BR socketley_tunnel_streams_total ,
.BR _refused_total ,
.B _visitors_refused_total
(requests a protected service's auth refused),
.BR _bytes_in_total ,
.BR _bytes_out_total ,
.B _datagrams_total
and
.BR _datagrams_dropped_total .
.SS Lua
A script attached with
.B \-\-lua
may define three hooks on a storage runtime.
.TP
.B on_request(req) \(-> nil | true | false | string | table
Called for every request after authentication and the bucket policy, before anything is done.
The hook therefore sees only requests that both let through.
It can refuse any of them, but cannot let in a request that they refused.
It runs on a coroutine, so it may wait for another runtime (see
.B socketley.storage
below).
The connection is not read from until the hook answers.
A refused upload is therefore answered before its body is read, and its connection
is closed.
.IP
.I req
has
.BR method ,
.B operation
(the name S3 gives it: GetObject, PutObject, DeleteObjects, ListObjectsV2, CreateMultipartUpload,
UploadPart, UploadPartCopy, CompleteMultipartUpload, AbortMultipartUpload, ListParts,
ListMultipartUploads, PutBucketLifecycleConfiguration, .\|.\|.),
.BR bucket ,
.BR key ,
.B path
and
.B query
(as sent),
.BR host ,
.BR request_id ,
.B principal
(the access key, empty for a request with no credentials),
.B tls
(boolean),
.B client
(\fBip:port\fR, or \fBunix\fR),
.B content_length
(absent for a chunked body) and
.B headers
(lowercase names).
.IP
Returning nothing or
.B true
lets the request go on.
.B false
refuses it as AccessDenied (403).
A string refuses it as that S3 error code, with the status S3 gives that code (403
for a code the runtime does not know).
A table
.B {deny="Code", message="...", status=403}
sets all three.
An error raised in the hook, or a return value of any other type, refuses the request
with 500 InternalError and is logged.
This is deliberate: a hook whose failure let requests through would protect nothing.
.TP
.B on_put(bucket, key, meta)
Called after an object is committed.
It is called for a put, for the destination of a copy, for an object a script put with
.BR socketley.storage.put ,
and for a completed multipart upload (once, at completion, and never for its parts).
It is never called for a write that was refused.
.I meta
has
.BR size ,
.BR etag ,
.BR content_type ,
.B mtime_ms
and
.B metadata
(the user metadata, without its prefix).
The hook is called after the client's answer is on its way.
.TP
.B on_delete(bucket, key)
Called after an existing object is removed by DeleteObject, DeleteObjects or
.BR socketley.storage.delete .
It is not called for a key that did not exist.
.PP
When a script writes an object in
.BR on_put ,
its
.B on_put
is called again for that object.
.SS socketley.storage
Any runtime's script may use a storage runtime by name, in this daemon or in another
process.
The daemon does not stop while the script waits:
.PP
.nf
socketley.storage.put(runtime, bucket, key, data [, {content_type=, metadata={}}] [, callback])
socketley.storage.get(runtime, bucket, key [, callback])
socketley.storage.head(runtime, bucket, key [, callback])
socketley.storage.delete(runtime, bucket, key [, callback])
socketley.storage.list(runtime, bucket [, prefix] [, {delimiter=, max_keys=, start_after=}] [, callback])
.fi
.PP
Some callbacks can pause:
.BR on_message ,
.BR on_client_message
and the storage hooks above.
In these, a call without a callback waits and returns the answer.
The connection that a server handler is answering is kept open while it waits, as it
is for a linked cache.
Anywhere else (an
.BR on_tick ,
a cache's
.BR on_write ,
a proxy's filter), pass a function as the last argument.
The call then returns
.B true
at once, and the function is called with the answer on a later turn.
A call that must wait, made where waiting is not possible and without a function,
returns
.B nil
and a message saying so.
.PP
The answers are:
.IP \(bu 2
.B put
and
.B head
return a table with
.BR size ,
.BR etag ,
.BR content_type ,
.B mtime_ms
and
.BR metadata .
.IP \(bu 2
.B get
returns the data and that table.
.IP \(bu 2
.B delete
returns
.BR true ,
also for a key that was not there, as S3 does.
.IP \(bu 2
.B list
returns a table with
.B objects
(each with
.BR key ,
.BR size ,
.BR etag ,
.BR content_type ,
.BR mtime_ms ),
.BR prefixes ,
.B truncated
and, when truncated,
.B next
(pass it as
.B start_after
for the following page).
.PP
A failure returns
.B nil
and the S3 error code and message
.RB ( NoSuchKey ,
.BR NoSuchBucket ,
.BR InvalidBucketName ,
.BR EntityTooLarge ).
When the request did not reach the runtime, the call returns
.B nil
and a reason
.RB ( "no such runtime" ,
the request channel is full).
Metadata names are lowercased and stored as
.BR x\-amz\-meta\-* .
Buckets are created with the S3 API.
An object moves whole in memory, and the request crosses a process boundary in a frame
that waits in a 4 MiB queue.
A script may therefore put or get at most 1 MiB per call, and at most 1024 calls may
be waiting on one runtime.
Use the S3 port for larger objects.
.SH DNS FLAGS
A
.B dns
runtime is an authoritative name server. It answers queries over UDP and TCP on
one port, and only for the zones in the files it is given.
.PP
.nf
socketley create dns ns \-\-zone example.com \-\-publish 203.0.113.7 \-s
dig @127.0.0.1 example.com SOA
.fi
.PP
When a zone is given by its name, the runtime writes it with this machine's
public address for its name servers.
.B \-\-publish
gives that address. It may be left out only where a network interface has a
public address, and behind NAT none has one.
.B create
also makes an entrance: a proxy of the runtime's own (see
.BR \-\-proxy ).
As soon as the entrance starts, it asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate for each
zone and its wildcard. It does so for every name except those reserved for
examples and tests, such as
.BR example.com ,
.B .test
and
.BR .internal ,
because no public CA issues certificates for them. For a lab or a test that
uses real names, give
.BR "\-\-proxy none" .
The runtime also answers one client network at most 100 times a second
.RB ( \-\-rrl ).
A load test from a single host therefore sees most answers dropped unless it
gives
.BR "\-\-rrl 0" .
.PP
The runtime asks no other server and caches nothing. A name that is in none of
its zones is answered REFUSED and is never looked up. So the runtime cannot be
used as an open resolver, and every answer it gives is its own. Like every other
runtime, it runs in a worker process of its own. It may run as
.B \-\-shards
processes that share the port. A UDP query goes to the shard its ID picks, so a
resolver that asks from one port is still spread over every shard. It binds IPv4, and also IPv6 when the host has it
and no
.B \-\-bind
address is given. The default port is 53. A port below 1024 needs root or
.BR CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE ,
which the packaged service grants.
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-bind ,
.BR \-\-cpus ,
.B \-\-max\-connections
(TCP connections; default 256) and the common flags apply as documented under
.BR "COMMON FLAGS" .
.B \-\-listen
and
.B \-\-tls
are refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-zone " name\fR|\fIfile"
A zone to serve, given by its name or as a zone file. Required. Give the flag
once for each zone, or give several zones separated by commas.
.IP
A name, such as
.BR example.com ,
needs no file. The first time, the runtime writes one under
.IR state\-dir /zones/ name .zone
and serves it from then on. The file has an SOA record and names ns1 and ns2 as
the zone's name servers. The addresses of
.B \-\-publish
(or this machine's public addresses) become the addresses of ns1 and ns2, and of
the zone's own name, which is the entrance's. You tell the registrar these two
names and addresses as the domain's name servers and their glue. The file is
yours to edit; the runtime never writes it again while it exists.
.IP
A path is made absolute against the caller's directory. If a file called
.IB zone .zone
does not exist there, it is written in the same way. A zone file uses the
master file format of RFC 1035. The zone's apex is the owner of its one SOA
record. Every record must lie at or below the apex, and the apex must name its
servers with NS records. A CNAME must be the only record of its name. Below a
delegation, only the addresses of the delegated servers may appear.
.B create
and
.B start
refuse a file that is not such a zone, and name the line that is wrong.
.B $ORIGIN
and
.B $TTL
are read.
.B $INCLUDE
is refused, since it would let a zone file read any file the worker can read.
Types A, AAAA, NS, CNAME, SOA, PTR, MX, TXT, SRV, CAA and HINFO are written in
their usual form. Any other type is written in RFC 3597's form,
.B TYPE\fIn\fB \e# \fIlength hex\fR.
A TXT string longer than 255 bytes is split into several strings, as a DKIM key
needs.
.IP
The runtime checks the file once a second, and reads it again when it was
edited. The new zone is served once the whole file has been read and checked.
If the file no longer parses, the previous zone stays in place and the log gives
the reason. A changed zone whose serial did not change is still served. The log
then says that its secondaries will not notice the change until the serial is
raised.
.TP
.BI \-\-transfer " list"
The addresses and address blocks that may copy a whole zone by AXFR over TCP
(RFC 5936). Give them comma\-separated: a secondary's address, such as
192.0.2.53, or a block, such as 2001:db8::/48. Everyone else is answered
REFUSED, so the names a zone holds are not handed to whoever asks. An IXFR from
these addresses over UDP is answered with the SOA alone. This tells the
secondary the serial and that it must come over TCP. Over TCP, an IXFR is
answered as an AXFR.
.TP
.BI \-\-notify " list"
The secondaries that get a NOTIFY (RFC 1996) when the runtime starts and
whenever a zone's serial changes. Give them as
.IR host [: port ],
comma\-separated. The runtime sends the NOTIFY again after 1, 3, 7, 15 and 31
seconds until the secondary answers it. Only the first shard sends them.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish " list"
This machine's public IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, comma\-separated, used to answer
for the names of the daemon's runtimes. A runtime of the daemon is reached by
names given with
.BR \-\-domain ,
.BR \-\-acme ,
or a storage runtime's
.B \-\-domain
and
.BR \-\-website\-domain .
Every such name that falls within a zone is answered with these addresses.
Without this flag, those names are answered with this machine's public
addresses when the runtime has an entrance (below), and not at all with
.BR "\-\-proxy none" .
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy " name\fR|\fBnone"
The entrance through which the names of runtimes are reached, or
.B none
for no entrance. An entrance is a proxy without
.BR \-\-backend .
It routes each HTTP request, by the name the request asks for, to the runtime
that has that name (see
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" ).
.IP
.B create
makes the entrance as this runtime's child. Its name is
.IB dns \-proxy
when the flag is not given. It listens on
.B \-\-proxy\-port
with
.B \-\-tls
and
.BR "\-\-backend\-timeout 5m" ,
because a site may take a while to begin answering, as behind any web server.
The entrance asks the CA for its own certificate: each zone with its wildcard,
proven over DNS\-01 by this runtime's answers. Before that,
.B acme issue
waits
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_WAIT
seconds and asks the resolver of
.I /etc/resolv.conf
for a TXT record that exists only in this runtime's answers. It then asks every
name server the zone is delegated to for that record directly, since a CA may
ask any of them. Some zones are not ready in that time: a zone that the world's
DNS does not delegate here yet, or a zone with a secondary that has not taken up
the record. Each name of such a zone is proven by itself instead, over
TLS\-ALPN\-01 on 443, without the wildcard. The wildcard is asked for again
every six hours until the zone is delegated.
.IP
An entrance on 443 comes with
.IB dns \-proxy\-http
on port 80, which sends every request to HTTPS
.RB ( \-\-redirect\-https ).
Behind the entrance, every response over TLS carries
.B "Strict\-Transport\-Security: max\-age=31536000"
unless the answering runtime set its own. Over TLS, the entrance speaks HTTP/2
to a client that offers it by ALPN, and HTTP/1.1 to a client that does not. Each
HTTP/2 stream is routed by the name it asks for, like an HTTP/1.1 request. A
stream that asks for a name other than the one its connection's handshake gave
is answered 421. A client may have eight streams open at once.
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP2=0
turns HTTP/2 off. Over UDP on the same port, the entrance speaks HTTP/3
(RFC 9114), and every answer over TCP names it in
.BR "Alt\-Svc: h3" .
An HTTP/3 stream is routed by its own
.B :authority
exactly as an HTTP/2 stream is. A browser that cannot reach the UDP port goes on
over TCP.
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP3=0
opens no UDP socket.
.IP
The entrance stops, starts and is removed with this runtime. If this runtime is
removed while another dns runtime also names the entrance, the entrance becomes
that runtime's instead. A proxy of that name that already exists is used as it
is, provided it is an entrance.
.B none
makes no entrance. With an entrance and no
.BR \-\-publish ,
the names are answered with this machine's public addresses. The runtime reads
them from the machine's interfaces and checks them again every minute.
.IP
.nf
socketley create dns ns \-\-zone example.com \-s     # and ns\-proxy on 443
socketley create server chat \-\-domain \-\-http /srv/chat \-s
curl https://chat.example.com/
.fi
.TP
.BI \-\-proxy\-port " port"
The port that the entrance made by
.B create
listens on. Default: 443.
.TP
.BI \-\-rrl " n"
Response rate limiting: the most answers a second the runtime gives one client
network. A client network is an IPv4 /24 or an IPv6 /56. Past the limit, every
second answer is replaced by a truncated one (TC set, no records, as large as
the query), and the others are dropped. A resolver in that network then retries
over TCP, which is not limited, and is served. A victim whose address a flood
forges receives nothing larger than what was sent. Default: 100. 0 means no
limit. The limit is for the whole runtime: with
.BR \-\-shards ,
each shard allows its share of
.IR n ,
since one network's queries are spread over the shards by their IDs, which a
resolver draws at random.
.TP
.B \-\-dnssec
Signs every zone as it is loaded (RFC 4033 to 4035). Each zone gets one key of
ECDSA P\-256 with SHA\-256 (algorithm 13). The key is made at the first start
and kept in
.IR state\-dir /dns/ name .dnssec/
for every shard. The runtime adds the key's DNSKEY at the apex, an NSEC chain
through the names, and an RRSIG over every record set the zone holds. The
signatures last three weeks and are made again a week before they end. A query
with the DO bit is answered with the signatures. A name or type that does not
exist is answered with the NSEC records that prove it. A zone transfer carries
them too. Give the parent zone the DS record that
.B socketley dnssec
prints.
.SS Names runtimes are reached by
Whenever the names of its runtimes change, the daemon writes them into
.IR state\-dir /.dns/names .
While a CA checks a DNS\-01 answer,
.B acme issue
writes the answer there as
.BI acme\- token .
A
.B dns
runtime checks that directory once a second, as it checks its zone files. It
adds to each zone the names that fall within it, with the addresses of
.B \-\-publish
and TTL 300. It adds the DNS\-01 answers as TXT records of
.BI _acme\-challenge. name ,
with TTL 10. What the zone file says about a name wins: a name with addresses of
its own, or an alias, keeps them, and nothing is added below a delegation.
.PP
.nf
socketley create dns ns \-\-zone /etc/zones/example.com.zone \\
    \-\-publish 203.0.113.7,2001:db8::7 \-s
socketley create storage files \-p 9301 \-\-domain files.example.com ...
dig files.example.com            # 203.0.113.7
dig photos.files.example.com     # 203.0.113.7
.fi
.PP
So that a secondary notices changes, a zone is served with its file's serial
plus the seconds from 2023\-11\-14 to the last change of that directory. The
served serial grows with every change of the file's serial and of the published
records. It is the same in every shard and after every restart.
.SS What it answers
A name that is found is answered authoritatively with its records. A CNAME is
followed, up to eight steps, while its target is in the same zone. A wildcard
.RB ( *.label )
stands for every name below its parent that does not exist. A name below a
delegation gets a referral, without the authoritative flag: the NS records of
the cut, plus the addresses of those servers where the zone holds them. An
absent name is answered NXDOMAIN. A name without the type asked for is answered
NODATA. Both answers carry the zone's SOA, with a TTL of the lesser of its own
TTL and its MINIMUM (RFC 2308). The addresses of the hosts that an NS, MX or SRV
answer names follow in the additional section, when the zone has them and they
fit.
.PP
ANY is answered with one HINFO record, as RFC 8482 allows, rather than with
every record, so that it amplifies nothing. Classes other than IN, such as
CHAOS's version.bind, are answered REFUSED. A query with EDNS (RFC 6891) is
answered with EDNS. An answer over UDP stays within the size the query offers
and never exceeds 1232 bytes, the size that needs no fragments. An answer to a
query without EDNS stays within 512 bytes. An answer that does not fit is sent
as the question alone with TC set. The client then asks again over TCP, where it
gets the whole answer. A response is never answered. A malformed query is
answered FORMERR, and opcodes other than QUERY are answered NOTIMP.
.SS TCP
A connection may carry several queries one after another, and they are answered
in order (RFC 7766). A connection that is quiet for 10 seconds, with nothing
left to write, is closed. Only a whole query or a write counts as activity, so a
query begun and not finished leaves the connection quiet. A shard holds at most
.B \-\-max\-connections
connections (default 256). One more connection closes the quietest connection
that has nothing left to write, to make room. When a peer has not read 64 KiB of
answers, its connection's further queries wait unread until it has. A
connection whose answers have not moved for 30 seconds is closed.
.SS Stats
.B stats
adds these figures for each shard:
.BR zones ,
.BR records ,
.BR udp_queries ,
.BR tcp_queries ,
the answers by rcode
.RB ( noerror ,
.BR nxdomain ,
.BR refused ,
.BR formerr ,
.BR notimp ,
.BR other_rcode ),
.BR truncated ,
.BR referrals ,
.B dropped
(packets that were not queries),
.B rrl_dropped
and
.BR rrl_slipped ,
.B send_failed
(answers the socket would not take),
.BR tcp_conns ,
.BR tcp_refused ,
.B tcp_evicted
(connections closed to make room for a new one),
.BR transfers ,
.BR transfers_refused ,
.BR notify_sent ,
.BR notify_acked ,
.BR notify_given_up ,
.B zone_reloads
and
.BR zone_reload_failures .
.SH SYNC FLAGS
A
.B sync
runtime is one job that carries files between a directory and a storage.
Because each job is its own runtime, a job that goes wrong has its own line in
.BR "socketley ls" ,
its own figures, its own lines in the log and its own alert.
.PP
.nf
socketley create sync photos \-\-dir /srv/photos \-\-storage ui/photos \e
    \-\-credentials /etc/socketley/sync.creds \-\-mode out \-s
.fi
.PP
A sync runtime listens on nothing and takes no
.BR \-p .
.B \-\-shards
is refused, because the shards would share no socket and several processes
would write one index.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dir " path
The directory whose files are carried. Required. The path must be absolute, and
the directory must exist when the runtime starts.
.TP
.BI "\-\-storage " target\fR[\fB,\fItarget\fR...]
Where the files go. Required. Each target takes one of three forms, with the
bucket last:
.RS
.TP
.B ui/photos
A storage runtime of this daemon, reached on its port at 127.0.0.1. A name with
no scheme and no port always names a runtime, even when it contains dots.
.TP
.B 10.0.0.5:9000/photos
A host and a port, over plain HTTP.
.TP
.B https://files.example/photos
A URL, http or https, with nothing between the host and the bucket.
.RE
.IP
A storage runtime that serves TLS itself must be named by its URL, since its
port does not speak plain HTTP. Naming it by its runtime name is refused, and
the error gives the URL to use. A bucket name follows the S3 rules: 3 to 63
characters of
.BR a\-z ,
.BR 0\-9 ,
.B .
and
.BR \- .
More than one target is accepted only with
.BR "\-\-mode out" .
Carrying two targets into one directory could not tell which target a file came
from when they differ.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode out" | in | inout
Which way files are carried, in the same three words a server and a client take.
.B out
sends the directory to the storage,
.B in
brings the storage to the directory, and
.B inout
does both. Default:
.BR out .
.TP
.BI "\-\-credentials " file
The file the job signs its requests with. Required, since a sync signs every
request it makes. The file holds
.B access_key
and
.B secret_key
as
.I key = value
lines, plus
.B session_token
and
.B region
when they are needed.
.B #
begins a comment. A file that other users may read is refused, at create and at
every read:
.B chmod 600
it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-prefix " key
The key that everything is written under, below the bucket. Default: the
bucket's root.
.TP
.BI "\-\-every " duration
How often the job walks the whole directory, and with
.B in
or
.B inout
also lists the storage. The duration is written as
.BR 30s ,
.BR 10m ,
.B 1h
or
.BR 1d .
.B never
walks the directory once, at start. Default: one hour. A job that brings the
storage here lists it once a minute when
.B \-\-every
is never.
.IP
Between walks, inotify reports which files changed. A file is carried once it
is written and closed, moved in or touched. It is removed once it is deleted. A
directory that appears or disappears, or more events than the kernel keeps,
causes a walk. When inotify is not available or its watches run out
.RB ( fs.inotify.max_user_watches ),
the log says so once, and the walks alone find changes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-repositories " file
Makes the job pull the git repositories one user keeps in a storage runtime,
instead of carrying a directory. See
.B "Git repositories"
under
.BR "STORAGE FLAGS" .
The job is then that user's own, and takes no
.BR \-\-dir ,
.B \-\-storage
or
.BR \-\-credentials .
The storage runtime makes such a job for each of its users and writes the file;
it is not meant to be made by hand.
.TP
.BI "\-\-exclude " pattern\fR[\fB,\fIpattern\fR...]
Shell patterns for files and directories that are not carried, separated by
commas. Spaces around the patterns are ignored:
.BR "\(aq*.tmp, *.md, node_modules\(aq" .
A pattern with no slash names a file or directory at any depth, like a
.I .gitignore
line. A pattern with a slash is matched against the whole path below
.BR \-\-dir ,
and its
.B *
crosses slashes. Nothing that matches is carried, and a matching directory is
not walked.
.PP
.B "What it remembers."
A sync keeps an index of what it last carried, in
.IR state\-dir /sync/ name .index,
with mode 0600. Without the index, a file deleted here could not be told apart
from a file never carried. With it, the deleted file is removed from the far
side and the new file is sent. Every record carries a checksum, and the first
record that fails its checksum ends the file. So a crash costs at most carrying
again what the last moments carried. A file is never believed carried when it
was not. The index belongs to one directory and one prefix. When either
changes, the index is started again: everything is carried once more and
nothing is removed.
.B "socketley remove"
deletes the index, and a rename carries it along.
.PP
.B "How it carries."
The job sends up to eight requests at a time per target, each on a connection
kept between requests, and never two requests about one file. A file above
512 MiB goes as a multipart upload in parts of 64 MiB, since one request
carries at most 5 GiB. If a file changes while its parts are being sent, the
upload is given up, and the file is carried again once it is written. Without
TLS, a file's bytes go from the disk to the socket by splice. A link to a file
is carried as the file it names. The link is removed at the walk that finds it
pointing at nothing, since no event reports that a link's target is gone. A
link to a directory is not followed. A
.B \-\-dir
that holds the directory of a storage runtime it names, or lies inside it, is
refused, since every object carried would be carried again.
.PP
.B "What it takes away."
A file is removed from the storage only when it is provably gone here: deleted,
or a link to nothing. Three kinds of file are left where they are: a file the
walk could not read, a file below a directory the walk may not enter, and a
file that an
.B \-\-exclude
names since it was carried. If a directory is empty while the index says files
were carried from it, nothing is removed, and
.B last_error
says why. A disk that is not mounted looks exactly like that, and a bucket
emptied because of it cannot be filled again.
.PP
.B "Both ways."
With
.B in
or
.BR inout ,
the job lists the storage first, at start and then at every
.BR \-\-every .
It compares each object with the index and with the file here. With
.BR inout ,
the walk follows each listing. A file the storage has and this side does not is
brought here. It is fetched in ranges of 8 MiB, written below
.I .socketley\-sync
in
.BR \-\-dir ,
and given its name once it is whole. A file the storage changed since is
brought again. A file the storage removed is removed here, unless it changed
here since. At the start, a file that both sides have with the same length is
taken to be the same file.
.PP
A file that both sides changed is kept twice, on both sides. This side's
version stays at its name and is carried out over the storage's. The storage's
version is kept beside it as
.IR "name (conflict date time).ext" .
With
.BR inout ,
every write and removal sent carries the etag this side last saw. So if the
storage made a change meanwhile, it refuses the request, and the next listing
keeps both versions, rather than the later write winning. With
.BR in ,
nothing is carried out: a file changed here is put back as the storage has it,
and this side's version is kept beside it. A key with an empty,
.B .
or
.B ..
segment is not brought. A folder that is a link out of
.B \-\-dir
is not written through. A listing with nothing in it removes nothing here.
.PP
.B "What a failure means."
A file the storage refuses, and a target that does not answer, are counted.
Each is logged once for as long as the reason stays the same. A walk thirty
seconds later tries again, whatever
.B \-\-every
says. A target that stops answering holds up only its own queue, not the
others. The runtime keeps running. A sync that keeps failing for three readings
in a row raises an alert, with
.B last_error
as its detail. The alert clears once something is carried again. A named
runtime that is not a storage, or does not exist, is a failure of the same kind.
.PP
.B "Its figures"
(\fBsocketley stats\fR) are
.BR mode ,
.BR dir ,
.B indexed
(files the index holds),
.BR carried ,
.BR removed ,
.BR bytes_carried ,
.B waiting
(what is queued),
.BR failures ,
.B failing
(failures since the last thing carried),
.B watched
(directories inotify watches),
.B brought
(files brought from the storage),
.B removed_here
(files removed here because the storage removed them),
.B conflicts
(files both sides changed),
.B last_run_secs
and
.BR last_error .
.SH IDP FLAGS
An
.B idp
runtime is an OpenID Connect provider that keeps its own accounts. A user signs
in once. After that, every application that asks the provider knows who they
are, and so do every route a proxy puts behind it, a storage's web interface and
the dashboard.
.PP
.nf
socketley create idp auth \-p 9400 \-\-domain auth.example.com \e
    \-\-clients /etc/socketley/idp.clients \-\-cookie\-domain example.com \-s
socketley auth user add smiley \-\-password \- \-\-groups admins
.fi
.PP
An account is a fixed random id plus fields with no schema. The provider reads
these fields:
.BR username ,
.B password
(a scrypt hash),
.BR disabled ,
.B groups
(comma\-separated; members of
.B admins
are admins),
.BR name ,
.BR email ,
.B picture
and
.BR reset .
It keeps every other field as it is, for the applications. So a field an
application adds needs no change here, and an older build keeps the fields it
does not know. Data that only one application uses, such as a game's
characters or a shop's orders, belongs in that application, under the account's
id. Every change is synced to a journal under the state directory before it is
answered. Changes are folded into a snapshot that names its format. A build
older than that format refuses the snapshot rather than rewriting it. See
.B idp\-user
under
.BR COMMANDS .
.PP
The provider serves:
.IP \(bu 2
the authorization code flow with PKCE, and refresh tokens;
.IP \(bu 2
discovery at
.BR /.well\-known/openid\-configuration ;
.IP \(bu 2
its key at
.BR /jwks ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR /userinfo ,
which answers from the account as it is now;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR /logout ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR /account ,
where a user changes their name, address and password, and signs out
everywhere.
.PP
Turning a second step on or off, and making an app password, ask for the
password again. Where a second step is on, they also ask for a code. These
checks count as a sign-in's do, so a session alone can change neither. A second
step that is on can be replaced only by turning it off. Signing out everywhere,
and setting a new password, end the account's app passwords along with its
sessions.
.PP
Tokens are signed ES256 with a P\-256 key. The key is made at the first start
and kept in the state directory, so a restart signs nobody out. A refresh token
is read back only by the provider, and is signed HS256 under a secret made from
that key. A refresh token is good once. A refresh gives the next token of its
sign-in, which is kept with the account. A refresh token used a second time ends
that sign-in, including its newest token. Passwords are hashed and checked off
the event loop. The runtime uses one thread for each core it may use, up to
four, shared out among its shards. Each thread computes up to four sign-ins'
scrypt at once with AVX2. The provider serves plain HTTP and is reached through
the entrance, which terminates TLS for its names as for every other runtime's.
.B \-\-tls
is refused.
.PP
.B Guessing.
Five failed sign-ins from one address within fifteen minutes refuse that address
until the failures age out. An IPv6 address is counted by its /64. Sign-ins that
are still being checked count with the failures, so many sign-ins sent at once
are all refused. Ten failures at one account, from anywhere, make its next try
wait half a minute. Each further failure doubles the wait, up to five minutes. A
browser that signed in to the account before keeps a count of its own, through a
provider cookie that lasts ninety days. So guesses from elsewhere never hold up
the account's user. Three wrong codes end a sign-in's second step, and the
password is then asked for again. Five wrong codes at an account hold up its
codes, as failed passwords do.
.PP
A mail runtime checks passwords for some clients that it does not guard itself:
a client on its own machine, or one behind a forwarder there. These checks are
counted at the account apart from its sign-ins. Ten failures hold up the next
check in the same way. At most 64 password checks wait at a shard. A sign-in
past them is told to try again in a moment (503).
.PP
.B On several cores.
With
.BI "\-\-shards " N
the provider is served by
.I N
worker processes on its one port
.RB ( SO_REUSEPORT ).
Each shard signs in, gives tokens and answers
.B /userinfo
with the same key. Shard 0 writes the accounts, as a provider with one shard
does. Every other shard reads shard 0's journal from the same files before it
answers. So a change made with
.B idp\-user
or on the account page holds at every shard once it is answered. A change made
at another shard is sent to shard 0. A code is redeemed at the shard that gave
it, which the code names, wherever the exchange arrives. It is good once there.
A sign-in is counted at every shard while it is being checked. A failed sign-in
is answered only once every shard counts it against its address and its
account. So guesses sent at once over the shards share one budget. The number
of shards may change across a restart.
.PP
A sign-in is a cookie of the provider's. Three kinds of runtime verify it
themselves: a proxy with
.BR "\-\-auth idp:" ,
a storage with
.BR \-\-idp ,
and the dashboard with
.BR daemon.dashboard_idp .
They verify it against the key the provider publishes beside its state, once for
each session until it expires. They also check each account's standing, which
the provider publishes there too. So a session ended at the provider ends there
at once, and at those runtimes within a second. A session ends when the user
signs out at
.BR /logout ,
signs out everywhere, or when the account is disabled.
.PP
.B /logout
ends the session the browser sends, and any copy of its cookie, and keeps the
account's other sessions. If another site sent the browser to it, the user is
asked first, unless the application names the account by an
.B id_token_hint
it was given. Past 32 sessions signed out this way within their lifetimes, or
for a cookie from an earlier build, a sign-out ends all of the account's
sessions and its refresh tokens, as signing out everywhere does.
.TP
.BI "\-\-clients " file
The applications that sign in through the provider, one per line. Each line is
.IR "id secret redirect" " [" redirect ...].
A secret of
.B \-
marks a public client, which must then send a PKCE challenge. A redirect is an
https address, compared whole, or an http address on this machine (localhost,
127.0.0.1). An http address on this machine is taken at any port, since an
application on the machine picks its port as it runs.
.IP
In place of a redirect,
.B device
lets the client sign a person in by the device grant, as
.B socketley login
does. One address, or one IPv6 /48, may hold at most twenty grants waiting at a
shard.
.B ssh
lets the client's access tokens ask for SSH certificates. The file is read again
when it changes. If it breaks while the runtime runs, the clients read before
stay in use.
.TP
.BI "\-\-ssh\-ca " hours
Runs an SSH certificate authority that gives user certificates lasting
.I hours
(1 to 168). Its Ed25519 key is made at the first start and kept in the state
directory. The public key is served at
.BR /ssh/ca.pub .
.B /ssh/user\-cert
gives a user certificate for the account whose access token is sent. A
certificate is given only to a client marked
.BR ssh ,
never to a disabled account, and never on a sign-in more than twelve hours old,
refreshed tokens included. Such a sign-in is told to sign in again. A user
certificate permits a terminal, port forwarding and agent forwarding. It permits
neither X11 forwarding nor the user's
.IR ~/.ssh/rc .
Every certificate is logged with its serial, the account, its principals and
the key's fingerprint. It is kept until it expires so that it can be revoked
(see
.BR "idp\-ssh revoke" ).
See
.B ssh\-cert
and
.BR idp\-ssh .
.TP
.BI "\-\-issuer " url
The https address the provider is reached at, which every token names. Default:
.BI https:// name
for the first
.BR \-\-domain .
.TP
.BI "\-\-resources " list
The resource servers an access token may be given for (RFC 8707), as
comma\-separated https addresses. An example is
.BR https://mcp.example.com/mcp .
An application that sends
.B resource
with one of them at
.B /authorize
gets an access token that names that resource as its audience. A runtime that
takes tokens for its own clients does not take such a token; an
.B mcp
runtime with
.B \-\-idp
does.
.IP
With any resource set, an application may also sign in with the https address
of its client metadata document as its client id, as MCP clients such as Claude
Code do. The document is fetched from public addresses only, and is at most five
kilobytes. It is kept as long as its
.B Cache\-Control
says, within a minute and a day. It is taken only for a public client that sends
a PKCE challenge, with redirects that are https or on this machine. Such an
application is let in only for one of these resources. It is let in only once
the signed-in person allows it, every time it asks. The page that asks names
the application, its host, the resource and where the person is sent back to.
.B stats
counts
.BR consents ,
.B client_fetches
and
.BR client_fetch_failures .
.TP
.BI "\-\-cookie\-domain " domain
The domain whose names share the sign-in cookie. A user signed in here is then
signed in for a proxy, a storage or the dashboard on any name below it.
Default: the cookie is for the provider's host alone.
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " N
The number of worker processes that serve the provider, 1 to 64. Shard 0 writes
the accounts, and every shard signs in (see
.B On several cores
above). The number can change only while the runtime is stopped.
.PP
An address that failed to sign in five times in fifteen minutes is refused
until the oldest of those failures is fifteen minutes old. When the request
came through a proxy on this machine, the address counted is the last address
that
.B X\-Forwarded\-For
names.
.SH DB FLAGS
A
.B db
runtime keeps JSON documents for applications, such as a game's characters, a
shop's orders and addresses, or a user's settings. Each document is owned by an
account of an
.B idp
runtime. An account never writes another account's documents, and each
collection's access in
.B \-\-schema
says who reads them. Large things,
such as a picture or a model, belong in a storage, with their address kept
here.
.PP
.nf
socketley create db data \-p 9500 \-\-domain data \-\-idp auth \e
    \-\-schema /etc/socketley/db.schema \-\-keys /etc/socketley/db.keys \-s
socketley create db copy \-p 9500 \-\-replicate https://data.example.com \e
    \-\-replicate\-key /etc/socketley/db.replica\-key \-\-keys /etc/socketley/db.keys \-s
.fi
.PP
A document lives at
.BI /c/ collection / id .
.B GET
reads it,
.B PUT
replaces it,
.B PATCH
changes it in place and
.B DELETE
removes it. A
.B POST
to
.BI /c/ collection
makes a new document under a new id. The answer holds the document's fields
plus
.BR _id ,
.B _v
(its version: 1 when made, one more with each change, and also its
.BR ETag )
and
.B _owner
(the account's id).
.B "If-Match: \(dq3\(dq"
writes only at that version, and
.B "If-None-Match: *"
writes only where no document exists. Otherwise the answer is 412. A patch
looks like
.B {"set":{"a.b":1},"unset":["c"],"inc":{"n":1},"push":{"tags":"x"},"pull":{"tags":"y"}}
and works on dotted paths.
.PP
.BI POST " /c/" collection /_query
takes a query such as
.BR {"where":{"level":{"gte":5},"name":"x"},"sort":"\-level","limit":50,"skip":0} .
The conditions are eq, ne, gt, gte, lt, lte, in, prefix and exists.
.B "count":true
answers the number alone. The answer is
.BR {"docs":[...]} ,
plus
.B next
when there are more. A range compares a field's values of one kind only, in the
order an index uses: null, false, true, numbers, strings. A condition or a sort
on a field the schema does not index is judged by reading the documents. The
query reads at most 8 MiB of them, unless a key asks for more. Past that, it is
refused with 400
.BR needs_index :
index the field.
.B POST /tx
takes
.B {"ops":[{"op":"put","c":..,"id":..,"doc":{..}},{"op":"patch",..,"changes":{..},"if":3},{"op":"insert",..},{"op":"delete",..}]}
and makes every change or none.
.B GET /me
names who is asking.
.PP
A request comes from one of three kinds of caller:
.IP \(bu 2
a key, sent as
.BI "Authorization: Bearer " secret\fR,
which reaches everything;
.IP \(bu 2
an account of the
.B \-\-idp
runtime, by its sign-in cookie or by an access token the provider gave one of
its clients, sent as
.BR "Authorization: Bearer" ;
.IP \(bu 2
nobody.
.PP
A write made with the cookie must be
.B application/json
and come from the database's own host or an origin in
.BR \-\-origins .
A form that another site posts cannot meet that. A document over 1 MiB, or
nested deeper than 16 levels, is refused.
.PP
The documents live on the disk, in segments under the state directory. Each
write is one record, appended to the newest segment. A segment is closed at
64 MiB and another is begun. Memory holds where each document lies, its
version, its owner and the values its indexes order it by. Memory also holds a
cache of the documents last read
.RB ( \-\-cache ).
The rest are read from the disk, so a database holds far more than memory.
.PP
A read may need a document that the page cache does not hold. Such a read is
first tried without it, the document is brought in through the ring, and the
read is answered then. So a slow disk holds up that read and not every other.
Past three tries, the read waits in line.
.B stats
counts these reads as
.BR fetched .
Some sealed segments have mostly been written again or deleted. They are merged
off the event loop into one segment of their living documents, and the old
segments are removed. A record torn by a crash ends the newest segment where
the last whole record ends. Damage anywhere else refuses the start, so the
database must be restored.
.PP
No answer is given before what it rests on is on the disk: a write's own record,
or the change a read saw. So nothing is read that a crash could take back. One
sync runs at a time, off the event loop. It covers every record written while
the previous sync ran. So a burst of writes costs a few syncs rather than one
each, and reads go on being answered meanwhile. A read behind a write on one
connection is answered after the write. A script of this daemon reaches the
documents as
.BR socketley.db .
The runtime serves plain HTTP behind the entrance.
.PP
.B On several cores.
With
.BI "\-\-shards " N
the database is served by
.I N
worker processes on its one port
.RB ( SO_REUSEPORT ).
Shard 0 writes the one log, as a database with one shard does. Every other
shard reads that log from the same files, as shard 0 puts it on the disk. Those
shards answer reads, queries and streams themselves, and send writes to shard 0.
A write is answered once it is on shard 0's disk. Before a shard answers, it
takes in what shard 0 has made durable. So a read on any shard sees every write
that was answered before the read began. Transactions, unique indexes, streams,
replicas and backups hold as they do on one shard. The number of shards may
change across a restart, since the documents are one log whatever the number.
.PP
Every shard answers reads and queries. Writes wait for one disk, so the shards
together take at most as many writes as one shard takes. Writes also cost more
cores than on one shard, since each write that reaches another shard is handed
on to shard 0, and its answer is handed back. A database that is mostly written
is served best by one shard.
.PP
.B Changes, as they happen.
.BI GET " /c/" collection /_feed
is a stream of Server-Sent Events, which a browser reads with
.BR EventSource .
Each change is sent once it is on the disk, numbered by the change. A change is
an event
.B put
with
.B {"collection":..,"id":..,"owner":..,"version":..,"doc":{..}}
or an event
.B delete
with
.BR {"collection":..,"id":..,"deleted":true} .
The reader sees only what it may read, as with a query: an account sees its own
documents of an owner collection.
.B GET /_feed
streams the changes of every collection, to a key. A stream that broke resumes
from the last change it read, with
.B Last\-Event\-ID
(or
.BR ?since= ).
The database keeps about 32 MiB of changes. A stream resuming from an older
change is told
.B reset
and should read again what it shows. A comment every fifteen seconds keeps a
quiet stream from looking dead.
.PP
.B Documents with a time to go.
A collection whose schema line names
.BI ttl: field
loses a document once that field's number, in seconds since 1970, has passed.
From that moment the document is answered as absent. Its deletion is written
and streamed within the minute.
.PP
.B A copy on another machine.
A db runtime with
.B \-\-replicate
follows another database's
.B /_replicate
stream, with a key of that database. It takes every document once, then every
change. It writes them to its own disk with each change's own number, so after
a restart of either side it goes on where it was. When the source no longer
keeps the changes the replica missed, the source sends everything again. A
replica answers reads, queries and streams of its own. It refuses writes with
403, naming where to make them. Removing
.B \-\-replicate
.RB ( "import \-\-restart" )
makes it a database of its own.
.PP
.B Backups.
.BI "socketley db\-backup " "db file"
writes every document into one file, off the event loop, while the database
serves.
.BI "socketley db\-restore " "db file"
stops the database, keeps its documents aside in
.IB dir .before\-restore ,
and starts it again on the backup's documents. With
.BR \-\-backup\-dir ,
a backup is taken every
.BR \-\-backup\-every ,
and the newest
.B \-\-backup\-keep
backups are kept. A sync runtime can carry that directory to a storage.
.TP
.BI "\-\-schema " file
The collections, one per line: a name, who reaches it, and the fields indexed.
.B unique:
before a field marks an index whose values no two documents share.
.RS
.nf
# collection  access   indexes
characters    owner    level unique:name
posts         shared   created
scores        public   score
products      catalog  unique:sku price
sessions      owner    ttl:expires_at
.fi
.RE
.IP
The access is one of these:
.B private
(and any collection the file does not name): only a key reaches it.
.BR owner :
an account reads and writes its own documents, and another account's documents
are answered as not there.
.BR shared :
an account reads every document and writes its own.
.BR public :
anybody reads, and an account writes its own.
.BR catalog :
anybody reads, and a key writes. The file is read again when it changes. If the
new file does not read, or a unique index is broken by the documents already
there, the previous schema stays, and
.B stats
says why.
.TP
.BI "\-\-keys " file
Service keys for backends, one per line:
.IR "name secret" .
The secret must be at least 16 characters. The file is read again when it
changes. A key may set a document's
.BR _owner .
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " runtime
The idp runtime whose accounts own documents.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " clients
The ids of the idp's clients whose access tokens the runtime takes,
comma\-separated. A token the provider gave any other application is refused,
since it belongs to that application and is no key to this runtime. Without the
flag no access token is taken, while keys and the sign-in cookie reach it as
before.
.TP
.BI "\-\-origins " list
The web origins a browser may call the database from, comma\-separated. They
are answered with CORS and the browser's sign-in.
.B *
allows any page, without the sign-in.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-bytes " size
The most the documents may hold. A write past it is refused with 507.
.TP
.BI "\-\-cache " size
The memory that holds the documents last read. Default: 64M. The rest are read
from the disk.
.TP
.BI "\-\-replicate " url
The http or https address of the database this one copies, as its entrance
answers it. An https address is verified against the system's roots.
.TP
.BI "\-\-replicate\-key " file
A file holding the secret of a key of that database.
.TP
.BI "\-\-backup\-dir " dir
The directory scheduled backups are written to, each as
.IB name \- time .dbbackup .
.TP
.BI "\-\-backup\-every " duration
How often a scheduled backup is taken. Default: 1d.
.TP
.BI "\-\-backup\-keep " n
How many scheduled backups are kept. Default: 7.
.TP
.BI "\-\-shards " N
The number of worker processes that serve the database, 1 to 64. Shard 0
writes, and every shard reads (see
.B On several cores
above). The number can change only while the runtime is stopped.
.PP
In a script:
.nf
local c, err = socketley.db.get("data", "characters", id)
socketley.db.put("data", "characters", id, { name = "Merlin", level = 3 }, { ["if"] = c._v })
socketley.db.patch("data", "characters", id, { inc = { level = 1 } })
local r = socketley.db.query("data", "scores", { sort = "\-score", limit = 10 })
socketley.db.insert("data", "orders", { item = "sword" })
socketley.db.delete("data", "orders", id)
socketley.db.tx("data", { { op = "put", c = "a", id = "x", doc = {} }, { op = "delete", c = "b", id = "y" } })
.fi
.PP
Each function answers the document (or the transaction's results), or nil, an
error code and a sentence. Inside a callback that can wait, the call waits.
Anywhere else, the last argument is a function that is called with the answer.
.SH MQTT FLAGS
An
.B mqtt
runtime is an MQTT broker for versions 3.1, 3.1.1 and 5.0, over TCP, TLS and
WebSocket. Devices, apps and browsers publish to topics and subscribe to them.
A client that goes away keeps its session until it comes back or the session
expires. The session holds its subscriptions and the messages sent to them
while the client was gone. The broker is a runtime like the others. Its own
rules pass messages to a cache, a db, a storage, a server or another broker.
A storage's events and a script's messages reach its subscribers.
.PP
.nf
socketley mqtt passwd /etc/socketley/mqtt.users sensor\-17
socketley create mqtt broker \-\-users /etc/socketley/mqtt.users \e
    \-\-acl /etc/socketley/mqtt.acl \-\-ws 8083 \-\-persistent on \-s
socketley create mqtt devices \-p 8883 \-\-tls \-\-cert dev.pem \-\-key dev.key \e
    \-\-ca devices\-ca.pem \-\-routes /etc/socketley/mqtt.routes \-s
.fi
.PP
The broker listens on 1883, or on 8883 with
.BR \-\-tls ,
unless
.B \-p
gives another port.
.B \-\-max\-connections
and
.B \-\-write\-buffer
work as for a server. When a slow client owes more than the write buffer, its
QoS 0 messages are dropped and counted, and its QoS 1 and 2 messages wait in its
session.
.PP
.B What it implements.
The broker implements:
.IP \(bu 2
QoS 0, 1 and 2. QoS 2 is delivered exactly once, by its packet identifier.
.IP \(bu 2
Retained messages, cleared by an empty one.
.IP \(bu 2
Wills. A will is sent when a connection is lost, or when a 5.0 client
disconnects and asks for it. It is not sent when the client disconnects
cleanly. A 5.0 will delay is waited out if one is given, and the will is not
sent at all when the client comes back within it.
.IP \(bu 2
Sessions, kept by 3.1.1's clean session 0 and by 5.0's session expiry. A client
id has one connection. A second connection takes the session over, and the
first is told so. A session belongs to its user, so a CONNECT under another user
(or none) is refused as not authorized.
.IP \(bu 2
Keep alive: a client that is silent for one and a half times its keep alive is
disconnected.
.IP \(bu 2
A CONNECT is required within ten seconds; otherwise the connection is closed.
.IP \(bu 2
From 5.0: session and message expiry, the receive maximum both ways, topic
aliases, the maximum packet size, subscription identifiers, no local, retain as
published, and retain handling.
.IP \(bu 2
From 5.0: shared subscriptions
.RB ( $share/\fIgroup\fB/\fIfilter\fR,
each message to one member, in turn), and request and response (the response
topic and correlation data are carried).
.IP \(bu 2
From 5.0: user properties, the content type and the payload format, and reason
codes and strings on what it refuses.
.PP
The wildcards
.B +
and
.B #
do not match a topic that begins with
.BR $ .
The broker publishes its own figures, retained, under
.B $SYS/broker/
every ten seconds: version, uptime, clients, messages and subscriptions. It
does not take the 5.0 AUTH exchange: a CONNECT that names an authentication
method is refused with 0x8C. It does not bridge to brokers outside the daemon.
.PP
.B Who may connect.
At least one of
.BR \-\-users ,
.BR \-\-idp ,
.B \-\-ca
or
.B \-\-anonymous
must be given. A client certificate that
.B \-\-ca
verified lets its client in under the certificate's common name. A password is
checked against the users file, off the event loop. Otherwise, with
.BR \-\-idp ,
the password is taken as an access token that provider gave a client
.B \-\-idp\-audience
names, and the token
account's name is the user. A user name given with the token must be that
name. Without credentials, a client is let in only with
.BR \-\-anonymous .
A refusal is 0x86 (bad user name or password), or 4 to a 3.1.1 client. A
script's
.B on_auth
has the last word (see
.B Lua
below).
.PP
.B What each may do.
.B \-\-acl
names rules in Mosquitto's form. The broker reads them again within two seconds
of a change:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# before any user line: clients without one
topic read public/#
user alice
topic readwrite home/alice/#
topic write telemetry/#
topic deny telemetry/secret/#
pattern readwrite users/%u/#
pattern write devices/%c/state
.fi
.RE
.IP
.B topic
.RB [ read | write | readwrite | deny ]
.I filter
applies to the user named last.
.B pattern
applies to everyone. In a pattern,
.B %u
stands for the user and
.B %c
for the client id, each as one level. A deny wins over any grant, and what no
rule grants is refused. A refused publish is answered 0x87 to a 5.0 client. To a
3.1.1 client, it is acknowledged and dropped. A subscription is refused only
when no rule grants all of it. A message that a deny covers is held back from a
subscription that the deny covers in part. Without
.BR \-\-acl ,
every client may do everything.
.PP
.B Sessions across a restart.
With
.BR \-\-persistent ,
the broker writes a snapshot every
.B \-\-persistent\-interval
when something changed, off the event loop, and at stop. The snapshot holds the
sessions, their queues and messages in flight, the retained messages and the
topic rules' queues. A start reads them back. So a stop, a restart of the daemon
or a killed worker loses at most one interval. At the start that finds them, a
session with no expiry left is ended, and the wills it was owed are sent.
Without
.BR \-\-persistent ,
the broker keeps nothing across a restart.
.PP
.B Topic rules.
.B \-\-routes
names a file of rules, one per line, each a filter and a target. Every message
the filter matches is handed to another runtime of the daemon, besides the
broker's own subscribers:
.RS
.PP
.nf
# filter          target
sensor/+/temp     cache://telemetry/stream/temps\-%1
alarm/#           cache://events/channel/alarms
device/+/state    db://fleet/devices/%1
log/#             storage://raw/mqtt/%Y/%m/%d/
chat/#            server://chat
share/#           mqtt://other/from\-edge/%t
door/#            https://hooks.example.com/door
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
.BI cache:// runtime /stream/ key
adds a stream entry with the fields
.BR topic ,
.BR payload ,
.B qos
and
.BR client .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI cache:// runtime /channel/ name
publishes the payload on the channel.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI db:// runtime / collection\fR[\fB/\fIid\fR]
puts a document under
.I id
when given, and as a new document otherwise. The document is the payload when
the payload is a JSON object, and otherwise
.BR {"topic":..,"payload":..,"client":..,"qos":..} .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI storage:// runtime / bucket\fR[\fB/\fIprefix\fR]
gathers the messages as JSON lines,
.BR {"at":..,"topic":..,"payload":..,"qos":..,"client":..} ,
into one
.B .ndjson
object a minute (or a megabyte, or at stop) under the prefix.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI server:// runtime
sends every client of the server
.B {"topic":..,"payload":..}
as a line.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI mqtt:// runtime / topic
publishes on that broker, at the message's QoS and retained as it was. A
message that has passed eight such rules on its way is passed on by no more of
them, so rules that lead to each other end.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR http:// " or " https:// " URL"
POSTs
.B {"topic":..,"payload":..,"qos":..,"retain":..,"client":..}
to the URL, one at a time and in order, on a connection it keeps. The host is
resolved on the broker's loop (see What can stop the event loop). A 2xx counts
as delivered. A 400 or another refusal is counted as failed and not sent again.
No answer, a 408, a 429 or a 5xx is tried again, as for a stopped runtime.
.IP
.B %1
to
.B %9
stand for the levels the filter's
.B +
matched,
.B %t
for the whole topic and
.B %c
for the client that published. A storage prefix also takes
.BR %Y ,
.BR %m ,
.BR %d ,
.B %H
and
.BR %M .
A line beginning with
.B #
is a comment, and so is a
.B #
after a target. The file is read at start. A file that does not read stops the
runtime, and the error names the line.
.IP
Each rule has a queue of its own, in order. A target that is stopped or does
not answer holds its messages, without holding up the broker or the other
rules. The rule tries again after a pause that grows to five seconds. It holds
up to 100000 messages; past that, the oldest are dropped and counted. What a
rule holds goes into the snapshot, with messages in flight in their place, so a
restart sends them again. A target gets each message at least once.
.B stats
names each rule with what it delivered, what its target refused, what was
dropped, what it holds and its last error.
.PP
.B From the other runtimes.
.B socketley mqtt\-pub
and a script's
.B socketley.mqtt.publish
publish as the broker itself. A storage's
.BI mqtt:// runtime / topic
notification target publishes the storage's events (see
.BR \-\-notify\-targets ).
The
.B mqtt://
rules of another broker publish that broker's messages:
.RS
.PP
.nf
local ok, err = socketley.mqtt.publish("broker", "game/" .. id .. "/move", move, { qos = 1 })
socketley.mqtt.publish("broker", "chat/lobby", text, { retain = false }, function(ok, err) end)
.fi
.RE
.IP
The call answers true once the broker has the message, or nil and the reason.
Inside a callback that can wait, it waits. Anywhere else, the last argument is
a function that is called with the answer.
.PP
.B WebSocket.
.BI "\-\-ws " port
serves MQTT inside WebSocket on a second port, for browsers. Any path works, and
.B /mqtt
is the convention. The subprotocol
.B mqtt
or
.B mqttv3.1
is chosen when offered. A packet may be carried in any number of frames, and a
frame may hold any number of packets. A frame may be no longer than four times
.BR \-\-max\-packet .
With
.BR \-\-tls ,
it is wss.
.PP
.B On several cores.
With
.BI "\-\-shards " N
the broker is
.I N
worker processes sharing its ports
.RB ( SO_REUSEPORT ),
and its clients see one broker. Each shard tells the others what its clients
subscribe to, and hands them the messages their subscribers want. Every shard
keeps each retained message. A shared subscription's message goes to one member
of the group, across all the shards. A client that connects to a different shard
than before takes its session with it. The newest connection of a client id
wins.
.PP
A shard may not take what another shard hands it as fast as it comes. It then
holds back that other shard's publishers, rather than lose their messages: they
are not read until it can take more. At QoS 1 and 2 this holds back all of
them, and at QoS 0 those whose last message went to it. A shard that has
answered nothing for a second holds back only QoS 1 and 2 publishers, whose
answer waits. A message for that shard is then dropped at QoS 0, or once 8 MiB
wait for it. At any QoS, a message is dropped once what the shard was handed
has failed for three seconds. Drops are counted in
.BR shard_dropped .
Each shard keeps a snapshot of its own,
.IB file . shard\fR,
and publishes its figures under
.BI $SYS/broker/shard/ n /\fR.
.PP
.B Lua.
A broker's
.B \-\-lua
script may decide who connects and what passes. Each hook that decides answers
.B true
to allow,
.B false
to refuse, or nothing to keep what the broker decided. A hook that fails
refuses. A client is a table of
.BR id ,
.BR username ,
.B peer
and
.BR version .
.RS
.PP
.nf
function on_auth(c)          \-\- c.id c.username c.password c.peer
    if c.username == "robot" then return c.password == "beep" end
end                          \-\- c.certificate c.authenticated c.account c.version
function on_publish(client, msg)              \-\- msg.topic msg.payload msg.qos msg.retain
    if msg.topic == "shout" then return { payload = string.upper(msg.payload) } end
end
function on_subscribe(client, filter, qos) return filter ~= "secret/#" end
function on_connect(client) self.publish("presence/" .. client.id, "online", { retain = true }) end
function on_disconnect(client, why) self.publish("presence/" .. client.id, "offline", { retain = true }) end
.fi
.RE
.IP
.B on_auth
is told whether the credentials checked out
.RB ( authenticated )
and as whom
.RB ( account ).
If it lets in a client whose credentials were refused, the user name the client
gave becomes its user.
.B on_publish
may also answer a table: the message as it is to go on, with any of
.BR topic ,
.BR payload ,
.B qos
and
.BR retain .
A refused publish is answered 0x87.
.B self.publish(topic, payload [, {qos=, retain=}])
publishes as the broker.
.B self.retained(topic)
reads a retained payload.
.B self.sessions()
lists this shard's sessions.
.B self.kick(client_id [, end])
disconnects one. While a hook decides, that connection's next packets wait.
.TP
.BI "\-\-users " file
The accounts that sign in with a password, one
.IB user : hash
per line, as
.B socketley mqtt passwd
writes them. The file is read again within two seconds of a change.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " runtime
The idp runtime whose access tokens, given as the password, sign clients in.
It takes only tokens given to a client that
.B \-\-idp\-audience
names.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " clients
The ids of the idp's clients whose access tokens the runtime takes,
comma\-separated. A token the provider gave any other application is refused,
since it belongs to that application and is no key to this runtime. Without the
flag no access token is taken, while the users file and client certificates
sign in as before.
.TP
.BI "\-\-acl " file
Who may publish and subscribe to what (see
.B What each may do
above).
.TP
.B \-\-anonymous
Lets in clients without credentials, as far as the rules allow.
.TP
.BI "\-\-routes " file
Where messages go besides their subscribers (see
.B Topic rules
above).
.TP
.BI "\-\-ws " port
A second port, which serves MQTT over WebSocket.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent " file \fR|\fBon
The snapshot file that sessions and retained messages are kept in.
.B on
means
.IB state /mqtt/ name .snapshot
under the state directory.
.TP
.BI "\-\-persistent\-interval " duration
How often changes are written to the snapshot. Default: 1s.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-packet " size
The largest packet a client may send, from 64 bytes to 256M. Default: 256K. A
larger packet is answered 0x95, and the connection is closed. The limit is told
to 5.0 clients. No message larger than a client says it takes is sent to that
client.
.TP
.BI "\-\-session\-expiry " duration
The longest a session outlives its connection, whatever the client asks.
Default: 1d. A 3.1.1 session kept by clean session 0 lasts this long.
.TP
.BI "\-\-session\-queue " n
The most messages kept for one session while its client is away or slow.
Default: 1000. Past it, the oldest are dropped and counted.
.TP
.BI "\-\-retained\-max " size
The most bytes that retained messages may take. Default: 64M. A new retained
message past it is not kept, and is counted.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-sessions " n
The most sessions, connected or not. Default: 100000. A new client past the
limit makes the broker look at the owner that holds the most sessions. An owner
is a user or, for clients without one, an address (IPv6 by its /64). If that
owner holds two or more, the broker ends its session that has been away
longest, and counts it as
.BR sessions_evicted .
So one owner filling the table locks nobody else out. Otherwise the new client
is refused with 0x97.
.TP
.BI "\-\-keepalive\-max " seconds
The longest keep alive the broker grants. Default: 1200. A 5.0 client that asks
for more, or for none, is told this value.
.PP
A client that leaves more than twice
.B \-\-write\-buffer
(default 8M) of what it was sent unread is not read until it has read half of
it. So its pings and acks cannot grow what it is owed.
.PP
.B stats
reports:
.IP \(bu 2
the sessions, and how many are connected;
.IP \(bu 2
the subscriptions, the retained messages and their bytes;
.IP \(bu 2
what is queued and in flight;
.IP \(bu 2
the messages received and sent at each QoS, and what was dropped and why;
.IP \(bu 2
the connects and the refusals;
.IP \(bu 2
the snapshots, and whether a change is in none yet;
.IP \(bu 2
each topic rule;
.IP \(bu 2
over shards, what was handed between them.
.SH MAIL FLAGS
A
.B mail
runtime is a mail server.
It accepts mail for its domains and mail that its users send.
It keeps each message in a spool until the message is delivered.
It hands mail for other domains to that domain's mail exchanger, or to a
smarthost.
It keeps its users' mailboxes in a storage runtime, and users read them over
IMAP.
.PP
The mail runtime works with the other runtimes.
A dns runtime publishes the records of its domains, an idp runtime provides
its users, and a storage runtime holds its mailboxes.
Other runtimes, the CLI and the daemon's alerts send mail through it.
.PP
Change its settings with
.BR import ;
add \-\-restart to apply all of them at once.
A running runtime applies new connection, rate and idle limits immediately,
and the other settings at its next start.
It reads the
.B \-\-users
and
.B \-\-aliases
files again whenever they change.
Renaming the runtime with
.B import
moves its spool, its keys and its mailbox state along with it.
A rename is refused while the runtime is running.
.B show
prints a
.B \-\-relay
password as ***.
Importing that printed output keeps the existing password unchanged.
.PP
.nf
socketley create mail m \-p 25 \-\-dns ns \-\-no\-reply \-s
socketley send m \-\-to ops@example.com \-\-subject "Backup done" \-\-text ok

socketley create mail m \-p 25 \-\-dns ns \-\-idp auth \-\-storage files/mail \e
    \-\-submission 587 \-\-imap 143 \-\-tls \-s
.fi
.PP
The runtime listens for SMTP on port 25 by default;
.B \-p
sets another port.
Port 25 requires that the daemon is allowed to bind it.
With
.BR \-\-shards ,
only the first shard accepts mail (on SMTP, submission and LMTP), so a single
spool commits all of it with one sync.
Every shard serves the mailboxes.
.PP
These flags open further ports:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-submission
opens a port where users send mail, with STARTTLS and a password.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-submissions
opens the same kind of port, but speaking TLS from the first byte.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-imap
and
.B \-\-imaps
open the same two kinds of port, for reading the mailboxes.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-pop3
and
.B \-\-pop3s
let clients read INBOX over POP3 (RFC 1939, with STLS, SASL PLAIN, UIDL and
TOP).
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-lmtp
accepts mail from this machine's own programs into the mailboxes over LMTP
(RFC 2033), with one answer for each recipient.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-jmap
serves JMAP (RFC 8620, RFC 8621) for mail clients and web apps, over HTTPS
with
.BR \-\-tls .
.PP
When a signed\-in user sends a message without a Date or a Message\-ID header,
the runtime adds the missing header, as Postfix's cleanup does.
A message from another server is passed on unchanged.
Without
.BR \-\-bind ,
each port accepts both IPv6 and IPv4.
An IPv4 client is identified, checked and logged by its IPv4 address.
.PP
With
.B \-\-tls
and
.BR \-\-dns ,
but no
.BR \-\-cert ,
the runtime uses the certificate of the dns runtime's entrance.
That certificate holds the names the runtime is reached by, and the entrance
renews it.
The runtime picks up the renewed certificate as it does any other.
If the entrance has not written the certificate yet, the runtime waits for it.
This happens when the mail runtime is created before its dns runtime, or on a
machine where the entrance has not yet been given its names.
Meanwhile, the runtime accepts mail on SMTP without STARTTLS.
The ports that take a password or speak TLS from the first byte open within a
second of the certificate appearing.
A certificate given with
.B \-\-cert
must exist when the runtime starts; otherwise the runtime does not start.
.PP
.B Signing in.
A user signs in in one of these ways:
.IP \(bu 2
with their password over TLS;
.IP \(bu 2
with their password in the clear, from this machine only;
.IP \(bu 2
with a token their idp gave a client that
.B \-\-idp\-audience
names, by XOAUTH2 or OAUTHBEARER on SMTP, IMAP and POP3.
Mail apps that sign in through an identity provider work this way.
.PP
If the account has its second sign\-in step turned on, a mail app signs in
with one of the account's app passwords (see
.BR idp\-user ).
.PP
Ten failed sign\-ins from one address within ten minutes block that address
for ten minutes.
Twenty failed sign\-ins to one account, from three or more addresses, block
that account for ten minutes.
The account block does not apply to the addresses the account signed in from
within the last thirty days.
So guesses spread over many addresses do not lock out the user's own devices.
Both blocks survive a restart.
The log records each sign\-in on one line, with the user, the protocol and the
address.
.PP
.B Whose mail it takes.
The runtime's domains are those given by
.BR \-\-mail\-domain ,
or else the zones of the dns runtime named by
.BR \-\-dns ,
which the runtime follows as they change.
It accepts mail for an address in its domains when:
.IP \(bu 2
an alias names the address
.RB ( \-\-aliases );
.IP \(bu 2
the address belongs to a user
.RB ( \-\-idp :
the local part is a username, or the address is the account's email);
.IP \(bu 2
the address is
.B no\-reply@
and the message is a bounce;
.IP \(bu 2
the script accepts it
.RB ( on_rcpt ).
.PP
Any other recipient is refused at RCPT with 550.
A local part with a tag,
.IB user + tag @\fR,
belongs to the user, and Sieve's subaddress extension reads the tag.
.B \-\-recipient\-delimiter
sets other separator characters, or none.
An address that this runtime rewrote under SRS (see below) is accepted as the
original sender it stands for.
Such an address is accepted for up to 21 days, and only if it was made with the
runtime's own key.
.PP
Mail for other domains is accepted only from a signed\-in user, or from
127.0.0.1 (this machine's own programs).
From anyone else it is refused with 554, so the server is not a relay.
.PP
.B Taking mail.
The runtime speaks ESMTP with PIPELINING, SIZE, 8BITMIME, SMTPUTF8,
ENHANCEDSTATUSCODES, STARTTLS, AUTH PLAIN and LOGIN, CHUNKING (BDAT), DSN,
LIMITS (RCPTMAX, RFC 9422) and, inside TLS, REQUIRETLS (RFC 8689).
Replies come in the order of the commands.
.PP
A message larger than
.B \-\-max\-size
is refused.
The refusal comes at MAIL when the client's SIZE parameter shows the size, and
after the end of the message otherwise.
A message ends only at a CRLF, a dot and a CRLF, or with the chunk that BDAT
marks as the last.
A bare LF or a lone CR inside a message is written as CRLF.
So a dot after either cannot end a message, here or on the next server.
Anything a client sent after STARTTLS before the handshake is dropped.
A recipient given twice counts as one recipient.
An EHLO name that contains a control byte or a space is refused.
.PP
AUTH is offered over TLS, and in the clear only to 127.0.0.1.
The answer to a wrong password comes one second later.
Ten wrong passwords from one address within ten minutes lock that address out
for ten minutes.
During that time even the right password is refused (454, or [UNAVAILABLE] on
IMAP).
.PP
On the submission ports, the runtime accepts nothing from a client that has
not signed in.
A signed\-in user may send only as their own address, in both MAIL FROM and the
From header.
Their own address is their username at one of the runtime's domains, the email
of their idp account, or the username itself when it is an address.
A connection that is idle for five minutes is closed.
.PP
.B Limits.
One client address may hold at most 50 connections to port 25, and 200 to each
other port.
These limits do not apply to 127.0.0.1.
Without
.BR \-\-max\-connections ,
the runtime holds at most 8192 connections.
A connection past a limit is answered 421 (BYE on IMAP and ManageSieve) and
closed.
.PP
A client is answered 421 and closed in two cases: it sends more than its
largest message plus one mebibyte while a command is pending, or it leaves 32
MiB of replies unread.
If it still reads nothing five seconds later, the connection is reset.
.PP
.B \-\-rate\-limit
.I n
accepts
.I n
messages a second from one client address or one signed\-in user, and answers
the next MAIL with 450.
.B \-\-global\-rate\-limit
sets the same kind of limit for the whole runtime.
.PP
.B What it checks.
With
.BR \-\-dnsbl ,
the runtime looks up the address of each connection on port 25 in each list
while it greets the client.
Connections from 127.0.0.1 are not looked up.
If the answer has not arrived by the time of MAIL, MAIL waits for it.
A client that a list names (with an address in 127/8) cannot send anything and
is answered 554, unless it signs in.
An error answer from a list (127.255.255.x) names nobody, and a list that does
not answer lets the mail through.
Each answer is kept for five minutes.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-greylist ,
a stranger's first attempt with a given sender and recipient from a given
network (a /24, or a /64) is answered 451.
The message is accepted when the client tries again after the delay and within
a day.
From then on, that combination is accepted for 36 days.
What one shard learns, every shard knows.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-rspamd ,
each message from other servers is also scanned by that Rspamd's
.BR /checkv2 ,
alongside the checks below.
Rspamd's actions take effect as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
reject refuses the message with 554;
.IP \(bu 2
soft reject and greylist answer it with 451;
.IP \(bu 2
add header and rewrite subject file it in Junk.
.PP
The score goes into Authentication\-Results as x\-spam, which Sieve's
spamtest reads.
If the scanner does not answer within ten seconds, the mail is let through.
.PP
Mail from other servers (on port 25, with no user signed in, and not from
127.0.0.1) is checked before it is accepted:
.IP \(bu 2
SPF (RFC 7208) for the sender's domain, or for the HELO name for a bounce;
.IP \(bu 2
every DKIM signature, rsa\-sha256 and ed25519\-sha256, up to ten, those of the
From domain's organization first.
A signature whose l= leaves part of the body out counts as neutral.
An RSA key under 1024 bits is refused;
.IP \(bu 2
the ARC chain;
.IP \(bu 2
DMARC (RFC 7489) for the domain of the From header, with labels past ASCII as
A\-labels.
SPF or DKIM must be aligned as the domain's record says, and pct= samples the
messages the policy applies to.
.PP
A message that fails DMARC under
.B p=reject
is refused with 550 and not kept.
The same happens to a message whose author cannot be determined: one with two
From headers, a From header that cannot be read, or authors from two domains.
A message that fails under
.B p=quarantine
goes to the recipient's Junk folder.
A passing ARC chain whose last seal is from a domain in
.B \-\-arc\-trust
lifts the policy.
.PP
The result of each check is written into the
.B Authentication\-Results
line of the stored message.
The runtime removes an Authentication\-Results line that a sender added under
this host's name, and it also removes the sender's Return-Path.
.PP
The lookups run on sixteen threads of their own, never on the event loop.
All lookups for one message must finish within twenty seconds; after that they
count as a temporary error.
Each answer is kept for one minute.
When 512 messages are already waiting to be checked, the next one is answered
451.
.PP
For DMARC, the organizational domain of a name is its registrable domain under
the Public Suffix List of the publicsuffix package
.RB ( /usr/share/publicsuffix ).
For example, mail.example.co.uk belongs to example.co.uk.
The list's rules past ASCII are compared as A\-labels.
Without the package, each name is its own organizational domain, so relaxed
alignment behaves as strict; the log says so once.
.PP
.B The spool.
A message is synced to disk before the client is answered 250.
It is kept under
.IB state /mail/ name /spool
until every recipient is done.
A message of up to one mebibyte is stored as a record in a segment.
A segment is a 32 MiB file that is fully written out before it is used.
So one fdatasync commits every message that ended while the previous
fdatasync ran.
A larger message is stored in a file of its own.
.PP
Each record carries its segment's generation and a CRC.
So a record that a crash cut short is dropped at the next start, and the log
says so.
The outcome for each recipient is kept in a log beside the messages.
So a restart retries what was waiting and never brings back what was dropped.
If only a few deferred messages keep a segment in use, they are moved out after
a minute and the segment is used again.
A message under one mebibyte is also kept in memory, so its delivery reads
nothing back from disk.
.PP
.B Sending it on.
Recipients at one domain go in one transaction.
A connection to a domain carries one message after another while more are
waiting for that domain.
The runtime opens at most four connections to one domain, and 128 in all.
Where the other side offers PIPELINING, MAIL, RCPT and DATA go in one write.
.PP
The exchangers are the domain's MX records, looked up by the resolver on the
event loop, or the domain itself if it has no MX records.
Delivery fails at once to a domain that does not exist or that says it accepts
no mail (RFC 7505).
With
.BR \-\-relay ,
all mail for other domains goes through that smarthost.
.PP
The runtime uses TLS wherever the other side offers it.
It checks an exchanger's certificate where the exchanger's domain asks for
that:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
DANE (RFC 7672): where DNSSEC signed a domain's MX records, the runtime looks
up the TLSA records of each exchanger.
If there are any, the mail goes over TLS only, and the certificate must be one
that the records name (DANE\-TA or DANE\-EE).
A record is trusted only if the resolver set the AD bit and every nameserver
that
.B /etc/resolv.conf
names is on this machine, such as a validating resolver like unbound.
Otherwise no record is trusted and nothing is checked.
.IP \(bu 2
MTA\-STS (RFC 8461): where a domain has an
.B _mta\-sts
TXT record, the runtime fetches its policy from
.BI https://mta\-sts. domain /.well\-known/mta\-sts.txt
on a separate thread.
It fetches the policy again when the record's id changes, and keeps it under
the runtime's state for the next start.
Under
.BR "mode: enforce" ,
only the exchangers the policy names are tried, over TLS.
Each must present a certificate for its name from the system's CAs.
Under
.BR testing ,
what would have failed is logged and counted, and the mail is sent as usual.
.RE
.IP
Mail that cannot be sent under these rules waits and is tried again (4.7.5).
For other domains the certificate is not checked, since no mail server checks
one there.
A smarthost's certificate is always checked, and the smarthost password is
never sent in the clear.
.IP
A recipient refused for now is tried again after 1m, 5m, 15m, 30m, 1h, 2h and
4h, and then every 8h, until
.B \-\-queue\-lifetime
runs out.
A recipient refused for good, or out of time, is bounced to the sender as a
delivery status report from the null sender.
A server that offers CHUNKING receives the message in BDAT chunks, without dot
stuffing.
A server whose LIMITS say how many recipients a transaction takes receives that
many recipients at a time.
.IP
After a server answers 421, the runtime opens no new connection to its domain
for thirty seconds.
Each further 421 doubles that pause, up to fifteen minutes, until the server
accepts a message.
.IP
REQUIRETLS mail is sent on only to a server that offers REQUIRETLS, over TLS
whose certificate DANE or an enforced MTA\-STS policy vouched for.
A smarthost's certificate always counts as vouched for.
Otherwise the message is returned with 550 5.7.10, and the notice carries no
more than its header.
A Sieve redirect keeps the requirement.
.IP
Mail from other servers that the runtime sends on goes out from an SRS address
of its first domain (SRS0=hash=time=domain=local@).
So SPF at the next hop judges this host, and a bounce still finds its way
back.
.IP
The delivery reports a sender asked for (RFC 3461) are kept with each
recipient.
NOTIFY, ORCPT, RET and ENVID are passed on to a server that offers DSN, and
that server reports from there.
For any other server, this runtime reports itself.
It reports a SUCCESS that was asked for as delivered or relayed, and a DELAY
once a message has waited four hours.
It reports no failure where NOTIFY said NEVER.
With RET=FULL the report carries the whole message, up to 4 MiB; otherwise it
carries the header.
.IP
Mail from one of its domains is signed with DKIM twice, both relaxed:
rsa\-sha256 under the selector, and ed25519\-sha256 (RFC 8463) under
.IB selector \-ed .
The keys are made at the first start and kept in
.IB state /mail/ name .dkim. selector .key
for every shard.
To rotate a key, first name the new selector with
.BR \-\-dkim\-next ,
which makes its key pair and publishes it beside the old one.
Once DNS has the new key, name the selector with
.BR \-\-dkim\-selector .
.IP
Mail from other servers that the runtime sends on, as an alias does, is sealed
with ARC (RFC 8617).
The seal holds what the runtime's checks found, signed and sealed for its
first domain.
So the next hop can know what the message was when it arrived here.
.PP
.B Reports.
With
.BR \-\-reports ,
the runtime sends the reports other domains ask for:
.IP \(bu 2
DMARC aggregate reports (RFC 7489), to the rua of each domain whose mail it
checked;
.IP \(bu 2
TLS reports (RFC 8460), to the rua in the
.B _smtp._tls
record of each domain it sent mail to under DANE or MTA\-STS.
.PP
It sends them once a day, after midnight UTC, gzip'd.
Each shard reports what it saw.
.B \-\-dmarc\-rua
and
.B \-\-tls\-rpt
publish where other domains should send their reports about this runtime's
domains.
.PP
.B Its records.
With
.BR \-\-dns ,
the runtime publishes these records for each of its domains, through the dns
runtime:
.RS
.PP
.nf
example.com.                 MX   10 mail.example.com.
example.com.                 TXT  "v=spf1 mx \-all"
skl._domainkey.example.com.  TXT  "v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p=..."
skl\-ed._domainkey.example.com. TXT "v=DKIM1; k=ed25519; p=..."
_dmarc.example.com.          TXT  "v=DMARC1; p=quarantine; adkim=r; aspf=r"
_imaps._tcp.example.com.     SRV  0 1 993 mail.example.com.
_25._tcp.mail.example.com.   TLSA 3 1 1 ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
It also publishes:
.IP \(bu 2
an SRV record (RFC 6186, RFC 8314) for each of
.BR \-\-submission ,
.BR \-\-submissions ,
.BR \-\-imap ,
.BR \-\-imaps ,
.BR \-\-pop3 ,
.BR \-\-pop3s ,
.B \-\-jmap
and
.B \-\-sieve
that it opens, so a mail client finds them from the address alone;
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-tls ,
the TLSA record of its certificate's key, for senders that check DANE where the
zone is signed;
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-mta\-sts ,
an
.B _mta\-sts
record whose id follows the policy;
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-mta\-sts\-port ,
.BI mta\-sts. domain
as a CNAME of its host, which serves the policy there over TLS;
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-tls\-rpt ,
an
.B _smtp._tls
record;
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-dmarc\-rua ,
a rua in its DMARC record;
.IP \(bu 2
its host,
.BI mail. domain
unless
.B \-\-hostname
names another, with the addresses that the dns runtime's
.B \-\-publish
gives its names;
.IP \(bu 2
an SPF record for the host itself,
.BR "v=spf1 a \-all" ,
for a receiver that checks the name the runtime greets with (RFC 7208 2.3).
.PP
A record that the zone file has at the same name wins.
With
.BR \-\-relay ,
the SPF record ends in ~all, since mail leaves from the smarthost's addresses.
Without a dns runtime,
.B socketley mail
.I m
.B dns
prints the lines for a zone kept elsewhere.
.PP
.B No\-reply.
With
.BR \-\-no\-reply ,
a message that
.B socketley send
or a script writes without a sender goes out from
.BI no\-reply@ domain\fR.
Mail to that address is refused, but a bounce is taken and read.
Such a message goes out with one recipient per transaction, from
.BI no\-reply+ tag @ domain\fR.
The tag holds the day and a keyed hash of the recipient's address and that
day, which only this runtime can make.
A bounce that comes back within seven days under an address's tag suppresses
that address.
No shard mails a suppressed address again until
.B socketley mail
.I m
.B forgive
.I address
releases it, or until ninety days pass.
.PP
A bounce with no tag, with an older tag, or with another address's tag is read
but has no effect.
So neither a stranger nor another recipient can stop mail to an address.
The runtime trusts its own bounce of such a message, so it learns that an
address is dead from the first attempt.
A recipient whose Status is 4.x.x, or 5.2.2 (mailbox full), is not a dead
address and is not suppressed.
.IP
A provider may send a feedback report (ARF, RFC 5965) saying that one of its
users called a message spam.
Microsoft's JMRP and the other feedback loops send these reports to whichever
address of the runtime's domains they were given.
If SPF or DKIM shows that the report's sender is who it claims to be, the
report unsubscribes that user from the runtime's advertisements, as the user's
own click would.
The report is still delivered.
.B socketley mail trace
names it.
.PP
.B Mailboxes.
With
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage ,
each message of a user is an object in the storage runtime's bucket, made when
first needed:
.IB user /INBOX/ id .eml\fR.
The bucket is mail unless
.B \-\-storage
names another.
The object holds the message under a Return\-Path, a Delivered\-To and the
Authentication\-Results line.
A message under one mebibyte is written whole; a larger one is written in
pieces of one mebibyte.
.PP
Each message stored in a mailbox is announced to the targets of
.B \-\-notify\-targets
as
.BR {"event":"mail.received","mailbox":..,"folder":..,"key":..,"from":..,"subject":..,"size":..} ,
under
.BI mail/ user
for a broker.
A bucket that encrypts or compresses its objects does the same to mail.
Either way, a message is read back one range at a time.
.PP
.B IMAP.
The runtime serves the mailboxes of
.B \-\-storage
over IMAP4rev1, and over IMAP4rev2 (RFC 9051) for a client that enables it.
It supports LITERAL+, SEARCHRES, SASL\-IR, IDLE, NAMESPACE, UIDPLUS, MOVE,
UNSELECT, CHILDREN, SPECIAL\-USE, ID, ENABLE, CONDSTORE and QRESYNC, and
ESEARCH.
It also supports LIST\-EXTENDED and LIST\-STATUS, STATUS=SIZE, QUOTA, SORT
(with DISPLAYFROM and DISPLAYTO), THREAD (ORDEREDSUBJECT, REFERENCES), WITHIN
(OLDER and YOUNGER), SAVEDATE, PREVIEW and COMPRESS=DEFLATE.
Under COMPRESS=DEFLATE, what the server says in one loop turn is compressed as
one flushed block.
A password is accepted only over STARTTLS or
.B \-\-imaps
(LOGINDISABLED before that), except from 127.0.0.1.
.PP
Every user has the folders INBOX, Sent, Drafts, Junk and Trash.
CREATE makes more, including the folders above a name such as Work/2026.
A client can set keywords of its own beside the system flags.
.PP
The UIDs, flags and keywords of each folder are kept per user in
.IB state /mail/ name .imap/ user ,
as a snapshot with a journal beside it.
Every shard serves IMAP.
A shard records a change by appending a line to the journal under the user's
lock, once it has read the journal to its end.
So each UID is given only once, whichever shard gives it.
Before each command, a shard reads the journal lines it has not yet read.
The journal is folded into the snapshot once it passes a quarter of a
mebibyte.
.PP
A delivery, an APPEND or a COPY writes its journal line as the message is
stored.
So IDLE reports new mail within a second, and the quota counts the message at
once.
The bucket remains the authority.
It is listed when a folder is selected, and every minute during NOOP and IDLE.
A message that has gone from the bucket is expunged.
A message that no journal line names gets the next UID once it is a minute
old.
Every change to a message takes the folder's next mod\-sequence.
So a client that reconnects can ask what changed and what vanished since it
last looked.
.PP
SEARCH, SORT and THREAD, like JMAP's text, body and header filters, read a
message's header and its text parts, but not its attachments.
They read the header both as written and with its encoded words decoded.
They read the text parts both as written and with their transfer encoding and
charset undone.
.PP
Searches use a filter of each message's trigrams.
The filter is made when a delivery or an APPEND stores the message.
For a delivered message over one mebibyte, it is made the first time a search
reads the message instead.
The filter is as large as the number of different trigrams the message holds.
It is kept in
.IB state /mail/ name .imap/ user .fts
for every shard.
The filter rules out the messages that cannot hold the string asked for, so a
search reads only the messages that may.
.PP
A user's
.B mail_quota
field in the idp runtime
.RB ( "socketley idp\-user" " ... " "\-\-set mail_quota=500M" )
limits what their mailboxes hold.
IMAP reports it as one quota root over every folder.
An APPEND or a COPY past the quota is refused with OVERQUOTA.
A delivery past it is refused with 552 5.2.2, as Dovecot refuses one.
A user with no
.B mail_quota
gets
.BR \-\-default\-quota .
.PP
When a user's mailboxes pass
.B \-\-quota\-warn
percent of their quota (default 90), the user is mailed a warning once.
The user is warned again only after the mailboxes fell below that level.
.PP
All of a runtime's mailboxes together, across all its domains, are limited by
the quota of their bucket (PutBucketQuota on the storage runtime).
A delivery past that quota waits, with 452 4.2.2, until room is made.
For a separate limit per domain, run one mail runtime per domain, each on a
bucket of its own.
.PP
IMAP also offers:
.IP \(bu 2
MULTIAPPEND, which stores every message it is given, or none;
.IP \(bu 2
UTF8=ACCEPT, under which folder names travel as UTF\-8 and a message may be a
literal8;
.IP \(bu 2
CREATE\-SPECIAL\-USE, which accepts a special use only for the folder whose
name has it;
.IP \(bu 2
BINARY, which returns a part with its transfer encoding undone;
.IP \(bu 2
OBJECTID, whose MAILBOXID a RENAME keeps;
.IP \(bu 2
METADATA (RFC 5464): entries of the server and of each folder, kept in the
index, at most 1000 per user of 64 KiB each;
.IP \(bu 2
NOTIFY (RFC 5465), which tells a session within a second of changes.
It reports new mail, expunges and flags in the folders it watches as STATUS,
folders made and removed as LIST, and the selected folder between commands;
.IP \(bu 2
ACL (RFC 4314), by which a user gives others rights on a folder.
The others see the folder under
.BI "Other Users/" owner /
with what those rights allow (l r s w i k x t e a);
.IP \(bu 2
APPENDLIMIT.
.PP
RENAME takes the folders under a name along with it.
RENAME INBOX moves its messages and leaves INBOX empty.
An IMAP connection that is idle for thirty minutes is closed.
.PP
.B JMAP.
.B /.well\-known/jmap
is the JMAP session.
The API at
.B /jmap/api
accepts these methods, with back\-references among them:
.IP \(bu 2
Core/echo;
.IP \(bu 2
Mailbox/get, query, set and changes;
.IP \(bu 2
Email/get, query, set, import and changes;
.IP \(bu 2
Thread/get, with every Email of a conversation in any mailbox;
.IP \(bu 2
Quota/get, query and changes (RFC 9425), with the octets of the user's quota;
.IP \(bu 2
Identity/get and set;
.IP \(bu 2
EmailSubmission/set, with its onSuccessUpdateEmail and onSuccessDestroyEmail.
.PP
.B /jmap/upload
and
.B /jmap/download
move blobs, and
.B /jmap/eventsource
reports state changes.
A user signs in with HTTP Basic and their idp password, over TLS or from
127.0.0.1, or with a bearer token the idp gave a client that
.B \-\-idp\-audience
names.
.PP
A user's JMAP account holds the same mailboxes IMAP reads.
A Mailbox id is the folder's MAILBOXID, and an Email id is the message's
EMAILID, which a move keeps.
A Thread is the conversation named by the Message-ID that a message's
References begin with, and collapseThreads folds a query by it.
A keyword is an IMAP flag.
So a client of either protocol sees at once what a client of the other did.
.PP
A state is a digest of the index, and is the same on every shard.
A */changes request since any other state is answered cannotCalculateChanges,
after which the client reads again.
An upload is kept for one day.
An EmailSubmission can send only as the user's own address.
Email/parse reads a blob, an upload or an attached message, as an Email.
.PP
VacationResponse is kept in the user's METADATA.
It answers the mail that the user's mailbox accepts, at most once a week per
sender.
It never answers a list, a bounce, spam or an automatic reply (RFC 3834).
It answers whether or not a Sieve script runs.
.PP
A user may have at most 50 PushSubscriptions, each lasting a week until it is
renewed.
A subscription is verified by a code pushed to its URL.
After that, it is sent a StateChange whenever the types it asked for change.
Pushes go over HTTPS to a public address, encrypted where the subscription gave
keys (RFC 8291).
.PP
.B Calendars and contacts.
The same ports serve CalDAV (RFC 4791) and CardDAV (RFC 6352) for every user.
.B /.well\-known/caldav
and
.B /.well\-known/carddav
lead to
.BR /dav/ .
There the user's principal names their calendars under
.IB /dav/ user /calendars/
and their address books under
.IB /dav/ user /contacts/\fR.
The collections
.B personal
and
.B default
are there for every user, and more can be made with MKCALENDAR or MKCOL.
.PP
A client puts, reads and deletes each event, task or card as a whole, under
If-Match and If-None-Match.
PROPPATCH stores a collection's name, colour and description.
The server answers the multiget and query REPORTs, including a query's
component filter, and sync-collection (RFC 6578).
.PP
The entries are objects in the
.B \-\-storage
bucket under
.IB .dav/ user /\fR,
apart from the mail folders.
Each change in a collection is a line in a journal under
.IB state /mail/ name .dav/\fR,
and the journal's length is the collection's sync token.
Through the entrance, a user signs in as for JMAP.
Thunderbird's autoconfig, the Apple profile, and the
.B _caldavs._tcp
and
.B _carddavs._tcp
records in the zone point a client there.
.PP
.B Webmail.
With
.B \-\-web
and
.BR \-\-domain ,
the entrance serves a mail client in the browser under that name.
It looks like the storage interface and offers:
.IP \(bu 2
the mailboxes with their unread counts, and a starred view;
.IP \(bu 2
the conversations a page at a time, each shown as its messages from every
mailbox, with quoted text folded away;
.IP \(bu 2
a search over all of them;
.IP \(bu 2
reading, writing, replying and forwarding with attachments, with drafts saved
while they are written;
.IP \(bu 2
moving, archiving, flags and deletion, and selecting or emptying a whole
folder;
.IP \(bu 2
a message's headers, and a print view;
.IP \(bu 2
sending that can be undone within seconds or put off until a set time, as
plain text or formatted;
.IP \(bu 2
a sender chosen from the user's addresses, with the address a message was sent
to used for its reply;
.IP \(bu 2
a name, a signature, and an out\-of\-office answer between two dates;
.IP \(bu 2
filters for arriving mail, kept as a Sieve script;
.IP \(bu 2
new mail shown as it arrives, with notices in the background where the browser
allows it.
.PP
The webmail speaks German where the browser asks for it, and English
otherwise.
It signs in through the idp runtime.
The idp's session cookie under its
.B \-\-cookie\-domain
signs the user in to every runtime under that domain at once.
A page that is not signed in is sent to the idp's
.BR /login .
.PP
A change that only the cookie authorizes must carry the page's
.B X\-Socketley\-UI
header, which another site cannot make a browser send.
No other origin can read the answer to a request that the cookie signs.
A message's HTML is cleaned of anything that runs, submits or reaches out.
It is shown in a frame sandboxed without scripts.
Its remote images wait until the reader asks for them, either for that message
or for its sender from then on.
The page's one script may run only by its hash.
.PP
On the same port, a JMAP client signs in as on
.BR \-\-jmap .
It may use a password only where the entrance forwarded HTTPS.
A client that fails ten times is locked out by the address that the entrance
forwards for it.
.RS
.PP
.nf
socketley create mail m \-p 25 \-\-dns ns \-\-idp auth \-\-storage files/mail \e
    \-\-imaps 993 \-\-submissions 465 \-\-tls \-\-cert mail.pem \-\-key mail.key \e
    \-\-web 9480 \-\-domain mail \-s
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B From the other runtimes.
Other parts of socketley send mail through this runtime:
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley send
and a script's
.B socketley.mail.send
write and queue a message;
.IP \(bu 2
a storage runtime's
.BI mail:// runtime / address
notification target mails the storage's events;
.IP \(bu 2
.B daemon.alert_mail
mails the daemon's alerts.
.RS
.PP
.nf
local id, err = socketley.mail.send("m", { to = "ops@example.com",
    subject = "order " .. order.id, text = "paid",
    attachments = { { name = "invoice.pdf", type = "application/pdf", data = pdf } } })
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Sieve.
Users file their own mail with Sieve (RFC 5228).
The supported extensions are fileinto, reject, ereject, envelope,
encoded\-character, imap4flags, copy, vacation, vacation\-seconds, subaddress,
body, relational, comparator\-i;ascii\-numeric, variables, date, index,
mailbox, regex, editheader, duplicate, include, environment, spamtest,
spamtestplus, virustest, enotify (mailto) and ihave.
.PP
Users upload their scripts over ManageSieve (RFC 5804) on
.BR \-\-sieve ,
with STARTTLS and SASL PLAIN using the password IMAP takes.
They can also use
.BR "socketley mail" " ... " sieve .
Each script is compiled as it is uploaded.
A user may have at most 32 scripts of 64 KiB each, and 1 MiB in all.
The scripts are kept under the runtime's state for every shard.
A script is made active only if it compiles together with what it includes.
.PP
The active script runs on a thread of its own for each message that reaches
the user's mailbox, and reads the whole message.
Its actions:
.IP \(bu 2
keep and fileinto file the message, with the imap4flags flags that IMAP shows.
fileinto into a folder that does not exist works only with :create; otherwise
the message goes into INBOX.
.IP \(bu 2
discard keeps nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
reject bounces the message to its sender if that sender signed in, is this
machine, or passed SPF; otherwise reject discards it.
.IP \(bu 2
redirect sends the message on from the user's own address, and never again if
it comes back.
.IP \(bu 2
vacation answers a sender once in its days or seconds, an hour at least, from
the null sender, and never answers a bounce or spam.
.IP \(bu 2
notify mails a mailto URI, marked auto\-notified, from the null sender, at most
three times per message.
.PP
What editheader adds or deletes appears in the message as it is kept and sent
on, except for the Received and Auto\-Submitted headers.
duplicate keeps a message's ID for twelve hours unless :seconds says
otherwise, and for a week at most.
duplicate and vacation remember at most 10,000 entries per user.
include :global reads the scripts uploaded for the user
.B .global
.RB ( "socketley mail" " m " "sieve .global put" " name file ).
include :personal reads the user's own.
environment provides host, domain, remote\-ip, location (MDA), phase (during),
name and version.
spamtest and virustest are 0 until a scanner says more.
If a script fails, the message is kept in INBOX.
.IP
imapsieve (RFC 6785): a folder's
.B /shared/imapsieve/script
entry, set by SETMETADATA, can name one of the user's scripts.
That script then runs for each message a client APPENDs, COPYs or MOVEs into
the folder, and for each message there whose flags a STORE changes.
environment's imap.cause, imap.mailbox, imap.user, imap.email and
imap.changedflags say what was done, and imap4flags starts from the message's
flags.
keep leaves the message with the flags the script left.
fileinto files a copy, and redirect sends a copy on.
If nothing keeps the message, it leaves the folder once its copies are in.
reject is an error, and vacation does nothing.
A message that the script's own fileinto puts in another folder runs no script
there.
.PP
.B Lua.
A script of the runtime decides on each recipient and is told about each
message:
.RS
.PP
.nf
function on_rcpt(r, default)          -- r.to, r.from, r.peer, r.user
    if r.to == "orders@example.com" then return true end   -- the script's alone
    if r.from:match("@spam%.test$") then return false end  -- refused 550
end                                   -- nil keeps the default
function on_message(m)                -- m.id, m.from, m.to, m.size, m.subject, m.message, m.peer, m.user
    socketley.mqtt.publish("bus", "mail/in", m.subject or "", { qos = 1 })
end
.fi
.RE
.IP
A recipient that
.B on_rcpt
accepts, and that no mailbox or alias has, belongs to the script.
Its message reaches
.B on_message
and nowhere else.
.B m.message
holds the whole message if it is under one mebibyte.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mail\-domain " domain\fR[,\fIdomain\fR...]
The domains the runtime accepts mail for.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dns " runtime
The dns runtime whose zones are the runtime's domains.
The runtime gives those domains their records through it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-hostname " name
The name the runtime greets with, which its MX records also name.
Default: mail.\fIfirst domain\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reply
Sends mail that has no sender from no\-reply@.
That address accepts no mail but bounces, and the runtime learns from those
bounces.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " runtime
The idp runtime whose users have mailboxes here and sign in to send and read
mail.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " clients
The comma\-separated ids of the idp clients whose access tokens the runtime
accepts.
A token the provider gave any other application is refused, since it belongs
to that application and is no key to this runtime.
Without this flag no access token is accepted, while cookies and passwords sign
in as before.
.TP
.BI "\-\-storage " runtime\fR[/\fIbucket\fR]
The storage runtime, and optionally its bucket, that holds the mailboxes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-users " file
A file of accounts that sign in to send mail.
It holds a name and a scrypt hash, one account per line, as
.B socketley mqtt passwd
writes them.
The runtime reads the file again when it changes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-aliases " file
A file of addresses the runtime accepts besides the users' own.
Each line is
.IR "address target" [, target ...].
A target is a mailbox (a name) or an address that the mail is sent on to.
.BI *@ domain
matches any local part of that domain that has no line of its own.
The runtime reads the file again when it changes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-relay " smtp\fR[\fBs\fR]\fB://\fR[\fIuser\fB:\fIpassword\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fIport\fR]
A smarthost that all mail for other domains goes through.
smtps:// speaks TLS from the first byte (465); smtp:// uses STARTTLS (587).
.TP
.BI "\-\-submission " port
A port where users send mail, with STARTTLS and a password.
Requires
.BR \-\-tls ,
and
.B \-\-users
or
.BR \-\-idp .
.TP
.BI "\-\-submissions " port
A port where users send mail over TLS from the first byte.
Requires
.BR \-\-tls .
.TP
.BI "\-\-imap " port
.TQ
.BI "\-\-imaps " port
A port where mail clients read the mailboxes, with STARTTLS
.RB ( \-\-imap )
or over TLS from the first byte
.RB ( \-\-imaps ).
Both require
.BR \-\-storage ,
and
.B \-\-imaps
also requires
.BR \-\-tls .
.TP
.BI "\-\-notify\-targets " file
A file of the targets that are told about each message stored in a mailbox.
It has the format of a storage runtime's targets file.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-size " size
The largest message the runtime accepts, from 1K to 1G.
Default: 25M.
.TP
.BI "\-\-max\-recipients " n
The most recipients one message may have.
Default: 100.
A RCPT past the limit is answered 452.
.TP
.BI "\-\-queue\-lifetime " duration
How long the runtime tries to deliver a message before it bounces it.
Takes 1m to 30d; default 5d.
.TP
.BI "\-\-autoexpunge " duration
How long a message stays in a user's Junk or Trash before it is expunged.
The time counts from when the message was put there.
Takes 1h to 3650d; by default messages are never expunged.
The first shard sweeps every user it has met, hourly.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dkim\-selector " name
The selector the DKIM key is published and signed under.
Default: skl.
The Ed25519 key's selector is
.IR name \-ed .
.TP
.BI "\-\-dkim\-next " name
A selector whose keys are made and published but not yet used for signing.
Use it to rotate a key.
.TP
.BI "\-\-arc\-trust " domain\fR[,\fIdomain\fR...]
Forwarders, such as a mailing list's domain, whose ARC seal lets through mail
that fails DMARC because they forwarded it.
The seal counts only on an ARC chain that passes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-sieve " port
A ManageSieve (RFC 5804) port, where users manage their Sieve scripts.
Requires
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-pop3 " port
A POP3 port, with STLS on it.
Requires
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-pop3s " port
A POP3 port that speaks TLS from the first byte.
Requires
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-jmap " port
A JMAP port for the mailboxes, served over HTTPS with
.BR \-\-tls .
Requires
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-web " port
A port that serves the webmail, and JMAP beside it, in plain HTTP on 127.0.0.1.
The entrance in front of it handles TLS (see
.BR Webmail ).
Requires
.B \-\-idp
and
.BR \-\-storage .
.TP
.BI "\-\-domain " name\fR[,\fIname\fR...]
The names under which the entrance of a dns runtime with
.B \-\-proxy
reaches the webmail.
A name without a dot, such as
.B webmail
or
.BR mail ,
is placed under each of the dns runtime's zones.
Requires
.BR \-\-web .
.IP
Under each of its zones, the webmail also answers at
.B autoconfig
and
.BR autodiscover ,
where mail clients ask how to reach a mailbox.
Thunderbird asks at
.B /mail/config\-v1.1.xml
(and
.BR /.well\-known/autoconfig/ ).
Outlook asks at
.BR /autodiscover/autodiscover.xml ,
or through the JSON lookup that points at it, with
.B _autodiscover._tcp
in the zone.
Each answer names the runtime's host, the ports it opens with TLS, and the
address as the login.
An address in a domain the runtime does not keep is answered 404.
A user signed in to the webmail finds the same settings under Settings.
There is also a profile that sets up an iPhone, an iPad or a Mac at once
.RB ( /~mail/mobileconfig ).
.TP
.BI "\-\-lmtp " port
An LMTP port for this machine's own programs, which delivers into the mailboxes
only.
A connection from elsewhere is closed.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mta\-sts " enforce\fR|\fBtesting
Publishes an MTA\-STS policy (RFC 8461) in that mode, naming the runtime's host.
.TP
.BI "\-\-mta\-sts\-port " port
Serves the MTA\-STS policy over HTTPS on this port, with the certificate of
.BR \-\-tls .
The daemon's ACME also asks for
.BI mta\-sts. domain
in that certificate.
Senders fetch the policy on port 443.
.TP
.BI "\-\-tls\-rpt " address
Publishes the address where TLS reports (RFC 8460) about the runtime's domains
should go.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dmarc\-rua " address
Publishes the address where DMARC aggregate reports about the runtime's domains
should go.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dmarc\-policy " none\fR|\fBquarantine\fR|\fBreject
What the DMARC record asks receivers to do with mail that claims the runtime's
domains and fails SPF and DKIM.
Default: quarantine.
Switch to reject once the reports show that only your own mail passes.
.TP
.BI "\-\-bimi " https://logo.svg\fR[,\fIhttps://certificate.pem\fR]
Publishes a BIMI record with its SVG logo, and optionally a mark certificate.
Providers that show a logo beside a message, Gmail among them, ask for the
certificate.
The logo is shown for a domain whose DMARC policy quarantines or rejects.
.TP
.B \-\-reports
Sends the DMARC and TLS reports that other domains ask for.
.TP
.BI "\-\-greylist " duration
Greylists strangers (RFC 6647) for this long, such as 5m.
0 disables it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-refuse\-attachments " ext\fR[,\fIext\fR...]|\fBdefault\fR|\fBnone
Refuses mail from other servers that has an attachment with one of these
extensions.
The answer is 554 and names the attachment.
By default, the list covers programs, scripts, shortcuts, disk images and
Office documents with macros (exe, scr, js, vbs, ps1, lnk, iso, docm, xlsm and
the rest of Gmail's list).
.TP
.BI "\-\-clamav " host:port\fR|\fIsocket
The clamd that every message from other servers is streamed to (INSTREAM)
before the message is accepted.
A message in which clamd names a virus is refused with that virus's name.
A message clamd does not answer for is accepted and logged, as when Rspamd is
silent.
.TP
.BI "\-\-rspamd " url
The Rspamd that scans mail from other servers, such as http://127.0.0.1:11333.
.IP
Without it, or when it did not answer, the runtime's own filter scores the
mail.
That filter learns from the users.
A message moved into Junk is spam.
A message moved out of Junk, or filed from INBOX into a folder of the user's
own, is not spam.
This holds over IMAP, JMAP and the webmail's Report junk and Not junk alike.
.IP
The filter's words are those of the subject, the text and the HTML's text, the
From domain and the links' hosts.
It weighs them as SpamBayes does (Robinson's chi\-square of Fisher).
A message is learned once, and learned again if it is later marked the other
way.
Once the filter has learned 20 messages of each kind, every message gets its
score as x\-spam\-bayes.
A message that scores 0.9 or more goes into Junk; nothing is refused for its
score.
.IP
.B "socketley mail learn"
.IB user / folder
.B \-\-as spam\fR|\fBham
teaches the filter a whole folder at once.
.B "socketley stats"
shows how much it learned
.RB ( mail_bayes_spam ", " mail_bayes_ham )
and how much it put in Junk
.RB ( mail_bayes_junked ).
.TP
.BI "\-\-recipient\-delimiter " chars\fR|\fBnone
The characters that separate a user from a tag in an address.
Default: +.
.TP
.BI "\-\-default\-quota " size
The quota of a user whose idp account names none.
0 means no limit.
.TP
.BI "\-\-quota\-warn " percent
How full, in percent, a user's mailboxes get before the user is warned.
Default: 90.
0 disables the warning.
.TP
.BI "\-\-dnsbl " zone\fR[,\fIzone\fR...]
DNS blocklists (RFC 5782) to check, such as zen.spamhaus.org.
The runtime looks up a stranger's address in each list as it connects.
MAIL from an address that a list names is refused with 554.
.PP
.B stats
reports:
.IP \(bu 2
the messages received, delivered, deferred, failed and bounced, and the other
delivery reports;
.IP \(bu 2
what is queued, and its bytes;
.IP \(bu 2
the spool's commits, segments, moves and failures;
.IP \(bu 2
the recipients and relays refused, and the messages that were too big;
.IP \(bu 2
what was checked, and what DMARC refused or quarantined;
.IP \(bu 2
the bounces taken and the addresses suppressed;
.IP \(bu 2
what was stored in mailboxes;
.IP \(bu 2
the sign\-ins and their failures, and the STARTTLS and TLS failures;
.IP \(bu 2
the outgoing connections, with their TLS and transactions;
.IP \(bu 2
the certificates DANE and MTA\-STS checked, and the connections refused for
want of TLS;
.IP \(bu 2
the policies fetched, and what testing policies would have failed;
.IP \(bu 2
what Sieve rejected, sent on and answered on vacation;
.IP \(bu 2
the MX lookups;
.IP \(bu 2
what the spam filter refused and filed in Junk;
.IP \(bu 2
what was greylisted;
.IP \(bu 2
the reports sent;
.IP \(bu 2
the IMAP connections and reads.
.SH TUNNEL FLAGS
A
.B tunnel
runtime makes a machine that has no reachable address of its own reachable.
Examples are a machine at home behind a router, one behind carrier-grade NAT,
or one on a network that lets only port 443 out.
.PP
The machine runs an
.BR agent .
The agent dials out to an
.BR edge ,
which is a tunnel runtime on a machine the internet reaches, and holds that one
connection open.
The edge serves the agent's services under its own names.
It carries each visitor through the connection the agent made.
Nothing is opened on the router.
.PP
.nf
# on the machine with the address
socketley create tunnel edge \-\-mode edge \-p 9450 \-\-domain edge \-\-idp auth \-s
# on the machine at home, signed in as an admin of auth
socketley tunnel join https://edge.example.com \-\-http home=127.0.0.1:8123 \-\-tcp mc:25565=127.0.0.1:25565
.fi
.PP
An HTTP service gets a name beside the edge's name; here it is
.BR https://home.example.com .
Once the agent has joined, the dns runtime answers that name.
The edge serves the service on a socket that the entrance routes the name to,
with the entrance's certificate.
.PP
A name that a runtime already has stays that runtime's.
No other runtime is given a name that a service has.
If two edges each have a service with the same name, the edge that comes first
by name keeps it.
An edge whose name is a whole domain has no name beside it, so its services
have no names either.
A TCP service is a port on the edge; here it is 25565.
.PP
The edge adds nothing to a stream it carries.
The agent receives exactly what the visitor sent, and the service answers as if
the visitor were on the same machine.
There are two exceptions, both for HTTP requests:
.IP \(bu 2
a request arrives without the idp's session and known-device cookies, which
sign in wherever the idp is trusted;
.IP \(bu 2
a request arrives without the
.B Authorization
header that a service's
.I auth
took.
.PP
A TCP visitor reaches a service at 127.0.0.1 or localhost from 127.0.0.2.
So a service that trusts this machine's own programs at 127.0.0.1, as mail lets
them relay, treats the visitor as a stranger.
A service named at [::1] is reached from ::1.
.PP
.B Who may visit.
An HTTP service invited as
.IB name = auth
lets through only the requests that
.I auth
admits.
.I auth
takes the same values as a proxy's
.BR \-\-auth :
.IP \(bu 2
.BI idp: runtime
sends a browser to sign in and back;
.IP \(bu 2
.BI bearer: file
and
.BI basic: file
ask for a token or a key;
.IP \(bu 2
several values joined by
.B +
let in a visitor that any one of them admits.
For example,
.B idp:auth+bearer:/etc/socketley/hub.token
admits a person who is signed in and a program with the token alike.
.PP
Every request is checked, not only the first on a connection, since the
entrance hands one connection to the edge from visitor to visitor.
No part of a refused request reaches the agent.
A body is carried as a body and never read as the next request.
A connection whose upgrade the service refused ends with that answer.
Guarding a TCP service is up to the agent.
.PP
Each connection to the edge is a multiplexed link: one TLS connection that
carries many streams.
Each stream has its own credit, so one slow reader holds back only its own
stream.
The link is opened by an HTTP upgrade at
.BR /.tunnel ,
which passes the entrance like any other request.
An agent pings every 15 seconds.
A link that is silent for 45 seconds is given up, and the agent dials again.
The pause before it dials again doubles each time, up to half a minute.
.PP
.B Who may connect.
There are two ways to add an agent.
An admin of the edge's
.B \-\-idp
adds one with
.BR "socketley tunnel join" .
Without an idp,
.B socketley tunnel
.I edge
.B invite
on the edge's machine prints an invitation once, for the agent's
.BR \-\-join .
The agent joins with the invitation at its first connection.
In its place the agent is given a key of its own, kept in its state directory
(0600), and the invitation is spent.
The edge keeps only hashes.
.B revoke
cuts an agent off within a second.
.B set
changes what an agent offers and keeps its key.
.PP
.B People.
An edge with
.B \-\-forward
carries a person signed in at its
.B \-\-idp
to the targets that
.B \-\-forward
names, and to nothing else.
The person asks for a target with
.B socketley tunnel connect
or
.BR "tunnel ssh" .
This is how ssh reaches a server through port 443.
.PP
The edge checks the person's sign\-in again at every stream and once a second.
Signing the account out everywhere, or disabling, resetting or removing it,
closes the person's link.
Taking the account out of the admins takes away the agents' TCP services at
its next stream.
An ssh configuration that uses the tunnel:
.RS
.PP
.nf
Host server
    ProxyCommand socketley tunnel ssh edge.example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BR "\-\-mode edge" | agent
Which end of the tunnel this runtime is.
Default: edge.
.TP
.BI "\-p " port
An edge's port for agents and people.
It listens on 127.0.0.1 unless
.B \-\-bind
says otherwise, since the entrance in front of it terminates TLS.
.TP
.BI "\-\-domain " name
An edge's name; its agents' HTTP services are named beside it.
For an edge
.BR edge.example.com ,
a service is named like
.BR home.example.com .
.TP
.BI "\-\-to " url
The edge an agent dials, as
.BI https:// host\fR[:\fIport\fR].
.B http://
is allowed only for an edge on the same machine.
.TP
.BI "\-\-join " invitation
The invitation that
.B invite
printed.
It can be used once.
.TP
.BI "\-\-offer " service = host : port ,...
Where an agent carries the edge's streams, listed by the service names the edge
invited it with.
A name that the agent does not offer is refused at the agent.
.TP
.BI "\-\-forward " host : port ,...
The targets where an edge may carry a signed\-in person's stream.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " idp
The idp runtime whose people may use
.BR \-\-forward ,
and whose admins add agents with
.BR "tunnel join" .
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " client ,...
The clients whose access tokens the edge accepts.
Default:
.BR socketley ,
the client that
.B "socketley login"
signs in as.
.PP
A tunnel does not accept
.BR \-\-shards :
each agent's link is held by one process.
.SH METRICS FLAGS
A
.B metrics
runtime stores what socketley and the machines around it measure, for years.
It answers queries in PromQL over the Prometheus HTTP API, so Grafana reads it
as it reads a Prometheus.
.PP
This daemon's own series reach the runtime without being written out as text.
At each tick, the runtime asks the daemon for what changed since the last tick.
These are the same series that
.B /metrics
writes, with
.B job="socketley"
and
.B instance
set to the daemon's name.
Other daemons and any Prometheus endpoint are scraped.
The runtime's rules raise alerts, which the daemon's
.BR daemon.alert_webhook ,
.B daemon.alert_mail
and
.B daemon.events
deliver.
The dashboard draws its history from this runtime.
.PP
.nf
socketley create metrics metrics \-p 9090 \-\-scrape /etc/socketley/targets \-s
socketley metrics query 'rate(socketley_runtime_messages_total[5m])'
.fi
.PP
Every sample is stamped with the time its tick was due.
So the samples of a series lie one interval apart and take one to three bytes
each.
Every two hours of samples are cut into a block on disk, and two-hour blocks
are merged into a day.
Beside them, the runtime builds coarser tiers with a step of five minutes and
of one hour.
For each step, a tier keeps the minimum, maximum, sum, count and last value,
and a counter's increase.
A query over a long range reads the coarsest tier that still answers it
exactly.
So a dashboard that shows a year reads a year of hourly points rather than
three million samples.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIport\fR"
The port of the API.
Default: 9090.
It listens on 127.0.0.1 unless
.B \-\-bind
names another address, and only a runtime guarded by
.B \-\-keys
or
.B \-\-idp
may do that.
From outside the machine, the API is reached through the entrance with
.BR \-\-domain ,
like any HTTP runtime.
.TP
.BI \-\-keys " file"
A file of service keys, one name and secret per line, in the format a
.B db
runtime reads.
The runtime reads the file again when it changes.
A request carries a key as
.B Authorization: Bearer
.IR secret ,
or as Basic auth with the name and the secret, which is what Grafana's data
source sends.
.TP
.BI \-\-idp " idp"
Admits the sign\-ins of that idp runtime: a browser's session cookie, or an
access token as Bearer.
.TP
.BI \-\-idp\-audience " clients"
The comma\-separated clients, one of which an access token's audience must
name.
.TP
.BR \-\-local ", " \-\-no\-local
Whether the runtime collects this daemon's own series.
On by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-scrape " file"
A file of the targets to scrape, one per line:
.IP
.nf
# name url [every=<duration>] [timeout=<duration>] [bearer=<file>] [label=value ...]
jetson http://127.0.0.1:9101/metrics every=15s site=home
node   https://box.example.com/metrics bearer=/etc/socketley/node.token
.fi
.IP
Each sample from a target gets
.B job
set to the target's name,
.B instance
set to its host and port, and the target's own labels.
Each target also writes
.BR up ,
.B scrape_duration_seconds
and
.BR scrape_samples_scraped .
The series a target stops writing end at once with Prometheus's staleness
marker, and so do all its series when it does not answer.
A changed file is read again at the next tick.
If the file cannot be read, the targets stay as they were.
.IP
A daemon at home that is reached through a
.B tunnel
is scraped as a loopback TCP service of its agent, so nothing is opened on its
router.
.TP
.BI \-\-every " duration"
How often the runtime collects this daemon's series and scrapes a target that
has no every= of its own.
Default: 10s; takes 1s to 1h.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " duration"
How long raw samples are kept.
Default: 30d.
.TP
.BI \-\-downsample " step" : keep\fR[,...]
The coarser tiers, each a step and how long it is kept.
Default: 5m:1y,1h:5y;
.B none
turns them off.
The first step must divide two hours.
Each later step must be a multiple of the one before it, and must divide a
day.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-series " n"
The most series the runtime holds at once.
Default: 200000.
A new series past the limit is refused and counted in
.BR socketley_metrics_series_refused_total .
So one target that sends a label per request cannot take the machine's memory.
.TP
.B \-\-durable
Logs every sample as it arrives, so a crash loses none.
Without it, a crash loses what the open chunks hold, and a stop loses nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-rules " file"
A file of recording and alerting rules, laid over the built\-in ones (the alerts
that
.B DASHBOARD
lists):
.IP
.nf
every=10s
alert DiskSpaceLow warning for=1m summary="{{ $labels.path }}: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} free"
    : socketley_disk_free_ratio < 0.15
alert JetsonGone critical for=2m : up{job="jetson"} == 0
record job:requests:rate5m : sum by (job) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))
off TargetDown
.fi
.IP
Each rule is one line, continued by lines that begin with a space.
Its expression comes after
.BR " : " .
.B alert
takes a name and a severity, and then
.BR for= .
.B keep=
sets how long the alert keeps firing once its condition is gone.
.B every=
sets its own interval.
.BI inhibits= rule\fR[,...]\&
names the alerts it silences on the labels both have.
.BR summary= ,
.B description=
and
.B runbook_url=
are annotations, and any other
.IB label = value
is a label.
Values and annotations take Prometheus's templates
.RB ( "{{ $value }}" ,
.BR "{{ $labels.name }}" ,
.BR humanize ,
.BR humanize1024 ,
.BR humanizePercentage ,
.BR humanizeDuration ,
.BR printf ).
.B record
writes its result as a series of that name.
A rule with the name of a built\-in rule takes its place, and
.BI off " name"
leaves a built\-in rule out.
.IP
The file is read again when it changes.
If it cannot be read, the rules stay as they were, and the log says so.
An alert is pending while its condition has held for less than
.BR for= .
After that it is firing, and a notice goes out.
When it is resolved, a notice goes out too.
Its state is kept in the runtime's directory.
So a restart neither sends a notice for it again nor starts its
.B for=
over.
The
.B ALERTS
and
.B ALERTS_FOR_STATE
series are written as Prometheus writes them.
.TP
.BI \-\-notify " where"
Where alert notices go:
.BR webhook ,
.B mail
and
.BR events ,
comma\-separated.
These are the daemon's
.BR daemon.alert_webhook ,
.B daemon.alert_mail
and
.BR daemon.events .
Default: all three;
.B none
sends no notices.
.TP
.B \-\-receive
Accepts Prometheus remote_write at
.BR "POST /api/v1/write" .
Each request is a Snappy-compressed WriteRequest of up to 16 MiB.
Its samples are stored as they come, and the refused ones are counted in
.BR stats .
A Prometheus, a Grafana Agent or another metrics runtime behind a firewall can
then send what it has, instead of being scraped.
.TP
.BR \-\-logs ", " \-\-no\-logs
Also keeps every line the daemon and its runtimes write, and answers queries for
them with LogQL over Loki's API.
Off by default.
.IP
The lines are collected with the series at each tick, into one file per day in
.B logs/
of the runtime's directory.
Grafana's Loki data source reads the runtime as it reads a Loki.
Each line is labelled
.BR job="socketley" ,
.B instance
(the daemon),
.B level
.RB ( debug ", " info ", " warn ", " error )
and
.BR name .
.B name
is the runtime the line is about: the one that wrote it, or the one that a
daemon line names.
A start never collects a line twice.
.B socketley_log_lines_total
counts the lines by
.B name
and
.BR level ,
for a rule such as
.BR "increase(socketley_log_lines_total{level=\(dqerror\(dq}[5m]) > 0" .
.IP
The supported LogQL is a selector with
.BR = ", " != ", " =~ ", " !~ ,
and the line filters
.BR "|= \(dqtext\(dq" ,
.BR "!= \(dqtext\(dq" ,
.B |~ \(dqregex\(dq
and
.BR "!~ \(dqregex\(dq" ,
which match anywhere in the line.
Over these, it supports
.BR count_over_time ,
.BR rate ,
.B bytes_over_time
and
.BR bytes_rate ,
with
.BR sum ", " count ", " min ", " max ", " avg
by or without labels.
A query without a selector is PromQL.
.TP
.BI \-\-logs\-keep " duration"
How long log lines are kept, in whole days.
Default: 14d.
.TP
.BI \-\-archive " url"
An S3 bucket, with an optional key prefix, that blocks on disk are copied to.
A block is copied once it holds a day or more, or once it ended two days ago.
The URL has the form
.BI http[s]:// host / bucket\fR[/\fIprefix\fR]
and may point at a storage runtime of this daemon or at any S3.
.IP
The copied blocks are listed in
.B archived
in the runtime's directory and are never copied again.
A refused copy is tried again each minute, and logged once.
The archive keeps what
.B \-\-keep
and
.B \-\-downsample
delete here.
Blocks copied back into
.B blocks/
of a stopped runtime are read at its start.
A piece that a later merge replaced is dropped, as it lies inside a bigger
block.
A block that retention removes while the archive refuses every copy is not
copied.
.TP
.BI \-\-archive\-credentials " file"
A file with the bucket's
.B access_key
and
.BR secret_key ,
and also
.B region
and
.B session_token
when the bucket takes them.
Each is a
.IB key " = " value
line, as a sync runtime's
.B \-\-credentials
holds them.
The file is read again at each copy.
.PP
.B The API.
The runtime serves:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR /api/v1/query ,
.BR query_range ,
.BR series ,
.BR labels ,
.BI label/ name /values\fR,
.BR metadata ,
.BR targets ,
.BR rules ,
.BR alerts ,
.BR status/buildinfo ,
.B status/runtimeinfo
and
.BR query_exemplars ,
by GET or a POSTed form, answered in Prometheus's envelope;
.IP \(bu 2
.B /-/healthy
and
.BR /-/ready ;
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-logs ,
.BR /loki/api/v1/query_range ,
.BR query ,
.B labels
and
.BI label/ name /values\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR /metrics ,
with the runtime's own figures.
.PP
A Grafana data source of type Prometheus is pointed at
.BI https:// domain
or
.BI http://127.0.0.1: port\fR,
with the key as Basic auth or a Bearer header.
The PromQL is Prometheus's:
.IP \(bu 2
selectors, ranges and
.BR offset ;
.IP \(bu 2
the arithmetic, comparison and set operators, with
.BR on ,
.BR ignoring ,
.B group_left
and
.BR group_right ;
.IP \(bu 2
the aggregations, with
.B by
and
.BR without ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR rate ,
.BR irate ,
.BR increase ,
.BR delta ,
.BR deriv ,
.B predict_linear
and the
.B _over_time
functions;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR histogram_quantile ,
.BR label_replace ,
.BR label_join ,
.BR absent ,
and the time and maths functions.
.PP
Not supported: subqueries,
.BR @ ,
and native histograms.
.PP
.B The CLI.
.nf
socketley metrics <rt> query '<promql>' [--at <time>]
socketley metrics <rt> query_range '<promql>' [--start <time>] [--end <time>] [--step <duration>]
socketley metrics <rt> series '<selector>' | targets | rules | alerts [--recent] | stats   [--json]
socketley metrics <rt> logs ['<logql>'] [--since <duration>] [--limit <n>]
.fi
.PP
.B socketley export
writes a metrics runtime's settings, as it does for any runtime.
.B socketley export \-\-full
first has a running metrics runtime write out what its head holds, and hold its
merges until the archive is written.
So a restore brings back every sample up to that moment.
.PP
A time is
.BR now ,
.BR \-1h ,
unix seconds or RFC 3339.
By default, a range ends now and starts an hour back.
.B \-\-json
prints what the API answers.
.PP
A metrics runtime does not accept
.BR \-\-shards :
each series is written by one process, and one process holds up to
.B \-\-max\-series
series (200000 by default).
.SH MCP FLAGS
An
.B mcp
runtime is a Model Context Protocol server.
An AI client, such as Claude Code or an agent built on an MCP SDK, connects to it.
The runtime offers that client the tools, resources and prompts its script declares.
With
.BR "\-\-tools daemon" ,
it also offers this daemon's runtimes as tools.
.PP
.nf
socketley create mcp shop \-p 9600 \-\-domain mcp \-\-keys /etc/socketley/mcp.keys \e
    \-\-lua /etc/socketley/shop.lua \-\-tools daemon \-s
.fi
.PP
The runtime speaks Streamable HTTP at
.B /mcp
(and at
.BR / ).
Each
.B POST
carries one JSON-RPC message and is answered as JSON.
A notification is answered
.B 202
with no body.
.B GET
and
.B DELETE
are answered
.BR 405 ,
because the runtime has no stream of its own and no session.
.PP
The runtime speaks revision
.B 2026\-07\-28
as that revision defines it, and is stateless.
Every request carries the
.B MCP\-Protocol\-Version
header, and carries its version and client capabilities in
.BR _meta .
The
.B Mcp\-Method
header names the method, and
.B Mcp\-Name
names the tool, prompt or resource.
A mismatch is answered
.B 400
.RB ( \-32020 ).
The
.B server/discover
method lists what the server offers.
.PP
A client that opens with
.B initialize
is answered as revisions 2025\-11\-25, 2025\-06\-18 and 2025\-03\-26 specify.
Its requests are then accepted without the 2026 headers.
This matters because the MCP SDKs of 2026 speak that older revision.
A request with a version that neither era defines is answered
.B 400
.RB ( \-32022 ),
and the answer names the versions the runtime speaks.
.PP
Each request is answered on its own, so
.B \-\-shards
spreads requests over processes, as it does for a server.
The runtime listens on plain HTTP.
Clients reach it through the entrance proxy, which terminates TLS for it.
.TP
.BI "\-\-keys " file
A file of the keys that clients may call the runtime with, one per line, as
.IR "name secret" .
Add
.B admin
after the secret of a key that may change the daemon.
A secret is at least 16 characters long, and the client sends it as
.BR "Authorization: Bearer" .
A request without a key from the file is answered
.B 401
with
.BR "WWW\-Authenticate: Bearer" .
The runtime reads the file again when it changes.
If the new file does not read, the runtime keeps the keys it had and logs why.
.TP
.B \-\-anonymous
Lets anyone call the runtime without a key.
Use it only on a port that only this machine reaches
.RB ( "\-\-bind 127.0.0.1" ).
One of
.BR \-\-keys ,
.B \-\-idp
and
.B \-\-anonymous
is required.
.B \-\-anonymous
cannot be combined with either of the others.
.TP
.BI "\-\-origins " list
The web origins from which a browser may call the runtime, comma-separated, or
.B *
for any.
A request with any other
.B Origin
is answered
.BR 403 ,
as the revision requires of every server.
A client that is not a browser sends no
.BR Origin .
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp " runtime
The idp runtime whose users sign in to this runtime, in the way the MCP revisions specify for a client.
A request without a token is answered
.B 401
with
.B WWW\-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata
naming
.BR /.well\-known/oauth\-protected\-resource/mcp .
That document names the idp, and says that the server is
.BI https:// name /mcp\fR,
where
.I name
is the first
.BR \-\-domain ,
written as a whole name such as
.BR mcp.example.com .
The client signs in at the idp for that resource.
The runtime accepts the client's access token for that audience only.
The idp issues such tokens when its
.B \-\-resources
names this address.
A key from
.B \-\-keys
is accepted as well.
An account in the idp's
.B admins
group is an admin here.
An account that is disabled, or signed out everywhere, is refused from its next request on.
.TP
.BI "\-\-idp\-audience " clients
The idp clients, comma-separated, whose own access tokens the runtime also accepts.
.TP
.BR "\-\-tools none" | daemon
Selects whether the runtime offers this daemon's runtimes as tools.
With
.BR daemon ,
every key is offered
.B socketley_list
and
.B socketley_get
(name, type, state, port, connections, owner).
Only an admin's key is offered
.B socketley_start
and
.BR socketley_stop ;
a key that may not administer does not see them.
The server does not stop itself.
If the script declares its own tool with the same name, the script's tool is used.
.TP
.BI "\-\-instructions " text
The text that tells a client what the server is for.
.PP
The script declares its tools, resources and prompts at its start:
.RS
.nf
mcp.tool{ name = "order_status", description = "An order's state.", read_only = true,
  input = { type = "object", properties = { id = { type = "string" } }, required = { "id" } },
  handler = function(args, who) return { structured = { id = args.id, state = "sent" } } end }
mcp.resource{ uri = "shop://catalog", mime = "text/plain", ttl_ms = 60000,
  read = function(who) return "..." end }
mcp.prompt{ name = "summarize", arguments = { { name = "order", required = true } },
  get = function(args, who) return "Summarize order " .. args.order end }
.fi
.RE
.IP
A tool's
.B input
and
.B output
are each a JSON Schema, given as a table or as JSON text.
Write an empty object as JSON text, because a table cannot tell it apart from an empty array.
A tool takes four hints:
.B read_only
(default
.BR false ),
.B destructive
(default: the opposite of read_only),
.B idempotent
(default: the same as read_only) and
.B open_world
(default
.BR true ).
A tool with
.B admin
is offered only to an admin's key.
.IP
A handler's return value becomes the tool's result:
.IP \(bu 2
A string is sent as text.
.IP \(bu 2
A table with
.B text
or
.B content
is sent as content blocks, in the revision's format.
.IP \(bu 2
A table with
.B structured
is sent as structured content, and as text too.
.IP \(bu 2
A table with
.B "error = true"
is a failure that the model reads.
A handler that raises is treated the same way.
.IP
A handler may wait on
.BR socketley.http ,
.B socketley.db
and the other calls that wait.
The runtime answers other calls meanwhile.
.IP
A resource's
.B read
returns a string, or a table with
.B text
or
.B blob
(base64), and
.BR mime .
A prompt's
.B get
returns a string, which becomes a user's message, or a table with
.B description
and
.BR messages .
Each message has a
.B role
and a
.BR text .
.IP
Each handler,
.B read
and
.B get
receives
.BR who .
It holds the key's or the account's
.BR name ,
whether it is
.BR admin ,
and, for an account, its
.BR sub ,
the id that never changes.
Each runs on the event loop, within the callback deadline.
A declaration that cannot be read returns
.B nil
and the reason, and the reason is logged.
.PP
.B stats
shows
.BR requests ,
.BR tool_calls ,
.BR tool_errors ,
.B errors
(requests answered with a JSON-RPC error),
.B legacy_requests
(requests in a revision before 2026),
.B refused
(refused for a key, an origin or a method),
.BR tools ,
.BR resources ,
.B prompts
and
.BR keys .
.SH WEB FLAGS
A
.B web
runtime serves a site of files to browsers and does nothing else: no script, no
WebSocket, no raw TCP, no upload. It is what a documentation site, a blog built
by a static site generator or a single\-page app's build is put behind.
.PP
.nf
socketley create web docs \-p 9500 \-\-root /srv/docs/current \-\-domain docs \-s
socketley create web www  \-p 9501 \-\-root /var/www/html \-\-domain example.com \-s
.fi
.PP
It speaks plain HTTP/1.1 on its port and is reached through the entrance proxy,
which terminates TLS and HTTP/2 for its
.B \-\-domain
as for every other name; a name with a dot is taken whole, so the second line
above answers the zone's own name. Unlike the other runtimes it binds
.B 127.0.0.1
unless
.B \-\-bind
says otherwise, since what it speaks is plain HTTP that only the entrance should
reach;
.B "\-\-bind 0.0.0.0"
opens it to the network.
.B \-\-tls
and
.B \-\-lua
are refused.
.TP
.BI "\-\-root " directory
The directory whose files are the site, absolute; required. It may be a
symbolic link to a release: a deploy that swaps the link to a new directory
.RB ( "ln \-s r2 next && mv \-T next current" )
is served from the next request on, with no restart.
.TP
.BI "\-\-index " name
The file a directory's address is answered with,
.B index.html
unless given.
.TP
.BI "\-\-fallback " page
The page, under
.BR \-\-root ,
an address with no file behind it is given, with
.BR 200 ,
as a single\-page app routes its own addresses. Only an address whose last part
has no dot: a missing
.B app.js
stays a
.BR 404 .
.PP
.B "How an address resolves."
.B /guide/
is
.BR guide/index.html ;
.B /guide
is answered
.B 301
to
.BR /guide/ ,
the query kept, so the page's relative links resolve under it;
.B /about
is
.B about.html
when there is no file
.BR about ,
as static site generators write it. A directory with no index lists nothing. An
address nothing answers is the site's
.B 404.html
with status
.BR 404 ,
or a short
.B 404
without one. Repeated slashes are one, and a path that leaves
.B \-\-root
\(em
.BR .. ,
its encoding
.BR %2e%2e ,
or a symbolic link that points out \(em is a
.BR 404 ,
as is any segment that begins with a dot
.RB ( .env ,
.BR .git/ ),
except
.BR .well\-known .
.PP
.B "Methods."
.B GET
and
.BR HEAD ;
.B OPTIONS
is
.B 204
saying so, and every other method
.B 405
with
.BR "Allow: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS" .
A WebSocket upgrade is answered with the page.
.PP
.B "Caching."
Every file answer carries
.BR ETag ,
.BR Last\-Modified ,
.B Date
and
.BR "X\-Content\-Type\-Options: nosniff" ;
.B If\-None\-Match
and
.B If\-Modified\-Since
are answered
.BR 304 ,
.B If\-Match
and
.B If\-Unmodified\-Since
.BR 412 ,
and a single
.B Range
.BR 206 ,
under an
.B If\-Range
that names this copy, the whole file otherwise.
A script, a style sheet, a font, an image or wasm whose name a build stamped
with a content hash
.RB ( main.3f9a1c2b.js ,
.BR index\-B_x3\-9Qa.js )
is sent with
.BR "Cache\-Control: public, max\-age=31536000, immutable" ,
since a new build is a new name; everything else, a page above all, with
.BR no\-cache ,
which a browser answers with a revalidation that costs a
.B 304
and never shows a page older than the files. A hash has digits and letters and
no run of four lowercase letters, so
.B holiday\-2024\-beach.jpg
is a name its owner may replace.
.PP
.B "Compression."
A
.B .br
or
.B .gz
copy a build left beside a text file, no older than it, goes to a client whose
.B Accept\-Encoding
takes it, br first. A text file with no
.B .gz
is compressed once, by a helper thread, after its first answer, and the copy is
kept until the file changes; the request that asked for it is never held for
it. A range is always of the file itself.
.PP
.B "Memory and the disk."
What an address resolves to is kept, a file up to 32 KiB with its bytes, a
larger one with an open descriptor it is sent from with
.BR sendfile (2),
or, when a window of it is not in the page cache, read through the ring, so the
event loop never waits on the disk. inotify watches every directory below
.B \-\-root
and every one above it, so a change is seen at once, a release swapped in at
any name on the way
.RB ( current ,
or
.B current
in
.BR current/public )
too, and no request asks the disk whether a file changed. Kept bytes are bounded
at 256 MiB and kept descriptors at 4096, past which a large file is opened for
each request. Under
.B \-\-shards
each shard keeps, compresses and watches for itself, since any of them may be
asked for any file: the bounds are a shard's, and the watches count once a shard
against
.BR fs.inotify.max_user_watches . An address with no file shares the one
.B 404.html
or fallback answer, so a flood of made\-up addresses keeps nothing. A file that
cannot be opened for want of a descriptor or memory is a
.B 503
with
.BR "Retry\-After: 1" ,
never a remembered
.BR 404 .
Without inotify, or with no watch left
.RB ( fs.inotify.max_user_watches ),
each kept file and its copies are looked at again with a
.BR stat (2)
per request, and
.B \-\-root
once a second.
.PP
.B "Connections."
Pipelined requests are answered in order; a client that sends them faster than
it reads the answers has its next requests wait once a megabyte is owed to it,
and one that stops reading, mid\-file or so, is let go after a minute without
progress. After a
.B "Connection: close"
or a refused request nothing more on the connection is answered. An HTTP/1.0
client that asks to stay open is told
.BR "Connection: keep\-alive" .
.PP
.B stats
shows
.BR requests ,
.BR not_modified ,
.BR not_found ,
.BR redirects ,
.B refused
(a method or a malformed request),
.B unavailable
(a
.B 503
for want of a descriptor or memory, never remembered as a
.BR 404 ),
.BR ranges ,
.B from_memory
and
.B zero_copy
(how bodies went),
.B compressed
(answers sent as a copy),
.BR compress_jobs ,
.B reloads
(releases swapped in),
.B stalled
(connections let go after a minute of not reading),
.BR addresses ,
.BR kept_bytes ,
.B kept_files
and
.B watching
(1 while inotify works).
.SH REGISTERED TYPES
A runtime type beyond the built-in fifteen is delivered as a manifest plus a
worker binary.
The manifest is a declarative file, never code.
From it, the daemon learns the type's name, its worker executable and the schema of its configuration keys.
The behaviour lives in the binary, which the daemon forks and execs exactly as it does its own workers.
No third-party code ever runs in the daemon's process.
.PP
The daemon reads manifests at startup.
A system daemon reads
.BR /etc/socketley/types/*.toml ,
and a development daemon reads
.BR ~/.config/socketley/types/*.toml :
.PP
.RS
.nf
name = "greeter"
exec = "/usr/local/lib/socketley/greeter-worker"
manifest_version = 1

[config.greeting]
kind = "string"        # string | int | bool | enum
required = true

[config.repeat]
kind = "int"
default = "1"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each
.BI [config. key ]
section becomes a
.BI \-\- key
flag on
.B create
and
.BR edit .
Its value is validated against its kind and stored under an
.B extra
object in the state file.
.B show
prints that object, and
.B import
accepts it.
A key that the manifest does not declare is refused by name, exactly as a misspelled built-in flag is.
A required key must be given at
.BR create .
A declared default is filled in when the key is omitted.
An edit takes effect at the next start, because the worker reads its configuration only once, when it is spawned.
.PP
The worker binary is built with the SDK's
.BR <socketley/worker.h> .
It calls
.B register_type
with a factory for the type, then
.BR "run(argc, argv)" .
It speaks the same versioned control-socket protocol as the daemon's own workers.
It therefore survives daemon upgrades that do not change frame meanings.
A worked example is in
.BR sdk/examples/custom\-type/ .
.PP
A runtime's Lua script cannot create a registered type.
If a state file's type has no manifest at the next boot, the daemon leaves the file on disk and does not restore the runtime.
The daemon log gives the reason.
.SH LUA CONFIGURATION
.B "Two different files are both called \(lqa Lua script\(rq, and they do not mix."
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I daemon configuration
\(em
.BR "socketley \-\-lua config.lua" .
This file runs in the CLI process.
The CLI reads its
.B daemon
and
.B runtimes
tables, brings the daemon to what they say, and exits.
The daemon reads the same tables from its own configuration file when it starts.
This file never becomes part of any runtime.
A callback defined in it is dead code: nothing loads it, nothing calls it, and nothing reports it.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I runtime handler
\(em
.BR "socketley create server api \-p 9000 \-\-lua handler.lua" ,
or the same path given as a runtime's
.B config
key inside a configuration.
The daemon loads this file into that runtime's own interpreter.
It is the only file whose
.BI on_ *
callbacks,
.B filters
and
.B routes
mean anything.
.RE
.PP
A configuration attaches a handler by naming its path.
It never defines the callbacks itself:
.nf

runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "api", port = 9000,
      config = "handler.lua",     \-\- the file whose callbacks run
      start = true },
}
.fi
.PP
.B config
and
.B lua
are two accepted spellings of the same key.
Everything below this point in this chapter, except
.B "Configuration File Format"
itself, describes a
.IR "runtime handler" .
.PP
.B Module loading:
The Lua
.B require()
function resolves modules relative to the script's directory.
For a script at
.IR /home/user/handler.lua ,
.B require("utils")
loads
.IR /home/user/utils.lua .
The default Lua package path is appended, so standard modules still work.
.PP
.B Standard library:
.BR base ", " string ", " table ", " math ", " os ", " io " and " package
are open.
Two
.B os
functions are removed, because each one damages the process that every runtime
shares:
.B os.exit
and
.BR os.setlocale .
.BR os.execute ,
.BR os.remove ,
.B os.rename
and
.B os.tmpname
are present (see
.BR "Handler Privileges" ).
.PP
.B os.setlocale
is removed for a reason of its own.
.B setlocale
is process-wide.
.B socketley.json_encode
formats numbers with
.BR %.14g ,
and
.B socketley.json_decode
parses them with
.BR strtod ,
and both read
.BR LC_NUMERIC .
If one script switches to a comma-decimal locale,
.I every
runtime in the daemon emits
.B 1,5
and truncates
.B 1.5
to
.BR 1 ,
for as long as the daemon lives.
.BR os.time ,
.BR os.clock ,
.B os.date
and
.B os.difftime
stay, because they only read.
.SS Configuration File Format
.nf
\-\- Optional: configure the daemon itself
daemon = {
    name = "node1",
    cluster = "/shared/cluster"
}

runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "api", port = 9000, group = "api",
      config = "handler.lua", start = true },
    { type = "client", name = "cli", port = 9000, target = "127.0.0.1:9000" },
    { type = "proxy",  name = "gw", port = 8080,
      backends = {"@api", "127.0.0.1:9000"}, strategy = "round-robin" },
    { type = "cache",  name = "store", port = 9001,
      persistent = "/data/store.bin" }
}
.fi
.PP
The
.B daemon
table is processed first (name, then cluster).
If it is omitted, the daemon runs without cluster mode.
.PP
The daemon itself reads this table's keys
.RB ( name ", " cluster ", " log_level ", " metrics_port " and " cpus )
at startup, from the file that
.B SOCKETLEY_CONFIG
names.
The last three are settled before the ring exists, and cannot be told to a daemon that is already serving.
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
therefore refuses them by name rather than dropping them.
The exception is the daemon's own file, where they take effect at the daemon's next start.
A flag typed on
.B "socketley daemon"
outranks the same setting in the file.
.B config
is the older name of this table and still works.
A file that names both is refused.
.PP
The recognised
.B runtimes
keys, and the flag each one becomes, are listed below.
Every flag that
.B create
accepts can be set from here.
A configuration file and a command line are therefore two ways to write the same thing, not two different capabilities:
.nf
\(em\(em every type \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
type, name                 (required)
port                       \-p
listen                     \-\-listen
shards                     \-\-shards
log                        \-\-log
write                      \-w
group                      \-g
cpus                       \-\-cpus
max_connections            \-\-max\-connections
rate_limit                 \-\-rate\-limit
global_rate_limit          \-\-global\-rate\-limit
idle_timeout               \-\-idle\-timeout  300, or 5m / 2h / 1d
congestion                 \-\-congestion    bbr
write_buffer               \-\-write\-buffer   8M, 256K, 65536
read_buffer                \-\-read\-buffer    1M, 8M, 65536
drain (bool)               \-\-drain
test (bool)                \-\-test
tls (bool)                 \-\-tls
cert, key, ca              \-\-cert / \-\-key / \-\-ca
reconnect (bool or n)      \-\-reconnect     true keeps trying
config / lua               \-\-lua           the runtime's handler script
bash                       \-b / \-bp / \-bt / \-bpt
start (bool)               \-s
\(em\(em server \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
mode                       \-\-mode
udp (bool)                 \-\-udp
http                       \-\-http
http_cache (bool)          \-\-http\-cache
compress (bool)            \-\-compress
origins                    \-\-origins
master_pw                  \-\-master\-pw
send_to (string)           \-\-send\-to
cache                      \-\-cache
upstreams (array)          \-\-upstream a;b;c
\(em\(em client \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
target                     \-t
mode                       \-\-mode
udp (bool)                 \-\-udp
\(em\(em proxy \(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
backends (array)           \-\-backend a,b,c
protocol                   \-\-protocol
strategy                   \-\-strategy
root                       \-\-root
fastcgi_root               \-\-fastcgi\-root
