.PP
Every other flag of a type has a key of the same name, without the leading dashes and with each hyphen turned into an underscore:
.B \-\-mail\-domain
is
.BR mail_domain ,
.B \-\-submission
is
.BR submission ,
and
.B \-\-queue\-lifetime
is
.BR queue_lifetime .
A list key takes a table, or one string for a list of one.
A key that no flag of the type has is refused by name, not silently dropped.
A value of the wrong shape is refused the same way.
So is a value that contains a tab, because the daemon's command line splits on tabs.
A password containing a tab would arrive as two arguments.
A space stays inside its value, as it does inside a quoted argument on the command line.
.PP
A key can pass this check and still be refused by the daemon for that type, such as
.B \-\-client\-ca
on a cleartext proxy.
The daemon's own error is then shown under the name of the runtime the key belongs to.
The remaining entries are still applied, and the exit code is 1.
.PP
The
.B daemon_process
key has been removed, together with the flag it produced.
A configuration file that still contains it is refused, naming the line.
The key is not silently dropped, because that would leave an operator believing a runtime is in the daemon when it is not.
.PP
Nothing replaces it, because the whole cross\-runtime API works in a worker:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and
.B socketley.cluster
are answered from the replica map that the daemon pushes.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.B .stop
and
.B .remove
are sent to the daemon, and wait for its answer.
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley.pipe
and
.B self.route
reach a runtime in another process.
.PP
See
.B "Process Isolation"
above.
.PP
The health\-check, circuit\-breaker, retry and TLS keys were added when the table above was made to name every flag.
A file written before then still reads the same way.
.PP
The interpreter that reads a configuration file is not the daemon's, and it is much smaller.
It opens
.BR base ,
.BR package ,
.BR table ,
.B string
and
.BR math .
There is no
.B os
and no
.BR io .
So
.RB ` "os.execute(\(dqmkdir -p /tmp/logs\(dq)" `
at the top of a configuration is not a sandbox refusal.
It fails with
.BR "attempt to index global 'os' (a nil value)" :
the whole file is refused, the exit code is 1, and no runtime is created.
Create the directories that a configuration needs from the shell that runs it.
.B string
and
.B math
are open because a configuration computes names and port numbers.
The daemon's own configuration file opens the same set, so either interpreter can read the same file.
.SS A machine's setup
A setup is a machine's runtimes, written as Lua files.
Another machine that is given the files gets the same runtimes.
The runtimes' data is not in the files.
.PP
.B include
reads a file and returns what that file returns.
Given a directory, it reads each
.B .lua
file in it, in name order, and returns the list of their results.
A list inside
.B runtimes
is expanded into its entries.
A relative path is resolved beside the first file, wherever the command was typed.
This applies to an include's path and to a path value, such as a credentials file, a certificate, a handler script or a zone file:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\-\- /etc/socketley/config.lua
daemon = { metrics_port = 9190, dashboard_idp = "auth" }
runtimes = {
    include "runtimes/ns.lua",
    include "runtimes/auth.lua",
    include "runtimes/sites",            \-\- every .lua in it
}

\-\- /etc/socketley/runtimes/ns.lua
return { type = "dns", name = "ns", zone = { "zones/example.com.zone" },
         publish = { "203.0.113.7" }, start = true }
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
brings the daemon to what the files say:
.IP \(bu 2
A runtime that the daemon does not have is created.
.IP \(bu 2
For a runtime that the daemon has, the CLI plans it as
.B create
would make it from the file, and compares the plan with what
.B show
reports.
Where they differ, the runtime is told what differs, across a restart if it is running, and is then asked again.
A setting that the file no longer contains is cleared.
A runtime that does not take a setting is named, and the exit code is 1.
.IP \(bu 2
A runtime that already matches the file is not touched.
.IP \(bu 2
.B start
starts or stops the runtime.
A runtime without
.B start
is left running or stopped, as it is.
.IP \(bu 2
A runtime of the daemon's that the files do not name is left alone.
.PP
When the daemon starts, it reads the
.B runtimes
of its own configuration file, after restoring the runtimes it kept.
It creates each runtime it does not have, and starts it where
.B start
says so.
A runtime that it already has is left as it is, for
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
to bring in line with the file.
A new machine therefore needs only the files in
.BR /etc/socketley ;
nothing else has to be run.
.B "socketley export"
writes a running machine's setup in this form.
.SS Handler Privileges
.B "A Lua handler runs with the daemon's full privileges."
It is not sandboxed, and there is no supported way to sandbox it.
Read a handler as you would read a program you are about to run as the daemon's user, because that is exactly what it is.
.PP
This makes the control socket's permission the whole security boundary.
A handler is attached with
.BR "create \-\-lua" ,
which is an ordinary command on that socket.
Anyone who may open the socket may therefore run code as the daemon's user.
The socket is
.B 0600
for a daemon you start yourself, and
.B "0660 socketley:socketley"
for the packaged one.
Membership of that group is therefore equivalent to being the daemon's user, and should be granted on that understanding.
Docker uses the same arrangement.
It is stated here so that nobody has to infer it: nothing else in this daemon stands between a local account and the handler.
.PP
Concretely, a handler can:
.TP
.B run any command
.B io.popen("...")
and
.B os.execute("...")
are both present.
.TP
.B read or write any file that user can
.B io.open()
is present in every mode, including
.BR w ,
which truncates.
.TP
.B load native code
.B package.loadlib()
is present, as are
.BR load (),
.BR loadstring (),
.B dofile()
and
.BR loadfile ().
.TP
.B read the daemon's environment
.B os.getenv()
is present.
.PP
This is deliberate.
Handlers legitimately read and write files.
A logging pipeline appends to one, and a configuration\-driven handler reads its table from disk.
Closing
.B io
would therefore break supported use.
In practice,
.B "the right to hand this daemon a \-\-lua script is the right to run code as its user."
The permission on the IPC socket controls that right, not anything in the interpreter.
See
.B THE IPC SOCKET
under
.BR FILES .
.PP
A handler's interpreter opens
.BR base ,
.BR string ,
.BR table ,
.BR math ,
.BR os ,
.B io
and
.BR package .
Two names are removed from
.BR os .
Calling either one fails with
.BR "attempt to call field '...' (a nil value)" ,
not with a refusal that gives a reason:
.PP
.nf
os.exit    os.setlocale
.fi
.PP
Neither removal is a security boundary; nothing here is.
Both pass a narrower test.
Each is a call that damages the process that every
.I other
runtime in this daemon shares, and that a script makes by accident rather than by intent.
.IP \(bu 2
.B os.exit()
ends the daemon from inside a callback.
A handler is very often a standalone script that grew into one, and it ends the way standalone scripts end.
.IP \(bu 2
.B os.setlocale()
changes the process's
.BR LC_NUMERIC .
.B socketley.json_encode
and
.B socketley.json_decode
use it to format and parse numbers.
One line in one handler makes every other handler's JSON write 1.5 as 1,5 and decode it as 1.
This happens silently, for as long as the daemon lives.
.PP
.BR os.time (),
.BR os.clock (),
.BR os.date ()
and
.BR os.difftime ()
remain: they only read, and reading the clock or the locale harms nothing.
.PP
.BR os.execute (),
.BR os.remove (),
.BR os.rename ()
and
.BR os.tmpname ()
are present.
Removing them would deny no capability, because
.B io.popen
is a shell and
.B io.open(path, \(dqw\(dq)
truncates.
It would only suggest, to anyone reading a handler, that the daemon guards its files and its shell, which it does not.
A fence with an open gate beside it is worse than no fence, because people plan around it.
.PP
.B io.open
and every write through it block the event loop for as long as the write takes.
.PP
.B require()
resolves against the script's directory, and then against the default
.BR package.path .
.B package.cpath
is left at its default, and no binary module ships with socketley.
A
.B require
of a C module, such as
.BR cjson ,
.B lsqlite3
or a
.B luasql
driver, therefore fails unless that module is installed where the system Lua would find it.
For JSON, use the built\-in
.B socketley.json_encode
and
.B socketley.json_decode
instead; they need nothing installed.
.SS Available Callbacks
.TP
.B on_start()
Called when the runtime starts.
.TP
.B on_stop()
Called when the runtime stops.
.TP
.B on_message(msg)
Called when a message is received (server and client).
When a server defines this callback, it takes over message handling: the default broadcast
(in \fBinout\fR and \fBmaster\fR modes) is suppressed.
Use \fBself.broadcast(msg)\fR to relay messages yourself.
.TP
.B on_client_message(client_id, msg)
Called when a message is received, with the sender's id (server only).
It fires alongside on_message, for backward compatibility.
.TP
.B on_http_body(client_id, chunk, remaining)
Called with each piece of a request body and the number of bytes still to come after it.
.B remaining
therefore reaches 0 exactly once per body.
Defining this callback is the opt\(hyin.
A server whose script defines it streams request bodies to the callback instead of collecting them.
A server whose script does not define it keeps its usual behaviour, including the 413 for a body larger than
.BR \-\-read\-buffer .
.IP
This callback is what makes a body larger than memory possible.
The bytes are handed over and erased as they arrive.
So
.B req.body
in the
.B on_http_request
that follows is deliberately EMPTY: keeping the bytes a second time is the memory cost this callback exists to avoid.
Measured on one worker, one connection at a time:
.IP
.nf
start             19,796 kB
after 50 MB #1    37,344 kB
after 50 MB #2    37,344 kB     unchanged
after 50 MB #3    37,344 kB     unchanged
after 200 MB      37,428 kB     +84 kB
after 500 MB      37,444 kB     +16 kB
.fi
.IP
The first upload costs 17.5 MB once, which is the provided\(hybuffer ring being faulted in.
Every upload after it costs nothing: half a gigabyte is received inside 37 MB resident.
.IP
Keep per\(hyupload state keyed by
.BR client_id ,
and clear it in
.BR on_disconnect .
An abandoned body never reaches
.BR on_http_request ,
so nothing there can reset the state.
.B on_disconnect
is the only signal an abandoned body produces.
It does fire, however much of the body arrived.
That is what a script writing to a temporary file needs in order to remove the file.
.IP
.nf
local st = {}
function on_http_body(id, chunk, remaining)
    st[id] = (st[id] or 0) + #chunk
    if remaining == 0 then ... end
end
function on_disconnect(id) st[id] = nil end
.fi
.IP
.B "A body of unknown length."
A client that cannot say how long its body is sends
.BR "Transfer-Encoding: chunked" .
Examples are fetch() with a ReadableStream body, anything piping a process's output, and anything that cannot stat what it is sending.
With
.B on_http_body
defined, such a body is decoded and delivered like any other, without a limit, because there is no length to limit it by.
Without the callback, it is answered 501.
.IP
.B remaining
is
.B nil
for every chunk of such a body, because how much is still to come is genuinely unknowable.
The end of the framing produces ONE final call, with an empty chunk and
.BR "remaining = 0" .
So "remaining reaches 0 exactly once per body" holds for both forms, and a script written for
.B Content-Length
needs no change.
Measured with 62 MB in 1000 chunks: 1708 calls, 1707 of them nil and one zero.
250 MB more after that costs 148 kB more resident memory.
.IP
Only chunked is decoded.
.B "Transfer-Encoding: gzip, chunked"
can be framed, because the chunk sizes do delimit it, but its contents are still compressed.
Removing the framing and handing the bytes on as the body would hand on gzip and call it the body.
That stays 501.
A coding that is not chunked at all leaves the message with no way to say where the body ends, and is answered 400.
.IP
Trailers are consumed, limited to 8 KB, and discarded.
Turning them into headers after the request has been dispatched is how a trailer becomes request smuggling.
Framing that contradicts itself is a 400, because nothing after it can be trusted to be payload.
Examples are a chunk not followed by CRLF, a size that is not hex, and a size line longer than 256 bytes.
.IP
Header names keep the case the client sent them in, so use
.B req.headers["Host"]
and not
.BR req.headers["host"] .
.TP
.B on_upstream_writable(conn_id)
Called when an upstream's write queue has come back under
.BR self.watermark() .
A producer that stopped because
.B self.upstream_pending(conn_id)
was high can then carry on.
This is a separate callback from
.B on_writable
because upstream ids and client ids are different spaces.
One function taking both would have to be told which kind of id it had been given.
.TP
.B on_writable(client_id)
Called when this connection's write queue has come back under
.BR self.watermark() .
A producer that stopped because
.B self.pending(client_id)
was high can then carry on.
The callback is edge\(hytriggered: it fires once per crossing, not once per completed write.
.IP
A client runtime has the same callback without the argument,
.BR on_writable() ,
because it has one connection, and a number naming it would be a fiction.
A client's refusal to send also names its real cause.
A connected
.B \-\-mode out
client whose server has stopped reading is refused with a reason that names the backlog.
It is not told it "is not connected to its server, or was created \-\-mode in and does not send", three statements that are all false of it.
Measured with 400 chunks of 64 KB sent to a peer reading nothing: 129 taken, 271 refused, 8,396,928 bytes queued.
.IP
This callback is what makes a body larger than the queue limit sendable at all.
Without it, a script had only the hard limit, with nothing leading up to it: at 8 MiB the connection is closed.
The only safe strategy was to know the limit in advance and never approach it.
Measured with a client reading in 256 KB bites with a 5 ms pause, sending 200 chunks of 64 KB (13,107,400 bytes):
.IP
.nf
unpaced   8,454,273 bytes delivered, then the connection closed
paced    13,107,400 bytes delivered, on_writable fired 3 times
.fi
.IP
Pace against
.BR self.watermark() ,
not against a number of your own.
It is a call and not a field because
.B \-\-write\-buffer
can be edited, and a number copied into the script's table at load would go stale as soon as the setting changed.
The callback fires on the crossing.
A producer that stops below the watermark never crosses it, so it is never called back.
It would wait for an event that its own caution prevented:
.IP
.nf
local function pump(id)
    while remaining > 0 do
        if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end
        if not self.send(id, next_chunk()) then return end
        remaining = remaining \- 1
    end
end

function on_writable(id) pump(id) end
.fi
.IP
The call is made one loop turn after the write that emptied the queue, not on that write's completion.
A connection that closes in between is simply not called.
.TP
.B on_connect(client_id)
Called when a client connects (server and client).
It is also called on a sub\-server when a connection is routed to it.
The client_id is opaque.
For this runtime's own clients, it is the file descriptor.
For a connection routed here from another runtime, it is a handle above 16777216 that names the connection wherever it lives.
Pass it back to
.BR self.send() ,
.BR self.owner_send() ,
or anything else that takes a client_id.
Do not compare it against a descriptor or assume anything about its value.
.TP
.B on_disconnect(client_id)
Called when a client disconnects (server and client).
.TP
.B on_rcpt(rcpt, default) \(-> bool
Decides, in a mail runtime, whether to accept each recipient as the client names it.
Each message the runtime accepts is then passed to
.BR on_message(msg) .
See
.B Lua
under
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.TP
.B on_publish(client, msg) \(-> bool | table
.TQ
.B on_subscribe(client, filter, qos) \(-> bool
Decide, in an mqtt runtime, which publishes and subscriptions pass.
In that runtime,
.BR on_auth ,
.B on_connect
and
.B on_disconnect
are given a client table.
See
.B Lua
under
.BR "MQTT FLAGS" .
.TP
.B on_send(msg)
Called when a message is sent.
.TP
.B on_route(method, path, backend_path)
Chooses the backend for a request by returning its index (0-based).
It is called by a proxy only, and only with
.BR "\-\-strategy lua" .
It answers only "where does this go".
To accept or refuse a request before any backend is chosen, see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.IP
The strategy decides whether the callback is consulted.
A proxy created without
.B "\-\-strategy lua"
keeps round\-robin (or
.BR random ,
if that was requested) and never looks the callback up.
A script that defines
.B on_route
on such a proxy loads without any report, and the proxy routes exactly as if the function were not there.
That is the usual reason why an
.B on_route
that was tested and works appears to do nothing.
.IP
.I path
is the path
.I as the client sent it,
including this proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix.
.I backend_path
is the same path with that prefix removed, which is what the backend will actually receive.
A proxy named
.B gw
that is handed
.B /gw/api/users
sees both:
.nf

function on_route(method, path, backend_path)
    -- path         == "/gw/api/users"
    -- backend_path == "/api/users"
    if backend_path:match("^/api/") then return 0 end
    return 1
end

.fi
Both are offered because each is the truth about a different thing.
The filter chain judges the request the client made, so it is handed
.IR path .
A routing decision is usually about the endpoint being addressed, which is
.IR backend_path .
.IP
.I backend_path
exists so that scripts stop computing it.
Doing it by hand means stripping
.B "\(dq/\(dq .. self.name .. \(dq/\(dq"
and getting the bare case right, and the bare case is the one nobody gets right.
The gateway's own root arrives as
.BR /gw ,
three characters against a five-character prefix, and must become
.BR / .
A
.B sub()
past the end yields the empty string, which matches no pattern at all.
.IP
The arguments are passed with
.B "\-\-protocol http"
only.
A TCP proxy has no request line to read them from, and calls
.B on_route
with none.
So a body written for HTTP, such as
.RB ` "return hash(path) % n" `,
raises on the first request when it is moved to a TCP proxy.
.IP
A handler that declares only two parameters is unaffected.
Lua discards arguments that a function does not name, so every
.B on_route
written before this existed behaves exactly as it did.
.IP
If a call raises, or returns anything other than an index inside the backend list, it selects nothing.
The proxy reports the error on stderr and falls back to the selection it would have made without the callback.
The request is forwarded either way.
Refusing a request is the filter chain's job, not this callback's.
.TP
.B filters
Not a callback but an array of them, for a proxy only: see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B routes
Not a callback either, but a table in which this runtime declares what it answers.
The daemon publishes it to the proxies in front of the runtime: see
.BR "Declared Routes" .
.TP
.B on_proxy_request(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Called for every chunk forwarded from a client to a backend (proxy only).
Return a modified string to replace the data, or
.B nil
to drop the chunk entirely, so that the backend never receives it.
.IP
Returning nothing at all forwards the chunk unchanged, which is not the same as returning
.BR nil .
A hook written to watch traffic
.RB ( "function on_proxy_request(id, data) log(data) end" )
ends without a return.
If that were read as
.BR nil ,
the most natural three lines in this API would discard every byte through the proxy.
The filter chain draws the same distinction: a filter that returns nothing has no opinion, and the chain continues.
.IP
The hook fails closed, like the filter chain and for the same reason.
If it raises a Lua error, or returns anything other than a string or
.BR nil ,
the request is refused.
The connection to the client and the one to the backend are both closed, and the reason is logged.
The hook is where a script strips a header or rejects a request.
Forwarding the unmodified bytes when it could not run would admit exactly what it was there to refuse.
And
.B "return false"
is a rejection, not a value to guess at.
.IP
The refusal closes the connections rather than answering with a status.
This callback is on the byte path: it is called with body chunks as well as with the request head, and it carries TCP as readily as HTTP.
To refuse a request with a status line, use the filter chain; see
.BR "Proxy Filter Chain" .
.TP
.B on_proxy_response(client_id, data) \(-> string | nil
Called for every chunk forwarded from a backend to a client (proxy only).
It works like
.BR on_proxy_request .
Returning nothing forwards the chunk unchanged, a replacement string replaces it, and nil drops it.
An error, or a return of any other type, refuses.
Here the refusal closes the client connection, because the response this hook was to redact must not be delivered unredacted.
.TP
.B on_master_auth(client_id, password) \(-> bool
Decides whether a client gets the master role, on a server in master mode.
Return
.B true
to grant the master role.
If this callback is defined, it overrides
.BR \-\-master\-pw ,
so it is the entire gate on that server.
.IP
Its result is read strictly, and it fails closed: only the boolean
.B true
grants.
Anything else denies:
.BR false ,
.BR nil ,
no return at all, and every truthy non\-boolean, such as a string, the number 0 or a table.
If the callback raises a Lua error, the attempt is denied and the error is logged.
A denial from this callback is counted against the source address exactly as a wrong
.B \-\-master\-pw
is, so the per-address lockout described there applies here too.
.TP
.B on_auth(client_id) \(-> bool
Decides whether to admit a client, right after it is accepted (server only).
It is called before
.B on_connect
fires.
Return
.B true
to admit the client, or
.B false
to reject it and close the connection.
If the callback raises a Lua error, the client is also rejected (fail-closed).
When no
.B on_auth
callback is defined, all clients are admitted.
.TP
.B on_websocket(client_id, headers)
Called right after a WebSocket upgrade handshake completes (server only).
By then,
.B on_connect
has already fired.
The
.I headers
argument is a Lua table with any subset of these keys from the HTTP upgrade request:
.BR cookie ,
.BR origin ,
.BR protocol
(Sec-WebSocket-Protocol) and
.BR authorization .
Only keys present in the request are set; absent headers are nil.
This callback never fires for plain TCP clients.
.TP
.B on_http_request(req) \(-> table
Called when the server receives an HTTP request (server only).
The server detects HTTP from the first bytes; see
.BR "Multi-Protocol Auto-Detection" .
The
.I req
argument is a table with these fields:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", etc.)
path        string   Request path (e.g. "/api/users")
query       string   Query string without leading ? (empty if none)
body        string   Request body (empty for GET)
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "1.1")
headers     table    Request headers {["Host"]="...", ...}
.fi
.IP
Header names arrive as the client spelled them.
Values arrive with optional whitespace (spaces and tabs) removed from both ends, so
.B "X\-Token:\ \ abc\ "
reaches the callback as
.BR abc .
A request whose framing this server refuses never reaches the callback.
See
.B "Request Framing"
above for those requests and how they are answered.
.IP
Return a table with the HTTP response:
.nf
{
    status  = 200,                              -- HTTP status code
    headers = {["Content-Type"] = "text/html"}, -- response headers
    body    = "<h1>Hello</h1>"                  -- response body
}
.fi
.IP
If no
.B on_http_request
callback is defined, the server falls back to static file serving
.RB ( \-\-http )
or returns 404.
Returning nothing, or anything that is not a table, has the same effect: the request falls through to the static file or the 404.
Only
.B nil
and no return at all do this silently; they are how a handler declines.
Any other value means the script meant to answer, so the daemon's log names the fall-through as
.BR "[lua <runtime>] on_http_request returned a string; expected a table or nil ..." .
.IP
In the returned table,
.B status
must be a number,
.B body
a string, and
.B headers
a table of string to string.
A field the script did not set takes its default: 200, an empty body, and no headers.
A
.B status
or
.B body
that is present but of the wrong type makes a response this server cannot build.
The request is then answered
.BR "500 Internal Server Error" ,
and the field and the type it arrived as are written to the daemon's log.
A header whose name or value is not a string costs the client only that one header, not the whole answer, and is logged the same way.
.IP
HTTP/1.1 keep-alive is supported: the connection is reused for later requests unless the client's
.B Connection
header asks to close.
That request is honoured for a Lua response exactly as it is for a static file.
.IP
A callback that
.B raises
is not a callback that declined.
The request is answered
.BR "500 Internal Server Error" ,
and the error is written to the daemon's log as
.BR "[lua <runtime>] on_http_request error: ..." .
Falling through to the 404 would make a broken handler look the same, to the client, as one that had no answer.
.IP
Example:
.nf
function on_http_request(req)
    if req.method == "GET" and req.path == "/health" then
        return {status = 200, body = "ok"}
    end
    return {
        status  = 200,
        headers = {["Content-Type"] = "application/json"},
        body    = '{"method": "' .. req.method .. '", "path": "' .. req.path .. '"}'
    }
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_tick(dt)
Called at a configurable interval while the runtime is running.
.I dt
is the actual time elapsed since the last tick, in milliseconds (a double).
The interval is set by a top-level
.B tick_ms
global in the Lua script: a whole number of milliseconds, 10 or more.
Default: 100, when the global is omitted.
A value below 10, or one that is not a whole number, makes the script refuse to load, with a reason; it is not rounded into something else.
10 is the shortest interval
.B on_tick
can be armed at, and the daemon will not do what a script asking for less requests.
Example:
.nf
tick_ms = 50
function on_tick(dt)
    self.broadcast("ping")
end
.fi
.TP
.B on_miss(key) \(-> value [, ttl_seconds]
Called when
.B GET
finds nothing (cache only), so the script can fetch the value from somewhere else.
What it returns is stored and answered as a hit.
See
.B "Database Backend"
for the four cache callbacks in full, with their ordering and their failure behaviour.
.TP
.B on_write(key, value, ttl)
Called after a successful
.BR SET / SETEX / SETNX / MSET " (cache only)."
.TP
.B on_delete(key)
Called after a
.B DEL
that removed something (cache only).
.TP
.B on_expire(key)
Called after the TTL sweep removes a key (cache only).
.TP
.B on_upstream(conn_id, data)
Called for each newline-delimited message received from an upstream connection (server only).
Use \-u to configure upstreams.
.TP
.B on_upstream_connect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream connects, including automatic reconnects (server only).
.TP
.B on_upstream_disconnect(conn_id)
Called when an upstream disconnects (server only).
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Called when a new remote daemon is discovered (cluster only).
See
.BR "Cluster Event Callbacks" .
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Called when a remote daemon becomes stale or disappears (cluster only).
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Called when the member count of a group changes (cluster only).
.SS Callback Deadline
Every invocation of a Lua callback has a wall\-clock time budget. Default:
.B 10000
milliseconds. When a callback is still running at the end of its budget, the
runtime stops it with an error, exactly as if the script had called
.BR error() .
The daemon logs the reason, the callback's usual failure path runs, and the
runtime goes on serving.
.PP
The bound exists because a process serves its runtimes on one event loop, on
one thread. Without it,
.B "while true do end"
in any callback would freeze that process for good. In a worker it would stop
that runtime; in the daemon it would also stop the metrics endpoint and the IPC
socket. Only
.B "kill \-9"
would end it.
.PP
The budget applies to:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
each invocation of any
.BI on_ *
callback, separately. A periodic
.B on_tick
gets a fresh budget on every tick.
.IP \(bu 2
each invocation of a
.B socketley.set_timeout
or
.B socketley.set_interval
callback, and of a
.B socketley.subscribe
handler.
.IP \(bu 2
the whole
.B filters
chain for one request. The chain shares one budget; each filter does not get
its own.
.IP \(bu 2
the script's top level, which runs inside
.BR create .
If the top level never returns, the create is refused with a reason, so the
command does not hang the terminal.
.RE
.PP
The budget counts time spent running on the loop, not elapsed clock time. This
matters for the callbacks that can wait, such as
.BR on_message ,
.B on_client_message
and an
.B mcp
tool's handler (see
.BR socketley.http ).
They run on a coroutine. A handler that is waiting in
.B self.cache
does not hold the thread and does not use up its budget. Each stretch in which it
actually runs gets the budget. How long it may stay suspended is a separate
bound of ten seconds, with its own result. See
.B self.cache
under
.BR "Runtime Methods" .
.PP
A callback stopped at its deadline is treated as a callback that raised an
error. Wherever a raised error already has a defined result, the deadline gives
the same result:
.IP \(bu 2
.B on_auth
denies the request and says why.
.IP \(bu 2
.B on_route
falls back to the usual backend selection.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B filters
entry refuses the request with a 500.
.IP \(bu 2
In
.B on_proxy_request
and
.BR on_proxy_response ,
a timeout counts as a refusal, not as "returned nothing". The connection
closes. The proxy does not forward the bytes of a request whose hook never
finished deciding, as if the hook had let them through.
.PP
.B socketley.http
runs inside the same budget. Its
.B timeout_ms
is clamped to the time left in the callback's budget. A callback that spends its
budget waiting on a host therefore gets an ordinary
.B "ok = false"
result instead of being stopped. A script that needs a long fetch raises its
callback budget to fit. Nothing can interrupt a request in the middle of a
socket operation: the mechanism is a VM instruction hook, and a blocking call
executes no instructions.
.PP
.B Setting it.
A script sets its own budget with a top\-level
.B callback_timeout_ms
global, next to
.BR tick_ms :
.nf

callback_timeout_ms = 30000   \-\- this script does slow work on purpose
callback_timeout_ms = 0       \-\- no bound at all for this script

.fi
An operator changes the default for every script that does not set its own with
.B SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS
in the daemon's environment; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
.B What it costs.
The cost is not the JIT. On this build LuaJIT compiles traces with a count hook
installed. The cost of the hook is the hooked dispatch path, which checks on
every VM instruction instead of every ten thousandth. Measured at 64 bytes on one
connection:
.IP \(bu 2
A callback that only answers a message showed no difference above the
run\-to\-run noise: 42581 and 43130 messages a second with the hook, against
42571 and 42644 without.
.IP \(bu 2
A callback doing about 1300 arithmetic operations per message ran at 85% of its
unhooked rate.
.PP
.B What 0 buys.
Setting the bound to
.B 0
installs no hook. It also opens LuaJIT's
.B jit
library, which is the only thing that turns the trace compiler on. Without it,
every Lua callback the daemon runs is interpreted. On the same arithmetic
callback, the bounded run served 11722 messages a second and the unbounded run
28085, at 85.2 and 30.3 microseconds of worker CPU per message.
.PP
A callback that only answers a message gains very little, because there is
almost no loop to compile. On the echo fixture the two arms do not differ in
throughput: 42850 and 43929 messages a second bounded, against 43577 and 44350
unbounded. That gap is smaller than the spread inside either arm. What does
differ is Lua's own share of the worker's CPU: 5.2 and 5.1% bounded, against 3.9
and 4.0% unbounded. That is the compiler doing a little work on two bytecodes.
End to end this is about 1%, against 2.4x on a callback that computes.
.PP
A streaming producer falls between those two cases. It matters because it is the
shape that runs per byte rather than per message. A handler that generates
40,000 CSV rows into
.B self.send_chunk
gains 25 to 48 per cent, not 2.4x. The profile shows why. With the hook
installed, the interpreter takes 92.7 per cent of the worker's core. About 35
per cent of that is spent in
.BR lj_str_new ,
.BR lj_str_resize ,
.B gc_sweepstr
and the allocator. A compiled trace allocates and interns every string it
builds exactly as the interpreter does. The compiler removes the dispatch cost
but leaves the string cost in place.
.PP
So the way the script builds its strings is the larger lever, and the two
effects multiply rather than add. The table shows the same handler using
.B string.format
instead of a chain of
.B ..
concatenations, at 8 concurrent requests and then at 64:
.IP
.nf
                        bounded      unbounded
\&.. concatenation        97.8/s      130.6/s   (+34%)
string.format           93.8/s      160.0/s   (+71%)
\&.. at 64 concurrent      68.0/s      100.8/s   (+48%)
string.format at 64     71.5/s      142.7/s  (+100%)
.fi
.IP
Both arms put byte\-identical responses on the wire. The unbounded column is the
one that changes. In the interpreter,
.B string.format
gains nothing: it is 4 per cent behind concatenation, inside the spread, because
there each row costs one C call either way.
.B string.format
pays off only when the compiler is on, and then it pays twice. It builds one
string per row instead of a chain of intermediate strings, and it uses an
integer formatter instead of the double formatter, which alone took 15.6 per
cent of the core. Compare the default with concatenation against the compiler
with
.BR string.format .
End to end, the second gives 64 per cent more throughput at 8 streams and 110 per cent more at 64, for
about half the CPU per request.
.PP
A C server that builds the same rows with
.BR snprintf (3)
still serves 2.4x the best of those results. The choice is therefore about how
much of a stream's work belongs in Lua at all. No setting closes that gap.
.PP
.B What decides the size of the gain
is how much of the work happens at the Lua level, not how many bytes pass
through. This was measured on a proxy, a second runtime type with a per\(hybyte
hook. Both handlers forward the bytes unchanged, so only the work differs:
.IP
.nf
                              bounded   unbounded   CPU per request
one C library call per chunk    120.9      121.9     8223 -> 8155 us
a Lua loop over every row       417.1     1452.0     2378 ->  669 us
.fi
.IP
The first handler runs one
.B gsub
over each chunk and gains one per cent. The call is C either way, and there is
almost no Lua around it to compile. The second walks the rows in a Lua
.B while
loop and gains 248 per cent, with 72 per cent less CPU. Every runtime type reads
this setting from the same place. The five shapes measured so far therefore
rank by that one question, how much work is done in Lua:
.IP \(bu 2
about one per cent for a C call per byte, or for a callback that only answers;
.IP \(bu 2
34 to 48 per cent for building strings, where the allocator and the intern
table are the cost and the compiler cannot remove them;
.IP \(bu 2
2.4x for arithmetic;
.IP \(bu 2
248 per cent for Lua control flow over many items.
.PP
.B How the reply is assembled decides both,
and the number of pieces decides whether it matters at all. The tables below
compare four idioms that build one reply out of N parts. They ran on a server,
with one binary and one C client, interleaved. In the N = 1000 table, each pair
of rows is the same script with the compiler closed and then open:
.IP
.nf
  N = 24, compiler closed       exchanges/s   spread
    s = s .. piece                 160,495      22%
    s = s .. string.format(..)     153,658      24%
    t[i] = piece, concat once      159,721      23%
    t[i] = format, concat once     166,023      15%
.fi
.IP
This table shows no real difference: every spread is larger than every gap.
Assemble a short reply in whatever way reads best.
.B string.format
is not faster here.
.IP
.nf
  N = 1000                      exchanges/s   us cpu/req   spread
    s = s .. piece                   1,251         801      6%
                  compiler open      1,426         703      6%
    s = s .. string.format(..)         923       1,087      8%
                  compiler open        843       1,192      5%
    t[i] = piece, concat once        7,618         131      6%
                  compiler open     13,361          75      2%
    t[i] = format, concat once       8,656         115      7%
                  compiler open     17,675          57      5%
.fi
.IP
At a thousand pieces, the best shape serves 14.1 times as many exchanges as the
worst, at a fourteenth of the CPU per request. Joining once accounts for most of
that: 6.1x on its own. The formatter adds a further 14 per cent on top of joining
once. When the formatter is used to accumulate instead, it is 26 per cent
.IR slower .
The same call has opposite effects, depending on the code around it.
.IP
The effect on the compiler is the part worth remembering. Opening the compiler
gains 14 per cent on the accumulating shape and nothing outside the spread on
the accumulating shape that formats. It gains 75 and 104 per cent on the two
shapes that join once. This is the rule above seen from the other side.
Accumulating moves the work out of Lua and into the allocator, where the
compiler cannot reach it. A script that is slow because of its idiom does not
become fast because of this setting.
.PP
.B What 0 gives up.
A compiled trace never calls the instruction hook, so with the compiler on, a
runaway callback cannot be interrupted at all. In a measurement,
.B "while true do end"
with a 300 millisecond deadline was stopped after 300.2 milliseconds with the
compiler off, and never with the compiler on. A hard ceiling inside the hook did
not help either, because the hook is never reached from inside a trace. That is
why the bound and the compiler are one setting, not two knobs that can disagree.
.PP
A runaway callback affects one runtime, not the whole daemon. In a measurement
with a server whose callback never returns, that server's worker process sat at
83% of a core. Meanwhile a second server on the same daemon went on echoing, and
the daemon went on answering
.BR ps .
A runaway in the script's top level is always refused, whatever
.B callback_timeout_ms
says. The top level runs under the default budget, before the script's own
setting has been read.
.PP
.B What it does not bound.
A callback stopped at its deadline has still held the loop for that long. A CLI
command issued during the stall can hit its own five second receive timeout and
report the daemon unreachable; the command succeeds on retry. A runaway
.B on_tick
is stopped on every tick, not once. Name resolution inside
.B socketley.http
executes no VM instructions, so it is not interrupted. Instead, the call gives up
on it at the call's deadline.
.SS Proxy Filter Chain
This applies to a proxy in HTTP mode only.
.B filters
is a global array of functions that the proxy runs in order on each request,
\fIbefore\fR it selects a backend. Each filter either returns nothing, and the
next filter runs, or returns a response, which ends the request there.
.PP
The order is the point. A request that a filter refuses never reaches backend
selection. No endpoint is resolved, no circuit breaker's half-open probe is
claimed, no pooled socket is used and no connection is opened. Authentication,
rate limiting and header rewriting belong here.
.B on_route
runs after a backend has been chosen, and answers a different question.
.PP
The chain is independent of
.BR \-\-strategy .
It runs whatever selects the backend afterwards, and it also runs on a proxy
with no
.B on_route
at all. A proxy whose script declares no
.B filters
array pays nothing for the chain: the chain is guarded by the same callback mask
as every other hook.
.PP
Each filter is called with one argument:
.nf
method      string   HTTP method ("GET", "POST", ...)
path        string   Request path as the client sent it, this proxy's
                     prefix included \(em the same path on_route sees
version     string   HTTP version (e.g. "HTTP/1.1")
client_id   number   File descriptor of the client; pass it to
                     self.peer_ip() to identify the peer
headers     table    Request headers. Names are lowercased, values
                     are trimmed, and a field sent more than once is
                     joined with ", ".
.fi
.PP
And returns one of three things:
.TP
.B nil
The filter has no opinion (returning nothing means the same), and the next
filter runs.
.TP
.BI "{ status = " N ", body = " string ", headers = " table " }"
The response to send. The chain stops, the request is never forwarded, and this
response goes to the client.
.I status
must be 200\-599.
.I body
and
.I headers
are optional. The proxy closes the connection afterwards, as it does for every
other response it writes itself.
.TP
.BI "{ set_headers = " table " }"
Continue, with these headers set on the request that goes to the backend. A
header set here replaces a client header of the same name instead of being
joined to it. The proxy sends it with a lowercased name.
.TP
.BI "{ remove_headers = { " name ", " name ", ... } }"
Continue, with these headers removed from the request before it goes to the
backend. Every occurrence of each name is removed, so a field the client sent
twice disappears entirely. Use it for a header the backend must never see,
whatever the client sent: an internal trust header that a caller forged, or a
cookie that has no business past the edge.
.I set_headers
and
.I remove_headers
may appear in the same table. When a name is both set and removed, the later of
the two statements wins, in the same way that two
.I set_headers
for one name are resolved.
.PP
A filter that returns anything else, or that raises an error, refuses the
request with 500. A filter that did not say "continue" is never treated as if it
had. For the same reason, an entry in
.B filters
that is not a function makes the whole script fail to load: a silently dropped
filter looks exactly like one that lets everybody in.
.PP
Some header names cannot be set \fIor removed\fR by a filter. The proxy drops
them and writes a line to stderr:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Content\-Length
and
.B Transfer\-Encoding
on either side. The proxy forwards exactly the body that the parsed request
declared. A filter that changed the length would make the proxy and the backend
disagree about where the request ends.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Host
on the request, because the proxy rewrites it to the endpoint it selected.
.IP \(bu 2
the hop-by-hop set
.RB ( Connection ", " Keep\-Alive ", " TE ", " Trailer ", " Upgrade ", " Proxy\-* ).
.PP
The proxy also drops any name that is not a bare token and any value that
contains CR or LF, because a filter often echoes something the client supplied.
.PP
Filters run on the event loop thread. Every request on the proxy waits for
whatever a filter does, so a filter must not block, do synchronous I/O or run
long loops.
.PP
Example:
.nf
filters = {
    function(req)
        if not req.headers["authorization"] then
            return {
                status  = 401,
                body    = "authentication required\\n",
                headers = {["WWW-Authenticate"] = "Bearer"},
            }
        end
        return {set_headers = {["x-authenticated-user"] = "alice"}}
    end,
}
.fi
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/filter\-chain.lua
for authentication, per-IP rate limiting and header rewriting as three filters
in one chain.
.SS Declared Routes
.B routes
is a global array in which a runtime states what it answers: each entry is a path
and the methods allowed on it. The array is declared in the \fIhandler's\fR
script. It is published to the proxies that forward to that runtime. Those
proxies then refuse other methods at the edge, before any endpoint is resolved,
any socket is taken from the pool or any connection is opened.
.PP
The declaration lives with the handler on purpose. A method list configured on
the proxy would be a second copy of something the handler already states in its
own code, in another runtime and soon in another process. Two copies drift
apart. For example, the handler adds
.BR PATCH ,
the gateway goes on refusing it, and neither side alone explains the 405.
Nothing is configured on the proxy. To change what the gateway enforces, edit
the handler's script and run
.BR "socketley reload\-lua" .
.PP
.nf
routes = {
    { path = "/endpoint/login", methods = {"POST", "GET"} },
    { path = "/static/*",       methods = {"GET"} },
}
.fi
.PP
.B path
is the path as the \fIhandler\fR sees it, with the proxy's own
.BI / name /
prefix already stripped. A query string is not part of it. A trailing
.B *
makes the path a literal prefix; there is no other pattern syntax. An exact
match wins over a prefix, and among prefixes the longest one wins.
.B methods
is a list of method names; a single method may be given as a bare string. The
proxy uppercases the declared methods when the script loads, so
.B "methods = {\(dqpost\(dq}"
declares POST. The method a client sends is compared exactly as sent. RFC 9110
9.1 makes the method token case-sensitive, and the handler would also refuse a
request for
.BR post .
.PP
What the proxy does with the declaration:
.TP
A declared method
is forwarded, as always.
.TP
An undeclared method on a declared path
is answered with
.B 405 Method Not Allowed
and an
.B Allow
header that lists the methods that would have worked. No backend is involved.
.TP
.B HEAD
is allowed wherever
.B GET
is (RFC 9110 9.3.2). A handler that serves GET serves HEAD, whether or not it
listed HEAD.
.TP
.B OPTIONS
is never refused. A plain OPTIONS request ("what may I do here", RFC 9110 9.3.7)
is answered with
.B 204
and an
.B Allow
header built from the declaration. A CORS \fIpreflight\fR (an OPTIONS carrying
.B Origin
and
.BR Access\-Control\-Request\-Method )
is always forwarded to the handler instead. The declaration says nothing about
origins, and an answer without
.B Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin
breaks the preflight just as a 405 would: the browser never sends the real
request and reports a CORS error. A handler that declares
.B OPTIONS
itself always receives it.
.TP
A path nobody declared
is forwarded, whatever the method. A declaration covers only the paths it names
and says nothing about the others. It is not a whitelist: declaring one route
does not hide the rest.
.TP
A backend given as \fIhost:port\fR
has no declaration and can never have one, because the published snapshot is
keyed by runtime name and by group. The proxy forwards to such a backend as
before.
.PP
For a proxy with several backends, or with an
.BI @ group
backend, the declarations are merged under one rule: the proxy may not refuse
anything that \fIeither\fR possible target might have served.
.IP \(bu 2
A path that only some of the targets declare is dropped, because a runtime that
never mentioned it may well serve it.
.IP \(bu 2
A path that all of the targets declare keeps the \fIunion\fR of their methods,
because the proxy does not know which target a request will reach.
.PP
So if one backend or group member declares nothing, that proxy enforces nothing
at all. This is silent and deliberate: that target has said nothing about what
it answers, so nothing can be refused on its behalf.
.PP
.B "Edge rejection is not input validation."
The proxy can confirm that the method is one the handler declared. It cannot
confirm that the body is a well-formed login. The proxy is also not the only way
in: a socketley server listens on its own port, and a request that arrives there
passed no edge at all. A handler that stops checking its input because "the
gateway checks" is safe only while every request comes through the gateway. That
is a property of the deployment, not of the code. Declare routes to save the
round trip and to state your intent, and keep validating input.
.PP
.B socketley stats \fIproxy\fR
reports
.BR declared_routes ,
the number of routes that proxy actually enforces. Check it first when a method
that should be refused is not. From the client side, all of these look the
same, and all of them show 0 here:
.IP \(bu 2
a backend that declares nothing;
.IP \(bu 2
a backend named by address;
.IP \(bu 2
a script that failed to load;
.IP \(bu 2
a proxy whose handler had not started yet.
.PP
A malformed entry makes the script fail to load, with the reason on stderr; the
entry is not silently dropped. A declaration that differs from the one in the
file would be invisible from both sides. A
.B routes
table with no entry at index 1 is refused too. It must be an array, and
.B routes = {["/a"] = {"GET"}}
would otherwise load and declare nothing.
.PP
Neither end has to run in the daemon. A proxy in its own worker process enforces
declarations the same way: the table reaches it in the endpoint frame that
already carries the addresses. A
.I handler
in a worker also declares routes the same way, which is less obvious. The
declaration is produced by running the script, and only the worker runs it. So
the worker sends it back to the daemon in a
.B routes
frame on the control socket, and the placeholder publishes the worker's copy.
Where either runtime runs makes no difference to which methods are refused.
.IP
See
.B examples/proxy/declared\-routes.lua
for a handler that declares an AJAX endpoint and the gateway in front of it.
.SS Runtime Methods (in Lua)
.nf
-- Every type
self.name      -- Runtime name (read-only)
self.port      -- Port number (read-only)
self.state     -- Current state (read-only)
self.type      -- Runtime type (read-only)
self.connections()         -- Current connection count (all four types).
                              With --shards, the whole runtime's count summed
                              across its worker processes, not the shard's own

self.protocol  -- server/client: "tcp" or "udp"; proxy: "tcp" or "http"
               -- (not set on a cache)
self.send(msg)             -- Send message (client)
self.send(client_id, msg)  -- Send to specific client (server)
self.broadcast(msg)        -- Broadcast to all clients (server).  Answers two
                           -- numbers: how many took it, and how many were
                           -- dropped because their queue was full.  See below.
self.pending(client_id)    -- Queued for that client and not yet on the wire,
                           -- in bytes (server); nil if the id is not one of
                           -- this runtime's connections.  See below.
self.respond_stream(id, status, headers)
                           -- Begin a response this script will write itself,
                           -- chunk by chunk (server).  See below.
self.send_chunk(id, data)  -- One chunk of it; false means it was not taken
self.end_response(id)      -- The last chunk has gone; finish the response
self.watermark()           -- The byte count on_writable fires on the way down
                           -- through (server and client).  Pace against this,
                           -- not a literal.  A call and not a field, because
                           -- --write-buffer is editable.  See on_writable.
on_http_body(id, chunk, n) -- A request body streamed, rather than gathered
                           -- whole (server).  Defining it is the opt-in.
self.pending()             -- The client's own form, without an id: a client
                           -- has one connection.  Bytes queued for its server
                           -- and not yet on the wire.

-- ─── Sending faster than the socket ───
-- A write is queued, not performed, and the queue has a bound.  Reach it and
-- the runtime closes the connection -- which used to be the only way a script
-- found out: the message that filled the queue was dropped and answered true,
-- and the one after it was refused with "there is no open connection", the
-- sentence a peer hanging up gets.
--
-- self.send() now answers false for a message that was not taken, and names
-- the backlog when that is the reason, so a producer can tell "slow down" from
-- "it is gone".  self.pending() is the number to watch BEFORE sending, which
-- is the difference between pacing and a post-mortem:
--
--     if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end
--
-- and on_writable(id) is how a producer that stopped there is started again,
-- which is what makes a body past the bound sendable rather than merely
-- survivable.  See on_writable in CALLBACKS.
--
-- self.broadcast() has the same bound at its own door, and used to report
-- nothing at all: a broadcast to 3000 clients that dropped a third of them was
-- indistinguishable from one that reached every single one.  It answers two
-- numbers now --
--
--     local taken, behind = self.broadcast(msg)
--
-- counting THIS shard's connections, which is the scope a script can act on; a
-- held message counts as taken, because an unclassified connection's hold is a
-- deferred take and not a drop.  And a client dropped by the broadcast gets
-- the backlog sentence from a later self.send() rather than the peer's, which
-- it did not before -- one flag, both entrances.
--
-- Measured with a client that connects and never reads, against 400 chunks of
-- 64 KB: 129 taken, 271 refused, and the queue standing at its bound.

-- ─── A response written rather than returned ───
-- on_http_request has to RETURN its body, so a generated answer had to fit in
-- memory.  A FILE of any size was already served by the splice path and a Range
-- of it by the 206 path; a generated one of any size was not expressible at all
-- -- transcoding, an export assembled from a cache, a CSV of ten million rows.
--
--     self.respond_stream(id, 200, {["Content-Type"] = "text/csv"})
--     for row in rows() do
--         if (self.pending(id) or 0) >= self.watermark() then return end   -- wait
--         self.send_chunk(id, row)
--     end
--     self.end_response(id)
--
-- Paced through self.pending()/on_writable like any other producer, which is
-- what makes it a stream rather than a slower way to run out of memory.
-- Measured: 200 MB of generated rows through an 8 MiB queue, delivered in
-- 51,200 chunks with on_writable resuming it 67 times, for 6 MB of resident
-- memory.
--
-- The handler returns nothing after starting one: the response is already on
-- its way, and the static-file and 404 fallbacks are skipped for this request.
-- A handler that only tried to send a chunk has NOT answered, so its ordinary
-- return still applies.
--
-- Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding and Connection are dropped from the header
-- table if given: a Content-Length beside a chunked body is a message carrying
-- two answers to its own length, which this server refuses with a 400 on the
-- way in.  Measured with it passed through: the client read 119 bytes where 5
-- were sent, having believed the wrong header.
--
-- send_chunk on a HEAD request answers false and names it, rather than dropping
-- the bytes: a HEAD asked for the headers and nothing else, and a script that
-- thinks it is sending a body to one has a bug this refusal is the only report
-- of.  An empty chunk is accepted and not sent, because a zero-length chunk is
-- how the terminator is spelled on the wire -- passing one through as payload
-- would end the response while the script was still writing it.

.fi
.PP
.nf
-- A question whose answer is somewhere else (server with --cache)
self.cache(command)        -- Run one command against the LINKED cache and
                           -- answer with what it said.  The handler STOPS here
                           -- and goes on when the reply arrives; the daemon
                           -- keeps serving meanwhile.  nil plus a reason when
                           -- the answer is not coming.

-- ─── A handler that waits ───
-- In a handler that can wait, a call that reaches outside the runtime
-- (self.cache, socketley.http) suspends the handler until the answer comes,
-- and the runtime keeps serving other clients meanwhile.
--
--     function on_client_message(id, msg)
--         local v, err = self.cache("get " .. msg)
--         self.send(id, v or ("miss: " .. err))
--     end
--
-- The handler runs on a coroutine.  At the call it suspends, the request goes
-- to the cache, and the event loop carries on with every other connection and
-- every other runtime; when the reply lands the handler picks up at the same
-- line with the answer as that call's return value.  Nothing else in the script
-- changes, and a handler that never calls it is not affected: a callback that
-- runs straight through costs the same as it did, measured at 0.158 us against
-- 0.161 for the plain call it replaced, because the coroutines are pooled.
--
-- Where it may be called from: on_message and on_client_message.  Anywhere
-- else -- a timer, on_http_request, a filter -- answers nil and says so,
-- because those call sites still expect an answer on the spot.
--
-- Three things are guaranteed, and each exists because the shape without it
-- was reachable:
--
--   * It always returns.  A cache that accepts the request and never answers
--     leaves the handler stopped for ever otherwise -- measured against a
--     SIGSTOPped cache worker, whose request is parked against a ticket nothing
--     completes.  After ten seconds the call answers nil, "timeout".
--
--   * The connection is still there.  While the handler waits, the connection
--     it is answering is held open exactly as a client's own `cache get` is:
--     a promised reply, which the close path defers on.  Without it every
--     `echo | nc` fails, because the peer half-closes the moment it has sent
--     its line and the answer does not exist yet.
--
--   * It cannot pile up.  4096 stopped callbacks per runtime, and a server that
--     is already owed as many answers as one connection may have refuses the
--     call with a reason rather than stopping the handler.
--
-- The command is the cache's own text protocol, so the answer is that
-- protocol's: `get` on a key that is not there answers the string "nil" rather
-- than a Lua nil, which is the same answer a client reading the port gets.
.fi
.PP
.nf
-- Upstream actions (server with -u)
self.upstream_send(conn_id, msg) -- Send to a specific upstream; false means
                                 -- the bytes are not going.  See below.
self.upstream_broadcast(msg)     -- Send to all connected upstreams.  Answers how
                                 -- many took it and how many were dropped
self.upstream_pending(conn_id)   -- Bytes queued for that upstream and not yet on
                                 -- the wire; nil if the id is not one of this
                                 -- runtime's upstreams
self.upstreams()                 -- Table of connected upstream conn_ids

-- ─── The same bound, pointed outwards ───
-- An upstream's write queue fills exactly as a client's does, and these used to
-- report none of it.  upstream_send() tested only whether the upstream was
-- connected; a queue that reached the bound sets `closing` and leaves
-- `connected` alone, so every later send answered TRUE for ever while nothing
-- left the process.  Measured, 400 sends of 64 KB at an upstream that accepted
-- the connection and then read nothing:
--
--     before   sent=400  failed=0    with 8,396,928 bytes standing in the queue
--     after    sent=129  failed=271  the same 129/271 the client-facing side
--                                    reports, because it is the same bound
--
-- The refusal names the backlog apart from a missing upstream, because "is not
-- connected" is false of a connected upstream whose queue this runtime filled --
-- and the two want opposite reactions.  Pace with self.upstream_pending() and
-- not self.pending(): that one resolves a CLIENT id, and an upstream is in
-- another map entirely.
--
-- on_upstream_writable(conn_id) is the other half, and without it the number
-- above is only a post-mortem: a producer could ask how deep the queue was and
-- never be told when there was room.  The same 400 sends of 64 KB, at an
-- upstream that reads:
--
--     unpaced   sent=129  failed=271   and the upstream closed
--     paced     sent=400  refused=0    resumed 6 times
--
-- Same messages, same bound, same upstream.  129 of 400 is what a producer that
-- does not look gets, and it is not a limit on how much can be sent.

-- Cache: mode
-- A call and not a field, because --mode is editable and a field written when
-- this table was built would go stale.  "readonly", "readwrite" or "admin" --
-- the same three names --mode, `show` and the wire protocol use.
self.cache_mode()   -- Which mode this cache is in

-- Cache: which keys are this process's
-- Under --shards the script runs in all N workers and the calls below act only
-- on the slice this one owns, answering a foreign key the way they answer a
-- refusal.  True for every key when the cache is one process.
self.owns(key)      -- Does this shard own the key?

-- Cache: strings
self.get(key)       -- Get string value
self.set(key, val)  -- Set string value; false in readonly mode
self.del(key)       -- Delete key (any type); false in readonly mode
self.exists(key)    -- Is the key there?  See below.

-- Cache: lists
self.lpush(key, val)        -- Push to front; false in readonly mode
self.rpush(key, val)        -- Push to back; false in readonly mode
self.lpop(key)              -- Pop from front; "" in readonly mode
self.rpop(key)              -- Pop from back; "" in readonly mode
self.llen(key)              -- List length

-- Cache: sets
self.sadd(key, member)      -- 1 added, 0 already a member,
                            -- nil refused (readonly mode)
self.srem(key, member)      -- Remove member; false in readonly mode
self.sismember(key, member) -- Check membership
self.scard(key)             -- Set cardinality

-- Cache: hashes
self.hset(key, field, val)  -- Set hash field; false in readonly mode
self.hget(key, field)       -- Get hash field
self.hdel(key, field)       -- Delete hash field; false in readonly mode
self.hexists(key, field)    -- Is the field there?  See below.
self.hlen(key)              -- Hash field count

-- Cache: TTL
self.expire(key, seconds)   -- Set expiry; false in readonly mode
self.ttl(key)               -- Get remaining TTL
self.persist(key)           -- Remove expiry; false in readonly mode

-- Cache: pub/sub
-- Under --shards the message reaches every shard's subscribers, but the count
-- is this shard's own: the siblings answer on a later turn and this call
-- answers on the turn it was made.
self.publish(channel, msg)  -- Publish to channel; returns this shard's count

-- Server: connection control
-- Every mutator below answers `true`, or `false, "reason"`.  They returned
-- nothing at all before, so an id that named no connection was a silent no-op.
self.disconnect(client_id)         -- Terminate a connection from Lua
self.peer_ip(client_id)            -- Get client IP (IPv4 or IPv6 string)
                                   -- also on a proxy, same meaning
self.ws_headers(client_id)         -- Get WS upgrade headers table (nil for TCP)

-- Server: client enumeration and multicast
self.clients()                     -- Return table of all connected client_ids
                                   -- also on a proxy, same meaning
self.multicast(ids, msg)           -- Send msg to a subset of clients (ids = array of
                                   -- client_ids).  Returns how many were reached.
                                   -- An entry that is not a whole number refuses the
                                   -- whole call rather than being dropped from it.

-- Server: named groups
self.join_group(id, group)         -- Add client to a named group
self.leave_group(id, group)        -- Remove client from a group
self.group_send(group, msg)        -- Send msg to all clients in a group.  Returns how
                                   -- many were reached; 0 is a group with nobody in it.

-- Server: zero-copy pipe pairing (bypasses Lua callbacks)
self.pipe(id_a, id_b)             -- Pair two clients: data forwarded directly between them
self.unpipe(id)                    -- Remove pipe pairing for a client
socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)   -- Pair two connections of one server
socketley.pipe(src, fd, dst)           -- Route a connection to another server.
                                   -- Despite the name it hands no descriptor over:
                                   -- src keeps the socket and relays the bytes

-- Server: per-connection metadata
self.set_data(id, key, val)        -- Set string metadata on a connection (nil val deletes key)
self.get_data(id, key)             -- Get metadata string or nil

-- Server: client routing
self.route(client_id, target)      -- Forward client to sub-server, in this or
                                   -- any other process. This server keeps the
                                   -- socket and relays; the target answers.
self.unroute(client_id)            -- Stop forwarding
self.get_route(client_id)          -- Get current route (string or nil)

-- Server: owner-targeted sending (sub-server only)
self.owner_send(client_id, msg)    -- Send to a connection routed here, through
                                   -- the runtime that owns it. Returns false if
                                   -- that connection has gone.
self.owner_broadcast(msg)          -- Broadcast to all owner's clients

-- Proxy
self.clients()                     -- Table of connected client_ids
self.peer_ip(client_id)            -- Client IP (IPv4 or IPv6 string)
self.close_client(client_id)       -- Close one client connection
self.backends()                    -- Array of backend addresses, in the
                                   -- order on_route indexes them (1-based
                                   -- in Lua; on_route returns 0-based)
self.backend_health()              -- Circuit-breaker state per backend,
                                   -- same 1-based order:
                                   -- "closed" | "open" | "half_open"
self.stats()                       -- {connections, bytes_in, bytes_out,
                                   --  total_connections, total_messages,
                                   --  backend_count}
.fi
.PP
.B "Why exists and hexists are there."
.B self.get
returns the value, and returns the empty string both for an absent key and for a
key that holds an empty string. A script cannot tell the two apart from
.B self.get
alone, and this page's own example relies on that:
.I examples/cache/cache\-integration\-handler.lua
reads
.B "self.get(\(dqconfig:timeout\(dq) == \(dq\(dq"
as "not set yet".
.PP
Making
.B get
return
.B nil
for an absent key would answer the question, but it would break every script that
reads the result that way. An empty string is also a value a cache may
legitimately hold. So
.B exists
and
.B hexists
ask the question separately, under the names the wire protocol already uses.
.nf
.fi
.PP
.B "The proxy table is smaller than the server's on purpose."
A proxy relays between a client and a backend and speaks for neither. It has no
message of its own to put on the wire, so it has no
.BR broadcast() ,
no
.B send()
and no per\-connection metadata. To change what crosses a proxy, use
.B on_proxy_request
and
.BR on_proxy_response .
To refuse a request, use the filter chain.
.PP
.B self.backends()
is indexed from 1, like every Lua array, but
.B on_route
returns an index from 0. So when
.B on_route
returns
.BR n ,
the selected address is
.BR "self.backends()[n + 1]" .
.PP
.B self.owner_send()
accepts only a client_id that this runtime was actually given: one that arrived
through
.B self.route()
and was reported by
.BR on_connect() .
It returns
.B false
for any other id, including a client_id whose connection has since closed. A
script that holds an id after its connection has ended therefore cannot write to
whichever client later received that descriptor.
.SS Global Functions
.TP
.B socketley.log(msg)
Writes one line to the daemon's log at
.B INFO
level, prefixed with the name of the runtime that wrote it:
.BR "[12:04:31] [INFO] [lua my\-server] ..." .
The line goes through the daemon's logger, so
.B log_level
in the configuration file can suppress it like any other message. At the default
level, every line is shown. Every Lua diagnostic carries the same
.B [lua <runtime>]
prefix. The prefix is what separates two scripts that share one stderr. Two
runtimes often share one script file, so the file name in an error does not
identify the runtime.
.TP
.B socketley.set_timeout(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Calls
.I fn
once after
.I ms
milliseconds, using an io_uring timeout SQE.
Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_timeout() .
The timer shares the event loop with the runtime and uses no extra threads.
.TP
.B socketley.set_interval(ms, fn) \(-> timer_id
Calls
.I fn
every
.I ms
milliseconds. Returns an integer timer ID that can be passed to
.BR socketley.clear_interval() .
.IP
Both kinds of timer are cancelled when the runtime stops, and are
.B not
restored if it is started again. A stopped runtime executes no script, so its
timers do not survive the stop. This includes a timer armed inside
.BR on_stop ,
which is cancelled with the rest. The script registers its timers at its top
level, which runs once, when the runtime's worker is forked. A
.B start
after a
.B stop
forks a new worker, which runs the top level again and so arms the timers again.
Nothing the previous worker held is carried over: the timer is not restored, but
re-armed by a script that runs from the beginning.
.IP
Both functions return
.B 0
and arm nothing when
.I ms
is not greater than zero, when the runtime has no event loop, or when the
runtime has been stopped. The last case covers a script that calls
.B socketley.stop
on its own runtime and carries on. Since
.B stop
returns to the script, everything after that line runs on a runtime that is
already down. A
.B start
lifts the stopped state before
.BR on_start ,
so a restarted runtime arms its timers exactly as it did the first time.
.B socketley.subscribe
returns 0 in the same situation, for the same reason. Real ids start at 1, so
.B 0
is unambiguous. The reason is written to the daemon's log, because a timer that
will never fire would otherwise fail silently.
.TP
.B socketley.clear_timeout(timer_id)
Cancels a pending timeout, so the timer does not fire. Calling it with an
already-expired or invalid ID is safe and does nothing.
.TP
.B socketley.clear_interval(timer_id)
Cancels a repeating interval, so the timer does not fire again. Calling it with an already-expired or invalid ID is safe and does
nothing.
.IP
Both functions remove the timer completely rather than just silencing it. The
io_uring timeout is removed, the timer's storage is released, and the reference
to the callback is dropped. The closure and everything it captured can then be
garbage collected at once, not when the timer would have expired.
.IP
Both are safe to call from inside the timer's own callback. That is how to write
a repeating timer that fires only once.
.TP
.B socketley.json_encode(value) \(-> string | false, why
Encodes a Lua value (table, string, number, boolean, nil) as a JSON string.
Tables with consecutive integer keys starting at 1 are encoded as JSON
arrays; all other tables as objects.
.IP
Object keys are written in sorted order, so encoding equal data twice gives the
same string. A Lua table has no order of its own: a table filled with
.IR alpha ", " beta ", " gamma ", " delta
may iterate as
.IR beta ", " gamma ", " delta ", " alpha .
Without sorting, two tables holding the same data could encode differently.
Anything that compares encoded documents depends on this: an ETag, a cache key,
a signature, a deduplication hash, or a diff of two logged payloads.
.IP
Returns
.BR false " and a reason when the value cannot be written as a document:"
a table that contains itself (directly or through any chain of fields), or a
table nested deeper than 128 levels. A table reachable twice by different paths
is not a cycle and encodes normally.
.IP
A string is truthy and
.B false
is not. So code such as
.B "local s = socketley.json_encode(t)"
needs no change, and
.B "if not s then"
tests for the refusal.
.TP
.B socketley.json_decode(str) \(-> value | false, why
Decodes a JSON string into a Lua value. Objects become tables with string
keys, arrays become tables with integer keys (1-based). Null becomes nil.
.IP
Returns
.BR false " and a reason for a document nested deeper than 128 levels, for"
bytes that are not a complete JSON document, and for trailing data after the
document ends.
.IP
Refusing malformed input is what makes the return value trustworthy. Without
it, a handler could not tell a truncated body from a complete one. For example,
.B [1,2
(a request body cut short by a dropped connection) would decode to an ordinary
two-element table, and
.B {"a":}
to an empty table.
.BR nul ", " garbage
and an empty string would all decode to nil, the same result as a document that
really is
.BR null .
.IP
The depth limit is not a formatting preference. This call is meant for data
from the network, such as a request body or a message payload, so whoever sent
the data chooses its nesting depth, and the decoder recurses to follow it.
Without the limit, a few hundred kilobytes of
.B [[[[
took the whole daemon down. No callback deadline covers this case: deadlines
measure Lua execution, and this recursion is native code.
.TP
.B socketley.subscribe(cache_name, channel, fn) \(-> subscription_id
Registers
.I fn(channel, message)
to be called whenever the named cache runtime publishes to
.IR channel .
The function is called on the subscribing runtime's event loop turn, not from
the cache itself. When several subscriptions exist for the same channel, all of
them fire, in registration order.
.IP
Returns an integer id for
.BR socketley.unsubscribe() .
Ids start at 1 and are never reused, so
.B 0
means the call was refused. The runtime has been stopped, and this registration
would have outlived the stop that ended the others. See
.B socketley.set_interval
for the details: both calls refuse in the same cases, and a
.B start
lifts the refusal for both.
.IP
Messages are delivered only to a
.B running
runtime. Every registration a runtime holds is ended when it stops. A runtime
that has never been started receives nothing either. Its top level has run, so
the registration exists, but a runtime that is not running executes no script.
The same rule cancels the timers. Both come back when the runtime is started
again in a worker: a
.B start
forks a new worker, which runs the top level and
.B on_start
again (see
.BR socketley.set_interval ).
A server, client, proxy, cache, storage or dns runtime made by
.B socketley.create
runs in the daemon instead.
There a
.B start
runs
.B on_start
but not the top level, so a subscription made in
.B on_start
comes back and a timer armed at the top level does not.
.TP
.B socketley.unsubscribe(subscription_id) \(-> bool
Ends one subscription. Returns
.B true
when it removed a live one, and
.B false
when the id names none (never issued, or already unsubscribed).
.IP
The id names one registration, not a channel, so two handlers on the same
channel can be ended independently. It is safe to call from inside a
subscriber's own callback; that is how to write a subscriber that runs only
once. The removal takes effect from the next message, not in the middle of
delivering the current one.
.IP
Unsubscribing explicitly matters while the runtime is up.
.B stop
ends all subscriptions, and
.B remove
and
.B reload\-lua
destroy the context they live in. But none of those happens to a runtime that is
simply serving. A script that subscribes from a callback that runs more than
once, such as
.BR on_tick ,
registers the same handler again on every call unless it unsubscribes. Each
publish is then delivered to every copy.
.TP
.B socketley.mqtt.publish(runtime, topic, payload [, {qos=, retain=}] [, fn]) \(-> true | nil, why
Publishes a message on an mqtt runtime of this daemon, as if the broker itself
had published it. Returns true once the broker has the message. See
.B From the other runtimes
under
.BR "MQTT FLAGS" .
.TP
.B socketley.mail.send(runtime, {to=, cc=, bcc=, from=, reply_to=, subject=, text=, html=, headers=, attachments=, raw=} [, fn]) \(-> id | nil, why
Has a mail runtime of this daemon write a message and queue it. Returns the
message's queue id once it is in the spool.
.BR to ,
.B cc
and
.B bcc
each take one address or a list of addresses. Each attachment is a table
.BR {name=,type=,data=} .
See
.BR "MAIL FLAGS" .
.TP
.B socketley.http(opts) \(-> table
Makes an HTTP or HTTPS request and returns the response. In a callback that can
wait, the callback suspends at the call, and the runtime serves everything else
until the answer arrives. The callbacks that can wait are the ones
.B socketley.db
may be called from without a function, and an
.B mcp
tool's handler. Anywhere else
.RB ( on_start ,
.BR on_stop ,
a timer,
.BR on_http_request ,
a filter, an
.B mcp
resource or prompt), the call blocks the event loop thread until the exchange
ends. In those places, use it only in
.BR on_start / on_stop
or in low-frequency
.B on_tick
callbacks.
.PP
.I opts
table keys:
.nf
url         string   Request URL (http:// or https://)
method      string   HTTP method (default "GET")
body        string   Request body (optional)
headers     table    Extra headers e.g. {["Content-Type"]="application/json"}
timeout_ms  number   Timeout in milliseconds, 1..3600000 (default 5000)
.fi
.PP
A key that is present but has the wrong type or is out of range makes the call
return
.B "ok = false"
with the reason in
.BR error .
The key is not replaced by its default, so `timeout_ms = "soon"` does not
silently become 5000. A key that is absent keeps its default; that is not an
error and is not reported.
.PP
Returns a table:
.nf
ok      bool    true if HTTP status 2xx
status  number  HTTP status code (e.g. 200, 404)
body    string  Response body
error   string  Error message on failure
.fi
.PP
.I timeout_ms
is one deadline for the whole exchange, not a separate timeout per operation.
Connecting, the TLS handshake, the request and the response are all measured
against it. A call that blocks resolves the host name before the deadline is
armed, on a thread of its own, with the resolution bounded by the same timeout.
A call that waits counts the resolution against the deadline. A response larger
than 8 MB is refused rather than buffered without limit.
.PP
A response that declares a
.B Content-Length
and then ends before reaching it is also refused, with the shortfall named in
.BR error .
Nothing else in the answer would show the problem. The close that cut the
response short looks the same as the close that ends a response with no length
at all. Over HTTPS, the
.B close_notify
alert would tell the two apart, but many servers omit it, and this client
accepts its absence. Only a response with a declared length can fall short. A
chunked response, or one with no length, is read until the close and returned
as usual.
.PP
HTTPS uses OpenSSL and validates the peer. The certificate chain is
verified against the system CA store, and the certificate must have been
issued to the host in the URL. A host name is matched against the
certificate's DNS names, with partial wildcards refused; an address
literal is matched against its IP SAN. TLS 1.2 is the minimum accepted protocol
version. If either check fails, the request is aborted with the reason in
.IR error .
There is no option to disable the checks. A service that presents a
self-signed or private-CA certificate must have that CA in the trust
store OpenSSL reads (overridable with
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
or
.BR SSL_CERT_DIR ).
If no CA store is available at all the call fails immediately with
.BR "no system CA store available" .
.SS Runtime Management API
Create, start, stop, and remove runtimes from Lua scripts:
.nf
socketley.create(type, name, config)  -- Create runtime
socketley.start(name)                 -- Start runtime
socketley.stop(name)                  -- Stop runtime
socketley.remove(name)                -- Stop + remove runtime
socketley.send(name, msg)             -- Send/broadcast to runtime
socketley.list()                      -- List all runtime names (table)
socketley.get(name)                   -- Get info table or nil
socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)  -- Pair two connections of one server
socketley.pipe(src, fd, dst)          -- Route a connection to another server
.fi
.P
.B "Every call above answers " true " when it happened, or " false ", \(dqreason\(dq" " when it did not."
.RS
.PP
The reason is a sentence that names the runtime and what was wrong with the
request. A script can use it to tell these four situations apart:
.PP
.nf
local ok, why = socketley.start("web")
--  false  there is no runtime called "web"
--  false  "web" is already running
--  false  "web" is failed, and a failed runtime cannot be started again ...
--  false  "web" could not be started; it is failed now, and the reason is
--         in the daemon's log        <- a held port, a TLS pair that is not one
.fi
.PP
This is the convention Lua itself uses, in
.BR io.open ,
.B os.remove
and
.BR pcall .
So
.B "assert(socketley.start(n))"
prints the reason with no other change. Success returns ONE value, not
.BR "true, nil" .
.PP
.B What a second return value changes.
Lua truncates a multiple-return call to one value everywhere except in three
places. These are unaffected: assignment, \fBif not f() then\fR,
\fBand\fR/\fBor\fR, concatenation,
.BR tostring ,
comparison, and any argument position that is not the last. These are
affected:
.IP \(bu 2
.B print(f())
prints two values;
.IP \(bu 2
.B "{ f() }"
builds a two-element table;
.IP \(bu 2
.B g(f())
passes
.B g
two arguments instead of one.
.RE
.PP
.B socketley.pipe
is two calls, and the number of arguments selects which one. With four
arguments it pairs two connections of ONE server; both descriptors are named
and both are used. With three arguments it hands the connection to another
.IR runtime ,
which decides what answers it. That form has no destination descriptor, so a
fourth argument across two runtimes is refused rather than accepted and
ignored.
.P
.B Config table keys:
.BR port ,
.B config
(or
.BR lua ,
a script path),
.BR target ,
.B mode
(server only),
.B http
and
.B http_cache
(server only),
.BR group ,
.B on_parent_stop
("remove" or "stop"), and
.B start
(bool).
.B The table is validated, and a refusal leaves nothing behind.
Each of these returns
.B false
and a reason, which also goes to the log, and creates no runtime:
.IP \(bu 2
a port outside 0..65535;
.IP \(bu 2
a mode or policy that is not one of the listed values;
.IP \(bu 2
a key that is not on this list;
.IP \(bu 2
a script that will not load;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B start
that cannot bind.
.PP
Runtimes created from Lua have parent-child ownership. port=0 creates an
internal-only server that receives only forwarded messages.
.PP
.B "Every call above works in a worker, which is where every runtime runs."
.RS
.PP
These calls act on the runtime map, which lives in the daemon. A script that
uses them therefore runs in a process that does not hold the map. All of the
calls still work there, in two different ways:
.RS
.PP
.BR socketley.send ,
.BR .list ,
.B .get
and the four
.B socketley.cluster
calls are answered from a
.I replica.
The daemon pushes the worker a copy of the map and a snapshot of the cluster
view, and refreshes both when they change.
.PP
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
.I change
the map, and changing a copy would be wrong. The worker sends these calls to the
daemon instead, over a socket it holds for exactly this purpose, and waits for
the answer. So
.B "if socketley.create(...) then"
tests what actually happened. The wait is what makes the result accurate, and it
is bounded. A call that does not fork anything took 9.95, 10.20 and 10.52
microseconds over three runs of 20000. A call that forks waits at most five
seconds. These calls are made in
.B on_start
or on a rare control message, never per request, which is why a wait is
affordable. Compare
.B socketley.http()
in the same table, which blocks the same loop for a network round trip.
.PP
The answers are the daemon's own, word for word what the same call says in the
daemon:
.B "there is no runtime called \(dqx\(dq" ,
.B "\(dqx\(dq is already running" ,
.BR "a runtime called \(dqx\(dq already exists" .
By design, a script cannot tell from them which process it runs in.
.RE
.PP
.B "Calls that cross runtimes work there too."
.B socketley.pipe
across two runtimes and
.B self.route()
both work in a worker and cross the process boundary, with no change to how
either is called.
.PP
One refusal remains; read it carefully if you meet it.
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop
and
.B .remove
raise an error in a worker that was forked
.I without
the socket these calls travel on. This happens with an older daemon, or with a
daemon that could not create the socket. The error message says so and tells
you to restart the daemon. It is not a permanent refusal.
.PP
Across two runtimes,
.B socketley.pipe
does not hand a file descriptor over at all, so no
.B SCM_RIGHTS
passing through
.B shared/net/conn_transfer.h
is involved. Both calls use
.BR route_client () ,
which writes into the target's connection maps, runs the target's
.BR on_connect ,
and then keeps the socket where it is and relays the bytes through it. This
needs a way to name another runtime's connection and reach it, not a way to move
one. That is
.BR shared/ipc/route_relay.h .
.PP
Nothing changes for a script: the same call, the same return value, the same
target name. The origin stays in the data path either way, because that is what
routing is. A message from a routed client reaches the target's
.BR on_client_message ,
and the target's reply reaches the client through the origin.
.PP
The four\(hyargument form,
.BR "socketley.pipe(src, fd_a, src, fd_b)" ,
pairs two connections of one server and crosses no process boundary. It must be
called from that server's own script, because only that process holds its
connections.
.PP
When a runtime that holds routed connections goes away (stopped, removed,
crashed or killed), every runtime on the other end of those routes is told to
release them, and the target's
.B on_disconnect
runs. The daemon does this, not the departing runtime, because a killed worker
runs no teardown at all.
.PP
.BR unroute() ,
.B get_route()
and
.B owner_send()
read the runtime's own tables, in a worker as anywhere else.
.PP
Plan for where that error is raised, when one of those four calls raises it. At
a script's top level it fails the load,
and a worker whose script did not load does not serve: the
.B create
is refused with a reason and the runtime is left
.BR failed .
Inside a callback, the error is caught and logged like any other callback
error, and the runtime carries on. In
.BR on_start ,
that means the runtime is up and serving, but nothing
.B on_start
was meant to do has happened.
.B ls
shows it green and it accepts connections. So the command that started it exits
1 and names the callback and the reason. The runtime is left running either way;
the exit code and the message tell the two cases apart.
.PP
Everything that does not use the runtime map works in a worker exactly as it
does in the daemon:
.BR socketley.log ,
.BR .http ,
.BR .json_encode / .json_decode ,
the timers,
.BR .subscribe ,
the whole
.B self
table, and every connection and request callback. See
.B "Out-of-process runtimes"
under
.B LIMITATIONS
for the full account.
.RE
.SS Cluster Introspection API
When the daemon runs in cluster mode (\fB\-\-cluster\fR), the
.B socketley.cluster
table provides read-only introspection into the full cluster topology.
All functions return empty tables when not in cluster mode, so scripts work in
both standalone and cluster setups without modification.
.PP
In a worker process the four functions are answered from a snapshot of the
cluster view, which the daemon pushes before the frame that loads its script.
.PP
One exception remains, and it is in a worker only. A daemon that is in no cluster has no view to push. An
empty table is also what a
.I successful
call returns for a daemon that is alone in a cluster. So when no view is
installed, the four functions raise an error that names the function, instead
of returning. If a daemon
.I joins
a cluster after a worker's script has loaded, that script keeps getting the
error until it is reloaded.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.daemons() \(-> table
Returns an array of daemon info tables. Each entry has:
.nf
name      string   Daemon name (from \-\-name)
host      string   Advertised address, from gethostname(2), falling
                   back to the daemon name if that call fails
runtimes  number   Count of runtimes on this daemon
.fi
.IP
The local daemon is always the first entry. Remote daemons follow in discovery order.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.runtimes() \(-> table
Returns a flat array of all runtimes across every daemon in the cluster:
.nf
daemon       string   Name of the daemon hosting this runtime
name         string   Runtime name
type         string   "server", "client", "proxy", or "cache"
port         number   Listen port (0 = internal-only)
group        string   Group tag (empty string if unset)
state        string   "created", "running", "stopped", or "failed"
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.IP
The type is the name of any runtime type the daemon knows, built in or
registered, not only these four.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.group(name) \(-> table
Returns running members of the named group that have a valid port.
Each entry has:
.nf
daemon       string   Daemon name
host         string   Advertised address
port         number   Listen port
connections  number   Current connection count
.fi
.IP
Useful for building custom routing, health checks, or failover logic from Lua.
.TP
.B socketley.cluster.stats() \(-> table
Returns aggregate cluster statistics:
.nf
daemons   number   Total daemon count (local + remote)
runtimes  number   Total runtime count across all daemons
running   number   Count of runtimes in "running" state
groups    table    Maps group name (string) \(-> member count (number)
.fi
.SS Cluster Event Callbacks
These optional callbacks fire when the cluster topology changes.
Events are detected during the 2-second scan/publish cycle, so callbacks
may fire up to 2 seconds after the actual change.
They fire only on a
.B running
runtime. A runtime that has been stopped or has never been started executes no
script, and that includes cluster callbacks. If one event in a batch stops the
runtime, the remaining events in that batch are not delivered to it.
A daemon is considered stale and triggers
.B on_cluster_leave
after 10 seconds without a heartbeat update.
.TP
.B on_cluster_join(daemon)
Fired when a new remote daemon is discovered in the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with
.B name
(string) and
.B host
(string) fields.
.TP
.B on_cluster_leave(daemon)
Fired when a remote daemon's heartbeat file becomes stale (>10s old) or is
removed from the cluster directory.
.I daemon
is a table with a
.B name
(string) field.
.TP
.B on_group_change(group, members)
Fired when the total member count of a group changes (a runtime joins or
leaves the group, or a daemon with group members appears/disappears).
It fires only on actual count changes, not on every scan cycle.
.I group
is the group name (string).
.I members
is an array of tables, each with
.BR daemon ", " host ", and " port
fields representing the current group membership.
.PP
Example \(em log topology changes and maintain a local routing table:
.nf
local backends = {}

function on_cluster_join(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon joined: " .. daemon.name .. " at " .. daemon.host)
end

function on_cluster_leave(daemon)
    socketley.log("daemon left: " .. daemon.name)
end

function on_group_change(group, members)
    if group == "api" then
        backends = members   \-\- update routing table
        socketley.log("api group now has " .. #members .. " members")
    end
end

function on_start()
    \-\- Initial snapshot
    local stats = socketley.cluster.stats()
    socketley.log("cluster: " .. stats.daemons .. " daemons, "
                  .. stats.running .. " running runtimes")
end
.fi
.SH DASHBOARD
When
.B daemon.metrics_port
is set in the daemon configuration file, the metrics endpoint serves the
following routes:
.TP
.B GET /
Serves the web dashboard, which has the storage interface's look and takes its
stylesheet and helpers from the same binary.
The overview shows the daemon's runtimes, connections, messages, bytes in and
out, CPU and resident memory, each with a line graph of its last three minutes.
The runtime list shows each runtime with its state and the same figures, and
can be filtered by type and name.
Each runtime also has a page with its configuration and its
.BR stats .
.IP
A signed-in user can start, stop, restart and remove a runtime, and reload its
Lua. The user can also edit a runtime, either in a form for its type, filled in
with what
.B show
prints, or as the JSON that
.B import
takes.
To create a runtime, the user picks the type and then fills in a form of every
flag that
.B create
takes for that type, grouped by section.
The page shows the command it will send while the form is filled in.
.IP
If
.B daemon.dashboard_idp
names an
.B idp
runtime, the page offers sign-in through it.
An account of the group
.B admins
that is signed in at that idp is signed in to the dashboard too.
Signing out of the dashboard also signs out at the provider.
.IP
Otherwise, signing in takes the access key and secret of an admin in the
credentials file that
.B daemon.dashboard_credentials
names.
The file has a storage runtime's format, and the dashboard checks the key and
secret the same way a proxy's
.B "\-\-auth basic:"
does.
An address that fails five times in fifteen minutes is refused with 429, even
with the right secret.
Behind a proxy on this machine, the address counted is the one in
X\-Forwarded\-For.
.IP
The session is a cookie sealed with a key that the daemon generates at start.
The cookie is HttpOnly and SameSite=Strict, and also Secure behind HTTPS.
It is valid for twelve hours.
The daemon ends a session when the user signs out, when the user's key is
removed from the file, or when that key is given another secret.
Each command also carries the page's own header, which a request from another
site cannot carry.
The command goes over the daemon's control socket, as a CLI command does, and
is logged with who sent it.
The page sends only
.BR create ,
.BR edit ,
.BR import ,
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR remove ,
.BR reload ,
.BR reload\-lua ,
.BR show ,
.BR stats ,
.B ls
and
.BR ps ,
and never a word that contains a space.
.IP
When credentials are configured, every route except the page and the sign\-in
needs a session.
.B /metrics
needs a session too, except for a scraper on this machine that connects without
a proxy in between.
Without credentials, the page shows the figures but changes nothing.
The log and a zone's text always need a session, whatever is configured.
.IP
The dashboard's history and alerts come from a
.B metrics
runtime (see
.BR "METRICS FLAGS" ).
The daemon creates one named
.B metrics
the first time it starts with the dashboard on and a way to sign in to it
.RB ( daemon.dashboard_credentials
or
.BR daemon.dashboard_idp ),
or with any of
.BR daemon.alert_webhook ,
.B daemon.alert_mail
and
.B daemon.events
set.
The metrics runtime listens on 127.0.0.1:9090, or on the next free port above
it.
The daemon creates it only once, so a metrics runtime that was removed stays
removed.
With no metrics runtime running, the page shows the current figures and no
history.
With several, the page reads the one named
.BR metrics ,
or else any running one that collects this daemon's series.
.IP
The graphs cover a chosen range: the last fifteen minutes, hour, six hours, day,
week, month or year.
The value and time under the pointer are shown.
For each runtime and for the daemon, connections and memory are drawn as they
are, and messages, bytes and CPU as rates per second.
The figures are read from the metrics runtime's store, which keeps them as long
as its
.B \-\-keep
and
.B \-\-downsample
allow.
.BR "GET /api/history?runtime=" name "&range=" seconds "&points=" n
returns the history of one runtime, or of all runtimes together when no name is
given, as
.I n
points.
A point with no data is null.
The answer is an object with
.B from
(in seconds since 1970),
.B step
(the seconds per point) and the arrays
.BR conn ", " msg ", " in ", " out ", " cpu
(percent of one CPU) and
.BR rss .
The range is held between a minute and a year, and the number of points between
10 and 1440.
.IP
The Alerts view shows the metrics runtime's rules.
These are the built\-in rules below, unless its
.B \-\-rules
file changes them.
A runtime failed, or was started again three times within the hour.
A sync runtime cannot carry what it owes.
A certificate that a runtime serves expires within two weeks; the alert is
critical within three days, or once the certificate has expired.
The disk under a storage runtime or under the state directory, or the host's
memory, is at 90% (critical at 95%).
A storage runtime is at 90% of its
.BR \-\-max\-bytes ,
or a cache that refuses writes is at 90% of its
.BR \-\-maxmemory .
A git repository's last pull failed.
A mail runtime has a hundred messages waiting (critical at a thousand), or its
own address is listed by a blocklist that its
.B \-\-dnsbl
names.
A scraped target does not answer for two minutes.
A tunnel edge is missing an agent that joined it, or drops more than 1% of its
datagrams.
An idp runtime barred more than twenty sign-ins in ten minutes.
.IP
A critical alert silences the warning on the same thing.
Each alert is logged, listed on the page with the recent notifications, and
shown on the overview.
When
.B daemon.alert_webhook
is set to an http or https URL, each alert is also posted to it as JSON.
The
.B content
and
.B text
fields carry the message; these are the fields Discord, Slack and Telegram's
sendMessage read.
The
.B state
.RB ( firing ,
.BR resolved )
and
.B alert
fields carry the rest.
If the hook does not accept a message with a 2xx status, the daemon posts it
again after a pause that doubles each time, up to eight seconds.
A message the hook has not accepted is kept in
.B .notify
in the state directory and posted after a restart.
.IP
When
.B daemon.alert_mail
is set to
.BI mail:// runtime / address\fR[,\fIaddress\fR...]
that mail runtime mails each alert, as well as or instead of the webhook.
The subject is the alert's first line.
Mail is held the same way until the mail runtime runs.
The metrics runtime keeps its alerts' state in its directory, so a metrics
runtime that is started again does not report the same alerts a second time.
.B "GET /api/alerts"
lists the alerts, and
.B POST /api/alerts/test
sent with the page's header posts a test through the hook.
.IP
When
.B daemon.events
names an mqtt runtime, the daemon publishes the state of each runtime on it, at
QoS 1 and retained.
It publishes on
.BI socketley/runtimes/ name /state
as JSON with the runtime's
.BR name ", " type ", " state " and " port .
It publishes at every change, and clears the topic when the runtime is removed.
The daemon also publishes each alert while it holds, on
.BI socketley/alerts/ id
as the JSON that the
.B alert
field above carries, and clears it when the alert clears.
A subscriber to
.B socketley/#
receives the current state as soon as it subscribes, and every change after
that.
The broker may be created after the daemon starts.
Until it runs, the messages owed to it wait in
.B .notify
in the state directory, as the webhook's do.
.IP
The Logs view follows the daemon's recent log live, and filters it by runtime,
by level and by text.
Each runtime's page shows that runtime's own log.
A failed runtime shows the last warning or error it wrote, both in the list and
on its page, next to how often it was started again.
Each worker writes its stderr into its own pipe, which the daemon reads.
The daemon passes every line on unchanged to its own stderr, so the journal
gets every line.
It also keeps the last 10,000 lines in memory, its own and the workers', each
tagged with the runtime's name:
.BR "GET /api/logs?since=" seq "&max=" n "&runtime=" name "&level=" warn .
.IP
The Domains view shows three things.
The first is every certificate that a runtime serves: its names, its issuer,
how long it has left, whether it is self\-issued, whether a wildcard is waiting
for its zone's delegation, and the last line of a failed issuance.
The second is each entrance's routes, each mapping a name to the runtime it goes
to.
The third is the zones of the dns runtimes, whose records the page can edit.
Before an edited zone is written, its text is checked the way the dns runtime
reads a zone file.
If the serial was left as it was, the dashboard raises it, so secondaries take
the change.
The view uses
.BR "GET /api/edge" ,
and
.B GET
and
.B PUT /api/zone
for a file a dns runtime serves and no other.
.IP
The endpoint listens on 127.0.0.1 only.
To reach it on a server, open an SSH tunnel with
\fBssh \-L 9100:127.0.0.1:9100\fR \fIhost\fR, and then open
\fBhttp://localhost:9100/\fR.
On an installed system the port is set in
\fB/etc/socketley/config.lua\fR, as \fBdaemon = { metrics_port = 9100 }\fR,
and is read when the service starts.
.TP
.B GET /metrics
Serves the daemon's metrics in Prometheus exposition format.
It is suitable for scraping by Prometheus, Grafana Agent, or similar monitoring
stacks.
.TP
.B GET /api/overview
Returns a JSON object with high-level daemon statistics:
.nf
{
    "version": "1.1.0",
    "uptime_seconds": 86400,
    "runtimes_total": 5,
    "runtimes_running": 4,
    "connections_total": 18230,
    "connections_active": 142,
    "messages_total": 9120044,
    "bytes_in": 12345678,
    "bytes_out": 9876543,
    "cpu_seconds": 812.40,
    "rss_bytes": 163577856,
    "memory_bytes": 94371840,
    "processes": 5,
    "cpus": 4
}
.fi
.IP
.B cpu_seconds
is the user and system CPU time of the daemon and of every process its runtimes
run in, read from
.BR /proc .
The change between two readings, over the time between them, is the CPU used.
.B rss_bytes
is the sum of their resident memory, which counts a page shared between shards
once per shard.
.B memory_bytes
is what they cost the machine: each shared page is counted by the share of it
that each process holds (the kernel's Pss).
So the runtimes' figures add up to the memory they hold between them.
.B processes
is how many processes there are, and
.B cpus
is the number of CPUs online.
.TP
.B GET /api/runtimes
Returns a JSON array of all runtimes with name, type, port, state, connections
and message/byte counters.
Each entry also has
.BR cpu_seconds ", " rss_bytes ", " memory_bytes " and " processes
for the runtime's own processes: one per shard, and none while it is stopped.
.TP
.BI "GET /api/runtime/" name
Returns a JSON object for the single runtime named
.IR name .
Returns 404 if the runtime does not exist.
.PP
The
.B dashboard
CLI command prints the dashboard URL:
.nf
$ socketley dashboard
Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
.fi
.SH SERVICE MESH
The proxy runtime includes service-mesh primitives that can be combined
to build sidecar proxies, API gateways, or resilient service-to-service
communication layers.
.SS Health Checks
Active health checks probe backends at a regular interval
.RB ( \-\-health\-interval )
using either a TCP connect or an HTTP GET
.RB ( \-\-health\-check ).
A backend is marked unhealthy after
.B \-\-health\-threshold
consecutive failures and is excluded from the load balancer pool.
A single successful check restores the backend.
.SS Circuit Breaker
The circuit breaker prevents cascading failures by tracking consecutive
errors per backend. It has three states:
.TP
.B closed
The normal state: requests are forwarded to the backend and errors are
counted.
.TP
.B open
The state the circuit enters when errors reach
.BR \-\-circuit\-threshold .
All requests fail immediately, without attempting a connection. After
.B \-\-circuit\-timeout
seconds, the circuit moves to half-open.
.TP
.B half-open
The circuit lets a single probe request through to the backend.
It is one request in total, not one per selection: while that probe is
outstanding, the backend is unavailable to every other request.
If the probe succeeds, the circuit returns to
.BR closed .
If it fails, the circuit re-opens.
.SS Response Deadline
A backend that accepts connections and then stops answering fails no
check.
A TCP health probe connects to it successfully, and a request that never
completes counts as neither an error nor a success, so the circuit breaker has
nothing to open on.
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
(default 2 seconds) limits the wait for the first byte of a response.
When it expires, the proxy records a backend error, which puts such a backend
through the breaker like any other failure.
The request is retried elsewhere if
.B \-\-retry
allows it, and answered
.B 504 Gateway Timeout
otherwise.
.PP
The deadline covers the handshake as well as the answer.
If a backend never completes its connect within the deadline, the client is
also answered
.BR "504 Gateway Timeout" ,
instead of being closed without a response.
A request routed through an
.B @group
backend is charged to the member it actually reached.
So a group member that stalls is taken out of rotation by the same breaker as a
listed backend.
.PP
The deadline is on by default and covers only the wait for the first byte, so
a large or streaming response body is not affected.
An endpoint that legitimately needs more than 2 seconds to start answering must
be given a longer
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
or
.BR "\-\-backend\-timeout 0" ;
otherwise the proxy treats it as hung.
The deadline applies to HTTP mode only, because in TCP mode there is no request
boundary to measure from.
.SS Request Framing
In HTTP mode the proxy is strict about how a request's header block is framed.
It refuses anything that it and a backend could read differently.
These are the same rules, from the same implementation, that a socketley server
applies to a request it receives directly; see
.B "Request Framing"
under
.BR "SERVER FLAGS" .
The proxy forwards bytes to a backend that parses them independently.
If the two disagree about where a request ends, the next request on the same
connection starts in the wrong place: this is request smuggling.
Normalising the request instead would mean guessing how the backend will read
it, so the proxy refuses rather than guesses.
.PP
A request is answered
.B 400 Bad Request
and its connection is closed, without contacting any backend, when the
header block contains any of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a bare LF or a bare CR \(em every line must end with CRLF
.IP \(bu 2
whitespace between a field name and its colon
.RB ( "Content\-Length : 6" )
.IP \(bu 2
a continuation (obs\-fold) line, i.e. one starting with a space or tab
.IP \(bu 2
a field line with no colon
.IP \(bu 2
both
.B Content\-Length
and
.BR Transfer\-Encoding ,
or more than one
.B Content\-Length
field
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Content\-Length
that is not a whole decimal number
.RB ( "Content\-Length: 6x" )
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B Transfer\-Encoding
whose final coding is not
.B chunked
.RB ( "Transfer\-Encoding: gzip" ,
.BR "Transfer\-Encoding: chunked, gzip" ,
or
.B chunked
listed twice); such a request has no determinable body length at all
.RE
.PP
Every request on a connection is framed and checked on its own, including one
pipelined behind another.
Only the body that a request declares travels with it.
Bytes past its
.B Content\-Length
are not body: the proxy holds them back and parses them as the next request
instead of forwarding them.
Pipelining still works, and a second request sent before the first is answered
is still served.
But it is answered in turn, after the same checks.
A smuggling attempt hidden behind a clean request is refused exactly as if it
had been sent first.
.PP
A request held this way has the same 10 second deadline as a half\-arrived one.
The exchange in front of it may never reach an end this proxy can detect, for
example when a backend frames its response in a way the proxy cannot read.
Then the held request is answered
.B 408 Request Timeout
rather than left waiting.
A
.B chunked
request is the one body this proxy cannot measure, because it never
de\-chunks.
Its body is forwarded as an opaque stream, so a request pipelined behind a
chunked one is framed by the backend alone.
.PP
Compliant clients send none of these. A request whose header block is
still arriving is held until the terminating blank line. It is subject to the
16 KiB header limit
.RB ( "431 Request Header Fields Too Large" )
and a 10 second header deadline
.RB ( "408 Request Timeout" ).
.PP
Behind the header limit there is a second, larger limit.
It counts everything buffered for an exchange, not just one header block.
At most 1 MiB is held for a connection whose request has already been
forwarded.
That covers the body the client sends past its declared
.BR Content\-Length ,
and the requests pipelined behind it.
Nothing drains this buffer until the response in front of it is finished.
So a client that writes faster than the backend answers can reach the limit on
a well\-formed connection.
The client is then answered
.B 413 Request Entity Too Large
and the connection is closed.
The one exception is the same as for the gateway statuses.
If part of the backend's response is already on that client's wire, no status
line is written behind it, because it would land at an offset that is not a
message boundary.
Instead, the connection is closed with the response left visibly truncated.
.PP
The header deadline starts at the first byte of a request and ends when its
header block is complete.
It is fixed and not configurable.
It is deliberately independent of
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout ,
which defaults to 0 (never) and so cannot be relied on to end a
half\-sent request.
A client that sends a request line and then stops sends no more bytes for the
16 KiB limit to count.
Without the deadline, that client could hold its connection, its descriptor and
its 64 KB buffer for as long as it liked.
The deadline does not measure an idle keep\-alive connection between requests,
or a slow request body.
It measures only the gap between a request's first byte and its terminating
blank line.
.PP
The proxy applies the same strictness to backend responses, but only to decide
whether the backend connection may be reused.
Sometimes the length of a response cannot be established with certainty:
its header block was split across reads, or was not strictly CRLF-framed, or
more bytes arrived than the length it declared.
Such a response is forwarded to the client unchanged, and its connection is
closed instead of returned to the pool.
A backend that answers
.B Connection: close
is not pooled either, because it has said the socket is going away.
.PP
A
.B Connection: close
sent by the
.I client
does not do this.
It is a hop-by-hop field about the client's own connection to the proxy.
So the proxy does not forward it upstream, and it does not end the backend
connection.
The proxy answers the request, returns the backend socket to the pool, and
closes the client connection as asked.
The field is a comma-separated list.
As on the server, it is parsed token by token and case\-insensitively.
So
.B "Connection: keep\-alive, close"
counts as close, while a value that merely begins with those letters, such as
.BR "Connection: clientinfo" ,
does not.
A client that opens a connection per request (curl, a health checker, any HTTP
library used without a session) therefore costs one accept and no backend
connect, the same as a keep-alive client.
.PP
Before a connection from the pool is handed to the next request, the proxy
checks it.
A connection with unread data on it is closed rather than reused.
Nothing was asked of an idle pooled socket, so anything waiting on it was sent
unbidden by the backend.
It is the tail of an answer whose length was mis\-declared, or a second answer
volunteered behind the first.
Reusing the socket would make those bytes the head of the next client's
response, so the connection is dropped and a fresh one opened.
The proxy logs this once per instance.
.PP
Reuse covers an
.B @group
backend as well as a listed one.
A pooled connection is filed under the endpoint it was opened to, never under
the group.
So it is only ever handed back to a request routed to that same member.
When a member leaves the group, its pooled connections are closed at once
rather than left to the idle sweep.
.PP
Reuse covers a
.B TLS
backend too.
The session is pooled together with the descriptor, so a reused connection
continues on the record layer it already had, at the sequence number it had
reached.
A TLS connection carries more than an address: a negotiated session, a peer
identity verified against a name, a client certificate that may have been
presented, and a record layer part way through a stream.
The endpoint the socket is filed under accounts for three of those.
That is because the name was pinned to that endpoint before the handshake, and
the certificate was checked against it.
The client certificate and the trust store belong to the runtime and are rebuilt
on every start, and the pool is emptied on every stop.
So a pooled session can never outlive the configuration that verified it.
.PP
A pooled TLS socket must not inherit an answer nobody asked for.
A cleartext socket with bytes waiting on it is destroyed rather than reused,
because a socket in the pool has been asked nothing since its last answer
completed.
That test cannot be carried across a record layer unchanged.
A TLS peer that has said nothing still sends records: a session ticket, a key
update, a
.BR close_notify .
So the proxy drains the socket into the session, and puts the question to the
record layer instead of to the kernel.
Records that produce no application data are the peer's own housekeeping, and
the connection is kept.
Application data is a response that belongs to a previous exchange, and the
connection is dropped.
The second case is logged once per runtime.
.SS Retries
When
.B \-\-retry
is set, failed requests are automatically retried on the next backend
in the load balancer pool. By default only idempotent methods are
retried; use
.B \-\-retry\-all
to include POST and PATCH.
.SS mTLS
Both legs can be encrypted.
.BR "\-\-tls \-\-cert \-\-key"
terminates TLS on the proxy's own port, and
.B \-\-client\-ca
makes the proxy demand a certificate from its callers.
An
.B https://
backend address, or any of
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.BR \-\-client\-key ,
switches the backend leg to TLS.
On that leg the proxy verifies the chain and the endpoint's name, and presents a
client certificate when one is configured.
A full sidecar pair looks like this:
.nf
socketley create proxy edge -p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert edge.pem \-\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.7:8443 \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert edge.pem \-\-client\-key edge\-key.pem \\
    \-\-sidecar \-s
.fi
.PP
A TLS backend connection is returned to the connection pool and reused, so a
busy sidecar pays for one handshake per idle period rather than per request.
The pooled socket keeps its session and goes back only to the endpoint it was
opened to.
It is dropped rather than reused if the backend has left application data on
it.
.B \-\-health\-check http
works against a TLS backend and probes it over TLS, verified to the same
standard as the data path.
See
.B LIMITATIONS
for what the probe does and does not establish.
See
.B "mTLS Flags"
under
.B "PROXY FLAGS"
for what is verified and what cannot be switched off.
.SS Sidecar Deployment
The
.B \-\-sidecar
flag enables a production-ready set of defaults: TCP health checks, a circuit
breaker (threshold 5, timeout 30s), 2 retries, and graceful drain. Deploy it
alongside your application:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.PP
The proxy listens on port 8080 and forwards to the local application on
port 3000. Unhealthy backends are removed automatically, and the circuit
breaker prevents thundering-herd retries during outages.
.PP
.B \-\-sidecar
does not change
.BR \-\-backend\-timeout .
That deadline is 2 seconds by default, and its expiry is charged to the circuit
breaker that
.B \-\-sidecar
enables. If the application can take longer than that to send its first
response byte, set a longer timeout explicitly:
.nf
socketley create proxy myapp \-\-sidecar \-\-backend\-timeout 30 \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -p 8080 -s
.fi
.SH RUNTIME STATES
.TP
.B created
The initial state after a runtime is created.
.TP
.B running
The runtime is active and processing I/O.
.TP
.B stopped
The runtime is stopped and can be started again.
.TP
.B failed
The runtime could not start, or its worker died more than five times in ten
seconds.
.B ls
shows the reason.
It stays failed until it is started again: fix the cause, then run
.BR "socketley start" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a simple server (on an installed system, systemd manages the daemon):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server myserver -p 9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create a client that connects to the server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client myclient -t 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Create an HTTP proxy with load balancing:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    --backend 127.0.0.1:9001,127.0.0.1:9002 \\
    --strategy round-robin -s
.fi
.PP
Create a server with bash output (messages go to stdout):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -b -s
# With timestamp and prefix:
socketley create server echo -p 9000 -bpt -s
.fi
.PP
Send a message to a runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley send myserver "Hello from CLI"
socketley send myclient "Message to server"
.fi
.PP
Create a persistent cache with a memory limit:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache store -p 9000 \\
    --persistent /data/cache.bin \\
    --maxmemory 256M --eviction allkeys-lru -s
.fi
.PP
Create a cache in RESP mode (Redis-compatible):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache redis-compat -p 6379 --resp -s
redis-cli -p 6379 SET mykey myval
redis-cli -p 6379 GET mykey
.fi
.PP
Create a server with a connection limit and rate limiting:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 \\
    --max-connections 100 --rate-limit 1000 -s
.fi
.PP
View runtime statistics:
.PP
.nf
socketley stats myserver
# Output: name, type, port, connections, total_messages, bytes_in/out, ...
.fi
.PP
Restart a running runtime:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload myserver
# Stops and restarts the runtime
socketley reload *
# Restarts all running runtimes
.fi
.PP
Hot-reload a Lua script:
.PP
.nf
socketley reload-lua myserver
# Re-loads the Lua script without stopping the runtime
socketley reload-lua *
# Hot-reloads Lua on all runtimes with scripts
.fi
.PP
Start, stop or remove all runtimes:
.PP
.nf
socketley start *
# Starts all non-running runtimes
socketley stop *
# Stops all running runtimes
socketley remove *
# Stops all and removes everything
.fi
.PP
Multiple names and glob patterns:
.PP
.nf
socketley stop myserver myclient
# Stops both runtimes
socketley stop my*
# Stops all runtimes starting with "my"
socketley stats cache?
# Shows stats for cache1, cache2, etc.
socketley start myserver cache*
# Starts myserver and all cache-prefixed runtimes
socketley remove api-* test-*
# Removes all api- and test- prefixed runtimes
.fi
.PP
Interactive mode (live terminal session):
.PP
.nf
# Start server and enter interactive session
socketley create server echo -p 9000
socketley start echo -i
# Type messages to broadcast, see received messages
# Press Ctrl+C to detach (server keeps running)

# Attach to already-running cache
socketley start mycache -i
# Type cache commands: set foo bar, get foo, etc.
.fi
.PP
Edit interactively (opens the JSON configuration in $EDITOR):
.PP
.nf
socketley edit myserver           # Opens config in editor
socketley edit myserver -r        # Opens editor, auto-reloads Lua after save
socketley edit myserver -p 9001   # Flag-based edit (direct)
.fi
.PP
Cache replication (leader-follower):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache leader -p 9000 -s
socketley create cache follower -p 9001 --replicate 127.0.0.1:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Encrypted server. The listener terminates TLS itself.
Everything above it (line protocol, HTTP, WebSocket, RESP) runs on the
decrypted stream, so this one runtime serves
.B https://
and
.B wss://
on the same port:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server secure -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem -s
curl --cacert server.pem https://localhost:9443/
.fi
.PP
Mutual TLS, where a client must present a certificate signed by the given CA:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server mtls -p 9443 \\
    --tls --cert server.pem --key server-key.pem \\
    --ca trusted-clients.pem -s
.fi
.PP
Renewing a certificate: the files are read at start, so
.B reload
picks up a new one.
.PP
.nf
socketley reload secure
.fi
.PP
An incomplete configuration is refused rather than served in the clear:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server oops -p 9443 --tls --cert server.pem
# --tls: a TLS listener needs --key <file> to go with --cert
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Encrypted client. The client mirrors the listener above: one flag turns TLS on.
The client verifies the peer in two ways: the chain against the trust store,
and the certificate against the host in
.BR \-t .
.PP
.nf
# verify against the system trust store
socketley create client feed -t events.example.com:9443 --tls -s

# verify against a private CA instead
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem -s

# and present a certificate of our own, for a peer that demands one
socketley create client feed -t 10.0.0.7:9443 \\
    --tls --ca internal-ca.pem \\
    --cert client.pem --key client-key.pem -s
.fi
.PP
If the peer's certificate does not verify, the client closes the socket.
It re-dials on its backoff and never falls back to cleartext.
There is no skip-verify flag.
Encryption without verification protects against somebody recording the wire,
but not at all against somebody sitting on it, and from the outside the two
look identical.
.PP
The same rules apply as on the listener. Half a certificate pair is refused,
and a trust store without
.B \-\-tls
is refused.
.B reload
installs a renewed client certificate:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client bad -t host:9443 --tls --cert client.pem
# --cert: needs --key <file> as well -- a certificate with no key
# presents nothing
# exit status 1
.fi
.PP
Graceful shutdown (drain pending writes):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --drain -s
socketley stop api   # Flushes write queues before closing
.fi
.PP
UDP server (fire-and-forget messaging):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server stats -p 9000 --udp -b -s
echo "cpu.load 0.42" | socat - UDP:localhost:9000
.fi
.PP
UDP client:
.PP
.nf
socketley create client udpcli -t 127.0.0.1:9000 --udp -s
.fi
.PP
Create a read-only cache (for shared lookups):
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache lookup -p 9000 --mode readonly -s
.fi
.PP
Master mode server (one controller, many viewers):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server broadcast -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw secret -s
# Client A: sends "master secret" \(-> "master: ok"
# Client A: sends "hello" \(-> broadcast to all others
# Client B: sends "hello" \(-> silently dropped
.fi
.PP
Master mode with forwarding:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ctrl -p 9000 \\
    --mode master --master-pw admin123 --send-to master -s
# Non-master messages forwarded to master as "[<fd>] msg"
.fi
.PP
WebSocket server (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server ws -p 9000 -s
# Browser: new WebSocket("ws://localhost:9000")
# Raw TCP clients also work on the same port
.fi
.PP
Cache access through a server:
.PP
.nf
socketley create cache db -p 9001 -s
socketley create server api -p 9000 --cache db -s
# Client sends: "cache set user:1 alice" \(-> "ok"
# Client sends: "cache get user:1" \(-> "alice"
# Client sends: "hello" \(-> broadcast as usual
.fi
.PP
Dynamic proxy with group-based backend discovery:
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api1 -p 9001 -g api -s
socketley create server api2 -p 9002 -g api -s
socketley create proxy gw -p 8080 --backend @api -s
# Proxy routes to all running members of group "api"

# Add a new member dynamically (no proxy restart needed)
socketley create server api3 -p 9003 -g api -s

# Mix group and direct backends
socketley create proxy gw2 -p 8081 \\
    --backend @api,10.0.0.5:9000 -s
.fi
.PP
Lua HTTP worker (auto-detected, no flags needed):
.PP
.nf
socketley create server api -p 9000 --lua handler.lua -s
# handler.lua:
# function on_http_request(req)
#     return {status=200, headers={["Content-Type"]="application/json"},
#             body='{"hello":"world"}'}
# end
# curl http://localhost:9000/api
.fi
.PP
Sidecar proxy with health checks and circuit breaker:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy sidecar -p 8080 \-\-sidecar \\
    \-\-backend 127.0.0.1:3000 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with HTTP health checks and retries:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy gateway -p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend 10.0.0.1:9000,10.0.0.2:9000 \\
    \-\-health\-check http \-\-health\-path /ready \\
    \-\-health\-interval 10 \-\-health\-threshold 5 \\
    \-\-retry 3 \-\-circuit\-threshold 10 -s
.fi
.PP
Proxy with mTLS on both legs. The listener terminates TLS and demands a
certificate from its callers.
The backend leg dials TLS, verifies the chain and the endpoint's name, and
presents a certificate of its own:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy secure \-p 8443 \\
    \-\-tls \-\-cert proxy.pem \-\-key proxy\-key.pem \\
    \-\-client\-ca trusted\-clients.pem \\
    \-\-client\-cert backend\-client.pem \-\-client\-key backend\-client\-key.pem \\
    \-\-ca mesh\-ca.pem \\
    \-\-backend https://10.0.0.1:9000 -s
# Backend connections are pooled with their sessions: a handshake per
# idle period, not per request.
# --health-check http probes over TLS and verifies the backend too.
.fi
.PP
TLS to the backend only. The proxy listens in cleartext on loopback and
encrypts the hop that leaves the machine:
.PP
.nf
socketley create proxy egress \-p 8080 \\
    \-\-backend https://api.example.com \-s
# Verified against the system CA store, name checked against the certificate.
# A private CA goes in --ca; there is no way to skip verification.
.fi
.PP
Open the live dashboard:
.PP
.nf
socketley dashboard
# Dashboard: http://localhost:9100/
# Open in browser for live runtime view
.fi
.PP
Query the dashboard API:
.PP
.nf
curl http://localhost:9100/api/overview
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtimes
curl http://localhost:9100/api/runtime/myserver
.fi
.PP
Load a complex configuration from a Lua file:
.PP
.nf
socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.SH CLUSTER MODE
.B "The cluster directory is as privileged as the daemons that read it."
A proxy with an
.BI @ group
backend picks its targets from the files in that directory, so whoever can
write there decides where that traffic goes.
Treat write access to it exactly as you would treat access to the control
socket: it belongs to the daemons and to nobody else.
The intended arrangement is a shared volume between containers you control,
not a directory that any other workload can write to.
.PP
Multi-daemon cluster discovery lets proxies route to runtimes on remote
daemons. Each daemon publishes its state to a shared directory (typically a
Docker volume) every 2 seconds. Entries whose heartbeat is older than 10s are
stale, and are excluded automatically.
.PP
.B How it works:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Each daemon writes its state to
.IR dir / name .json
.IP 2. 3
Every 2 seconds, each daemon scans the directory for peer files
.IP 3. 3
Proxy
.B @group
resolution includes both local and remote group members
.IP 4. 3
On graceful shutdown, the daemon deletes its file
.RE
.PP
.B Restarting a node, and name conflicts:
.PP
A name belongs to one node.
If two daemons published the same file, every peer would get an endpoint set
that belongs to neither of them.
Traffic for
.I api1
would also be split between two machines that each believe they are api1.
So a daemon that finds its name held by a live entry is refused
.BR --cluster ,
and is told so.
.PP
A node that was killed and restarted is a different case.
Every restart policy this project documents (a container restart, a redeploy,
systemd's
.BR "Restart=on-failure" ,
a crash loop) brings the node back within a second or two.
That is well inside the 10s threshold, so the node finds its own entry still
fresh, and it reclaims it.
.PP
The entry records who wrote it: the advertised host, the daemon's control
socket path, the kernel boot id, and the pid.
A daemon may take over a fresh entry only when the first three are its own.
That is a proof rather than a guess.
A unix socket path admits one process at a time, and everything else that
reaches a daemon connects to that path instead of binding it.
So on one machine, under one hostname, whoever holds the path
.I is
the daemon, and an entry carrying our own three values was written by a
process that is gone.
A different machine has a different boot id, and a fresh entry cannot predate
a reboot.
The pid is published for the operator and never used to decide, because a
restarted container has a new pid namespace.
.PP
What this cannot tell apart is two containers on
.I one
host that are given the
.I same
hostname and both use the default socket path.
Their entries are identical, and so is the address every peer would dial for
either of them.
.PP
A daemon that could not join keeps serving: it is a working server that is
not in a cluster.
It reports the failure in three places:
.IP \(bu 2
the exit status of the command;
.IP \(bu 2
an
.B ERROR
line in its log that names the holder;
.IP \(bu 2
every
.B socketley cluster
subcommand, which reports
.B "cluster mode is off"
with the reason, instead of the "not in cluster mode" that a daemon never
given
.B --cluster
reports.
.PP
Clustering is never accepted and silently not applied.
.PP
.B Docker Compose example:
.PP
A complete, working example is
.IR examples/cluster/docker-compose.yml ,
and
.I test/docker/
holds the same setup with assertions on it.
Three things about it are not optional:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B seccomp.
Docker's default profile does not permit
.BR io_uring_setup (2),
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
or
.BR io_uring_register (2).
socketley's whole I/O layer is a ring, so under that profile every node dies at
startup with
.BR "failed to init event loop" .
Run the containers with a profile that allows those three syscalls; during
development, use
.BR "security_opt: [seccomp:unconfined]" .
.IP \(bu 3
.B entrypoint.
The image's ENTRYPOINT is
.BR socketley ,
so a compose
.B command:
is passed to it as arguments.
A node that needs a shell must set
.BR "entrypoint: [\(dq/bin/sh\(dq, \(dq-c\(dq]" ,
and give its script as a one\-element list.
Compose splits a bare string into an argv, and everything past the first
.B &
is lost.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Readiness.
.B daemon --name
and
.B daemon --cluster
are configuration commands sent to a daemon that is already running.
When the socket does not answer, they start a detached daemon automatically.
Sleeping for a second before running them is a race.
If the race is lost, a second daemon is forked and the container goes down
without a message.
Wait for the daemon's own
.B "daemon started"
line instead.
.RE
.PP
Alternatively, use a Lua config file.
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
does not start a daemon; when none answers yet, it fails and applies nothing.
The one\-second sleep below is the same race, so a real entrypoint should wait
for the
.B "daemon started"
line here too:
.PP
.nf
\-\- /etc/socketley/setup.lua
daemon = { name = "api1", cluster = "/cluster" }
runtimes = {
    { type = "server", name = "web", port = 9001, group = "api", start = true }
}
.fi
.PP
.nf
socketley daemon &
sleep 1 && socketley --lua /etc/socketley/setup.lua
.fi
.PP
.B Inspecting the cluster:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster ls
socketley cluster ps
socketley cluster group api
socketley cluster stats
socketley cluster watch
.fi
.PP
.B Stale detection:
.TS
l l l.
Scenario	Behavior	Recovery
_
Graceful shutdown	File deleted	Immediate
Container killed	Stale heartbeat	~10 seconds
Container restart	File overwritten	Immediate
.TE
.SS Raft mode (experimental, opt-in)
.B Experimental.
A daemon started with
.B \-\-raft\-listen
runs one Raft node on its own loop.
Nothing else changes: the cluster directory above, and every runtime, proxy and
cache that is not declared as a cluster runtime, behave exactly as they do
without it.
The Raft cluster elects a leader, replicates a log to disk and keeps the
membership.
The state machine behind the log holds the cluster's runtimes (see
.B Cluster runtimes
below).
A cluster runtime is declared once, and the leader places it on distinct nodes.
When a node dies, its runtimes are started on a survivor.
A cache is replicated.
When the node that holds its primary dies, the leader promotes the follower
that holds the most (see
.B Cluster caches
below).
.PP
.B Flags
(given on the command line that starts the daemon, like
.BR \-\-cpus ;
refused together with
.B \-\-name
and
.BR \-\-cluster ):
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-listen " ip:port"
Enables Raft mode and accepts peer connections on this address.
The address must be numeric, because a peer's address travels through the
replicated log and is dialled without a DNS lookup.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-advertise " ip:port"
The address that peers dial, when it differs from the listening address.
Required when
.B \-\-raft\-listen
is a wildcard.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-name " name"
This node's name in the membership, which its certificate must also carry.
The certificate carries it as a DNS subjectAltName or common name.
Default: the advertised address.
A name belongs to one node.
The leader refuses a node that joins under the name of a member.
It also refuses a name that reads the same as a member's once
.B {node}
has made it safe for a command line (see
.BR "Cluster runtimes" ).
The refused node stops, with the reason in its log and in
.BR "cluster raft\-status" .
Two nodes with the same name would give every cluster runtime the same socket
path or file.
.TP
.B \-\-raft\-init
Founds a new cluster with a single voter, on first start only.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-join " ip:port"
Joins the cluster that the member at this address belongs to, on first start
only.
The node is added as a learner and receives the log without voting.
Once its log has caught up to the leader's commit index, a single-server change
promotes it to voter.
A member that is not the leader passes the request on.
.TP
.BI "\-\-raft\-cert " file " \-\-raft\-key " file " \-\-raft\-ca " file
Mutual TLS between members, which is required.
Every member's certificate is signed by the CA.
A connection is closed if its certificate does not name the member it
introduces itself as.
That name is the one the membership records for its id or, for a newcomer, the
name it claims.
The join target's id is not known yet, so its certificate is held to the
address that was dialled.
.TP
.B \-\-raft\-insecure
Runs Raft in cleartext.
It is refused together with the certificate flags.
Without this flag and without the certificate flags, the daemon refuses to
start.
.TP
.BI \-\-raft\-down\-after " duration"
How long the leader waits for a node's lease request before it declares the
node down and moves its replicas.
Takes seconds, or a value with an
.B s
or
.B ms
suffix (\c
.BR 4 ,
.BR 4s ,
.BR 3500ms ).
Default: four times the shortest election timeout, which is 4 s at the default
tick.
Values below three times the shortest election timeout (3 s at the default
tick) are refused, because a leader change would then look like a node loss.
Every member of one cluster should use the same value; see
.B Cluster runtimes
for what depends on it.
.PP
A daemon whose state directory already holds Raft state ignores
.B \-\-raft\-init
and
.BR \-\-raft\-join ,
and comes back as the member it was.
A daemon with no state and neither flag refuses to start.
A daemon that cannot bind the port or read its state exits 2, rather than serve
without the cluster it was asked for.
.PP
.B On disk:
.IR state-dir /raft/
holds
.B node\-id
(a random 64-bit id, generated once),
.BI wal\- seq .log
(CRC32C-checked records: hard state, entries, truncations) and
.BI snap\- index \- term
files, all with mode 0600.
Every write is followed by an fsync linked behind it in io_uring.
Nothing a node says to a peer leaves before that fsync reports.
Recovery cuts off a torn tail at the first bad record.
The log is compacted every 1000 applied entries.
.PP
.B Timing:
Raft ticks every 100 ms, measured from elapsed monotonic time.
The election timeout is 1\(en2 s, a heartbeat is sent every tick, and pre-vote
and check-quorum are used.
A leader stopped by SIGTERM hands leadership to its most up-to-date voter
(TimeoutNow) before it exits, so the cluster does not wait out an election
timeout.
.PP
.B "socketley cluster raft\-status"
prints this node's id and name, role, term, leader, commit and applied indexes,
the log's range, the voters and the learners.
It also prints whether a quorum of voters was heard from within an election
timeout, and the transport and WAL counters.
It exits 0 when there is a leader and a reachable quorum, and 2 when there is
not, saying so on its last line.
It exits 1 on a daemon that is not in Raft mode.
.PP
.B Cluster runtimes.
A cluster runtime is declared once, for the whole cluster, and every node
applies the same log:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster create server api \-p 8080 \-\-replicas 2
socketley cluster create server api \-\-listen unix:/run/api\-{node}.sock
socketley cluster scale api 3
socketley cluster remove api
socketley cluster placement
.fi
.PP
.B cluster create
takes
.IR type ,
.I name
and the flags that
.B create
takes, plus
.B \-\-replicas
(default 1). The type is a server, a proxy, a client or a cache (see
.BR "Cluster caches" ).
The flags are stored as a list of arguments, so a value with a space in it
stays one argument.
They are run as they are on every node that holds a replica, so a file they
name must exist on each node.
A relative path is made absolute against the caller's directory, as for
.BR create .
Any
.B {node}
in the flags is replaced by the name of the node running the replica
.RB ( \-\-raft\-name ,
with every character other than letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.B _
and
.B .
replaced by
.BR _ ).
That is what lets several nodes of a test share one host.
Two replicas that both ask for
.B \-p 8080
would collide, and
.B \-\-listen unix:/run/api\-{node}.sock
does not.
On separate hosts, each node binds its own
.B \-p
and needs no placeholder.
The node that takes the command also runs the flags through
.BR "create \-\-test" .
So a typo is refused there, with exit 1, instead of being found on every node
at once.
.B \-\-test
itself is refused in a spec.
.PP
A command may be given to any node; a follower hands it to the leader.
Success is silent.
When the command returns, it has taken effect on the node that took it.
If it has not within five seconds, the CLI says so and exits 2.
Exit 1 is a refusal: a duplicate, an unknown name, a replica count outside 1 to
64, a runtime of that name already on this node, or bad flags.
Exit 2 means the cluster has no leader that can take the command.
On a daemon that is not in Raft mode, every verb says so and exits 1.
.PP
.B cluster scale
changes the replica count.
When the count shrinks, it takes a replica from the most loaded node.
A cluster runtime has no edit: remove it and declare it again.
.PP
.B Placement.
The leader alone decides placement, and writes each assignment to the log, so
every node agrees on it.
The replicas of one runtime go to distinct nodes.
Each replica goes to the node that holds the fewest replicas, and to the lowest
node id on a tie.
When a node is declared down, its replicas are moved to live nodes.
A node that comes back, or a new node that joins, is not given work back.
Nothing is rebalanced, so a running replica is never moved for tidiness.
If more replicas are asked for than there are live nodes, the cluster runs what
fits, and
.B cluster placement
says how many replicas are unplaced.
.B cluster placement
prints:
.IP \(bu 2
the runtimes with their replicas, where each is placed and how many are
unplaced;
.IP \(bu 2
each cache's primary and the copies it has (see
.BR "Cluster caches" );
.IP \(bu 2
the nodes with the number of replicas each holds and, on the leader, whether the
leader counts each one up and when it last heard from it;
.IP \(bu 2
this node's replicas with what each is doing: running, waiting for a lease,
failed with the reason, or blocked by a local runtime of the same name.
.PP
.B On each node,
a reconciliation runs at the end of every Raft tick.
It compares what the log says the node owns with what the node runs.
It creates a replica with
.B create \-s
and stops, starts and removes it with
.BR stop ,
.B start
and
.BR remove .
This is the same code the CLI uses, and nothing else spawns a replica.
The reconciliation never waits for a worker to come up, and runs at most four
commands per tick.
So a replica whose startup hangs, or fifty replicas arriving at once, cannot
stall the Raft loop; a failure shows as the replica's state on a later tick.
A replica that fails to start is tried again every three seconds.
Its reason is logged once and shown by
.BR "cluster placement" .
A local runtime with the same name is never touched.
A replica is marked
.B [cluster]
after its state in
.B ls
and
.BR ps ,
.B \(dqcluster\(dq:true
in
.BR "\-\-json" ,
and
.B cluster:replica
in
.BR stats .
A replica has no state file, so a node that restarts does not bring back
replicas that have moved away.
.BR stop ,
.BR start ,
.B remove
and
.B edit
refuse a replica by name, since the next tick would undo them.
.PP
.B Liveness and the lease.
Every quarter election timeout, a follower asks the leader to count it alive.
The leader answers when it holds a quorum.
The leader declares a node down when it has not heard a request from it for
.B \-\-raft\-down\-after
(the failure timeout).
A new leader starts every node's clock at its own election.
A node may run its replicas only while it holds a lease.
The lease lasts three quarters of the failure timeout, counted from when the
request was sent, so a late answer shortens it and a stalled loop cannot extend
it.
The leader's own lease is its last look at a quorum.
The margin between the lease and the failure timeout is a quarter of the
failure timeout (1 s by default).
That margin is the time a node has to stop its replicas before the leader gives
them to another node.
A lease that had lapsed is used again only once the node has applied the
leader's log up to where it ended when the lease was granted.
So a node that comes back does not run a replica that moved while it was away.
.PP
.B Partitions and duplicates.
A node that cannot reach a quorum stops serving its replicas within its lease.
At the defaults this was measured at 2.9\(en3.8 s after the last answer.
The node keeps the replicas defined, so they start again the moment it is
granted a lease.
The majority moves them after the failure timeout, at least a quarter of that
timeout later.
So a minority never serves what it cannot confirm it owns, even a minority that
holds the old leader, and a healed node runs only what the log still gives it.
The same holds for a leader that loses its quorum.
It means that a cluster that loses its quorum stops serving every cluster
replica, on every node, until it has a quorum again: the cluster chooses one
copy or none over two.
.PP
A leader change can take longer than the lease, as with a split vote (about one
in ten kills of a leader in simulation).
It then stops a survivor's replicas until the new leader has granted a lease, a
few hundred milliseconds after it is elected.
The lease is enforced by the daemon's loop.
So a daemon that is hung and not dead (SIGSTOP, a blocked disk) cannot stop the
workers of a server, a proxy or a client, which are separate processes.
A cache's worker fences itself (see
.BR "Cluster caches" ).
Whatever a replica does on its way down, such as a
.B \-\-drain
that flushes its connections, spends part of the margin.
.PP
.B Failover time,
from kill \-9 of a node to its replica running on a survivor, was measured at
the defaults.
It was measured on one host with a real daemon, and in 60 seeds of a simulation
of the same logic.
It is about 3.9\(en4.3 s when the node was a follower (the failure timeout and
a commit).
It is about 5.1\(en6.0 s when the node was the leader (an election first).
In the simulation it reached 8.7 s when a split vote stretched the election.
.PP
.B Cluster caches.
A cache is a cluster runtime that holds data, so keeping it running is not
enough:
.PP
.nf
socketley cluster create cache db \-p 6379 \-\-replicas 3
socketley cluster create proxy gw \-p 7000 \-\-backend @db \-\-replicas 3
.fi
.PP
The leader places the replicas as for any runtime, and the log names one of
them the
.B primary
of an
.BR epoch .
An epoch is a number that only grows, so two leaders that race to promote agree
on which was first.
The other replicas are its followers.
They follow it with the cache's own replication (\c
.BR \-\-replicate :
a full dump, then every write), dialling the primary's
.B \-p
on the host that its node advertises to Raft.
That is why a cluster cache needs
.BR \-p .
It is refused with
.BR \-\-listen ,
.BR \-\-shards ,
.BR \-\-tls ,
.B \-\-replicate
(the cluster sets it) and without a port.
On one host each replica needs its own port, which is what
.B {node}
is for: with nodes named 1, 2 and 3,
.B \-p 630{node}
gives ports 6301, 6302 and 6303.
On separate hosts a follower is a peer on another machine, and a cache serves
such a peer only with
.BR \-\-public .
Put it in the spec (the tests here ran on one host and did not try it).
A cache with one replica has a primary and nothing to promote.
The first primary is the replica on the lowest node id.
It is chosen once every replica has said it exists, or after an election
timeout.
A replica is a process the log can name (its
.BR incarnation ).
A restarted replica is another incarnation, holding nothing the old one did.
.PP
.B Who may write.
A replica takes a client's command only under its worker's last order from its
daemon.
The daemon sends an order every 100 ms, and each order stands for the lease the
daemon held when it sent it.
A replica is in one of three roles:
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B fenced
replica answers every command, reads included, with an error, and refuses
.BR replicate ;
.IP \(bu 2
a
.B follower
answers reads and refuses writes;
.IP \(bu 2
the
.B primary
takes writes.
.PP
A replica starts fenced.
It leads only if this node holds a lease AND the log names the very process it
runs as the primary.
Otherwise it follows whoever the log names, or waits when nobody is named.
A fence
.B keeps the data:
the process stays up.
So a node that lost its quorum for a minute and got it back is the primary
again, with everything it had.
A follower keeps its link and its copy through a period without a lease.
When the orders stop for any reason (the node lost its quorum, or the daemon
hung or died), the worker fences itself when the last order runs out, without
the daemon's help.
Measured in nine runs with real daemons, a worker whose daemon was stopped with
SIGSTOP refused writes after 2.7\(en3.1 s.
A primary that lost its quorum stopped taking writes after 2.8\(en3.2 s.
The lease is three quarters of the failure timeout, 3 s at the defaults.
.PP
.B Promotion.
The leader replaces a primary in three cases:
.IP \(bu 2
nobody leads;
.IP \(bu 2
the primary's node has been declared down (in the same round that takes its
replica away);
.IP \(bu 2
the primary's node is alive, but for the failure timeout it has not named, in a
lease request, the process the log calls the primary.
.PP
The third case covers a primary whose daemon restarted: it comes back empty and
does not lead, and a replica that has the data is promoted after that timeout.
For each cache replica a node runs, every lease request carries the replica's
incarnation, the epoch of the primary whose stream its copy came from, its
offset in that stream, and whether it leads.
The offset is a count of records.
The primary counts every record it produces, whether or not a follower is
connected.
The end-of-dump marker carries the count at which the dump was taken.
A follower's offset is that number plus the records it has applied since.
A copy is empty from the first line of a dump to its last.
.PP
The leader promotes the replica whose copy is the most up to date.
It compares epochs first (offsets of two primaries are not on one scale), then
offsets, then the lowest node id.
It writes the chosen replica to the log with the next epoch.
The offsets the leader has are up to about half a second old (a stats push and
a request interval).
But by the time the leader looks, the primary has been gone for the failure
timeout, so the followers' last reports are their final positions.
A primary that comes back, or a node that is given a replica again, is a new
process.
It follows the primary of the epoch it finds, and takes a full dump that
replaces whatever it held.
So nothing it wrote that the cluster did not have survives, and it does not
lead a cache whose log names another primary.
A cache that is scaled down never loses its primary.
.PP
.B How long,
from kill \-9 of the node that holds the primary to a survivor taking a write,
was measured on one host with real daemons at the default timing.
It was 3.9\(en4.1 s when the node was not the Raft leader (the failure timeout
and a commit; eleven kills).
It was 5.2\(en5.7 s when the node was the leader (an election first; four
kills).
In a simulation of the same logic over 60 kills it was 4.1\(en4.2 s and
5.6\(en7.2 s; the longer ones were split votes.
A follower dials the new primary within about a fifth of a second of being
told.
A proxy follows through the endpoint group that the primary is published as.
The first write through a proxy on a survivor succeeded 20\(en95 ms after the
first write made directly to the new primary (five runs).
.PP
.B What a failover can lose.
Replication is asynchronous: the primary answers a write and streams it
afterwards.
So when the primary's node dies, the promoted follower lacks the acknowledged
writes it had not received.
What survives is always a prefix of the primary's history.
Replication is ordered, and the tests read every acknowledged key back to check
that no write is lost that a later one outlived.
How long the lost tail is depends on how far behind the best follower was.
It was measured with a client writing continuously and kill \-9 of the
primary's node, three kills each:
.IP \(bu 2
one connection writing one key at a time, about a thousand a second (4,500 to
4,900 acknowledged): none missing;
.IP \(bu 2
200 writes pipelined at a time, as fast as one connection goes (630,000 to
720,000 acknowledged, some 230,000 a second): none missing;
.IP \(bu 2
the same, with both followers' workers stopped (SIGSTOP) for the four seconds
before the kill, so that neither was receiving: about 1.6 million acknowledged
and 876,000 to 922,000 of them missing.
That is every write the primary took while no follower had it.
.PP
So the window is how far behind the best follower was when the primary died.
It is a few writes when the followers keep up (the first two conditions lost
none), and everything since a follower stopped reading when they do not.
Only what a follower had already received survives.
In a separate check, a stopped follower held about 20,000 of 925,000
acknowledged keys after its leader was killed.
A primary drops a follower that is 8 MiB behind.
After that, the primary keeps acknowledging writes that no follower is
receiving, until that follower dials again.
A machine that loses power, or a network that is cut, could not be tried
without root.
There is no synchronous mode: a write that must survive its primary is written
to two caches.
.PP
.B Finding the primary.
.B "cluster placement"
prints, under
.BR "CACHE PRIMARIES" ,
each cache's primary and epoch.
For each replica it also prints the epoch and offset of its copy, and how old
that news is.
The leader hears every node's news; the other nodes hear only their own.
A proxy with
.BI \-\-backend " @name"
follows the primary.
On every node that holds a lease, the cache's name is published as a group of
one, the primary's host and port, and it is republished when the primary moves.
The
.B stats
of a replica carry
.BR cluster_role ,
.BR cluster_epoch ,
.B cluster_offset
and
.BR cluster_incarnation .
.PP
.B Limits.
A cache whose replicas all die at once starts again empty, because the data
lived in memory.
A
.B \-\-persistent
file is loaded by a restarted process, but it does not take part in choosing
the primary.
It is replaced when the replica syncs from the primary that was chosen.
A cache with one replica loses its data with its node.
A worker that answers its daemon but takes no orders is not replaced.
.PP
.B Not yet:
.IP \(bu 2
removing a member;
.IP \(bu 2
snapshots sent in chunks (a snapshot goes in one frame of at most 16 MiB);
.IP \(bu 2
a cluster id that keeps two clusters from mixing;
.IP \(bu 2
editing a cluster runtime in place;
.IP \(bu 2
moving a replica off a node before the node is stopped for maintenance (a node
stopped with SIGTERM keeps its assignments until the failure timeout);
.IP \(bu 2
a worker of a server, a proxy or a client that fences itself when its daemon is
hung (a cache's worker does);
.IP \(bu 2
synchronous replication of a cache;
.IP \(bu 2
a replica that fails to start on one node being tried on another.
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS Release Package
.nf
curl \-fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/HiImSmiley/Socketley/main/install.sh | sudo sh
.fi
.PP
The script installs the newest release
.BR .deb .
It asks before it installs each dependency that is missing.
If the package was built for a newer distribution than this one, the script
stops without changing anything.
The script can also install a package you already have, answer every question
with its default, and remove socketley; its
.B \-\-help
describes these options.
.SS From Source
.nf
git clone https://github.com/HiImSmiley/Socketley
cd Socketley
bash scripts/install\-from\-source.sh
.fi
.PP
The script performs these steps:
.IP \(bu 2
checks the kernel, architecture and compiler;
.IP \(bu 2
installs the build dependencies;
.IP \(bu 2
builds liburing and the static archives the tree links against;
.IP \(bu 2
builds the Release configuration;
.IP \(bu 2
installs the binary, this manual page and the systemd service;
.IP \(bu 2
starts the daemon.
.PP
Its
.B \-\-help
explains how to list every command without running any, and how to remove what
the script installed.
.SS Quick Install
To install from a tree that is already built:
.nf
sudo bash packaging/install.sh
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Debian Package
.nf
bash packaging/build-deb.sh 1.1.0
sudo dpkg -i socketley_1.1.0_amd64.deb
sudo systemctl start socketley
.fi
.SS Uninstall
.nf
sudo bash packaging/uninstall.sh
.fi
.SH STATE PERSISTENCE
The daemon persists runtime configurations as JSON files. When a runtime is
created, its configuration is saved. When the daemon starts, it restores all
persisted runtimes. Runtimes that were running when the daemon stopped are
started again automatically.
.PP
Runtimes therefore survive daemon restarts and system reboots, like Docker
containers with the restart-always policy.
.PP
State is saved after the create, start, stop, remove and edit commands.
The
.B was_running
field records whether a runtime should be started automatically when the daemon
starts.
.SS A State File Is A Secret
A runtime's state file is not a note about the runtime: it
.I is
the runtime. The file holds these fields in plaintext:
.TP
.B master_pw
The password given to
.BR "\-\-master\-pw" ,
stored verbatim.
.TP
.BR key_path ", " mesh_client_key
The paths of the TLS and mTLS private keys that this runtime uses.
.TP
.B lua_script
The path of the handler script that the daemon loads.
The handler runs with the daemon's privileges; see
.B Handler Privileges
under
.BR "LUA CONFIGURATION" .
.PP
The daemon restores every runtime from these files when it starts. Whoever can
.I write
one of these files therefore chooses what the daemon runs at its next start.
That is the same power as being able to send the daemon commands.
.PP
For this reason the daemon creates the files with mode
.BR 0600 ,
set explicitly at creation rather than inherited from its umask. The packaged
state directory has mode
.B 0750
and is owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
Neither mode is configurable, and there is no supported reason to widen them.
An operator reaches this data through the CLI, over a socket with the same
group boundary, not by reading the directory.
.PP
.B "Earlier versions wrote these files 0666 & ~umask."
That gave 0644 under systemd's default umask, and 0666 for a daemon started
from a shell with
.BR "umask 0" .
Any local user could read a master password, and in the 0666 case could also
rewrite the record. On its first start after an upgrade, the daemon narrows
every file it finds in its state directory to 0600 and logs how many it
changed:
.PP
.nf
state: tightened 3 file(s) in /var/lib/socketley/runtimes to 0600 ...
.fi
.PP
The repair only ever narrows modes, and it is idempotent. It does not touch the
directory itself, which belongs to the installer. If this line appears on every
start, something outside socketley is putting the old modes back.
.PP
.B "A password that was exposed should be treated as exposed."
The repair closes the hole, but it cannot un-read what was already readable.
Rotate any
.B \-\-master\-pw
that lived on a multi-user machine under an affected version, either with
.B "socketley edit"
or by recreating the runtime.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
All of these variables are optional.
Each is read by the process that needs it, which for most is a runtime's worker
process rather than the daemon.
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
and
.B SOCKETLEY_CONFIG
select which daemon this is and which file it reads.
.BR EDITOR ", " VISUAL " and " TMPDIR
are read by the command line, and the
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_*
variables by the command that uses them.
.B SOCKETLEY_FAST_LO
is set by the daemon for its workers.
The entries that begin "A test hook" are for the test suite.
The rest are tuning knobs. Their defaults suit a general-purpose deployment and
only need changing for an unusual workload.
.PP
You can also set each knob in the
.B settings
table of the daemon's configuration file, using its name in lower case and
without
.B SOCKETLEY_
(see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ).
.B socketley settings
lists every knob with its value and where that value came from. If a variable
in the environment holds a number outside the knob's range, or not a whole
number, the process that reads it logs this once and uses the default. The same
value in the
.B settings
table stops the daemon instead (see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" ).
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
Selects which daemon this is, or which daemon the CLI talks to.
It overrides the default socket lookup
.RB ( /run/socketley/socketley.sock
when installed and privileged, otherwise
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock ).
.IP
No workload calls for setting it. A deployed node, whether a container or a
host, has a filesystem namespace to itself. One daemon on the default path is
then the whole arrangement, and that is what the systemd unit ships. Clustering
does not use this variable either. Cluster members publish themselves into a
shared directory and reach each other over network addresses, never over
socket paths. See
.BR "CLUSTER MODE" .
.IP
The variable exists to run several daemons inside
.I one
namespace, which happens only in development and testing. Without it, two
checkouts on one workstation, or two test runs at once, end up as the same
daemon. Whichever process binds the default path first owns it, and every
later one silently talks to that process instead of starting its own. Anything
that starts a daemon on the default path claims the identity that the real
daemon runs under. The test suite therefore gives every test a socket of its
own.
.IP
The variable selects the state directory as well as the socket, and it must.
Several daemons in one namespace that read one state directory do not behave as
separate daemons. Each one restores everything in the directory at startup,
including the others' runtimes, onto ports the others are already serving. And
each
.B remove
deletes a state file that another, still running daemon depends on. See
.BR "WHERE STATE LIVES" .
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CPUS=\fImax\fR|\fI0\-3\fR|\fI0,1,2,3\fR
Sets the CPUs that the daemon's I/O thread may run on.
Default:
.BR max ,
which applies no pinning at all, rather than a mask of the CPUs that happen to
be online now.
.IP
If the set names a CPU this machine does not have, or comes out empty, the
daemon refuses it, logs a warning and runs unpinned. It never rounds the set up
to
.BR max .
.IP
.BI "socketley daemon \-\-cpus " set
sets the same thing on the command line, and wins when both are given. On the
command line, an impossible set is refused outright and the daemon does not
start. The reason is that a flag typed for this one run is a requirement, not an
inherited default.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_SIZE
Sets the size in bytes of each buffer in a cache runtime's io_uring
provided-buffer ring.
Default:
.BR 65536 .
Larger buffers let one read completion carry more commands. The cost of the
ring is its footprint (count times size) competing for the core's cache; see
the section on the receive ring for what was measured.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_BUF_COUNT
Sets the number of buffers in that ring.
Default:
.BR 64 .
Together with the buffer size, it bounds how much concurrent traffic the
runtime can absorb before the pool runs dry. When the pool is dry, reads fall
back to a slower per-read path. The pool costs count times size of memory per
cache runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_KILL_AFTER_RENAME
A test hook that kills a sharded storage worker in the middle of a commit.
When a commit's key begins with this string, the worker is killed with
.B SIGKILL
after it renames the body into place and before it writes the journal record.
A test can thus put a crash at the one step that leaves a file the journal does
not name. Unset by default.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_KILL_AFTER_EVENT
A test hook that kills a storage worker after it records an event and before it
makes the change.
It applies to a change of a key that begins with this string, in a bucket that
sends events for that change. The worker is killed with
.B SIGKILL
once the change's event record is durable and before the change itself is made.
A test can then show that the restarted worker sends no event for the change.
Unset by default.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HOLD_CONFIRM
A test hook that makes a storage worker answer a change but never confirm it.
For a key that begins with this string, the worker makes the change and answers
it. It never writes the record that says the change was made, and never sends
the change's events. This is the state a crash right after the answer would
leave. A test can then kill the worker and show that the restarted worker sends
the events. Unset by default.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_PART_LIST_MAX
A test hook that limits how many bytes a multipart object's part lists may take
in its header.
The limit applies separately to the part sizes, to the part numbers of an
encrypted object, and to the parts' checksums. A list that would take more is
stored after the object's body instead.
Default:
.BR 4096 ,
which takes some five hundred parts of unequal sizes to exceed. A small value
lets a test reach a list stored after the body with only a few parts.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_DOC_FAULT
A test hook that makes a sharded storage runtime drop one phase of a bucket
document change.
The value is a directory. While a file named
.IB shard . phase
(\fBprepare\fR, \fBcommit\fR or \fBabort\fR) exists in it, that shard answers
the message for that phase as an unreachable sibling would, without acting on
it. A test can thus lose a message at each step of the change. Unset by
default.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP3
Set to
.B 0
to stop a
.B \-\-tls
entrance from opening a UDP socket for HTTP/3.
The entrance then offers HTTP/3 to no client and names none in
.BR Alt\-Svc .
HTTP/2 over TCP is unaffected.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_HTTP2
Set to
.B 0
to turn HTTP/2 off at an entrance.
The entrance then speaks HTTP/1.1 to every client over
.BR \-\-tls .
See the entrance under the dns runtime's
.BR \-\-proxy .
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP2
Set to
.B 0
to turn HTTP/2 off for storage runtimes.
A storage runtime then speaks HTTP/1.1 to every client. See
.B HTTP/2
under the storage runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3
Set to
.B 0
to stop a
.B \-\-tls
storage runtime from opening a UDP socket for HTTP/3.
The runtime then offers HTTP/3 to no client. See
.B HTTP/3
under the storage runtime.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_ADVERTISE
Sets the port that the
.B Alt\-Svc
header names for HTTP/3.
Use it when a proxy in front forwards UDP from a port other than the runtime's
own. 0 advertises no port. Default: the runtime's port.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_CC
Selects the congestion controller that HTTP/3 sends with:
.BR bbr ,
.B cubic
or
.BR reno .
Default:
.BR bbr .
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HTTP3_RETRY_AFTER
Sets how many QUIC handshakes may be under way before a new client is sent a
Retry.
A client that is sent a Retry must prove its address first. 0 sends a Retry to
every client. Default: 64.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_SPLICE_MIN
Sets the body size in bytes from which a storage GET is sent by splice from the
file to the socket.
Smaller bodies are read into memory and written instead.
Default:
.BR 65536 ;
.B 0
splices every body. Below the default, a read and a write cost less than the
pipe a splice needs. This setting changes what
.B zero_copy_bytes
counts and how much CPU a GET takes, never what a GET returns.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HOT_BYTES
Sets how many bytes of small objects a storage shard keeps in memory to answer
repeated GETs.
An object counts as small up to 60000 bytes. A GET for a kept object is
answered without opening its file. A shard keeps objects of any key, whichever
shard owns it.
Default:
.B 33554432
(32 MiB);
.B 0
keeps nothing. This changes CPU use, GET throughput and the memory a worker
holds, never what a GET returns.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_COMPACT_FLOOR
A test aid that sets the fewest records a bucket's journal must hold before a
storage shard compacts it.
Compaction rewrites the journal without its superseded records.
Default:
.BR 4096 .
A test lowers it to see compactions without thousands of writes. This changes
how often a journal is rewritten, never what it holds.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS
Sets how many helper threads a storage worker uses to compute the digests of
large uploads.
The digests are the ETag's MD5, any \fBx\-amz\-checksum\-\fR the request asked
for or the default CRC64NVME, and the SHA\-256 that verifies a signed body (a
hex payload hash or aws\-chunked signatures). The threads keep the worker's
loop from being the one core that all upload hashing has to fit in.
Default:
.BR 3 ,
or the machine's cores divided by the runtime's shards when that is fewer, but
at least one. This way the shards' threads together do not outnumber the cores.
.B 0
hashes on the loop.
.IP
The first upload of 256 KiB or more (or of unknown length) starts the threads,
and they end with the runtime. Smaller bodies are hashed in line, because the
hand-off costs more than their digest. For the same reason, a body that arrives
a few segments at a time is handed over 64 KiB at a time. This changes CPU use
and the throughput of large puts, never what is stored.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_LIFECYCLE_DAY_SECS
A test aid that sets how many seconds a bucket lifecycle rule counts as one
day.
The range is 1 to 86400. Only the lifecycle sweep reads it, and the sweep then
also runs as often as every second. Unset, a day is a day. Because it changes
when objects expire, never set it for a runtime holding data anyone wants (see
Bucket lifecycle).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SHARE_NOTIFY_ALLOW
Lists the URL prefixes, comma\-separated, that a share link's webhook may begin
with.
Unset, the allowed prefixes are the webhooks of Discord, Slack and ntfy.sh (see
Drive, links, media and the web interface).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_SHARE_BURN_GRACE_SECS
A test aid that sets how many seconds a burning share link's files outlive its
last allowed download.
Default: 600 (see Drive, links, media and the web interface).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RESOLV_CONF
A test aid that names the file from which the resolver on a runtime's loop
reads its nameservers, search list and options.
The file uses the
.BR resolv.conf (5)
format, so a test can point the resolver at a nameserver of its own. Default:
/etc/resolv.conf (see What can stop the event loop). A mail runtime's SPF, DKIM
and DMARC lookups also ask these nameservers.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_MX_PORT
A test aid that sets the port on which a mail runtime hands mail to a domain's
exchangers.
Default: 25. A test's own server can then act as an exchanger.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_TLS_CA
A test aid that names the CA file a mail runtime checks an exchanger's
certificate against.
It applies where DANE or MTA\-STS asks for that check. Default: the system's
roots. A test's own exchanger can then be trusted.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_STS_CA
A test aid that names the CA file a mail runtime checks an MTA\-STS policy
server against.
Default: the system's roots.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_STS_CONNECT
A test aid that sets the
.IR host : port
from which every MTA\-STS policy is fetched.
The name in the policy's URL is still the one checked. A test can thus serve
the policies of domains it does not own. Default: each policy's own host.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_DELAY_NOTICE_MS
A test aid that sets how many milliseconds a mail runtime tries a message
before it tells the sender the message is late.
This applies only to senders who asked for the notice (NOTIFY=DELAY). Default:
four hours.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_SPOOL_MOVE_MS
A test aid that sets how many milliseconds a sealed spool segment waits before
it is used again.
Before the segment is reused, the few messages still in it are moved. Default:
one minute.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_DEAD_DOMAIN_MS
A test aid that sets how many milliseconds a mail runtime holds on to a domain
that cannot take mail.
This covers a domain it found not to exist or to take no mail. After this time,
the runtime looks the domain up again. Default: ten minutes.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_PUSH_CONNECT
A test aid that sets the
.IR host : port
to which every JMAP push is posted.
The certificate is still checked for the name in the push URL. A test can thus
receive the pushes of a URL it does not own. Default: the URL's own host.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_PUSH_CA
A test aid that names a file of CA certificates for checking a JMAP push URL's
certificate.
Default: the system's CA certificates.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_IDLE_MS
A test aid that sets how many milliseconds a connection to a mail runtime may
stay quiet before it is closed.
The same limit applies to how long a TLS handshake may take. The runtime tells
the client before it closes the connection. Defaults: five minutes for SMTP,
POP3 and ManageSieve, thirty minutes for IMAP, and thirty seconds for a
handshake.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_BAYES_FOLD_MS
A test aid that sets how many milliseconds new spam filter learning stays in a
mail runtime's journal.
After this time, the first shard folds it into the filter's file. Default: ten
minutes.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_IDP_CLIENT_CA
A test aid that names a file of CA certificates for checking the client
metadata documents an idp fetches.
The certificate served with each such document is checked against this file.
Default: the system's CA certificates.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_IDP_CLIENT_CONNECT
A test aid that sets the host:port from which every client metadata document is
fetched.
The name in the document's address is still the one sent and checked. Default:
the host in that address.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_IDP_COMPACT_AFTER
A test aid that sets how many account changes an idp runtime journals before it
folds them into its snapshot.
A test can thus see the snapshot written while changes go on. Default: 1000.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_SPOOL_FROZEN_US
A test aid that names every message of a mail runtime after this fixed
microsecond instead of the clock.
Writers in two shards then produce the same name, as they would if they wrote
in the same microsecond. Default: the clock.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_QUEUE_SCALE
A test aid that runs a mail runtime's retries and
.B \-\-queue\-lifetime
this many times faster.
A test can then see a message tried again and given up within seconds.
Default: 1.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_BUSY_HOLD_MS
A test aid that sets how many milliseconds a mail runtime pauses a domain after
one of its exchangers answered 421.
During the pause, the runtime opens no new connection to that domain. Each
further 421 doubles the pause, up to fifteen minutes, until a message is taken.
Default: thirty seconds.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MAIL_SPOOL_KEEP_BYTES
A test aid that sets how many bytes of message bodies a mail spool keeps in
memory beside its segments.
Default: 64 MiB. A small value lets a test have bodies read back from disk.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_NOTIFY_HOSTS
A test aid that names a hosts file for a storage runtime's notification
webhooks.
The file holds one
.I name address
pair per line. The webhooks' resolver reads it before the system resolver, on
every resolve, so a test can point a webhook's host name at another address.
Unset, a name is asked of the resolver on the loop and then of the system's
resolver (see Event notifications).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_NOTIFY_QUEUE
Sets the most undelivered events a storage shard holds.
Beyond that, a new event is dropped and counted in
.BR notify_dropped .
Default: 10000. A test lowers it to reach the bound.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_CLUSTER_QUEUE
Sets the most changes a storage shard queues for another node of its cluster.
Beyond that, the shard lets the queued changes go. Once that node answers, the
shard sends it everything instead (see Cluster). Default: 100000. A test lowers
it to reach the bound with a few writes.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_REPLICATION_HOSTS
A test aid that names a hosts file for a storage runtime's replication targets.
The file holds one
.I name address
pair per line. The targets' resolver reads it before the system resolver, on
every resolve, so a test can name a target by a host name. Unset, a name is
asked of the resolver on the loop and then of the system's resolver (see
Replication).
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_STOP_HOLD_MS
A test aid that makes a storage shard's stop wait before it lets go of its
directory.
The value is in milliseconds, 0 to 3000. The wait starts after the shard's
in\-flight work has finished, and it is multiplied by the shard's number plus
one. A test can thus start a runtime beside shards that are still stopping.
Unset, a stop waits only for its own work. It slows every stop and restart, so
never use it on a runtime in use.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_START_SPIN_MS
A test aid that makes a storage shard's start keep the CPU busy before it opens
its store.
The value is in milliseconds, 0 to 30000. The shard uses CPU the way reading a
large store's files does.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_START_SLEEP_MS
A test aid that makes a storage shard's start wait idle before it opens its
store.
The value is in milliseconds, 0 to 60000. This imitates a wedged start.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_LOG_FLUSH_MS
A test aid that sets how long a storage shard holds server access log lines
before it delivers them.
The value is in milliseconds, 100 to 3600000. Default: one minute.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_THREADS
Sets how many threads a storage worker uses to read its object headers when it
starts after an unclean stop.
The range is 1 to 64. Default: the number of CPUs the worker may run on, at most
16. The threads end before the listener opens. This changes how long such a
start takes, never what it finds.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECOVERY_DEPTH
Sets how many opens and header reads each recovery thread keeps in flight on an
io_uring of its own.
The range is 0 to 256. Both this value and the thread count are lowered to fit
within half of what
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
leaves.
Default:
.BR 32 ;
.B 0
reads one file at a time without a ring. This changes how long a start from a
cold cache takes, never what it finds.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_RECV_BUFS
Sets how many 16 KiB buffers a storage worker's receive ring holds; the value
must be a power of two.
Default:
.BR 64 ;
.B 0
removes the ring, and every connection reads into a buffer of its own. A
connection borrows a buffer only for the length of one completion, so an idle
keep\-alive connection holds none. When a receive finds the ring empty, it uses
a buffer of its own for that one read. Such reads are counted as
.B recv_fallbacks
in
.BR stats .
This changes the memory a worker holds, never what it returns.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_TLS_FAILURE_REPORT_SECS
Sets how often a runtime serving TLS logs one line counting the inbound
handshakes that failed since its last report.
Default: every 60 seconds; the range is 1 to 3600. A failed handshake comes from
a port scanner far more often than from a client, so each one is counted
rather than logged.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DIRECTORY
Sets the directory URL of the ACME CA that
.B \-\-acme
and
.B acme issue
use.
Default: Let's Encrypt,
.BR https://acme\-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory .
Its staging CA is
.BR https://acme\-staging\-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory .
Each CA has an account of its own.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_CHALLENGE
Sets the challenge type that
.B acme issue
and
.B \-\-acme
use to prove a name when no type is requested.
The values are
.B http\-01
(the default),
.B tls\-alpn\-01
and
.BR dns\-01 .
A wildcard name is always proven over DNS\-01, whatever this variable says.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_WAIT
Sets the seconds between publishing a DNS\-01 answer and telling the CA to look
for it.
The answer is published where the
.B dns
runtimes serve it. The wait is the time a secondary of the zone has to take the
new serial, and the delegation probe gives the secondary the same time.
Default: 10, at most 3600. A secondary that is told by NOTIFY and transfers on
it takes seconds. For a secondary that waits for its refresh, set this to the
minutes the refresh takes.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_DNS_PROBE
Names the resolver that
.B acme issue
asks whether a zone is delegated to this daemon's
.B dns
runtimes.
The value has the form
.IR host [: port ].
.B acme issue
asks about a zone of
.B \-\-dns\-zones
before it proves anything there over DNS\-01. It then asks each name server of
the zone, by its IPv4 addresses, the way a CA would.
.B off
asks nothing and treats every zone as delegated. Default: the first
.B nameserver
of
.IR /etc/resolv.conf .
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_ACME_CA
Names a PEM file of root certificates for checking the ACME CA's own HTTPS.
Use it for a CA whose certificate no system store holds, such as Pebble.
Default: the system's roots.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_ACCESS_KEY
Holds the access key that
.B git\-sync
uses to write into a library.
A storage runtime that starts git\-sync gives it temporary credentials of the
user for whom the repository is kept.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_SECRET
Holds the secret of that access key.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_SESSION_TOKEN
Holds the session token of temporary credentials, sent as x-amz-security-token.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_TOKEN
Holds a GitHub token that
.B git\-sync
uses to read a private repository.
git\-sync gives it to git as an http.extraHeader in git's environment, never on
a command line.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_FFMPEG
Names the ffmpeg that a storage runtime uses to make thumbnails and streams
under
.BR /~media/ .
The runtime reads what a film is with the
.B ffprobe
beside that ffmpeg, or the one on
.BR PATH .
Default: the first
.B ffmpeg
on
.BR PATH .
With neither, a media request is answered
.BR "501" .
The runtime reads this variable at its first media request.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_HWACCEL
Selects where a storage runtime encodes its renditions.
.B off
always uses x264 on the CPU. A render node such as
.B /dev/dri/renderD129
uses that GPU. The default is
.BR /dev/dri/renderD128 .
.IP
At its first medium, a worker runs one second of test picture through the GPU's
whole chain (upload, scale, H.264 encode). It tries the full encoder and then
the low-power one. The worker uses the GPU for renditions only when one of them
works; until it knows, renditions use x264. A rendition keeps the encoder that
its first job chose. If a rendition's first GPU job fails before anything is
made, that rendition continues with x264. HLG is tone-mapped on the CPU, HDR10
on the GPU.
.B vaapi\-unchecked:\fInode\fR
skips the test run, for testing that fallback.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_MEDIA_CACHE_MB
Sets the size in MiB beyond which a storage runtime removes the media it made
from
.IR dir /.media/.
It removes the least recently requested media first. Default: 10240. The medium
made last is kept even when it alone is larger, and no medium is removed while
it is being made.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CACHE_TTL_SWEEP_MS
Sets the interval in milliseconds between periodic sweeps for expired cache
keys.
Default:
.BR 500 .
This never affects correctness: an expired key is not served no matter when the
sweep runs, because every read path checks the expiry itself. The interval
controls only how quickly keys that are never accessed again release their
memory.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_LUA_TIMEOUT_MS
Sets how long, in milliseconds, one Lua callback invocation may hold the
event\-loop thread.
Default:
.BR 10000 .
.B 0
switches the bound off entirely, and also turns on LuaJIT's trace compiler.
The compiler is worth about 2.4x on a callback that computes, and about 1% on
one that does not. See
.BR "Callback Deadline" ,
which prices both halves of that trade.
.IP
This is the default for every script that does not declare its own
.B callback_timeout_ms
global; a script that declares one overrides it. A value that is not a whole
number of milliseconds in 0\(en3600000 is refused with a warning, and the
default is used.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_HTTP_REVALIDATE
Set to
.B 0
to switch off the response cache of plain
.BR \-\-http .
Each request then costs an open, a read and a close again. Any other value,
or leaving the variable unset, keeps the cache on. The variable is read when
the runtime starts, so a
.B restart
picks up a change. The server serves the same content either way, because the
cache is revalidated on every request. The switch exists for measurement, and
for ruling the cache out when diagnosing something else.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_KERNEL_TLS
Set to
.B 0
to keep every TLS record in this process.
Otherwise, some TLS 1.3 connections hand their sending to the kernel's TLS
layer. This happens in three cases. A server, storage or proxy connection
sends a file or object over 256 KiB. A TCP proxy's backend streams that much to
the client. An HTTP/1.1 proxy sends a client an answer body of that known
length.
.IP
The connection then changes its send key and hands the new key to the kernel's
TLS layer. The kernel encrypts what is sent, by sendfile from the page cache or
spliced from the backend, at about half the CPU of encrypting a copy in this
process. A connection that never sends that much stays as it was, because for
small answers encrypting a copy here costs less. HTTP/2 connections are never
moved.
.IP
This needs the kernel's
.B tls
module
.RB ( "modprobe tls" ).
Without the module, the connection continues in user space under its new key,
and the runtime says so once in its log. The variable is read when the runtime
starts.
.IP
Inherited by worker processes, which have no command line of their own.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_PROXY_SPLICE
Makes a
.B \-\-protocol tcp
proxy splice a pair from its first byte to its last, not only while a side
streams.
Unset by default; any value other than
.B 0
turns it on. It is read once per process and inherited by worker processes.
.IP
Without the variable, each side of a pair decides for itself. A side copies
through a buffer until four reads in a row have filled it, which marks a
stream. It then moves its bytes with
.BR splice (2)
through a pipe. The splice is called on the event loop, with both sockets
non\-blocking for the call. It never goes through io_uring, whose splice is an
io\-wq hand\-off per transfer. Four splices in a row that would not have filled
the buffer mark messages. The side then goes back to copying and gives its pipe
back. Each switch waits until nothing of the other path is in flight or queued,
so no byte overtakes another. Measured on one core, over loopback, through the
proxy:
.IP
.nf
                        copy             adaptive         from the first byte
4 GB stream             3.4\-4.2 GB/s     9.3\-9.9 GB/s     10.1 GB/s
  CPU per GB            0.24\-0.30 s      0.08\-0.11 s      0.08\-0.10 s
64 B messages, 32 conns 6.95 us           6.86 us          10.36 us
4 KB messages           7.41 us           7.37 us          9.28 us
.fi
.IP
So the variable is for measuring, not for speed. A TLS listener or TLS backend
never splices. Neither does a pair with a 127.0.0.1 leg while the daemon runs
with
.BR \-\-fast\-lo .
A proxy with an
.B on_proxy_request
or
.B on_proxy_response
hook never splices either, because the kernel would move those bytes without
the script ever seeing them. Nor does a proxy with a
.B \-\-rate\-limit
or
.BR \-\-global\-rate\-limit ,
which is charged per read.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_FAST_LO
Set by a daemon started with
.B \-\-fast\-lo
once the shortcut is attached; it is not a setting.
The value is
.BR own ,
.B all
or
.BR trust .
It tells the daemon's workers to keep a proxied pair with a 127.0.0.1 leg off
splice. The daemon clears any inherited value at startup.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NAPI_BUSY_POLL_US
Enables io_uring NAPI busy polling with this timeout in microseconds.
Unset by default. It trades CPU for lower receive latency on busy links.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_HUGEPAGE
Set to
.B 0
to stop cache workers from asking glibc for transparent huge pages.
On by default, and only a cache asks for them. A cache's hot path walks a keyed
structure larger than the TLB covers, which is what a huge page is for. A server
showed no measured gain for the extra resident memory. glibc reads the tunable
at process startup, and a running process cannot ask for itself. The daemon
therefore puts it in the worker's environment at exec.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RECV_MULTISHOT
Set to
.B 0
to arm one read at a time instead of a multishot receive.
On wherever the kernel supports it. A multishot arm is one submission for a
stream of messages instead of one per message. The switch exists so that the
two can be measured against each other on one binary.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RECV_BUNDLE
Set to
.B 0
to stop packing several provided buffers into one receive
.RB ( IORING_RECVSEND_BUNDLE ).
On where the kernel supports it. Support is probed once rather than assumed,
and the receive falls back to the plain multishot arm wherever the flag is
refused.
.IP
Both of these variables read
.B only
the exact string
.BR 0 .
.B SOCKETLEY_RECV_MULTISHOT=off
leaves multishot on, which is the opposite of what it looks like. The
.BI SOCKETLEY_NO_ *
switches below follow the other rule: any value except
.B 0
turns the feature off.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_SHARD_MESH
Any value but
.B 0
stops shards from linking to each other directly.
The shards then relay through the daemon, and the daemon logs at startup that
the mesh is off. This is the measurement control for the mesh: the two paths
differ only in the route, not in the build.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_PEER_LINKS
Any value but
.B 0
stops runtimes from opening direct links to their peers.
It exists for the same reason as
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_SHARD_MESH
and is read the same way.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_COMPANION
Any value but
.B 0
stops a unix\-socket listener from being placed beside a port.
.TP
.B RES_OPTIONS
Passes options to glibc's resolver; socketley itself does not read it.
If you have not set it, socketley sets it for worker processes to
.BR "timeout:1 attempts:2" .
.IP
It is set because
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
blocks the thread that calls it. In a worker, that thread is the event loop,
with the daemon's control socket armed on the same ring. While a lookup waits,
.B stop
and
.B stats
go unanswered. glibc's own default is five seconds, twice. Against a nameserver
that drops packets, that measured 10.0\ s for one lookup. And a client
re\-resolves on every reconnect once its cached address stops accepting. One
second twice has the same ceiling as two seconds once, and still retries a lost
packet.
.IP
If you set it yourself, your value is used unchanged, for the daemon and every
worker. Raise it if a slow but working nameserver is being given up on. The
cost of giving up is one more reconnect round, not a failed runtime.
.TP
.B EDITOR ", " VISUAL
Name the editor that
.B socketley edit
opens when given no flags.
.B VISUAL
is tried first.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Sets where that editor's temporary file is written.
While the editor is open, the file holds the runtime's entire configuration;
for a cache, that includes
.BR master_pw .
If
.I /tmp
is shared, point this at a directory of your own. The file is created 0600 and
removed when the editor exits.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_DNS_CACHE_MS
Sets how long, in milliseconds,
.B socketley.http()
reuses a resolved address before it asks the resolver again.
Default:
.BR 60000 .
.B 0
disables reuse and makes every call resolve.
.IP
This is a ceiling, not a promise. If a connect to the cached address is refused
or times out, the entry is dropped at once. An endpoint that moved is then
followed on the next call. So this value only decides how long the cache stays
wrong about an endpoint that moved
.I without
refusing, which is when something else answers on the old address.
.IP
The cache exists because
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
blocks the thread that calls it, glibc caches nothing of its own, and in a
worker that thread is the event loop. Every other resolver in this daemon is
bounded by something. A client and a server re-resolve at most once per second
of reconnect backoff, a proxy once per thirty-second sweep, and the rest once
at start. Without the cache,
.B socketley.http()
would resolve on every call. It resolves on a thread of its own and gives up at
the call's deadline. A resolver that does not answer therefore costs a call its
timeout, rather than costing the loop every retry the resolver makes.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RAFT_TICK_MS
Sets the Raft tick, in milliseconds, of a daemon started with
.BR \-\-raft\-listen .
Default:
.BR 100 .
The election timeout is ten to twenty ticks, and a heartbeat is sent every
tick. Every member of one cluster should therefore use the same value.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_RAFT_COMPACT_EVERY
Sets how many applied Raft entries go by between snapshots.
Default:
.BR 1000 .
A small value makes a joining node catch up through a snapshot; the test suite
sets it for that purpose.
.TP
.B SOCKETLEY_CONFIG
Sets the path to the daemon configuration file.
.SH FILES
.SS System Mode (installed via package)
.TP
.I /usr/bin/socketley
The socketley binary.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/socketley.service
The systemd unit file.
.TP
.I /run/socketley/socketley.sock
The Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
Mode
.BR 0660 ,
owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
.TP
.I /var/lib/socketley/runtimes/
The per-runtime JSON configuration files.
The directory has mode
.B 0750
and the files
.BR 0600 ,
all owned by
.BR socketley:socketley .
The files carry master passwords and private-key paths; see
.B A State File Is A Secret
under
.BR "STATE PERSISTENCE" .
.TP
.I /etc/socketley/config.lua
The daemon configuration file; see
.BR "Daemon Configuration" .
.TP
.I /etc/socketley/types/*.toml
The manifests of registered types, read at daemon startup; see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" .
.SS Development Mode (no system install)
.TP
.I /tmp/socketley.sock
The Unix domain socket for daemon IPC.
Mode
.BR 0600 ,
owned by the user who started the daemon.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes/
The per-runtime JSON configuration files, mode
.BR 0600 .
.TP
.I ~/.config/socketley/config.lua
The daemon configuration file.
The SOCKETLEY_CONFIG environment variable can name another path.
.TP
.I ~/.config/socketley/types/*.toml
The manifests of registered types, read at daemon startup; see
.BR "REGISTERED TYPES" .
.SS THE IPC SOCKET
Every
.B socketley
command travels over the socket above, and through it a client can reach
everything the daemon can do. That includes stopping a runtime, reading
.BR stats ,
rewriting a runtime's configuration with
.BR edit ,
and running arbitrary code as the user the daemon runs as. The last is possible
because a
.B \-\-lua
handler runs with the daemon's privileges; see
.B Handler Privileges
under
.BR "LUA CONFIGURATION" .
.PP
Opening the socket is therefore an administrative right, and the socket's mode
reflects that:
.PP
.nf
/run/socketley/socketley.sock   0660  socketley:socketley
any other path                  0600  the user who started the daemon
.fi
.PP
The packaged daemon runs as
.BR socketley ,
and the operator does not. The packaged socket is therefore shared with one
group, and membership of that group grants the right:
.PP
.nf
sudo usermod \-aG socketley "$USER"     # then log in again
.fi
.PP
A daemon on any other path keeps its socket to itself. This covers a
development daemon and a daemon started with
.BR SOCKETLEY_SOCKET .
The mode is owner\-only rather than owner\-and\-group because a shared primary
group is not unusual. Several distributions put every user in
.BR users ,
and there the daemon's group is every human on the machine.
.PP
.B "Earlier versions made this socket 0666"
(world\-writable) on both paths. Any local uid could drive any daemon on the
machine, whoever it belonged to. After an upgrade, a user who has been running
.B socketley
against a system daemon without being in the
.B socketley
group gets this error:
.PP
.nf
not permitted to open the daemon socket at /run/socketley/socketley.sock
.fi
.PP
That user needs to be added to the group. A user who is not added is not
locked out of the program. With the system socket unreachable, the CLI falls
back to development mode and starts a daemon of the user's own, with that
user's privileges and that user's runtimes.
.PP
On a machine where two people use development mode at once, the second person
gets the same message about
.IR /tmp/socketley.sock .
The path has one owner, and the socket is no longer shared. Sharing it was never
a supported arrangement; it was a shared daemon that nobody had asked for. Give
each person a socket of their own:
.PP
.nf
export SOCKETLEY_SOCKET="/tmp/socketley-$USER.sock"
.fi
.PP
This also gives each person a state directory of their own; see
.BR "WHERE STATE LIVES" .
.SS WHERE STATE LIVES
The two directories above are where a daemon's runtimes live when the daemon
serves the default socket. That covers every deployed daemon, and every daemon
that has never heard of
.BR SOCKETLEY_SOCKET .
This has not changed and will not change.
.PP
A daemon on any
.I other
socket path gets a directory of its own. The directory is a sibling of the
default one and is named after the socket path: the leading slash is dropped,
and every character outside
.BR A-Za-z0-9._-
becomes an underscore.
.PP
.nf
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET unset          ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET=/tmp/a.sock    ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes.tmp_a.sock
SOCKETLEY_SOCKET=/tmp/socketley.sock
                                ~/.local/share/socketley/runtimes
.fi
.PP
Setting the variable to the path that would have been used anyway counts as the
default, not as a second daemon. A script that spells out the default therefore
keeps the default directory. The directory name is readable rather than hashed
on purpose: the answer to "which of these is mine" should come out of an
.BR ls .
.PP
At startup the daemon logs the directory it is using
.RB ( "state directory: ..." ),
and that line is the authoritative answer. Learn where to find it before you
need it.
.PP
.B "Upgrading."
The first time a daemon uses one of these keyed directories, it copies
everything from the shared directory it used to read into the new directory,
once. An INFO line says how many runtimes were adopted and from where. The
originals stay exactly where they are, so an older binary rolled back onto the
machine still finds what it has always found. A
.I .adopted
file in the new directory records what happened, and makes the copy a one-time
event. Without that file, an operator who later removed every runtime would
find them all back after the next restart.
.PP
.B "...but never out from under a running daemon."
Adoption is skipped entirely while a daemon is serving the default socket path.
Those runtimes belong to that daemon, and adopting them would mean binding
ports it is already serving. The check is a probe of the default path; anything
answering there, daemon or not, is enough to skip adoption.
.PP
This is not a rare corner. An operator debugging beside a running daemon gets
into this situation with one command. The second bind
.I succeeds
under
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
so nothing but the port count would ever have reported the problem.
.PP
The daemon logs the skipped adoption at
.BR WARN ,
with both directory paths and what to do about it, because from the outside it
looks exactly as if the runtimes had vanished. It writes no
.I .adopted
marker, because "somebody is using these right now" is a fact about this minute,
not a decision. So once the daemon on the default path is stopped, restarting
this one adopts the runtimes as normal.
.PP
.B "The failure this trades against."
A daemon started with a
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
that it did not have last time finds no runtimes. On a development machine that
is correct: it is a different daemon, and it owns nothing. It cannot happen to
a deployed node. A deployed node runs on the default path with the variable
unset, and the default path is exempt from the rule for exactly that reason. If
it happens anyway, nothing has been destroyed. The other directory is still on
disk with everything in it, and starting the daemon with the variable it had
before brings it all back.
.PP
The alternative, one state directory for every daemon in the namespace, is not
the safe direction. Two daemons then bind each other's ports on boot, and each
reports the other's runtimes as its own. A
.B remove
sent to either one deletes a state file the other is still serving, and that
cannot be undone.
.SS Daemon Configuration
The daemon configuration file is a Lua script that sets a
.B daemon
table:
.nf
daemon = {
    name = "node1",          -- this daemon's name in a cluster
    cluster = "/shared/dir", -- cluster directory; needs name
    log_level = "info",      -- debug, info, warn, error
    metrics_port = 9100,     -- Prometheus metrics HTTP endpoint
    dashboard_credentials = "/etc/socketley/admin.creds",
                             -- the dashboard's admins sign in from here
    dashboard_idp = "auth",  -- or as admins of this idp runtime
    alert_webhook = "https://discord.com/api/webhooks/...",
                             -- where alerts are posted
    alert_mail = "mail://m/ops@example.com",
                             -- and mailed, by this mail runtime
    events = "bus",          -- an mqtt runtime the runtimes' states and
                             -- the alerts are published on
    cpus = "0-3",            -- where this daemon's I/O runs
    settings = {             -- any SOCKETLEY_* knob, by its short name
        storage_hash_threads = 4,
        lua_timeout_ms = 2000,
        proxy_splice = true,
    }
}
.fi
.PP
.B config
is the older name of the same table and still works. A file that sets both is
refused rather than merged.
.B name
and
.B cluster
are the two keys that
.B "socketley \-\-lua"
can also set on a daemon that is already running. Every other key is read only
from this file, when the daemon starts. A
.B \-\-cpus
typed on
.B "socketley daemon"
outranks the file.
.PP
.B settings
sets any variable under
.B ENVIRONMENT
that an operator may change, by its name in lower case and without
.BR SOCKETLEY_ .
For example,
.B storage_hash_threads
is
.BR SOCKETLEY_STORAGE_HASH_THREADS .
Write a number as a number, an on-or-off value as
.B true
or
.BR false ,
and anything else as a string. The daemon puts each setting in the environment
it starts its workers with, so a runtime reads the file's value just as it
would read the variable's.
.PP
The file takes precedence. A variable in the daemon's own environment counts
only for a setting that the file does not name. The default applies only when
neither sets a value. The daemon reports each of these on standard output and
does not start:
.IP \(bu 2
a name that is not a setting;
.IP \(bu 2
a value outside the setting's range;
.IP \(bu 2
a variable that a command reads from its own environment: the
.B SOCKETLEY_GIT_*
variables, and
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
and
.BR SOCKETLEY_CONFIG ,
which choose this file; also a variable the daemon sets for its workers, such as
.BR SOCKETLEY_FAST_LO .
.PP
.B socketley settings
shows the values that were taken.
.PP
When
.B metrics_port
is set, the daemon starts an HTTP endpoint. It serves a live web dashboard
at
.BR "GET /" ,
Prometheus exposition format metrics at
.BR "GET /metrics" ,
and JSON API endpoints at
.BR "/api/overview" ", " "/api/runtimes" ", and " "/api/runtime/<name>"
(see
.BR DASHBOARD ).
The metrics include runtimes total/running, connections total/active, messages
total, bytes in/out, and per-runtime breakdowns. Among the per-runtime metrics
are
.BR socketley_runtime_cpu_seconds_total ,
.B socketley_runtime_resident_memory_bytes
(resident memory, with a shared page counted once per shard) and
.B socketley_runtime_memory_bytes
(what the runtime costs the machine, with a shared page counted by the share it
holds). The daemon's own metrics are
.B socketley_daemon_cpu_seconds_total
and
.BR socketley_daemon_memory_bytes .
.PP
Each running runtime also exports the figures of its own
.BR stats .
Each figure becomes a metric in a family named after the runtime's type, with
the label
.BR name .
The families are:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_server_ *
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_client_ *
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_proxy_ *,
among them
.BR _backends ,
.BR _healthy_backends ,
.B _backend_responses_total
and
.B _backend_failures_total
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_cache_ *,
among them
.BR _keys ,
.B _get_hits_total
and
.B _get_misses_total
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_db_ *
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_dns_ *,
with
.B socketley_dns_queries_total
by
.B transport
and
.B socketley_dns_responses_total
by
.B rcode
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_idp_ *,
among them
.B _sign_ins_barred_total
(sign-ins refused for too many failures)
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_mqtt_ *
(messages by
.BR qos ,
drops by
.BR reason )
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_sync_ *,
among them
.B _failing
.IP \(bu 2
the storage, mail and tunnel families under
.B "STORAGE FLAGS"
(Metrics). A tunnel edge also adds
.B socketley_tunnel_agents_joined
(the agents expected to be linked) and
.B socketley_tunnel_agents_connected
(the agents that are linked).
.PP
Counters end in
.BR _total .
A figure that no family names is exported as
.BI socketley_ type _stat
with its name in the label
.BR stat .
.PP
Beside these are the facts that the daemon's alerts look at. Each is a series
that an alert rule can read:
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_runtime_state
(labels
.BR name ,
.BR type ,
.BR state ):
1 for the state a runtime is in, 0 for the other three.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_runtime_restarts_total :
the times a worker went down and was started again.
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_cert_expiry_seconds
(labels
.BR name ,
.BR path ):
the seconds until the certificate a runtime serves with ends, below 0 once it
has ended. It is read every five minutes.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_disk_free_ratio ,
.B socketley_disk_free_bytes
and
.B socketley_disk_size_bytes
(label
.BR path ):
the state directory's disk and each storage runtime's disk, each disk once.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_host_memory_available_ratio ,
.B _available_bytes
and
.BR _total_bytes .
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_runtime_quota_ratio :
how much of its limit a storage with
.BR \-\-max\-bytes ,
or a cache with
.B \-\-maxmemory
and no eviction, holds.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR socketley_mail_blocklisted :
1 while a blocklist from a mail runtime's
.B \-\-dnsbl
names the address the runtime sends from.
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_git_mirror_failed
(labels
.BR name ,
.BR repo ,
.BR id ):
1 when the last pull of a storage's git mirror failed, read every minute.
The
.B error
label holds what the failed pull said.
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_runtime_failure
(labels
.BR name ,
.BR type ,
.BR reason ):
1 for a runtime that failed, with its last warning or error line as the reason.
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_runtime_bytes_received_total
and
.BR _sent_total ,
per runtime.
.PP
The time work takes is a Prometheus histogram per runtime. The buckets start at
64 microseconds and double up to 16.8 seconds, plus
.BR +Inf .
Each request is measured from the start of the loop turn that read it to the
start of the turn that answered it. A request answered in the turn it came in
therefore measures 0, and no clock is read for it. The histograms are:
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_proxy_request_duration_seconds
(from an HTTP request's head to its answer's last byte)
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_proxy_backend_connect_duration_seconds
and
.B socketley_proxy_backend_first_byte_seconds
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_server_http_request_duration_seconds
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_cache_batch_duration_seconds
(from a read's commands to their replies being handed on)
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_db_request_duration_seconds
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_idp_sign_in_duration_seconds
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_mail_delivery_duration_seconds
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley_storage_request_duration_seconds
.PP
Each histogram has
.B _bucket
.RB ( le ),
.B _sum
and
.BR _count ,
with the shards' values added together. For example,
.B histogram_quantile(0.99, rate(socketley_proxy_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m]))
reads the 99th percentile of the proxy request histogram.
.SH C++ SDK
Socketley ships a modular C++ SDK in three tiers, in
.BR include/linux/socketley/ .
Tiers 1 and 3 are self-contained, header-only files.
Copy them anywhere and compile with
.BR "g++ -std=c++17" ;
no library is needed. Tier 2 must be built from source.
.PP
.TS
l l l l .
Tier	Header	Use Case	Dependencies
_
1	socketley/control.h	Control a running daemon	None (POSIX)
2	socketley/{server,cache,proxy,client}.h	Embed the full engine	libsocketley_sdk.a
3	socketley/attach.h	Register with a daemon	None (Tier 1)
.TE
.SS Tier 1: Daemon Control (header-only)
.B socketley/control.h
gives a program full control over a running daemon.
It uses the same Unix socket IPC as the CLI and needs nothing beyond POSIX.
.PP
.B "Result type:"
.nf
struct socketley::result {
    int         exit_code;  // 0=success, 1=bad input, 2=fatal, -1=connect fail
    std::string data;       // response body
};
.fi
.PP
.B "Runtime management functions"
(all in namespace
.BR socketley::ctl ):
.nf
command(cmd)               // raw command (glob patterns work)
command(socket_path, cmd)  // custom socket path
create(type, name, flags)  // "server", "client", "proxy", "cache"
start(name)                stop(name)
remove(name)               send(name, message)
ls()   ps()   stats(name)  show(name)
reload(name)               reload_lua(name)
edit(name, flags)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache string operations"
(over direct IPC; no TCP connection is needed):
.nf
cache_get(cache, key)           cache_set(cache, key, value)
cache_del(cache, key)           cache_exists(cache, key)
cache_incr(cache, key)          cache_decr(cache, key)
cache_incrby(cache, key, n)     cache_decrby(cache, key, n)
cache_append(cache, key, val)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache list operations:"
.nf
cache_lpush(cache, key, val)    cache_rpush(cache, key, val)
cache_lpop(cache, key)          cache_rpop(cache, key)
cache_llen(cache, key)          cache_lrange(cache, key, start, stop)
cache_lindex(cache, key, idx)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache set operations:"
.nf
cache_sadd(cache, key, member)  cache_srem(cache, key, member)
cache_sismember(cache, key, m)  cache_scard(cache, key)
cache_smembers(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache hash operations:"
.nf
cache_hset(cache, key, f, v)    cache_hget(cache, key, field)
cache_hdel(cache, key, field)   cache_hlen(cache, key)
cache_hgetall(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache TTL:"
.nf
cache_expire(cache, key, sec)   cache_ttl(cache, key)
cache_persist(cache, key)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache pub/sub:"
.nf
cache_publish(cache, ch, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache admin:"
.nf
cache_size(cache)               cache_memory(cache)
cache_keys(cache, pattern)
cache_flush(cache [, path])     cache_load(cache [, path])
.fi
.PP
To find the daemon's socket, the functions check the
.B SOCKETLEY_SOCKET
environment variable first, then
.BR /run/socketley/socketley.sock ,
then
.BR /tmp/socketley.sock .
Set the variable only to reach a daemon other than the one for this namespace.
In practice that means a second daemon on a development machine.
A deployed node runs one daemon on the default path.
.PP
.B "Example: lifecycle management"
.nf
#include <socketley/control.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    auto r = socketley::ctl::create("server", "myapp", "-p 9000 -s");
    if (r.exit_code != 0) return 1;

    socketley::ctl::send("myapp", "Hello from C++!");
    auto stats = socketley::ctl::stats("myapp");
    printf("%s\\n", stats.data.c_str());

    socketley::ctl::stop("myapp");
    socketley::ctl::remove("myapp");
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: fleet management with globs"
.nf
for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
    std::string name  = "api_" + std::to_string(i);
    std::string flags = "-p " + std::to_string(9000+i) + " -s";
    socketley::ctl::create("server", name, flags);
}
socketley::ctl::command("start api_*");    // start all
socketley::ctl::command("stats api_*");    // query all
socketley::ctl::command("stop api_*");     // stop all
socketley::ctl::command("remove api_*");   // remove all
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: cache operations"
.nf
socketley::ctl::create("cache", "store", "-p 6379 --mode admin -s");
socketley::ctl::cache_set("store", "user:1", "alice");
auto r = socketley::ctl::cache_get("store", "user:1");
printf("user:1 = %s\\n", r.data.c_str());  // "alice"
socketley::ctl::cache_del("store", "user:1");
socketley::ctl::cache_flush("store", "/tmp/store.bin");
.fi
.SS Tier 2: Embed Engine (build from source)
Tier 2 embeds the full io_uring event loop and all four runtime types in your own binary.
It gives maximum performance, with no IPC overhead.
It requires
.B libsocketley_sdk.a
and the link flags
.BR "-luring -lssl -lcrypto [-lluajit]" .
.PP
Headers:
.B socketley/core.h
(event_loop, runtime_manager, enums),
.B socketley/server.h ,
.B socketley/client.h ,
.B socketley/proxy.h ,
.B socketley/cache.h .
.PP
Define
.B SOCKETLEY_NO_LUA
to build without LuaJIT, and omit
.B -lluajit
from the link flags.
.PP
.B "High-Level Wrapper Classes (recommended)"
.PP
The
.B socketley::server\fR, \fBsocketley::client\fR, \fBsocketley::proxy\fR, and \fBsocketley::cache
classes wrap the raw engine API in a fluent, chainable interface.
They handle all the boilerplate (event loop, runtime manager, signal handling) internally.
.PP
.B "socketley::server"
.nf
server(uint16_t port)

// Config (all return server& for chaining)
tls(cert, key)         // terminates TLS on this listener
client_ca(ca)          // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
                       // (tls_ca(ca) is the older spelling; it also sets tls)
max_connections(n)     rate_limit(r)
global_rate_limit(r)   idle_timeout(s)       lua(path)
mode(m)                udp()                 http_dir(path)
http_cache()           upstream(addr)        master_pw(pw)
drain()                group(g)              tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks (return server& for chaining)
on_start(void())                             on_stop(void())
on_connect(void(int fd))                     on_disconnect(void(int fd))
on_message(void(int fd, string_view msg))    on_tick(void(double dt_ms))

// Actions
send(fd, msg)       broadcast(msg)      disconnect(fd)
peer_ip(fd)         clients()           multicast(fds, msg)
set_data(fd,k,v)    get_data(fd,k)

// Lifecycle
start()   stop()

// Escape hatches (raw engine access)
instance() -> server_instance*
manager()  -> runtime_manager&
loop()     -> event_loop&
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::client"
.nf
client(string_view host, uint16_t port)

// Config
tls()              // dial over TLS; the host from the constructor is pinned
                   // and the chain verified against the system trust store
tls_ca(ca)         // verify against that CA instead of the system store
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate this client presents when the peer asks
                   // for one - the client half of mutual TLS; needs tls()
reconnect(max)     mode(m)            udp()
lua(path)          tick_interval(ms)

// Callbacks
on_start    on_stop    on_connect    on_disconnect
on_message(void(string_view msg))   on_tick

// Actions
send(msg)

// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::proxy"
.nf
proxy(uint16_t port)

// Config
backend(addr)      strategy(s)        protocol(p)
tls(cert, key)     // terminates TLS on this proxy's listener
client_ca(ca)      // mutual TLS on it: clients must present a certificate
backend_ca(ca)     // trust store for the backend leg; turns that leg to TLS
client_cert(c, k)  // the certificate presented to a backend that asks
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "socketley::cache"
.nf
cache(uint16_t port)

// Config
persistent(path)   max_memory(bytes)   eviction(policy)
resp()             mode(m)
tls(cert, key)
max_connections(n) idle_timeout(s)     replicate(target)
lua(path)          group(g)

// Callbacks: on_start  on_stop
// Lifecycle: start()  stop()
// Escape hatches: instance()  manager()  loop()
.fi
.PP
.B "Limitations:"
Only one
.B start()
may run per process, because the signal handler points to one event_loop.
.B start()
blocks until SIGINT or SIGTERM.
For several runtimes in one process, or for asynchronous patterns, use the raw API,
either through the escape hatches or directly.
.PP
.B "TLS: on a server, a proxy, a client and a cache."
.B server().tls()
and
.B proxy().tls()
terminate TLS on that runtime's listener.
.BR proxy().backend_ca() ,
.B proxy().client_ca()
and
.B proxy().client_cert()
configure the proxy's two legs, exactly as the CLI flags do.
This works because
.B start()
goes through
.B runtime_manager::start
and reaches the same gate as the CLI.
.B client().tls()
dials over TLS, and
.B cache().tls()
terminates it on the cache's listener, through the same gate. See
.B TLS
under
.BR LIMITATIONS .
.PP
.B "Example: echo server (3 lines)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    socketley::server srv(9000);

    srv.on_connect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d connected\\n", fd);
    });
    srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
        srv.send(fd, "echo: " + std::string(msg));
    });
    srv.on_disconnect([](int fd) {
        printf("client %d disconnected\\n", fd);
    });

    srv.start();
}
// g++ -std=c++23 echo.cpp $(sdk/socketley-config --cflags --libs)
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: server with Lua scripting"
.nf
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    socketley::server srv(9000);
    srv.lua(argv[1]);
    srv.start();
}
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: TCP proxy"
.nf
socketley::proxy px(8080);
px.backend("127.0.0.1:9001")
  .backend("127.0.0.1:9002")
  .protocol(protocol_tcp)
  .strategy(strategy_round_robin);
px.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: Redis-compatible cache"
.nf
socketley::cache c(6379);
c.persistent("/var/data/store.dat")
 .resp()
 .max_memory(256 * 1024 * 1024)
 .eviction(evict_allkeys_lru);
c.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: client with auto-reconnect"
.nf
socketley::client cli("127.0.0.1", 9000);
cli.reconnect(0);  // 0 = infinite
cli.on_connect([&](int) { cli.send("hello"); });
cli.on_message([](std::string_view msg) {
    printf("%.*s\\n", (int)msg.size(), msg.data());
});
cli.start();
.fi
.PP
.B "Raw Engine API (advanced)"
.PP
For processes with several runtimes, or for custom control of the event loop,
use the raw types directly.
The wrapper classes use this API internally.
.PP
.B "Core pattern:"
create an event_loop, create a runtime_manager, create the runtimes,
configure them, run them, and run the loop.
.PP
.B "C++ callbacks"
(an alternative to Lua scripts; they can be set on any runtime):
.nf
set_on_start(function<void()>)
set_on_stop(function<void()>)
set_on_connect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_disconnect(function<void(int fd)>)
set_on_client_message(function<void(int fd, string_view)>)
set_on_message(function<void(string_view)>)
set_on_tick(function<void(double dt_ms)>)
.fi
.PP
.B "Common runtime setters:"
.nf
set_port(uint16_t)            set_event_loop(event_loop*)
set_runtime_manager(mgr*)     set_cache_name(string_view)
// set_tls / set_cert_path / set_key_path / set_ca_path are honoured
// on a server runtime and refused on every other type (see LIMITATIONS).
set_max_connections(uint32_t)
set_rate_limit(double)        set_idle_timeout(uint32_t)
set_log_file(string_view)     set_group(string_view)
set_drain(bool)               load_lua_script(string_view)
.fi
.PP
.B "Server-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(mode_inout|mode_in|mode_out|mode_master)
set_udp(bool)          set_http_dir(string_view)
broadcast(msg)         send_to(fd, msg)
disconnect(fd)         peer_ip(fd)
clients()              multicast(fds, msg)
.fi
.PP
.B "Cache-specific:"
.nf
set_mode(cache_mode_readonly|readwrite|admin)
set_persistent(path)   set_max_memory(bytes)
set_resp_forced(bool)  set_replicate_target(host:port)
.fi
.PP
.B "Proxy-specific:"
.nf
set_protocol(protocol_http|protocol_tcp)
set_strategy(strategy_round_robin|random|lua)
add_backend(addr)      clear_backends()
.fi
.PP
.B "Example: multi-runtime with raw API (server + cache)"
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/cache.h>

// ... (event_loop + runtime_manager setup as above)

mgr.create(runtime_cache, "store");
auto* cache = static_cast<cache_instance*>(mgr.get("store"));
cache->set_port(6379);
cache->set_mode(cache_mode_admin);
cache->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
cache->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.create(runtime_server, "api");
auto* srv = static_cast<server_instance*>(mgr.get("api"));
srv->set_port(9000);
srv->set_cache_name("store");  // clients can "cache set k v"
srv->set_runtime_manager(&mgr);
srv->set_event_loop(&loop);

mgr.start("store", loop);
mgr.start("api", loop);
loop.run();
.fi
.SS Cross-Platform SDK (Windows / macOS)
These header-only TCP clients connect to Socketley runtimes from non-Linux platforms.
One copy lives in
.BR include/portable/socketley/ ,
and a program reaches it as
.B <socketley/ws_client.h>
from any of
.IR include/linux ", " include/darwin " or " include/windows .
.PP
.B socketley/ws_client.h
is a WebSocket (RFC 6455) and raw TCP client for server runtimes.
It embeds its own SHA-1 and Base64, so it needs no OpenSSL.
Its modes are
.BR ws_mode::websocket " and " ws_mode::raw_tcp .
.PP
.B socketley/cache_client.h
is a text-protocol client for cache runtimes. It supports all cache commands
(get, set, del, lpush, sadd, hset, expire, publish, subscribe, etc.).
It returns a
.B cache_result
with the fields
.IR ok ", " value ", " values ", " integer .
.PP
Build with C++17; there are no external dependencies:
.RS
.nf
# MSVC
cl /std:c++17 /I include\\windows app.cpp

# clang++ (macOS)
clang++ -std=c++17 -I include/darwin app.cpp -o app

# g++ (Linux)
g++ -std=c++17 -I include/linux app.cpp -o app
.fi
.RE
.SS Tier 3: Daemon Attach (header-only)
.B socketley/attach.h
registers your own process as a runtime with a running daemon.
After it attaches, your process shows up in
.BR "socketley ls" ,
.BR "socketley stats" ,
and
.BR "socketley ps" .
The header is self-contained (it builds on control.h).
It deregisters automatically on
.BR exit() .
.PP
.B "API:"
.nf
bool daemon_attach(name, type, port)  // register, auto-detach on exit
void daemon_detach(name)              // explicit deregister
void daemon_detach()                  // deregister attach'd name
.fi
.PP
.B "Example:"
.nf
#include <socketley/attach.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    // Start your own TCP server (any framework)
    // ...bind, listen on port 8080...

    if (socketley::daemon_attach("myservice", "server", 8080))
        printf("registered with daemon\\n");
    else
        printf("daemon not running (standalone mode)\\n");

    // Your accept loop here...
    // atexit() automatically calls daemon_detach()
}
// g++ -std=c++17 myapp.cpp -Iinclude/linux -o myapp
.fi
.PP
.B daemon_attach()
returns false when the process is not allowed to open the daemon's IPC socket.
Every
.B socketley
command needs the same permission; see
.B THE IPC SOCKET
under
.BR FILES .
A program that runs as a different user from the daemon must be in the
.B socketley
group to register with it.
A false result is not an error to abort on.
The example above shows the intended pattern: the program runs standalone
and is simply not listed in the daemon's inventory.
.SS Mixed: Engine + Daemon Attach (Tier 2 + 3)
Embed the full engine for maximum performance, and register with the daemon
for fleet monitoring. This combines the strengths of both tiers.
.PP
.nf
#include <socketley/server.h>
#include <socketley/attach.h>

socketley::server srv(9000);
srv.on_message([&](int fd, std::string_view msg) {
    srv.send(fd, msg);
});

// Register with daemon so socketley ls/stats can see it
socketley::daemon_attach("app_srv", "server", 9000);

srv.start();  // daemon sees the runtime
.fi
.PP
.B "When to use each tier:"
.PP
.TP
.B "Tier 1"
Use it for orchestration scripts, deployment tools, monitoring and CI/CD.
.TP
.B "Tier 2"
Use it for custom daemons, game servers, IoT gateways and performance-critical services.
.TP
.B "Tier 3"
Use it for legacy services, third-party processes, and fleet visibility for external apps.
.TP
.B "Mixed"
Use it for an embedded engine with daemon monitoring: maximum performance with fleet management.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.SS WebSocket and other protocol upgrades
A proxy in
.B http
mode relays a connection that has switched protocols.
This is what lets a
.B ws://
client work through it.
This section describes what that relay does, and what it deliberately does not do.
.PP
Only the two endpoints negotiate the upgrade; nothing between them takes part.
The proxy forwards the request's
.B Upgrade
and
.B Connection
headers unchanged, instead of stripping them as hop-by-hop headers.
It relays the
.B 101
response verbatim and computes no part of it.
It does no
.B Sec\-WebSocket\-Accept
calculation, does not check that the key matches, and has no opinion about the
protocol named.
The proxy reacts to the 101, not to the token, so it relays any upgrade that two
endpoints agree on in the same way.
The exception is
.B h2c
(and
.BR h2 ).
The proxy strips that offer, together with its
.B HTTP2\-Settings
field, and answers it as a plain request.
HTTP/2 behind a 101 would carry every later request past the checks the proxy
makes on each request head.
.PP
.B "Frames are never parsed."
After the 101, the connection carries opaque bytes.
The proxy does not know where one frame ends and the next begins.
So it does no fragmentation handling, answers no ping or pong on a backend's
behalf, negotiates no
.BR permessage\-deflate ,
and applies no per-message limit.
.B \-\-max\-connections
still counts connections; nothing counts frames.
The
.B on_proxy_request
and
.B on_proxy_response
callbacks still see these bytes.
However, they see whatever one read delivered, not whole frames.
A callback that rewrites them corrupts the stream, because a frame carries its
own length and a client-to-server frame is masked.
.PP
.B "A tunnelled backend connection is never pooled."
There is no message boundary at which to return it to the pool.
So one WebSocket uses one backend TCP connection for its whole life, however
long that is. A proxy in front of many long-lived WebSocket clients holds that
many backend sockets.
.PP
The deadlines behave as follows.
.B \-\-backend\-timeout
limits the wait for the backend's first response byte, and the 101 is that byte.
So it applies to the handshake and stops applying the moment the protocol
switches. A tunnel is not measured against it, and the proxy never answers
.B 504
because a tunnel is quiet.
That is intended: on a tunnel, nothing may arrive for minutes.
.B \-\-idle\-timeout
still applies. On a tunnelled connection it counts bytes in
.I either
direction, not only what the client sends, so a stream the backend is feeding
is not idle. A tunnel that is silent in both directions for longer than the
timeout is closed, like any other connection.
.PP
The circuit breaker counts a tunnel as one successful response (the 101) and
nothing more. A session that ends is a connection closing, and the proxy does
not charge it to the backend. In
.BR stats ,
the bytes relayed after the 101 are counted in
.B bytes_in
and
.BR bytes_out .
.B total_messages
counts the reads that carried them, not frames or requests.
So a proxy carrying WebSocket traffic reports figures that do not match messages
in either protocol's sense.
.PP
A proxy in
.B \-\-protocol tcp
mode is not affected by any of this. It was already a byte relay and never looked
at a status line.
.SS What can stop the event loop
A process serves its runtimes on one thread: a worker serves its one runtime,
and the daemon serves the runtimes that scripts make in it. A call that waits
therefore stops every runtime of that process. Five places make such calls,
and they are listed here.
.PP
The figures were measured where measuring was possible, on a Lua echo server
and a static file server under load. Each cost is given as a share of a
worker's CPU on a healthy machine. The figures do not say what the same call
costs when the disk is busy or the resolver is down, which is the case where
they matter.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B socketley.http()
outside a callback that can wait.
The whole exchange blocks, once per call from a script: name resolution, the
connect and the transfer. It is bounded by the call's
.B timeout_ms
and by the callback's remaining budget. In
.BR on_message ,
.BR on_client_message ,
an
.B mcp
tool's handler and the other callbacks that can wait, the call instead waits on
the loop and blocks nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR open (2)
and
.BR fstat (2)
in the static file path, once per request that
.B \-\-http\-cache
does not answer.
Measured at about 4.8 per cent of the worker: 1.7 in the kernel's open path
and 3.1 in
.BR statx ,
which is what glibc turns an
.B fstat
into.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
on a client's first dial and in a proxy's setup.
Both happen before the runtime serves anything, so a start against a resolver
that is not answering takes as long as the resolver does.
Other lookups ask the nameservers of
.I /etc/resolv.conf
from the loop itself, after checking
.IR /etc/hosts .
These are a client's reconnect, a server's upstream, a cache's
.BR \-\-replicate ,
a proxy's later lookups, and every webhook, replication target and tier named
by a host. They use UDP on a socket that never waits, and take the answer the
next time they look. No call there takes longer than a tenth of a millisecond,
even when a nameserver does not answer; that nameserver is passed over for the
next one at its timeout. A name these lookups cannot answer, such as one only
an NSS module knows or an answer that was cut short, goes to the system's
resolver on a thread.
.IP \(bu 2
The mutation journal's flush, once per
.B \-\-persistent\-interval
while anything is buffered. On a healthy disk this is only a write to the page
cache. On a full or slow disk, the write keeps a whole interval's worth of
clients waiting.
.IP \(bu 2
The cache's snapshot and its
.BR fsync ,
at teardown rather than periodically.
That is why it is listed last among the disk costs rather than first.
A stop takes as long as the dump takes.
.RE
.PP
A runtime's state file is not among them. The runtime writes and syncs it,
together with its directory, on a thread of its own whenever its state changes,
so no loop waits for that disk.
.PP
.B "A storage runtime blocks twice, and never while it serves."
On start, before its listener exists, it reads the directory and removes
temporary files. After any stop that was not clean, it also reads every
object's header to reconcile the journals. That start takes as long as the
object count requires. The work runs on threads with many reads at once, and
all of it has ended before the runtime serves. On stop, the runtime syncs a
marker file that records a clean stop. Between the two, every file read and
write is an io_uring operation. So a slow disk adds latency to the requests
waiting for it; it does not stop the runtime from answering its other
connections.
.PP
.B "What is NOT on this list, though it was suspected and measured."
A static file server spends 7.3 per cent of its worker in the kernel clearing
pages. That looks like allocation churn, but it is not. The same server took
fourteen minor page faults in total while answering half a million requests.
So the pages are the kernel's own, for the 1.7 GB a second it puts on the
wire. The malloc and free around each file buffer are served from the heap and
never reach the kernel.
.SS Out\-of\-process runtimes
Every runtime made with
.B create
or restored at boot, of any type, runs in its own supervised worker process.
There is no flag to keep one in the daemon.
Only a server, client, proxy, cache, storage or dns runtime that a script makes
with
.B socketley.create
runs in the daemon.
This section states plainly what that costs.
.PP
.B "The script runs in the worker and nowhere else."
An out\-of\-process runtime exists twice.
The worker owns the listening socket and serves every byte.
A placeholder in the daemon carries the runtime's name, port, group and state,
so that
.BR ls ,
.B stats
and the endpoint table have something to answer with. The script belongs to the
worker. The placeholder compiles the script and then discards it without
executing a line. Because it compiles the script,
.B create
still refuses a file that does not parse on the spot, instead of reporting
"the worker exited and the runtime is failed".
.IP
The script's
.B routes
declaration still reaches the daemon. The declaration is produced by running
the script, and only the worker runs it. The worker encodes the declaration
into a
.B routes
frame on the control socket it already holds. The supervisor decodes the frame
onto the placeholder, and the daemon publishes the worker's copy in preference
to a local one. Without it,
.B publish_endpoints
would publish nothing for the runtime. A gateway in front of it would then
forward every method instead of refusing undeclared ones at the edge with a
405, and that enforcement would stop existing without notice. A
.B reload\-lua
on such a runtime waits for the new declaration before republishing.
So the gateway never enforces the old script's methods against the new
script's handler. The declaration belongs to a handler rather than a proxy,
which is why it matters now that every type has its own process by default.
.PP
.B "create waits for the worker's verdict."
Compiling the script in the placeholder catches a file that does not parse, and
nothing else. The rest of the configuration is judged in the worker, where the
script actually runs, the port is actually bound and the TLS pair is actually
read. So
.B create
does not answer as soon as it has forked a process. It waits until that process
says whether it can serve what it was given.
.IP
Three things end the wait. If the worker reports that it is serving,
.B create
exits 0. If the worker refuses, or starts but cannot bind, resolve its backend
or load its certificates,
.B create
exits 1 and the daemon's log gives the reason. The runtime is then left
.BR failed ,
where
.B ls
shows it, rather than removed, so there is still something to ask about.
If neither answer arrives within two seconds,
.B create
exits 2 and says so. The runtime stays under supervision, and
.B ls
reports whatever state it settles on.
.IP
The two-second bound is a backstop, not a budget. A worker that has to compile
a script, build its ring and bind a listener reports back in about 20 ms.
A refusal comes back sooner, because the child stops at the script. Only a
worker that has wedged reaches two seconds.
.B create
never blocks indefinitely on a child.
.PP
.B "A runtime in a worker can still ask another runtime by name."
.B \-\-cache
works across the process boundary in both directions. When a process gets a
request for a name it does not hold, it sends the request out on the control
socket. The daemon holds the whole map and is the only process that can route.
It looks the name up, posts the request to whatever answers for it (in the
daemon or in another worker), and writes the answer back. Neither the asking
runtime nor the client on the other end of the socket can tell which
arrangement served it. A request for a name that nothing answers to is refused
with the same
.B "error: no such runtime"
that an in\-process runtime gives; it is not dropped silently.
.PP
.B "The cross\-runtime management API works inside a worker."
.BR socketley.create ,
.BR .start ,
.BR .stop ,
.BR .remove ,
.BR .send ,
.BR .list ,
.BR .get ,
.B .pipe
and the
.B socketley.cluster
table are answered as they are in the daemon; see
.B Process Isolation
under
.BR "PROXY FLAGS" .
A worker refuses them only when the daemon that forked it could give it no
control socket, which means an older build.
.IP
.B "self.route() crosses the boundary."
It names another runtime, but it works from a worker.
.BR route_client ()
does not need a file descriptor to travel to the other process. It needs the
target's connection tables, which it reaches by
.B conn_ref
through
.BR shared/ipc/route_relay.h .
The origin keeps the socket and relays the bytes, exactly as it does when both
runtimes are in one process.
.IP
.BR unroute() ,
.B get_route()
and
.B owner_send()
answer from this runtime's own tables.
.PP
.B "Cross\-runtime pub/sub reaches a worker."
A cache's
.B PUBLISH
is delivered to a worker's
.B subscribe
callbacks over the worker's control socket. The daemon does not run those
callbacks against the placeholder it keeps for the runtime. Delivery is
one\-way and best\-effort, like every other frame on that socket. A publish is
an event, not a state. So a frame dropped because the child is not reading is
not re\-sent. A frame that arrives before the worker has been configured is
discarded, not replayed to a subscriber that did not exist when it happened.
.PP
.B "Interactive mode (start \-i) is not offered for a proxy, on either path."
Interactive mode is a stream. What is typed reaches a server's clients, a
client's server, or a cache's command parser. A proxy relays between a client
and a backend and speaks for neither. So typed input has nothing to mean, and
offering it would mean inventing a meaning rather than exposing one.
The command refuses and exits 1
.IR "without starting anything" .
.PP
.B "A worker's figures are read when they are printed."
For an out\-of\-process runtime,
.BR connections ,
.BR total_connections ,
.BR total_messages ,
.B bytes_in
and
.B bytes_out
are summed from a small shared\-memory region at the moment the command prints
them. Each worker stores its own line in that region once per turn of its
event loop, and the daemon adds the lines up. There is no round trip, and
nothing waits on a child that may be wedged. There is also no interval between
reading and printing in which a figure could go stale.
.PP
So every figure is identical on both paths, which is the property the split
exists to have. The
.B figures:pushed
and
.BI figures_age_ms: n
lines of earlier releases no longer exist, and nothing replaces them:
there is no age left to report.
.PP
The part of
.B stats
that is not a summable counter is still computed inside the worker. Examples
are a cache's key count, a proxy's healthy backends and the ring's granted
polling mode. They are walks of tables that exist only in the worker. The worker
sends this part over the control socket when it changes. For a runtime with no
traffic, that is never: an idle worker sends nothing. It wakes only for a TLS
listener's check of its certificate files, once every two seconds.
.PP
Until the first such report arrives,
.B stats
prints
.B worker_report:none
in place of those figures. Ordinarily that window is the first fraction of a
second after a start. A runtime that shows it while serving traffic has a
worker that is alive but not publishing: it is wedged, or its frames are being
lost. That line is the difference between "no data" and "no traffic".
.PP
.B "And which process it is."
.B stats
prints
.BI pid: n
for a runtime that has its own process: an out\-of\-process proxy's worker, or a
managed external runtime's child. Nothing else in the output says that the
runtime runs elsewhere. So this line names the process to strace, to read
.B /proc
for, or to charge a machine's memory to. An in\-process runtime prints no such
line. Neither does a runtime whose worker has been stopped or reaped. A pid
that has been returned to the system is worse than none, because somebody will
go and look at that number.
.PP
A sharded runtime prints
.BI shards: n
and
.B shard_pids:
instead, because there are
.I n
processes and naming one of them arbitrarily would be wrong. Its counters
are still one set of figures: the shared region has one line per shard, and the
sum over them is what the runtime is serving.
.PP
Every type is split, and no flag keeps one in the daemon.
The management API,
.B self.route()
and the figures work in a worker as they do in the daemon.
.SS TLS
.B "Every runtime type speaks TLS."
This covers a server's and a cache's own listener, both legs of a proxy, and a
client runtime's dial.
A
.B server
created with
.B "\-\-tls \-\-cert"
.I file
.B \-\-key
.I file
accepts connections, handshakes and moves records itself.
OpenSSL never touches the socket. Each connection gets a memory BIO pair.
The same io_uring submissions that carry every other byte in the daemon carry
the ciphertext in and out. The handshake advances one completion at a time, so
nothing blocks the event loop. Protocol detection, WebSocket, HTTP and RESP all
run on the decrypted stream, so
.B wss://
and
.B https://
work on a listener configured this way.
.BI \-\-ca " file"
adds mutual TLS: a client must present a certificate signed by that CA.
.PP
A peer that opens a connection and never finishes the handshake is dropped
after 30 seconds. This limit is fixed, not configurable, and deliberately
separate from
.BR \-\-idle\-timeout .
A connection that completed its handshake and then went quiet is legitimate on
a daemon built for long\-living ones. A peer that has sent nothing the record
layer accepted has not become a client at all. Without the limit, it could hold
a slot against
.B \-\-max\-connections
for as long as it liked. The clock starts when the handshake does, and later
bytes do not move it. So a peer that dribbles inside an unfinished record is
dropped on the same schedule as one that sends nothing at all. A
.B client
runtime applies the same 30 seconds to a target that accepts its connection
and then never handshakes. After that,
.B \-\-reconnect
applies to it like any other lost connection.
.PP
A
.B proxy
runs the same record layer on both of its legs. The same three flags terminate
TLS on its listening port, and
.BI \-\-client\-ca " file"
makes it demand a certificate from its callers.
The backend leg uses TLS when the backend address is
.BR https:// ,
or when
.BR \-\-ca ,
.B \-\-client\-cert
or
.B \-\-client\-key
is given; those three apply to every backend.
.B \-\-client\-cert
and
.B \-\-client\-key
present a certificate to a backend that asks for one.
The proxy always verifies a TLS backend connection: against
.B \-\-ca
or the system store, with the endpoint's name checked, and with TLS 1.2 as the
minimum version. There is no option to turn that off. These connections are
pooled and reused together with their sessions, so a handshake is paid once per
idle period rather than once per request. A pooled socket is only ever returned
to the endpoint it was opened to, which is the name it was verified against.
.PP
.B "\-\-health\-check http"
works against a TLS backend and probes it over TLS. The probe uses the same
record layer as the data path: a memory BIO pair, records carried by ordinary
io_uring submissions, and a handshake advanced one completion at a time. It is
held to the same standard. The chain is checked against
.B \-\-ca
or the system store, the certificate against the endpoint's own name, and TLS
1.2 is the minimum. A backend whose certificate does not verify is marked
.B unhealthy
rather than reported healthy. That is the only answer that agrees with what
the data path would do with it. Everything above the record layer is unchanged:
the probe fetches the same
.BI \-\-health\-path " path" ,
and only a 2xx counts.
.PP
A
.B client
runtime runs the dialling half of the same record layer on its outbound
socket. When created with
.BR \-\-tls ,
it puts a ClientHello on the wire as soon as it has a socket. It advances the
handshake one completion at a time, exactly as the server and the proxy do. The
line framing and the Lua callbacks above it see the same plaintext they always
did. The client verifies the peer against
.BI \-\-ca " file"
or the system store. The certificate must be valid for the host in
.BR \-t :
its DNS SAN must match, or its IP SAN for an address literal.
.BI \-\-cert " file"
and
.BI \-\-key " file"
present a certificate to a peer that asks for one. By design, there is no
skip-verify option. When a peer fails either check, the client drops the
socket and re-dials on its usual backoff. It never retries in cleartext.
.PP
A
.B cache
terminates TLS on its own listener with the same three flags a server uses, and
.BI \-\-ca " file"
demands a client certificate there in the same way. RESP is auto-detected from
the first byte of the decrypted stream. So an existing Redis client speaks the
protocol it always did, one layer up, and the line protocol works on the same
port.
.PP
.B "A cache still cannot replicate over TLS."
Replication is not a listener, and nothing encrypts it. A follower dials its
leader itself, in cleartext. On a leader, a promoted follower is written to by
a second writer that goes around the record layer by design. So the
combination is refused rather than half-honoured.
.B \-\-tls
together with
.B \-\-replicate
is rejected at
.BR create ,
by
.BR edit " and " import ,
and again at every
.BR start .
A state file that carries both leaves the runtime
.B failed
rather than letting it come up quietly wrong. A TLS cache that a
.B REPLICATE
command asks to become a leader answers with an error instead of promoting
itself. A cache encrypted for its clients but cleartext for its replicas would
be worse than an honestly cleartext one, because only the first looks safe.
.PP
Two further combinations are refused for the same reason:
.B \-\-udp
on a client or a server, because DTLS is not implemented; and, on a server,
.BR \-\-upstream ,
because the upstream connection would still be made in cleartext. A proxy in
.B \-\-protocol tcp
mode terminates TLS on its listener like any other proxy and forwards the
decrypted stream to its backend. On any connection with a TLS session, it
bypasses the zero-copy splice path, because a splice would move bytes the
record layer never saw.
.PP
There is one outbound exception, and it is not transport.
.B socketley.http()
in a Lua callback speaks HTTPS. It verifies the certificate chain and the host
name, with TLS 1.2 as the minimum version (see
.BR "Global Functions" ).
It is a client call that a script makes. It encrypts nothing that socketley
itself serves or forwards.
.PP
Anything that is not implemented fails closed, and so does every incomplete
configuration:
.IP \(bu 2
.B create
rejects
.BR \-\-tls ", " \-\-cert ", " \-\-key " and " \-\-ca
on a
.BR dns ", " sync ", " idp ", " db ", " tunnel ", " metrics " or " mcp
runtime, which has no handshake, and
.BR \-\-client\-ca ", " \-\-client\-cert " and " \-\-client\-key
on anything but a proxy. It exits with status 1 and a message that names the
flag.
.IP \(bu 2
On a
.BR server ", " client " or " proxy
runtime the same flags are accepted, but only as a complete set.
.B \-\-tls
without a certificate and key on a
.BR server " or " proxy
is refused, and no runtime is created. So is a certificate or trust store without
.B \-\-tls
on any of the three. Either would describe a listener that could only serve
cleartext, or a dial that would be made in cleartext. On a
.BR client ,
.B \-\-tls
alone is complete, but half a
.BR \-\-cert / \-\-key
pair is refused: it would present nothing while the flags said a certificate
was configured. On a proxy,
.B \-\-client\-ca
without
.B \-\-tls
is refused, and so is half a
.BR \-\-client\-cert / \-\-client\-key
pair, for the same reason.
.IP \(bu 2
Flag-based
.B edit
applies the same rule. It restores the previous TLS settings if the edit
would leave them incomplete.
.B edit
accepts a subset of the flags of
.BR create ,
so most flags it does not accept come back as
.BR "unknown flag" .
These flags do not, because changing TLS on a live runtime is exactly what an
operator would be attempting.
.IP \(bu 2
.B import
applies the same rule to the JSON document's fields.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR start ", " reload
and the daemon's own boot-time restore refuse a runtime whose configuration
cannot be honoured. This is how a runtime restored from a state file written by
an older release is caught. Nothing is brought up, and the runtime goes to
.BR failed .
.IP \(bu 2
A certificate or key that cannot be read, or a key that does not match its
certificate, fails the runtime at start. The port is never bound.
.PP
To clear the settings from a runtime whose configuration is refused, dump it,
delete the TLS fields, and import it back (see
.BR import ).
.PP
The embedded C++ SDK reaches the same gate, because its
.B start()
goes through
.BR runtime_manager::start .
So
.B server().tls(cert, key)
and
.B proxy().tls(cert, key)
terminate TLS exactly as a daemon-managed runtime does.
.B client().tls()
dials over TLS, and
.B cache().tls(cert, key)
terminates it, through the same gate.
.PP
For encrypted transport on a server's upstream connections, terminate TLS in a
front-end proxy
(nginx, HAProxy, stunnel). Have it forward over loopback or another trusted
network path. A socketley
.B proxy
can play that part.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error, such as an invalid command, a runtime that was not found, or a rejected TLS flag.
.TP
.B 2
A fatal error, or no answer in time: the daemon could not be reached, or a
runtime's workers stopped answering before it was serving (see
.B create
and
.BR start ).
The runtime may still come up; check with
.BR "socketley ls" .
